window.OCHEM_DB=[{"q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with NaOH in DMSO (a polar aprotic solvent). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CCCCO","CC[C@@H](C)O","CC(C)CO","CC[C@H](C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"A secondary halide with a strong, small anionic nucleophile (hydroxide) in a polar aprotic solvent (DMSO) reacts by SN2: backside attack with inversion at the stereocenter. Because OH takes over the top CIP priority that Br held, the physical inversion also flips the descriptor, so (R)-2-bromobutane gives (S)-2-butanol. (R)-2-Butanol would require retention, which SN2 cannot give; 1-butanol and 2-methyl-1-propanol put the oxygen on the wrong carbon or the wrong skeleton; and (S)-2-bromobutane still carries the leaving group and is simply the enantiomer of the starting halide, not a product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 inversion correct, opt0 (S) verified by RDKit, all SMILES valid, single reactive center + stereo, in DAT scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated in water (H2O) as the solvent. What is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)CC","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)CCO","CCC(C)CO","CC(C)C(C)O"],"correct":1,"why":"A tertiary halide with only a weak, neutral nucleophile (water) reacts by SN1. Ionization gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation directly, so there is nothing to gain by rearranging; water attacks that cation and loses a proton to give 2-methyl-2-butanol, with the OH on the same carbon that held Br. The primary and secondary alcohols (3-methyl-1-butanol, 2-methyl-1-butanol, 3-methyl-2-butanol) put the OH on carbons that never carried positive charge. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the competing E1 product, an alkene, not the substitution product the question asks for.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN1 substitution product correct, exactly one substitution answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-Iodooctane (drawn) is treated with NaCN in acetone. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC[C@H](C)I","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=N[C@H](C)CCCCCC","CCCCCC[C@H](C)C#N","CCCCCCC(C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@@H](C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@@H](C)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Cyanide is a strong, unhindered nucleophile, and the substrate is a secondary halide with an excellent leaving group (iodide) in a polar aprotic solvent, so the reaction is SN2 with inversion. Cyanide bonds through carbon, and the nitrile carbon (its triple bond to N counts as N,N,N) outranks the hexyl chain, so CN takes the top priority that iodine held; the inversion therefore flips the descriptor, and (S)-2-iodooctane gives (R)-2-methyloctanenitrile. The (S)-nitrile would require retention; the nitrile drawn without a defined stereocenter ignores the stereospecific inversion; (R)-2-octanol would need hydroxide or water as the nucleophile; and the structure with nitrogen rather than carbon attached to the chain has the wrong connectivity, because cyanide attacks through carbon.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 + CIP-aware inversion to R verified, valid SMILES, two layered concepts (mechanism + stereo) acceptable at difficulty 3, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl bromide (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc(CBr)cc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Methoxide is a strong nucleophile, and the carbon bearing bromine is a primary benzylic CH2, unhindered and activated, so SN2 is fast and gives benzyl methyl ether. Benzyl alcohol would require hydroxide or water as the nucleophile; anisole puts the oxygen directly on the ring, which would mean breaking an aromatic C-H bond instead of the C-Br bond; styrene is an elimination product that cannot form because the ring carbon next to the CH2Br has no hydrogen to lose; and 4-methylbenzyl bromide adds a carbon to the ring that was never present.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 benzyl methyl ether correct, single answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-(1-Bromoethyl)benzene (drawn), a secondary benzylic halide, is solvolyzed in water (H2O). What best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](Br)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCCc1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"A secondary benzylic halide with a weak nucleophile (water) reacts by SN1 through a planar, resonance-stabilized benzylic cation. Water attacks either face of that flat cation at equal rates, so the stereocenter is scrambled and the product is racemic 1-phenylethanol, best drawn without a defined configuration. Either single enantiomer of 1-phenylethanol overstates what SN1 can deliver (one would be retention, the other inversion, and both form equally). 2-Phenylethanol puts the OH on the wrong carbon, and styrene is the E1 alkene, not the substitution product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN1 racemization concept correct, opt0 (no stereocenter) is the right racemic representation, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (2,2-dimethyl-1-bromopropane, drawn) is treated with NaI in acetone. Which product, if any, forms, and what governs the rate?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)I","COCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CI","CC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"Iodide is a strong nucleophile and the C-Br carbon is primary, so the only available path is SN2; no carbocation forms, so no rearrangement is possible. The reaction is very slow because the three methyls on the neighboring quaternary carbon block the backside approach, but when it does react it gives simple substitution: neopentyl iodide (1-iodo-2,2-dimethylpropane). 2-Iodo-2-methylbutane is the rearranged tertiary iodide, which would need a carbocation that SN2 never generates; neopentyl alcohol and neopentyl methyl ether use the wrong nucleophile; and 2,2-dimethylbutane adds a carbon and has no halogen at all.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 with no rearrangement correct, opt1 rearrangement trap is a strong distractor, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane (drawn) is treated with NaOH in DMSO. What is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](Br)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CC=CCC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydroxide is a strong nucleophile and the C-Br carbon is a secondary ring carbon, so substitution goes by SN2: backside attack with inversion. In the cis substrate the bromine sits on the same face as the tert-butyl group; inversion moves the new OH to the opposite face, giving trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol. The cis alcohol would require retention, which SN2 cannot give; 4-tert-butylcyclohexene is the elimination product, not a substitution product; the cyclopentanol has the wrong ring size; and the trans bromide is just the diastereomer of the starting material with no new bond formed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"Keyed answer (trans alcohol, opt0) is correct and question is unambiguous; minor wording slip in rationale: opt4 is actually the trans-bromide, not literally the cis starting material, but it remains a valid wrong distractor and does not affect the single correct answer."},{"q":"(R)-2-Chlorobutane (drawn) is treated with NaSH in ethanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)S","CC=CC","CCCCS","CC[C@H](C)S"],"correct":4,"why":"Hydrosulfide (HS-) is a strong, highly polarizable nucleophile but a weak base, so on a secondary halide it gives SN2 substitution with inversion rather than elimination. Sulfur takes over the top CIP priority that chlorine held, so the inversion flips the descriptor: (R)-2-chlorobutane gives (S)-2-butanethiol. The (R)-thiol would require retention; (S)-2-butanol uses the wrong nucleophile; 1-butanethiol puts the sulfur on the wrong carbon; and 2-butene is an elimination product that a weak base like HS- does not favor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None; SN2 inversion to (S)-thiol verified, valid SMILES, single reactive center + stereo, in scope."},{"q":"The substrate shown is treated with water (H2O) as the solvent and nucleophile, with no added base. Which mechanism best describes how this reaction proceeds?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["SN2, because the bulky methyl groups accelerate backside displacement","E2, because water is a strong enough base to force concerted elimination","SN2, because water performs a fast backside attack on the tertiary carbon","No reaction, because tertiary halides are completely unreactive toward water","SN1, because the substrate is tertiary and forms a stable 3 degree carbocation in the polar protic solvent"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"tert-Butyl bromide is 3 degree. A weak neutral nucleophile (water) in polar protic solvent favors SN1: ionization to the stable tertiary cation then capture by water; SN2 is sterically blocked.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; tertiary + weak Nu + polar protic = SN1; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"The primary alkyl halide shown is reacted with sodium iodide (NaI), a strong, small, unhindered nucleophile, in acetone. Which mechanism is operating?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["SN1, because primary carbocations are highly stabilized","SN1, because iodide is a good leaving group that triggers ionization","E1, because acetone promotes unimolecular elimination","SN2, because a primary substrate with a strong nucleophile in a polar aprotic solvent favors concerted backside attack","E2, because iodide is a strong base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"1-Bromobutane is primary; primary cations are too unstable for SN1. Strong unhindered I- in polar aprotic acetone is textbook SN2.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; primary + strong Nu + aprotic = SN2; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (shown) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","opts":["C[C@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](C#N)CC","C[C@@H](O)CC","C[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(C)C#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A secondary halide with a strong, small nucleophile (cyanide) in polar aprotic DMSO reacts by SN2 with backside attack and inversion. The nitrile group outranks the ethyl group in CIP priority, just as bromine did, so the inversion shows up as a change of descriptor: (R)-2-bromobutane becomes (S)-2-methylbutanenitrile. The (R)-nitrile would require retention; the nitrile drawn without a defined stereocenter ignores the stereospecific inversion; (R)-2-butanol uses the wrong nucleophile; and (S)-2-bromobutane still carries the leaving group (it is the enantiomer of the starting material, not a product).","difficulty":3,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All 5 SMILES valid; verified by 3D simulation the inversion product is the (S)-nitrile = opt0; correct=0 confirmed; distractors differ by stereo/regio/skeleton; in scope (single center, regio+stereo)."},{"q":"Benzyl chloride (shown) undergoes substitution readily by BOTH SN1 and SN2 pathways even though it is a primary halide. What is the reason it is unusually reactive in SN1?","q_smiles":"ClCc1ccccc1","opts":["The aromatic ring provides steric bulk that forces ionization","The chlorine is a better leaving group when attached to a benzylic carbon","Primary benzylic carbons cannot undergo SN2 at all","The benzylic carbocation is resonance-stabilized by the adjacent aromatic ring","The ring withdraws electron density, weakening the C-Cl bond by induction"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Benzylic halides are an exception: although primary, the cation on ionization is delocalized into the ring by resonance, enabling SN1; the unhindered carbon still allows SN2.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; resonance-stabilized benzylic cation is the correct reason; correct=0 right; distractors plausible-but-wrong; in scope."},{"q":"The optically active tertiary bromide shown is dissolved in aqueous ethanol (a polar protic solvent) with no added nucleophile. What is the expected stereochemical outcome at the original stereocenter?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@](C)(Br)CC","opts":["Racemization, because the planar carbocation is attacked from both faces with roughly equal probability","Complete retention of configuration","Complete inversion of configuration","No substitution and no change in stereochemistry","Formation of a single new stereocenter with defined R configuration only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Bromo-3-methylhexane is a genuine tertiary stereocenter (4 distinct groups). SN1 conditions (3 degree, weak Nu, polar protic) give a planar carbocation attacked on both faces, yielding near-racemization, unlike SN2 inversion.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; confirmed genuinely chiral tertiary halide so racemization concept holds; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"For the tertiary chloride shown reacting under SN1 conditions, which single change would MOST increase the reaction rate?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Cl","opts":["Adding a bulky, weakly nucleophilic base","Replacing the leaving group with fluoride","Using a polar aprotic solvent such as DMSO","Lowering the temperature of the reaction","Switching from a less polar solvent to a more polar protic solvent such as water or formic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SN1 rate depends only on the unimolecular ionization. A more polar protic solvent stabilizes the developing cation and anion, accelerating it. Nucleophile identity is irrelevant to SN1 rate; F- is a poor leaving group; aprotic solvent and lower temperature slow ionization.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; correct=0 right; all four distractors verified to slow or not affect SN1 rate; exactly one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, shown) reacts extremely slowly in SN2 reactions despite being a primary halide. Why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts":["The primary carbon forms a very stable carbocation, diverting it to SN1","The adjacent quaternary carbon's bulky tert-butyl group sterically blocks backside attack on the primary carbon","The C-Br bond is unusually strong in neopentyl systems","Bromide is a poor leaving group on primary carbons","Backside attack is electronically forbidden for primary halides"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Neopentyl halides are primary but bear a bulky adjacent quaternary carbon that crowds the backside trajectory, making SN2 very slow; a primary cation rules out SN1 too.","difficulty":2,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES; steric blocking by neighboring quaternary carbon is the correct reason; correct=0 right; distractors plausible-but-wrong; in scope."},{"q":"Rank the following alkyl bromides from FASTEST to SLOWEST in an SN2 reaction with sodium azide (NaN3): I. CH3Br  II. CH3CH2Br  III. (CH3)2CHBr  IV. (CH3)3CBr","q_smiles":"CBr","opts":["III > II > I > IV","IV > III > II > I","I > III > II > IV","I > II > III > IV","II > I > III > IV"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"SN2 rate is set by steric access, decreasing with substitution: methyl > primary > secondary > tertiary, so I > II > III > IV.","difficulty":1,"group":"sn1-sn2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid q_smiles (methyl bromide); standard SN2 steric ranking; correct=0 right; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in DMSO. Which structure is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@@H](C)OCC","CC[C@H](C)O","C=CCC","CC[C@H](C)OCC","CCC(C)OCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Ethoxide is a strong, unhindered nucleophile, and in polar aprotic DMSO on a secondary center the substitution path is SN2: backside attack with inversion. Oxygen holds the same top CIP priority that bromine held, so the inversion flips the descriptor and (R)-2-bromobutane gives (S)-2-ethoxybutane. (R)-2-Ethoxybutane would require retention; 2-ethoxybutane drawn without a defined stereocenter ignores the stereospecific inversion; (S)-2-butanol would need hydroxide; and 1-butene is the competing E2 product, not a substitution product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Substrate (R) and keyed (S) product confirmed by CIP; classic SN2 inversion, all SMILES valid, single concept (stereo)."},{"q":"(S)-3-chloro-3-methylhexane (drawn, a tertiary chloride) is stirred in methanol (CH3OH) at room temperature. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@@](C)(Cl)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC[C@](C)(CC)OC","CCCC(C)(O)CC","CCCC(C)(CC)OC","CC=C(C)CCC","CCC[C@@](C)(CC)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"A tertiary chloride with a weak, neutral nucleophile (methanol) in a polar protic solvent reacts by SN1: the planar tertiary carbocation forms first, and methanol then attacks either face equally, so the product is racemic. The best single drawing is therefore 3-methoxy-3-methylhexane with no defined configuration. Either single enantiomer of the ether would imply a stereospecific process that SN1 does not provide; 3-methyl-3-hexanol would require water as the nucleophile; and 3-methyl-2-hexene is the competing E1 alkene, not the substitution product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. SN1 racemization correctly keyed to the no-stereo ether; all SMILES valid; single concept."},{"q":"(R)-2-iodooctane (drawn) reacts with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMF. Which structure is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC[C@@H](C)I","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCC[C@@H](C)C#N","CCCCCC[C@H](C)N","CCCCCCC(C)C#N","[C-]#[N+][C@@H](C)CCCCCC","CCCCCC[C@H](C)C#N"],"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide attacks through its carbon, and a secondary iodide with a strong nucleophile in polar aprotic DMF reacts by SN2 with inversion. The nitrile carbon outranks the hexyl chain in CIP priority, just as iodine did, so inversion flips the descriptor: (R)-2-iodooctane gives (S)-2-methyloctanenitrile. The (R)-nitrile would require retention; the nitrile drawn without a defined stereocenter ignores the stereospecific inversion; the isonitrile (nitrogen bonded to the chain) is the wrong connectivity because cyanide bonds through carbon; and 2-octanamine has no nitrile at all and would need a nitrogen nucleophile such as ammonia.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. (R)->（S) inversion confirmed by CIP; isonitrile distractor valid; in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane, drawn) is treated with sodium iodide in acetone. Which statement best explains the observed outcome?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["It undergoes fast E2 elimination to give the more substituted alkene","It reacts rapidly by SN1 because the primary carbocation is stabilized by the tert-butyl group","It reacts rapidly by SN2 because primary substrates always favor SN2","It rearranges to a tertiary iodide via a hydride shift before substitution","The reaction is extremely slow because the adjacent quaternary carbon sterically blocks SN2 backside attack, even though the carbon bearing Br is primary"],"correct":4,"why":"Neopentyl bromide is the textbook case of a primary halide that resists SN2: the carbon bearing Br is primary, but the three methyl groups on the neighboring quaternary carbon crowd the backside and block the nucleophile's approach, so the Finkelstein reaction (NaI in acetone, classic SN2 conditions) is extremely slow. SN1 is not an alternative, because ionization would give an unstabilized primary carbocation; the tert-butyl group does not stabilize a cation on the adjacent carbon. E2 is ruled out because the beta carbon is quaternary and has no hydrogen, and iodide is not a base. Saying primary substrates always favor SN2 ignores exactly the steric effect that makes this substrate famous, and the rearrangement route requires a carbocation that these conditions never form (and even then it would be a methyl shift, since the quaternary carbon has no hydride to move).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Classic neopentyl steric reasoning correct; text-classification item with valid drawn substrate; in scope."},{"q":"(R)-(1-chloroethyl)benzene [(R)-1-phenylethyl chloride, drawn] is solvolyzed in warm water. Which structure best represents the substitution product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC(Cl)c1ccccc1O"],"correct":1,"why":"A secondary benzylic chloride in warm water undergoes SN1: chloride leaves to give a planar, resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, and water captures it from both faces equally, so the product is racemic 1-phenylethanol, best drawn without a defined configuration. Either single enantiomer of 1-phenylethanol claims a stereospecificity that a free carbocation cannot deliver; styrene is the E1 alkene, a minor pathway without base or heat; and the ring-hydroxylated chloride would require substitution on the aromatic ring, which does not occur under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. SN1 racemization correctly keyed to index 2 (no-stereo alcohol); all SMILES valid; single concept."},{"q":"cis-1-bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane (drawn; Br and the tert-butyl group on the same face) reacts with sodium hydroxide (NaOH) under SN2 conditions. Which structure is the substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@@H]1CC[C@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCC(O)C1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CC=CCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"SN2 is backside attack, so the nucleophile enters from the face opposite the leaving group and the carbon inverts. In the cis substrate Br and the tert-butyl group are on the same face, so after inversion the new OH ends up on the opposite face from tert-butyl: trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol. The cis alcohol would require retention; the alcohol drawn without cis/trans information throws away the stereochemical result that defines SN2; 3-tert-butylcyclohexanol places the OH on a carbon that never held the leaving group; and 4-tert-butylcyclohexene is the E2 product, excluded by the SN2 conditions in the stem.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. cis substrate -> trans product confirmed by 3D; keyed opt0 correct; SMILES valid; in scope."},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane, drawn) is dissolved in aqueous ethanol and slowly forms tert-butyl alcohol. Which description correctly identifies the rate-determining step and its single curved arrow?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The slow step is unimolecular ionization: one arrow goes from the C-Br bond to bromine, forming a tert-butyl carbocation and bromide","The slow step is bimolecular: one arrow goes from water's oxygen lone pair to carbon as bromide leaves in the same step","The slow step is the arrow from water's lone pair to the carbocation, forming the protonated alcohol","The slow step is loss of a beta-proton: one arrow from a C-H bond to form the alkene","The slow step is bromide leaving with an arrow from bromine's lone pair into the C-Br bond"],"correct":0,"why":"tert-Butyl bromide is tertiary and water is a weak nucleophile in a polar protic medium, so the mechanism is SN1. The slow, rate-determining step is unimolecular ionization: a single arrow starts at the C-Br bond and ends on bromine, generating the stable tertiary carbocation and bromide. A concerted step in which water attacks as bromide leaves describes SN2, which cannot occur at a tertiary center; water adding to the carbocation is the fast step that follows ionization; losing a beta proton is the E1 branch, also after the cation forms; and an arrow from bromine's lone pair into the C-Br bond is drawn backward, since the electrons must flow away from carbon toward the electronegative bromine.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"None. Single-arrow RDS identification (within the at-most-one-arrow allowance), correct answer, valid drawn substrate, in scope."},{"q":"3-chloro-3-methyl-1-butene (drawn, a tertiary allylic chloride) is solvolyzed in water. Besides the unrearranged tertiary alcohol, a rearranged constitutional isomer also forms. Which structure is that rearranged product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC(C)(C)O","C=CC(C)(C)Cl","C=C(C)CCO","OCC=C(C)C","OCC=CC(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Loss of chloride gives an allylic cation whose positive charge is shared by the tertiary carbon that held Cl and, through resonance, the terminal CH2 of the former double bond. Water can capture either end: attack at the tertiary carbon gives the unrearranged 2-methyl-3-buten-2-ol, while attack at the terminal carbon shifts the double bond inward and gives 3-methyl-2-buten-1-ol (prenol), the rearranged constitutional isomer the question asks for. 3-Methyl-3-buten-1-ol has its oxygen two carbons from the double bond and cannot come from this allylic cation; the six-carbon alcohol adds a carbon; and the remaining structure is just the starting chloride.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sn1-sn2","issue":"Keyed answer correct but the 'why' has errors: prenol's alkene is TRISUBSTITUTED, not 'tetrasubstituted'; and opt3 (OCC=CC(C)C) is C6H12O (six carbons), an impossible product/flawed distractor, not the 'C5 isomer' the rationale implies. Still exactly one right answer and in scope (allylic resonance is standard DAT); fix the explanation and ideally swap opt3 for a true C5 isomer."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, a strong unhindered base that favors E2 elimination. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts":["C/C=C/C","CCC(O)C","C=CCC","C/C=C\\C","CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"With a small, strong base, E2 on 2-bromobutane follows Zaitsev's rule: removing a hydrogen from the internal CH2 gives the disubstituted 2-butene rather than the monosubstituted 1-butene. Between the two 2-butene geometries, the anti-periplanar transition state that places the two methyl groups on opposite sides is less crowded, so trans-2-butene, the (E) isomer, is the major product. cis-2-Butene, the (Z) isomer, is the minor geometric isomer; 1-butene is the Hofmann product; 2-butanol is the SN2 side product; and 2-methylpropane is a different skeleton with no double bond.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by RDKit: all SMILES valid. E2/Zaitsev gives 2-butene, trans/E is major isomer (~6:1 experimental). optA=E-2-butene keyed idx0 correct. Two-concept (regio Zaitsev + E/Z stereo). All distractors plausible-but-wrong variants. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a bulky hindered base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCC(C)(O)C","CCC(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is a bulky base. It struggles to reach the more hindered internal CH2 hydrogens and instead removes a hydrogen from one of the accessible terminal methyl groups, giving the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev product that a small base like ethoxide would favor; 3-methyl-1-butene has its double bond away from the carbon that held Br, so it cannot form by E2; 2-methyl-2-butanol is the substitution product that a hindered base does not deliver; and 2-methylbutane is the alkane, which no elimination gives.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Bulky base -> Hofmann 2-methyl-1-butene. optA=CCC(C)=C (2-methyl-1-butene) keyed idx0 correct. Pairs with Q3 (same substrate, different base) - not a duplicate. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a small unhindered base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCC(C)=C","CCOC(C)(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)C","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethoxide is small and unhindered, so E2 follows Zaitsev's rule: the base removes a hydrogen from the internal CH2 to give the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene, which is more stable than the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann product that only a bulky base would favor; 3-methyl-1-butene cannot form because its double bond does not involve the carbon that held Br; 2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane is the substitution product, minor for a tertiary halide with a strong base; and 2-methylbutane is the alkane.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base -> Zaitsev trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene. optA=CC=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Single-concept regiochemistry. Distractor D ether is a valid SN1/E1-competition product. Keep."},{"q":"1-Chloro-1-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Ethoxide is a small, strong base, so E2 follows Zaitsev's rule. Removing a ring hydrogen gives the endocyclic, trisubstituted 1-methylcyclohexene, which is more stable than the exocyclic, disubstituted methylenecyclohexane that would come from removing a methyl hydrogen. Methylenecyclohexane is the Hofmann product that only a bulky base would favor; 1-methylcyclohexanol is the substitution product; methylcyclohexane is the alkane; and 1-methylcyclopentene has the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Zaitsev gives trisubstituted endocyclic 1-methylcyclohexene over exocyclic methylenecyclohexane. optA=CC1=CCCCC1 keyed idx0 correct. Distractor E correctly drawn as cyclopentene (wrong ring size). Keep."},{"q":"3-Bromopentane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), promoting E2 elimination. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)CC","opts":["CC/C=C/C","CCC(C)=C","CC/C=C\\C","CCC(O)CC","CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"3-Bromopentane is symmetric, so either beta carbon gives the same alkene, 2-pentene; the only decision is geometry. In the anti-periplanar E2 transition state, the conformer that keeps the methyl and ethyl groups on opposite sides is less crowded, so trans-2-pentene, the (E) isomer, forms faster and is major. cis-2-Pentene, the (Z) isomer, is the minor geometric isomer; 3-pentanol is the substitution product; pentane is the alkane; and 2-methyl-1-butene is a branched skeleton that cannot come from a straight-chain substrate.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Symmetric substrate -> only 2-pentene; question is purely E/Z and trans is major. optA=CC/C=C/C (E-2-pentene) keyed idx0 correct. Stem correctly frames the symmetry. Distractor E (CCC(C)=C, branched 5C) is a wrong skeleton. Keep."},{"q":"2-Chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium methoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl","opts":["CCC(C)=C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)O","C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methoxide is a small, strong base, so E2 follows Zaitsev's rule and removes the single hydrogen on the adjacent CH to give the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene, the most stable alkene available. 2,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (disubstituted) is the Hofmann product from a methyl hydrogen; 2,3-dimethyl-2-butanol is the substitution product; 2,3-dimethylbutane is the alkane; and the seven-carbon alkene adds a carbon that was never present.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base methoxide -> Zaitsev tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (beta-H abstracted from C3). optA=CC(C)=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Distractor E correctly adds a carbon (wrong skeleton). Keep."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane (the diastereomer with Br and the methyl group on opposite faces) is treated with sodium ethoxide under E2 conditions. Anti-periplanar geometry controls the outcome. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","CC1CCCC(Br)C1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"E2 requires the leaving group and the beta hydrogen to be anti-periplanar, which on a cyclohexane chair means both axial. For the trans-1,2 isomer, the chair that puts Br axial also puts the methyl group axial, so the hydrogen on the methyl-bearing carbon is equatorial and cannot be reached. The only axial beta hydrogen is on the other neighbor, the ring CH2, so elimination goes that way and gives 3-methylcyclohexene rather than the more substituted Zaitsev alkene. 1-Methylcyclohexene is the Zaitsev product, blocked by the stereochemistry; methylenecyclohexane would need a methyl on the carbon bearing Br, which does not exist here; methylcyclohexane is the alkane; and 1-bromo-3-methylcyclohexane is just a constitutional isomer of the starting material, not a product.","difficulty":3,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by RDKit 3D embedding: substrate SMILES is genuinely TRANS (Br and Me on opposite faces), matching stem. Classic anti-periplanar E2: trans isomer forces elimination to C6 -> 3-methylcyclohexene. optA=CC1CCCC=C1 keyed idx0 correct. Standard sophomore/Bootcamp stereochem-of-E2 item; in scope as difficulty-3. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylpentane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a small base. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(Br)C","opts":["CCCC(C)=C","CCCC(C)(C)O","CCC=C(C)C","C=CCC(C)C","CCCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethoxide is small, so E2 follows Zaitsev's rule: removing a hydrogen from the internal CH2 gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-pentene rather than the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-pentene from a methyl hydrogen. 2-Methyl-1-pentene is the Hofmann product that a bulky base would favor; 4-methyl-1-pentene places the double bond away from the carbon that held Br and cannot form by E2; 2-methyl-2-pentanol is the substitution product; and 2-methylpentane is the alkane.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid. Small base -> Zaitsev trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-pentene. optA=CCC=C(C)C keyed idx0 correct. Single-concept regiochemistry. All distractors plausible-but-wrong variants. Keep."},{"q":"The compound shown is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heating. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCC","CC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O"],"correct":1,"why":"The drawn halide is 2-bromo-2-methylbutane, a tertiary bromide. With ethoxide, a small strong base, and heat, E2 dominates and follows Zaitsev's rule: removing a hydrogen from the internal CH2 gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the disubstituted Hofmann alkene, minor with a small base; 2-methyl-2-butanol and 2-butanol are alcohols, not alkenes (and 2-butanol has the wrong carbon count); and 1-butene has only four carbons.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct E2 Zaitsev, single concept, all SMILES valid, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions would best convert the tertiary alkyl bromide shown into the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene as the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol, heat","Sodium iodide (NaI) in acetone","Concentrated H2SO4, heat","Dilute aqueous NaOH at room temperature","Potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol, heat"],"correct":4,"why":"To favor the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene you need a strong but bulky base that can only reach the exposed terminal methyl hydrogens: potassium tert-butoxide in tert-butanol with heat. Sodium ethoxide is small and gives the Zaitsev alkene; concentrated sulfuric acid with heat promotes E1 through the tertiary cation, which also follows Zaitsev; dilute aqueous hydroxide at room temperature mostly gives substitution (the tertiary alcohol) on a tertiary halide; and sodium iodide in acetone is a Finkelstein SN2 reagent, not an elimination reagent.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - reagent selection, one defensible answer, molecule drawn, valid SMILES."},{"q":"The substrate shown is heated in ethanol with no added base (solvolysis conditions). Which statement best classifies the elimination that occurs?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["E2: a concerted, anti-periplanar removal of H and Br in one step","SN2: backside attack inverts the stereocenter","E2: rate depends on the concentration of ethanol as a base","E1: a primary carbocation forms and the Hofmann alkene dominates","E1: the rate-determining step forms a tertiary carbocation, and the major product follows Zaitsev's rule"],"correct":4,"why":"A tertiary bromide heated in ethanol with no added base is a solvolysis: the rate-determining step is ionization to a tertiary carbocation, and the elimination that follows is E1, with the solvent removing a beta proton to give the more substituted (Zaitsev) alkene. E2 would need a strong base and a concerted step, and its rate would depend on base concentration, which is not the case here; a primary carbocation never forms from this tertiary substrate; and SN2 is impossible at a tertiary carbon.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - classification of E1 conditions, one correct answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Both reactions use a strong base (NaOEt) under E2 conditions. Which alkyl halide undergoes E2 elimination FASTER?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A 2-bromobutane (secondary, drawn) reacts faster than a 1-bromobutane (primary) under E2","Both react at exactly the same rate because E2 is base-limited","Neither undergoes E2; both give only substitution","A fluorobutane reacts faster because F is the best leaving group","1-Bromobutane (primary) reacts faster because there is less steric hindrance"],"correct":0,"why":"Under E2 conditions the rate increases with substitution at the carbon bearing the leaving group (tertiary faster than secondary, secondary faster than primary), because the transition state has partial double-bond character, and the more substituted the developing alkene, the more stable that transition state. So secondary 2-bromobutane eliminates faster than primary 1-bromobutane; the extra steric hindrance at the secondary carbon matters for SN2, not for E2. The two halides are not forced to react at the same rate, both can undergo E2, and fluoride is the worst halide leaving group, not the best.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - standard E2 rate trend, one defensible answer, molecule drawn, valid SMILES."},{"q":"The cyclic tertiary bromide shown is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol and heated. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(C)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"The drawn halide is 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane, a tertiary bromide. With ethoxide (a small, strong base) and heat, E2 follows Zaitsev's rule and gives the trisubstituted, endocyclic 1-methylcyclohexene. Methylenecyclohexane (exocyclic, disubstituted) is the Hofmann product that only a bulky base would favor; 1-methylcyclohexanol is the substitution product; 1-methylcyclopentene has the wrong ring size; and cyclohexene is missing the methyl group.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct cyclic E2 Zaitsev, distractors differ by regio/skeleton, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The tertiary bromide shown is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (a bulky base) in tert-butanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)O","CC/C=C/C","C=C(C)CCC","CCC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC"],"correct":2,"why":"The drawn halide is 2-bromo-2-methylpentane. With the bulky base tert-butoxide, E2 takes the exposed methyl hydrogens rather than the hindered internal CH2 hydrogens, so the Hofmann product 2-methyl-1-pentene is major. 2-Methyl-2-pentene is the Zaitsev alkene that a small base would give; 2-methyl-2-pentanol is substitution, which a bulky base does not favor; and trans-2-pentene and 2-methyl-1-butene have only five carbons, so they cannot come from this six-carbon substrate.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct Hofmann via bulky base, distractors plausible, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"For a substituted halocyclohexane to undergo E2 elimination, the relationship between the C-H bond and the C-leaving-group bond must be anti-periplanar, which on a chair requires both groups to be in which orientation?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen must be trans-diaxial (both axial, anti-periplanar)","One axial and one equatorial (gauche) so they are 60 degrees apart","Both must be eclipsed in a fully planar ring","Both must be equatorial so they point away from the ring","Cis-diequatorial, which is the lowest-energy conformer"],"correct":0,"why":"E2 is concerted, and the C-H and C-leaving-group bonds must be anti-periplanar (180 degrees apart) so the breaking C-H bond can feed directly into the forming pi bond. On a chair, the only pair of adjacent bonds that reaches 180 degrees is two axial bonds pointing in opposite directions: the trans-diaxial arrangement. An axial/equatorial pair is gauche (60 degrees apart), two equatorial bonds are also gauche, and the ring never adopts a planar, eclipsed geometry.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - standard E2 chair anti-periplanar requirement, one correct answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is heated in ethanol (no added base). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["C=CCCC","C=CC(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CC=CCC","CC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A tertiary bromide heated in ethanol with no added base ionizes to a tertiary carbocation and eliminates by E1. E1 follows Zaitsev's rule, so losing a proton from the internal CH2 gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene rather than the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene from a methyl proton. 3-Methyl-1-butene does not have its double bond at the carbon that carried the charge, and 2-pentene and 1-pentene are unbranched skeletons; none of them can come from this cation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemistry, scope, and SMILES all correct; opt0 is the right Zaitsev product.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (a bulky base). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts":["CC=CC","C/C=C\\C","CCC(C)O","CCC=C","C/C=C/C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"tert-Butoxide is a bulky base, so in E2 it removes the most accessible beta hydrogen, one on the terminal methyl, rather than a more crowded internal CH2 hydrogen. That gives the Hofmann (least substituted) alkene, 1-butene, as the major product. cis-2-Butene, trans-2-butene, and 2-butene drawn without geometry are all the Zaitsev alkene that a small base like ethoxide would favor; 2-butanol is the substitution product, which a hindered base does not give.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept Hofmann-via-bulky-base; all SMILES valid; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"trans-1-Chloro-2-methylcyclohexane (shown) reacts with sodium ethoxide by E2. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Cl","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1C=CCCC1","CC1CC=CCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"E2 on a cyclohexane needs the leaving group and the beta hydrogen trans-diaxial. In the trans-1,2 isomer, flipping to put Cl axial also makes the methyl group axial, so the hydrogen on the methyl-bearing carbon is equatorial and unavailable. The only anti-periplanar hydrogen is on the ring CH2 on the other side, so elimination gives 3-methylcyclohexene; the anti-periplanar requirement overrides Zaitsev's preference. 1-Methylcyclohexene is the Zaitsev alkene that this geometry forbids; 4-methylcyclohexene puts the double bond away from the carbon that held Cl; methylenecyclohexane would need a methyl on the carbon bearing Cl; and methylcyclohexane is the alkane.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic anti-periplanar-override on a ring; in DAT scope at diff 2; SMILES valid.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane reacts with sodium ethoxide (a small, strong base) by E2. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC=C(C)C","C=C(C)CC","CC(C)C(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethoxide is a small, strong base, so E2 follows Zaitsev's rule. The two beta positions are the terminal methyl and the internal CH that carries two methyls; eliminating toward the CH gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene, while eliminating toward the methyl gives only the monosubstituted 3-methyl-1-butene. 3-Methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann product; 2-methyl-1-butene has its double bond away from the carbon that held Br, so it would need a hydride shift that concerted E2 cannot provide; 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene has six carbons; and 3-methyl-2-butanol is the substitution product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept Zaitsev E2; valid SMILES; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"2-Bromopropane reacts with sodium ethoxide to give propene by an E2 mechanism. In the single concerted transition state, which set of bonding changes occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["The base removes a beta-H while the C-Br bond breaks and the pi bond forms, all in one step","The base adds to the C-Br carbon and bromide leaves from the beta carbon","Bromide leaves to give a carbanion, which then loses a proton","A carbocation forms first, then the base removes a beta-H in a separate second step","The base attacks the carbon bearing Br, displacing bromide in a backside substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"E2 is a single concerted step: the base removes a beta hydrogen, the electrons of that C-H bond become the new pi bond, and the C-Br bond breaks, all at once with no intermediate. A carbocation forming first and then losing a proton describes E1, a two-step mechanism; bromide leaving on its own would give a carbocation, not a carbanion; the base attacking the carbon bearing Br is SN2, not elimination; and bromine sits on the alpha carbon, so it cannot leave from the beta carbon while the base adds to carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Mechanism-recognition (one TS, not a full hand-drawn mechanism); in scope; molecule drawn.","group":"misc"},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is solvolyzed in warm ethanol. Which alkene is the MAJOR elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCC=C1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A tertiary bromide in warm ethanol with no strong base ionizes to a tertiary carbocation and eliminates by E1, which follows Zaitsev's rule. Losing a ring proton gives the endocyclic, trisubstituted 1-methylcyclohexene, which beats the exocyclic, disubstituted methylenecyclohexane from a methyl proton. 1-Methylcyclohexanol is a substitution product, not an alkene; 3-methylcyclohexene has the double bond away from the carbon that carried the charge; and methylcyclohexane is the alkane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-concept endo-vs-exo Zaitsev; valid SMILES; one defensible answer.","group":"misc"},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CBr","C=C(C)C","CCC(C)Br","OC(C)(C)C","CCOC(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"Tertiary halide + strong base (ethoxide) + heat favors E2; SN2 blocked by sterics. Product is isobutylene (2-methylpropene). All methyls equivalent so only one alkene possible; unambiguous. Ether/alcohol are substitution products; bromides are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed product C=C(C)C is the correct E2 alkene; all SMILES valid; single defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heating. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)OCC","CCC=C","CCCCO","C/C=C\\C","C/C=C/C"],"correct":4,"why":"Secondary halide + strong small base + heat gives E2 (Zaitsev) favoring the more stable trans alkene: trans-2-butene. cis-2-butene is the minor geometric isomer, 1-butene the Hofmann minor, ether/alcohol are substitution products.","difficulty":2,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correctly keys trans-2-butene; regio+stereo layering appropriate at difficulty 2; cis and 1-butene distractors well chosen."},{"q":"1-Bromobutane (drawn), a primary alkyl halide, is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)OCC","CCCCO","CCCCOCC","CCCCOCCC","CCC=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Unhindered primary halide + ethoxide: SN2 substitution predominates (Williamson ether synthesis) over the minor E2. Butyl ethyl ether is the major product. 1-Butene is the minor E2 product; other ethers have wrong skeleton/regiochemistry; alcohol needs a different nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. SN2-wins-for-primary is standard sophomore teaching; butyl ethyl ether correctly keyed; single defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Bromopropane (drawn) is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCN=C","CCCOC#N","CC=C","CCC#N","CCCC#N"],"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is an excellent nucleophile and weak base; primary substrate in polar aprotic DMSO gives clean SN2. Carbon attacks to give butanenitrile (one added carbon). Propene is minor E2; propanenitrile is wrong skeleton; O-cyanate and isonitrile reflect wrong connectivity.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Butanenitrile correctly keyed; isonitrile/cyanate connectivity distractors valid and instructive; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)=C"],"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide + bulky strong base (tert-butoxide) gives E2 with Hofmann selectivity (less-substituted, more accessible beta-H), yielding 2-methyl-1-butene. The Zaitsev alkene 2-methyl-2-butene is disfavored by the bulky base; bulky ether/tertiary alcohol are substitution products; last is wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified Hofmann product 2-methyl-1-butene (CCC(C)=C) correctly keyed; Zaitsev distractor present; difficulty 3 appropriate."},{"q":"Bromocyclohexane (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"BrC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary halide + strong base + heat gives E2 to cyclohexene. Cyclohexanol and ethoxycyclohexane are minor substitution products; starting bromide is not a product; cyclohexane would require reduction not provided.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Cyclohexene correctly keyed; all SMILES valid; single defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"(1-Bromoethyl)benzene (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(C=C)cc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(C)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Secondary benzylic halide + strong base + heat undergoes E2; only beta-H is on the methyl, and elimination gives styrene, strongly favored by conjugation with the ring. Ether/alcohol are substitution products; other alkenes have wrong skeleton or extra methyl.","difficulty":2,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Styrene (conjugation-favored E2) correctly keyed; distractors well formed; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (drawn) is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Secondary halide + strong small base + heat gives E2 (Zaitsev): elimination toward the more-substituted C3 gives the trisubstituted alkene 2-methyl-2-butene. 3-Methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann minor; ether/alcohol are substitution products; last is a reduced wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"sub-vs-elim","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: involves Zaitsev regiochemistry choice so arguably difficulty 2, but chemistry/answer are correct and unambiguous. Keyed 2-methyl-2-butene correct."},{"q":"tert-Butyl bromide (drawn) is dissolved in pure ethanol and warmed gently with no added base or nucleophile. Which pair of mechanisms operates, and which is favored?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["Only SN2 occurs because the substrate is tertiary","SN1 and E1 compete; both are equal in all cases regardless of temperature","Only E2 occurs because ethanol is a strong base","SN2 and E2 compete; E2 dominates","SN1 and E1 compete; SN1 (substitution) dominates because solvolysis with a weak nucleophile favors substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"tert-Butyl bromide is tertiary, ethanol is a weak nucleophile and a very weak base, and the solvent is polar protic, so the halide ionizes to the tert-butyl cation and SN1 and E1 compete for that cation. With gentle warming and no strong base, capture by the solvent (SN1, giving tert-butyl ethyl ether) outpaces proton loss (E1, giving isobutylene), so substitution dominates. SN2 and E2 are bimolecular paths that need a strong nucleophile or base and an accessible carbon, neither of which applies to a tertiary halide in neutral ethanol; ethanol is not a strong base; and the SN1/E1 ratio is temperature dependent (more heat shifts toward E1), so the two are not equal in all cases.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; valid SMILES; single-concept mechanism classification, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromopropane (drawn) is treated with a high concentration of sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a strong, small base. Which reaction is the major pathway?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["SN2 giving an ether with retention of configuration","No reaction occurs","E2 elimination","SN1 substitution","E1 elimination"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Secondary substrate + strong base (ethoxide) at high concentration = E2 major (standard textbook/Bootcamp answer: ethoxide is basic enough to favor elimination over SN2 on a secondary center). Distractor 'SN2 with retention' is chemically wrong (SN2 = inversion).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 is the keyed textbook answer (SN2 is competitive in reality but E2 is the expected DAT-depth answer for ethoxide on secondary)."},{"q":"1-Chlorobutane (drawn), a primary alkyl halide, is treated with sodium cyanide (NaCN) in DMSO. Which outcome is expected?","q_smiles":"CCCCCl","opts":["SN2 substitution to give the nitrile (pentanenitrile)","No reaction; primary halides are unreactive","SN1 substitution via a primary carbocation","E1 elimination via a primary carbocation","E2 elimination to give 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Primary halide + strong nucleophile/weak base (cyanide) + polar aprotic DMSO = clean SN2 to pentanenitrile. Primary carbocation routes (SN1/E1) are impossible.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; valid SMILES; single-concept, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (drawn) is heated strongly with concentrated potassium hydroxide in ethanol. The major organic product is which alkene?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["3-methyl-1-butene","2-methyl-1-butene (Hofmann, less substituted alkene)","2-methyl-2-butene (Zaitsev, more substituted alkene)","2-methyl-1-butanol","2-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A tertiary bromide with concentrated hydroxide (a strong, small base) and strong heat undergoes E2. Because hydroxide is unhindered, elimination follows Zaitsev's rule and the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene is the major alkene. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the Hofmann alkene that only a bulky base would favor; 3-methyl-1-butene has its double bond away from the carbon that held Br and cannot form by E2; and the two alcohols are substitution products, which strong base and heat suppress on a tertiary halide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; two-concept (reaction type + Zaitsev regio), in scope."},{"q":"Compare two substrates under identical SN1/E1 solvolysis conditions (warm aqueous ethanol). Substrate A is tert-butyl chloride (drawn). Substrate B is neopentyl bromide, (CH3)3C-CH2-Br. Which reacts substantially faster, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Cl","opts":["They react at equal rates because both have the same number of carbons","Neither reacts; both are too hindered for any mechanism","Substrate B, because the bromide leaving group always overrides carbocation stability under SN1","Substrate B, because neopentyl can ionize to a stable primary carbocation","Substrate A (tert-butyl chloride), because it ionizes to a stable tertiary carbocation while neopentyl is primary"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SN1 rate is set by how easily the substrate ionizes, which means carbocation stability. tert-Butyl chloride ionizes to a stable tertiary carbocation, so it solvolyzes readily; neopentyl bromide would have to form a primary carbocation, which is far too unstable, so it is essentially inert under SN1 conditions even though bromide is the better leaving group. A primary neopentyl cation is not stable, a leaving group cannot override a hopeless carbocation, equal carbon counts say nothing about rate, and tert-butyl chloride is not too hindered to react, because steric hindrance matters for SN2, not SN1.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; carbocation-stability concept, in scope. Second substrate is text-only but the drawn substrate is the focus."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide. To maximize the SUBSTITUTION (ether) product over elimination on this secondary substrate, which single change in conditions is most effective?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)Br","opts":["Switch ethoxide for a bulkier, stronger base like tert-butoxide","Add more of the same strong base and reflux","Lower the temperature (eliminate the heat that favors E2)","Increase the temperature strongly","Increase the concentration of ethoxide"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Elimination has the higher activation energy and gains entropy (one molecule becomes three), so raising the temperature favors E2 over SN2; the single most effective way to tip a secondary halide and ethoxide toward the SN2 ether product is to lower the temperature. Switching to the bulkier, stronger tert-butoxide, adding more base, raising the base concentration, or heating all push the system further toward elimination.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; single-concept temperature effect, in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (drawn) is treated with the bulky strong base potassium tert-butoxide. Which alkene is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)Br","opts":["2-methyl-2-butene (Zaitsev, more substituted alkene)","2-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)","3-methyl-1-butene (Hofmann, less substituted alkene)","3-methyl-2-butanol (substitution)","2-methyl-1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is bulky, so it removes the most accessible beta hydrogen, one on the terminal methyl, rather than the single crowded hydrogen on the CH that carries two methyls. That gives the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene, 3-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev product that a small base would deliver; 2-methyl-1-butene has its double bond away from the carbon that held Br, so it would need a hydride shift that E2 cannot provide; and the two alcohols are substitution products, which a bulky base does not favor.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; two-concept (E2 + Hofmann regio via bulky base), in scope."},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (drawn) is placed in warm methanol containing no added strong base. Classify the competing reactions and identify which substitution mechanism is operative.","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts":["Only E2 occurs, giving exclusively the Hofmann alkene","SN2 and E2 compete (bimolecular), because methanol is a strong base","Only SN2 occurs, with inversion of configuration at the tertiary carbon","SN1 and E2 compete, mixing unimolecular substitution with bimolecular elimination","SN1 and E1 compete (unimolecular), because the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary halide in warm methanol (polar protic, weak nucleophile, no strong base) = solvolysis: SN1 and E1 compete; substitution via SN1. SN2 blocked (tertiary), E2 needs strong base (absent).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"None. Correct=0 confirmed; mechanism classification, in scope (no mechanism drawing required)."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) in tert-butanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR organic product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":0,"why":"A tertiary bromide with the bulky strong base tert-butoxide and heat undergoes E2 with Hofmann selectivity: the hindered base removes an exposed methyl hydrogen rather than an internal CH2 hydrogen, giving the less substituted 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev alkene that a small base would give; 2-methyl-2-butanol and the tert-butyl ether are substitution products, negligible because the bulky base is a poor nucleophile; and 3-methyl-1-butene puts the double bond away from the carbon that held Br, so it cannot form by E2.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Hofmann reasoning and keyed answer (opt0 = 2-methyl-1-butene) correct. In scope, visual, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"cis-1-Bromo-4-tert-butylcyclohexane reacts with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](Br)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC=CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CCOC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"The tert-butyl group locks itself equatorial, and in the cis isomer that forces Br axial, exactly the geometry E2 needs: the axial hydrogens on both neighboring ring carbons are anti-periplanar to Br. A secondary axial bromide with a strong base and heat therefore eliminates rapidly to give 4-tert-butylcyclohexene. The alcohol and the ethyl ether are minor substitution products; the exocyclic methylene alkene would need a methyl on the carbon bearing Br, which does not exist (and it has an extra carbon); and tert-butylcyclohexane is the alkane.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Stem SMILES verified cis (3D face check). Conformational E2 logic and keyed answer correct. Only one alkene is possible (ring symmetric) so the answer is clean; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromobutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Considering both regiochemistry and alkene geometry, which is the SINGLE major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/C","C/C=C\\C","CCOC(C)CC","CCC(C)O","C=CCC"],"correct":0,"why":"A secondary bromide with a small, strong base and heat gives E2. Regiochemistry follows Zaitsev's rule, so the disubstituted 2-butene beats the monosubstituted 1-butene; geometry then favors the trans isomer, because the anti-periplanar transition state that keeps the two methyl groups apart is lower in energy. The single major product is therefore trans-2-butene, the (E) isomer. cis-2-Butene is the minor geometric isomer; 1-butene is the Hofmann product; and 2-ethoxybutane and 2-butanol are substitution products.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Regio + E/Z double layer, in scope. cis/trans SMILES valid and distinguishable. Keyed answer (trans-2-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (1-bromo-2,2-dimethylpropane) is treated with sodium hydrosulfide (NaSH), a strong nucleophile that is a weak base, in a polar aprotic solvent. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)CS","CC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CO","CC(C)(C)S","CC(C)=C"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydrosulfide is a strong nucleophile but a weak base, so it substitutes rather than eliminates, and a primary halide can only substitute by SN2 (no carbocation, so no rearrangement). The product is simple substitution: neopentyl thiol, 2,2-dimethyl-1-propanethiol. The reaction is sterically slow because the neighboring quaternary carbon crowds the backside, but there is no competing pathway: E2 is impossible because the beta carbon has no hydrogen, and SN1 would need a primary cation. Neopentyl alcohol uses the wrong nucleophile; tert-butyl thiol and isobutylene are four-carbon skeletons; and neopentane is the alkane.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid. No beta-H rules out elimination; SN2 thiol is the single defensible answer despite neopentyl steric slowdown. In scope. Keep."},{"q":"1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu) and heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1(Br)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"A tertiary bromide with the bulky base tert-butoxide undergoes E2 with Hofmann selectivity: the hindered base removes one of the exposed methyl hydrogens rather than a ring hydrogen, giving the exocyclic, less substituted methylenecyclohexane. 1-Methylcyclohexene is the Zaitsev product that a small base would give; 1-methylcyclohexanol is the substitution trap, which a bulky base does not deliver; 2-methylcyclohexanol has the oxygen on a carbon that never held the leaving group; and methylcyclohexane is the alkane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Hofmann vs Zaitsev single-concept, in scope. Keyed answer (methylenecyclohexane) correct. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol with heat. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this E2 reaction?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)C=C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCOC(C)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"E2 is concerted, so there is no carbocation and no opportunity to rearrange. The carbon bearing Br has two neighbors: a methyl group with hydrogens and a quaternary carbon with none, so the only beta hydrogens are on the methyl and the only possible alkene is 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene. 2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene is the trap: it would require a methyl shift to a rearranged cation, which E1 might allow but E2 cannot; 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene is also a rearranged skeleton; and the ethyl ether and the alcohol are substitution products.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; no-rearrangement E2 concept, in scope (no mechanism drawing). Keyed answer (3,3-dimethyl-1-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt). Remembering that E2 on a cyclohexane requires the leaving group and the beta-hydrogen to be trans-diaxial (anti-periplanar), which is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1C=CCCC1","CCOC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"E2 on a cyclohexane needs Br axial with a trans-diaxial beta hydrogen. In the trans-1,2 isomer, the chair that makes Br axial also makes the methyl axial, so the hydrogen on the methyl-bearing carbon is equatorial and cannot participate. Elimination therefore goes toward the ring CH2 on the other side, giving 3-methylcyclohexene. The Zaitsev alkene, 1-methylcyclohexene, cannot form because the hydrogen it needs is equatorial; methylenecyclohexane would need a methyl on the carbon bearing Br; and 2-methylcyclohexanol and its ethyl ether are substitution products.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"Stem SMILES verified trans (3D face check). Anti-periplanar regiochemistry and keyed answer (3-methylcyclohexene) correct. In scope, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(C)C(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CC(O)C(C)C"],"correct":1,"why":"A secondary bromide with a small, strong base and heat gives E2 with Zaitsev selectivity. Eliminating toward the CH that carries two methyls gives the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene; eliminating toward the terminal methyl gives only the monosubstituted 3-methyl-1-butene, the minor Hofmann product. The ethyl ether and 3-methyl-2-butanol are minor substitution products, and 2,3-dimethylbutane is a six-carbon alkane that cannot form.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"sub-vs-elim","issue":"All SMILES valid; Zaitsev regiochemistry single-concept, in scope. Keyed answer (2-methyl-2-butene) correct. Keep."},{"q":"The alcohol shown is treated with concentrated H2SO4 and warmed, generating a secondary carbocation that quickly converts to a more stable tertiary carbocation before any product forms. What type of rearrangement accomplishes this conversion?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","opts":["A 1,2-hydride shift","A ring expansion","A 1,2-methyl shift","A retro-aldol cleavage","A 1,2-chloride shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Methyl-2-butanol ionizes to the secondary (CH3)2CH-CH(+)-CH3 cation; the adjacent tertiary C-H migrates (1,2-hydride shift) to give the tertiary (CH3)2C(+)-CH2CH3 cation. No carbon skeleton group moves, so it is a hydride shift, not a methyl shift.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; secondary->tertiary via 1,2-hydride shift is correct; index 0 right; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol (pinacolyl alcohol, shown) is a classic substrate that gives a rearranged product under acidic ionizing conditions. Which set of conditions would generate a carbocation that undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift on its way to product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["NaBr in acetone","Concentrated HBr (forms a carbocation; SN1-type)","PBr3 in ether","NaH then CH3I","SOCl2 with pyridine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A 1,2-shift requires a free carbocation, formed only under SN1/E1 ionizing conditions. Conc. HBr protonates OH and ionizes to the secondary cation, which gains a methyl from the neighboring tert-butyl carbon (1,2-methyl shift) to a tertiary cation. PBr3, SOCl2/pyridine, and NaBr/acetone are SN2 (no free cation); NaH/CH3I is Williamson ether synthesis.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; only conc. HBr ionizes to a cation; index 0 right; reagent-selection text opts, in scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl bromide (shown) reacts extremely slowly by SN2 because its primary carbon is shielded by the bulky tert-butyl group. Under SN1-favoring solvolysis conditions, it instead ionizes and rearranges. What migration converts the initially formed primary cation into a more stable one?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CBr","opts":["No rearrangement; the primary cation is stable","A 1,2-methyl shift to give a tertiary carbocation","A ring expansion to a cyclobutyl cation","A 1,2-methyl shift to give a secondary carbocation","A 1,2-hydride shift to give a secondary carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Ionization gives a primary cation (CH3)3C-CH2(+); a methyl from the adjacent quaternary carbon migrates (1,2-methyl shift) to give the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation. A hydride shift is impossible (neighbor is quaternary, no H); there is no ring to expand.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; methyl shift gives tertiary (not secondary), distractors correctly wrong; index 0 right; in scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-methylbutane (shown) is heated in methanol (CH3OH), an SN1/E1 solvolysis. Which structure is the major substitution (ether) product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts":["C=C(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CCC(C)(C)OC","COC(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Methanol is a weak nucleophile and a polar protic solvent, so the secondary bromide ionizes to the secondary 3-methyl-2-butyl cation. That cation sits next to a tertiary CH, and a 1,2-hydride shift converts it into the more stable tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation before methanol captures it; methanol then attacks and loses a proton to give 2-methoxy-2-methylbutane. 2-Methoxy-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged ether, which forms only if the solvent traps the cation before the shift and is minor. 2-Methyl-2-butene, 2-methyl-1-butene, and 3-methyl-1-butene are E1 alkenes, not the substitution product asked for.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All 5 structure SMILES valid; rearranged tertiary ether is correct major SN1 product; index 0 right; regio+rearrangement two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene (shown) is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Markovnikov addition gives a secondary carbocation that rearranges before bromide adds. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)C(Br)C","CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation puts the hydrogen on the terminal CH2 and the positive charge on the internal carbon, giving a secondary cation that sits next to a tertiary CH. A 1,2-hydride shift moves that hydrogen over to give the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, and bromide then adds to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product that would form only if bromide trapped the secondary cation first; 1-bromo-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product that needs peroxides; and 1-bromo-2-methylbutane and neopentyl bromide put the bromine on carbons that never carried positive charge.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: layers Markovnikov + hydride-shift so closer to difficulty 2 than 1, but single reactive center; SMILES valid; index 0 right. Keep."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (shown) reacts with HBr by Markovnikov addition. The first-formed secondary cation rearranges, then bromide adds. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts":["CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CCBr","CC(C)=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of the terminal CH2 gives a secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon. That neighbor has no hydrogen, so the rearrangement is a 1,2-methyl shift, which produces the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation; bromide adds to it to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product; 1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product that requires peroxides; 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is an alkene (loss of a proton from the rearranged cation, not an addition product); and 2,2-dimethylbutane is an alkane, which HBr addition cannot give.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All structure SMILES valid; answer and unrearranged distractor confirmed distinct; methyl-shift product correct; index 0 right; two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Four alcohols are considered for an SN1 reaction in which the very first carbocation formed would immediately rearrange to a markedly more stable one. One example, neopentyl alcohol, is shown. Among the following, which ionizes to a carbocation that is MOST driven to undergo a 1,2-shift (i.e., gains the most stability by rearranging)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CO","opts":["tert-Butyl alcohol (already tertiary)","Neopentyl alcohol (primary cation rearranges to tertiary)","Cyclohexanol (secondary, symmetric, no driving force)","2-Butanol (secondary cation, no adjacent carbon that improves it)","Ethanol (primary, but no branch to migrate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The driving force is the stability gain on rearranging. Neopentyl alcohol's primary cation sits next to a quaternary carbon, so a methyl shift jumps primary->tertiary (largest gain). tert-Butyl is already tertiary; 2-butanol and cyclohexanol give secondary cations with no path to tertiary; ethanol's primary cation has no branched neighbor to migrate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. SMILES valid; neopentyl (primary->tertiary) is the unique largest driving force; index 0 right; ranking/classification text opts, in scope."},{"q":"(Bromomethyl)cyclopentane (shown) is solvolyzed in water, giving a single rearranged alcohol as the major product. The primary cation undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"BrCC1CCCC1","opts":["C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ionization gives a primary exocyclic cation; a ring C-C bond migrates (ring expansion) to give a secondary cyclohexyl cation, trapped by water to give cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1). Cyclopentylmethanol (OCC1CCCC1) is the unrearranged product; methylenecyclopentane, 1-methylcyclopentene, and cyclohexene are elimination products, not the alcohol.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All structure SMILES valid; ring expansion to cyclohexanol correct; index 0 right; single migration, hard but in DAT scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is treated with concentrated HCl. The reaction proceeds through a carbocation. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CCCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(C)(C)CCCl","CC(C)(Cl)C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CCl"],"correct":3,"why":"Concentrated HCl protonates the alcohol, and water leaves to give a secondary cation on the carbon that held OH, right next to a quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift from that neighbor produces the more stable tertiary cation, which chloride then traps to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-Chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane is the unrearranged secondary chloride; 2-chloro-2-methylpentane is a different carbon skeleton; and 1-chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane and 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane put chlorine on carbons that were never positively charged.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid; keyed index 0 matches 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutage. Correct."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene reacts with HCl by electrophilic addition. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCCl","CCC(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)Cl"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation puts the hydrogen on the terminal CH2 and leaves a secondary cation on the internal carbon, directly next to a tertiary CH. A 1,2-hydride shift gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, and chloride traps it to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. 2-Chloro-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product; 1-chloro-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product; and 1-chloro-2-methylbutane and 2-chloropentane put chlorine on carbons that never carried positive charge (2-chloropentane also has an unbranched skeleton).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. The reaction goes through a carbocation that rearranges. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)CCBr","CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation on the internal carbon, next to the quaternary tert-butyl carbon. Since that neighbor has no hydrogen, a 1,2-methyl shift occurs, producing a tertiary cation; bromide adds to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-Bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product; 1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product that needs peroxides; and 1-bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane and 1-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane put the bromine on carbons that never carried positive charge.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"In the HCl addition to 3-methyl-1-butene shown, a 1,2-hydride shift converts a secondary cation into a tertiary cation. Which single curved arrow correctly represents this hydride shift?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An arrow from the empty orbital on C2 to the C3-H bond","An arrow from the C3-H bond to the chloride ion","An arrow from the lone pair on chloride to the C3-H bond","An arrow from a C-C bond on C3 to C2 (a methyl/alkyl shift instead)","An arrow from the C3-H bond to the empty orbital on the adjacent C2 cation"],"correct":4,"why":"A 1,2-hydride shift is drawn as the migrating C-H bonding electrons moving to the adjacent electron-deficient carbon: arrow from C3-H bond to the empty orbital on C2. Arrows pointing away from the cation, to chloride, or representing a C-C (alkyl) shift are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"In scope (identifies ONE arrow, explicitly allowed). q_smiles valid. Correct."},{"q":"Cyclobutylmethanol (drawn) is treated with HCl. Ionization gives a primary cyclobutylmethyl cation that undergoes ring expansion. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCC1CCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["ClC1CCCC1","ClC1CCC1","C=C1CCC1","ClCC1CCCC1","ClCC1CCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Protonating the primary alcohol and losing water sets up a cyclobutylmethyl cation, which is too unstable to sit still. A ring bond shifts over to the cationic carbon (a 1,2-alkyl shift), expanding the strained four-membered ring into a five-membered ring and converting a primary cation into a secondary cyclopentyl cation. Chloride then traps that cation to give chlorocyclopentane, the major product. (Chloromethyl)cyclobutane is the unrearranged primary chloride you would get without the shift; chlorocyclobutane and (chloromethyl)cyclopentane have the wrong carbon count, and methylenecyclobutane is an alkene rather than a chloride.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct. Minor: stem name '(cyclobutylmethyl)methanol' is awkward (SMILES is cyclobutylmethanol) and distractor opt4 (C6H11Cl) is not mass-balanced with the C5 substrate, but it stays clearly wrong and single-answer holds."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4 (E1 dehydration). The carbocation rearranges before elimination. What is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["2,3-Dimethyl-1-butene","2-Methyl-2-butene","2-Methyl-1-pentene","3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (monosubstituted, no rearrangement)","2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene (tetrasubstituted)"],"correct":4,"why":"Ionization gives a 2 cation that undergoes a 1,2-methyl shift to a 3 cation. Loss of a proton gives the tetrasubstituted Zaitsev/rearranged alkene 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene. The monosubstituted alkene would be unrearranged and is disfavored.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Text opts with molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Keyed index 0 correct."},{"q":"Optically active (R)-2-bromo-3-methylbutane is solvolyzed in water (SN1). The intermediate cation rearranges. Which statement best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3-Methyl-2-butanol as a single enantiomer with inversion (R to S)","2-Methyl-2-butanol obtained as a single optically pure enantiomer","2-Methyl-2-butanol, formed after a 1,2-hydride shift to a tertiary cation; the new center is not a stereocenter, so the product is achiral","3-Methyl-2-butanol with retention of configuration at the original carbon","3-Methyl-1-butanol, a rearranged primary alcohol"],"correct":2,"why":"The 2 cation at C2 undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift from C3 to a 3 cation; water adds to give 2-methyl-2-butanol. Retention/inversion/optically-pure claims are wrong because the stereocenter is destroyed.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified product is achiral (0 chiral centers). Recognition-depth stereo reasoning, no R/S math; in scope. q_smiles valid. Correct."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-pentene undergoes acid-catalyzed hydration (dilute H2SO4, H2O). The carbocation rearranges. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)C(C)O","CCC(C)(C)CO","CCCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)(O)CC","CCC(C)CCO"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of the terminal alkene puts the positive charge on C2, a secondary cation sitting right next to the tertiary C3. A 1,2-hydride shift from C3 to C2 converts it into the more stable tertiary cation at C3, and water captures that carbon to give 3-methyl-3-pentanol, the major product. 3-Methyl-2-pentanol is the unrearranged Markovnikov alcohol (the trap if you forget the shift), 3-methyl-1-pentanol is anti-Markovnikov, and the remaining alcohols have skeletons that cannot come from this carbon chain.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Verified: keyed index 0 correct; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"2-Methylpropene (isobutylene) is treated with HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts":["CC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)CBr","CCC(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov addition: the proton adds to the terminal CH2 so that the positive charge lands on the central carbon as a tertiary cation, and bromide bonds there. The product is tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane). Isobutyl bromide is the anti-Markovnikov isomer that would need peroxides, tert-butyl alcohol is the hydration product (wrong reagent), the vicinal dibromide would require Br2, and 2-bromobutane has a straight-chain skeleton that does not come from isobutylene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; correct=0 confirmed (tert-butyl bromide); single concept (Markovnikov regiochem); exactly one defensible answer; visual structure options."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr (no peroxides). The only major product isolated is tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane). Which statement best explains this outcome?","q_smiles":"CC(=C)C","opts":["H+ adds to the more-substituted carbon to give a primary carbocation that is trapped by bromide","The reaction proceeds through a free-radical chain, so Br ends up on the less-substituted carbon","The product forms by a concerted syn addition with no carbocation intermediate","Br adds to the more-substituted carbon because Br is the larger, more nucleophilic atom","H+ adds to the less-substituted carbon, placing the positive charge on the more-substituted carbon to give the more stable (tertiary) carbocation (Markovnikov addition)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Ionic HX addition is Markovnikov: the proton adds to the carbon with more hydrogens so the carbocation forms on the more-substituted carbon (here tertiary). Bromide then traps that cation, putting Br on the more-substituted carbon (tert-butyl bromide). Radical pathways need peroxides, which are absent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid isobutylene SMILES; Markovnikov mechanism correct; option 0 is the single defensible answer; distractors clean. No change."},{"q":"Which set of reagents/conditions converts the alkene shown into 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide) as the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["HBr with ROOR (peroxides)","HBr","HBr followed by NaOH","Br2 with H2O","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov HBr addition to propene puts Br on the more-substituted (internal) carbon, giving 2-bromopropane. HBr/peroxides would give the anti-Markovnikov 1-bromopropane; Br2/CCl4 gives a vicinal dibromide; Br2/H2O gives a bromohydrin; HBr then NaOH would eliminate or substitute the product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid propene SMILES; reagent-selection item with one correct (HBr); distractors all give different products. No change."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) reacts with HCl to give predominantly 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane. This regiochemical outcome is an example of which type of addition?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Syn dihydroxylation","Markovnikov addition","Free-radical addition","Oxidative cleavage","Anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation of the alkene generates the more stable tertiary carbocation at the methyl-bearing ring carbon; chloride traps it there, so Cl ends up on the more-substituted carbon. That is the definition of Markovnikov addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 1-methylcyclohexene SMILES; tertiary cation gives Markovnikov product; classification item with one answer. No change."},{"q":"Addition of HBr to 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (shown) gives a substantial amount of 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane in addition to the simple Markovnikov product. The appearance of this 'extra' product is best explained by which feature of the mechanism?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["Loss of the tert-butyl group as a leaving group during addition","A concerted syn addition that scrambles the substituents","A 1,2-hydride/methyl shift converts the initial secondary carbocation into a more stable tertiary carbocation before bromide traps it","A radical chain that places Br on the terminal carbon","Anti addition of Br and H across the double bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation first gives a secondary carbocation adjacent to a quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift produces a more stable tertiary cation, which bromide traps to give the rearranged product 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane (C6H13Br). Carbocation rearrangement is the diagnostic feature.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid SMILES; rearrangement product naming/formula verified (both products C6H13Br); 1,2-shift to tertiary cation is correct mechanistic feature. Recognition-level, no full mechanism. No change."},{"q":"Two alkenes are each treated with HBr under identical ionic (no-peroxide) conditions. The alkene drawn is 2-methyl-2-butene; the second alkene is 1-butene (CH2=CHCH2CH3). Which alkene reacts faster, and why?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["1-Butene, because a primary carbocation forms faster than a tertiary one","They react at the same rate because both add one equivalent of HBr","2-Methyl-2-butene, because Br can only add to a terminal carbon","2-Methyl-2-butene, because it forms a more stable (tertiary) carbocation in the rate-determining protonation step","1-Butene, because a less-substituted alkene is more nucleophilic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The rate-determining step in ionic HX addition is protonation to form the carbocation. The more-substituted alkene (2-methyl-2-butene) gives a tertiary cation, which is more stable and forms faster, so it reacts faster than 1-butene, which gives a less stable secondary/primary cation.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 2-methyl-2-butene SMILES; rate governed by cation stability in RDS; reasoning (rate vs nucleophilicity layered) is two-concept. Option 0 is the one defensible answer. No change."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is treated with HBr in the absence of peroxides. Which describes the major product and the reason for its regiochemistry?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["(1-bromoethyl)benzene, because a primary carbocation forms on the terminal carbon","(2-bromoethyl)benzene, because the benzene ring directs Br anti-Markovnikov","A vicinal dibromide, because HBr adds twice to the ring-conjugated alkene","(2-bromoethyl)benzene, because Br always adds to the terminal carbon of a vinyl group","(1-bromoethyl)benzene, because protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic carbocation that bromide traps at the benzylic carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Protonation of styrene's terminal carbon gives a benzylic carbocation stabilized by resonance into the ring. Bromide adds there, so Br ends up on the benzylic carbon, giving (1-bromoethyl)benzene. This is Markovnikov-type control set by carbocation stability.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid styrene SMILES; benzylic cation regiochemistry correct; option 2 distractor names same product but wrong reason, option 0 has correct product AND reason, so still exactly one fully-correct choice. No change."},{"q":"The alkene shown (2-methyl-1-butene) is treated with HCl. Identify the regiochemical name of the addition and the carbon that bears the chlorine in the major product.","q_smiles":"CCC(=C)C","opts":["Free-radical addition; Cl on the internal carbon","Syn addition; Cl and H both on the terminal carbon","Markovnikov addition; Cl on the terminal CH2 carbon","Anti-Markovnikov addition; Cl on the terminal CH2 carbon","Markovnikov addition; Cl on the more-substituted (internal, tertiary) carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Protonation of the terminal =CH2 gives a tertiary carbocation at the internal carbon. Chloride traps there, so Cl sits on the more-substituted carbon. That is Markovnikov addition with Cl on the internal (tertiary) carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 2-methyl-1-butene SMILES (CCC(=C)C); tertiary cation gives Cl on internal carbon; option 0 combines correct name + correct position. No change."},{"q":"1-Hexene (shown) is treated with HBr in the presence of an organic peroxide (ROOR). Classify this reaction and predict where Br ends up relative to the simple HBr (no-peroxide) case.","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts":["Radical (peroxide-initiated) anti-Markovnikov addition; Br adds to the terminal carbon, opposite the Markovnikov product","Anti addition of Br2 across the double bond to give a dibromide","Oxidative cleavage of the double bond to give a carboxylic acid","Ionic Markovnikov addition; Br adds to the internal carbon, same as without peroxide","Radical addition that still follows Markovnikov; Br adds to the internal carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Peroxides switch HBr addition to a radical chain. The bromine radical adds to form the more stable secondary carbon radical, so Br lands on the terminal carbon, giving 1-bromohexane: the anti-Markovnikov product, opposite the ionic outcome. This peroxide effect is unique to HBr.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Valid 1-hexene SMILES; peroxide anti-Markovnikov effect correct; identifies radical regiochemistry without full mechanism, in DAT scope. No change."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCC(Br)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov addition: protonating C2 gives a tertiary cation at the methyl-bearing C1; Br- bonds there, giving 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane. Distractors: secondary anti-Markovnikov bromide, vic-dibromide (Br2), ring-expanded bromocycloheptane, remote-Br isomer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Predict the major product of the reaction of 2-methyl-2-butene with HBr.","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CC(Br)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation goes to the CH end of the trisubstituted alkene so that the positive charge develops on the carbon that carries two methyl groups, giving a tertiary cation; bromide then traps it. The major product is 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the secondary, anti-Markovnikov bromide, the two primary bromides (1-bromo-3-methylbutane and 1-bromo-2-methylbutane) put Br on a carbon that was never part of the double bond, and the vicinal dibromide would require Br2 instead of HBr.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"Minor: 'why' says Br adds to 'C2' with inconsistent numbering, but the keyed structure 2-bromo-2-methylbutane is correct."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["ClCC(Cl)c1ccccc1","ClCc1ccccc1","CC(Cl)c1ccccc1","ClCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation; Cl- bonds at the benzylic carbon to give (1-chloroethyl)benzene. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov primary chloride, ring chlorination (EAS, wrong site), vic-dichloride, wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product? (Consider carbocation stability carefully.)","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["BrCC(C)C(C)C","BrCCC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of the terminal CH2 gives a secondary cation at C2, directly beside the quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift from C3 to C2 converts it into a tertiary cation, and bromide traps that rearranged carbon to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane, the major product. The classic trap is 2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane, the unrearranged secondary bromide; the primary bromides (1-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane and the others) put Br on a carbon that never carried positive charge.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"What is the major product when propene reacts with HBr (no peroxides)?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["CC(Br)CBr","CC(Br)CO","CCBr","CCCBr","CC(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov: the more stable secondary cation forms at the internal carbon, so Br ends up on C2, giving 2-bromopropane. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov 1-bromopropane, vic-dibromide, a bromo-alcohol, and a two-carbon fragment.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"(E)-2-Butene is treated with HBr. A new stereocenter forms at the carbon that gains Br. Which option best represents the stereochemical outcome of the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["CC[C@H](Br)C","CCC(Br)C","CCCCBr","CC(Br)(C)C","CC[C@@H](Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation gives a planar achiral 2-butyl cation; Br- attacks both faces equally, giving racemic 2-bromobutane, best shown by the structure drawn without defined stereochemistry. The single-enantiomer drawings are each wrong alone; the others are wrong regiochemistry/skeleton. Defensible single answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Methylenecyclohexane (the exocyclic alkene shown) reacts with HI. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C1CCCCC1","opts":["ICC1CCCCC1","IC1CCCCC1","CCC1(I)CCCC1","CC1CCCCC1I","CC1(I)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Protonating the exocyclic =CH2 puts the cation on the ring carbon (tertiary); I- bonds there to give 1-iodo-1-methylcyclohexane. Distractors: anti-Markovnikov (iodomethyl)cyclohexane, ring-CH2 iodide, a one-carbon-short ring, and a wrong ring size/substitution.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"(E)-1-Phenyl-1-propene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCC=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Regiochemistry follows carbocation stability: protonation gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, so Br bonds at the benzylic (phenyl-bearing) carbon to give (1-bromopropyl)benzene. Distractors: non-benzylic secondary bromide, ring bromination, vic-dibromide, primary bromide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hx","issue":"none"},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3-CH=CH2  +  HBr,  (PhCO)2O2 (peroxide), heat","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)Br","CC(Br)C","CCCO","CCCBr","CCCCl"],"correct":3,"why":"With peroxide present, HBr adds by a radical chain, and the regiochemistry flips to anti-Markovnikov. The bromine radical adds to the terminal carbon so that the more stable secondary radical forms on the internal carbon, which then abstracts H from HBr. The major product is 1-bromopropane. 2-Bromopropane is the ionic Markovnikov product you would get without peroxide, 1-propanol and 1-chloropropane need different reagents entirely, and 2-bromopropene (a vinyl bromide) is not an addition product.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 SMILES valid, key correct, single concept, visual, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n2-methylpropene (isobutylene)  +  HBr,  ROOR (peroxide)","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)CO","CC(C)(Br)C","CCC(C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"Peroxide switches HBr addition to the radical, anti-Markovnikov pathway: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 so the stable tertiary radical forms on the central carbon, which then picks up H from HBr. The major product is isobutyl bromide (1-bromo-2-methylpropane). tert-Butyl bromide is the ionic Markovnikov product, 2-methyl-1-propanol is a hydration product from the wrong reaction, and 2-bromobutane has a straight-chain skeleton that cannot arise from isobutylene.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5-option MC); chemistry is correct, in scope, visual, with exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\nstyrene (vinylbenzene)  +  HBr,  peroxide, heat","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Radical (peroxide-initiated) HBr addition is anti-Markovnikov: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2, leaving a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical on the carbon attached to the ring, which then abstracts H from HBr. The major product is (2-bromoethyl)benzene, with Br on the terminal carbon. (1-Bromoethyl)benzene is the Markovnikov product of the ionic pathway, 2-phenylethanol comes from a different reaction, and bromination of the aromatic ring does not happen under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5); chemistry, scope, key, and visual are all sound."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n1-pentene  +  HBr,  (PhCOO)2 (peroxide)","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCO","CCCCCBr","CCCC(Br)C","CCC(Br)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Peroxide drives the radical, anti-Markovnikov addition of HBr: the bromine radical adds to the terminal carbon, the resulting secondary radical abstracts H, and the major product is 1-bromopentane. 2-Bromopentane is the Markovnikov product of the peroxide-free ionic reaction, 3-bromopentane puts Br on a carbon that was never part of the double bond, and 1-pentanol is a hydration product from a different reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options provided (spec calls for 5); otherwise correct, in scope, visual, single defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n3-methyl-1-butene  +  HBr,  peroxide","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(Br)C","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CCBr","CC(C)CCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Radical anti-Markovnikov addition puts the bromine radical on the terminal CH2, generating a secondary radical on the internal carbon. Radicals do not rearrange the way carbocations do, so no hydride shift occurs; the radical simply abstracts H from HBr. The major product is 1-bromo-3-methylbutane. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the Markovnikov product, 2-bromo-2-methylbutane is the tempting rearranged tertiary bromide that only a carbocation pathway could give, and 3-methyl-1-butanol belongs to a hydration reaction.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); two-concept (regio + no-rearrangement) item is correct, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n1-methylcyclohexene  +  HBr,  ROOR (peroxide), heat","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1CCCC(Br)C1","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(Br)CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"For this trisubstituted ring alkene, peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry: the bromine radical bonds to the less substituted ring CH so that the more stable tertiary radical forms on the methyl-bearing carbon, which then abstracts H. The major product is 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane, with Br and CH3 on adjacent ring carbons. 1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is the Markovnikov product of the ionic pathway, 1-bromo-3-methylcyclohexane puts Br on a ring carbon that was never part of the double bond, and 2-methylcyclohexanol is a hydration product.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); regiochemistry, scope, and key are all correct and visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\n4-methyl-1-pentene  +  HBr,  peroxide","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CC(Br)C","CC(C)CCCBr","CC(C)CCCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Peroxide gives radical anti-Markovnikov addition: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2, a secondary radical forms on C2 and abstracts H, and the major product is 1-bromo-4-methylpentane. The methyl branch at C4 is too far away to matter. 2-Bromo-4-methylpentane is the Markovnikov product, 2-bromo-2-methylpentane would require a carbocation rearrangement that radicals do not undergo, and 4-methyl-1-pentanol is a hydration product.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Only 4 options (spec calls for 5); single-concept item is correct, in scope, visual, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR organic product:\n\nallylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene, PhCH2-CH=CH2)  +  HBr,  peroxide, heat","q_smiles":"C=CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccc(Br)cc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Only the double bond reacts; the benzylic CH2 is saturated and not part of the alkene, so the ring cannot stabilize anything at the carbon where the radical forms. Peroxide gives anti-Markovnikov addition: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2, a secondary radical forms on the middle carbon and abstracts H, and the major product is (3-bromopropyl)benzene. (2-Bromopropyl)benzene is the Markovnikov product; (1-bromopropyl)benzene is a tempting benzylic bromide that cannot form from this alkene; ring bromination and the alcohol come from different reactions.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 SMILES valid, key correct, single reactive center, well-designed benzylic trap, in scope and visual."},{"q":"A chemist treats 1-hexene (drawn) and isolates 1-bromohexane (CH3CH2CH2CH2CH2CH2Br) as the major product. Which set of reagents/conditions was used?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["HBr in water (H2O)","HBr with a Lewis acid catalyst","HBr with peroxides (ROOR), heat or light","HBr alone (no peroxide)","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"1-Bromohexane places Br on the terminal (less substituted) carbon = anti-Markovnikov, which comes from the free-radical mechanism requiring HBr + peroxide initiator with heat/light. HBr alone gives Markovnikov (2-bromohexane).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. q_smiles C=CCCCC is valid 1-hexene; anti-Markovnikov product requires HBr/peroxides; key index 0 is right; distractors all plausibly wrong."},{"q":"Propene is drawn. Under which conditions does propene give 1-bromopropane (the anti-Markovnikov product) rather than 2-bromopropane?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["HBr in the presence of peroxides","HI with peroxides","HBr alone","HCl with peroxides","H2O with H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Only HBr undergoes the peroxide-initiated radical chain that reverses regiochemistry. The peroxide effect is specific to HBr; HCl and HI do not give useful anti-Markovnikov addition. So HBr + peroxides gives 1-bromopropane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. CC=C is valid propene; only HBr shows the peroxide effect; key 0 correct; classic high-yield DAT fact."},{"q":"Styrene (drawn) is reacted with HBr in the presence of peroxides. What is the correct name/type for this transformation and its regiochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Electrophilic addition; Markovnikov (Br on the benzylic carbon)","Electrophilic addition; anti-Markovnikov","Free-radical substitution; allylic bromination","Nucleophilic addition; Markovnikov","Free-radical addition; anti-Markovnikov (Br on the terminal CH2)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Peroxides make HBr add by a free-radical chain mechanism (anti-Markovnikov). For styrene, Br radical adds to terminal CH2 so the more stable benzylic radical forms; Br ends on the terminal carbon. This is addition, not substitution, and anti-Markovnikov.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=Cc1ccccc1 is valid styrene; mechanism = free-radical addition, anti-Markovnikov; key 0 correct; distractors are well-chosen wrong combinations."},{"q":"The reaction below is run with HBr and a peroxide initiator. Which species is the chain-carrying radical that adds to the alkene's pi bond in the first propagation step?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["Br (bromine radical)","RO (alkoxy radical, the initiator)","HBr radical cation","The carbocation","H (hydrogen radical)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The alkoxy radical from peroxide abstracts H from HBr to generate a bromine radical (Br). The Br radical adds to the alkene first, placing Br on the less substituted carbon and forming the more stable carbon radical. No carbocation forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. Valid 1-hexene; Br radical is the chain carrier in propagation step 1 (RO is initiation only); key 0 correct."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is drawn. It is treated with HBr and peroxides. On which carbon does the bromine end up, and why?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["On the methyl-bearing carbon, because that carbon is more substituted","No reaction occurs with an internal alkene","Br adds to both alkene carbons","On the ring CH carbon (less substituted), because the Br radical adds there to form the more stable tertiary carbon radical","On the methyl group carbon (exocyclic CH3)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In radical addition the Br radical bonds to the carbon yielding the more stable radical. The methyl-bearing ring carbon would give a tertiary radical, so Br adds to the adjacent ring CH (less substituted). Net anti-Markovnikov; product is 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. CC1=CCCCC1 is valid 1-methylcyclohexene; Br adds to the less-substituted ring carbon to give the tertiary radical; key 0 correct. The C1 methyl-bearing carbon is the substituted alkene carbon, so option 0's reasoning is sound."},{"q":"A student wants 1-bromo-4-methylpentane (Br on the terminal carbon) from 4-methyl-1-pentene (drawn). Which reagents give that product, and how does it compare to treating the same alkene with HBr alone?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(C)C","opts":["HBr + peroxides gives the terminal (anti-Markovnikov) bromide; HBr alone gives the Markovnikov 2-bromo isomer","HBr alone gives the terminal bromide; peroxides give the Markovnikov isomer","Br2/CCl4 gives the terminal bromide","Both conditions give the identical terminal bromide","HBr + peroxides gives the 2-bromo (Markovnikov) isomer"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Two concepts: peroxide (radical) pathway is anti-Markovnikov, putting Br on terminal CH2 to give 1-bromo-4-methylpentane; HBr without peroxide goes through a secondary carbocation (Markovnikov) to put Br on C2. The conditions give different regiochemistry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=CCC(C)C is valid 4-methyl-1-pentene; radical = terminal Br, ionic = C2 Markovnikov; key 0 correct; two-concept (regio + contrast) within scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene (drawn) is treated with HBr WITHOUT peroxides (ionic addition) versus WITH peroxides (radical addition). Which statement correctly contrasts the outcomes?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["The ionic pathway puts Br on the terminal carbon; the radical pathway puts Br on the internal carbon with rearrangement","With peroxides the radical rearranges but the ionic pathway does not","Both pathways give the same single product with no rearrangement","Without peroxides a carbocation forms and can rearrange (hydride/methyl shift) to a more stable cation; with peroxides the radical pathway adds Br to the terminal carbon and does NOT rearrange","Neither pathway adds HBr to this hindered alkene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Ionic HBr makes a secondary carbocation next to a quaternary carbon, which undergoes a methyl shift to a more stable tertiary cation (rearranged product). The radical pathway proceeds through a carbon radical that does not rearrange, adding Br anti-Markovnikov to the terminal carbon. Rearrangement (ionic) vs no rearrangement (radical).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. C=CC(C)(C)C is valid 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene; ionic addition gives a 2 cation that methyl-shifts to 3 tertiary; radical pathway no rearrangement; key 0 correct. Concept of carbocation rearrangement is standard sophomore depth."},{"q":"Two alkenes are considered for HBr/peroxide (radical) addition: styrene (C6H5-CH=CH2) and 1-hexene (drawn). Styrene reacts faster. Which factor best explains styrene's faster radical addition?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts":["Styrene's pi bond is stronger, so it reacts faster","Br radical addition to styrene generates a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, lowering the activation energy for the rate-determining addition step","Styrene forms a more stable carbocation, speeding the radical step","1-Hexene cannot undergo radical addition at all","Styrene is less sterically hindered than 1-hexene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Radical addition rate tracks the stability of the carbon radical formed in the rate-determining first propagation step. For styrene, Br adds to terminal CH2 and the resulting radical is benzylic (resonance-stabilized), so it forms faster than the secondary radical from 1-hexene. The carbocation trap is wrong because the radical mechanism has no carbocation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","issue":"Correct. Valid 1-hexene drawn; benzylic radical stabilization is the right rate explanation; key 0 correct; option 5 carbocation is a good trap. Within DAT depth (qualitative radical stability, no FMO/KIE math)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide (a radical initiator). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","BrCC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Benzoyl peroxide turns this into a radical, anti-Markovnikov addition. The bromine radical adds to the =CH ring carbon so that the more stable tertiary radical forms on the methyl-bearing carbon, which then abstracts H from HBr. Br ends up on the less substituted alkene carbon: the major product is 1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane. 1-Bromo-1-methylcyclohexane is the Markovnikov product of the ionic pathway; the remaining choices are a (bromomethyl)cyclohexane with the wrong skeleton, a dibromide, and an ethyl-substituted ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid and parse; no duplicate options. Anti-Markovnikov product confirmed; clean single-concept regiochemistry item."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HBr and a catalytic amount of ROOR (peroxide). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"With peroxide, HBr adds anti-Markovnikov through a radical chain: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 to create the resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, and H then caps the benzylic carbon. The major product is (2-bromoethyl)benzene, with Br on the terminal carbon. (1-Bromoethyl)benzene is the peroxide-free Markovnikov product; the alcohol, the vicinal dibromide, and the ring-brominated arene are not formed under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. Standard benzylic-radical anti-Markovnikov case."},{"q":"2-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. Choose the major product.","q_smiles":"C=C(C)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCCC(C)Br","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"correct":0,"why":"Radical (peroxide) conditions give anti-Markovnikov addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to form the more stable tertiary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br ends up on the primary carbon (1-bromo-2-methylbutane). The tertiary-bromide option is the Markovnikov product; the rest are wrong-regiochemistry, rearranged, or dibromide structures.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, no duplicates. Single-concept regiochemistry, in scope."},{"q":"Vinylcyclohexane is reacted with HBr in the presence of a peroxide initiator. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCCC1CCCCCC1","BrCCC1CCCCC1","BrCC(Br)C1CCCCC1","CC(Br)C1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Peroxide drives anti-Markovnikov addition. The bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2, the secondary radical that forms on the carbon next to the ring abstracts H from HBr, and Br ends up on the terminal (primary) carbon. The major product is (2-bromoethyl)cyclohexane. (1-Bromoethyl)cyclohexane is the Markovnikov isomer, and the other choices brominate the ring, form a dibromide, or use a seven-membered ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; opt4 is a valid cycloheptane wrong-skeleton distractor. One defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentene is treated with HBr and a peroxide (radical conditions). Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"C=CCC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)CC(Br)CBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CC(C)CCCBr","CCC(Br)C(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov (peroxide) addition: Br radical adds to the terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical at C2, then H caps C2, so Br is on the primary terminal carbon (1-bromo-4-methylpentane). The isopropyl branch is a spectator. The Markovnikov option puts Br on C2; the rest are rearranged, falsely tertiary, or dibromide structures.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; no duplicates. Single-concept regiochemistry, in scope."},{"q":"Allylbenzene (3-phenyl-1-propene) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. A student worries the benzene ring will redirect where bromine adds. Which structure is actually the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1Br","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"The terminal C=C is separated from the ring by a CH2, so it is NOT conjugated and gains no benzylic stabilization. Anti-Markovnikov addition simply puts Br on the terminal CH2: (3-bromopropyl)benzene. The trap options assume conjugation and place Br on an internal or benzylic carbon; others are a dibromide or ring-brominated arene.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid. Legit two-concept item (non-conjugation recognition + anti-Markovnikov regio); benzylic-trap distractor (opt2) is plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"1-Hexene reacts with HBr in the presence of a peroxide to give 1-bromohexane (anti-Markovnikov). In the chain-propagation steps, which species adds DIRECTLY to the C=C double bond, and to which carbon does it bond?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A bromide ion (Br-) adds to the more substituted carbon via nucleophilic attack","A bromine atom (Br radical) adds to the terminal CH2 carbon, generating the more stable secondary carbon radical","An alkoxy radical (RO) adds to the terminal carbon and is incorporated into the product","A bromine atom (Br radical) adds to the internal carbon, generating a primary carbon radical","A proton (H+) adds first to the terminal carbon to form a secondary carbocation"],"correct":1,"why":"The peroxide mechanism is a radical chain. In propagation, the chain carrier is the bromine ATOM (Br radical), which adds to the alkene first at the terminal CH2 so the resulting radical is the more stable (secondary) one. That radical then abstracts H from HBr, regenerating Br. No carbocation, no bromide-ion attack, and the alkoxy radical only initiates (it is not incorporated into the product).","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles is valid 1-hexene. Single propagation-step identification (not a full hand-drawn mechanism), so in scope; exactly one defensible answer with mechanistically wrong distractors."},{"q":"(E)-1-Phenyl-1-propene (PhCH=CHCH3) is treated with HBr and benzoyl peroxide. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1Br","CC(Br)C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"For this internal alkene the regiochemistry is set by radical stability. The bromine radical adds to the carbon bearing the methyl group because that generates a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical on the phenyl-bearing carbon; H then caps the benzylic carbon. The major product is (2-bromopropyl)benzene, with Br on the middle carbon and the benzylic carbon reduced to CH2. The classic trap is (1-bromopropyl)benzene, which puts Br on the benzylic carbon itself (the ionic Markovnikov outcome); the other choices are a terminal bromide, a dibromide, and a saturated-ring skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-radical-hbr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0); verified keyed SMILES equals PhCH2-CHBr-CH3. All options valid SMILES, no duplicates. Hard but in-scope (radical regiochemistry by benzylic stability)."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methyl-2-butene is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)=CCO","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)CCO","CC(C)C(O)C","OCC(C)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, and water adds to that more substituted carbon to give the tertiary alcohol 2-methyl-2-butanol. 3-Methyl-2-butanol is the anti-Markovnikov secondary alcohol, 3-methyl-1-butanol and 2-methyl-1-butanol put OH on a primary carbon that was never part of the double bond, and 3-methyl-2-buten-1-ol still has its double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Chemistry and keyed index correct. Minor: 'why' option-numbering is 1-indexed (slightly clunky vs 0-based key) but unambiguous. KEEP."},{"q":"Predict the major product of oxymercuration-demercuration of 1-methylcyclohexene (1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1C","CC1=CCCCC1O","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration adds water with Markovnikov regiochemistry and no rearrangement, because the bridged mercurinium ion never becomes a free cation. OH lands on the more substituted, methyl-bearing carbon to give 1-methylcyclohexanol, a tertiary alcohol. 2-Methylcyclohexanol is the anti-Markovnikov product, cyclohexylmethanol has the wrong skeleton, 1,2-dimethylcyclohexanol has an extra carbon, and the allylic alcohol still contains the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct, in scope, well-formed. KEEP."},{"q":"Including stereochemistry, give the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclohexene (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov and syn: boron (later replaced by OH with retention) goes to the less substituted ring carbon, and H goes to the methyl-bearing carbon on the same face. Because H and OH add to the same face, the methyl group and the OH end up on opposite faces, so the product is trans-2-methylcyclohexanol, drawn here as the (1S,2S) enantiomer of the racemic pair. The cis isomer would require anti addition, the flat 2-methylcyclohexanol omits the required trans relationship, 1-methylcyclohexanol is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, and cyclohexylmethanol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Confirmed opt0 = trans via 3D embedding. Correct and in scope. KEEP. (Same trans-2-methylcycloalkanol concept as Q8 - mild batch redundancy.)"},{"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(C)(C)C(O)C","CC(C)C(O)CC","CC(C)(C)C=CO","CCC(C)(C)CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration is concerted and anti-Markovnikov, and it never forms a carbocation, so the neopentyl skeleton stays intact. Boron, then OH, goes to the terminal carbon, giving the primary alcohol 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol. 3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is the Markovnikov alcohol, the enol still contains a double bond, and 2-methyl-3-pentanol and 2,2-dimethyl-1-butanol have skeletons that cannot arise from this alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Tests the no-rearrangement point cleanly. KEEP."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with aqueous H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed hydration). What is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCO","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)C(O)C","OCC(C)CC","CC(C)C=CO"],"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2, right beside the tertiary C3. A 1,2-hydride shift moves the charge to C3 as a tertiary cation, and water adds there to give the rearranged product 2-methyl-2-butanol. 3-Methyl-2-butanol is the unrearranged secondary Markovnikov alcohol (the trap), 3-methyl-1-butanol and 2-methyl-1-butanol are primary alcohols with the wrong regiochemistry, and the enol still contains a double bond.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Classic hydride-shift item, single reactive center, in scope. KEEP."},{"q":"Predict the major product of oxymercuration-demercuration of styrene (1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4).","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccccc1CO","OCCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1C(C)O","OC=Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration is Markovnikov: the benzylic carbon best stabilizes the partial positive charge in the mercurinium ion, so water attacks there and the OH ends up benzylic. After NaBH4 removes the mercury, the product is 1-phenylethanol. 2-Phenylethanol is the anti-Markovnikov alcohol, the enol still has its double bond, and the two ring-methylated alcohols have the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov is standard sophomore content. KEEP."},{"q":"What is the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-pentene (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH)?","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCO","CCC(O)CC","CCC(C)CO","CCCC(O)C","CCCC=CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the less hindered terminal carbon and is replaced by OH, giving the primary alcohol 1-pentanol. 2-Pentanol is the Markovnikov alcohol, 3-pentanol puts OH on a carbon that was never part of the double bond, 2-methyl-1-butanol is a branched isomer with the wrong skeleton, and the enol still contains a double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"None. Clean anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry item. KEEP."},{"q":"Give the major product of hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclopentene (1. BH3; 2. H2O2, NaOH), including stereochemistry.","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1CCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","OCC1CCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Two things happen at once: anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry puts OH on the less substituted ring carbon, and syn addition puts H on the methyl-bearing carbon on the same face as the OH. Since H and OH are cis to each other, the methyl group and OH are forced trans, giving trans-2-methylcyclopentanol (drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair). The cis isomer would require anti addition, the flat 2-methylcyclopentanol omits the stereochemistry the question asks for, 1-methylcyclopentanol is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, and cyclopentylmethanol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Confirmed opt0 = trans via 3D. Correct and in scope (single center, 2 layered concepts). KEEP. Note: near-duplicate of Q3 (trans-2-methylcycloalkanol via hydroboration); difficulty 3 is borderline but acceptable."},{"q":"The alkene shown is converted into 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (the tertiary alcohol, with OH on the ring carbon that bears the methyl group). Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Dilute H2SO4, H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration)","BH3-THF, then H2O2/NaOH","1. OsO4; 2. NaHSO3","Br2 in CCl4","1. O3; 2. Zn, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration (and oxymercuration) follow Markovnikov: water adds to the more substituted carbon, giving the tertiary alcohol via the more stable tertiary carbocation/mercurinium opening. BH3 then H2O2/NaOH is anti-Markovnikov and would give the secondary alcohol. The other reagents do not install a single OH.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Markovnikov hydration of 1-methylcyclohexene gives the tertiary alcohol; dilute H2SO4/H2O (index 0) is right and all distractors are clean. Valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene (shown) is treated with 1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4. The product is 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol (OH on C2, with the tert-butyl group intact, no skeletal rearrangement). What is the single most important reason oxymercuration is chosen over dilute acid for this substrate?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C=C","opts":["Oxymercuration gives anti-Markovnikov addition","Oxymercuration proceeds without carbocation rearrangement, so the carbon skeleton is preserved","Oxymercuration reduces the alkene to an alkane first","Oxymercuration adds water with syn stereochemistry to a single face","Oxymercuration is an oxidation that adds two OH groups"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Both acid-catalyzed hydration and oxymercuration are Markovnikov, but acid-catalyzed hydration goes through a free secondary carbocation that undergoes a hydride/methyl shift to the tertiary cation, scrambling the skeleton. Oxymercuration goes through a bridged mercurinium ion (no free open carbocation), so no rearrangement occurs and the Markovnikov alcohol with an intact skeleton is obtained.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Index 0 (no rearrangement / mercurinium ion) is the right reason; the other options are all false. Conceptual mechanism recognition, not a full hand-drawn mechanism, so within DAT scope. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"The product trans-2-methylcyclopentan-1-ol is desired from the alkene shown (1-methylcyclopentene), with the new OH placed on the LESS substituted alkene carbon. Which reagent sequence gives this anti-Markovnikov, syn-addition outcome?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts":["Dilute H3PO4, H2O","1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4","H2O, cat. H2SO4, heat","1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH","HBr, then NaOH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation adds H and OH across the double bond anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted carbon) and syn (H and OH add to the same face), which on a ring sets the trans relationship between the new OH and the adjacent methyl. Acid hydration and oxymercuration are both Markovnikov and would place OH on the more substituted carbon.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Hydroboration-oxidation of 1-methylcyclopentene giving trans-2-methylcyclopentanol is the standard textbook result (anti-Markovnikov + syn). Index 0 right; distractors are Markovnikov. Valid SMILES, two-concept (regio+stereo)."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation (1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH). Which statement correctly describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The OH ends up on the benzylic carbon, giving 1-phenylethanol (Markovnikov)","The double bond is reduced to give ethylbenzene with no OH","The ring is hydroxylated to give a phenol","Two OH groups are added to give a 1,2-diol","The OH ends up on the terminal carbon, giving 2-phenylethanol (anti-Markovnikov)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron (and ultimately OH) adds to the less hindered, less substituted terminal carbon, giving 2-phenylethanol. The Markovnikov product (1-phenylethanol, benzylic OH) would come from acid hydration or oxymercuration.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration of styrene gives 2-phenylethanol (primary, terminal OH). Index 0 right; the benzylic-OH distractor is the classic Markovnikov trap. Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Acid-catalyzed hydration begins with protonation of the alkene to form a carbocation, so reaction rate tracks the stability of that intermediate. Rank the following four alkenes from FASTEST to SLOWEST in acid-catalyzed hydration (the propene drawn is one of them): ethene, propene, 2-methyl-2-butene, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene.","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["All four react at the same rate","ethene > propene > 2-methyl-2-butene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene","2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > 2-methyl-2-butene > propene > ethene","2-methyl-2-butene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > ethene > propene","propene > ethene > 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene > 2-methyl-2-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The rate-determining step is carbocation formation, so the alkene that gives the most stable cation reacts fastest. 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene gives a tertiary cation (most stabilized, from the tetrasubstituted alkene), 2-methyl-2-butene also gives a tertiary cation but is less substituted overall, propene gives a secondary cation, and ethene gives an unstable primary cation (slowest). More alkyl substitution = faster.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Both top alkenes give tertiary cations, but the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is the most reactive, so 2,3-DM2B > 2-Me-2-butene > propene > ethene is defensible and unambiguous. Index 0 right. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"2-methylpropene (isobutylene, shown) is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4. Which product predominates, and by what rule?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C","opts":["a 1,2-diol, because dilute acid is an oxidant","methylpropene oxide (an epoxide)","tert-butanol (2-methylpropan-2-ol), by Markovnikov addition","isobutane, because the alkene is reduced","isobutanol (2-methylpropan-1-ol), by anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation gives the more stable tertiary carbocation at the central carbon, and water adds there to give tert-butanol. Anti-Markovnikov (giving isobutanol) would require hydroboration-oxidation. Dilute acid does not oxidize, reduce, or epoxidize the alkene.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Markovnikov hydration of isobutylene gives tert-butanol via the tertiary cation. Index 0 right; distractors are clean. Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"A chemist wants to convert 4-methylpent-1-ene (shown) cleanly into 4-methylpentan-2-ol (Markovnikov secondary alcohol) WITHOUT any risk of carbocation rearrangement scrambling the skeleton. Which reagent choice is best?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts":["HCl gas, then H2O","1. BH3-THF; 2. H2O2, NaOH","Dilute H2SO4, H2O, heat","Concentrated H2SO4 only (no water)","1. Hg(OAc)2, H2O; 2. NaBH4 (oxymercuration-demercuration)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Both oxymercuration and acid hydration are Markovnikov, but only oxymercuration avoids a free carbocation (it uses a bridged mercurinium ion), so there is no rearrangement. Acid hydration of this substrate could allow hydride shifts. Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov and would give the primary alcohol, not the desired secondary alcohol.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Oxymercuration gives the Markovnikov secondary alcohol without rearrangement; index 0 right. Hydroboration distractor (anti-Markovnikov) and acid (rearrangement-prone) are properly wrong. Valid SMILES."},{"q":"The alkene shown (1-ethylcyclohexene) is treated with BH3-THF followed by H2O2/NaOH. Considering BOTH regiochemistry (anti-Markovnikov) and stereochemistry (syn addition), which description matches the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC1=CCCCC1","opts":["The ethyl group is oxidized to a carboxylic acid","OH adds to the less substituted ring carbon (the CH), and H and OH add to the same face (syn), giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol","OH adds to the less substituted carbon, but H and OH add to opposite faces (anti)","Two OH groups add cis across the double bond","OH adds to the more substituted carbon bearing the ethyl group (Markovnikov)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov (OH to the less substituted ring CH, not the carbon bearing the ethyl) and syn (H and OH add to the same face). On the ring, syn addition of H and OH places the new OH and the boron-derived H on the same face, which makes the OH trans to the ethyl substituent, giving trans-2-ethylcyclohexan-1-ol.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-hydration","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Directly analogous to the canonical 1-methylcyclohexene -> trans-2-methylcyclohexanol result; anti-Markovnikov + syn gives trans-2-ethylcyclohexanol. Index 0 right; distractors cover Markovnikov, wrong stereo, diol, and oxidation. Valid SMILES, hard (regio+stereo)."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCC1CO","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation of the =CH carbon puts the positive charge on the methyl-bearing ring carbon as a tertiary cation, and water adds there to give 1-methylcyclohexanol. 2-Methylcyclohexanol is the anti-Markovnikov secondary alcohol, cyclohexylmethanol puts OH on an exocyclic primary carbon, and the two cyclopentane alcohols have the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Standard Markovnikov hydration, single concept, valid distractors."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with aqueous acid (H3O+). Markovnikov addition proceeds through a carbocation that can rearrange. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(O)C(C)C","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary cation at C2, adjacent to the quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift converts it into the more stable tertiary cation, and water traps that carbon to give 2,3-dimethyl-2-butanol. The trap is 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol, the unrearranged Markovnikov alcohol; 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol is anti-Markovnikov, and 2-methyl-3-pentanol and 2,2-dimethyl-1-butanol have skeletons that cannot arise from this alkene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Hydride/methyl-shift rearrangement is core DAT content; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) within scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["OCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)(C)CCO","CC(O)(C)C(C)C","OCC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration is Markovnikov with no rearrangement, because the bridged mercurinium ion never opens to a free cation. OH adds to the more substituted alkene carbon and the tert-butyl group stays intact, giving 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol. 2,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol is the rearranged product you would get only under aqueous acid (the trap), 3,3-dimethyl-1-butanol is anti-Markovnikov, the diol would need a dihydroxylation reagent, and 2,2-dimethyl-1-butanol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Pairs cleanly with Q2 to teach the no-rearrangement contrast; in scope."},{"q":"2-Methyl-1-butene is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)CC","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)CCO","CC(C)C(C)O","OCC(C)CC","O=CC(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the less substituted terminal CH2 of the alkene and is replaced by OH with retention, giving the primary alcohol 2-methyl-1-butanol. 2-Methyl-2-butanol is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, 3-methyl-2-butanol and 3-methyl-1-butanol have the wrong skeleton, and 2-methylbutanal is an aldehyde, which basic hydrogen peroxide does not produce from an alkylborane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Canonical anti-Markovnikov hydroboration, single concept."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Considering both regiochemistry and stereochemistry, which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C=C1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two layers. Regiochemistry: anti-Markovnikov addition puts OH on the less substituted ring carbon next to the methyl-bearing carbon. Stereochemistry: H and boron add syn, and OH replaces boron with retention, so H and OH sit on the same face; that forces the methyl group and the OH onto opposite faces, giving trans-2-methylcyclohexanol (drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair). The cis isomer would require anti addition, the flat 2-methylcyclohexanol has the right regiochemistry but ignores the stereochemistry the question asks for, 1-methylcyclohexanol is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol, and 2-methylenecyclohexanol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed; verified opt0=trans, opt1=cis via 3D embedding. Two-concept (regio+stereo) hard item at the upper edge of DAT depth but recognition-level, allowed."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with dilute H2SO4 / H2O. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration is Markovnikov: protonation of the terminal CH2 gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic cation, and water adds at the benzylic carbon to give 1-phenylethanol. 2-Phenylethanol is the anti-Markovnikov primary alcohol, phenylacetaldehyde and acetophenone are carbonyl compounds (oxidation products, not hydration), and benzyl alcohol has the wrong carbon count.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Benzylic Markovnikov hydration, single concept, classic substrate."},{"q":"1-Ethylcyclopentene is treated with (1) Hg(OAc)2, H2O then (2) NaBH4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC1=CCCC1","opts":["CCC1(O)CCCCC1","CCC1CCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","CCC1(O)CCCC1","OCCCC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration is Markovnikov with no rearrangement. OH goes to the more substituted ring carbon, the one bearing the ethyl group, giving the tertiary alcohol 1-ethylcyclopentanol. 2-Ethylcyclopentanol is the anti-Markovnikov secondary alcohol, 1-ethylcyclohexanol has a six-membered ring that cannot form here, 1-methylcyclopentanol is missing a carbon from the ethyl group, and 3-cyclopentyl-1-propanol is a ring-opened skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Markovnikov oxymercuration on a trisubstituted alkene, single concept."},{"q":"alpha-Methylstyrene (2-phenylpropene) is treated with (1) BH3 / THF then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)(C)c1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(C)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the less hindered terminal CH2, so the OH ends up on that primary carbon, giving 2-phenyl-1-propanol. The benzylic position does not receive the OH, which is the trap. 2-Phenyl-2-propanol is the Markovnikov benzylic tertiary alcohol, 1-phenyl-1-propanol and 1-phenyl-2-propanol have the wrong skeleton, and propiophenone is a ketone that this reaction does not produce.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed. Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration overriding benzylic stability is a high-value trap; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","BrC1CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br","BrC1(Br)CCCC1","BrC1CCC(Br)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds through a cyclic bromonium ion that bromide opens by backside (anti) attack, so the two bromines end up on opposite faces of the ring: trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane, drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair. The cis-1,2-dibromide is the meso compound that would require syn addition and is not formed; the gem-dibromide, the 1,3-dibromide, and the monobromide have the wrong connectivity for an addition across the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid, opt0 confirmed trans (anti-addition product) by 3D embedding, 5 distinct options, correct index 0."},{"q":"(E)-2-butene (trans-2-butene) reacts with Br2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["C/C=C(Br)/C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CC","CCC(C)(Br)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds anti through a bromonium ion. Anti addition to the trans (E) alkene produces 2,3-dibromobutane with opposite configurations at the two new stereocenters, (2R,3S), which has an internal mirror plane and is therefore meso. The (2S,3S) dibromide belongs to the racemic pair that comes from the cis alkene instead. 2-Bromo-2-butene, 2-bromobutane, and 2,2-dibromobutane are not products of adding Br2 across the double bond.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed meso by mirror-image test, correct anti-addition outcome for E-alkene. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"In water, the bromonium ion is opened by water rather than bromide. Water attacks the more substituted, methyl-bearing carbon because it carries the greater partial positive charge, so OH ends up on the tertiary carbon (Markovnikov OH) and Br on the adjacent secondary carbon, anti to the OH. The major product is 2-bromo-1-methylcyclohexanol. The isomer with Br on the tertiary carbon and OH on the secondary carbon reverses the regiochemistry; the diol, the dibromide, and 1-methylcyclohexanol come from different conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: regiochemistry correct (Markovnikov OH on tertiary C), all SMILES valid, 5 distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"Propene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts":["CC(O)CO","CC(Br)CO","CC(O)CCl","CC(O)CBr","CC(Br)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"In halohydrin formation water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted carbon, so OH goes to C2 (Markovnikov) and Br to C1, giving 1-bromo-2-propanol. 2-Bromo-1-propanol reverses the regiochemistry, 1,2-propanediol has no bromine, 1,2-dibromopropane is what forms in a dry inert solvent with no water, and 1-chloro-2-propanol has the wrong halogen.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 = 1-bromo-2-propanol (Markovnikov OH on C2). Valid SMILES, distinct options, index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclohexene reacts with Cl2 in CCl4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cl[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Cl","ClC1CCCCC1","ClC1(Cl)CCCCC1","Cl[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Cl","ClC1CCCC(Cl)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Cl2 adds through a cyclic chloronium ion that chloride opens by backside (anti) attack, placing the two chlorines on opposite faces of the ring: trans-1,2-dichlorocyclohexane, drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair. The cis-1,2-dichloride is the meso compound that would require syn addition and is not formed; the gem-dichloride, the 1,3-dichloride, and the monochloride have the wrong connectivity.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed trans (anti-addition) by 3D embedding; opt1 is cis/meso distractor. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OC(CBr)c1ccccc1","ClC(CBr)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the benzylic carbon, which carries the most positive character because the ring stabilizes it by resonance, so OH lands on the benzylic carbon and Br on the terminal CH2. The major product is 2-bromo-1-phenylethanol. 2-Bromo-2-phenylethanol reverses the regiochemistry, the diol has no bromine, the vicinal dibromide would form only without water, and the chloro-bromo compound has the wrong halogen.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 = 2-bromo-1-phenylethanol (OH benzylic). Valid SMILES, distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"(Z)-3-hexene (cis-3-hexene) reacts with Br2. Which structure represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C\\CC","opts":["CC/C=C(Br)\\CC","CC[C@H](Br)[C@H](Br)CC","CCC(Br)CCC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CC[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 to the cis (Z) alkene gives 3,4-dibromohexane in which both stereocenters have the same descriptor, (3R,4R) and (3S,4S), a racemic pair; the structure shown is the (3S,4S) member. The (3R,4S) diastereomer is the meso compound, which comes from the trans alkene instead. 3-Bromo-3-hexene, 3-bromohexane, and 3,3-dibromohexane are not products of adding Br2 across the double bond.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 confirmed chiral (d,l) by mirror test, correct anti outcome for Z-alkene; opt1 is meso distractor. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with Br2 in H2O. Which structure is the major product (halohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(Br)C(C)(C)O","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)Br","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)O","CC(O)C(C)(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted, tertiary carbon because it carries the greater partial positive charge, so the Markovnikov OH goes on the carbon bearing two methyl groups and Br goes on the adjacent secondary CH, anti to the OH. The major product is 3-bromo-2-methyl-2-butanol. 3-Bromo-3-methyl-2-butanol reverses the regiochemistry; the diol, the dibromide, and the chlorohydrin come from different reagents.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"Verified: opt0 has OH on tertiary C, Br on secondary C (Markovnikov). Valid SMILES, distinct, index 0 correct."},{"q":"An alkene is treated with Br2 in an inert solvent (CCl4). Which statement BEST classifies this reaction and its stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["Electrophilic substitution; one vinylic H is replaced by Br","Electrophilic addition; syn addition of the two Br atoms across the double bond","Electrophilic addition; anti addition of the two Br atoms across the double bond","Free-radical addition; both Br atoms add to the same carbon","Nucleophilic addition; Markovnikov addition of one Br and one H"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds by electrophilic addition through a bridged bromonium ion opened by backside bromide attack = anti addition (trans-1,2-dibromide). Single concept, in scope, exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-methyl-2-butene is reacted with Br2 in water. Markovnikov selectivity governs where the OH ends up. On which carbon does the OH (from water) add, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["On the less substituted carbon, because the bromonium is symmetric","Equally on both carbons, because the bromonium ion has no charge bias","On the less substituted alkene carbon, because water prefers the less hindered carbon only","On the more substituted alkene carbon, because that carbon bears the greater partial positive charge in the bromonium ion","On the more substituted carbon, because anti-Markovnikov rules apply with water"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The bromonium ion from an unsymmetrical alkene is not symmetric: the more substituted carbon bears more of the partial positive charge because it is better able to stabilize cation character. Water, the nucleophile, attacks that carbon, so the OH ends up on the more substituted alkene carbon (the one carrying two methyl groups in 2-methyl-2-butene) and Br on the less substituted carbon. This is Markovnikov regiochemistry, driven by charge distribution, not by steric preference and not by any anti-Markovnikov rule.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches name, answer correct, single concept (Markovnikov regiochemistry), in scope."},{"q":"You isolate the product shown below from treating an alkene with a halogen source. The product is a vicinal chlorohydrin (OH and Cl on adjacent carbons). Which single reagent/condition set would produce it from cyclohexene?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1Cl","opts":["Cl2, NaOH (concentrated)","Cl2, CCl4 (no water)","NaCl, H2O","HCl (gas)","Cl2, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A vicinal chlorohydrin (Cl and OH on adjacent carbons, anti to each other) comes from Cl2 in water: the chloronium ion is opened by water, which is present in huge excess as the nucleophile. Dry Cl2 in CCl4 gives the vicinal dichloride instead, HCl gas gives only chlorocyclohexane, NaCl in water does not react with an alkene, and concentrated NaOH would not produce the chlorohydrin.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Valid 1,2-chlorohydrin SMILES, reagent-selection answer correct and unique."},{"q":"An unknown alkene is treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 and the only product is the vicinal dibromide drawn below. Which reagent/condition set is responsible (no oxygen nucleophile incorporated)?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)Br","opts":["Br2, CH2Cl2 (anhydrous)","NBS, H2O","HBr, peroxides","Br2, light (hv) in excess","Br2, H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A clean vicinal dibromide with no oxygen incorporated means Br2 was added in an inert, anhydrous solvent such as CH2Cl2, so bromide (not water) opened the bromonium ion. Br2 in water or NBS in water would give the bromohydrin, HBr with peroxides adds only one Br plus one H, and Br2 with light drives radical substitution at the allylic position rather than addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Valid vicinal dibromide, reagent answer unique and correct."},{"q":"Styrene (phenylethene) is treated with Br2 in water. The bromonium ion can open at either carbon, but one carbon is strongly favored because of the adjacent benzene ring. Where does the OH end up, and why?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["OH adds to the benzylic carbon, because positive charge there is stabilized by resonance with the ring","OH adds to the terminal CH2 carbon, because it is least hindered","Br adds to the benzylic carbon and OH to the terminal carbon, anti-Markovnikov","Both carbons get OH because the intermediate is a free benzylic cation that traps two waters","OH adds to the benzylic carbon, because that carbon is the least substituted"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The benzylic carbon stabilizes positive charge by resonance with the ring, so the bromonium ion has most of its cation character there. Water attacks the benzylic carbon, putting OH on it and leaving Br on the terminal CH2, giving 2-bromo-1-phenylethanol. Steric arguments and substituent counting are not the reason; resonance stabilization of the developing charge is. No free benzylic cation that traps two water molecules is involved.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches styrene, regiochemistry answer correct and uniquely defensible."},{"q":"Two alkenes are each treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 under identical conditions. Alkene A is 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (tetrasubstituted, drawn) and alkene B is 1-butene (monosubstituted). Which reacts FASTER with Br2 and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["Alkene A, because tetrasubstituted alkenes react by a radical pathway that is faster","Alkene B, because the terminal alkene is less hindered so Br2 approaches more easily","Both react at the same rate, because bromonium formation is independent of substitution","Alkene B, because monosubstituted alkenes form more stable bromonium ions","Alkene A, because more alkyl substituents make the pi bond more electron-rich and the bromonium ion more stable"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Halogenation rate tracks how nucleophilic the alkene is. Each alkyl group donates electron density into the pi bond and stabilizes the developing positive charge in the bromonium ion, so the tetrasubstituted alkene, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (alkene A), reacts much faster with Br2 than monosubstituted 1-butene (alkene B). Reduced steric hindrance does not win here, the mechanism is ionic rather than radical, and bromonium formation is very much dependent on substitution.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. SMILES matches the drawn alkene A, reactivity answer correct and unique."},{"q":"trans-2-butene reacts with Br2 in CCl4 (anti addition through a bromonium ion). Considering both new stereocenters, what is the stereochemical relationship of the 2,3-dibromobutane product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["A single optically active (2R,3R) product only","A 1:1 mixture of the meso compound and the racemic pair","A racemic mixture of (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) enantiomers","A single meso compound (the two enantiomers superimpose due to an internal mirror plane)","A cis-dibromide formed by syn addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Br2 adds anti through a bromonium ion, placing the two bromines on opposite faces. Starting from trans-2-butene, anti addition produces 2,3-dibromobutane with opposite configurations at the two stereocenters, (2R,3S), which has an internal mirror plane: a single meso compound, not a racemate. The racemic (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair is what cis-2-butene would give, and a cis dibromide from syn addition does not occur with Br2.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Stereo-defined SMILES matches trans-2-butene, meso outcome correct, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2 in CH2Cl2 (an inert solvent). Which structure correctly shows BOTH the regiochemical and stereochemical outcome of the reaction?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1(Br)CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","BrC1CCCC1","Cl[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Cl","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds across the double bond with anti stereochemistry: bromide opens the cyclic bromonium ion by backside attack, so the two bromines end up on opposite faces of the ring. The product is trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane, drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair. The cis-1,2-dibromide (meso) would require syn addition and is not formed; 1,1-dibromocyclopentane and bromocyclopentane have the wrong connectivity, and the trans-1,2-dichloride has the wrong halogen.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (trans/anti confirmed in 3D), one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with Br2 in water (Br2/H2O). Which structure shows the correct regiochemistry AND stereochemistry of the halohydrin product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@@H]1O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1O"],"correct":1,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted, methyl-bearing carbon, which carries the greater partial positive charge, so the Markovnikov OH lands on the tertiary carbon and Br stays on the adjacent carbon. Because the opening is a backside attack, OH and Br end up anti, on opposite faces of the ring: trans-2-bromo-1-methylcyclohexanol. The cis diastereomer (OH and Br on the same face) cannot come from anti opening, the isomer with Br on the tertiary carbon and OH on the secondary carbon reverses the regiochemistry, and the diol and the dibromide come from different conditions.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (Markovnikov OH + trans/anti, both confirmed in 3D); one defensible answer; layered regio+stereo allowed."},{"q":"Propene (CH3CH=CH2) is treated with Br2 in water. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)CO","CCCBr","CC(O)CO","CC(O)CBr","CC(Br)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"Halohydrin formation: water attacks the more substituted carbon of the bromonium ion, so OH goes to C2 (Markovnikov) and Br to C1, giving 1-bromo-2-propanol. 2-Bromo-1-propanol swaps OH and Br, 1,2-dibromopropane is the product in a dry inert solvent, 1,2-propanediol has no bromine, and 1-bromopropane has only one new substituent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with Cl2 in water. Which structure is the major halohydrin product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(CO)c1ccccc1","ClCC1CCCCC1Cl","OC(CCl)c1ccccc1","ClC(CO)c1ccccc1","ClC(CCl)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The chloronium ion is opened by water at the benzylic carbon, which carries resonance-stabilized positive character, so OH ends up benzylic and Cl on the terminal CH2. The major product is 2-chloro-1-phenylethanol. 2-Chloro-2-phenylethanol swaps the regiochemistry, the vicinal dichloride forms only without water, the diol has no chlorine, and the dichlorinated cyclohexane has a saturated ring that cannot come from styrene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure represents the product with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCC(Br)CC1","BrC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti through a bromonium ion: bromide attacks from the face opposite the bromine bridge, so the two bromines end up trans. The product is trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane, drawn as one enantiomer of the racemic pair. The cis-1,2-dibromide would require syn addition and is not formed; 1,1-dibromocyclohexane, 1,4-dibromocyclohexane, and bromocyclohexane have the wrong connectivity for addition across the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct (trans/anti confirmed in 3D); one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Consider the bromonium-ion mechanism of Br2 addition to an alkene. Which statement BEST describes the curved arrow for the ring-opening step that sets the stereochemistry (substrate shown: cyclohexene)?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Bromide attacks a bromonium-ion carbon from the face opposite the C–Br+ bond (backside), giving anti (trans) addition","Bromide attacks the same face as the bromonium bridge, giving syn (cis) addition","Bromide adds to the less hindered carbon by a concerted syn pathway, giving the cis product","A free, planar carbocation forms first, so the bromide can add to either face equally (no stereochemical preference)","The pi electrons attack bromide, expelling Br+ and giving a Markovnikov rearranged product"],"correct":0,"why":"The step that sets the stereochemistry is the ring opening of the bromonium ion: bromide attacks one of the bridged carbons from the face opposite the carbon-bromine bond, in an SN2-like backside attack. That inverts the attacked carbon and places the two bromines anti to each other, which is why cyclohexene gives trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. Same-face attack or a concerted syn pathway would wrongly predict the cis product, a free planar carbocation does not form (the bridge is what prevents it), and the pi electrons attack Br2 in the first step, not bromide in the second.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Text reaction-mechanism item with molecule drawn; single-arrow identification within scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with Br2 in water. Which structure shows the correct halohydrin (consider where OH and Br go)?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)CBr","CC(Br)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)Br","CC(O)C(C)(C)O","CC(Br)C(C)(C)O"],"correct":4,"why":"Water opens the bromonium ion at the more substituted, tertiary carbon, which carries the greater partial positive charge, so the Markovnikov OH goes on the carbon bearing two methyl groups and Br goes on the adjacent secondary CH. The major product is 3-bromo-2-methyl-2-butanol. 3-Bromo-3-methyl-2-butanol reverses the regiochemistry, the dibromide forms only without water, the diol has no bromine, and the primary bromide 4-bromo-3-methyl-2-butanol has a rearranged skeleton that this reaction does not produce.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-halogenation","issue":"None. Markovnikov halohydrin regiochemistry correct; one defensible answer; in scope; visual."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-2-butene (trans-2-butene) is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA in CH2Cl2.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CC","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA delivers its oxygen to the alkene in one concerted, syn step, so the alkene geometry is preserved in the epoxide: a trans alkene gives the trans epoxide. The product is trans-2,3-dimethyloxirane, in which both ring carbons have the same descriptor; it is drawn as the (R,R) enantiomer of the racemic (R,R)/(S,S) pair, not the meso compound. The cis (meso) epoxide would come from cis-2-butene, the 2,3-diol would need a dihydroxylation reagent, 1,2-butanediol has the wrong skeleton, and 2-butanone is not an epoxidation product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed (R,R) trans-epoxide correct for E-alkene; all 6 SMILES valid; opt1 confirmed meso (cis) distractor."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCyclohexene is treated with a catalytic amount of OsO4 with NMO (N-methylmorpholine-N-oxide) as co-oxidant.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCC(O)C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O"],"correct":1,"why":"Catalytic OsO4 with NMO performs syn dihydroxylation: both C-O bonds form on the same face through a cyclic osmate ester, which is then hydrolyzed. The product is cis-1,2-cyclohexanediol, a meso compound with both OH groups on the same face. The trans-1,2-diol would require anti addition, cyclohexene oxide is what mCPBA gives, cyclohexanol has only one OH, and the 1,3-diol has the wrong regiochemistry.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed opt0 is cis (meso) diol — correct for OsO4 syn addition; opt1 confirmed trans distractor; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC12OC1CCCC2","CC1=CCCCC1O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1=O"],"correct":1,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes the alkene: the oxygen bridges the two former sp2 carbons, one of which carries the methyl group, giving 1-methylcyclohexene oxide (1-methyl-7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane). No diol forms unless the epoxide is hydrolyzed in a separate step, so both the cis and trans 1-methylcyclohexane-1,2-diols are wrong; the allylic alcohol and the alpha-hydroxy ketone are oxidation products that mCPBA does not produce from an alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed epoxide connectivity/formula correct; opt1=cis diol, opt2=trans diol distractors; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(Z)-2-butene (cis-2-butene) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic, aqueous).\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","C[C@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CC=O","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","CCC(O)CO"],"correct":1,"why":"Cold, dilute, basic KMnO4 performs syn dihydroxylation and does not cleave the double bond. Syn addition of two OH groups to the cis (Z) alkene produces 2,3-butanediol with opposite descriptors at the two stereocenters, (2R,3S), which has an internal mirror plane and is meso. The (2S,3S) diol belongs to the racemic pair that comes from the trans alkene, the cis epoxide needs a peroxyacid, acetaldehyde is the cleavage product of hot concentrated permanganate, and 1,2-butanediol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed opt0 confirmed MESO by RDKit; opt1 confirmed chiral (S,S) distractor; all SMILES valid. Hardest item, scope still sophomore."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nStyrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(O)cc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"mCPBA delivers one oxygen to the electron-rich vinyl C=C and leaves the aromatic ring alone, so styrene becomes styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane). The 1,2-diol would need OsO4 or cold KMnO4, benzaldehyde and phenylacetaldehyde would require cleavage or a skeletal change, and the ring-hydroxylated 4-vinylphenol puts oxygen on the wrong site entirely. The concept is chemoselectivity: a peracid attacks the isolated alkene, never the arene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; keyed styrene oxide correct; distractors are well-targeted (cleavage, diol, wrong-site)."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n1-Methylcyclopentene is treated with OsO4, then aqueous NaHSO3 workup.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCC(O)C1","CC12OC1CCC2","C[C@]1(O)CCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCC1","C[C@]1(O)CCC[C@@H]1O"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 adds both oxygens to the same face of the double bond through a cyclic osmate ester, and the NaHSO3 workup releases the diol, so 1-methylcyclopentene gives cis-1-methylcyclopentane-1,2-diol: a tertiary OH on the methyl-bearing carbon and a secondary OH on the neighboring carbon, both on the same face of the ring. The trans diol would require an anti route such as epoxide opening. The epoxide is the mCPBA product, the 1,3-diol puts an OH on the wrong carbon, and the single tertiary alcohol is a hydration product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified by 3D face-test: keyed opt0 cis, opt1 trans; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n(E)-3-hexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA.\n\nIncluding stereochemistry, which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)C(O)CC","CC[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CC[C@H]1O[C@@H]1CC","CC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","CCC1OC1CC"],"correct":2,"why":"Peracid epoxidation is concerted and syn, so the alkene geometry is locked into the ring: the E (trans) alkene gives trans-3,4-epoxyhexane, which is the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) pair with the two ethyl groups on opposite faces of the oxirane. The cis epoxide (the meso compound) would come from the Z alkene. The 1,2-diols are dihydroxylation products and need OsO4 or KMnO4, not mCPBA.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"Chemistry fully correct; keyed trans-epoxide verified. Minor: this is mechanistically the same 2-concept item as Q1 (rated 2) so a difficulty of 2 would be more consistent, but difficulty 1 is within tolerance — keep unchanged."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\n2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with a catalytic amount of OsO4 and NMO.\n\nWhich structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)C(C)(C)O","OCC(C)C(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(=O)C(C)C","CC1OC1(C)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Catalytic OsO4 with NMO performs syn dihydroxylation: an OH lands on each former alkene carbon. For 2-methyl-2-butene that means a secondary OH on the CH carbon and a tertiary OH on the carbon that carries two methyls, giving 2-methylbutane-2,3-diol. The epoxide is the mCPBA product, the single tertiary alcohol is Markovnikov hydration, the 1,3-diol puts an OH on the wrong carbon, and the ketone would require oxidative cleavage.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Verified: keyed vicinal diol (sec + tert OH) regiochem correct; opt3 is a wrong-regiochem 1,3-diol; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"A chemist treats cyclohexene (drawn) with one equivalent of meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid (mCPBA) in dichloromethane. Which product is formed?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1Cl","O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"correct":1,"why":"mCPBA transfers a single oxygen across the C=C in one concerted step, giving cyclohexene oxide, the epoxide fused to the six-membered ring. The trans-1,2-cyclohexanediols require a separate step (opening the epoxide with water), the chlorohydrin needs a halogen source, and cyclohexanone is at the wrong oxidation level and skeleton for this reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. mCPBA epoxidation; keyed epoxide is correct and unique. All SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with OsO4 followed by aqueous NaHSO3 workup. The reaction is best classified as which of the following?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Anti dihydroxylation (OH groups added to opposite faces)","Epoxidation followed by no further reaction","Syn dihydroxylation (both OH added to the same face)","Markovnikov hydration to a single alcohol","Oxidative cleavage to two carbonyl compounds"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 adds two hydroxyls to the SAME face via a cyclic osmate ester (syn/cis addition); NaHSO3 is reductive workup that does not cleave. Classification = syn dihydroxylation. Anti diol comes from epoxide opening; cleavage needs hot conc. KMnO4/ozonolysis. One defensible answer, molecule drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Classification correct and unique; substrate SMILES valid."},{"q":"You want to convert 1-methylcyclohexene (drawn) into a vicinal cis-diol in a single operation. Which reagent set should you choose?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hot concentrated KMnO4","O3, then (CH3)2S","OsO4, then NaHSO3 (or cold dilute KMnO4, OH-)","mCPBA in CH2Cl2","HBr with peroxides"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 with reductive workup (or cold dilute basic KMnO4) gives the syn cis-diol directly in one step. mCPBA stops at the epoxide; hot conc. KMnO4 and ozonolysis cleave the C=C; HBr/peroxides gives anti-Markovnikov hydrobromination. One defensible answer, substrate drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Reagent selection correct; 1-methylcyclohexene SMILES valid."},{"q":"Styrene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute, basic KMnO4. Which observation and product correctly describe this reaction?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The double bond is cleaved to give benzaldehyde and formaldehyde","HBr adds across the double bond","Purple color fades and 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol (a cis-diol) forms","An epoxide forms with no color change","Benzoic acid forms as the only organic product"],"correct":2,"why":"Cold dilute basic KMnO4 is a syn-dihydroxylation reagent (Baeyer test): gives the vicinal cis-diol 1-phenyl-1,2-ethanediol while purple permanganate is reduced (color fades to brown MnO2). Oxidative cleavage to carbonyls/acids requires HOT concentrated KMnO4. One defensible answer, styrene drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Cold/dilute KMnO4 = diol + decolorization; correct and unique."},{"q":"A student needs to prepare cyclopentene oxide from cyclopentene (drawn). Which single reagent accomplishes this epoxidation most directly?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","Cold dilute KMnO4, OH-","H3O+ (dilute aqueous acid)","mCPBA in CH2Cl2","OsO4, then NaHSO3"],"correct":3,"why":"A peroxyacid (mCPBA) epoxidizes the alkene in one concerted step to cyclopentene oxide. OsO4 and cold dilute KMnO4 give the syn-diol; dilute acid is inert to an isolated alkene; NaBH4 does not reduce simple alkenes. One defensible answer, cyclopentene drawn.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. mCPBA = epoxide; correct and unique; SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with OsO4 followed by reductive workup. What is the stereochemical outcome of the 2,3-butanediol formed?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C","CC(O)CC","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C","CC1OC1C","C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C"],"correct":2,"why":"OsO4 adds both OH groups to the same face of the alkene (syn addition), and with trans-2-butene that locks in the chiral (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair of 2,3-butanediol, formed as a racemic mixture; the structure shown is the (R,R) enantiomer, the standard way to represent that pair. The meso (2R,3S) diol is the trap: it is what syn addition to cis-2-butene gives. The epoxides are mCPBA products and 2-butanol is a hydration product, so neither fits this reagent.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. (R,R) key represents racemate; meso is the lone diol distractor; one defensible answer. No duplicates."},{"q":"Consider the two alkenes 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene (a tetrasubstituted alkene, drawn) and ethylene. Both are treated with mCPBA. Which alkene undergoes epoxidation FASTER, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene, because electron-withdrawing methyl groups speed the reaction","2,3-Dimethyl-2-butene, because more alkyl substituents make the alkene more nucleophilic (electron-rich)","They react at identical rates because mCPBA is not sensitive to substitution","Ethylene, because terminal alkenes are inherently more nucleophilic","Ethylene, because less steric hindrance always dominates the rate"],"correct":1,"why":"Peracid epoxidation is an electrophilic attack on the pi bond, so the more electron-rich the alkene, the faster it reacts. Alkyl groups donate electron density, which makes the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene far more nucleophilic than ethylene, so it is epoxidized much faster. The reasoning that methyl groups are electron-withdrawing is backwards, steric effects do not override this electronic trend for mCPBA, and terminal alkenes are the least nucleophilic, not the most.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","issue":"None. Substitution/nucleophilicity rationale is in scope and correct; tetrasubstituted alkene SMILES valid."},{"q":"trans-2-Butene (drawn) is treated with mCPBA in CH2Cl2. mCPBA delivers its oxygen to one face of the planar alkene in a single concerted step (a 'butterfly' transition state), so the two new C-O bonds form on the same face and the original alkene geometry is preserved. Which structure correctly represents the epoxide product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts":["C[C@@H]1O[C@@H]1C","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C","CCC(C)=O","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CCC1CO1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Because the oxygen is delivered to one face in a single concerted step, the trans relationship of the two methyl groups is preserved in the ring: trans-2-butene gives trans-2,3-dimethyloxirane, the chiral (2R,3R)/(2S,3S) pair, with the methyls on opposite faces of the epoxide. The cis (meso) epoxide would come from cis-2-butene, the 2,3-butanediol is an OsO4 or KMnO4 product, and 1,2-epoxybutane has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (trans/chiral epoxide from E-alkene). FLAW: opt1 and opt4 are the identical meso cis-epoxide (duplicate distractor). Correct answer remains the only right choice, so still keyable but the duplicate should be replaced (e.g., with a genuinely distinct distractor)."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with OsO4, then aqueous NaHSO3 workup. OsO4 adds both oxygens to the same face of the double bond through a cyclic osmate ester. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O=C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"OsO4 adds both oxygens to the same face through a cyclic osmate ester, and NaHSO3 reductively cleaves that ester to release the diol, so cyclohexene gives cis-1,2-cyclohexanediol, the meso compound with both OH groups on the same face. The trans diol would require anti addition (epoxide opening), the epoxide is the mCPBA product, cyclohexanol is a hydration product, and the 1,2-diketone would need further oxidation.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (cis/meso diol confirmed by 3D analysis). Clean single-concept item."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with mCPBA. Epoxidation is concerted and stereospecific, retaining the cis relationship of the two methyl groups. Which statement about the epoxide product is correct?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["A racemic mixture of (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) epoxides is formed.","Only an acyclic rearranged product, 1,2-epoxybutane, is formed.","A vicinal anti-diol is formed.","A single optically active (2R,3R) epoxide is formed.","A single achiral meso epoxide is formed (the cis-2,3-dimethyloxirane)."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Syn delivery of oxygen to the Z (cis) alkene keeps both methyls on the same face of the oxirane, giving cis-2,3-dimethyloxirane. That molecule has an internal mirror plane, so it is the meso (2R,3S) compound: a single achiral, optically inactive epoxide. The racemic (R,R)/(S,S) pair comes from the trans alkene, a single (R,R) enantiomer cannot form from an achiral alkene with an achiral reagent, and mCPBA never produces a diol.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"q_smiles valid; statement opt0 correct (meso/achiral cis-epoxide confirmed). In scope (recognition + single stereo concept)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic), then workup. This reagent performs syn-dihydroxylation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC12CCCCC1O2","CC1(O)CCCCC1=O","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cold, dilute, basic KMnO4 is a syn dihydroxylation reagent: both OH groups are delivered to the same face through a cyclic manganate ester. 1-Methylcyclohexene therefore gives cis-1-methylcyclohexane-1,2-diol, with the tertiary and secondary OH groups on the same face of the ring. The trans diol would require an anti route, the epoxide needs a peracid, the single tertiary alcohol is a hydration product, and the hydroxy ketone is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (cis diol confirmed by 3D analysis). Both diol options are chiral (no internal symmetry), so cis vs trans is the sole distinction - appropriate single-concept item."},{"q":"Styrene (phenylethene, drawn) is treated with mCPBA (1 equiv) in CH2Cl2 at room temperature. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"mCPBA epoxidizes only the alkene and leaves the aromatic ring untouched, so styrene gives styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane). The 1,2-diol is the OsO4 or KMnO4 product, benzaldehyde comes from oxidative cleavage, and acetophenone and 1-phenylethanol are at the wrong skeleton or oxidation level for a peracid.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (styrene oxide). Distractors span the standard mis-selections. Clean."},{"q":"(E)-Stilbene (trans-1,2-diphenylethene, drawn) is subjected to OsO4 (cat.) / NMO, a syn-dihydroxylation. Which structure correctly represents the product's stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C(/c1ccccc1)=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["OC(c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","c1ccccc1C1OC1c1ccccc1","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Syn dihydroxylation adds both OH groups to the same face of the alkene; with the E (trans) stilbene this gives the chiral (R,R)/(S,S) pair of 1,2-diphenyl-1,2-ethanediol, formed as a racemate, and the structure shown is the (S,S) enantiomer. The meso (R,S) diol is the classic trap: it would come from the cis alkene or from anti addition. The epoxide, benzil (the 1,2-diketone), and the mono-alcohol all come from other reagents.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (chiral d,l diol from E-alkene + syn addition; opt1 meso confirmed). Two layered stereo concepts (syn addition + E/Z->meso/dl) but single reactive center; difficulty 3 appropriate and within DAT recognition depth."},{"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with mCPBA, and the resulting epoxide is then heated with aqueous acid (H3O+). Acid-catalyzed epoxide opening proceeds by backside (anti) attack of water. Which structure is the final diol?","q_smiles":"C1CC=CC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCC1=O","OC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Two steps with opposite stereochemistry: mCPBA forms the epoxide by syn addition, then water opens that epoxide by backside (anti) attack, so the two OH groups end up on opposite faces. The final product is trans-1,2-cyclopentanediol, the chiral (1R,2R)/(1S,2S) pair. The cis (meso) diol is what direct OsO4 syn dihydroxylation would give, and the epoxide is only the intermediate, not the final product.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 correct (trans diol from anti opening, confirmed by 3D analysis). Two-concept (syn epoxidation + anti opening) but each is a single well-known step, not a 5-step synthesis - in scope."},{"q":"Three alkenes are considered for reaction with mCPBA: ethene, 2-methyl-2-butene (drawn), and (E)-2-butene. Epoxidation by a peracid is an electrophilic delivery of oxygen, so its rate increases with the number of electron-donating alkyl groups on the C=C. Which alkene reacts fastest, and what is its product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["2-Methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted) reacts fastest, giving 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane (its epoxide).","(E)-2-Butene reacts fastest, giving a vicinal anti-diol.","Ethene reacts fastest because it is the least hindered, giving oxirane.","All three react at the same rate; the peracid is not selective.","2-Methyl-2-butene reacts fastest, giving 3-methyl-2-butanone by rearrangement."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Peracid epoxidation is an electrophilic delivery of oxygen, so the rate rises with the number of electron-donating alkyl groups on the C=C: trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene reacts faster than disubstituted (E)-2-butene, which reacts faster than unsubstituted ethene. The product of 2-methyl-2-butene is its epoxide, 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane. mCPBA does not give diols, and it does not rearrange the alkene to a ketone.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-epoxidation-diol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"q_smiles valid; opt0 correct (trisubstituted reacts fastest; product correctly named 2,2,3-trimethyloxirane). Reactivity-trend recognition item, in scope. Batch contains 8 questions total (not 9)."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction sequence: the alkene shown is treated with (1) O3, then (2) (CH3)2S (dimethyl sulfide). Which set of carbonyl products forms?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.CCC=O","CC(C)=O.CC=O","CC(C)O.CCO","CC(=O)O.CC(C)=O","CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozone cleaves the C=C completely, and the (CH3)2S reductive workup stops at the carbonyl stage. The disubstituted carbon of 2-methyl-2-butene becomes acetone and the CH3CH= carbon becomes acetaldehyde, so the products are acetone plus acetaldehyde. The pair of alcohols is not an ozonolysis product, and acetic acid would require an oxidative workup such as H2O2.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 2-methyl-2-butene reductive ozonolysis gives acetone + acetaldehyde. All SMILES valid; distractors (oxidative acid, wrong skeletons, alcohols) plausibly wrong."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: cyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) H2O2 (oxidative workup). What is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)CCCC(=O)O","O=C1CCCCC1","O=C(O)CCCCCC(=O)O","O=C(O)CCCCC(=O)O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Oxidative ozonolysis of the cyclic alkene opens the ring; both former alkene carbons become COOH, giving hexanedioic (adipic) acid, HOOC(CH2)4COOH.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 6-carbon ring + oxidative workup = adipic acid (verified 6 carbons). Dialdehyde (reductive), C5/C7 diacids (miscount), cyclohexanone (no cleavage) are good distractors."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: 1-methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O (reductive workup). What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCCC=O","CC(O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Cleaving the endocyclic C=C opens the ring. The methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone and the other a CHO under Zn/H2O, giving 6-oxoheptanal (7 carbons).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 1-methylcyclohexene (7 C) reductive ozonolysis = 6-oxoheptanal. Two-concept (regio of ketone/aldehyde + reductive workup). Distractors solid."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: styrene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CO.OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1.O=C=O","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the vinyl C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the terminal =CH2 becomes formaldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Styrene reductive ozonolysis = benzaldehyde + formaldehyde. Distractors (oxidative acid+CO2, acetaldehyde/acetophenone wrong skeletons, alcohols) good."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: trans-2-butene is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O","CC(O)C(C)O","CC=O.CC=O","CC(=O)O.O=CO","CCC(=O)O.O=C=O"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot, concentrated KMnO4 cleaves the alkene oxidatively. Each internal CH3CH= disubstituted carbon becomes a carboxylic acid, giving two acetic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Symmetric 2-butene + hot KMnO4 = 2x acetic acid. Distractors: aldehydes (reductive), diol (cold KMnO4), wrong skeletons. E geometry irrelevant to products, fine."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: 1-hexene is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O.O=CO","CCCC(=O)O.O=C=O","CCCCC(=O)O.O=C=O","C=O.CCCCC=O","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Hot KMnO4 cleaves the C=C oxidatively. The internal carbon becomes pentanoic acid; the terminal =CH2 monosubstituted carbon is oxidized fully to CO2 and lost.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Terminal alkene + hot KMnO4 = pentanoic acid + CO2. The formic-acid pair is the classic trap (terminal CH2 -> CO2 not HCOOH). Two-concept (cleavage regio + terminal-to-CO2). Solid."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction: 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) (CH3)2S. What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)=O.CC(C)=O","CC(C)(O)C(C)(O)C","CC=O.CCC(C)=O","CC(C)=O.CC=O","CC(=O)O.CC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"This symmetric tetrasubstituted alkene cleaves so each carbon (bearing two methyls) becomes a ketone: two acetone. No aldehyde/acid possible since both carbons are fully substituted.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Tetrasubstituted symmetric alkene = 2x acetone regardless of workup. Acid/aldehyde options are impossible (good conceptual distractors); diol is OsO4/cold KMnO4 product."},{"q":"Predict the major organic products of the following reaction: (E)-1-phenylpropene (trans-beta-methylstyrene) is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H2O. Which set of products forms?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","CCC=O.O=Cc1ccccc1","C=O.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)O.O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the internal C=C. The phenyl-bearing carbon becomes benzaldehyde and the methyl-bearing carbon becomes acetaldehyde.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). 1-phenylpropene reductive ozonolysis = acetaldehyde + benzaldehyde. E/Z geometry is irrelevant to ozonolysis products so difficulty 3 is generous, but the formaldehyde trap and acetophenone/propanal distractors are valid. Keep."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with ozone (O3), followed by a reductive workup with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). Which TWO carbonyl compounds are formed?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["Acetone and carbon dioxide","2-methylbutanal only","Acetone and acetic acid","Acetone and acetaldehyde","Acetic acid and acetaldehyde"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"2-methyl-2-butene cleaves at C=C. The C(CH3)2 carbon (no H) becomes acetone (ketone); the CH carbon (one H) becomes acetaldehyde under reductive Me2S workup, which stops at carbonyls (no acid).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, reductive workup gives ketone+aldehyde, distractors plausible-but-wrong (acid would need oxidative). In scope, visual."},{"q":"A student wants to convert the alkene shown into a single ring-opened keto-ACID (a carboxylic acid and a ketone on the same chain). Which reagent set accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cold dilute KMnO4, OH-, 0 C","O3, then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S)","O3, then Zn / AcOH (reductive workup)","O3, then H2O2 (oxidative workup)","OsO4, then NaHSO3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"1-methylcyclohexene is cyclic, so cleavage opens the ring to one molecule. The CH carbon becomes a carboxylic acid only under OXIDATIVE workup (H2O2); the methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone. Reductive workups give a keto-aldehyde; cold KMnO4 and OsO4/NaHSO3 are syn-dihydroxylations (no cleavage).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right; reductive distractors give keto-aldehyde, dihydroxylation distractors give no cleavage. Exactly one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"The symmetric internal alkene shown is subjected to O3 then Zn/AcOH. What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts":["One equivalent of hexanedial","Propanal and propanoic acid","Two equivalents of acetone","Two equivalents of propanal","Two equivalents of propanoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"trans-3-hexene is symmetric; cleavage gives two identical fragments. Each sp2 carbon bears one H, so reductive workup (Zn/AcOH) yields two molecules of propanal (CH3CH2CHO). Oxidative workup would instead give propanoic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, symmetric alkene gives 2x propanal under reductive conditions. Distractors plausible (acid=oxidative, hexanedial=no cleavage). In scope, visual."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is treated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. What are the carbon-containing oxidation products?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzoic acid and formaldehyde","Benzoic acid and CO2","Benzaldehyde and CO2","Benzaldehyde and formaldehyde"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hot concentrated KMnO4 is oxidative cleavage. The CH carbon on the ring becomes benzoic acid; the terminal =CH2 is fully oxidized and lost as CO2. Benzaldehyde/formaldehyde would require reductive ozonolysis, not hot KMnO4.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right (benzoic acid + CO2 from terminal CH2 under hot KMnO4). Distractors are reductive-ozonolysis products. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The exocyclic alkene shown is treated with O3 followed by dimethyl sulfide. Which statement about the products is correct?","q_smiles":"C=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Cyclohexanone and formaldehyde are produced","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid and formic acid are produced","Only cyclohexanone is produced","Cyclohexanone and CO2 are produced","Cycloheptanone is produced by ring expansion"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Methylenecyclohexane has a disubstituted ring carbon (becomes cyclohexanone) and a terminal =CH2 (becomes formaldehyde under reductive Me2S workup). The exocyclic double bond means no ring opening. CO2 would require oxidative conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right, exocyclic CH2 -> formaldehyde under Me2S, ring stays intact (cyclohexanone). Distractors plausible-but-wrong. In scope, visual."},{"q":"For the tetrasubstituted alkene shown, how do the products of (i) O3 then Zn/AcOH compare with (ii) O3 then H2O2?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["Only the oxidative workup cleaves the alkene","Reductive gives acetaldehyde; oxidative gives acetic acid","Reductive gives a keto-aldehyde; oxidative gives a keto-acid","Reductive gives acetone; oxidative gives acetic acid","Both workups give the same product: two equivalents of acetone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"In 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene neither sp2 carbon bears a hydrogen, so each fragment is a ketone (acetone). With no C-H at the cleavage carbons, oxidative workup has nothing to over-oxidize, so reductive and oxidative workups give the identical product: two acetone molecules.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Keyed idx 0 right; tetrasubstituted alkene (no vinylic H) gives 2x acetone under both workups. Tests the 'no H -> no over-oxidation' insight. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Reaction of the cyclic alkene shown with O3 then Zn/AcOH gives a single dicarbonyl product. Classify the two carbonyl groups in that product.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","opts":["Two ketones (a diketone)","One ketone and one aldehyde","Two aldehydes (a dialdehyde)","One ketone and one carboxylic acid","Two carboxylic acids (a diacid)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"1,2-dimethylcyclopentene is tetrasubstituted at the C=C (each sp2 carbon carries a methyl and a ring carbon, no H). Ring-opening cleavage places a ketone at both former alkene carbons, giving the open-chain diketone 2,6-heptanedione (C7H12O2). With no vinylic C-H, no aldehyde or acid forms.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Keyed answer (idx 0, two ketones/diketone) is CORRECT and the classification is the actual answer asked. BUT the original why-text mis-names the product as '2,7-octanedione' (C8); the true product of 1,2-dimethylcyclopentene ozonolysis is 2,6-heptanedione (C7). Corrected the rationale; answer/option unaffected, so keep=true."},{"q":"(E)-1-phenylpropene (shown) undergoes O3 followed by H2O2 (oxidative workup). What are the two products?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzaldehyde and acetaldehyde","Benzoic acid and acetic acid","Benzoic acid and CO2","Acetophenone and formic acid","Benzaldehyde and acetic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Each alkene carbon bears one hydrogen. Oxidative workup (H2O2) converts both CH carbons to carboxylic acids: the ring side gives benzoic acid and the methyl side gives acetic acid. Reductive workup would give benzaldehyde + acetaldehyde. Neither carbon is a terminal =CH2, so no CO2 is lost.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as-is. Valid SMILES, keyed idx 0 right; both internal CH carbons -> acids under H2O2. Distractors are reductive products or wrong CO2/acetophenone. Single answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with (1) O3, then (2) Zn / H3O+ (dimethyl sulfide also works). Because both alkene carbons are part of the ring, cleavage opens the ring to give a single product. Which structure is that product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCC=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"Reductive ozonolysis cleaves the endocyclic trisubstituted C=C; the methyl-bearing carbon becomes a ketone and the CH carbon becomes an aldehyde. Because both alkene carbons were in the ring, cleavage opens the ring into a single seven-carbon chain, 6-oxoheptanal, which keeps every carbon of the starting 1-methylcyclohexene. The reductive workup (Zn/H3O+ or dimethyl sulfide) stops at the aldehyde, so the carboxylic acid choices are out, and the six-carbon dialdehyde and keto aldehyde have lost a carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; correct=0 (6-oxoheptanal, C7H12O2, mass conserved). One defensible answer, distractors are wrong oxidation level or carbon count. In scope, visual."},{"q":"trans-3-Hexene is subjected to ozonolysis with an OXIDATIVE workup (O3, then H2O2). What are the organic products?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)O","CCC=O","CCC(=O)CC","CC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":0,"why":"Cleavage of the symmetric internal alkene gives two identical 3-carbon CH fragments; oxidative (H2O2) workup carries each aldehyde on to a carboxylic acid, giving two equivalents of propanoic acid. Propanal is the reductive-workup answer; the E geometry is irrelevant since the sp2 carbons are destroyed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Oxidative workup correctly gives propanoic acid (not propanal). All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with O3 followed by Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which pair of carbonyl compounds is formed?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC(=O)C(=O)C","CC(C)=O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC(C)=O","CC=O.CC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"The trisubstituted alkene cleaves so the =C(CH3)2 end gives acetone and the =CHCH3 end gives acetaldehyde. Reductive workup keeps the aldehyde (rules out the acetic-acid choice). The two-acetone and two-acetaldehyde choices misassign which carbon carried two methyls.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): acetaldehyde + acetone. All SMILES valid (dot-separated multi-fragment). One defensible answer; distractors are wrong oxidation level or regiochemistry. In scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Pentene (drawn) is heated with concentrated, hot KMnO4. Which statement best describes the products of this oxidative cleavage?","q_smiles":"CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Butanoic acid plus formic acid (HCOOH), with the CH2 surviving as a formic acid","Pentanal plus formaldehyde, since hot KMnO4 stops at the aldehyde stage","2-Pentanone, because KMnO4 oxidizes the alkene with Markovnikov regiochemistry","Pentanedioic acid (glutaric acid) from both carbons becoming -COOH","Butanoic acid plus CO2 and water (the terminal =CH2 is fully oxidized and lost as CO2)"],"correct":4,"why":"Hot/concentrated KMnO4 oxidatively cleaves the alkene: the internal CH becomes butanoic acid while the terminal =CH2 is over-oxidized to CO2. It is not formic acid, not an aldehyde (KMnO4 does not stop there), and no ketone forms.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Text options; q_smiles drawn (1-pentene). Terminal CH2 to CO2 is the standard hot-KMnO4 rule. One defensible answer. In scope, visual stem."},{"q":"Vinylcyclohexane is treated with O3, then dimethyl sulfide (CH3SCH3). Two carbonyl products form; formaldehyde is one of them. Which structure is the OTHER product?","q_smiles":"C=CC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=C(O)C1CCCCC1","O=CC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"The terminal vinyl cleaves: =CH2 leaves as formaldehyde and the ring-attached =CH- becomes cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (C7H12O). DMS is reductive, so the product is an aldehyde, not the acid. The CH2 homolog, residual ring alkene, and alcohol are wrong oxidation level or skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): cyclohexanecarbaldehyde. All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclopentene undergoes ozonolysis with a reductive (Zn/H3O+) workup. Because the alkene is in the ring, cleavage gives a single acyclic product. Which is it?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC(=O)C1","OC(=O)CCCC(=O)O","O=CCCCCC=O","O=CCCC=O","O=CCCCC=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Endocyclic cleavage of the 5-membered ring (both alkene carbons are CH) opens to pentanedial (glutaraldehyde, C5H8O2). Reductive workup forbids the diacid; butanedial (C4) and hexanedial (C6) have wrong carbon counts; cyclopentanone implies no cleavage.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): glutaraldehyde, C5H8O2, mass conserved from C5H8. All SMILES valid; one defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"In the ozonolysis of styrene (drawn), the alkene first reacts with O3 to form an unstable five-membered initial adduct that then fragments and recombines. Which sequence correctly names the species along the mechanism, in order?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Epoxide -> diol -> molozonide -> carbonyl products","Molozonide (primary ozonide) -> carbonyl oxide + carbonyl fragment -> ozonide (secondary ozonide) -> carbonyl products","Carbocation -> molozonide -> ozonide -> carbonyl products","Ozonide (secondary ozonide) -> molozonide -> carbonyl oxide -> carbonyl products","Carbonyl oxide -> ozonide -> molozonide -> carbonyl products"],"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis: [3+2] of O3 across the alkene gives the molozonide (primary ozonide); it fragments to a carbonyl + carbonyl oxide, which recombine to the secondary ozonide; workup delivers the carbonyls. Other orderings invert the sequence or invoke off-pathway species (epoxide, carbocation).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Recognition-level intermediate naming (molozonide/ozonide), not full arrow-pushing, so within DAT depth though most demanding scope-wise. Text options; q_smiles (styrene) valid. One defensible answer."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclohexene (both alkene carbons are fully substituted, each bearing a methyl) is heated with hot, concentrated KMnO4. Predict the cleavage product.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=C(C)CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=CC(C)CCCC(C)C=O","CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O"],"correct":2,"why":"Both alkene carbons are tetrasubstituted (no H), so cleavage gives only ketones even under harsh KMnO4. The endocyclic bond opens the ring to 2,7-octanedione (C8H14O2, mass conserved from C8H14). The C7 diketone is one carbon short, the dialdehyde is impossible (no H on the sp2 carbons), and a keto-acid would require an H-bearing alkene carbon.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-ozonolysis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0): 2,7-octanedione, 8 carbons, C8H14O2, mass conserved. All SMILES valid; tetrasubstituted alkene correctly yields two ketones. One defensible answer. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\ncyclohexene + H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC1","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2, Pd/C) adds H2 across the C=C, reducing cyclohexene to cyclohexane (o0). No hydration (no OH), the alkene is consumed, and the C6 ring skeleton is unchanged (not methylcyclopentane).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemistry correct, keyed index 0 (cyclohexane) is right, one defensible answer, in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n2-methyl-2-butene + H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(C)C","CCCCC","CCC(C)CO","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"H2 over Pd/C adds hydrogen across the trisubstituted C=C and nothing else, so 2-methyl-2-butene becomes 2-methylbutane. The carbon skeleton is unchanged (no rearrangement to neopentane or pentane), and no oxygen is introduced, so the alcohols are out.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Correct product 2-methylbutane at index 0; clean single-concept hydrogenation; valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n2-butyne + H2, Lindlar catalyst","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["C/C=C/C","CC#CC","C=CCC","CCCC","C/C=C\\C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst is poisoned palladium: it delivers one equivalent of H2 syn across the alkyne and stops at the alkene, so 2-butyne gives cis-2-butene, the (Z) alkene. The trans (E) alkene is the Na/NH3 product, butane would require over-reduction with an unpoisoned catalyst, and 1-butene has the wrong double-bond position.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Z-alkene correctly keyed (RDKit-verified STEREOZ); regio+stereo two-concept, in scope.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\nstyrene (vinylbenzene) + H2 (1 equiv), Pd/C","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C=Cc1ccccc1","C=CC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1","CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ordinary H2 over Pd/C reduces the isolated vinyl C=C but not the aromatic ring, which needs high pressure or a special catalyst, so styrene gives ethylbenzene. Ethylcyclohexane and vinylcyclohexane both have a reduced ring, unreacted styrene ignores the reagent, and 1-phenylethanol adds an oxygen that nothing supplies.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemoselective alkene-vs-arene reduction, ethylbenzene correctly keyed at 0; in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n1-hexyne + excess H2, Pd/C","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["CCCCCCO","C=CCCCC","CC#CCCC","CCCCCC","C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Unpoisoned Pd/C with excess H2 reduces the terminal alkyne all the way, two equivalents of H2, to hexane. Stopping at 1-hexene would require Lindlar's catalyst. The chain stays linear (no cyclohexane), the triple bond does not migrate to 2-hexyne, and no OH is introduced.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Full reduction to hexane correctly keyed at 0; Lindlar/positional/oxygenation distractors are plausible-but-wrong; in scope.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n3-hexyne + H2, Lindlar catalyst","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["C=CCCCC","CC/C=C/CC","CCCCCC","CC#CCCC","CC/C=C\\CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst adds one H2 syn across the internal alkyne and stops there, so 3-hexyne gives cis-3-hexene, the (Z) alkene. The trans (E) alkene is the dissolving-metal (Na/NH3) product, hexane is over-reduction, and 1-hexene and 2-hexyne have the wrong unsaturation position.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Z-alkene correctly keyed (RDKit STEREOZ); regio+stereo two-concept, in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Predict the major organic product of the following reaction:\n\n4-methylpent-3-en-2-one (mesityl oxide) + H2 (1 equiv), Pd/C","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CC(C)O","CC(O)CC(C)CO","CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(=O)C=C(C)C","CC(C)=CC(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"H2 over Pd/C reduces the C=C of the enone and leaves the ketone C=O alone, so mesityl oxide gives 4-methyl-2-pentanone. Pd/C does not reduce an isolated ketone to an alcohol, which rules out the alcohol and the diol; keeping the alkene while reducing the carbonyl is backwards; and the unchanged enone ignores the reagent.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. Chemoselective enone C=C reduction (ketone retained) correctly keyed at 0; single concept (chemoselectivity), in scope, valid visual SMILES.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with H2 gas in the presence of a Pd/C catalyst. Which classification BEST describes this transformation?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C","opts":["Electrophilic addition of HX following Markovnikov's rule","Free-radical substitution at the allylic position","Reduction by anti addition of two hydrogen atoms across the pi bond","Oxidation by anti addition of two hydroxyl groups across the pi bond","Reduction by syn (suprafacial) addition of two hydrogen atoms across the pi bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"H2 over a heterogeneous metal catalyst (Pd/C, Pt, Ni) reduces an alkene to an alkane by delivering both H atoms to the same face of the pi bond (syn addition) from the catalyst surface. Distractors (oxidation/anti-dihydroxylation, HX addition, radical substitution, anti H2) are all classic wrong descriptors.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 1-hexene SMILES valid; syn-addition reduction is the single defensible answer; distractors plausible-but-wrong. In scope."},{"q":"You need to convert the internal alkyne shown into the corresponding cis (Z) alkene WITHOUT over-reducing it to the alkane. Which reagent should you choose?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["H2 with PtO2 (Adams' catalyst)","Excess H2 with Raney nickel","H2 with Pd/C","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline)","Na metal in liquid NH3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned Pd catalyst stops at the alkene and delivers both H syn, giving the cis (Z) alkene. Pd/C, PtO2, and Raney Ni over-reduce to the alkane; Na/NH3 dissolving-metal gives the trans alkene.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 2-butyne SMILES valid; Lindlar is the unique answer for cis selectivity; trans (Na/NH3) and over-reduction distractors are the standard foils. In scope."},{"q":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2/Pd) of the cyclic alkene shown is performed. What is the major organic product of this reaction?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts":["Benzene","Cyclohexanol","Cyclohexanone","Cyclohexane","1,2-dibromocyclohexane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"H2/Pd adds two H across the single C=C of cyclohexene to give saturated cyclohexane. No O or halogen is introduced, and benzene (more unsaturated) is not formed by reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclohexene SMILES valid; cyclohexane is the unambiguous product; distractors are standard wrong functional groups. In scope."},{"q":"The product shown was obtained in high stereoselectivity from the catalytic hydrogenation of 2-butyne. Which set of reagents/conditions produced THIS specific geometric isomer?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["HBr, then KOtBu","H2, Lindlar's catalyst","Na, liquid NH3","H2, Pd/C (excess)","H2, Raney nickel"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The drawn product is cis-2-butene (Z, confirmed STEREOZ). Syn addition of one H2 to the alkyne with Lindlar's catalyst gives the cis alkene. Na/NH3 would give trans; Pd/C or Raney Ni would over-reduce to butane.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles C/C=C\\C confirmed Z (cis-2-butene); Lindlar is the unique reagent set giving this isomer. In scope and visual."},{"q":"Styrene (shown) is hydrogenated with one equivalent of H2 over Pd/C under mild conditions. Which statement correctly describes the chemoselectivity of this reaction?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Both the vinyl group and the aromatic ring are reduced, giving ethylcyclohexane","Only the vinyl C=C is reduced; the aromatic ring is untouched, giving ethylbenzene","Only the aromatic ring is reduced; the vinyl group is untouched","The vinyl group is converted to an alcohol via Markovnikov addition","No reaction occurs because conjugation prevents hydrogenation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Isolated/vinyl alkenes reduce readily with H2/Pd under mild conditions, but the aromatic ring needs forcing conditions (high P/T, Rh/Pt). Styrene gives ethylbenzene with the ring intact.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Styrene SMILES valid; chemoselectivity (alkene over arene) is the single defensible answer; layered concept (reactivity + chemoselectivity) is within DAT depth."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclopentene (shown) undergoes catalytic hydrogenation with H2/Pt. Because the reaction proceeds by syn addition of H2, which stereochemical descriptor applies to the 1,2-dimethylcyclopentane product?","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCC1","opts":["A racemic mixture of two enantiomers with no defined cis/trans relationship","trans (the two methyl groups end up on opposite faces of the ring)","The product is achiral and planar, so no descriptor applies","cis (the two methyl groups end up on the same face of the ring)","An equal mixture of cis and trans because the catalyst is not stereospecific"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Both H atoms add to the same face (syn). The two ring-bound methyls on C1/C2 end up on the same face relative to each other, giving cis-1,2-dimethylcyclopentane (a meso compound). Classic textbook syn-addition stereochemistry result.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = 1,2-dimethylcyclopentene (C7H12) valid; syn addition gives cis product; two-concept (mechanism stereo + ring descriptor) within scope. Single defensible answer."},{"q":"A chemist runs a competitive hydrogenation comparing the relative rates of H2/Pd uptake for the three alkenes below. Based on alkene substitution, which ranks them CORRECTLY from FASTEST to SLOWEST hydrogenation? I = monosubstituted (1-hexene), II = disubstituted (cyclohexene), III = tetrasubstituted (2,3-dimethyl-2-butene).","q_smiles":"CCCCC=C.C1CCC=CC1.CC(C)=C(C)C","opts":["I > II > III","II > I > III","III > I > II","III > II > I","I > III > II"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Heterogeneous hydrogenation rate decreases with increasing alkene substitution (more steric hindrance to flat adsorption on the catalyst; also slightly greater thermodynamic stability). Least-substituted reacts fastest: monosubstituted (I) > disubstituted (II) > tetrasubstituted (III).","difficulty":2,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. All three SMILES valid and match labels (1-hexene C6H12 mono, cyclohexene C6H10 di, 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene C6H12 tetra). Ranking I>II>III is the textbook result; single defensible answer. In scope."},{"q":"1,2-Dimethylcyclohexene is treated with H2 over Pd/C. Considering that the two hydrogen atoms add to the same face of the double bond, which compound, including stereochemistry, is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1C","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1C","CCCCCC(C)C","CC1CCCCC1C","C=C1CCCCC1C"],"correct":0,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation delivers both hydrogens from the metal surface to the same face of the alkene (syn addition). On 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene that puts the two methyl groups on the same face of the ring, giving cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane, which is a meso compound. The trans isomer would require anti addition, the methylenecyclohexane still has a double bond, and the open-chain 2-methylheptane has lost the ring.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All 6 SMILES valid; opt0 confirmed cis/meso by 3D; single right answer; in DAT scope (syn-addition cis/trans recognition)."},{"q":"2-Butyne is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C\\C","C=C(C)C","C/C=C/C","C=CCC","CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst is poisoned palladium: it adds one H2 syn across the alkyne and stops at the alkene, so 2-butyne gives cis-2-butene, the (Z) isomer. trans-2-Butene is the Na/NH3 product, butane is over-reduction, and 1-butene and 2-methylpropene have the wrong constitution.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; Z/E correctly assigned; canonical Lindlar item; in scope."},{"q":"trans-Benzalacetone (4-phenyl-3-buten-2-one) is stirred with 1 equivalent of H2 over Pd/C. Which is the product, given that Pd/C reduces the isolated C=C of an enone but leaves the ketone C=O and the aromatic ring intact under these mild conditions?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Under these mild conditions H2 over Pd/C reduces the conjugated C=C and leaves the ketone and the aromatic ring intact, so trans-benzalacetone gives 4-phenyl-2-butanone. 4-Phenyl-2-butanol would require reducing the ketone as well, 4-phenyl-3-buten-2-ol reduces the wrong group, the cyclohexyl ketone has a saturated ring, and the enol is not an isolable product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (incl. opt3 cyclohexyl ketone and opt5 enol); chemoselectivity standard; one right answer; in scope."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde is treated with H2 over Pd/C. Recalling that Pd/C reduces alkene C=C bonds but not aldehyde C=O bonds or aromatic rings, which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCCC1CCCCC1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","OC/C=C/c1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"H2 over Pd/C saturates only the C=C, so trans-cinnamaldehyde gives 3-phenylpropanal with the aldehyde and the ring untouched. Cinnamyl alcohol reduces the wrong group, 3-phenyl-1-propanol reduces both, 3-cyclohexylpropanal has a saturated ring, and ethylbenzene has lost a carbon, which no reduction can do.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; canonical chemoselective hydrogenation; single answer; in scope."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-1-propyne (an internal alkyne) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which alkene is formed?","q_smiles":"CC#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst performs syn addition of one H2 and stops at the alkene, so 1-phenyl-1-propyne gives cis-1-phenylpropene, the (Z) alkene with the methyl and phenyl on the same side. The trans (E) alkene is the Na/NH3 product, propylbenzene is over-reduction, styrene has the wrong skeleton, and the cyclohexyl alkene has a reduced ring.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; Z/E correctly assigned; standard Lindlar item; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with D2 (deuterium gas) over Pd. This experiment is used to demonstrate the stereochemistry of catalytic hydrogenation. Which product shows that the two deuterium atoms added with the expected stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[2H]C1([2H])CCCC1","[2H][C@@H]1CCC[C@H]1[2H]","[2H][C@@H]1CCC[C@@H]1[2H]","[2H]C1CCC([2H])C1","[2H]C1CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (or deuteration) is a syn addition: both atoms are delivered from the metal surface to the same face of the alkene. The product that demonstrates this is cis-1,2-dideuteriocyclopentane, with one D on each former alkene carbon and both on the same face. The trans isomer would require anti addition, the geminal and mono-deuterated products violate one D per alkene carbon, and the 1,3-pattern puts D on the wrong carbons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; opt0 confirmed cis by 3D; classic D2 syn-addition demonstration; single answer; in scope."},{"q":"You need to convert 1-heptyne into 1-heptene (the terminal alkene) without over-reducing it to heptane. Which set of reagents accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Na in liquid NH3","H2 over PtO2 (Adams' catalyst)","H2 with Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3, Pb, quinoline)","BH3 then H2O2/NaOH","Excess H2 over Pd/C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb and quinoline) is the standard way to stop at the alkene: 1-heptyne gives 1-heptene without over-reduction. Excess H2 over Pd/C or over PtO2 reduces all the way to heptane. Na in liquid NH3 is the route to trans alkenes from internal alkynes; a terminal alkyne is simply deprotonated to its acetylide under those conditions, so it is not the choice here. Hydroboration-oxidation adds the elements of water and leads to a carbonyl compound, not an alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkene-hydrogenation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid; reagent-selection (text opts, opts_are_structures=false) with molecule drawn in stem; standard reagent logic; one right answer; in scope."},{"q":"Propyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["CCC","CC(Br)(Br)C","C=C(Br)C","CC#C","CC=CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"One equivalent of HBr adds to a terminal alkyne with Markovnikov orientation: H goes to the terminal carbon and Br to the internal carbon, because that path goes through the more substituted (more stable) vinyl cation. Propyne therefore gives 2-bromopropene. 1-Bromopropene is the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, 2,2-dibromopropane needs a second equivalent, unreacted propyne ignores the reagent, and propane would require reduction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Correct, single answer, in scope, valid/distinct visual SMILES."},{"q":"2-Butyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["CC(Br)=CC","CC(Br)C(Br)C","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","CC(Br)(Br)C(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(/Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Br2 adds across the triple bond through a bridged bromonium-type intermediate, so the second bromide attacks from the opposite face and the two bromines end up trans on the new double bond. On 2,3-dibromo-2-butene each alkene carbon has Br as its higher priority group, so trans bromines means the (E) isomer. The (Z) isomer would require syn addition, the saturated dibromide and tetrabromide reflect extra reagent, and the monobromoalkene cannot come from Br2 by mass balance.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Anti-addition stereochem verified: keyed E (Br trans) is correct."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["CC=C(Cl)CC","C=C(Cl)CCC","CCC=C(C)Cl","CCCC=CCl","CCCC(Cl)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"HCl adds to a terminal alkyne with Markovnikov orientation: the proton goes to the terminal carbon, giving the more substituted vinyl cation, and chloride lands on C2. 1-Pentyne therefore gives 2-chloro-1-pentene. 1-Chloro-1-pentene is the anti-Markovnikov product, the internal vinyl chlorides (2-chloro-2-pentene and 3-chloro-2-pentene) have the wrong regiochemistry, and 2-chloropentane is saturated, which one equivalent of HCl cannot produce.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Correct, single answer, in scope, valid visual SMILES."},{"q":"Acetylene (ethyne) is treated with 2 equivalents of HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#C","opts":["C=CCl","CC(Cl)Cl","ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClCCCl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The first HCl converts acetylene to vinyl chloride. The second addition is also Markovnikov: protonation gives the cation on the chlorine-bearing carbon, which is stabilized by a chlorine lone pair, so the second Cl lands on the same carbon. Both chlorines end up geminal, giving 1,1-dichloroethane. 1,2-Dichloroethane is the vicinal isomer with the wrong regiochemistry, the tri- and tetrachloro products are over-chlorinated, and vinyl chloride is only the one-equivalent intermediate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Markovnikov x2 gem-dichloride correct."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C#Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCc1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","BrC=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation at the terminal carbon of phenylacetylene gives a vinyl cation on the benzylic carbon, which is stabilized by resonance with the ring, so Br adds there (Markovnikov), giving alpha-bromostyrene, (1-bromovinyl)benzene. beta-Bromostyrene is the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, the gem-dibromide needs a second equivalent, ring bromination is the wrong site, and ethylbenzene would require reduction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Benzylic Markovnikov regiochem correct."},{"q":"4-Octyne (a symmetric internal alkyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC#CCCC","opts":["CCCC(Br)C(Br)CCC","CCC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CCC","CCC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CCC","CCCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)(Br)CCC","CCCC=C(Br)CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Because 4-octyne is symmetric there is no regiochemistry to decide, only stereochemistry: Br2 adds anti through a bromonium-type intermediate, so the two bromines end up trans across the new double bond. With Br outranking propyl on each alkene carbon, trans bromines means (E)-4,5-dibromo-4-octene. The (Z) isomer would require syn addition, the saturated dibromide and the tetrabromide reflect extra reagent, and the monobromoalkene cannot form from Br2 by mass balance.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. Anti-addition stereochem verified: keyed E (Br trans) correct."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["C=C(Br)CCCC","CCCC=C(C)Br","CCCCC(Br)C","CCCCC=CBr","CCC=C(Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Without peroxides, HBr adds ionically with Markovnikov orientation: H to the terminal carbon, Br to C2 via the more substituted vinyl cation, so 1-hexyne gives 2-bromo-1-hexene. 1-Bromo-1-hexene is the radical (peroxide) regiochemistry, which is not operating here; 2-bromo-2-hexene and 3-bromo-3-hexene have the double bond in the wrong place; and 2-bromohexane is saturated, which one equivalent of HBr cannot deliver.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Chemistry correct/single answer; difficulty overrated (labeled 3, actually single-concept Markovnikov = 1). Corrected difficulty to 1."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown (1-butyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which statement best describes the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts":["A geminal dibromide on C2","A vicinal dibromide across C1 and C2","A vinyl bromide with Br on C2 (the more substituted carbon), formed by Markovnikov addition","2-butanone, via Markovnikov hydration","A vinyl bromide with Br on C1 (the terminal carbon), formed by anti-Markovnikov addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"One equivalent of HBr adds to 1-butyne with Markovnikov orientation: the proton goes to the terminal CH, generating the more stable secondary vinyl cation, and Br lands on C2 to give 2-bromo-1-butene, a vinyl bromide. A single equivalent stops at the vinyl halide, so the geminal and vicinal dibromides are out; the anti-Markovnikov vinyl bromide needs peroxides; and 2-butanone is a hydration product that needs water and HgSO4.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCC). Markovnikov regiochemistry correct, one defensible answer, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Starting from the alkyne shown (propyne), which reagent conditions would convert it to 2,2-dibromopropane (a geminal dibromide)?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["1 equivalent of HBr","Br2 in water (bromohydrin conditions)","2 equivalents of HBr","1 equivalent of Br2","2 equivalents of Br2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Two equivalents of HBr add to propyne in two Markovnikov steps: the first gives 2-bromopropene, and the second protonates to form a cation on the bromine-bearing carbon (stabilized by the halogen lone pair), so the second Br lands on C2 as well, giving 2,2-dibromopropane. One equivalent of HBr stops at the vinyl bromide; Br2 gives vicinal dibromides or the tetrabromide, never a geminal product; and Br2 in water gives a bromohydrin.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CC). gem-dihalide from 2 eq HX is standard; one defensible answer."},{"q":"You want to convert the terminal alkyne shown (1-pentyne) into a methyl ketone (2-pentanone). Which reagent set accomplishes this Markovnikov hydration?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["1. R2BH (disiamylborane); 2. H2O2, NaOH","O3, then Zn/H2O","H2O, H2SO4 only (no catalyst)","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","2 equivalents of HCl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Mercury(II)-catalyzed hydration (H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4) adds water with Markovnikov orientation: the OH goes to the internal carbon, forming an enol that tautomerizes to the methyl ketone, 2-pentanone. Hydroboration-oxidation is the anti-Markovnikov route and gives pentanal; two equivalents of HCl give a gem-dichloride; ozonolysis cleaves the triple bond; and acid alone without the mercury catalyst is too slow to be the standard answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCCC). Mercuric-catalyzed Markovnikov hydration correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"The internal alkyne shown (2-butyne) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. Which best classifies this reaction and its stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["Markovnikov addition giving a geminal dibromide","Syn (cis) addition of Br2, giving the (Z)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene","Free-radical substitution at the methyl groups","Anti (trans) addition of Br2 across the triple bond, giving the (E)-2,3-dibromo-2-butene vinyl dibromide","Electrophilic addition with no defined stereochemistry because the alkyne is symmetric"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Br2 adds to 2-butyne through a bromonium-type intermediate, so the second bromide attacks from the opposite face and the two bromines end up trans: anti addition. On each carbon of the resulting 2,3-dibromo-2-butene, Br outranks methyl, so trans bromines corresponds to the (E) isomer. Syn addition would give the (Z) isomer, a geminal dibromide needs HBr not Br2, radical substitution needs light and a radical initiator, and the symmetry of the alkyne removes regiochemistry but not stereochemistry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (CC#CC). E-label of anti product confirmed by RDKit (Br>CH3, Br trans => E); regio+stereo single layered concept, in scope."},{"q":"The compound 2,2-dichlorohexane was prepared from a six-carbon alkyne. Which alkyne and reagent combination is the correct precursor?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["1-hexyne + 1 equivalent of HCl","1-hexyne + 2 equivalents of Cl2","1-hexyne + 2 equivalents of HCl","3-hexyne + 2 equivalents of HCl","2-hexyne + 1 equivalent of HCl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A geminal dichloride at C2 comes from two successive Markovnikov additions of HCl to a terminal alkyne: 1-hexyne first gives 2-chloro-1-hexene, then the second HCl places a second Cl on the same carbon, giving 2,2-dichlorohexane. One equivalent of HCl stops at the vinyl chloride; Cl2 gives vicinal dichlorides or the tetrachloride, never a geminal product; and the internal alkynes 2-hexyne and 3-hexyne cannot place both chlorines on C2.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCCCC, 1-hexyne). Retrosynthetic gem-dihalide reasoning correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (shown) is treated with 1 equivalent of HCl. Which carbon bears the chlorine in the major vinyl chloride product, and why?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The internal (benzylic) carbon, because that vinyl cation is resonance-stabilized by the ring (Markovnikov)","Both carbons equally, giving a 1:1 mixture","The internal carbon, but only after a hydride shift","The terminal carbon, because of anti-Markovnikov addition","Neither carbon; HCl does not add to aryl-conjugated alkynes"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Protonation at the terminal carbon generates a vinyl cation on the internal, benzylic carbon, which is resonance-stabilized by the ring. Chloride therefore adds to that internal carbon, giving (1-chlorovinyl)benzene, the Markovnikov product. No hydride shift is involved, the reaction is highly regioselective rather than a 1:1 mixture, and HCl adds readily to aryl-conjugated alkynes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#Cc1ccccc1). Benzylic vinyl-cation stabilization reinforces Markovnikov; one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Consider electrophilic addition of HBr to alkynes. Rank the relative rate of the FIRST HBr addition for these three substrates: 1-butyne (shown), 2-butyne, and acetylene (HC#CH).","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts":["1-butyne > 2-butyne > acetylene","all three react at essentially the same rate","acetylene > 1-butyne > 2-butyne","2-butyne > acetylene > 1-butyne","2-butyne > 1-butyne > acetylene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Electrophilic addition of HBr to an alkyne is rate-limited by formation of a vinyl cation, and alkyl groups stabilize that cation by electron donation. 2-Butyne, which can form a vinyl cation flanked by two alkyl groups, reacts fastest; 1-butyne, whose Markovnikov cation carries one alkyl group, is intermediate; and acetylene, which can only form an unstabilized vinyl cation, is slowest. The order is 2-butyne, then 1-butyne, then acetylene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C#CCC). Standard substitution-stabilizes-cation ranking; ranking item with molecule drawn, opts text. One defensible order."},{"q":"The symmetrical internal alkyne 3-hexyne (shown) is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2 in CH2Cl2. Which single product is formed, and by what stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["3,4-dibromo-3-hexene as an inseparable E/Z mixture because the alkyne is symmetric","(E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene, by anti addition through a bromonium-ion-like intermediate","a racemic mixture of 3,4-dibromohexane (full saturation)","3,3-dibromohexane, a geminal dibromide","(Z)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene, by syn addition"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Br2 adds to 3-hexyne through a bromonium-type intermediate, so the two bromines end up trans across the new double bond: anti addition. On each alkene carbon Br outranks ethyl, so the trans arrangement is the (E) isomer, (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene, formed as a single stereoisomer rather than an E/Z mixture. One equivalent of Br2 does not go on to the saturated tetrabromide or a racemic dibromoalkane, and a geminal dibromide would need HBr, not Br2.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (CCC#CCC, 3-hexyne). E-label of anti product confirmed by RDKit; stereospecific anti addition correct, one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Butyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts":["CC/C(Br)=C","CC/C=C\\Br","CCC(Br)C","CC/C=C/Br","C=CCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"One equivalent of HBr adds to a terminal alkyne with Markovnikov orientation: H goes to the terminal carbon and Br to C2, the carbon that supports the more stabilized vinyl cation, so 1-butyne gives 2-bromo-1-butene. The (E) and (Z) 1-bromo-1-butenes are the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, 2-bromobutane would require a second addition of hydrogen, and 4-bromo-1-butene has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; spurious / on terminal =CH2 is cosmetic. In scope, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Butyne reacts with 1 equivalent of HCl. Halogen acids add to alkynes with anti addition of H and Cl. Which structure correctly shows the major vinyl chloride product, including its geometry?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts":["C/C(Cl)=C/C","C/C(Cl)=C\\C","CCC(Cl)C","CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","CC(Cl)CCl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The stem specifies anti addition, so the new H (on C3) and the new Cl (on C2) end up on opposite sides of the double bond, which puts the Cl cis to the methyl on C3. Assigning priorities: on C2, Cl outranks methyl; on C3, methyl outranks H. The two higher-priority groups (Cl and the C3 methyl) are on the same side, so the product is (Z)-2-chloro-2-butene. The (E) isomer would correspond to syn addition, and the remaining structures are saturated or carry the wrong number of chlorines.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = Z). RDKit-confirmed Z/E. Anti-HCl stipulated by stem so self-consistent; regio+stereo, in scope."},{"q":"Propyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["CC(Br)CBr","C=C(Br)C","BrCCCBr","CC(Br)(Br)CBr","CC(Br)(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Both equivalents of HBr add with Markovnikov orientation. The first gives 2-bromopropene; the second protonates at the CH2 end to form a cation on the bromine-bearing carbon, stabilized by the halogen lone pair, so the second Br also lands on C2. The product is the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromopropane. 1,2-Dibromopropane and 1,3-dibromopropane have the wrong regiochemistry, the tribromide is over-brominated, and 2-bromopropene is only the one-equivalent intermediate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2. Halogen addition to an alkyne proceeds with anti stereochemistry. Which structure is the major product, including its geometry?","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts":["CCC/C(Br)=C/Br","CCCC(Br)(Br)CBr","CCCC(Br)CBr","CCC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC/C(Br)=C\\Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti across the triple bond, placing the two new C-Br bonds on opposite sides of the resulting double bond. Assigning priorities: on C1, Br outranks H; on C2, Br outranks propyl; with the two bromines trans, the product is (E)-1,2-dibromo-1-pentene. The (Z) isomer would come from syn addition, and the saturated dibromides and the tribromide reflect extra reagent or the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = E), RDKit-confirmed. Regio+stereo, in scope, one answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCC(Cl)CCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)C","ClCCCCCCCl","CCCCC(Cl)(Cl)CCl","CCCC(Cl)(Cl)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Two equivalents of HCl add to 1-hexyne in two Markovnikov steps: the first gives 2-chloro-1-hexene, and the second places a second Cl on C2 because the cation on the chlorine-bearing carbon is stabilized by a chlorine lone pair. The product is the geminal dichloride 2,2-dichlorohexane. 1,2-Dichlorohexane and 1,6-dichlorohexane have the wrong regiochemistry, the trichloride is over-chlorinated, and 3,3-dichlorohexane would come from 2-hexyne or 3-hexyne, not from the terminal alkyne.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrC=Cc1ccccc1","BrC/C=C/c1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov addition: H goes to the terminal carbon and Br to the internal benzylic carbon, because that vinyl cation is stabilized by resonance with the ring. The product is alpha-bromostyrene, (1-bromovinyl)benzene. beta-Bromostyrene is the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, cinnamyl bromide is a different skeleton, (1-bromoethyl)benzene would require a second addition of hydrogen, and ring bromination is a different reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Hexyne is treated with 1 equivalent of Br2, which adds anti across the triple bond. Which structure is the major product, including geometry?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)CC","CCCC(Br)(Br)CC","CCC(Br)C(Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(\\Br)CC","CC/C(Br)=C(/Br)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Anti addition of Br2 places the two bromines on opposite sides of the new double bond. On each alkene carbon Br outranks ethyl, so trans bromines corresponds to the (E) isomer: (E)-3,4-dibromo-3-hexene. The (Z) isomer would require syn addition, and the saturated dibromide, geminal dibromide, and tribromide are at the wrong oxidation level or regiochemistry for one equivalent of Br2 on an alkyne.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct (index 0 = E), RDKit-confirmed. Regio trivial (symmetric), stereo concept, in scope, one answer."},{"q":"4-Methyl-1-pentyne is treated with 1 equivalent of HBr. Which structure correctly represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC#C","opts":["CC(C)(Br)CC=C","CC(C)CC(Br)C","CC(C)CC(Br)(Br)C","CC(C)C/C(Br)=C","CC(C)C/C=C/Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov addition to the terminal alkyne: H goes to C1 and Br to C2, the carbon that supports the more substituted vinyl cation, so 4-methyl-1-pentyne gives 2-bromo-4-methyl-1-pentene. The isobutyl branch is a spectator and does not rearrange. The (E)-1-bromoalkene is the anti-Markovnikov product, the tertiary bromide at C4 would need a rearrangement that does not occur, 2-bromo-4-methylpentane is saturated, and the gem-dibromide needs a second equivalent.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-addition","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid. Difficulty over-tagged as 3 (true single-concept Markovnikov, branch inert); kept since chemistry is sound."},{"q":"Predict the major product. CH3-C#CH (propyne) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4 (catalytic). Choose the structure of the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)O","CC(C)=O","CC(O)CO","CCC(=O)O","CCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Hg(II)/acid hydration is Markovnikov: OH adds to the more substituted carbon (C2), giving an enol that tautomerizes to acetone (a methyl ketone). Propanal is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (hydroboration); the enol is only the intermediate; the diol and carboxylic acid are wrong reactions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer acetone (CC(C)=O) is the correct Markovnikov ketone; exactly one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 1-Hexyne (CH3CH2CH2CH2-C#CH) is treated with dilute H2SO4, H2O, and HgSO4. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(O)CO","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC=O","C=C(O)CCCC","CCCC(=O)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Mercury(II)-catalyzed hydration adds water to a terminal alkyne with Markovnikov orientation: OH goes to the internal carbon to give an enol, which tautomerizes to the methyl ketone, 2-hexanone. Hexanal is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde that hydroboration-oxidation would give, 3-hexanone has the carbonyl on the wrong carbon, the enol (1-hexen-2-ol) is only the intermediate before tautomerization, and the 1,2-diol is a different reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 2-hexanone correct; enol distractor verified as genuine tautomer (C6H12O); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 1-Pentyne (CH3CH2CH2-C#CH) is treated with (1) BH3.THF (or a disiamylborane), then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CC","CCCCC=O","CCCCC(=O)O","CCCC=CO","CCCC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron then OH ends up on the terminal carbon, giving an enol that tautomerizes to pentanal (aldehyde). 2-Pentanone is the Markovnikov methyl ketone; 3-pentanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the acid is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer pentanal correct (anti-Markovnikov aldehyde); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. Phenylacetylene (Ph-C#CH) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1OC1c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov Hg/acid hydration places OH on the more substituted benzylic carbon, giving acetophenone (a methyl ketone). Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde (hydroboration); benzaldehyde loses a carbon; the epoxide and benzoic acid are unrelated reactions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid (styrene oxide distractor parses); keyed answer acetophenone correct; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. Phenylacetylene (Ph-C#CH) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (a hindered borane), then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, placing the carbonyl on the terminal carbon to give phenylacetaldehyde (PhCH2CHO). Acetophenone is the Markovnikov methyl ketone (Hg/acid); benzaldehyde loses a carbon; the enol is the intermediate; benzoic acid is over-oxidation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer phenylacetaldehyde correct (anti-Markovnikov); one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 4-Methyl-1-pentyne ((CH3)2CHCH2-C#CH) undergoes hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the structure of the major product.","q_smiles":"C#CCC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)C(C)C","CC(C)C=O","CC(C)CC=CO","CC(=O)CC(C)C","O=CCCC(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration of the terminal alkyne gives the aldehyde at the terminal carbon: 4-methylpentanal, with the isopropyl branch untouched. The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; a short branched aldehyde loses carbons; the enol is the intermediate; no carbocation rearrangement occurs.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 4-methylpentanal correct; two-concept (regio + branch retention) still in DAT scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 2-Butyne (CH3-C#C-CH3) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Because the alkyne is internal and symmetrical, decide the single major product.","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CC","CC=C(C)O","CC(O)C(C)O","CCC(C)=O","CCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"An internal alkyne has no terminal carbon, so hydration can only give a ketone, never an aldehyde. Because 2-butyne is symmetrical, adding OH to either carbon gives the same enol, which tautomerizes to a single product, 2-butanone. Butanal would need a terminal alkyne, 3-pentanone has an extra carbon, 2,3-butanediol is a different reaction, and the enol is only the intermediate before tautomerization.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 2-butanone (C4H8O) correct; 3-pentanone distractor confirmed as 5-carbon mismatch; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product. 3-Hexyne (CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: (1) BH3.THF, (2) H2O2, NaOH. Choose the single major product and note why an aldehyde is not an option here.","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)C(=O)CC","CCCCCC=O","CCC=C(O)CC","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCC(=O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"3-Hexyne is internal and symmetrical, so hydration (by either method) can only give a ketone, and both orientations converge on 3-hexanone. Hydroboration's anti-Markovnikov selectivity is irrelevant for a symmetrical internal alkyne. An aldehyde (hexanal) is impossible without a terminal C-H; 2-hexanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the intermediate; the 1,2-diketone is over-oxidation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed answer 3-hexanone (C6H12O) correct; enol distractor verified as true tautomer; one defensible answer; in scope (difficulty=3 is a touch generous for a symmetry-override item but acceptable)."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown is treated with H2O, dilute H2SO4, and HgSO4. Which reagent set would instead convert this same alkyne into the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["1. Na, NH3 (l), 2. H2O","HBr (excess), peroxides","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S","1. Disiamylborane (or BH3), 2. H2O2 / NaOH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Hg2+ and aqueous acid hydrate a terminal alkyne with Markovnikov orientation, giving the methyl ketone. To get the anti-Markovnikov aldehyde instead, use hydroboration-oxidation: a hindered borane such as disiamylborane (or BH3), then H2O2/NaOH, which places the oxygen on the terminal carbon and tautomerizes to the aldehyde. Ozonolysis cleaves the triple bond, Na/NH3 is a reduction, and HBr with peroxides adds bromine, so none of those is a hydration.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 1-hexyne valid; exactly one defensible answer. The parenthetical '(or BH3)' is slightly loose since BH3 can add twice to terminal alkynes, but disiamylborane is named and the answer is still the only anti-Markovnikov route. Keep."},{"q":"1-Octyne is subjected to: (1) disiamylborane, then (2) H2O2, NaOH. What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCCCC=O","CCCCCCCO","CCCCCCC(O)=C","CCCCCCC(=O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal carbon, and after oxidation/tautomerization the carbonyl forms there, giving octanal (CCCCCCCC=O). 2-Octanone is the Markovnikov product; 3-octanone is wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the non-tautomerized intermediate; octanol is a wrong-formula over-reduction distractor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = octanal). All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct; distractors differ by regio/tautomer/over-reduction. q_smiles is 1-octyne (C8). Keep."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (shown) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Mercuric-catalyzed Markovnikov hydration adds OH to the more substituted (internal, benzylic) sp carbon; the enol tautomerizes to acetophenone (CC(=O)c1ccccc1), a methyl ketone. Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov hydroboration product; the others are wrong oxidation states or the non-tautomerized enol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = acetophenone). All option SMILES valid and distinct. Classic Markovnikov benzylic case; one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"A chemist wants to make hexanal from 1-hexyne (shown). Which single statement correctly classifies the transformation and the reagents required?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["Anti-Markovnikov hydration; use (1) disiamylborane (2) H2O2/NaOH","Oxidative cleavage; use (1) O3 (2) Zn","Markovnikov hydration; use (1) BH3 (2) H2O2/NaOH","Markovnikov hydration; use H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","Anti-Markovnikov hydration; use H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hexanal has its carbonyl on the terminal carbon, so making it from 1-hexyne is an anti-Markovnikov hydration, and the reagents that do that are hydroboration-oxidation: disiamylborane followed by H2O2/NaOH. The choices that pair Markovnikov with borane, or anti-Markovnikov with H2O/H2SO4/HgSO4, mismatch the label with the reagent (mercury is Markovnikov and gives 2-hexanone; borane is anti-Markovnikov). Ozonolysis is a cleavage, not a hydration.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 1-hexyne valid. Distractors are deliberate name/reagent mismatches; exactly one self-consistent answer. Keep."},{"q":"The internal, symmetrical alkyne 3-hexyne (shown) is treated with H2O, H2SO4, and HgSO4. What is the result, and how many carbonyl products form?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["No reaction occurs with internal alkynes","A single aldehyde, because hydration is anti-Markovnikov","A single ketone, 2-hexanone, by Markovnikov addition","A single ketone, 3-hexanone, because both alkyne carbons are equivalent","A mixture of two regioisomeric ketones"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"3-Hexyne is symmetric, so both sp carbons are equivalent; hydration gives one enol regardless of which carbon is hydrated, tautomerizing to a single ketone, 3-hexanone. No regioisomeric mixture (would require an unsymmetrical internal alkyne), no aldehyde (needs a terminal alkyne), and internal alkynes do react. 2-Hexanone is structurally impossible from this symmetric substrate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). q_smiles 3-hexyne valid and symmetric. Symmetry-recognition concept, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Two reactions are run. Reaction A: 1-pentyne + H2O/H2SO4/HgSO4. Reaction B: 1-pentyne + (1) disiamylborane (2) H2O2/NaOH. Which statement about the products is correct?","q_smiles":"CCCC#C","opts":["A gives pentanal; B gives 2-pentanone","Both A and B give pentanal","A gives 3-pentanone; B gives pentanal","Both A and B give 2-pentanone","A gives 2-pentanone (a methyl ketone); B gives pentanal (an aldehyde)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Reaction A (H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4) is Markovnikov hydration: the carbonyl ends up on the internal carbon, giving 2-pentanone, a methyl ketone. Reaction B (disiamylborane then H2O2/NaOH) is anti-Markovnikov hydration: the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving pentanal, an aldehyde. The choice that reverses them swaps the regiochemistry of the two reagents, 3-pentanone would require an internal alkyne, and the choices claiming both give the same product ignore the regiochemical contrast.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Two-concept (Markovnikov vs anti-Markovnikov contrast) within scope. q_smiles 1-pentyne valid. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (shown) is converted to a product whose IR shows a strong carbonyl absorption near 1715 cm-1 and whose 1H NMR shows a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm but NO aldehydic proton near 9-10 ppm. Which reagents produce this compound?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["H2, Lindlar catalyst","H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","1. Disiamylborane, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. BH3, 2. H2O2/NaOH","1. O3, 2. (CH3)2S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A strong carbonyl band near 1715 cm-1 plus a 3H singlet near 2.1 ppm and no aldehyde proton identify a methyl ketone, so the product is 1-cyclohexylethanone. That is the Markovnikov hydration product of a terminal alkyne, made with water, sulfuric acid, and mercuric sulfate: water adds to the internal alkyne carbon and the enol tautomerizes to the ketone. Either hydroboration-oxidation route gives anti-Markovnikov hydration and therefore an aldehyde, which would show a CHO signal near 9 to 10 ppm; ozonolysis cleaves the triple bond; hydrogen over Lindlar catalyst only reduces the alkyne to vinylcyclohexane.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Spectroscopy is qualitative region-recognition (carbonyl band, methyl singlet, absent CHO) used only to distinguish ketone vs aldehyde, not J-coupling/exact-shift math, so within DAT scope. q_smiles valid. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"1-Hexyne (drawn) is treated with HgSO4, dilute H2SO4, and H2O. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCC=O","CCCC(=O)CC","CCCC=C(C)O","CCCCCCO","CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hg(II)/H3O+ hydration of a terminal alkyne is Markovnikov: water adds to the more substituted (internal) carbon, giving an enol that tautomerizes to a methyl ketone (2-hexanone), not the aldehyde. Hexanal is the anti-Markovnikov product; 3-hexanone has wrong regiochemistry; the enol is the un-tautomerized intermediate; 1-hexanol is simple alkene-style hydration thinking.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: 1-hexyne + Hg/H2SO4 = Markovnikov, gives 2-hexanone (opt0). All 5 SMILES valid; distractors are anti-Markov aldehyde, wrong-regio ketone, enol intermediate, and alkene-hydration alcohol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Octyne (drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCC#C","opts":["CCCCCCCC=O","C=C(O)CCCCCC","CCCCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCCCCO","CCCCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal carbon, and after oxidation the enol tautomerizes to an aldehyde (octanal). 2-Octanone is the Markovnikov (Hg-catalyzed) product; 3-octanone is wrong regiochemistry; 1-octanol comes from over-reduction/alkene thinking; the enol is the intermediate before tautomerization.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: 1-octyne + BH3/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives octanal (opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov methyl ketone, wrong-regio ketone, alcohol, and enol intermediate. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","C=C(O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov (Hg/acid) hydration places the carbonyl on the more substituted carbon (the one bearing the ring), giving the methyl ketone acetophenone. Phenylacetaldehyde is the anti-Markovnikov product; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol is over-reduction; benzoic acid would require oxidative cleavage, which does not occur here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: aryl-conjugated terminal alkyne strongly favors Markovnikov, gives acetophenone (opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are anti-Markov aldehyde, enol, alcohol, and a no-reaction cleavage product. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (drawn) is treated with (1) disiamylborane (Sia2BH) then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron adds to the terminal CH carbon, and oxidation/tautomerization gives the aldehyde phenylacetaldehyde. Acetophenone is the Markovnikov product; 2-phenylethanol is alkene-hydration thinking; the enol is the intermediate; 1-phenylethanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: Sia2BH/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives phenylacetaldehyde (opt0). Sia2BH is a standard bulky borane for terminal-alkyne hydroboration (sophomore-level). All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov ketone, alcohol, enol, wrong-regio alcohol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"3-Hexyne (a symmetric internal alkyne, drawn) is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","opts":["CCC(O)=CCC","CCCCCC=O","CCC(=O)CCC","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A symmetric internal alkyne gives a single ketone regardless of which carbon is hydrated, because both regiochemical outcomes are identical: 3-hexanone. 2-Hexanone and hexanal cannot form (those would require an unsymmetrical or terminal alkyne); the enol is the intermediate; 3-pentanone has the wrong carbon count.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: symmetric internal alkyne gives single ketone 3-hexanone (opt0). Two-concept (recognize symmetry + apply hydration), correctly labeled difficulty 2. All SMILES valid; distractors are wrong-regio ketone, aldehyde, enol intermediate, and a C5 ketone (wrong count). Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"5-Methyl-1-hexyne (drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CCC#C","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(=O)CCC(C)C","CC(C)CCCCO","CC(C)CCCC=O","CC(C)CCC=CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Anti-Markovnikov hydroboration-oxidation puts the carbonyl on the terminal carbon, giving the aldehyde 5-methylhexanal; the branch at C5 is a spectator and is unchanged. The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov (Hg) product; the primary alcohol is alkene-style thinking; the shorter ketone has lost a carbon; the enol is the intermediate.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: terminal alkyne + BH3/H2O2 = anti-Markovnikov, gives 5-methylhexanal (opt0); isopropyl branch is a spectator. All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov methyl ketone, alcohol, a carbon-short ketone, and enol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Pentyne (drawn), an UNsymmetrical internal alkyne, is treated with H2O, HgSO4, and H2SO4. Which statement best describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"CC#CCC","opts":["A roughly equal mixture of two regioisomeric ketones (2-pentanone and 3-pentanone) forms, because neither alkyne carbon is strongly favored","A single aldehyde forms because Markovnikov addition to an internal alkyne gives the aldehyde","A carboxylic acid forms by oxidative cleavage of the triple bond","Only 2-pentanone forms, because Markovnikov addition always places the C=O on C2","No reaction occurs, because internal alkynes are unreactive toward Hg(II)/acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"For an unsymmetrical INTERNAL alkyne, the two carbons of the triple bond are similar in substitution, so Markovnikov selectivity is poor and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a mixture of both possible ketones. Internal alkynes give ketones, never aldehydes; the reaction does occur; and no C-C cleavage happens under these conditions.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: unsymmetrical internal alkyne gives a regioisomer mixture (2-pentanone + 3-pentanone) due to poor selectivity (opt0). Classic textbook conceptual point. Text options; substrate drawn in q_smiles (valid). Distractors (aldehyde from internal alkyne, overstated selectivity, no-reaction, cleavage) all clearly wrong. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane, drawn) is treated with (1) BH3 then (2) H2O2, NaOH. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["OC=CC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","O=CCC1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"This is a terminal alkyne (C#C-) bearing a cyclohexyl group; hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov, so the carbonyl ends up on the terminal carbon, giving cyclohexylacetaldehyde (a two-carbon aldehyde side chain). The methyl ketone is the Markovnikov product; cyclohexanecarbaldehyde (ring-CHO) has lost a carbon and the wrong skeleton; the alcohol is over-reduction; the enol is the intermediate.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-hydration","issue":"Verified: terminal alkyne, anti-Markovnikov gives cyclohexylacetaldehyde C8H14O (opt0), correct mass balance from C8H12 substrate + H2O. Strong skeleton distractor opt3 (cyclohexanecarbaldehyde C7H12O, carbon-short) is clearly wrong. All SMILES valid; distractors are Markov ketone, alcohol, ring-aldehyde, enol. Correct, unambiguous, in scope, visual."},{"q":"2-Hexyne is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with quinoline). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCC","CC#CCCC","C=CCCCC","C/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar delivers H2 syn across the triple bond and stops at the alkene, giving cis (Z) 2-hexene. Trans is the Na/NH3 product; unchanged alkyne, hexane (unpoisoned Pd over-reduction), and a regio-shifted terminal alkene are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed cis(Z) verified by RDKit as the correct Lindlar product; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Heptyne is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#CCCC","CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","CC#CCCCC"],"correct":3,"why":"Dissolving-metal (Na/NH3) adds H atoms anti via a radical-anion pathway, giving trans (E) 3-heptene. The cis isomer is the Lindlar product; unreacted alkyne, heptane (over-reduction), and a regio-shifted 2-heptyne are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. opt[4] (CC#CCCCC) is 2-heptyne, a valid same-formula regio distractor; keyed trans(E) verified correct."},{"q":"1-Butyne is treated with NaNH2, and the resulting acetylide is then treated with bromoethane (CH3CH2Br). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C#CCC","CC#CCCC","CCCC#CCCC","CCC#CCC","C#CCCCC"],"correct":3,"why":"NaNH2 deprotonates the terminal alkyne; the acetylide does SN2 on ethyl bromide, adding -CH2CH3 to the terminal carbon. CH3CH2-C#CH becomes CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH3 = 3-hexyne. Terminal 1-hexyne (wrong carbon), 2-hexyne, 4-octyne (over-alkylation), and unreacted alkyne are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] CCC#CCC = 3-hexyne, the correct alkylation product; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with NaNH2, and the resulting acetylide is then treated with iodomethane (CH3I). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC#CCCC","C#CCCC","CC#CCC","CCC#CCC","C#CCCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"The terminal acetylide of 1-pentyne (HC#C-CH2CH2CH3) does SN2 on CH3I, installing methyl at the terminal carbon to give CH3-C#C-CH2CH2CH3 = 2-hexyne. 1-Hexyne and 3-hexyne are wrong regiochemistry; 2-pentyne lost a carbon; unreacted 1-pentyne is no reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] CC#CCCC = 2-hexyne (C6H10), correct product; distractors valid and distinct."},{"q":"Phenylacetylene (ethynylbenzene) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","C#CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C#Cc1ccccc1","C1CCCCC1/C=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar reduces the C-C triple bond to an alkene only and leaves the aromatic ring intact, giving styrene (vinylbenzene). Ethylbenzene is the over-reduced product; unreacted alkyne is no reaction; chain-extended and ring-reduced options change the skeleton wrongly.","difficulty":1,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed opt[0] = styrene; opt[4] parses validly as vinylcyclohexane (ring-reduced distractor). All SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3I; (3) Na, NH3 (l). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","CC#CCCCC","C/C=C\\CCCC","C/C=C/CCCC"],"correct":4,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the terminal acetylide with methyl to give 2-heptyne (CH3-C#C-CH2CH2CH2CH3). Step 3 (Na/NH3) reduces it anti to trans (E) 2-heptene. Cis would be the Lindlar outcome; 2-heptyne is incomplete; trans-3-heptene has wrong regiochemistry; heptane is over-reduction.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (alkylation + anti reduction) within scope; keyed trans(E)-2-heptene verified correct."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","CCC#CCCC","CC/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the terminal acetylide with ethyl: HC#C-CH2CH2CH3 becomes CH3CH2-C#C-CH2CH2CH3 (3-heptyne). Step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z) 3-heptene. Trans-3-heptene is the Na/NH3 outcome; 3-heptyne is incomplete; cis-2-heptene has wrong regiochemistry; heptane is over-reduction.","difficulty":2,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (alkylation + syn reduction) within scope; keyed cis(Z)-3-heptene verified correct."},{"q":"Diphenylacetylene (1,2-diphenylethyne) is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1C#Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["c1ccccc1C#Cc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","c1ccccc1CCc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1/C=C/c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Na/NH3 reduces the internal alkyne by anti addition of two H atoms, stopping at the alkene to give trans (E) stilbene. Cis-stilbene is the Lindlar product; bibenzyl is over-reduction; unreacted alkyne is no reaction; 1,1-diphenylethene is a rearranged skeleton that cannot form by simple reduction.","difficulty":3,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed trans(E)-stilbene verified; all SMILES valid. Effectively single anti-reduction concept but acceptable as the harder item."},{"q":"The alkyne shown is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst (Pd poisoned with lead, quinoline). Which best describes the alkene product that forms?","opts":["cis-2-butene (the Z alkene)","An equal mix of cis- and trans-2-butene","trans-2-butene (the E alkene)","A racemic mixture of 2-butanol","Butane (full reduction to the alkane)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"2-butyne + H2/Lindlar gives syn addition that stops at the alkene, producing cis (Z)-2-butene. Lindlar does not over-reduce, and dissolving-metal would be needed for trans.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CC#CC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid, single concept (syn reduction stereochemistry), one defensible answer."},{"q":"The internal alkyne shown is converted into trans-3-hexene (the E alkene). Which reagent set accomplishes this transformation?","opts":["H2 with Lindlar's catalyst","Na metal in liquid NH3","BH3 then H2O2/NaOH","HBr (1 equivalent)","H2 with Pt catalyst (excess)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3) of 3-hexyne adds the two H atoms anti via a radical-anion pathway, giving the trans (E) alkene. Lindlar gives cis; excess H2/Pt gives the alkane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CCC#CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid, reagent-selection for anti reduction, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The terminal alkyne shown is treated first with NaNH2 and then with CH3CH2Br (bromoethane). What is the role of the NaNH2 in this sequence?","opts":["It reduces the triple bond to a cis double bond","It oxidizes the terminal carbon to a carboxylic acid","It deprotonates the terminal alkyne to form a nucleophilic acetylide ion","It acts as the electrophile that is attacked by the alkyne","It adds across the triple bond in a hydroamination"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"NaNH2 removes the acidic terminal acetylenic proton (pKa ~25) to form the acetylide nucleophile, which then does SN2 on the alkyl halide. NaNH2 is a base here, not a reductant, electrophile, or oxidant.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#CCCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (1-pentyne), role-identification, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The product shown was formed by partial reduction of 4-octyne. Based on the stereochemistry of the C=C bond drawn, which reagent was used?","opts":["Na in liquid NH3","H2, Pd/C","H2, Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3, Pb, quinoline)","Li in liquid NH3","Br2 in CCl4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A cis alkene from an internal alkyne requires syn addition with Lindlar; both Li/NH3 and Na/NH3 give the trans isomer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CCC/C=C\\CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES is valid Z-alkene matching the cis claim; reagent-from-stereochemistry, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Sodium amide (NaNH2) is added to each of two alkynes: phenylacetylene (terminal, shown) and 1-phenylpropyne (CH3-C#C-Ph, internal). Which alkyne is deprotonated by NaNH2, and why?","opts":["Only the terminal alkyne, because only it has an sp C-H bond that can be removed","Only the internal alkyne, because its proton is more acidic","Neither, because NaNH2 is too weak a base for alkynes","Both equally, because NaNH2 deprotonates any C-H bond","Only the internal alkyne, because conjugation lowers its pKa"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acetylide formation requires an acidic sp C-H on a terminal carbon. The internal alkyne has no acetylenic hydrogen, so only the terminal alkyne (phenylacetylene) is deprotonated.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#Cc1ccccc1","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (phenylacetylene); acidity/terminal-vs-internal concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Starting from the alkyne shown (2-pentyne), which single reagent or reagent set gives trans-2-pentene as the major product?","opts":["H2O, H2SO4, HgSO4","H2 (excess), Pd/C","HCl (1 equivalent)","Na in liquid NH3","H2, Lindlar's catalyst"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Trans alkenes from internal alkynes come from dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3), which adds H anti. Lindlar gives cis, excess H2/Pd gives pentane, and acid-catalyzed hydration gives a ketone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"CC#CCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid; distractors layer stereochemistry vs over-reduction vs hydration, one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne (shown) is treated with NaNH2, then with 1-bromopropane, and finally with Na in liquid NH3. Which statement correctly describes the final product?","opts":["1-nonyne, a terminal alkyne","cis-4-nonene, an internal Z alkene","4-nonyne, an internal alkyne (no reduction occurs)","trans-2-nonene, an internal E alkene","trans-4-nonene, an internal E alkene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acetylide alkylation of 1-hexyne with 1-bromopropane gives 4-nonyne; Na/NH3 then reduces that internal alkyne by anti addition to trans-4-nonene. Numbering: propyl(C3)-C#C-butyl(C4) places the bond at C4.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid; two-step alkylation+anti-reduction is at the upper edge of DAT depth but standard textbook sequence, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Sodium acetylide (shown) is reacted with an alkyl halide in an SN2 alkylation. Which halide gives the highest yield of the alkylated alkyne?","opts":["bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","2-bromopropane (a secondary alkyl halide)","bromoethene (vinyl bromide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acetylides are strong bases and good nucleophiles, so the SN2 alkylation works well only on unhindered primary halides. Tertiary/secondary halides favor E2 elimination; aryl and vinyl halides do not undergo SN2, so 1-bromobutane gives the best yield.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"q_smiles":"[Na+].[C-]#C","group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"Correct as keyed. SMILES valid (sodium acetylide); SN2 substrate-selection concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Hexyne is treated with Na in liquid NH3. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC#CCCC","opts":["CCCCCC","C/C=C/CCC","C/C=C\\CCC","CCCC(C)C","C=CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Dissolving-metal reduction (Na/NH3) adds the two H atoms anti across the triple bond, giving the trans (E) alkene, trans-2-hexene. Lindlar would give cis; hexane needs H2/Pd; terminal/branched skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C6H10 alkyne); keyed [0] is the correct E/trans alkene; exactly one right answer."},{"q":"3-Heptyne is hydrogenated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst (Pd/CaCO3 poisoned with Pb). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CCCC","opts":["CC=CCCCC","CC/C=C\\CCC","CCCCCCC","C=CCCCCC","CC/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Lindlar's poisoned catalyst delivers H2 syn to the same face, stopping at the cis (Z) alkene, cis-3-heptene. The trans isomer is the Na/NH3 product; heptane is over-reduced; the position-shifted/terminal skeletons are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C7H12 alkyne); keyed [0] is the correct Z/cis alkene; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Propyne is treated first with NaNH2, then with 1-bromobutane. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCCC","CC#CCCCCC","CC#CC(C)CC","CC#CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"NaNH2 forms the acetylide, which does SN2 on 1-bromobutane (n-butyl, 4 C). Propyne (3 C) + butyl (4 C) gives 2-heptyne, C7H12. [1] is 1-heptyne (lost methyl), [2] is 2-hexyne (C6), [3] is 2-octyne (C8), [4] is branched.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Chain count correct (C3+C4=C7); keyed [0] right; distractors are valid wrong-length/regio isomers; one answer."},{"q":"1-Pentyne is treated with NaNH2 followed by bromoethane (CH3CH2Br). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCC","opts":["CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCCC","CCC#CCC","CC#CCCC","CCC#CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The terminal acetylide of 1-pentyne (C5) does SN2 on bromoethane (C2), installing ethyl at the alkyne carbon to give 3-heptyne, C7H12. [1] 1-heptyne and [2] 2-heptyne are C7 regio-distractors; [3]/[4] are C6.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Keyed [0] is the correct regiochemistry; [1] and [2] are constitutional-isomer distractors; exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Octyne is reduced with Na dissolved in liquid NH3. Which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC#CCCC","opts":["CC=CCCCCC","C=CCCCCCC","CCCCCCCC","CCC/C=C\\CCC","CCC/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Na/NH3 reduces via anti addition to give the trans (E) alkene, trans-4-octene. The cis isomer is the Lindlar product; octane is fully reduced; the terminal/position-shifted alkenes are wrong.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. q_smiles valid (C8H14); keyed [0] is the correct E/trans alkene; one right answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexylacetylene (ethynylcyclohexane) is treated with NaNH2, then with iodomethane (CH3I). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C#CC1CCCCC1","opts":["CC=CC1CCCCC1","C#CCC1CCCCC1","CC#CC1CCCCC1","CCC#CC1CCCCC1","C#CC1CCCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NaNH2 deprotonates only the terminal sp C-H (most acidic); the acetylide does SN2 on CH3I, capping the alkyne with methyl to give 1-cyclohexylpropyne. Deprotonation never occurs on ring CH2, so the ring-methylated [2] and chain-inserted [1] distractors are wrong.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-concept (chemoselective deprotonation + alkylation) within DAT depth; keyed [0] correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-1-propyne (Ph-C#C-CH3) is treated with H2 and Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC=CC1CCCCC1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar does syn addition and stops at the alkene, giving cis (Z)-1-phenyl-1-propene; the aromatic ring is untouched under mild conditions. Trans is the Na/NH3 product; propylbenzene is over-reduced; allylbenzene has wrong regiochemistry; [4] wrongly reduced the ring to cyclohexyl.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-concept (syn/cis + ring-stays-aromatic); keyed [0] correct Z isomer; one right answer."},{"q":"1-Hexyne is subjected to the following sequence: (1) NaNH2; (2) CH3CH2Br; (3) H2, Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the final major product?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["CC/C=C\\CCCC","CC/C=C/CCCC","CCCCCCCC","C/C=C\\CCCCC","CCC#CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Steps 1-2 alkylate the C6 acetylide with ethyl (C2) to give 3-octyne; step 3 (Lindlar) does syn reduction to cis (Z)-3-octene. Trans needs Na/NH3; octane is over-reduced; [3] is the alkyne (skipped Lindlar); [4] is cis-2-octene (wrong position).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alkyne-reduction-alkylation","issue":"None. Two-operation sequence (alkylation then Lindlar), within recognition depth; keyed [0] correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(O)O","CCCC=O","CCC(=O)C","CCCCO","CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous chromium(VI) reagent, so it oxidizes a primary alcohol only as far as the aldehyde and stops: 1-butanol gives butanal. Butanoic acid would require an aqueous oxidant such as Jones reagent, because over-oxidation goes through the aldehyde hydrate and PCC has no water to form it. Butan-2-one would need a secondary alcohol, the gem-diol is not an isolable product, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical PCC primary-alcohol question, keyed answer correct, all SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Pentan-2-ol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, acetone). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(O)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(O)C","CCCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)C","CCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Pentan-2-ol is a secondary alcohol, so any chromium(VI) oxidant, including Jones reagent, converts it to the ketone pentan-2-one, and no over-oxidation is possible because the ketone carbon has no hydrogen left to remove. Pentanal and pentanoic acid would require a primary alcohol, pentan-3-one puts the carbonyl on the wrong carbon, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct ketone product, good regiochemistry distractor (pentan-3-one), all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Hexanol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, aqueous H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)C","CCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCCC(O)O","CCCCCCO","CCCCCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Jones reagent is aqueous chromium(VI), so it oxidizes a primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid: 1-hexanol gives hexanoic acid. The intermediate hexanal forms a hydrate in water, and that hydrate is oxidized a second time. Hexanal is where PCC would stop, hexan-2-one would require a secondary alcohol, the gem-diol is only a transient intermediate, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correctly contrasts aqueous-Jones (acid) vs PCC (aldehyde); keyed answer correct, all SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1O","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyclohexanol is a secondary alcohol, so PCC oxidizes it to the ketone cyclohexanone; the result would be the same with Jones reagent, because a ketone cannot be oxidized further without breaking a carbon-carbon bond. The unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant, the hydroxy ketone has an extra oxygen with no source, cyclohexanecarboxylic acid has an extra carbon, and cyclopentanone has lost a ring carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct cyclohexanone; cyclopentanone skeleton distractor is plausible-but-wrong; all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylpropan-2-ol (tert-butanol) is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C=O","CC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)=C","CC(C)(O)CO","CC(C)(C)O"],"correct":4,"why":"tert-Butanol is a tertiary alcohol: the carbon bearing the OH has three carbons attached and no hydrogen, and chromium(VI) oxidation must remove a hydrogen from that carbon. So Jones reagent leaves it untouched and the starting alcohol is recovered. 2-Methylpropanal and pivalic acid would require rearranging or adding carbon, isobutylene would be an acid dehydration rather than an oxidation, and the diol adds an oxygen with no source.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic 3deg no-oxidation teaching point; recovered SM is the single defensible answer; all SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","O=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Benzyl alcohol is a primary (benzylic) alcohol, and PCC is the mild anhydrous oxidant that stops at the aldehyde, so the product is benzaldehyde. Benzoic acid would need an aqueous oxidant such as Jones reagent or potassium permanganate. The unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant, acetophenone has an extra carbon, and phenol is not an oxidation product of a CH2OH group.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct benzaldehyde; layered concept (benzylic + PCC stops at aldehyde); all SMILES valid, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"(R)-1-Phenylethanol is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"(R)-1-Phenylethanol is a secondary alcohol, so Jones reagent oxidizes it to the ketone acetophenone. The carbinol carbon goes from sp3 to a flat sp2 carbonyl, so the only stereocenter is destroyed and the product is achiral; there is no (R) or (S) left to track. The unchanged (R) alcohol ignores the oxidant, benzaldehyde and benzoic acid would require cleaving off the methyl group, and ethylbenzene is a reduction, not an oxidation.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-concept (oxidation + loss of stereocenter); acetophenone correct; all SMILES valid including chiral SM; single answer, in scope."},{"q":"Pent-4-en-1-ol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCCCC=O","C=CCCC=O","C1CCCO1","C=CCCCO","C=CCCC(=O)O"],"correct":1,"why":"PCC is chemoselective: it oxidizes the primary alcohol of pent-4-en-1-ol to the aldehyde, giving pent-4-enal, and leaves the isolated carbon-carbon double bond untouched. Because PCC is anhydrous, it does not over-oxidize to pent-4-enoic acid, which is the Jones outcome. The dialdehyde would require oxidizing the alkene end as well, the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant, and tetrahydrofuran is unrelated to this reaction.","difficulty":3,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hard item layering chemoselectivity + no-over-oxidation; pent-4-enal correct; all SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"The alcohol shown (1-butanol) is treated with a reagent and the only organic product isolated is butanal, CH3CH2CH2CHO (the aldehyde, with NO over-oxidation to the carboxylic acid). Which reagent was used?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["KMnO4, hot, basic then acidic workup","H2CrO4 (chromic acid) in aqueous acetone","PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2","Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, H2O)","Na2Cr2O7, H2SO4, H2O"],"correct":2,"why":"A primary alcohol stops cleanly at the aldehyde only with a mild, anhydrous chromium(VI) oxidant, and that is PCC in dichloromethane: 1-butanol gives butanal with no over-oxidation. Hot basic permanganate, chromic acid in aqueous acetone, Jones reagent, and acidic dichromate are all strong aqueous oxidants; in water the aldehyde forms a hydrate that is oxidized again, carrying the compound on to butanoic acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol (shown) is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, H2O). What is the major organic product, and how is this transformation best classified?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexanone; oxidation of a 2 degree alcohol to a ketone","Cyclohexanone; a reduction of the C-O bond","No reaction; 2 degree alcohols are inert to Jones reagent","Cyclohexene; an E1 dehydration","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid; oxidation of a 2 degree alcohol to an acid"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyclohexanol is a secondary alcohol, so Jones reagent oxidizes it to the ketone cyclohexanone, and the transformation is classified as oxidation of a secondary alcohol to a ketone. The ketone carbon has no remaining hydrogen, so it cannot be oxidized further to an acid. Secondary alcohols are not inert to Jones reagent, chromium(VI) is an oxidant rather than a reducing agent, and dehydration to cyclohexene is an acid-and-heat reaction, not what an oxidant does.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol (shown) is stirred with excess Na2Cr2O7 / H2SO4 for several hours at room temperature. What is the outcome?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Oxidation to the corresponding ketone","Cleavage of the C-C bond to give two ketones","Oxidation to an aldehyde that resists further oxidation","Oxidation to the corresponding carboxylic acid","No reaction; the carbinol carbon is tertiary and bears no C-H to remove"],"correct":4,"why":"In 2-methyl-2-butanol the carbon bearing the OH carries an ethyl group and two methyls and no hydrogen; it is a tertiary alcohol. Chromium(VI) oxidation works by removing a hydrogen from that carbinol carbon, so with no hydrogen there the dichromate does nothing and the alcohol is recovered. A ketone, an aldehyde, or a carboxylic acid would all require breaking a carbon-carbon bond, which this reagent does not do.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Diagnostic tertiary = no reaction; structure matches name."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol (shown) must be converted to BENZALDEHYDE (PhCHO) and the aldehyde must NOT be further oxidized to benzoic acid. Which reagent best accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hot HNO3","K2Cr2O7, H2SO4, reflux","KMnO4, H2O, heat","PCC, CH2Cl2","CrO3, H2SO4, H2O (Jones)"],"correct":3,"why":"Benzyl alcohol is a primary alcohol, and the only listed reagent that oxidizes a primary alcohol to the aldehyde and stops is PCC in dichloromethane; it is mild and anhydrous, so the aldehyde cannot form the hydrate that leads on to the acid. Jones reagent, hot acidic dichromate, aqueous permanganate, and hot nitric acid are all strong aqueous oxidants that carry benzyl alcohol through benzaldehyde to benzoic acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"2-Phenylethanol (PhCH2CH2OH, shown) is treated with Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, aqueous acetone). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"OCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["No reaction; chromic acid does not oxidize primary alcohols","Benzoic acid, PhCOOH, from cleavage at the benzylic carbon","Acetophenone, PhCOCH3, a ketone","Phenylacetic acid, PhCH2COOH","Phenylacetaldehyde, PhCH2CHO; oxidation stops at the aldehyde"],"correct":3,"why":"The OH sits on a primary carbon, and Jones reagent is a strong aqueous chromium(VI) oxidant, so the primary alcohol is oxidized through the aldehyde all the way to the carboxylic acid, phenylacetic acid. In water the aldehyde forms a hydrate that is oxidized again, so the reaction does not stop at phenylacetaldehyde (PCC in dry CH2Cl2 would). Acetophenone would need the OH on the benzylic carbon, and Jones conditions do not cleave the side chain down to benzoic acid; that is hot KMnO4 chemistry.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Rewritten 2026-09-30: with hot KMnO4, benzylic cleavage to benzoic acid was also defensible; reagent changed to Jones."},{"q":"Consider these two oxidations of 4-methyl-2-pentanol (shown): Reaction A uses PCC/CH2Cl2 and Reaction B uses Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4). Compare the products.","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A gives an aldehyde and B gives a carboxylic acid","Both A and B give the same product, 4-methyl-2-pentanone (a ketone)","A gives no reaction and B gives a carboxylic acid","A gives the ketone and B cleaves the molecule into two fragments","A gives the ketone and B gives no reaction"],"correct":1,"why":"4-Methyl-2-pentanol is a secondary alcohol, and a secondary alcohol gives the same product with a mild oxidant and with a strong one: both PCC and Jones reagent produce the ketone 4-methyl-2-pentanone. The aldehyde-versus-acid distinction between PCC and Jones applies only to primary alcohols, and over-oxidation of a ketone is impossible because the carbonyl carbon has no hydrogen left. Neither reagent cleaves the carbon skeleton or leaves a secondary alcohol unreacted.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"You have three separate alcohols: 3-methyl-1-butanol (a primary alcohol, shown), cyclohexanol (secondary), and 2-methyl-2-propanol (tertiary). Each is treated with CrO3 / H2SO4 (Jones). Which statement about which substrates REACT is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The 1 degree and 2 degree alcohols react (to an acid and a ketone); the 3 degree alcohol does not react","All three react to give carboxylic acids","Only the 1 degree alcohol reacts; 2 degree and 3 degree are inert","Only the 3 degree alcohol reacts; 1 degree and 2 degree are inert to Cr(VI)","The 1 degree gives an aldehyde, the 2 degree gives no reaction, the 3 degree gives a ketone"],"correct":0,"why":"With Jones reagent the outcome depends on how many hydrogens the carbinol carbon has. The primary alcohol 3-methyl-1-butanol is oxidized all the way to the carboxylic acid, the secondary alcohol cyclohexanol is oxidized to the ketone cyclohexanone, and the tertiary alcohol 2-methyl-2-propanol has no hydrogen on its carbinol carbon, so it does not react. The correct statement is therefore that the primary and secondary alcohols react (to an acid and a ketone) while the tertiary alcohol is untouched.","difficulty":2,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem wording was convoluted (ranking framing that the options don't actually rank); rewritten to the clean 'which react' question. Chemistry and keyed answer unchanged and correct."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC in dry CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCCC","CCCCO","CCCC=O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous chromium(VI) oxidant that converts a primary alcohol to the aldehyde and stops, so 1-butanol gives butanal. Butanoic acid is the over-oxidation product from Jones reagent or permanganate in water, butan-2-one would require a secondary alcohol, butane would be a reduction, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. SMILES all valid (RDKit). Keyed opt0 butanal is the correct PCC product. Clean single-concept, properly visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexylmethanol is treated with the Jones reagent (CrO3, H2SO4, acetone). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCC1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cyclohexylmethanol is a primary alcohol, and Jones reagent is aqueous chromium(VI), so the CH2OH group is oxidized through the aldehyde hydrate all the way to the carboxylic acid: the product is cyclohexanecarboxylic acid. Cyclohexanecarbaldehyde is where PCC would stop, cyclohexanone would require the OH to be on the ring (it is not), methylenecyclohexane would be a dehydration, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed acid is correct Jones product on a primary alcohol. Good distractors."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol is stirred with CrO3 in aqueous H2SO4 at room temperature. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts":["CCC(C)=O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"2-Methyl-2-butanol is a tertiary alcohol: the carbon bearing the OH has no hydrogen, and chromium(VI) oxidation requires removing a hydrogen from that carbon. So aqueous chromium trioxide at room temperature leaves the alcohol unchanged and it is recovered. The ketone, aldehyde, and carboxylic acid would all require carbon-carbon bond cleavage, and the alkene 2-methyl-2-butene would be a dehydration that needs heat with strong acid, not an oxidation at room temperature.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"Minor: CrO3/H2SO4 is acidic and can dehydrate a 3 alcohol on heating, but stem specifies room temperature so the standard no-reaction answer is the single defensible one. Valid."},{"q":"Menthol (a secondary cyclohexanol bearing a methyl and an isopropyl group, three stereocenters) is treated with PCC. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","opts":["OC(=O)C1CC(C)CCC1C(C)C","C=C1CC(C)CCC1C(C)C","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(O)C1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(=O)O1","CC1CCC(C(C)C)C(=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Menthol is a secondary alcohol, so PCC oxidizes it to the ketone menthone (2-isopropyl-5-methylcyclohexanone). The carbinol stereocenter becomes a flat sp2 carbonyl carbon and is lost, while the two other ring stereocenters, the carbons bearing the methyl and the isopropyl, are untouched. A secondary alcohol cannot become a carboxylic acid, PCC does not dehydrate to an exocyclic alkene, the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant, and the lactone has an oxygen inserted into the ring with no reaction that could do that.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None substantive. q_smiles drawn without stereochemistry, but the oxidation answer is diastereomer-independent and the stereocenter-loss claim holds. RDKit confirms substrate C10H20O (3 centers) -> menthone C10H18O (2 centers). Valid two-concept item."},{"q":"trans-4-tert-Butylcyclohexan-1-ol (the OH and the tert-butyl group on opposite faces of the ring) is oxidized with Na2Cr2O7/H2SO4. Which statement best describes the product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","opts":["The cis alcohol forms by epimerization before oxidation can occur.","The product is a carboxylic acid because Cr(VI) always over-oxidizes a cyclohexanol.","The product retains the trans relationship between the carbonyl and the tert-butyl group.","The product is 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone, which is achiral and has no cis/trans relationship at C1 because that carbon is now sp2.","No reaction occurs because the alcohol is on a ring carbon."],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Oxidation of the secondary ring alcohol gives 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone; the former carbinol carbon becomes planar sp2, so the cis/trans descriptor at C1 disappears and the ketone is achiral. A 2 alcohol gives a ketone not an acid, no retained cis/trans at a carbonyl, no epimerization step, ring 2 alcohols react normally.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. Text options, molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Exactly one defensible answer (opt0); other four contain clear chemical errors."},{"q":"A student needs to convert benzyl alcohol (PhCH2OH) selectively to benzaldehyde without forming benzoic acid. Which reagent is the correct choice?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts":["Jones reagent (CrO3, aqueous H2SO4)","PCC in anhydrous CH2Cl2","CrO3 in aqueous H2SO4 with excess oxidant","Hot KMnO4 in aqueous base, then acid workup","Na2Cr2O7 in dilute aqueous H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Stopping a primary/benzylic alcohol at the aldehyde requires a mild anhydrous Cr(VI) reagent, PCC. The other four are aqueous/strong oxidants that drive the alcohol to benzoic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. Reagent-selection item, substrate drawn (valid SMILES). Only PCC stops at the aldehyde; single defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Penten-1-ol (which contains both a primary alcohol and a terminal C=C) is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCCO","opts":["CCCCC=O","C=CCCC(=O)O","C=CCCC=O","OCCCCCO","O=CC1CCCO1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC is chemoselective: it oxidizes the primary alcohol to the aldehyde and ignores an isolated carbon-carbon double bond, so 4-penten-1-ol gives 4-pentenal. Because PCC is anhydrous, it does not over-oxidize to 4-pentenoic acid. Pentanal would require reducing the alkene, the 1,5-diol would require adding an OH with no source, and the cyclic aldehyde would require a ring-forming step that PCC does not perform.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; keyed 4-pentenal is correct. Tests chemoselectivity (oxidation + alkene tolerance), two-concept, properly visual."},{"q":"(R)-2-methyl-1-butanol (the stereocenter is the carbon adjacent to the CH2OH) is oxidized with the Jones reagent. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)CO","opts":["CC(C)CC(=O)O","CC[C@@H](C)C(=O)O","CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O","CCC(C)CO","CC[C@@H](C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Jones reagent oxidizes the primary CH2OH of (R)-2-methyl-1-butanol all the way to the carboxylic acid. No bond to the stereocenter is touched, so the spatial arrangement at that carbon is retained. The group that changes (CH2OH becoming COOH) stays the top CIP priority because it still carries the oxygen, with ethyl second and methyl third, so the descriptor is also unchanged: the product is (R)-2-methylbutanoic acid. The (S) acid would require an inversion that never happens in an oxidation, the aldehyde is where PCC would stop, 3-methylbutanoic acid has the methyl on the wrong carbon, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the oxidant.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-oxidation","issue":"Verified the subtle point: RDKit confirms substrate is R and keyed opt0 is R; priority order (COOH>Et>Me>H) parallels the alcohol (CH2OH>Et>Me>H), so retention keeps the R label. Distractor opt1 (S) is the realistic trap. Hard but in DAT recognition scope. Valid."},{"q":"What is the major product when 1-butanol is treated with PBr3?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCCl","CCCCBr","CCC(C)Br","CCCCO","CCC=C"],"correct":1,"why":"PBr3 converts a primary alcohol to the primary alkyl bromide: bromide displaces the activated oxygen at the same carbon, so 1-butanol gives 1-bromobutane with no rearrangement. 2-Bromobutane would require the bromine to move to the second carbon, which a direct displacement does not do; 1-chlorobutane has the wrong halogen; 1-butene would be an elimination; the unchanged alcohol ignores the reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; all SMILES parse in RDKit."},{"q":"(R)-2-butanol (drawn) is treated with PBr3. What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=CC","CC[C@H](C)Br","CC[C@@H](C)Br","CC[C@H](C)Cl","CCC(C)Br"],"correct":1,"why":"PBr3 converts the alcohol oxygen into a good leaving group (a dibromophosphite), and bromide then attacks from the back side, so the carbon is inverted. In (R)-2-butanol the OH is the top CIP priority; in the product the Br is also the top priority, so the geometric inversion shows up as a flip of the descriptor: the product is (S)-2-bromobutane. The (R) bromide would mean retention, which this mechanism does not give; the bromide drawn without stereochemistry ignores the fact that the product is a single enantiomer; the chloride has the wrong halogen; 2-butene would be an elimination.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Verified substrate R, product S via RDKit CIP; correct=0."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)CO","C=C(C)CC","C/C=C/C","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC=C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Heating a tertiary alcohol with concentrated sulfuric acid is an E1 dehydration: protonate the OH, lose water to form the tertiary carbocation, then lose a proton to give the more substituted (Zaitsev) alkene. From 2-methyl-2-butanol that is 2-methyl-2-butene, a trisubstituted alkene with three carbons on the double bond. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the disubstituted Hofmann alkene and is the minor product, 2-butene has lost a carbon, the tertiary chloride needs a chloride source, and 2-methyl-1-butanol is a different alcohol, not a dehydration product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Cosmetic: rationale calls 2-methyl-2-butene tetrasubstituted; it is trisubstituted (RDKit-confirmed). Answer/index unaffected, keep."},{"q":"What is the major product when 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C"],"correct":2,"why":"Protonation and loss of water from 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol gives a secondary carbocation sitting next to a quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift converts it to a more stable tertiary carbocation, and Zaitsev elimination then gives 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene, a tetrasubstituted alkene in which all four groups on the double bond are carbons. 3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is the unrearranged, less substituted alkene and is minor; the chloride needs a chloride source; the unchanged alcohol ignores the reagent; the remaining alkene has an extra carbon that the starting material never had.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Rearrangement to tertiary cation correct; correct=0; tetrasubstituted product RDKit-confirmed."},{"q":"What is the major product when cyclohexanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","ClC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot concentrated sulfuric acid dehydrates cyclohexanol by E1: protonation of the OH, loss of water to the secondary cyclohexyl carbocation, and loss of a neighboring proton. Because every ring carbon is equivalent, only one alkene is possible: cyclohexene. There is no chloride source, so chlorocyclohexane cannot form; sulfuric acid does not oxidize the alcohol to cyclohexanone or reduce it to cyclohexane; the unchanged alcohol ignores the reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"What is the product when 1-propanol is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine?","q_smiles":"CCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCOS(=O)(=O)C","CCC=O","CCCCl","CCCOS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1","CCCOC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Tosyl chloride with pyridine converts an alcohol to its tosylate: the oxygen of 1-propanol attacks sulfur, pyridine absorbs the HCl, and the carbon-oxygen bond is never broken, so the carbon skeleton is intact and nothing is inverted. The product is propyl tosylate (propyl p-toluenesulfonate). The mesylate would come from methanesulfonyl chloride, the acetate from acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride, 1-chloropropane from thionyl chloride or HCl, and propanal from an oxidant.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; tosylate SMILES parses."},{"q":"What is the major product when 1-methylcyclohexanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"OC1(C)CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","ClC1(C)CCCCC1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Heating 1-methylcyclohexanol with concentrated sulfuric acid is an E1 dehydration through the tertiary carbocation, and the Zaitsev rule picks the more substituted alkene. Losing a ring proton gives 1-methylcyclohexene, a trisubstituted alkene, which beats methylenecyclohexane, the disubstituted (Hofmann) alkene formed by losing a methyl proton. The tertiary chloride needs a chloride source, methylcyclohexane would be a reduction, and the unchanged alcohol ignores the reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; substitution degree RDKit-checked."},{"q":"What is the major product when 2-methylcyclopentanol is heated with concentrated H2SO4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC1O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCC1","CC1=CCCC1","CC1CCC=C1","CC1CCCC1O","CC1CCCC1Cl"],"correct":1,"why":"Heating 2-methylcyclopentanol with concentrated sulfuric acid is an E1 dehydration. Loss of water gives a carbocation, and losing the proton on the methyl-bearing ring carbon gives the trisubstituted alkene 1-methylcyclopentene, the Zaitsev product. 3-Methylcyclopentene, formed by eliminating away from the methyl, is only disubstituted; methylenecyclopentane would require the methyl to sit on the carbocation carbon; the chloride needs a chloride source; the unchanged alcohol ignores the reagent.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"No issue. Correct=0 confirmed; all SMILES parse."},{"q":"The tertiary alcohol shown (tert-butanol) is to be converted into tert-butyl chloride. Which reagent accomplishes this conversion most readily, taking advantage of the fact that this substrate forms a stable carbocation?","opts":["NaCl in water","PBr3","SOCl2 with pyridine","TsCl with triethylamine","Concentrated HCl (no catalyst needed)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Tertiary alcohols react with concentrated HX directly through an SN1 mechanism because the tertiary carbocation is stable; tert-butanol + conc. HCl gives tert-butyl chloride. SOCl2/PBr3 are for 1deg/2deg alcohols, and TsCl gives a tosylate, not a chloride. NaCl in water provides no leaving-group activation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)O","issue":"Correct. 3deg alcohol + conc. HCl -> SN1 chloride; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches tert-butanol; one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"1-Butanol (shown) is treated with PBr3. What is the role of PBr3 and the type of product formed?","opts":["It converts the alcohol to a tosylate","It converts the alcohol to the alkyl bromide (1-bromobutane)","It converts the alcohol to the alkyl chloride","It oxidizes the alcohol to butanoic acid","It dehydrates the alcohol to 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"PBr3 converts 1deg and 2deg alcohols into the corresponding alkyl bromides via an SN2-type pathway (no rearrangement). 1-Butanol gives 1-bromobutane; it does not dehydrate, oxidize, tosylate, or chlorinate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCCCO","issue":"Correct. PBr3 -> alkyl bromide on 1deg alcohol; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 1-butanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"To convert the secondary alcohol shown (3-methyl-2-butanol) to 2-chloro-3-methylbutane WITHOUT carbocation rearrangement, which reagent is the best choice?","opts":["Concentrated HCl (SN1 conditions)","Aqueous HCl, room temperature","SOCl2 with pyridine","Concentrated H2SO4 with heat","NaCl with heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Concentrated HCl turns the secondary alcohol into a secondary carbocation, which undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable tertiary cation and gives mostly 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. SOCl2 with pyridine converts the OH into a chlorosulfite leaving group that chloride displaces by backside (SN2) attack, so no free carbocation forms and the Cl lands on the original carbon. Concentrated H2SO4 with heat dehydrates the alcohol to alkenes, and NaCl cannot displace hydroxide, which is a poor leaving group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)C","issue":"Rewritten 2026-09-30: neopentyl alcohol does not undergo clean SN2 with SOCl2 (too hindered); substrate changed to 3-methyl-2-butanol."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butanol (shown) is heated with concentrated H2SO4. By Zaitsev's rule, what is the major product and what reaction type is occurring?","opts":["E1 dehydration giving 2-methyl-1-butene (less substituted alkene)","SN1 substitution giving 2-chloro-2-methylbutane","E1 dehydration giving 2-methyl-2-butene (more substituted alkene)","Oxidation giving a ketone","SN2 substitution giving an ether"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed dehydration of a tertiary alcohol is E1; Zaitsev predicts the more substituted (trisubstituted) 2-methyl-2-butene over the disubstituted 2-methyl-1-butene. H2SO4 provides no chloride, so no alkyl chloride forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","issue":"Correct. E1 Zaitsev -> 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted, verified); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 2-methyl-2-butanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanol (shown) is heated with concentrated phosphoric acid. Classify the overall transformation that produces cyclohexene.","opts":["Free-radical halogenation","Acid-catalyzed dehydration (loss of water, beta-elimination)","Esterification","Nucleophilic substitution (SN2)","Oxidation to a ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Heating cyclohexanol with strong acid protonates the OH, water leaves, and a beta hydrogen is lost to form the alkene. This is acid-catalyzed dehydration, a beta-elimination producing cyclohexene plus water.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","issue":"Correct. Classify as acid-catalyzed dehydration / beta-elimination; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches cyclohexanol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-Butanol (shown) is treated with SOCl2 and pyridine. Considering the stereochemical outcome typical of this reagent combination, what happens at the stereocenter?","opts":["Complete racemization to a 50:50 mixture","Migration of the stereocenter to an adjacent carbon","Retention of configuration with no change in connectivity","Loss of the stereocenter through elimination","Inversion of configuration at the carbon bearing the leaving group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"SOCl2 with pyridine forms a chlorosulfite that undergoes SN2-like backside attack by chloride, giving inversion. (Without pyridine the SNi pathway gives retention; the stem specifies pyridine, so inversion is the defensible answer.)","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CCC(C)O","issue":"Correct under stated with-pyridine conditions (standard Wade/Klein teaching: pyridine -> inversion vs SNi retention); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 2-butanol skeleton (note: drawing does not encode the (S) label, but answer is conceptual/text so still works); two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl alcohol (shown) is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine. What is the purpose of forming the tosylate product?","opts":["To convert the poor OH leaving group into an excellent leaving group for later substitution/elimination","To reduce the alcohol to an alkane","To dehydrate the alcohol to an alkene","To oxidize the alcohol to an aldehyde","To protect the ring from electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Tosylate formation (ROH + TsCl/pyridine) replaces OH with OTs while retaining C-O configuration. The tosylate is an excellent leaving group, priming the molecule for SN2/SN1/E2 reactions that hydroxide cannot undergo.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","issue":"Correct. Tosylate purpose = make OH a good leaving group; key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches benzyl alcohol; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol (shown), a secondary alcohol, is heated with concentrated H2SO4. The major product is 2-methyl-2-butene rather than 3-methyl-1-butene. Which explanation best accounts for this?","opts":["The methyl group blocks elimination toward the more substituted side","Zaitsev's rule does not apply to secondary alcohols","A 1,2-hydride shift converts the secondary carbocation to a more stable tertiary carbocation, which then loses a beta H to give the Zaitsev (more substituted) alkene","An anti-periplanar requirement forces formation of the terminal alkene","The reaction goes by SN2, so no rearrangement is possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"E1 dehydration gives a secondary carbocation at C2; a 1,2-hydride shift from the adjacent CH yields a more stable tertiary cation, and loss of a beta hydrogen gives the most substituted (Zaitsev) alkene, 2-methyl-2-butene. This is the hallmark of carbocation rearrangement.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","issue":"Correct. Hydride-shift rearrangement 2deg->3deg cation then Zaitsev -> 2-methyl-2-butene (verified); key index 0 valid; q_smiles matches 3-methyl-2-butanol; recognition-level explanation (not a full drawn mechanism), in scope."},{"q":"The alcohol shown below is treated with thionyl chloride (SOCl2) in the presence of pyridine. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)CC","opts":["C[C@@H](Cl)CC","CC(O)CCl","C[C@H](Br)CC","CC=CC","C[C@H](Cl)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The substrate is (R)-2-butanol. Thionyl chloride with pyridine converts the OH into a chlorosulfite leaving group, and chloride then displaces it from the back side in an SN2-type step, so the stereocenter is inverted. Because OH in the starting material and Cl in the product are each the top CIP priority, geometric inversion flips the descriptor: the product is (S)-2-chlorobutane. The (R) chloride is the retention trap, the bromide has the wrong halogen, the chlorohydrin keeps the OH and misplaces the chlorine, and 2-butene would be an elimination.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: (R)->(S) inversion, opt0 correct, one defensible answer, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol (shown) is heated with concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC1(O)CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CCC1=CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The tertiary alcohol is protonated and loses water to give a tertiary carbocation at C1. Loss of a ring hydrogen from the neighboring CH2 gives 1-methylcyclohexene, the trisubstituted alkene, which is the Zaitsev (major) product. Methylenecyclohexane, with the exocyclic C=CH2, is only disubstituted and minor. 3-Methylcyclohexene puts the double bond away from the cation carbon, 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane would need a chloride source that H2SO4 does not supply, and ethylcyclopentene is a ring-contracted skeleton this tertiary cation has no reason to form.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Rewritten 2026-09-30: old explanation pointed at the wrong option and called the product tetrasubstituted; 1-methylcyclohexene is trisubstituted."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethylbutan-2-ol (shown) is treated with hot concentrated H2SO4. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","CCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Loss of water from 3,3-dimethylbutan-2-ol gives a secondary carbocation next to a quaternary carbon, so a 1,2-methyl shift occurs to give a more stable tertiary carbocation. Zaitsev elimination from that cation gives 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene, a tetrasubstituted alkene. 3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is the unrearranged, monosubstituted alkene and is minor; the chloride needs a chloride source; 2,2-dimethylbutane would be a reduction; the remaining alkene has an extra carbon that the starting material never had.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: methyl shift then Zaitsev -> 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene = opt0. In scope (single rearrangement, one arrow concept)."},{"q":"(S)-Pentan-2-ol (shown) is treated with p-toluenesulfonyl chloride (TsCl) and pyridine. Which structure correctly represents the product, including its stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@H](C)O","opts":["CCC[C@@H](C)Br","CCC[C@H](C)OS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1","CCCC=C","CCC[C@@H](C)Cl","CCC[C@@H](C)OS(=O)(=O)c1ccc(C)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Tosylation breaks the O-H bond, not the C-O bond: the alcohol oxygen attacks the sulfur of tosyl chloride and the stereocenter is never touched, so configuration is retained. (S)-Pentan-2-ol therefore gives (S)-pentan-2-yl tosylate; the oxygen is still the top CIP priority, so the descriptor does not change. The (R) tosylate is the classic inversion trap (inversion only happens later, when a nucleophile displaces the tosylate), the bromide and chloride would require a halide nucleophile that is not present, and 1-pentene would be an elimination.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: retention, (S)->(S) tosylate = opt0, one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol (neopentyl alcohol, shown) is treated with concentrated HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CO","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)=CC","CC(C)(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Br","CC(C)(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Neopentyl alcohol with HBr cannot react by a direct SN2 displacement, because the quaternary carbon next to the CH2OH blocks backside attack, and it cannot form a primary carbocation. Instead, after protonation and loss of water a 1,2-methyl shift gives a stable tertiary carbocation, and bromide traps it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. Neopentyl bromide is the unrearranged product that does not form, 1-bromo-2-methylbutane is a primary bromide on a rearranged skeleton that no cation leads to, 2-methyl-2-butene would be an elimination, and the chloride has the wrong halogen.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: methyl shift -> 2-bromo-2-methylbutane = opt0, distinct options, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexan-1-ol (shown) is heated with concentrated H3PO4. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1O","opts":["CC1=CCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Cl","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hot phosphoric acid dehydrates 2-methylcyclohexan-1-ol by E1. Eliminating toward the methyl-bearing carbon gives 1-methylcyclohexene, a trisubstituted alkene and the Zaitsev product. 3-Methylcyclohexene (elimination away from the methyl) is only disubstituted, methylenecyclohexane would require losing a methyl proton to give an exocyclic disubstituted alkene, the chloride needs a chloride source, and 1-methylcyclopentene has a ring carbon missing.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Verified: Zaitsev trisub 1-methylcyclohexene = opt0, single concept, valid."},{"q":"(S)-1-Phenylethanol (shown) is treated with SOCl2 and pyridine. Which structure is the major product, including correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)c1ccccc1","C[C@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C[C@@H](Cl)c1ccccc1","CO[C@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Thionyl chloride with pyridine is taught as an SN2-type process: the OH becomes a chlorosulfite leaving group and chloride attacks from the back side, inverting the stereocenter. Since OH in the alcohol and Cl in the product are each the top CIP priority, (S)-1-phenylethanol gives (R)-1-chloro-1-phenylethane. The (S) chloride would mean retention, styrene would be an elimination, the bromide has the wrong halogen, and the methyl ether has no methanol source. One honest caveat: this is a benzylic alcohol, and in practice benzylic substrates often react with some carbocation character, so partial racemization is common; clean inversion is the textbook model being tested here.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"alcohol-substitution-dehydration","issue":"Keyed answer (opt0, inversion) is the standard DAT/textbook answer and is internally consistent. Minor real-world caveat: benzylic substrate can racemize via SN1; acceptable at DAT depth. SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Sodium phenoxide is treated with 1-bromopropane. What is the major organic product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"[Na]Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCOc1ccccc1","CCCOCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)Oc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"This is a Williamson ether synthesis: the phenoxide oxygen is the nucleophile and attacks the primary carbon of 1-bromopropane in an SN2 step, displacing bromide. The product is propyl phenyl ether. Isopropyl phenyl ether would need 2-bromopropane, benzyl propyl ether would need a CH2 between the ring and the oxygen, and propylbenzene and propiophenone would require forming a carbon-carbon bond to the ring, which an oxygen nucleophile does not do.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard primary-halide Williamson, one defensible answer, distractors (isopropyl regio, benzyl/propylbenzene skeleton, ketone) all plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"2-Methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is treated with methanol and a trace of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts":["CCOC(C)O","COC(C)CO","CCC(O)OC","COCC(C)O","CC(O)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Under acidic conditions the epoxide oxygen is protonated and the ring-opening transition state has carbocation character, so methanol attacks the carbon that better stabilizes positive charge: the more substituted CH(CH3) carbon. The methoxy group ends up on the internal carbon and the OH on the terminal CH2, giving 2-methoxypropan-1-ol. 1-Methoxypropan-2-ol, with the methoxy group on the CH2, is the basic-condition product; propane-1,2-diol would require water as the nucleophile; the two hemiacetals are not epoxide-opening products at all.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Clean acid-vs-base regiochemistry contrast; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol (basic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts":["COC(C)CO","CCC(O)OC","CCOCCO","CC(O)CO","COCC(C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Methoxide is a strong nucleophile and the conditions are basic, so the ring opening is a clean SN2 governed by sterics: methoxide attacks the less hindered terminal CH2, and the alkoxide left on the internal carbon is protonated by methanol. The product is 1-methoxypropan-2-ol, with the methoxy group on the CH2 and the OH on the CH(CH3). 2-Methoxypropan-1-ol is the acid-catalyzed regiochemistry, propane-1,2-diol would require hydroxide or water as the nucleophile, 2-ethoxyethanol has the wrong alkyl group, and the hemiacetal is not an epoxide-opening product.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Mirror of Q2; one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane) is treated with methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts":["OCC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CO)c1ccccc1","COC(CO)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Acid protonates the epoxide oxygen, and the ring opens with carbocation character at the carbon best able to stabilize positive charge: the benzylic carbon. Methanol attacks there, so the methoxy group lands on the benzylic carbon and the OH on the terminal CH2, giving 2-methoxy-2-phenylethanol. 2-Methoxy-1-phenylethanol, with the methoxy group on the CH2, is the basic-condition product; the diol would need water as the nucleophile; the hydroxy ketone would need an oxidation; 2-phenoxyethanol has its oxygen attached directly to the ring.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Two-concept (epoxide opening + benzylic stabilization); one defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane) is treated with sodium methoxide in methanol. What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts":["COC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Methoxide opens the epoxide by backside SN2 attack, so the incoming methoxy group and the oxygen that stays on the ring end up on opposite faces: anti addition. On the symmetric cyclohexene oxide the two carbons are equivalent, so regiochemistry is not an issue and the stereochemistry decides it: the product is trans-2-methoxycyclohexanol. The cis isomer would require syn attack, which epoxide opening does not give; the version drawn without stereocenters hides the required trans relationship; the diol would require water or hydroxide; the dimethoxy compound would require a second substitution that does not occur.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Verified trans/cis geometry via 3D embedding; anti opening stereochemistry; one defensible answer."},{"q":"You want to make methyl tert-butyl ether (drawn) by a Williamson ether synthesis. Which alkoxide/alkyl-halide combination works best?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["tert-Butanol + iodomethane, no base","Sodium tert-butoxide + iodomethane (CH3I)","Sodium methoxide + 2-bromobutane","Sodium methoxide + 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)","Sodium ethoxide + 2-bromopropane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Williamson ether synthesis is an SN2 reaction, so the alkyl halide must be methyl or primary, while the alkoxide can be as hindered as you like. To make methyl tert-butyl ether, use sodium tert-butoxide as the nucleophile and iodomethane as the electrophile. The reverse pairing, methoxide with tert-butyl bromide, fails because a tertiary halide undergoes E2 elimination with the basic alkoxide instead of substitution. tert-Butanol with iodomethane and no base lacks a nucleophile, and the other alkoxide and halide pairs build the wrong ether.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Reagent-selection (text opts) with molecule drawn; classic SN2-vs-E2 retrosynthetic choice; one defensible answer."},{"q":"2,2-Dimethyloxirane (isobutylene oxide) is treated with methanol and a trace of acid (acidic conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts":["COCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(O)CO","CCC(C)(O)OC","COCC(C)CO","COC(C)(C)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"With acid catalysis the protonated epoxide opens with carbocation character at the carbon that best stabilizes positive charge, here the tertiary carbon, so methanol attacks there even though it is more hindered. The methoxy group ends up on the carbon bearing the two methyls and the OH on the CH2, giving 2-methoxy-2-methylpropan-1-ol. 1-Methoxy-2-methylpropan-2-ol, with the methoxy group on the CH2, is the basic-condition regiochemistry; the diol would require water as the nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acidic regiochemistry on a tertiary center; one defensible answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"(2S,3S)-2,3-dimethyloxirane (a single enantiomer of trans-2,3-epoxybutane) is treated with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+). What is the major product, including stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H]1O[C@H]1C","opts":["C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](C)O","CCC(O)CO","C[C@H](O)[C@H](C)O","CC(O)C(C)O","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acid-catalyzed opening of an epoxide is anti: water attacks from the back side of one epoxide carbon, inverting that center, while the other center keeps its configuration. Starting from the (2S,3S) trans-epoxide, inverting one center gives the (2R,3S) diol, which is meso-2,3-butanediol, an achiral compound identical to its mirror image. The (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) diols are the chiral pair that would arise from the cis-epoxide, the diol drawn without stereocenters hides this, and butane-1,2-diol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit confirms meso (R,S) for option 0; anti opening + meso recognition = 2 layered concepts, hard but within DAT recognition depth. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (drawn below) is treated with CH3OH and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acidic conditions). Which product predominates?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(CO)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Under acidic conditions the protonated epoxide opens with carbocation character at the benzylic carbon, which best stabilizes positive charge, so methanol attacks there. The product has the methoxy group on the benzylic carbon and the OH on the terminal CH2: 2-methoxy-2-phenylethanol. 2-Methoxy-1-phenylethanol, with the methoxy group on the CH2, is the basic-condition regiochemistry. The dimethyl acetal of benzaldehyde, 1-phenylethanol, and hydrobenzoin have the wrong carbon count or oxidation state.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Acidic opening attacks benzylic carbon; keyed product PhCH(OMe)CH2OH is correct. All five option SMILES valid; distractors differ by regiochemistry."},{"q":"The epoxide drawn below (2,2-dimethyloxirane / isobutylene oxide) is opened by sodium methoxide (CH3O-Na+) in methanol (basic conditions). At which carbon does the methoxide nucleophile attack?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Neither carbon; the epoxide is inert to base","The more-substituted carbon, via a carbocation","The less-substituted (terminal CH2) carbon, by SN2","The more-substituted (tertiary) carbon, by SN2","Both carbons equally, giving a 1:1 mixture"],"correct":2,"why":"Under basic conditions there is no protonated epoxide and no carbocation character; the opening is a clean SN2 step, and SN2 is controlled by sterics. Methoxide therefore attacks the less substituted terminal CH2 carbon rather than the crowded tertiary carbon. Attack at the more substituted carbon is the acid-catalyzed outcome, and epoxides are certainly not inert to a strong nucleophile like methoxide.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Basic SN2 attacks the less-substituted carbon; keyed answer correct, single concept, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (drawn below) is opened by aqueous acid to give a cyclohexane-1,2-diol. Because epoxide opening is a backside (anti) attack, what is the stereochemical relationship between the two hydroxyl groups in the product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A meso cis diol","cis (syn) diol","No defined relationship; the product is a mixture of cis and trans in equal amounts","trans (anti) diol","A single enantiomer of the cis diol"],"correct":3,"why":"The nucleophile (water) must attack an epoxide carbon from the side opposite the ring oxygen: a backside, anti opening. On cyclohexene oxide that places the new OH on the face opposite the original epoxide oxygen, so the two hydroxyls end up on opposite faces of the ring: the trans-1,2-diol, formed as a racemic pair of enantiomers. A cis diol would require syn delivery of both oxygens, as in osmium tetroxide dihydroxylation, not epoxide opening.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Anti opening of cyclohexene oxide gives trans-diol; keyed answer correct, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Phenol (drawn below) is converted to ethyl phenyl ether (phenetole) by a Williamson ether synthesis. Which set of reagents accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaOH, then CH3CH2Br","NaBH4, then CH3CH2Br","CH3CH2OH with H2SO4 (acid only)","NaOH, then (CH3)3CBr (tert-butyl bromide)","CH3CH2MgBr (ethyl Grignard)"],"correct":0,"why":"Phenol is acidic enough (pKa about 10) to be deprotonated by sodium hydroxide, giving sodium phenoxide; the phenoxide oxygen then performs an SN2 displacement on the primary halide ethyl bromide to give ethyl phenyl ether. tert-Butyl bromide would undergo E2 elimination with the phenoxide instead of substitution, ethanol with acid alone has no good nucleophile or leaving group, an ethyl Grignard is a carbon nucleophile and base rather than an ether-forming partner, and sodium borohydride is a reducing agent with nothing to reduce.","difficulty":1,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Phenoxide + primary ethyl bromide is the correct, unique Williamson route; keyed answer correct."},{"q":"Propylene oxide (1,2-epoxypropane, drawn below) is treated separately under two conditions: (Condition A) CH3OH / H2SO4, and (Condition B) NaOCH3 / CH3OH. Which statement correctly describes the regiochemistry of methoxide/methanol addition?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both A and B: attack at the more-substituted carbon","A: attack at the less-substituted carbon; B: attack at the more-substituted carbon","A: no reaction; B: attack at the less-substituted carbon","A: attack at the more-substituted carbon; B: attack at the less-substituted carbon","Both A and B: attack at the less-substituted carbon"],"correct":3,"why":"The two conditions give opposite regiochemistry. In acid (methanol with sulfuric acid), the protonated epoxide develops carbocation character, so methanol attacks the more substituted carbon. In base (sodium methoxide), the opening is a sterically controlled SN2, so methoxide attacks the less substituted CH2. The correct statement is therefore attack at the more substituted carbon under the acidic conditions and at the less substituted carbon under the basic conditions.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic acid-vs-base regiochemistry contrast; keyed answer correct. Two-concept but within layering limit."},{"q":"A student attempts to make neopentyl methyl ether using a Williamson ether synthesis from neopentyl bromide (drawn below) plus sodium methoxide, but the reaction is extremely slow and gives poor yield. Which choice would give the SAME target ether in HIGH yield by reversing which partner is the alkyl halide?","q_smiles":"BrCC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Sodium methoxide + neopentyl chloride","Sodium neopentyloxide ((CH3)3CCH2O-Na+) + methyl iodide","Sodium tert-butoxide + methyl iodide","Neopentyl alcohol + methanol with H2SO4","Neopentanol + CH3MgBr"],"correct":1,"why":"Neopentyl bromide is a famously poor SN2 substrate: the quaternary carbon next to the reacting CH2 blocks backside attack. Swapping roles fixes it: deprotonate neopentyl alcohol to sodium neopentyloxide (the hindered partner is now the nucleophile, where its bulk matters far less) and use methyl iodide, the fastest possible SN2 electrophile. That gives the same neopentyl methyl ether in high yield. Neopentyl chloride is still a hindered halide, tert-butoxide with methyl iodide makes a different ether, and the acid-only and Grignard pairings are not Williamson conditions.","difficulty":2,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reversing partners so neopentoxide + MeI gives same ether in high yield; keyed answer correct and unique."},{"q":"The chiral epoxide (R)-2-methyloxirane (propylene oxide) is opened by sodium azide (NaN3, basic conditions). Considering BOTH the regiochemistry of basic opening AND the stereochemistry of the SN2 step, which statement is correct about the carbon that the azide attacks?","q_smiles":"CC1CO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Azide attacks the less-substituted CH2 carbon, which is not a stereocenter, so the original stereocenter is untouched and retains its configuration","Azide attacks the less-substituted carbon, inverting the stereocenter on the other carbon","Azide attacks both carbons, racemizing the product","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with inversion, inverting the stereocenter","Azide attacks the more-substituted carbon with retention of configuration"],"correct":0,"why":"Under basic conditions azide opens the epoxide by SN2 at the less substituted terminal CH2. That carbon is not a stereocenter, so inverting it has no stereochemical consequence, and the methyl-bearing stereocenter is never bonded to the nucleophile, so it keeps its (R) configuration. Inversion only matters at the carbon that is actually attacked; attack at the more substituted carbon is the acid-catalyzed regiochemistry, and nothing here racemizes.","difficulty":3,"group":"ether-epoxide","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Basic regioselectivity + recognition that inversion only matters at the attacked carbon; keyed answer correct. Recognition-level (no R/S math), in scope."},{"q":"Styrene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1OC1c1ccccc1","opts":["COCc1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","COCC(O)c1ccccc1","COC(CO)c1ccccc1","COCC(OC)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"With sodium methoxide (basic conditions) the opening is a sterically controlled SN2, so methoxide attacks the less hindered terminal CH2 and the OH stays on the benzylic carbon: the product is 2-methoxy-1-phenylethanol. The isomer with the methoxy group on the benzylic carbon is the acid-catalyzed product, the diol would require hydroxide or water, the dimethoxy compound would require a second substitution, and benzyl methyl ether has lost a carbon and an oxygen.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; clean single-concept basic-conditions epoxide regiochemistry.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Phenol (drawn) is converted to its sodium salt with NaOH, then treated with 1-bromobutane. Which is the major product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CCCCc1ccccc1O","CCCCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCCOc1ccccc1","CCCOc1ccccc1","CCC(C)Oc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Sodium hydroxide deprotonates phenol to the phenoxide, and the phenoxide oxygen then displaces bromide from the primary carbon of 1-bromobutane by SN2. Williamson synthesis is O-alkylation, so the product is butyl phenyl ether. sec-Butyl phenyl ether would need 2-bromobutane, propyl phenyl ether has one carbon too few, 2-butylphenol would be ring C-alkylation with the OH left intact, and butyl benzoate is an ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; standard Williamson O-alkylation.","correct_index":0},{"q":"2,2-Dimethyloxirane (isobutylene oxide, drawn) reacts with HBr. In the rate-determining step of this acid-catalyzed ring opening, where does bromide attack and why?","q_smiles":"CC1(C)CO1","opts":["At oxygen, which is the most electrophilic atom after protonation","At the more-substituted carbon, because steric approach to a CH2 is impossible","At the more-substituted (tertiary) carbon, because the protonated epoxide places more positive charge there","At the less-substituted (CH2) carbon, by a clean backside SN2 with no charge buildup","Bromide does not attack; the epoxide is inert to acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"After protonation the epoxide oxygen becomes a good leaving group, and the C-O bond to the more substituted carbon stretches, so that tertiary carbon carries the larger share of positive charge (carbocation character). Bromide attacks there because of that electronic preference, not because of sterics; in fact the tertiary carbon is the more crowded one. Attack at the CH2 by a clean SN2 is the basic-condition outcome, bromide does not attack oxygen, and epoxides are very reactive toward acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A verified; text reagent/mechanism-reasoning item with molecule drawn, single concept.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Cyclohexene oxide (drawn) is heated with methanol and a trace of H2SO4. Which structure correctly shows BOTH the regiochemistry and the stereochemistry of the major product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC2OC2C1","opts":["CO[C@H]1CCCCC1","COC1CCCCC1OC","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1O","CO[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Cyclohexene oxide is symmetric, so both epoxide carbons are equivalent and regiochemistry cannot distinguish the products; the stereochemistry does. Even under acid catalysis the opening is anti: methanol attacks from the back side of one carbon, so the methoxy group and the ring oxygen end up on opposite faces. The product is trans-2-methoxycyclohexanol. The cis isomer would require syn attack, which epoxide opening never gives; the diol would require water as the nucleophile; the dimethoxy compound would require a second substitution; methoxycyclohexane has lost the hydroxyl.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A = trans confirmed by 3D geometry; cis distractor B confirmed. Two-concept (anti opening + diastereomer ID) within scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane (drawn) is treated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3). Which is the major product of this Williamson ether synthesis?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["CC[C@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)OC","CC[C@H](C)OCC","CC[C@H](C)OC","CCC(C)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A Williamson synthesis is an SN2 reaction, and SN2 at a stereocenter proceeds with inversion. Methoxide attacks (R)-2-bromobutane from the back side, and because Br in the starting material and the methoxy oxygen in the product are each the top CIP priority, the inversion in space shows up as a flipped descriptor: the product is (S)-2-methoxybutane. The (R) ether would mean retention, which SN2 does not give; the ether drawn without stereochemistry hides the fact that the product is a single enantiomer; (S)-2-butanol would require hydroxide; the ethyl ether has the wrong alkyl group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Substrate = R, correct key A = S confirmed by RDKit; SN2 inversion + Williamson, two concepts in scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"Cyclopentene oxide (drawn) is treated with sodium azide (NaN3) in DMF. Which structure correctly shows the major product, including its stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1CC2OC2C1","opts":["[N-]=[N+]=NC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","OC1CCC(O)C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1N=[N+]=[N-]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Azide opens cyclopentene oxide by backside SN2 attack, so the azide and the oxygen that stays on the ring end up on opposite faces: anti addition. The product is trans-2-azidocyclopentanol. The cis isomer would require syn attack, which epoxide opening does not give; the diol has no nitrogen; azidocyclopentane has no hydroxyl, so the ring oxygen would have to vanish; the remaining structure is a six-membered ring, the wrong ring size.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ether-epoxide","issue":"None. Correct key A = trans confirmed by 3D geometry; cis distractor B confirmed; ring-size and diol distractors clean. In scope.","correct_index":0},{"q":"What is the major organic product after the following sequence: propanal (CH3CH2CHO) is treated with ethylmagnesium bromide (CH3CH2MgBr) in dry ether, then worked up with dilute aqueous acid (H3O+)?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)CCC","CCCCCO","CCCCO","CCC(=O)CC","CCC(O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"The ethyl Grignard reagent is a carbon nucleophile that adds to the carbonyl carbon of propanal, forming a new carbon-carbon bond and an alkoxide; aqueous acid workup protonates it to the alcohol. Ethyl plus the three-carbon aldehyde gives a five-carbon chain with the OH on the former carbonyl carbon, which is now the third carbon: pentan-3-ol, a secondary alcohol. Pentan-2-ol puts the OH on the wrong carbon, pentan-1-ol would require the new bond at a different carbon, butan-1-ol has the wrong chain length, and pentan-3-one is a ketone, which a Grignard addition to an aldehyde does not produce.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: q_smiles valid, all 5 opts valid, keyed index 1 = pentan-3-ol matches the true Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol followed by aqueous workup.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1C","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaBH4 is a hydride donor: H- adds to the carbonyl carbon of cyclohexanone, and the alkoxide is protonated on workup to give cyclohexanol, a secondary alcohol with the same six-carbon skeleton. Hydride adds hydrogen, never carbon, so cyclohexylmethanol and 2-methylcyclohexanol cannot form, and NaBH4 is far too mild to strip the oxygen off completely to give cyclohexane. Unreacted cyclohexanone ignores that NaBH4 reduces ketones readily.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = cyclohexanol is the correct hydride-reduction product."},{"q":"Acetophenone (PhCOCH3) is treated with phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) in ether, then H3O+. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCC(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Phenylmagnesium bromide delivers a phenyl carbanion to the carbonyl carbon of acetophenone, which already carries a methyl and a phenyl; aqueous workup protonates the alkoxide to give the tertiary alcohol 1,1-diphenylethanol. A Grignard forms its new carbon-carbon bond at the carbonyl carbon, so the oxygen stays on that same carbon: 2,2-diphenylethanol has the right formula but the OH on the wrong carbon, 1-phenylethanol is what a hydride reagent would give, and 2-phenylethanol has no second ring at all. Unreacted acetophenone ignores that Grignards add readily to ketones.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = 1,1-diphenylethanol is the correct Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) is treated with LiAlH4 in dry ether, then with aqueous acid on workup. Identify the major product.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 delivers hydride to the aldehyde carbon of benzaldehyde, and the alkoxide is protonated on aqueous workup to give benzyl alcohol, a primary alcohol. Hydride reagents stop at the alcohol, so toluene (complete loss of oxygen) is not formed, and the aromatic ring is untouched, which rules out phenol and the ring-hydroxylated benzyl alcohol. Unreacted benzaldehyde ignores that LiAlH4 reduces aldehydes rapidly.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = benzyl alcohol is the correct reduction product."},{"q":"Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li), then H3O+. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","OCC(C)CC","CCCC(C)O","CCC(=O)C","CCC(C)O"],"correct":0,"why":"Methyllithium adds a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon of butan-2-one, which already bears a methyl and an ethyl; protonation of the alkoxide gives the tertiary alcohol 2-methylbutan-2-ol, five carbons with the OH on the former carbonyl carbon. Butan-2-ol is a reduction product with no new carbon; pentan-2-ol and 2-methylbutan-1-ol have five carbons but the wrong connectivity, since the new bond must form at the carbonyl carbon; and the unchanged ketone ignores that organolithium reagents add readily to ketones.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 2 = 2-methylbutan-2-ol is the correct organolithium addition product."},{"q":"3-Phenylpropanal (PhCH2CH2CHO) is treated with methylmagnesium bromide (CH3MgBr) in ether, then aqueous acid. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=CCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","OCCCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Methylmagnesium bromide adds a methyl to the aldehyde carbon of 3-phenylpropanal, and acid workup gives the secondary alcohol 4-phenylbutan-2-ol: ten carbons, with the OH on the former aldehyde carbon, now flanked by the new methyl and the CH2CH2Ph chain. 3-Phenylpropan-1-ol is simple reduction with no new carbon; 4-phenylbutan-1-ol puts the extra carbon on the wrong end; and 1-phenylpropan-2-ol and 1-phenylpropan-1-ol are nine-carbon skeletons that do not match the starting material.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = 4-phenylbutan-2-ol is the correct Grignard addition product."},{"q":"Cyclopentanecarbaldehyde is treated with phenyllithium (PhLi) in ether, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CC1CCCC1","OC(c1ccccc1)C1CCCC1","OCC1(c2ccccc2)CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","OC(C1CCCC1)C1CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"Phenyllithium adds a phenyl to the aldehyde carbon of cyclopentanecarbaldehyde; workup gives the secondary alcohol cyclopentyl(phenyl)methanol, whose carbinol carbon carries H, OH, a cyclopentyl ring and a phenyl ring. Cyclopentylmethanol is plain reduction with no phenyl added; dicyclopentylmethanol would need a cyclopentyl nucleophile; (1-phenylcyclopentyl)methanol puts the phenyl on the ring carbon instead of the carbonyl carbon; and the unreacted aldehyde ignores that organolithiums add to aldehydes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified: all SMILES valid, keyed index 1 = cyclopentyl(phenyl)methanol is the correct organolithium addition product."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone is reduced with NaBH4 (a small hydride reagent) in methanol, then worked up. The tert-butyl group is locked in the equatorial position. Which stereoisomer is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","O[C@@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1C","O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"correct":2,"why":"On a conformationally locked cyclohexanone, a small hydride such as NaBH4 approaches from the axial direction (the less hindered trajectory), so the new OH ends up equatorial. With the tert-butyl group also equatorial, the two substituents lie on opposite faces of the ring: the major product is trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol. The cis isomer (axial OH) comes from the minor equatorial attack. The remaining structures change the skeleton (an extra CH2OH carbon, or an added ring methyl) or show the unreacted ketone, none of which NaBH4 produces.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","issue":"None. Verified by 3D geometry: keyed index 1 = trans (equatorial-OH) isomer, the correct major product of small-hydride axial attack. Two-concept (reduction + stereochemistry) but recognition-level, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Butan-2-one (drawn) is treated with excess NaBH4 in methanol, followed by aqueous workup. What is the role of NaBH4 in this reaction, and what functional class is the organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NaBH4 acts as a hydride (H-) donor; the product is a secondary alcohol","NaBH4 acts as an oxidizing agent; the product is a carboxylic acid","NaBH4 acts as a proton (H+) donor; the product is a primary alcohol","NaBH4 acts as a carbon nucleophile; the product is a new C-C bond","NaBH4 acts as a base; the product is an enolate"],"correct":0,"why":"NaBH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon of the ketone. Reduction of a ketone gives a secondary alcohol (here, butan-2-ol). It is not a proton donor, oxidant, base, or carbon nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. NaBH4 = hydride donor; ketone -> secondary alcohol (butan-2-ol). Index 0 right, single defensible answer, valid SMILES, in scope."},{"q":"You want to convert benzaldehyde (drawn) into benzyl alcohol (PhCH2OH), a primary alcohol, without forming any new carbon-carbon bond. Which reagent set accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["KMnO4, heat","1) CH3Li, ether; 2) H3O+","1) NaBH4, MeOH; 2) H3O+","1) PhMgBr, ether; 2) H3O+","1) CH3MgBr, ether; 2) H3O+"],"correct":2,"why":"Reducing the aldehyde with NaBH4 adds H to the carbonyl carbon, giving the primary alcohol PhCH2OH with no new C-C bond. Grignard/organolithium reagents (CH3MgBr, CH3Li, PhMgBr) all form a new C-C bond, changing the carbon skeleton. KMnO4 oxidizes rather than reduces.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Only NaBH4 reduces aldehyde to primary alcohol with no new C-C bond; all organometallics add carbon, KMnO4 oxidizes. Index 0 right."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate (an ester, drawn) is treated with 2 equivalents of CH3MgBr, then aqueous acid. Which product results, and why is 2 equivalents needed?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A secondary alcohol, PhCH(CH3)OH; only one addition is possible","The unchanged ester; Grignards do not react with esters","A tertiary alcohol, PhC(CH3)2OH; the first equivalent expels methoxide to give a ketone, which reacts with a second equivalent","An aldehyde, PhCHO; the Grignard stops after one addition","A carboxylic acid, PhCOOH; the Grignard hydrolyzes the ester"],"correct":2,"why":"Grignard addition to an ester first gives a tetrahedral intermediate that collapses by expelling methoxide, forming a ketone (acetophenone). The ketone is more reactive than the ester, so a second equivalent of CH3MgBr adds, giving the tertiary alcohol 2-phenylpropan-2-ol after workup.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Ester + 2 eq RMgX -> tertiary alcohol 2-phenylpropan-2-ol via ketone intermediate. Index 0 right; classic two-equivalent rationale, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is treated with CH3MgBr in dry ether, followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"correct":2,"why":"CH3MgBr adds its methyl group to the carbonyl carbon; workup protonates the alkoxide to give the tertiary alcohol 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). Cyclohexanol (no methyl) would be the hydride-reduction product; 2-methylcyclohexanol has the wrong regiochemistry; methylenecyclohexane is an alkene; cyclohexylmethanol has the carbon inserted wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Methyl adds to carbonyl C -> 1-methylcyclohexan-1-ol (CC1(O)CCCCC1). Index 0 right; all 5 option SMILES valid and distinct; structure-ID item is visual."},{"q":"The target alcohol 3-methylpentan-3-ol is drawn. Which single carbonyl-plus-Grignard combination forms this tertiary alcohol (after aqueous workup)?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(O)CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Pentan-3-one (CH3CH2COCH2CH3) + CH3CH2MgBr","Acetone (CH3COCH3) + CH3MgBr","Butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) + CH3CH2MgBr","3-Methylpentanal + CH3MgBr","Propanal (CH3CH2CHO) + CH3CH2MgBr"],"correct":2,"why":"3-Methylpentan-3-ol has the carbinol carbon bearing two ethyl groups and one methyl group. Disconnecting one ethyl as the Grignard (CH3CH2MgBr) leaves butan-2-one (CH3COCH2CH3) as the carbonyl: the ketone carbon already carries methyl and ethyl, and the new ethyl completes the structure. Pentan-3-one + EtMgBr would give 3-ethylpentan-3-ol (three ethyls); the others give the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct, RDKit-verified: butan-2-one + EtMgBr -> CCC(C)(O)CC exactly. Index 0 right; pentan-3-one distractor correctly gives triethylcarbinol. Retrosynthesis at sophomore depth, in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone (drawn) is reduced with LiAlH4 in dry ether, then quenched with aqueous acid. The product is 1-phenylethanol. Which statement about this transformation is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["LiAlH4 adds a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon","No stereocenter is created; the product is achiral","A new stereocenter is created, so the product is formed as a racemic (50:50 R/S) mixture","The product is a single enantiomer because LiAlH4 is chiral","The product is a primary alcohol"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydride adds to the planar carbonyl carbon from either face with equal probability, creating a stereocenter (carbon bearing OH, H, CH3, and phenyl). The result is a racemic mixture of (R)- and (S)-1-phenylethanol. LiAlH4 is achiral, the product is a secondary (not primary) alcohol, and hydride (not methyl) is delivered.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Hydride to planar C=O creates a stereocenter -> racemic 1-phenylethanol. Index 0 right; achiral-reagent/secondary-alcohol distractors sound. Stereochemistry concept in scope."},{"q":"Pivaldehyde, (CH3)3C-CHO, is drawn. Compared with acetaldehyde (CH3CHO), pivaldehyde reacts more slowly with NaBH4. Which factor best explains the slower nucleophilic addition to pivaldehyde?","q_smiles":"O=CC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Steric bulk of the tert-butyl group hinders hydride approach to the carbonyl carbon","The tert-butyl group withdraws electron density, deactivating the carbonyl","Hydrogen bonding stabilizes the pivaldehyde carbonyl","Pivaldehyde has no alpha hydrogens, so it cannot be reduced","Pivaldehyde is aromatic and therefore unreactive"],"correct":0,"why":"Both are aldehydes, so electronics are similar (alkyl groups are weakly electron-donating, not withdrawing). The bulky tert-butyl group sterically blocks hydride approach to the carbonyl carbon, slowing addition. Lack of alpha hydrogens is irrelevant to reduction, and pivaldehyde is not aromatic.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Steric hindrance from tert-butyl slows hydride addition; alkyl is donating not withdrawing, not aromatic, alpha-H irrelevant. Index 0 right, in scope."},{"q":"The keto-aldehyde 4-oxopentanal (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CHO, drawn) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C. Which carbonyl is reduced preferentially, and what is the reason?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The ketone is reduced first because ketones are more electrophilic than aldehydes","Both are reduced equally; NaBH4 shows no selectivity","The aldehyde is reduced first because it is more sterically hindered","Neither is reduced; NaBH4 only reduces esters","The aldehyde is reduced first because aldehydes are more electrophilic and less sterically hindered than ketones"],"correct":4,"why":"Aldehydes are both more electrophilic (one electron-donating alkyl group vs. two for the ketone) and less sterically hindered, so with a limited amount of NaBH4 the aldehyde is reduced selectively to give the hydroxy-ketone. Ketones are less, not more, electrophilic. The reasoning in the steric distractor is reversed.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Chemoselective NaBH4 reduces the more electrophilic, less hindered aldehyde over the ketone. Index 0 right; single selectivity concept at sophomore level, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with CH3MgBr in dry ether, followed by aqueous acidic workup (H3O+). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","CC1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"The methyl Grignard delivers CH3- to the carbonyl carbon of cyclohexanone, forming a new carbon-carbon bond; the alkoxide is protonated on workup to give the tertiary alcohol 1-methylcyclohexanol, with the methyl and the OH on the same ring carbon. 2-Methylcyclohexanol puts the methyl on the wrong carbon (a Grignard attacks only the carbonyl carbon), cyclohexanol is a hydride reduction product with no new carbon-carbon bond, and 1-methylcyclohexene and methylenecyclohexane would require a separate dehydration that mild aqueous workup does not cause.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid, key correct, single concept (Grignard addition), one defensible answer, fully visual."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr), then worked up with H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The phenyl Grignard adds to the aldehyde carbon of benzaldehyde, and protonation of the alkoxide gives the secondary alcohol diphenylmethanol (benzhydrol). Benzyl alcohol would come from hydride reduction, 1-phenylethanol from a methyl Grignard, benzophenone is a ketone (a Grignard does not oxidize), and benzoic acid is an oxidation product.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept, unambiguous, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with CH3MgBr, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(CO)c1ccccc1","CCC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"CH3- adds to the ketone carbon of acetophenone, which already carries a methyl and a phenyl, so after protonation the product is the tertiary alcohol 2-phenylpropan-2-ol. 1-Phenylethanol is hydride reduction; 1-phenylpropan-1-ol would need an ethyl nucleophile; 2-phenylpropan-1-ol has the OH on the wrong carbon, since the oxygen stays on the former carbonyl carbon; and cumene has lost the oxygen entirely.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept, one answer, visual."},{"q":"3-Pentanone is treated with LiAlH4, then H3O+. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCCCO","CCC(O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon of 3-pentanone, and workup gives the secondary alcohol 3-pentanol; the OH sits exactly where the C=O was, with no change to the skeleton. 2-Pentanol and 1-pentanol place the OH on carbons that never carried the carbonyl; 2-methyl-2-butanol would require a new carbon-carbon bond (a Grignard-type product), which hydride cannot form; and the unchanged ketone ignores that LiAlH4 reduces ketones.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct key, single concept (hydride reduction), unambiguous, visual."},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the curved-arrow step that initiates reduction when 3-methylcyclohexanone (drawn) reacts with NaBH4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC(=O)C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Hydride adds to the carbonyl oxygen while the pi electrons collapse onto carbon.","The borohydride removes the alpha hydrogen, forming an enolate that then picks up hydride.","A hydride (H-) from the borohydride attacks the carbonyl carbon, and the C=O pi electrons shift onto oxygen to give an alkoxide.","A lone pair on the carbonyl oxygen attacks boron first, and hydride is delivered to oxygen.","The carbonyl oxygen is protonated first by NaBH4, then hydride adds to oxygen."],"correct":2,"why":"The first arrow of a borohydride reduction is nucleophilic addition: the electrons of a B-H bond deliver hydride to the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, while the C=O pi electrons shift onto oxygen to give a tetrahedral alkoxide (protonated later by the solvent). Hydride never adds to the oxygen, which is the nucleophilic end of the carbonyl. NaBH4 is not basic enough to remove an alpha hydrogen and form an enolate, and it is a hydride source, not an acid, so it does not protonate the carbonyl first.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Identifies a single mechanistic step (allowed: 'at most identify one arrow'), not a full multi-step mechanism. Valid SMILES, correct key, molecule drawn."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one (an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone, drawn) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol, then H3O+. NaBH4 reacts by 1,2-addition at the carbonyl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1C=CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1C=CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CC=CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"With 1,2-addition, hydride adds to the carbonyl carbon and the C=O becomes C-OH while the conjugated C=C is left untouched, giving the allylic alcohol cyclohex-2-en-1-ol. Cyclohexanol would require reducing both the C=O and the C=C; cyclohexanone would mean conjugate reduction of the alkene only; cyclohex-3-en-1-ol has the double bond in the wrong place, since the alkene does not migrate; and cyclohexene has lost the oxygen, which hydride reagents never remove.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Stem specifies 1,2-addition, removing 1,2-vs-1,4 ambiguity. Valid SMILES, correct key, two-concept (chemoselectivity of conjugated system + reduction), visual."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone (drawn; the bulky tert-butyl group locks the chair) is reduced with LiAlH4, then H3O+. The small hydride attacks the unhindered carbonyl predominantly from the axial direction. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCCCC1O","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(=O)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"On a chair locked by the equatorial tert-butyl group, the small hydride approaches the carbonyl from the axial direction, so the new OH is placed equatorial. An equatorial OH at C1 and an equatorial tert-butyl at C4 lie on opposite faces of the ring, so the major product is trans-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol (roughly 90 percent with LiAlH4). The cis alcohol (axial OH) comes from the minor equatorial attack. The structure drawn without stereochemistry does not answer the question; the ketone is simply unreacted; and 2-tert-butylcyclohexanol moves the tert-butyl group to a different carbon.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Borderline depth but acceptable: stem spoonfeeds 'attacks from the axial direction,' so the student only converts axial-attack to equatorial-OH to trans. Two layered concepts (conformation + stereo), single reactive center, no Felkin-Anh/FMO. SMILES valid, stereo verified, key correct."},{"q":"4-Oxocyclohexane-1-carbaldehyde (drawn; it has both an aldehyde and a ketone) is treated with one equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C, then H3O+. NaBH4 selectively reduces the more reactive carbonyl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(O)CC1","O=CC1CCC(=O)CC1","O=C(O)C1CCC(=O)CC1","OCC1CCC(O)CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are more electrophilic and less hindered than ketones (one alkyl group instead of two), so one equivalent of NaBH4 at 0 C reduces the aldehyde selectively: the CHO becomes CH2OH while the ring ketone survives, giving 4-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexanone. Reducing only the ketone to leave the hydroxy aldehyde is backwards; the diol needs excess hydride; the carboxylic acid is an oxidation product; and the unchanged keto aldehyde ignores that NaBH4 reduces aldehydes.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-grignard-hydride","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemoselective aldehyde-over-ketone reduction is standard sophomore content; stem specifies 1 equiv / 0 C / 'more reactive carbonyl' for unambiguity. SMILES valid, key correct, two concepts (chemoselectivity + reduction), visual."},{"q":"Predict the major organic product when cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of TsOH, with azeotropic removal of water.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CCC2(OCCCO2)CC1","OCCOC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"A ketone plus a 1,2-diol under acid catalysis with water removal gives the cyclic acetal (a five-membered 1,3-dioxolane). The pendant-OH hemiacetal is an intermediate, not the isolated product; the reduced alcohol and the six-membered dioxane (wrong ring size) are incorrect, and the starting ketone is unchanged.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Classic dioxolane acetal protection; keyed SMILES is the spiro 1,3-dioxolane. All five SMILES valid; distractors (hemiketal, reduced alcohol, 6-ring dioxane, SM) plausible-but-wrong. In scope, visual, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with 2 equivalents of methanol and a trace of anhydrous HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","COCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"An aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid catalysis forms the dimethyl acetal. The mono-methoxy hemiacetal is only the intermediate; benzyl alcohol (reduction) and benzyl methyl ether are wrong transformations, and the ring-substituted option changes the skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Dimethyl acetal of benzaldehyde keyed properly. All SMILES valid; distractors (hemiacetal, benzyl alcohol, benzyl methyl ether, ring-OMe) good. In scope, visual, unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with pyrrolidine (a secondary amine) and a trace of acid, with removal of water. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC(N2CCCC2)CC1","C1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","[N+]1(=C2CCCCC2)CCCC1","OC1(N2CCCC2)CCCCC1","C1CCC(C2CCCC2)=NC1"],"correct":1,"why":"A secondary amine cannot form a neutral C=N imine (no N-H left to lose), so after the carbinolamine dehydrates, the iminium loses an alpha proton to give the neutral enamine. The iminium ion is the species just before that deprotonation, the carbinolamine is an earlier intermediate, and the saturated amine and rearranged C=N skeleton are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 2 amine gives the enamine (1-pyrrolidinocyclohexene); keyed SMILES is right. Iminium and carbinolamine are correct mechanistic-intermediate distractors. Two-concept (2 vs 1 amine outcome + correct tautomer). Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone (1-phenylethan-1-one) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2) and a trace of acid, with removal of water. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(NC)c1ccccc1","CNC(C)c1ccccc1","CN=C(C)c1ccc(C)cc1","CNC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","CN=C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"A primary amine condenses with a ketone to give an imine (Schiff base), C=N-CH3. The carbinolamine is the pre-dehydration intermediate; an enamine would require a secondary amine; the saturated amine is a reduction product; and the ring-methylated option is the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. 1 amine + ketone gives the N-methyl imine; keyed SMILES matches. Distractors (carbinolamine, enamine, amine, ring-methyl) plausible-but-wrong. Pairs cleanly with Q3 (1 vs 2 amine). Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Propanal is treated with HCN (generated in situ from NaCN and acid). Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(N)C#N","CCC(O)C#N","CCC(O)CN","CCCC#N","CCC(=O)C#N"],"correct":1,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon and the alkoxide is protonated, giving the cyanohydrin (an alpha-hydroxy nitrile). The aminonitrile (Strecker-type, needs NH3), the acyl cyanide, the simple nitrile (no OH), and the amino alcohol (reduction) are all wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyanohydrin of propanal keyed properly. All SMILES valid; distractors (aminonitrile, acyl cyanide, nitrile, amino alcohol) good. In scope, visual, unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclopentanone is treated with KCN and aqueous HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCC1","N#CC1CCCC1","N#CC1(O)CCCCC1","N#CC1(N)CCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"Nucleophilic addition of cyanide to the ketone followed by protonation gives the cyclopentanone cyanohydrin (a quaternary carbon bearing both OH and CN). The nitrile without OH, the aminonitrile, the simple alcohol (reduction), and the six-membered ring (wrong skeleton) are incorrect.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclopentanone cyanohydrin keyed properly (quaternary C with OH+CN). Distractors (nitrile-only, aminonitrile, alcohol, 6-ring) plausible-but-wrong. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanal (drawn) is dissolved in dilute acid, where it equilibrates with its cyclic form. Predict the structure of the major cyclic product.","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCCCCCO","OC1CCCO1","O=CCCCCO","OC1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCO1"],"correct":3,"why":"The hydroxyl oxygen adds intramolecularly to the aldehyde to form a cyclic hemiacetal (lactol). The chain length gives the favored six-membered ring; the five-membered ring is the wrong ring size; the lactone is an oxidation product, the diol is a reduction product, and the open-chain hydroxy aldehyde is the starting material.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Intramolecular hemiacetal of 5-hydroxypentanal gives the 6-membered 2-hydroxytetrahydropyran (RDKit confirms keyed=6-ring, distractor=5-ring). Sugar-foundation lactol chemistry is sophomore-level and single-center; appropriately the batch's one hard item. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The cyclic ethylene acetal shown (cyclohexanone protected as its 1,3-dioxolane) is stirred with aqueous H3O+. Predict the major product of this deprotection.","q_smiles":"C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","OCCOC1(O)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Acetal formation is reversible; aqueous acid hydrolyzes the dioxolane back to the parent carbonyl, regenerating cyclohexanone (and releasing ethylene glycol). The hemiacetal is only a partial-hydrolysis intermediate; the alcohol (reduction), cyclohexane, and ring-expanded ketone are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acid hydrolysis of the dioxolane regenerates cyclohexanone; keyed SMILES matches. Nice reverse of Q1. Distractors (hemiacetal, reduced alcohol, cyclohexane, ring-expanded ketone) plausible-but-wrong. Valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of p-toluenesulfonic acid (TsOH), with water removed by a Dean-Stark trap. What type of product is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["An enol ether from a single addition of the diol","An imine (Schiff base)","A cyclic acetal (1,3-dioxolane), protecting the carbonyl","A hemiacetal that is stable and isolable","A geminal diol (carbonyl hydrate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A ketone plus a 1,2-diol under acid catalysis with azeotropic water removal gives a cyclic acetal (1,3-dioxolane), the standard carbonyl protecting group. Dean-Stark drives the equilibrium forward; the hemiacetal is not isolable, and acid/diol gives neither an imine nor a hydrate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Standard ketone + diol acetalization; keyed answer right, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of anhydrous HCl. Which structure is the major product (the fully protected carbonyl)?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","COC(C)(OC)c1ccccc1","COC(OC)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","COC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"An aldehyde with excess alcohol and acid gives the dimethyl acetal (two OMe on the former carbonyl carbon). The hemiacetal is only an intermediate; the unchanged aldehyde and the benzyl alcohol (a reduction product) are wrong, and the extra-methyl skeleton is not derivable from benzaldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. All five SMILES parse; opt0 is the true dimethyl acetal; distractors differ by skeleton/oxidation state. Keyed answer right."},{"q":"Acetophenone is heated with aniline (PhNH2) and a trace of acid, with water removed. The product shown is CC(=Nc1ccccc1)c1ccccc1. This product is best classified as which of the following?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["A nitrile","An acetal","An enamine","An imine (Schiff base)","A hemiaminal (carbinolamine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A primary amine condensing with a ketone gives an imine (C=N, Schiff base). The stated product C14H13N has a C=N and is the correct condensation product. Enamines come from secondary amines; the carbinolamine is the pre-dehydration intermediate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Stated product formula C14H13N verified (ketone + aniline - H2O), contains C=N; keyed answer right."},{"q":"To convert cyclohexanone into the enamine 1-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)pyrrolidine, which amine reagent (with acid catalysis and water removal) is required?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["A tertiary amine (triethylamine)","Hydroxylamine (NH2OH)","Ammonia (NH3)","A primary amine (methylamine)","A secondary amine (pyrrolidine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Enamines form only from secondary amines: the carbinolamine has no N-H left to lose, so elimination goes toward the alpha carbon to give C=C-N. Primary amines and ammonia give imines, tertiary amines cannot condense, and hydroxylamine gives an oxime.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Classic enamine-requires-secondary-amine recognition; keyed answer right, in scope."},{"q":"Acetone is treated with KCN followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the product (the cyanohydrin)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC(C)C#N","CC(C)(O)C#N","CC(=O)C#N","CC(O)CC#N","CC(C)(O)C=N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon and the oxygen is protonated on workup, giving the cyanohydrin (OH and C#N on the same carbon), C4H7NO. The nitrile lacking OH, the wrong-regiochemistry isomer, the acyl cyanide, and the imine-type carbon are all incorrect.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. opt0 cyanohydrin formula C4H7NO verified (acetone + HCN); all options valid SMILES; keyed answer right."},{"q":"Cyanohydrin formation (and nucleophilic addition to carbonyls generally) is fastest for the least sterically hindered, most electrophilic carbonyl. Among the carbonyl compounds shown as choices, which reacts FASTEST with HCN?","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC=O (acetaldehyde)","O=C1CCCCC1 (cyclohexanone)","CC(C)=O (acetone)","O=C(c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1 (benzophenone)","C=O (formaldehyde)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Reactivity toward nucleophilic addition falls as alkyl/aryl substitution rises (more steric bulk and more electron donation to the carbonyl). Formaldehyde, with two H's, is the most reactive; benzophenone, with two bulky electron-donating phenyls, is the least.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Standard sterics+electronics reactivity ranking; formaldehyde unambiguously fastest. Stem draws benzophenone (the slowest), slightly atypical but each molecule is labeled in the options; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The compound 4-hydroxybutanal (OCCCC=O) exists in equilibrium with a cyclic species formed when the hydroxyl oxygen adds intramolecularly to the carbonyl. The cyclic form OC1CCCO1 is best described as which of the following?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCO","opts":["An enol ether","A cyclic ether (no new OH)","A cyclic acetal","A cyclic hemiacetal (lactol)","A lactone (cyclic ester)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Intramolecular addition of OH to the aldehyde gives a hemiacetal: one ring-O (OR) and one OH on the former carbonyl carbon, here a 5-membered ring (lactol), the same equilibrium that cyclizes sugars. A full acetal needs a second alkoxy group; a lactone requires oxidation.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Open (C4H8O2) and cyclic OC1CCCO1 (C4H8O2) are isomers; ring is 5-membered with a hemiacetal carbon (one ring-O + one OH). Lactol classification right."},{"q":"A chemist wants to reduce the ketone of a keto-aldehyde to an alcohol while leaving the aldehyde untouched. They first convert the aldehyde to a 1,3-dioxolane with ethylene glycol/TsOH, run the reduction, then treat with aqueous acid. Why is the acetal an effective protecting group for this sequence?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetals are stable to base and nucleophiles/hydride but are hydrolyzed back to the carbonyl by aqueous acid","Acetals spontaneously revert to the carbonyl on standing, requiring no deprotection step","Acetals are stable to acid but are cleaved by base, so basic workup restores the carbonyl","Acetals are inert under all conditions and must be removed by oxidation","Acetals are reduced by hydride, regenerating the aldehyde directly"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acetal formation/hydrolysis are acid-catalyzed equilibria. With no carbonyl present, acetals are inert to hydride, base, and nucleophiles (the protection), and are removed by aqueous acid that reverses the equilibrium to carbonyl plus diol. They are not base-cleaved or hydride-reduced.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","issue":"Correct. Tests the stability-profile rationale of acetal protecting groups (recognition-level, not full mechanism); keyed answer right, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with excess ethylene glycol (HOCH2CH2OH) and a catalytic amount of p-toluenesulfonic acid (TsOH), with water removed azeotropically. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2(SCCS2)CC1","OC1(O)CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1.OCCO","OC1(OCCO)CCCCC1","C1CCC2(OCCO2)CC1"],"correct":4,"why":"A ketone plus a 1,2-diol under acid catalysis with water removal gives a cyclic acetal (a ketal): both OH groups of ethylene glycol add to the carbonyl carbon, and the spiro five-membered 1,3-dioxolane ring forms with loss of water. Removing water azeotropically (Dean-Stark) drives the equilibrium fully to this cyclic acetal. The open hemiketal (one OCH2CH2OH and one OH on the ring carbon) is only an intermediate; the gem-diol hydrate needs water, not glycol; the dithiolane would require ethanedithiol rather than the diol; and the unreacted ketone plus glycol ignores the acid and the water removal.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; cyclic acetal correctly keyed at index 0; distractors (hemiketal, hydrate, dithiolane, no-reaction) are plausible-but-wrong. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde reacts with methylamine (CH3NH2) under mildly acidic conditions to form an imine. Before the final C=N product forms, the reaction passes through a key neutral tetrahedral intermediate. Which species is that intermediate (the carbinolamine/hemiaminal) that then loses water?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CN=C(O)c1ccccc1","OC(NC)c1ccccc1","CN=Cc1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1","N=Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Methylamine adds to the carbonyl carbon of benzaldehyde, and proton transfer gives the carbinolamine (hemiaminal): a tetrahedral carbon bearing OH, NHCH3, phenyl and H. Acid then protonates the OH, water leaves, and loss of the N-H proton gives the imine. The N-methyl imine (N-benzylidenemethylamine) is the final product, not the intermediate; N-methylbenzylamine is a reduction product; the iminol (C=N bearing an OH, an amide tautomer) has the wrong oxidation level; and the unsubstituted imine lacks the methyl that methylamine brings.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; the carbinolamine (sp3 C bearing OH and NHCH3) is correctly keyed at index 0. The 'imine as distractor' (B) is the most tempting trap; stem unambiguously asks for the tetrahedral intermediate. Visual structure-ID, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is heated with the secondary amine pyrrolidine and a trace of acid, with water removed. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1.C1CCNC1","OC1(CCCCC1)N1CCCC1","C1CCC(CC1)=NC","C1CCC(=CC1)N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"A secondary amine such as pyrrolidine has only one N-H, so after it adds to cyclohexanone and forms the carbinolamine it cannot lose a second proton from nitrogen to become a neutral imine; instead the iminium loses a proton from the alpha carbon, giving the enamine 1-(cyclohex-1-en-1-yl)pyrrolidine, with the C=C in the ring next to nitrogen. The carbinolamine is the intermediate, not the product; 1-cyclohexylpyrrolidine is a saturated amine that would require a reducing agent; the N-methyl imine uses the wrong amine and the wrong product class; and the unreacted mixture ignores the acid and the water removal.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified option A has N bonded to the sp2 alkene carbon (true N-C=C enamine, 1-pyrrolidinocyclohexene). Correctly keyed at index 0; distractors are carbinolamine, saturated amine, wrong-class imine, no-reaction."},{"q":"Acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) is treated with NaCN followed by aqueous acid workup (equivalently, HCN/KCN). Which structure is the cyanohydrin product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)(C#N)c1ccccc1","OC(C#N)c1ccccc1","CC(O)Cc1ccccc1","COC(C#N)c1ccccc1","CC(C#N)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon of acetophenone, and protonation of the alkoxide on workup gives the cyanohydrin 2-hydroxy-2-phenylpropanenitrile: one carbon bearing OH, CN, CH3 and phenyl. That carbon becomes a stereocenter, formed as a racemate because cyanide attacks either face equally. Mandelonitrile is the cyanohydrin of benzaldehyde and lacks the methyl; the methoxy nitrile is an ether, not an alcohol; 2-phenylpropanenitrile lacks the OH; and 1-phenylpropan-2-ol has no nitrile at all.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; cyanohydrin (C bearing OH, CN, CH3, Ph) correctly keyed at index 0. Distractors differ by one functional group each. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is condensed with pyrrolidine (acid catalyst, water removed) to form an enamine. Which regiochemical product predominates?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CN=C1CCCCC1C","OC1(C)CCCCC1N1CCCC1","CC1=C(N2CCCC2)CCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1N1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"With an unsymmetrical ketone, enamine formation favors the less substituted double bond. Putting the C=C toward the methylated carbon would force the methyl and the bulky pyrrolidine nitrogen into the same plane with severe allylic (A-1,3) strain, so that tetrasubstituted enamine is disfavored. The major product is the enamine with the double bond on the unsubstituted side, 1-(6-methylcyclohex-1-en-1-yl)pyrrolidine, in which the methyl-bearing carbon stays sp3. The remaining structures are a saturated amine (which would need a reducing agent), an amino alcohol with the OH and the nitrogen on different carbons (not a true carbinolamine), and an N-methyl imine (wrong amine, wrong product class).","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified with RDKit: in option A the C=C is between the former carbonyl C (bearing N) and the non-methyl alpha carbon (away from methyl = less-substituted enamine, correct), while option B places the C=C on the methyl-bearing carbon (more-substituted, disfavored). Correctly keyed at index 0. Standard enamine regioselectivity, in Orgo II scope."},{"q":"p-Anisaldehyde (4-methoxybenzaldehyde) is stirred with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of anhydrous HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(O)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(OC)c1ccc(OC)cc1","O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","COC(O)c1ccc(OC)cc1","COCc1ccc(OC)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"An aldehyde with excess alcohol and anhydrous acid goes all the way to the acetal: two methanol molecules add and the carbonyl oxygen leaves as water, giving the dimethyl acetal of 4-methoxybenzaldehyde, a benzylic carbon bearing two OCH3 groups. The hemiacetal (one OCH3 and one OH) is only an intermediate and is not isolated under excess methanol and acid; the gem-diol hydrate needs water; 4-methoxybenzyl methyl ether would require a reduction; and the unreacted aldehyde ignores the conditions. The methoxy group on the ring is a spectator and is unchanged.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Dimethyl acetal correctly keyed at index 0; the aryl methoxy is correctly left as a spectator across all options. Distractors (hemiacetal, benzylic ether, hydrate, no-reaction) are plausible. Single concept, DAT depth."},{"q":"A chemist must perform a Grignard reaction (CH3MgBr) on the aldehyde of the keto-aldehyde shown while leaving the ketone untouched is not possible directly, so a protecting-group strategy is needed. Which sequence correctly protects the more reactive carbonyl, then later restores it?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acetals require base to form and base to hydrolyze, so use NaOH for both protection and deprotection","Convert the aldehyde to a cyclic acetal (HOCH2CH2OH/H+), run the desired chemistry, then hydrolyze the acetal back to the aldehyde with aqueous acid","Protect the carbonyl as a cyanohydrin, which is stable to strong base and aqueous acid indefinitely","Reduce the aldehyde to an alcohol with NaBH4, then oxidize it back with H+ alone","Convert the aldehyde to an enamine, which is stable to aqueous acid and never needs to be removed"],"correct":1,"why":"Acetals are the standard carbonyl protecting group: they form under acid with a diol (water removed) and are stable to base, nucleophiles, and Grignard/hydride reagents, yet are cleanly removed by aqueous acid to regenerate the carbonyl. The aldehyde, being more electrophilic, is protected selectively. Enamines and cyanohydrins are acid-labile (hydrolyze back), reduction is not a true protect/restore cycle, and acetal chemistry is acid-catalyzed, not base.","difficulty":2,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem phrasing is slightly awkward ('...untouched is not possible directly') but parseable and unambiguous. Reagent-selection text item; molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid keto-aldehyde). Acetal protect/deprotect correctly keyed at index 0; the four distractors are each chemically false. In DAT scope (recognition of acetal as protecting group, no full mechanism)."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanal (shown) is dissolved in a trace of acid. Rather than reacting intermolecularly, it cyclizes. Which structure is the predominant product at equilibrium?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O1CCCCC1","OCCCCCO","OC1CCCO1","OC1CCCCO1","O=CCCCCO"],"correct":3,"why":"The hydroxyl at C5 adds intramolecularly to the aldehyde carbon, forming a cyclic hemiacetal. Counting atoms, the ring oxygen plus five carbons gives a six-membered ring, the favored size, so the product is 2-hydroxytetrahydropyran, with the OH and the ring oxygen on the same carbon, exactly like the ring form of glucose. The five-membered hemiacetal has the wrong atom count for this chain; tetrahydropyran has no OH at all and would require a reduction; pentane-1,5-diol is also a reduction product; and the open-chain hydroxy aldehyde is only the minor component at equilibrium.","difficulty":3,"group":"carbonyl-acetal-imine","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified with RDKit: option A is a 6-membered ring (2-hydroxytetrahydropyran), matching the intramolecular hemiacetal of 5-hydroxypentanal (C1...C5-O closes 6 atoms); option B is a 5-membered ring (wrong size). Correctly keyed at index 0. Distractors are wrong ring size, missing anomeric OH, diol, and starting material. In DAT scope."},{"q":"What is the major product of the following Wittig reaction? Benzaldehyde is treated with the non-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH3 (generated from ethyltriphenylphosphonium bromide and n-BuLi).","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["C/C=C/c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A non-stabilized alkyl ylide such as ethylidenetriphenylphosphorane reacts with an aldehyde under kinetic control to give mainly the Z (cis) alkene. The carbonyl carbon of benzaldehyde and the ylide carbon become the new C=C, so the product is (Z)-1-phenylpropene, with the phenyl and the methyl on the same side. The (E)-isomer is the classic trap: that is the geometry a stabilized ylide would favor. Styrene, allylbenzene and ethylbenzene have the wrong carbon count or connectivity.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. Substrate and all 5 option SMILES parse; Z geometry correctly keyed (index 0). Classic non-stabilized Wittig stereochemistry, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexanone reacts with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et (a carbethoxymethylene phosphorane).","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)/C=C/C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1=CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The Wittig replaces the ketone C=O with a C=C to the ylide carbon bearing CO2Et, giving the exocyclic alkene ethyl 2-cyclohexylideneacetate. Methylenecyclohexane drops the ester (wrong ylide); the trans-vinyl, saturated, and endocyclic esters are wrong skeletons/positions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; exocyclic ester alkene correctly keyed (index 0). Standard stabilized-ylide predict-product, in scope."},{"q":"What is the major organic product when acetophenone is treated with methylenetriphenylphosphorane (Ph3P=CH2)?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["C=CCc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","C/C=C/c1ccccc1","C=C(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ph3P=CH2 delivers a CH2 that replaces the carbonyl oxygen, converting C=O into terminal C=CH2. Acetophenone (Ph-CO-CH3) becomes 2-phenylpropene (alpha-methylstyrene). The 1-phenylpropene isomers and cumene are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; alpha-methylstyrene correctly keyed (index 0). Methylenation of a ketone, in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product of the reaction of cyclohexanecarbaldehyde with the ylide Ph3P=CH2.","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCCC1","opts":["C=CC1CCCCC1","CCC1CCCCC1","CC=C1CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","C=CCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The aldehyde carbon (the CHO on the ring) becomes the alkene carbon; Ph3P=CH2 supplies the terminal =CH2, giving vinylcyclohexane. Methylenecyclohexane and ethylidenecyclohexane traps put the new double bond on a ring carbon instead of the former aldehyde carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; vinylcyclohexane correctly keyed (index 0). Standard methylenation of an aldehyde, in scope."},{"q":"What is the major product when p-anisaldehyde (4-methoxybenzaldehyde) reacts with the semi-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHC6H5 (benzylidenetriphenylphosphorane)?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["COc1ccc(/C=C\\c2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(Cc2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(C=C)cc1","COc1ccc(CCc2ccccc2)cc1","COc1ccc(/C=C/c2ccccc2)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A semi-stabilized (benzylic) ylide adds to the aldehyde to form a stilbene, and the thermodynamically preferred E (trans) alkene predominates. The new double bond joins the former aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, so the product is (E)-4-methoxystilbene, with the two aryl rings on opposite sides of the C=C. The (Z)-stilbene is the geometry trap; 4-methoxybibenzyl is the saturated skeleton with no alkene; and the diarylmethane and 4-methoxystyrene have the wrong carbon count between or on the rings.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; E-stilbene correctly keyed (index 0). Semi-stabilized ylide E-preference is standard textbook, in scope."},{"q":"Predict the major product of the Wittig reaction between 3-pentanone (diethyl ketone) and Ph3P=CH2.","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["CC=C(C)CC","CC/C=C/CC","C=CC(CC)CC","C=C(CC)CC","CCC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The central carbonyl carbon of 3-pentanone becomes a C=CH2 when Ph3P=CH2 swaps out the oxygen, giving 2-ethyl-1-butene. The 3-hexene and internal methylpentenes are constitutional-isomer traps moving the double bond into the chain rather than to the new exocyclic CH2.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; 2-ethyl-1-butene correctly keyed (index 0). Methylenation of a symmetric ketone, in scope."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde is treated with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2CH3. Predict the major product, including alkene geometry.","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)/C=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","COC(=O)/C=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","COC(=O)/C=C\\C=C/c1ccccc1","C=CC=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A stabilized ester ylide gives the E (trans) alkene, so the new double bond forms between the former aldehyde carbon and the ylide carbon with the ester and the rest of the chain trans. The existing E cinnamyl double bond is untouched, so the product is methyl (2E,4E)-5-phenylpenta-2,4-dienoate. The (2Z,4Z) ester has the wrong geometry at both alkenes; the saturated ester has lost both double bonds; the free dienoic acid would need an acid ylide rather than the methyl ester; and the hydrocarbon diene has no ester at all.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Minor: opt1 is actually 2Z,4Z per RDKit (stem calls it the 2Z trap) but it remains a valid wrong-geometry distractor; correct answer (index 0, 2E,4E) unaffected. Stabilized-ylide regio+geometry recognition, within DAT predict-product scope."},{"q":"What is the major product when 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone is treated with Ph3P=CH2?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","CC1=CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","C=CC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","C=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ph3P=CH2 converts the ring carbonyl into an exocyclic =CH2, giving 4-tert-butylmethylenecyclohexane. The ethylidene option uses the wrong ylide, the endocyclic methylcyclohexene moves the double bond into the ring, and the tertiary alcohol is a non-Wittig (organometallic addition) product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; exocyclic methylenecyclohexane correctly keyed (index 0). Standard ketone methylenation, in scope."},{"q":"The ketone shown is converted into methylenecyclohexane (a terminal alkene, C=CH2 replacing the C=O). Which reagent accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Ph3P=CH2 (methylenetriphenylphosphorane)","H2, Pd/C","PCC in CH2Cl2","CH3MgBr, then H3O+","NaBH4, then H3O+"],"correct":0,"why":"The Wittig reaction converts a C=O into a C=C: the unstabilized methylene ylide Ph3P=CH2 adds to cyclohexanone, and the oxaphosphetane collapses to methylenecyclohexane plus triphenylphosphine oxide. Methylmagnesium bromide gives 1-methylcyclohexanol (a tertiary alcohol, not an alkene); NaBH4 gives cyclohexanol; PCC is an oxidant and does nothing useful to a ketone; and H2 over Pd/C reduces alkenes rather than creating them.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid (cyclohexanone). Reagent-selection Wittig, single concept, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (shown) is to be converted to (E)-stilbene, PhCH=CHPh, with high E-selectivity. Which reagent is best suited?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Zn(Hg), HCl (Clemmensen)","PhLi, then H3O+","LiAlH4, then H3O+","An unstabilized ylide, Ph3P=CH2","A resonance-stabilized ylide, Ph3P=CHPh (benzylidene phosphorane)"],"correct":4,"why":"The benzylidene ylide Ph3P=CHPh is conjugatively (resonance) stabilized by the phenyl and is the only reagent that builds the PhCH=CHPh skeleton, giving predominantly the E (trans) stilbene. Ph3P=CH2 lacks the second phenyl; the others are addition/reduction reagents that do not form the C=C.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct, keep. Minor terminology nuance: benzylidene ylide is more precisely semi-stabilized, but it IS resonance-stabilized by phenyl and gives E, so option 0 is the single defensible answer."},{"q":"The alkene shown, (E)-2-pentene, is the target of a Wittig disconnection. Which carbonyl + ylide pair would form it (the new double bond is between the two central alkene carbons)?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CH3CH2CHO + CH3MgBr","CH3CH2CH2CHO + Ph3P=O","CH3CHO + Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 (propylidene ylide)","CH3CH2CH2CH2CHO + Ph3P=CH2","Acetone + Ph3P=CHCH3"],"correct":2,"why":"Disconnect (E)-2-pentene at its C2=C3 double bond: one fragment is CH3CH= (two carbons) and the other is =CHCH2CH3 (three carbons). One partner supplies the carbonyl carbon and the other the ylide carbon, so acetaldehyde plus the propylidene ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 rebuilds exactly this skeleton (propanal plus an ethylidene ylide would also work in principle, but that pair is not offered). Pentanal plus the methylene ylide gives 1-hexene; acetone plus the ethylidene ylide gives 2-methyl-2-butene; propanal with a Grignard gives an alcohol, not an alkene; and triphenylphosphine oxide is the Wittig byproduct, not a reagent.","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Connectivity correct, one defensible answer (only option 0 gives the 2-pentene skeleton). Caveat: propylidene is non-stabilized so it would actually favor Z, mildly inconsistent with the (E) label in the stem; but the question keys on the disconnection/skeleton, so it stays unambiguous. Keep."},{"q":"Ethyl (E)-cinnamate, the conjugated ester-alkene shown, is the product of a Wittig reaction on benzaldehyde. The reaction proceeds with high E-selectivity. What type of ylide was used, and why is the geometry E?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A semi-stabilized ylide, which gives only the Z alkene","A stabilized ylide, which favors the cis (Z) alkene","A stabilized ylide (ester-conjugated), which favors the trans (E) alkene","A non-stabilized ylide, which favors the cis (Z) alkene","A non-stabilized ylide, which favors the trans (E) alkene"],"correct":2,"why":"The ylide carbon bears an ester (EWG), so it is a stabilized ylide; stabilized ylides give predominantly the E (trans) alkene, matching ethyl (E)-cinnamate. The stabilized=E pairing is uniquely correct.","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid (ethyl E-cinnamate). Two-concept (ylide type + stereochemistry rule), one defensible answer."},{"q":"The product shown is alpha-methylstyrene (2-phenylpropene). Which carbonyl/ylide combination gives it via a Wittig reaction?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzaldehyde (PhCHO) + Ph3P=CHCH3","Propiophenone (PhCOCH2CH3) + Ph3P=CH2","Acetophenone + Ph3P=O","Acetone + Ph3P=CHPh","Acetophenone (PhCOCH3) + Ph3P=CH2"],"correct":4,"why":"The =CH2 comes from Ph3P=CH2 and the C(CH3)(Ph)= carbon from the carbonyl, requiring acetophenone. Benzaldehyde + ethylidene gives beta-methylstyrene (different connectivity); acetone + benzylidene gives PhCH=C(CH3)2; propiophenone gives a different alkene; Ph3P=O is a byproduct.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid. Disconnection uniquely fixes acetophenone + Ph3P=CH2; distractors are genuine regiochemistry/skeleton variants. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclopentanone (shown) is treated with Ph3P=CHCH3 to give ethylidenecyclopentane plus triphenylphosphine oxide. How is this reaction best classified?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An aldol condensation","A Wittig olefination (carbonyl-to-alkene via a phosphorus ylide)","An E2 elimination","A Grignard addition","A nucleophilic acyl substitution"],"correct":1,"why":"A phosphorus ylide + carbonyl forming C=C and expelling Ph3P=O is the defining Wittig signature. Aldol gives a beta-hydroxy carbonyl, acyl substitution needs a leaving group on the carbonyl, Grignard gives an alcohol, E2 needs a leaving group on a saturated substrate.","difficulty":1,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Reaction-type classification, one defensible answer. q_smiles valid (cyclopentanone)."},{"q":"Propanal (shown) is treated with the non-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH2CH3 (a butylidene ylide) under salt-free conditions. Which statement about the product alkene is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The product is 3-heptene, formed predominantly as the E (trans) isomer","No reaction occurs because aldehydes are unreactive toward ylides","The product is heptanal, with no new C=C","The product is 3-heptene, formed predominantly as the Z (cis) isomer","The product is 2-heptanone, by nucleophilic addition"],"correct":3,"why":"Joining propanal's carbonyl carbon (CH3CH2CH=) to the butylidene ylide carbon (=CHCH2CH2CH3) gives CH3CH2CH=CHCH2CH2CH3 (3-heptene). Non-stabilized ylides under salt-free conditions give predominantly the Z (cis) alkene. Other options misstate the chemistry.","difficulty":2,"group":"wittig","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Connectivity verified (7 carbons, 3-heptene). Non-stabilized + salt-free -> Z is standard textbook. Two-concept (connectivity + Z-selectivity), one defensible answer. q_smiles valid (propanal)."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with the stabilized phosphorus ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et (carbethoxymethylenetriphenylphosphorane). Which structure is the major Wittig product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1=CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCC(=O)CC1","CCOC(=O)C=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Wittig replaces the carbonyl C=O with a C=C to the ylide carbon at the original carbonyl carbon, giving the exocyclic alkene ethyl 2-cyclohexylideneacetate. Distractors move the alkene into the ring (endocyclic), saturate the linkage, shrink the ring, or add a spurious ketone.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; exocyclic alkene at carbonyl C is the right product; distractors are clean regio/skeleton variants. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde reacts with the NON-stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCH2CH3 (an unstabilized propylidene ylide) under salt-free conditions. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCC=Cc1ccccc1","C/C=C\\Cc1ccccc1","CC/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC/C=C\\c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Non-stabilized ylides under salt-free conditions give predominantly the Z (cis) alkene, because the kinetically formed cis-oxaphosphetane collapses before it can equilibrate. The new C=C joins the aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, so benzaldehyde plus the propylidene ylide gives (Z)-1-phenyl-1-butene, with the phenyl and the ethyl on the same side. The (E)-isomer is the classic trap (that is what a stabilized ylide favors); the 1-phenyl-2-butene isomer has the double bond in the wrong position; propylbenzene has no alkene; and the structure drawn without geometry does not answer the question.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is the Z isomer, option 1 the E. Standard non-stabilized-ylide stereochemistry. In scope."},{"q":"In the Wittig reaction of phenylacetaldehyde (drawn) with a phosphorus ylide, the first carbon-carbon bond-forming step is the nucleophilic addition of the ylide. Which single curved arrow correctly represents this first bond formation?","q_smiles":"O=CCc1ccccc1","opts":["From the phosphorus to the carbonyl carbon","From the aldehyde C-H bond to the ylide carbon","From the carbanionic ylide carbon to the carbonyl carbon of the aldehyde","From the C=O pi bond to the phosphorus atom","From the carbonyl oxygen lone pair to the ylide carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The ylide carbon is the nucleophile; the first bond-forming arrow goes from that carbanionic carbon to the electrophilic carbonyl carbon, forming the betaine/oxaphosphetane C-C bond. Phosphorus binds oxygen only later, and the carbonyl carbon (not P) is the first electrophile attacked.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single-arrow identification, which the scope rules explicitly permit; not a full mechanism. q_smiles valid. Exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Butanone (methyl ethyl ketone, drawn) is treated with methylenetriphenylphosphorane, Ph3P=CH2. Which is the Wittig product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(C)C","C=C(C)CC","C/C=C(\\C)CC","C/C=C/CC","C=CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methylenation converts C=O into a terminal =CH2 at the original carbonyl carbon, giving 2-methyl-1-butene. No E/Z issue arises (the new alkene carbon bears two H). Distractors add a carbon, reduce, or misplace the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Option 0 = 2-methyl-1-butene, exactly the methylenation product. SMILES valid. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclopentanecarbaldehyde (drawn) reacts with the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Me. Which product, with correct alkene geometry, predominates?","q_smiles":"O=CC1CCCC1","opts":["COC(=O)/C=C\\C1CCCC1","COC(=O)/C=C/C1CCCC1","COC(=O)C=C1CCCC1","COC(=O)CCC1CCCC1","COC(=O)CC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A stabilized (ester) ylide gives the thermodynamically favored E (trans) alkene. The new double bond joins the former aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, so cyclopentanecarbaldehyde plus Ph3P=CHCO2Me gives methyl (E)-3-cyclopentylacrylate, with the ester and the cyclopentyl ring on opposite sides. The (Z)-ester is the geometry trap; the exocyclic alkylidene ester would come from cyclopentanone, not the aldehyde; and the two saturated esters have no double bond and the wrong chain lengths.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is E, option 1 Z. Stabilized-ylide E-selectivity is standard. In scope (Wittig + geometry recognition)."},{"q":"A chemist wants to convert 2-pentanone (drawn) into 2-methyl-1-pentene (terminal exocyclic-type alkene at the carbonyl carbon). Which reagent accomplishes this in one step?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["Ph3P=CHCO2Et (stabilized ester ylide)","Ph3P=CH2 (methylenetriphenylphosphorane)","NaBH4, then H2SO4 heat","PCC in CH2Cl2","Ph3P=CHCH3 (ethylidene ylide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"To install a terminal =CH2 on the original carbonyl carbon (2-methyl-1-pentene), the ylide must carry only CH2: Ph3P=CH2. An ethylidene ylide adds an extra carbon, the ester ylide installs CO2Et, and reduction/oxidation reagents do not olefinate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Target 2-methyl-1-pentene = methylenation of 2-pentanone; Ph3P=CH2 is the unique one-step reagent. q_smiles valid. In scope."},{"q":"Butanal (drawn) is treated with the SEMI-stabilized benzylidene ylide Ph3P=CHC6H5 under standard (lithium-salt-containing) conditions. Which alkene is the major geometric isomer expected?","q_smiles":"CCCC=O","opts":["CCC/C=C\\c1ccccc1","CCCCc1ccccc1","CCC/C=C/c1ccccc1","CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","CCC/C=C/Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylidene ylides are semi-stabilized (carbanion conjugated into the ring), so they favor the E (trans) alkene, especially with lithium salts present. The new C=C links the aldehyde carbon to the ylide carbon, giving predominantly (E)-1-phenyl-1-pentene. Z is minor.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit confirms option 0 is E. Semi-stabilized benzylidene E-preference is the standard textbook teaching; real selectivity is modest but the keyed answer is the defensible major isomer. Two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"The keto-aldehyde 5-oxohexanal (drawn) is treated with exactly one equivalent of the stabilized ylide Ph3P=CHCO2Et. Predict the major product, accounting for which carbonyl reacts AND the alkene geometry.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)/C=C\\CCCC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)/C=C/CCCC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)/C=C/CCC/C=C/C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)/C=C(\\C)CCCC=O","CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Two layers: chemoselectivity and geometry. The more electrophilic, less hindered aldehyde reacts preferentially over the ketone with a limited amount of a stabilized ylide, leaving the methyl ketone intact; the stabilized ylide gives the E (trans) acrylate. Distractors react at the ketone, give Z, do a double Wittig, or form no alkene.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"wittig","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit: mono-Wittig products (0,1,2) are C10H16O3, double (3) is C13H20O4. Option 0 = E-acrylate at the aldehyde, ketone intact. Two layered concepts matched to difficulty 3; single addition event, in scope."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde is treated with dilute aqueous NaOH followed by warming. What is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["C/C=C/C=O","O=CCCO","CC(O)CC=O","CC=CC=O","CCC=CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two acetaldehyde molecules undergo aldol addition (enolate adds to the second carbonyl) to give 3-hydroxybutanal, which dehydrates on warming to the conjugated (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde). The trans-conjugated isomer is the major product. All 5 options unique and parseable.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Keyed (E)-but-2-enal is the correct conjugated condensation product; single concept, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Propanal is treated with a catalytic amount of NaOH at low temperature (no heating). What is the major product of this aldol reaction?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCCC(O)C=O","CCC(O)C(C)CO","CCC(O)C(C)C=O","CC=C(C)C=O","CCC(O)CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Propanal has a single alpha-carbon (the CH2). Its enolate adds to a second propanal carbonyl giving the aldol ADDITION product 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal (no heat = no dehydration). Distractors: the 2-methylpent-2-enal condensation product, a wrong-connectivity adduct (enolate at non-alpha methyl), and an over-reduced diol. All valid, unique.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single alpha center so no regiochemical ambiguity; addition product correctly keyed; in scope."},{"q":"Acetone is treated with base and then heated to drive off water. What is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC=C(C)C(C)=O","CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)(C)O","CC(=O)C=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone self-aldol gives diacetone alcohol (4-hydroxy-4-methylpentan-2-one) which dehydrates on heating to mesityl oxide, 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one. Distractors: non-dehydrated beta-hydroxy ketone, isomeric/unrelated diketones. All valid and unique.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Mesityl oxide correctly keyed; classic textbook reaction, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with acetone and aqueous NaOH, then warmed. Benzaldehyde has no alpha-hydrogens. What is the major crossed-aldol condensation product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1CC(C)=O","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)C","O=C(C)CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=C(C)C=Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Only acetone can enolize; its enolate adds to benzaldehyde and dehydrates to (E)-4-phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone), fully conjugated with ring and carbonyl. Distractors: non-dehydrated aldol, wrong-partner regiochemistry, and a reduced allylic alcohol. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Crossed aldol (one partner lacks alpha-H) correctly resolved to benzalacetone; regio+stereo two-concept, in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with benzaldehyde and NaOH, then warmed (Claisen-Schmidt conditions). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)C=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)CCc1ccccc1","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Benzaldehyde has no alpha-H, so acetophenone's methyl is deprotonated; the enolate adds to benzaldehyde and dehydrates to (E)-chalcone, (E)-1,3-diphenylprop-2-en-1-one (C15H12O). Distractors: non-dehydrated aldol, over-reduced 1,3-diphenylpropan-1-one, a reduced-ketone alcohol, and the unspecified-geometry chalcone. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic Claisen-Schmidt to (E)-chalcone correctly keyed; in scope."},{"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione is treated with aqueous NaOH (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1C","CC1CCC(=O)C1=O","CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"An enolate at a terminal methyl attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly to close a favorable five-membered ring; dehydration gives conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one (C6H8O). Distractors: cyclopentane-1,2-dione (no aldol), a regioisomeric enone, a methyl 1,2-dione, and an extra-methyl ring. All valid, unique.","difficulty":3,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Verified 5-ring conjugated enone (3-methylcyclopent-2-enone) is the major product; ring-size + regiochemistry, in scope as a single-concept predict-product."},{"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione is treated with NaOH and warmed (intramolecular aldol condensation). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1=CC(=O)CCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Base removes a proton from one terminal methyl of heptane-2,6-dione, and that enolate attacks the other ketone to close a six-membered ring, the favored size; warming then dehydrates the beta-hydroxy ketone to the conjugated enone 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one, in which the C=C is conjugated with the C=O. The isomer with the double bond moved one position away is not conjugated and is less stable; cyclohexanone and the methylcyclopentenone have the wrong carbon count (the five-membered enone is what the shorter hexane-2,5-dione would give); and the methylcyclohexane-1,2-dione keeps both oxygens, which an aldol condensation does not.","difficulty":3,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Confirmed keyed structure is the conjugated 3-methylcyclohex-2-enone, opt1 is the non-conjugated regioisomer distractor; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with formaldehyde (HCHO) and a small amount of NaOH at room temperature. Formaldehyde has no alpha-hydrogens and is a very reactive electrophile. What is the major crossed-aldol product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1=CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1=CO","OCC1CCCCC1=O","OC1CCCCC1C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Only cyclohexanone enolizes (formaldehyde has no alpha-H). The enolate adds to the reactive formaldehyde carbon, installing a hydroxymethyl group alpha to the ketone, giving 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexan-1-one (C7H12O2); mild conditions stop at the beta-hydroxy ketone. Distractors: a dehydrated/enol form, an exocyclic enol, a wrong-connectivity aldehyde alcohol, and an over-reduced product. All valid, unique.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydroxymethylation alpha to the ketone correctly keyed as 2-(hydroxymethyl)cyclohexanone; mono-addition framing is clean, in scope."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with dilute aqueous NaOH, then warmed. After loss of water, the major product is crotonaldehyde (CH3CH=CHCHO). What type of reaction converts the β-hydroxy aldehyde intermediate into crotonaldehyde?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["Conjugate (1,4) addition","E1cb dehydration (aldol condensation)","Cannizzaro disproportionation","Retro-aldol cleavage","Nucleophilic acyl substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Aldol addition gives 3-hydroxybutanal; on warming, base removes the alpha-H to form an enolate and water is eliminated by an E1cb pathway, giving the conjugated enal crotonaldehyde. This dehydration is what makes it an aldol condensation. Distractors (Cannizzaro needs no alpha-H aldehyde, NAS needs a leaving group, 1,4-addition needs a preformed enone, retro-aldol breaks C-C) are all wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC=O valid; E1cb dehydration step correctly identified; single-concept reaction-type ID, in scope."},{"q":"Acetone (drawn) undergoes base-catalyzed self-aldol to give the β-hydroxy ketone diacetone alcohol; on heating with acid it loses water to give mesityl oxide, (CH3)2C=CHCOCH3. Which single reagent/condition set best accomplishes the overall conversion of acetone into mesityl oxide?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","LiAlH4 then H3O+","Dilute NaOH at low temperature only","Catalytic base for the addition, then acid and heat to dehydrate","O3 then Zn/H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Ketone aldol is reversible and stops at diacetone alcohol under mild base; driving it to the enone (mesityl oxide) requires dehydration pushed by acid and heat. LiAlH4/NaBH4 reduce, ozonolysis cleaves, and cold dilute base alone stops at the beta-hydroxy ketone. Only the base-then-acid/heat sequence completes the condensation.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC(C)=O valid; index 1 right; two-concept (addition + dehydration conditions), in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is combined with acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) under aqueous NaOH to give (E)-chalcone, C6H5COCH=CHC6H5, in high yield as a single crossed-aldol product. Why is this crossed aldol clean rather than a mixture?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["The reaction proceeds by a radical chain mechanism","Benzaldehyde has no α-hydrogen, so only acetophenone forms the enolate","Both partners form enolates equally","Benzaldehyde is the only enolate source","Acetophenone has no α-hydrogen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A clean crossed aldol needs one partner that cannot form an enolate. Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogen, so it can only be the electrophile; acetophenone, which does have alpha hydrogens, is the only enolate source. That single enolate adds to benzaldehyde and dehydrates to (E)-chalcone. The reverse claims are false: acetophenone does have alpha hydrogens, benzaldehyde cannot be an enolate source, the two partners are not equivalent, and no radical chain is involved.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. O=Cc1ccccc1 valid; only one defensible answer (option 1 is a true-sounding but false trap); in scope."},{"q":"The symmetric dienone shown, (1E,4E)-1,5-diphenylpenta-1,4-dien-3-one (dibenzylideneacetone), is a classic crossed-aldol condensation product. Which pair of starting materials and conditions makes it?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["1 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetone, NaOH","2 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetone, NaOH","2 equiv acetophenone + 1 equiv formaldehyde, NaOH","2 equiv benzaldehyde + 1 equiv acetaldehyde, NaOH","1 equiv benzaldehyde + 2 equiv acetone, H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Acetone has alpha hydrogens on both methyl groups, so under NaOH each side in turn forms an enolate, adds to a benzaldehyde (which has no alpha hydrogen and serves only as the electrophile), and dehydrates. Two condensations give the symmetric, doubly conjugated dibenzylideneacetone, so the recipe is two equivalents of benzaldehyde to one of acetone with base. One equivalent of benzaldehyde stops at the mono-condensation product (benzalacetone); acetophenone with formaldehyde, or benzaldehyde with acetaldehyde, builds a different skeleton; and excess acetone under sulfuric acid favors acetone self-condensation rather than the double crossed product.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. DBA SMILES valid (E,E geometry parses); stoichiometry 2:1 correct; two-concept (reactivity + stoichiometry), in scope."},{"q":"Heptane-2,6-dione (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH and warmed. The major product is 3-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-one. This transformation is best classified as which kind of reaction?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","opts":["Robinson annulation","Cannizzaro reaction","Intramolecular aldol condensation","Intermolecular crossed aldol condensation","Claisen condensation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Both reacting centers are in the same molecule, so it is an intramolecular aldol condensation. Claisen needs an ester; Robinson needs a Michael donor/enone; Cannizzaro needs a non-enolizable aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Regiochemistry verified atom-by-atom (terminal-methyl enolate -> distal carbonyl -> 6-ring -> 3-methylcyclohex-2-enone); index 0 right; in scope."},{"q":"In a base-catalyzed crossed aldol, propanal (drawn) and acetone compete as the electrophilic carbonyl that accepts the enolate. Which carbonyl is the more reactive electrophile toward nucleophilic addition, and why?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["Propanal, because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and less stabilized than the ketone","They are equally reactive electrophiles","Acetone, because it is more sterically accessible","Acetone, because two alkyl groups make it more electrophilic","Propanal, because aldehydes have no α-hydrogens"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are more electrophilic than ketones. Propanal is the better acceptor because its carbonyl carbon carries only one alkyl group: it is less sterically hindered and less stabilized by electron donation than the carbonyl of acetone, whose two alkyl groups push electron density toward the partially positive carbon and crowd it. The claim that two alkyl groups make acetone more electrophilic has the effect backwards; acetone is not the more accessible carbonyl; the two are not equally reactive; and the statement that aldehydes have no alpha hydrogens is simply false for propanal and is unrelated to electrophilicity anyway.","difficulty":1,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CCC=O valid; aldehyde > ketone electrophilicity reasoning sound; only one defensible answer; in scope."},{"q":"The molecule drawn, 3-hydroxybutanal, is the direct product of a base-catalyzed aldol of a single simple carbonyl compound before any dehydration. Which starting carbonyl, used as both nucleophile and electrophile, gives this β-hydroxy aldehyde?","q_smiles":"CC(O)CC=O","opts":["Acetaldehyde (two equivalents)","Formaldehyde (two equivalents)","Acetone (two equivalents)","Benzaldehyde (two equivalents)","Propanal (two equivalents)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"3-Hydroxybutanal has four carbons and the formula C4H8O2, exactly two acetaldehyde units (C2H4O each) joined with no loss of water. The enolate of one acetaldehyde (a CH2CHO carbanion) attacks the carbonyl of a second, placing the new OH beta to the aldehyde; this is the self-aldol of acetaldehyde. Acetone self-aldol gives a six-carbon hydroxy ketone, propanal gives a six-carbon aldehyde with an alpha-methyl, and formaldehyde and benzaldehyde have no alpha hydrogens and cannot self-condense at all.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. CC(O)CC=O valid; carbon-count to acetaldehyde verified; arguably difficulty 1 (single-concept recognition) but 2 is acceptable; in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is treated with excess formaldehyde (HCHO) and dilute NaOH. Formaldehyde is chosen as the second partner specifically to control the crossed aldol. What property of formaldehyde makes it useful here?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["It is the strongest enolate-forming carbonyl available","It has no α-hydrogens, so it cannot enolize and serves only as the electrophile","It is non-electrophilic and only acts as a base","It prevents any dehydration from occurring","It reduces cyclohexanone to the alcohol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Formaldehyde has no alpha hydrogens, so it cannot form an enolate and can act only as the electrophile; it is also an unhindered, highly reactive carbonyl acceptor. The cyclohexanone enolate therefore adds to formaldehyde cleanly (hydroxymethylation) instead of cyclohexanone condensing with itself. Formaldehyde cannot be an enolate source, it is strongly electrophilic rather than a base, it does not block dehydration, and it is not a reducing agent.","difficulty":2,"group":"aldol","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. O=C1CCCCC1 valid; chemistry sound; single non-enolizable-partner concept (same as Q3) so difficulty 3 is inflated, lowered to 2; in scope, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with dilute NaOH and then warmed. Which structure is the major product of this self-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC=O","opts":["C/C=C\\C=O","C=CC=O","CC(O)CC=O","C/C=C/C=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Hydroxide removes an alpha hydrogen from one acetaldehyde, the enolate adds to the carbonyl of a second, and the resulting 3-hydroxybutanal dehydrates on warming to the conjugated enal. The major product is (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde), with the methyl and the CHO on opposite sides of the C=C, the less crowded and more stable geometry. The (Z)-isomer is minor; 3-hydroxybutanal is the addition product that has not yet dehydrated (warming drives the elimination); acrolein has only three carbons; and propanal is not formed at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Valid, in scope, exactly one defensible answer; E geometry correctly drawn in opt0."},{"q":"Propanal (drawn) is treated with a catalytic amount of NaOH at low temperature (no heat). Which structure is the major aldol ADDITION product?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CC/C=C(C)/C=O","CCC(O)C(C)C=O","CCCC(O)C(C)=O","CCC(O)CCC=O","CCC(=O)C(C)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Base removes a proton from the alpha carbon of propanal (the CH2 next to the CHO), and that enolate adds to the carbonyl of a second propanal. With only catalytic base and no heat, the reaction stops at the beta-hydroxy aldehyde 3-hydroxy-2-methylpentanal, which carries the nucleophile's methyl on the alpha carbon and the OH on the beta carbon. 2-Methylpent-2-enal is the dehydrated condensation product, which requires heat; 4-hydroxyhexanal omits the alpha-methyl and has the wrong connectivity; 3-hydroxyhexan-2-one is a ketone, not an aldehyde; and 2-methyl-3-oxopentanal has a ketone where the OH should be.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Stereocenters left unspecified is fine for an identity question; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is mixed with acetophenone (PhCOCH3) and aqueous NaOH, then warmed. Which structure is the major crossed-aldol condensation product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(/C=C\\c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1.CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CC(O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogens, so it can only be the electrophile; acetophenone supplies the enolate. The enolate adds to benzaldehyde, and the beta-hydroxy ketone dehydrates on warming to the conjugated enone (E)-chalcone, with the phenyl and the benzoyl group on opposite sides of the C=C, the less crowded geometry. The (Z)-chalcone is minor; the beta-hydroxy ketone is the addition product before dehydration; deoxybenzoin (1,2-diphenylethanone) has the wrong carbon count; and the unreacted pair ignores the base and the heat.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Classic directed crossed aldol; one defensible answer, correct E geometry."},{"q":"Hexane-2,5-dione (drawn) is treated with NaOH and heated, undergoing an intramolecular aldol condensation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC1=CC(=O)CC1","CC1CCC(=O)C1O","CC1=CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Base removes a proton from one terminal methyl of hexane-2,5-dione, and that enolate attacks the other carbonyl to close a five-membered ring, the favored size here; heating then dehydrates the beta-hydroxy ketone to the conjugated enone 3-methylcyclopent-2-en-1-one, with the C=C conjugated to the C=O. The isomer with the double bond shifted one position away is not conjugated and is less stable; cyclopentanone and cyclohexanone have the wrong carbon count; and the hydroxy ketone has not lost water (and its OH is not even on the carbon where the real aldol adduct would carry it).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Verified opt0 is the conjugated 3-methylcyclopent-2-enone via mechanistic re-derivation; in scope (single ring closure + dehydration)."},{"q":"In a crossed aldol reaction, ethyl acetoacetate (drawn) is often chosen as the nucleophilic partner instead of a simple ketone. Which statement best explains its behavior under mild base?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["It undergoes dehydration before any C-C bond forms","Its central CH2 is flanked by two carbonyls, so it is readily and selectively deprotonated to a stabilized enolate that acts as the nucleophile","It is deprotonated at the terminal ester oxygen to form an alkoxide nucleophile","Mild base reduces its ketone to an alcohol before the aldol step","It has no alpha hydrogens, so it can only serve as the electrophilic carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The central CH2 of ethyl acetoacetate sits between a ketone carbonyl and an ester carbonyl, so its hydrogens are far more acidic than ordinary alpha hydrogens (pKa about 11 versus about 20). Mild base therefore removes one of them selectively, producing a doubly resonance-stabilized enolate (an active-methylene nucleophile) that adds to the electrophilic partner. Ethyl acetoacetate clearly does have alpha hydrogens; deprotonation does not occur on the ester oxygen (there is no O-H); mild base does not reduce the ketone; and no dehydration can occur before a carbon-carbon bond has formed.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Reagent-rationale (text opts), molecule drawn, qualitative acidity only; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetone (drawn) is treated with two equivalents of benzaldehyde and NaOH, then warmed. Which structure is the major product of this double crossed-aldol condensation?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["O=C(CC(O)c1ccccc1)CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C\\c1ccccc1)/C=C\\c1ccccc1","O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)/C=C/c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone has alpha hydrogens on both methyl groups, so with two equivalents of benzaldehyde (no alpha hydrogens, so electrophile only) and NaOH both sides condense and dehydrate. The product is (1E,4E)-dibenzylideneacetone, with each phenyl trans to the carbonyl across its C=C, the less crowded and fully conjugated geometry. Benzalacetone is only the mono-condensation product; the bis-hydroxy ketone is the double addition product before dehydration; the (Z,Z)-isomer is disfavored sterically; and 1,3-diphenylacetone has no alkenes and would require a reduction.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Two-fold condensation but single concept (crossed aldol); correct E,E geometry; in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess acetaldehyde and NaOH, then heated. Acetaldehyde is in excess so it provides the enolate. Which structure is the major crossed product, and what governs its alkene geometry?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C/C=C\\c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC=O","O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","O=CCCc1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogens, so it serves only as the electrophile, while acetaldehyde in excess supplies the enolate. Addition gives 3-hydroxy-3-phenylpropanal, which dehydrates on heating to the conjugated enal (E)-cinnamaldehyde. The E geometry wins because the phenyl and the CHO are trans, which avoids steric clash and keeps the aryl ring, the C=C and the C=O fully conjugated. The (Z)-cinnamaldehyde is minor; the beta-hydroxy aldehyde is the addition product before dehydration; and phenylacetaldehyde and 3-phenylpropanal have the wrong connectivity and no alkene.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aldol","issue":"None. Compound stem (product + qualitative E rationale) is still a two-concept item with one structure answer; no grad-level theory; in scope, correct E geometry."},{"q":"Propiophenone is treated with LDA (1 equiv) in THF at -78 C, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br) is added. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(=O)c1ccc(Cc2ccccc2)cc1","CCC(Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=Cc1ccccc1)CCc1ccccc1","CC(C(=O)c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(=O)c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"LDA deprotonates the single alpha CH2 of propiophenone (PhCO-CH2-CH3) to give the enolate, which alkylates benzyl bromide by SN2 at that carbon, giving PhCO-CH(CH3)-CH2Ph. Distractors put benzyl on the wrong carbon, dialkylate, alkylate the aromatic ring, or show the enol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. Stem and all five SMILES valid; opt0 (PhCOCH(CH3)CH2Ph) is the genuine alpha-benzylation product, key index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with Br2 (1 equiv) in acetic acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["BrC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1Br","O=C1CCC(Br)CC1","O=C1CCCC(Br)C1","BrC1=CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed alpha-halogenation goes through the enol, which brominates at the alpha carbon adjacent to the carbonyl, giving 2-bromocyclohexanone. Distractors brominate a beta or gamma carbon, remove the carbonyl, or show an enol bromide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (2-bromocyclohexanone) is the alpha product; opt2/opt3 are non-alpha distractors; key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with excess I2 and NaOH, and the mixture is then acidified with HCl. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1.[Na+]","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","C(C(=O)c1ccccc1)(Cl)(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetophenone is a methyl ketone, so excess iodine and hydroxide trigger the haloform reaction: the methyl is iodinated three times (each halogen makes the remaining alpha hydrogens more acidic), then hydroxide adds to the carbonyl and expels the stabilized CI3- leaving group, which becomes iodoform. The organic product before workup is sodium benzoate, and because the stem acidifies with HCl, the isolated product is benzoic acid. The carboxylate is the species before acidification; the trihalomethyl ketone is the intermediate before cleavage (and would carry iodine, not chlorine); benzaldehyde and benzyl alcohol would require reductions that do not occur here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. Because the stem explicitly acidifies, neutral benzoic acid (opt0) is right rather than the Na carboxylate (opt1, a strong but defeated distractor). Key index 0 correct."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone is treated with LDA in THF at -78 C, then CH3I is added. What is the major product (kinetic enolate)?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1C","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1(C)C","O=C1CCCC(C)C1C","O=C1CCCCC1CC","CC1CCCCC1=O","O=C1C(C)CCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LDA is a bulky, strong base used at low temperature, so it removes the more accessible alpha proton on the less substituted side of 2-methylcyclohexanone (the CH2, away from the existing methyl), giving the kinetic enolate irreversibly. Methyl iodide then alkylates that carbon, placing a methyl on each alpha carbon: the product is 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone. 2,2-Dimethylcyclohexanone would come from the thermodynamic (more substituted) enolate, formed under reversible conditions with a weaker base; 2,3-dimethylcyclohexanone has a methyl on a beta carbon, which enolate chemistry cannot reach; 2-ethylcyclohexanone would require growing the existing methyl into an ethyl, which alkylation does not do; and the unchanged ketone ignores the alkylation step.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"MIS-KEYED: keyed opt0 is 2,3-dimethylcyclohexanone (methyl on C2 and C3), not 2,6. The true 2,6-dimethylcyclohexanone kinetic product is opt4. Corrected correct to index 4. Chemistry/scope/SMILES otherwise sound, so keep=true with the fixed key."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with one equivalent of sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), then one equivalent of ethyl bromide (CH3CH2Br). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CC)(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(CCC)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The CH2 between the two ester carbonyls of diethyl malonate is unusually acidic (pKa about 13), so one equivalent of ethoxide removes one of its protons and the doubly stabilized enolate performs an SN2 attack on ethyl bromide at that carbon. With one equivalent of base and one of alkyl halide, only one ethyl is installed, giving diethyl ethylmalonate. The methyl and propyl analogs would need a different alkyl halide; the 2,2-diethyl compound is the dialkylation product, which needs a second equivalent of base and bromide; and diethyl succinate inserts a carbon into the chain, which alkylation never does.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (diethyl 2-ethylmalonate) is the mono-ethylated product; the di-ethyl distractor (opt4) is correctly defeated by the 1-equiv stoichiometry. Key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Butanoic acid is treated with Br2 and a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky conditions), followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC(Br)C(=O)O","BrCCCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)Br","CCC(Br)C(=O)Br","CC(Br)CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Under Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky conditions, PBr3 converts butanoic acid to its acyl bromide, which (unlike the acid) enolizes readily; the enol brominates at the alpha carbon, and aqueous workup hydrolyzes the acyl bromide back to the carboxylic acid. The product is 2-bromobutanoic acid, with the bromine on the carbon next to the COOH. 3-Bromobutanoic acid and 4-bromobutanoic acid put the bromine on carbons that cannot enolize; butanoyl bromide is the unbrominated acyl bromide intermediate; and 2-bromobutanoyl bromide is the species before the aqueous workup returns it to the acid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. opt0 (2-bromobutanoic acid) is the HVZ alpha-bromo acid; opt1 (3-bromo) is the key regiochemical distractor. Key index 0 correct, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Phenyl-2-propanone (phenylacetone, PhCH2COCH3) is treated with LDA, then CH3I. Deprotonation favors the more resonance-stabilized (benzylic) enolate. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)C(CC)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccc(C)cc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Phenylacetone has two alpha carbons, but the benzylic CH2 gives the more stable enolate because the negative charge is delocalized into the ring as well as onto the carbonyl oxygen, and the stem specifies that this enolate is the one formed. Methyl iodide then alkylates the benzylic carbon, giving 3-phenylbutan-2-one, with the new methyl on the carbon between the phenyl and the carbonyl. 1-Phenylbutan-2-one alkylates the methyl side instead; 3-phenylpentan-2-one adds an ethyl rather than a methyl; 3-methyl-3-phenylbutan-2-one is the double alkylation product; and the ring-methylated ketone is not formed because the enolate reacts at the alpha carbon, not on the aromatic ring.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"Correct as written. The stem explicitly supplies the benzylic-enolate premise, which resolves the LDA kinetic-vs-stabilized ambiguity and leaves exactly one defensible answer (opt0, PhCH(CH3)COCH3). Single concept (regioselective alkylation), in scope; key index 0 correct."},{"q":"An unsymmetrical ketone is treated with excess I2 and NaOH (the iodoform reaction). The substrate shown below is acetophenone (CC(=O)c1ccccc1). After acidic workup, which pair of products is obtained?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzoic acid (PhCOOH) plus iodoform (CHI3)","Phenylacetic acid plus CHI3","alpha-iodoacetophenone plus NaI","Iodobenzene plus acetic acid","Benzaldehyde plus CHI3"],"correct":0,"why":"Acetophenone is a methyl ketone (CH3 next to C=O), so it undergoes the haloform reaction. The three alpha-H's are sequentially replaced by I, then hydroxide cleaves the C-C bond to give the carboxylate (benzoate -> benzoic acid after workup) and the triiodomethyl anion, which picks up a proton to form iodoform (CHI3, a yellow precipitate).","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Methyl ketone -> haloform cleavage: benzoic acid + CHI3 (index 0). q_smiles valid, classic Booster-level recall, single correct answer."},{"q":"A student wants to install a single Br atom at the alpha-carbon of the carboxylic acid shown (butanoic acid, CCCC(=O)O) to make 2-bromobutanoic acid. Which set of reagents accomplishes this alpha-halogenation of a carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["NBS with light (radical conditions)","Br2 with a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky), then H2O","HBr with peroxides","Br2 with FeBr3","Br2 in CCl4 only"],"correct":1,"why":"Carboxylic acids do not enolize readily, so direct Br2 fails. The Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky (HVZ) reaction uses Br2 with catalytic PBr3 (or red P): PBr3 converts the acid to the acyl bromide, which enolizes and is brominated at the alpha-carbon; aqueous workup regenerates the acid as the alpha-bromo carboxylic acid. NBS/light and Br2/peroxides are radical (allylic/free-radical) brominations, and Br2/FeBr3 is electrophilic aromatic, none of which targets the alpha-C of an acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. HVZ (Br2/cat. PBr3) is the textbook alpha-halogenation of a carboxylic acid (index 0). Distractors are mechanistically distinct (radical/EAS). In scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (O=C1CCCCC1) exists in equilibrium with a small amount of its enol tautomer. Which statement about this keto-enol tautomerism is correct?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Keto and enol are resonance structures, not tautomers, so they cannot be separated even in principle","The enol form predominates because the C=C bond is stronger than the C=O bond","The keto form predominates because the C=O bond plus C-H bonds are more stable than the C=C plus O-H bonds of the enol","The two tautomers are non-superimposable mirror images (enantiomers)","Tautomerization requires breaking the C-C sigma bond of the ring"],"correct":2,"why":"For a simple ketone the keto form is strongly favored (>99.9%) because the carbonyl C=O bond is much stronger than the enol C=C bond. Keto and enol are constitutional isomers (tautomers) that interconvert by moving an alpha-H to oxygen and shifting a pi bond, NOT resonance structures (atoms actually move). They are not enantiomers and no C-C bond breaks.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Keto favored; tautomers are constitutional isomers, not resonance (index 0). Distractors capture the classic tautomer-vs-resonance trap. In scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone, CC(=O)CC(=O)C) is shown. Which proton is the MOST acidic, and why is this compound unusually acidic (pKa ~9) compared to a simple ketone (pKa ~20)?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The proton is on oxygen (it is an enol), so it ionizes like an alcohol","The central CH2; it is acidic only because of the inductive effect of two sp2 carbons","A terminal CH3 proton; the resulting anion is stabilized by hyperconjugation","The central CH2 between the two carbonyls; its conjugate base is delocalized onto both oxygens","All six methyl protons are equally most acidic by symmetry"],"correct":3,"why":"The most acidic protons are on the central methylene flanked by two carbonyls. Removing one gives an enolate whose negative charge is delocalized by resonance onto BOTH carbonyl oxygens, which is far more stabilizing than the single-oxygen delocalization in a mono-ketone enolate. This double resonance stabilization (not mere induction) drops the pKa to about 9. Terminal CH3 protons are alpha to only one C=O and far less acidic.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Central CH2; doubly-delocalized enolate (index 0). pKa cited as a recall benchmark, not a calculation, so in scope. Valid SMILES, single defensible answer."},{"q":"The reaction below is run on 3-pentanone (CCC(=O)CC): (1) LDA, -78 C; (2) CH3I. Which structure is the major product of this enolate alpha-alkylation?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCC","CC=C(CC)OC","CCC(=O)C(C)(C)C","CCC(=O)CCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C"],"correct":4,"why":"LDA is a strong, bulky, non-nucleophilic base that completely deprotonates one alpha carbon of 3-pentanone to give the enolate. The enolate carbon then does an SN2 displacement on methyl iodide, installing one methyl on the alpha carbon: 3-pentanone becomes 2-methyl-3-pentanone. 3-Hexanone would require an ethyl halide; 2,2-dimethyl-3-pentanone is the over-alkylation product with two methyls on one alpha carbon; the O-methyl enol ether is the O-alkylation product, which is minor with an alkyl iodide because C-alkylation dominates; and 2-pentanone has the carbonyl in the wrong place and the wrong carbon count.","difficulty":1,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Alpha-methylation gives 2-methyl-3-pentanone (index 0). All 5 option SMILES valid; option D is a chemically sound O-alkylation enol-ether distractor. Visual, in scope."},{"q":"You need to selectively make ONE alpha-bromo ketone from propiophenone (CCC(=O)c1ccccc1) without over-bromination or cleaving the molecule. Which conditions are best for a controlled, single alpha-bromination at the carbon next to the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Excess Br2 with excess NaOH (basic conditions)","Br2 with FeBr3 to brominate the alpha-carbon","HBr gas with no catalyst","One equivalent of Br2 under acidic conditions (acid-catalyzed enolization)","NBS with a radical initiator (AIBN), heat"],"correct":3,"why":"Acid-catalyzed alpha-halogenation stops cleanly after one substitution: putting one electron-withdrawing Br on the alpha-carbon makes the second enolization slower, so a single equivalent of Br2 under acid gives the mono-alpha-bromo ketone. Under BASE, the opposite is true (each added halogen makes the next H more acidic), driving poly-halogenation/haloform-type cleavage. Br2/FeBr3 is electrophilic aromatic substitution and NBS/AIBN is radical (benzylic), neither giving clean alpha-bromination here.","difficulty":2,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acid-catalyzed gives clean mono-alpha-bromination; base drives poly/haloform (index 0). Two-concept (regiocontrol + acid vs base selectivity), valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"Four compounds are candidates for the most acidic alpha C-H. The structure drawn is ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-ketoester, CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC). Rank the acidity of the most acidic C-H in these four: ethyl acetoacetate (pictured), acetone (CC(=O)C), diethyl malonate (CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC), and ethyl propanoate (CCC(=O)OCC). Which ordering (most acidic first) is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ethyl propanoate > acetone > diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate","acetone > ethyl propanoate > ethyl acetoacetate > diethyl malonate","ethyl acetoacetate > diethyl malonate > acetone > ethyl propanoate","acetone > diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate > ethyl propanoate","diethyl malonate > ethyl acetoacetate > ethyl propanoate > acetone"],"correct":2,"why":"Doubly activated C-H's (between two carbonyls) are most acidic. A ketone stabilizes an enolate better than an ester (ester O lone pair donates into its own C=O, weakening that carbonyl's anion stabilization), so ketone+ester (acetoacetate, pKa ~11) beats ester+ester (malonate, pKa ~13). Both beat singly activated substrates: acetone (one ketone, pKa ~20) is more acidic than ethyl propanoate (one ester, pKa ~25). Order: acetoacetate > malonate > acetone > propanoate.","difficulty":2,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified order acetoacetate(~11) > malonate(~13) > acetone(~20) > propanoate(~25) = index 0. All four SMILES valid (pictured one drawn). pKa values are recall benchmarks, not math. Two-concept ranking, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylcyclohexanone (O=C1CCCCC1C) has two different alpha-carbons: a more-substituted one (bearing the methyl) and a less-substituted one. A chemist treats it with LDA at -78 C, then adds CH3I. Which alpha-carbon is alkylated, and what is the governing principle?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The less-substituted alpha-carbon, because LDA gives the kinetic enolate (fast removal of the less hindered proton)","The less-substituted alpha-carbon, because it forms the more stable, more-substituted enolate","The more-substituted alpha-carbon, because LDA gives the thermodynamic enolate","Neither; LDA is too weak a base to deprotonate a ketone alpha-carbon","Both alpha-carbons equally, because LDA shows no selectivity"],"correct":0,"why":"LDA is strong and bulky, and at -78 C deprotonation is fast and irreversible (kinetic control), so it removes the most accessible alpha proton: one on the less substituted CH2, giving the kinetic enolate. Methyl iodide then alkylates that less substituted alpha carbon. The statement that names the same carbon but credits a 'more stable, more substituted enolate' has the reasoning backwards: the less substituted enolate is the less stable one, and it forms because it forms fastest. The more substituted alpha carbon is alkylated only under thermodynamic control (weaker base, higher temperature, reversible deprotonation). LDA is far more than strong enough to deprotonate a ketone (pKa about 20) and is highly selective, not indiscriminate.","difficulty":3,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. LDA/-78 = kinetic enolate at less-substituted alpha-C (index 0). Option C names the same carbon but with self-contradictory reasoning (less-substituted carbon yet more-substituted enolate), so only A is fully correct. Standard sophomore kinetic-vs-thermo enolate concept, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Acetophenone is treated with excess Br2 in aqueous NaOH, then acidified on workup. Which is the organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["Brc1ccccc1","O=C(CBr)c1ccccc1","O=C(C(Br)(Br)Br)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetophenone is a methyl ketone, so excess bromine in base runs the haloform reaction: all three alpha hydrogens are replaced to give the tribromomethyl ketone, hydroxide adds to the carbonyl, and the stabilized CBr3- leaves (becoming bromoform, CHBr3). The organic product is benzoate, which is protonated on acidic workup to benzoic acid. The tribromomethyl ketone is the intermediate before carbon-carbon cleavage; the monobromo ketone requires controlled acid-catalyzed conditions; bromobenzene would need electrophilic aromatic bromination with a Lewis acid; and benzaldehyde is a reduction product that these oxidative conditions never give.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct haloform product, one defensible answer, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Propanoic acid is treated with Br2 and a catalytic amount of PBr3 (Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky conditions), then water is added. Which is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)CC","opts":["O=C(O)C(Br)C","O=C(Br)CC","O=C(Br)C(Br)C","O=C(O)C(Br)(Br)C","O=C(O)CCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hell-Volhard-Zelinsky bromination occurs specifically at the alpha carbon: PBr3 converts propanoic acid to the acyl bromide, which enolizes and is brominated at the alpha position, and adding water hydrolyzes the acyl bromide back to the acid. The product is 2-bromopropanoic acid. 3-Bromopropanoic acid places the bromine on a carbon that cannot enolize; propanoyl bromide and 2-bromopropanoyl bromide are acyl bromide intermediates that hydrolyze on the water workup; and 2,2-dibromopropanoic acid would require excess bromine and a second substitution.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct alpha-bromo acid, single answer, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Consider ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate), drawn here. Which hydrogen is the MOST acidic, and what is the approximate reason?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["There are no acidic C-H bonds; only the ester O could lose a proton","The terminal CH3 next to the ketone (pKa ~20), because it is alpha to a carbonyl","The CH3 of the ethyl group, because it is the farthest from electron-withdrawing groups","The CH2 between the ketone and ester carbonyls (pKa ~11), because its conjugate base is delocalized onto two carbonyl oxygens","The OCH2 of the ethyl ester group, because it is attached to oxygen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The CH2 between the ketone and ester carbonyls is by far the most acidic site: its conjugate base is delocalized onto both carbonyl oxygens, so the pKa is about 11, compared with about 20 for an ordinary alpha hydrogen such as the terminal CH3 next to the ketone, which is stabilized by only one carbonyl. The OCH2 of the ethyl group is attached to oxygen but is not alpha to a carbonyl, and the ethyl CH3 is remote from any electron-withdrawing group, so neither is appreciably acidic. There is no O-H in the molecule, so the ester oxygen cannot lose a proton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct most-acidic-H identification, recognition-level, single answer, valid q_smiles."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with LDA, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br) is added. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["O=C1CCCC(Cc2ccccc2)C1","C(c1ccccc1)OC1=CCCCC1","OC1(Cc2ccccc2)CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C1CCCCC1Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LDA deprotonates cyclohexanone to its enolate, and the nucleophilic alpha carbon performs an SN2 attack on benzyl bromide, giving 2-benzylcyclohexanone. Carbon alkylation dominates over oxygen alkylation with an alkyl halide, so the O-benzyl enol ether is minor; the benzyl group goes on the alpha carbon, not the beta carbon (3-benzylcyclohexanone); with controlled stoichiometry only one benzyl is added, not two; and 1-benzylcyclohexanol would require a carbanion adding to the carbonyl, which the enolate does not do.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct C-alkylation product, single answer, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"3-Methylbutan-2-one is treated with one equivalent of Br2 in acetic acid (acid-catalyzed, monobromination). Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)C(C)C","opts":["O=C(CBr)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(C(Br)Br)C(C)C","O=C(C)C(C)(C)Br","O=C(C)C(C)CBr","O=C(CBr)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Under acid catalysis, alpha-halogenation goes through the enol, and the more substituted (more stable) enol forms preferentially. For 3-methylbutan-2-one the more substituted enol has its double bond toward the methine carbon bearing two methyls, so bromine attacks there to give 3-bromo-3-methylbutan-2-one. The less substituted 1-bromo ketone is the base-promoted regiochemistry; the 4-bromo product puts bromine on a beta carbon that never enolizes; and the dibromo products do not form because the first bromine withdraws electron density and slows further enolization under acid, so the reaction stops cleanly at monobromination.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct acid-catalyzed regiochemistry and monobromination, single answer, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Optically pure (R)-3-phenylbutan-2-one (shown) is stirred in aqueous NaOH and then re-isolated. The alpha carbon bearing the phenyl group is the stereocenter. What is the stereochemical outcome and why?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["The compound is destroyed by a haloform-type C-C cleavage at the stereocenter","A single diastereomer forms because a new stereocenter is created at the carbonyl carbon","The product is racemic (R and S in equal amounts) because base-promoted enolization at that alpha carbon makes it sp2 and planar, and reprotonation occurs equally on both faces","The (R) configuration is fully retained because enolization does not involve the stereocenter","It is converted entirely to the (S) enantiomer by a clean inversion at the alpha carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The stereocenter is the alpha carbon, and it carries an alpha hydrogen. Aqueous NaOH removes that proton to form an enolate, which is planar and sp2 at the former stereocenter, so the chirality is erased; reprotonation by water then occurs on either face with equal probability, giving a 50:50 mixture of (R) and (S), a racemate. There is no clean inversion to a single (S) enantiomer, no new stereocenter forms at the carbonyl carbon, retention is impossible because enolization does involve the stereocenter, and haloform cleavage needs a halogen (and would cleave at the methyl side, not at the stereocenter).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"enolate-alkylation-halogenation","issue":"None. Correct base-catalyzed racemization, single defensible answer, in scope, valid stereo-SMILES."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), then worked up with dilute acid. What is the major product of this Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)C(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethoxide removes an alpha proton from one ethyl acetate; the resulting enolate attacks the ester carbonyl of a second ethyl acetate, and the tetrahedral intermediate expels ethoxide. Two ester molecules join with loss of one ethanol to give the beta-ketoester ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate), a ketone and an ester separated by one CH2. Diethyl malonate has two ester groups rather than a ketone; ethyl pyruvate has the ketone directly on the ester carbon; acetylacetone has no ester at all; and ethyl 4-oxopentanoate places the ketone one carbon too far from the ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Valid SMILES, correct keyed product (ethyl acetoacetate), one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is treated with NaOEt followed by acidic workup. Draw the major beta-ketoester product of the self-Claisen condensation.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)CC","CCCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ethoxide deprotonates the alpha carbon of ethyl propanoate (the CH2 next to the ester, which carries a methyl); that enolate attacks a second ethyl propanoate, and loss of ethoxide gives the beta-ketoester ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate: a propanoyl group attached to the alpha carbon, which still bears its methyl. Ethyl 3-oxopentanoate has lost the alpha-methyl; ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxobutanoate carries an acetyl group instead of the propanoyl group that ethyl propanoate actually supplies; the hexanoate analog attaches a butanoyl group that is not available; and ethyl 4-oxohexanoate is a gamma-ketoester with the wrong connectivity.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct product retains the alpha-methyl; good regiochemistry distractors."},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate) is treated with NaOEt, then mild acid. What is the major product of this intramolecular (Dieckmann) condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","O=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A Dieckmann condensation is an intramolecular Claisen: ethoxide forms an enolate at one alpha carbon of diethyl adipate, and that enolate attacks the other ester carbonyl, expelling ethoxide. For a 1,6-diester the enolate carbon and the attacked carbonyl carbon close a five-membered ring, the favored size, giving the cyclic beta-ketoester ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate. The six-membered analog would come from a seven-carbon diester (diethyl pimelate); the four-membered ring is strained and not formed; cyclopentanone has lost the ester, which would need hydrolysis and decarboxylation not given in the stem; and ethyl cyclohexanecarboxylate has no ketone and the wrong ring.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct 5-membered ring product; ring-size distractors valid."},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate) is subjected to Dieckmann conditions: NaOEt, then acidic workup. Identify the major cyclic product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1=CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl pimelate is a 1,7-diester, so the ester enolate at one end attacks the far carbonyl, ethoxide is expelled, and a six-membered ring closes. After acidic workup the product is ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate, a cyclic beta-keto ester: one ester carbon becomes the ring ketone and the other survives as the carbethoxy group on the adjacent ring carbon. The five- and seven-membered beta-keto esters are the wrong ring size for a seven-carbon chain, plain cyclohexanone has lost the carbethoxy group, and the hydroxy cyclohexene ester has its double bond and hydroxyl in the wrong places and is not the keto product that is isolated.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct 6-membered ring product; opt4 enol form is a fair distractor."},{"q":"A crossed (mixed) Claisen condensation is run between ethyl benzoate (no alpha hydrogens) and ethyl acetate using NaOEt, followed by acidic workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(CC(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A crossed Claisen is clean when one ester has no alpha hydrogens. Ethyl benzoate cannot form an enolate, so it serves only as the electrophile: the enolate of ethyl acetate attacks the benzoate carbonyl and ethoxide is expelled, giving ethyl benzoylacetate (PhCO-CH2-CO2Et) after workup. Diethyl malonate would require a carbonate electrophile, dibenzoylmethane carries two benzoyl groups and no ester, ethyl phenylacetate is just a different ester with no new carbon-carbon bond, and the homolog with an extra CH2 between the ketone and the ring has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct directionality (acetate is nucleophile, benzoate electrophile); product valid."},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate (no alpha hydrogens) is condensed with ethyl acetate under NaOEt, then acidified. Draw the major crossed-Claisen product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(=O)C(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha hydrogens and acts as the electrophile. The ethyl acetate enolate attacks one oxalate carbonyl (displacing ethoxide), giving diethyl 2-oxosuccinate (diethyl oxaloacetate), EtO2C-CO-CH2-CO2Et. Distractors: malonate (no extra carbonyl), a missing CH2, and methyl-ketone/wrong-position variants.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Keyed SMILES verified = diethyl oxaloacetate; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Ethyl formate (HCO2Et, no alpha hydrogens) reacts with ethyl acetate under NaOEt, then acidic workup. What is the major formylation/Claisen product?","q_smiles":"CCOC=O.CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)=O","O=CCC=O","CCOC(=O)CC=O","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethyl formate has no alpha hydrogens, so it can only act as the electrophile, a formylating agent. The enolate of ethyl acetate attacks the formate carbonyl and ethoxide leaves, installing a CHO group on the alpha carbon: the product is ethyl 3-oxopropanoate (ethyl formylacetate, OHC-CH2-CO2Et). Ethyl pyruvate and ethyl glyoxylate put the new carbonyl in the wrong place, diethyl malonate would need a carbonate electrophile, and malondialdehyde has no ester at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Correct formylacetate product; valid SMILES; one answer."},{"q":"Ethyl phenylacetate undergoes a self-Claisen condensation with NaOEt, then acidic workup. Identify the major beta-ketoester product, accounting for which carbon is deprotonated.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)C(c1ccccc1)C(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl phenylacetate has only one kind of alpha hydrogen, on the benzylic CH2 next to the ester, so that carbon is deprotonated to give an enolate stabilized by both the ester and the phenyl ring. The enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ethyl phenylacetate and ethoxide leaves, giving ethyl 2,4-diphenyl-3-oxobutanoate (PhCH2-CO-CH(Ph)-CO2Et): the nucleophilic carbon keeps its phenyl, and the acyl group brought in is phenylacetyl, not benzoyl. The structure with a benzoyl group attached directly to the alpha carbon would require ethyl benzoate as the electrophile, the product missing a phenyl cannot come from this ester, dibenzyl ketone has lost the ester, and the diphenylpropanoate has no ketone.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"None. Two-concept (regiochemistry of acidic carbon + condensation) but single reactive center; correct product keyed."},{"q":"Two molecules of ethyl acetate (shown) are heated and then worked up with dilute acid to give ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-ketoester) in a Claisen condensation. Which reagent must be used to drive this reaction to completion?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Dilute sulfuric acid (H2SO4)","Lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4)","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt)","Pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC)","Sodium borohydride (NaBH4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The Claisen requires a strong alkoxide (matching ethoxide avoids transesterification) to form the ester enolate. The reaction is driven forward by deprotonation of the acidic beta-ketoester product. Hydrides reduce, PCC oxidizes, dilute acid cannot make an enolate.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl acetate SMILES valid; NaOEt (idx0) is the correct, sole defensible answer; reagent-selection text item, molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"The transformation shown converts an ester into a beta-ketoester by forming a new C-C bond between the alpha carbon of one ester molecule and the carbonyl carbon of a second identical ester molecule, ejecting an alkoxide leaving group. What is the name of this reaction?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["Wittig reaction","Aldol condensation","Friedel-Crafts acylation","Cannizzaro reaction","Claisen condensation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl acetoacetate is drawn, the classic Claisen product from two ethyl acetate units: an ester enolate attacks an ester carbonyl and the tetrahedral intermediate collapses by expelling alkoxide. Aldol gives beta-hydroxy carbonyls, Cannizzaro needs no alpha-H, Wittig uses ylides, Friedel-Crafts acylates arenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl acetoacetate SMILES valid; Claisen (idx0) correct; one defensible answer. No change."},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (the C6 diester shown) is treated with NaOEt and then dilute acid in an intramolecular Claisen (Dieckmann) condensation. Which cyclic beta-ketoester is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Diethyl adipate is a 1,6-diester. One ester enolate attacks the other carbonyl intramolecularly, ethoxide leaves, and the new ring contains five atoms (the four chain carbons between the carbonyls plus one carbonyl carbon). The product is ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate, the cyclic beta-keto ester. The six-membered beta-keto ester is what the seven-carbon pimelate gives, the four- and seven-membered rings are the wrong size for this chain, and plain cyclohexanone has no ester group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: all 5 option SMILES parse; opt0 ring=cyclopentanone is the correct Dieckmann product of adipate; keyed correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (the C7 diester shown) undergoes a Dieckmann condensation with NaOEt followed by acidic workup. Which product forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","O=C1CCCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Diethyl pimelate is a 1,7-diester: the enolate at one alpha carbon attacks the other ester carbonyl, ethoxide leaves, and the ring that closes is six-membered (five chain carbons plus the carbonyl carbon). After acidic workup the product is ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate (2-carbethoxycyclohexanone), the favored ring size. The five-membered beta-keto ester comes from the shorter adipate, the four- and seven-membered rings are the wrong size and less favorable, and cycloheptanone has no ester at all.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: all option SMILES valid; opt0 ring=cyclohexanone is correct for pimelate; correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Methyl propanoate (shown) is treated with sodium methoxide, then dilute acid, in a Claisen condensation. Which beta-ketoester is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OC","CCC(=O)CC(=O)OC","CCC(O)C(C)C(=O)OC","CCC(=O)CCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Methyl propanoate has an alpha CH2 bearing a methyl, so its enolate is CH3CH(-)CO2Me. That enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second methyl propanoate, methoxide leaves, and the product is methyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate: a beta-keto ester with the methyl branch retained on the alpha carbon and an ethyl ketone from the second molecule. The unbranched version drops the alpha methyl, the beta-hydroxy ester is an aldol-type product (there is no aldehyde here), the 4-oxohexanoate has its carbonyls in a 1,4 relationship, and dimethyl methylmalonate carries two esters and no ketone.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: methyl propanoate self-Claisen gives the branched beta-ketoester (opt0); all SMILES parse; distractors plausibly wrong; correct=0 matches. No change."},{"q":"Which of the following esters CANNOT undergo a normal Claisen (self-)condensation when treated with a strong base?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C(=O)OCC","opts":["Ethyl butanoate","Ethyl phenylacetate","Ethyl acetate","Ethyl propanoate","Ethyl 2,2-dimethylpropanoate (ethyl pivalate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A self-Claisen needs at least two alpha hydrogens so the beta-ketoester product still has an acidic alpha proton to drive the equilibrium. Ethyl pivalate (drawn) has a fully substituted alpha carbon with no alpha-H, so it cannot form an enolate. The other four esters all have alpha-H and react normally.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: pivalate SMILES valid, has no alpha-H; idx0 is the unique correct answer; classification text item with molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"Ethyl phenylacetate (shown) undergoes a base-promoted Claisen self-condensation. After NaOEt and acidic workup, the product is a beta-ketoester. Which class of product is formed, given that the alpha carbon already bears a phenyl group?","q_smiles":"O=C(OCC)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["A symmetrical anhydride","A beta-ketoester (1,3-dicarbonyl)","An alpha,beta-unsaturated ester","A 1,2-diketone","A beta-hydroxy ester"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"As in every Claisen, an ester enolate attacks a second ester carbonyl and alkoxide is expelled, giving a 1,3-dicarbonyl beta-ketoester. A beta-hydroxy ester would be an aldol product, the relationship is 1,3 not 1,2, and no anhydride or alkene forms.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: ethyl phenylacetate SMILES valid; product is a beta-ketoester (idx0); one defensible answer; classification text item with molecule drawn. No change."},{"q":"A student wants to make a six-membered cyclic beta-ketoester by a Dieckmann condensation. Of the diesters below, which gives a six-membered ring (cyclohexanone) product, while diethyl succinate (shown) fails to give a useful Dieckmann product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","opts":["Diethyl succinate (1,4-diester)","Diethyl malonate (1,3-diester)","Diethyl pimelate (1,7-diester)","Diethyl oxalate (1,2-diester)","Diethyl adipate (1,6-diester)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Ring size tracks chain length: a 1,7-diester (pimelate) closes to a favorable six-membered ring. Succinate (drawn, 1,4) would form a strained three-membered ring and fails, adipate (1,6) gives a five-membered ring, and malonate/oxalate are too short to cyclize. Only pimelate gives the six-membered cyclohexanone beta-ketoester.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen","issue":"Verified: succinate SMILES valid; pimelate (idx0) is the unique diester giving a 6-ring; single concept (ring-size logic), in scope despite difficulty 3. No change."},{"q":"Methyl vinyl ketone (drawn) is treated with the sodium salt of diethyl malonate (NaCH(CO2Et)2). What product is formed, and by what process?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)C","opts":["No reaction; malonate is not nucleophilic enough","1,2-addition: the malonate carbon adds to the carbonyl carbon, giving a tertiary alcohol","Nucleophilic acyl substitution to displace the methyl group","1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition: the malonate carbon adds to the beta-carbon, giving CH3C(=O)CH2CH2CH(CO2Et)2","Aldol addition at the alpha-carbon of the ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A stabilized enolate (soft nucleophile) such as malonate undergoes 1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition to an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone, bonding to the beta-carbon. The 1,2 distractor is the kinetic mode favored by hard organometallics, not soft enolates.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: soft stabilized enolate gives 1,4-Michael addition; product valid; standard sophomore item."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexenone (drawn) reacts with lithium dimethylcuprate, (CH3)2CuLi, followed by aqueous workup. Which statement correctly describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["A methyl group adds to the beta-carbon (1,4-addition) to give 3-methylcyclohexanone","The cuprate deprotonates the alpha-carbon and no addition occurs","The enone is reduced to cyclohexanone with no methyl incorporation","A methyl group adds to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition) to give 1-methyl-2-cyclohexenol","Two methyl groups add, one at the carbonyl and one at the beta-carbon"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Gilman reagents (lithium dialkylcuprates) are soft and selectively do 1,4-conjugate addition, delivering the alkyl group to the beta-carbon. After tautomerization of the enol, the product is 3-methylcyclohexanone. Organolithiums/Grignards favor 1,2-addition instead.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: Gilman cuprate gives 1,4 to beta-carbon; 3-methylcyclohexanone product valid."},{"q":"You want to add a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon of 2-cyclohexenone (drawn) to make 1-methyl-2-cyclohexen-1-ol (1,2-addition), avoiding conjugate addition. Which reagent is the best choice?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["CH3MgBr with catalytic CuI","(CH3)2CuLi (lithium dimethylcuprate)","CH3Li (methyllithium)","Sodium dimethyl malonate","CH3NH2 (methylamine)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hard, highly reactive organometallics such as organolithiums add fast and irreversibly to the carbonyl carbon (1,2-addition). Cuprates and Cu-catalyzed Grignards favor 1,4. Stabilized enolates and amines are soft nucleophiles that give conjugate addition.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: organolithium (hard) gives 1,2; only one reagent does so; product valid."},{"q":"Acrylonitrile (drawn) is combined with the enolate of diethyl malonate. Classify this reaction and identify where the new carbon-carbon bond forms.","q_smiles":"C=CC#N","opts":["Diels-Alder cycloaddition forming a six-membered ring","Michael addition; the malonate carbon bonds to the terminal CH2 (beta to the nitrile)","Aldol addition; the bond forms at the nitrile carbon","Claisen condensation; an ester is displaced","1,2-addition; the malonate adds across the C-N triple bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Acrylonitrile is a classic Michael acceptor; the nitrile is electron-withdrawing and conjugated to the alkene, so the soft malonate nucleophile adds to the beta-carbon (terminal CH2). This conjugate addition of a stabilized enolate is the Michael reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: acrylonitrile is Michael acceptor; malonate adds to terminal beta-CH2; product valid."},{"q":"In a Michael reaction, the alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl is the 'Michael acceptor' and the stabilized carbanion is the 'Michael donor.' Which of the following is the best Michael DONOR?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["Diethyl ether","tert-Butyllithium","Diethyl malonate (the drawn compound), deprotonated to its enolate","Cyclohexene","Methyl vinyl ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"A good Michael donor is a carbon nucleophile whose negative charge is resonance-stabilized by adjacent electron-withdrawing groups (here two esters flanking the CH2). Methyl vinyl ketone is an acceptor; ether and cyclohexene are not nucleophilic; t-BuLi is a hard base/nucleophile, not a stabilized donor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: deprotonated malonate is the canonical stabilized Michael donor; distractors clearly wrong."},{"q":"trans-Cinnamaldehyde (drawn) is treated with one equivalent of sodium cyanide in protic solvent. The product is the 1,4 (conjugate) addition adduct. Where does cyanide add, and why is 1,4 observed here?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["No addition occurs because the aldehyde is conjugated to a benzene ring","Cyanide adds to an aromatic ring carbon via electrophilic substitution","Cyanide adds twice, once 1,2 and once 1,4","Cyanide adds to the carbonyl carbon; cyanide always gives 1,2-addition to aldehydes","Cyanide adds to the beta-carbon (bearing the phenyl); cyanide is a soft, reversible nucleophile that favors the thermodynamic 1,4 product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is a soft, polarizable nucleophile and its addition is reversible, so on a conjugated enal the thermodynamically favored 1,4-adduct predominates: CN bonds to the beta (benzylic) carbon. This contrasts with the irreversible 1,2-cyanohydrin formed from simple aldehydes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Stem pre-specifies the 1,4 product, removing ambiguity; soft/reversible-nucleophile reasoning is textbook-correct and beta is the benzylic carbon. In scope."},{"q":"2-Cyclohexenone (drawn) is treated with two different reagents. Reagent A is CH3MgBr (no copper); Reagent B is CH3MgBr with catalytic CuBr. Which row correctly pairs each reagent with its major regiochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["A gives mainly 1,4-addition; B gives mainly 1,2-addition","Both A and B give exclusively 1,4-addition","Both A and B give exclusively 1,2-addition","A gives reduction to cyclohexanone; B gives an epoxide","A gives mainly 1,2-addition (allylic tertiary alcohol); B gives mainly 1,4-addition (3-methylcyclohexanone)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A bare Grignard is hard and adds 1,2 to the carbonyl, giving the tertiary allylic alcohol. Adding catalytic Cu(I) generates a cuprate-type species in situ that switches selectivity to 1,4-conjugate addition, placing the methyl at the beta-carbon to give 3-methylcyclohexanone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: bare Grignard 1,2 vs Cu-catalyzed 1,4; standard two-concept selectivity item; products valid."},{"q":"Two electrophiles are compared as Michael acceptors toward the same stabilized enolate: chalcone (drawn, PhCOCH=CHPh) and an ordinary non-conjugated ketone such as cyclohexanone. Which statement is correct about their relative reactivity in conjugate addition?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["Chalcone readily undergoes 1,4-conjugate (Michael) addition because its C=C is conjugated to the carbonyl, making the beta-carbon electrophilic; cyclohexanone has no such conjugated beta-carbon and cannot act as a Michael acceptor","Both react identically since both contain a C=O group","Neither can undergo conjugate addition because aryl groups block the beta-carbon","Chalcone only undergoes 1,2-addition because both faces are aryl-substituted","Cyclohexanone is the better Michael acceptor because it lacks aromatic rings"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Conjugate (Michael) addition requires an alpha,beta-unsaturated carbonyl: the C=C conjugated to C=O renders the beta-carbon electrophilic via the extended resonance. Chalcone has this; a saturated ketone like cyclohexanone has no conjugated beta-carbon and therefore cannot serve as a Michael acceptor. The phenyl rings do not block the conjugate-addition pathway.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"michael","issue":"Correct: chalcone is a conjugated enone (Michael acceptor); saturated cyclohexanone is not; chalcone SMILES valid."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl vinyl ketone is treated with the sodium enolate of dimethyl malonate (NaOMe, then workup). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)C(CCC(C)=O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(O)(C)C=C)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(=C)C(C)=O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(CCC(C)(O)O)C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C(C(=O)OC)C(C)CC=O"],"correct":0,"why":"Dimethyl malonate forms a soft, stabilized enolate, and such enolates add 1,4 (Michael addition) to the beta carbon of an enone, which in methyl vinyl ketone is the terminal CH2. After protonation of the resulting enolate and tautomerization the methyl ketone is restored, so the product is dimethyl 2-(3-oxobutyl)malonate, a 1,5-dicarbonyl compound. The tertiary allylic alcohol would be the 1,2-addition product (hard nucleophile behavior), the structure with the malonate on the alpha carbon has the wrong regiochemistry, the gem-diol is not a stable product, and the aldehyde isomer has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct and unambiguous; all 5 SMILES valid; in scope. Minor: why-text 'option 4 is a non-product diol' uses loose 1-indexing but does not affect the keyed index."},{"group":"michael","q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous acid. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(C)CCCC=C1","C=C1CCCCC1=O","O=C1CCCCC1C","O=C1CCCC(C)C1","O=C1CCC(C)CC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Gilman reagents (lithium dialkylcuprates) are soft nucleophiles and add 1,4 to alpha,beta-unsaturated ketones, delivering the methyl to the beta carbon (C3). Protonation of the resulting enolate on workup gives 3-methylcyclohexanone. The tertiary allylic alcohol would come from 1,2-addition (organolithium or Grignard behavior), 2-methylcyclohexanone puts the methyl on the alpha carbon, 4-methylcyclohexanone has it one carbon too far, and the exocyclic methylene ketone is a different skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = 3-methylcyclohexanone, verified); 5 valid options; in scope. Minor: why-text uses 'Option 1' loosely for the alcohol but keyed correct=0 is right."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl vinyl ketone reacts with thiophenol (PhSH) under mild base. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCS(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCSc1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Sc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CSCc1ccccc1","C=CC(C)(O)Sc1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Thiols are soft nucleophiles, and under mild base the thiolate adds 1,4 (thia-Michael addition) to the beta carbon of methyl vinyl ketone, the terminal CH2. Protonation of the enolate restores the methyl ketone, so the product is 4-(phenylthio)butan-2-one. Sulfur on the alpha carbon has the wrong regiochemistry, the benzyl thioether has the wrong skeleton, the structure with sulfur and a hydroxyl on the carbonyl carbon is a 1,2-adduct that does not form, and the sulfoxide is an oxidation product, not an addition product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = beta-thioether); 5 valid SMILES; single concept, in scope. No issues."},{"group":"michael","q":"2-Cyclohexen-1-one is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li) at low temperature, then aqueous workup. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCC(C)C1","OC1(C)CCCC=C1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","OC1C(C)CCC=C1","O=C1CCCCC1C"],"correct":1,"why":"Methyllithium is a hard, highly reactive nucleophile, so it adds 1,2 to the carbonyl carbon rather than to the beta carbon. Workup gives 1-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-ol, a tertiary allylic alcohol with the ring double bond still in place. 3-Methylcyclohexanone is the 1,4-product a cuprate would give, 2-methylcyclohexanone has the methyl on the alpha carbon, 1-methylcyclohexanol would require the alkene to be reduced as well, and the secondary allylic alcohol with the methyl on the neighboring ring carbon has the methyl in the wrong place.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Keyed answer correct (opt0 = 1,2 tertiary allylic alcohol); 5 valid SMILES; in scope. No issues."},{"group":"michael","q":"For (E)-pent-3-en-2-one (drawn), which single reagent most reliably gives clean 1,4 (conjugate) addition rather than 1,2 addition to the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Lithium aluminum hydride, LiAlH4","Lithium diphenylcuprate, Ph2CuLi","Phenylmagnesium bromide, PhMgBr","Sodium borohydride then strong acid","Phenyllithium, PhLi"],"correct":1,"why":"Soft organocuprates (Gilman, R2CuLi) favor 1,4 conjugate addition; hard organolithiums and Grignards favor 1,2; LiAlH4 is a hydride reducer, not a carbon nucleophile. Ph2CuLi is the reagent of choice.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Reagent-selection item, opts_are_structures=false (text) with molecule drawn in q_smiles (valid). Exactly one defensible answer (opt0). No issues."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with the following sequence: (1) NaOEt, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br); (2) NaOH, H2O, heat; (3) H3O+, heat. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["OC(=O)C(Cc1ccccc1)Cc1ccccc1","OCCCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(Cc1ccccc1)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Malonate alpha-carbon is benzylated once, saponified to the substituted malonic acid, then thermal decarboxylation of one COOH gives PhCH2CH2COOH (3-phenylpropanoic acid). opt[0] matches. Distractors: phenylacetic acid (carbon short), the non-decarboxylated diacid, the dibenzyl product, and an alcohol (wrong functional class).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; standard malonic ester synthesis, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is subjected to: (1) NaOEt, then 1-bromopropane; (2) dilute NaOH; (3) H3O+, heat. Identify the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCC(C)=O","CCCCC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis: monoalkylation by propyl at the active-methylene carbon, then hydrolysis and decarboxylation of the beta-keto acid gives the methyl ketone CH3CO-CH2CH2CH2CH3 = 2-hexanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: carbon-short ketone, aldehyde, non-decarboxylated diketone, and 2-heptanone (extra carbon).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; canonical acetoacetic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with one equivalent of NaOEt followed by one equivalent of methyl iodide, then NaOH/H2O with heat, then H3O+/heat. What carboxylic acid is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Single methylation gives CH3CH(COOH)2 after hydrolysis; decarboxylation removes one COOH leaving CH3CH2COOH (propanoic acid). opt[0] matches. Distractors: acetic acid (no alkylation), butanoic acid (over-counts a carbon), isobutyric acid (dimethylation), and a methyl ester (incomplete hydrolysis).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; standard single-alkylation malonic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated with benzyl bromide after deprotonation by NaOEt, then hydrolyzed with aqueous acid and heated. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylation at the central carbon, then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives the methyl ketone CH3CO-CH2CH2Ph = 4-phenyl-2-butanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: phenylacetone (carbon short), benzaldehyde, the non-decarboxylated diketone, and a carboxylic acid (wrong product class for an acetoacetic ester).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; clean acetoacetic synthesis, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt/CH3I, then a second round of NaOEt/CH3I, then aqueous NaOH followed by H3O+ and heat. What carboxylic acid forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","CCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Two sequential methylations dimethylate the malonate carbon; hydrolysis and decarboxylation give (CH3)2CH-COOH = 2-methylpropanoic (isobutyric) acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: butanoic acid (one methylation), pivalic acid (three methyls), non-decarboxylated methylmalonic diacid, and propanoic acid.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; dialkylation + decarboxylation, two-concept but in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt and allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then with dilute acid and heat. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["C=CCCC(C)=O","OC(=O)CCC=C","C=CCC(C)=O","C=CCCCC(C)=O","C=CCC(=O)CC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"SN2 allylation at the active-methylene carbon (alkene untouched), then hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH2CH2CH=CH2 = hex-5-en-2-one. opt[0] matches. Distractors: carbon-short enone-ketone, carbon-long ketone, non-decarboxylated diketone, and a carboxylic acid (wrong class).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; chemoselective allylation + decarboxylation, in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (drawn), then NaOH/H2O, then H3O+/heat. What is the major product of this intramolecular dialkylation?","q_smiles":"BrCCCCBr","opts":["OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","OC(=O)C1CCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)C1(C(=O)O)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Both alpha-C-H positions are alkylated by the two ends of the dibromide, closing a ring; malonate carbon plus four CH2 units form a five-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. opt[0] matches. Distractors: cyclobutane (miscount), cyclohexane (extra carbon), gem-diacid (no decarboxylation), open-chain adipic acid (no cyclization).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; intramolecular dialkylation is hard but recognition-level and in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is alkylated sequentially: (1) NaOEt then CH3I; (2) NaOEt then CH3CH2I; then (3) dilute H2SO4 and heat. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["CCC(C)C(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O","CCC(C)C(=O)CC(C)=O","CCC(CC)C(C)=O","CC(=O)CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Sequential methylation then ethylation puts a methyl and an ethyl on the central carbon; hydrolysis/decarboxylation gives CH3CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3 = 3-methyl-2-pentanone. opt[0] matches. Distractors: 2-hexanone (one alkylation), non-decarboxylated diketone, 3-ethyl-2-pentanone (wrong group, two ethyls), and 4-methyl-2-pentanone (wrong substitution position).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct as keyed; dialkylation + decarboxylation, hard but in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with (1) NaOEt, then (2) one equivalent of 1-bromobutane, then (3) aqueous HCl with heat. What is the final organic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCCC(=O)O","CCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"This is the malonic ester synthesis. Ethoxide removes the doubly activated central hydrogen of diethyl malonate, and the enolate displaces bromide from 1-bromobutane (SN2) to give diethyl butylmalonate. Hot aqueous acid then hydrolyzes both esters, and the substituted malonic acid loses CO2 to leave a carboxylic acid with the butyl group on the alpha carbon: hexanoic acid, CH3(CH2)4COOH. Pentanoic and heptanoic acids miscount the chain, 2-methylpentanoic acid is branched where the product is not, and pentanal is the wrong oxidation state.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed product hexanoic acid (C6) matches n-butyl monoalkylation + decarboxylation. All SMILES valid, exactly one right answer, in scope, visual.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is subjected to (1) NaOEt, (2) one equivalent of 1-bromopropane, then (3) dilute aqueous acid with heat. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC","CC(=O)CCC","CC(=O)CCCC","CCC(=O)CCC","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives methyl ketone CH3-CO-CH2-R; R=n-propyl = 2-hexanone. B/C miscount (2-pentanone/2-heptanone), D is 3-hexanone (not a methyl ketone), E is the malonic-pathway carboxylic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed 2-hexanone matches methyl-ketone product of n-propyl alkylation. Valid SMILES, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with two equivalents of NaOEt and one equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (intramolecular dialkylation), followed by aqueous acid and heat. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","OC(=O)CCCCC","OC(=O)C1CCCCCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCCC1"],"correct":4,"why":"Dialkylation closes a ring: malonate carbon + 4 CH2 = 5-membered ring; after hydrolysis/decarboxylation one COOH remains = cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. B/C/E are off-ring-size, D is open-chain (no closure).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Ring count = central C + 4 CH2 = cyclopentane; keyed cyclopentanecarboxylic acid right. Valid SMILES, in scope (regio/ring-size, 1 concept), visual."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt, then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br), then warm aqueous acid. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Methyl-ketone product CH3-CO-CH2-R, R=benzyl = 4-phenyl-2-butanone. B drops a CH2 (phenylacetone), C is an ethyl ketone (wrong pattern), D adds a CH2, E is a carboxylic acid (malonic confusion).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed 4-phenyl-2-butanone matches benzyl alkylation methyl-ketone product. Valid SMILES, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt then iodomethane, and the resulting product is again treated with NaOEt then bromoethane; finally it is hydrolyzed and heated. What carboxylic acid forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(CC)C(=O)O","CC(C)CC(=O)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Sequential methyl + ethyl dialkylation; after decarboxylation the alpha carbon bears CH3, CH2CH3, H, COOH = 2-methylbutanoic acid. B adds a carbon, C uses two ethyls, D branches wrong (3-methyl), E omits methylation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Two-step alkylation (Me + Et) gives 2-methylbutanoic acid; keyed SMILES CCC(C)C(=O)O matches. Valid SMILES, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with two equivalents of base and one equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (intramolecular dialkylation), then warm aqueous acid. What is the product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)C1CCC1","OC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCC"],"correct":0,"why":"Alpha carbon bonds both ends of 1,3-dibromopropane: that C + 3 CH2 = 4-membered ring; acetyl group survives decarboxylation = acetylcyclobutane. B/C off-ring-size, D open-chain, E swaps methyl ketone for acid (wrong precursor).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Ring = alpha C + 3 CH2 = cyclobutane; keyed 1-cyclobutylethanone right. Valid SMILES, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt and then one equivalent of 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), followed by aqueous acid and heat. Which structure correctly shows the product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCC(=O)O","CCC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)CC(=O)O","CC(C)C(C)C(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"Isopropyl adds to the malonate carbon; after decarboxylation (CH3)2CH-CH2-COOH = 3-methylbutanoic acid (isovaleric). B over-methylates (2,3-dimethyl), C is 2-methylbutanoic (wrong connectivity), D too short, E straight-chain ignoring branch.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Keyed isovaleric acid CC(C)CC(=O)O matches isopropyl monoalkylation + decarboxylation. All SMILES valid, one answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"In a malonic ester synthesis, the enolate of diethyl malonate (shown) is alkylated at its central carbon to create a new stereocenter (for example, sequential methylation and ethylation followed by decarboxylation gives 2-methylbutanoic acid). What is the stereochemical outcome at that newly formed stereocenter, and why?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Only the R enantiomer forms, because alkylation always proceeds with retention of configuration","A single meso compound forms, because the two ester groups make the molecule internally symmetric","A racemic (50:50) mixture of R and S forms, because the planar sp2 enolate is attacked by the alkyl halide from both faces with equal probability","Only the S enantiomer forms, because the alkyl halide approaches anti to the larger ester group","An optically active single enantiomer forms, because the chiral base sets the configuration"],"correct":2,"why":"The nucleophilic enolate carbon is sp2/planar, so alkyl halide attack on each face is equally likely, generating the new stereocenter as a 50:50 racemate (optically inactive). No chiral influence is present, so single-enantiomer or retention options are wrong; a meso form is impossible with one stereocenter.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Correct. Planar-enolate-to-racemate reasoning is sound and DAT-level; single concept (stereo outcome of enolate alkylation), substrate drawn. Distractors plausibly wrong, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetic acid is heated with excess ethanol in the presence of a catalytic amount of concentrated H2SO4 (Fischer esterification). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOCC","CCOC(=O)CC","CC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)O","CCOC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification joins a carboxylic acid and an alcohol under acid catalysis with loss of water; the acyl group comes from the acid and the alkoxy group from the alcohol. Acetic acid plus ethanol therefore gives ethyl acetate, CH3CO2CH2CH3. Methyl acetate would require methanol, ethyl propanoate has a three-carbon acyl group, propanoic acid adds a carbon to the acid, and diethyl ether is a dehydration product of ethanol alone, not an ester.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: substrate and all 5 option SMILES valid; ethyl acetate (index 0) is the correct Fischer product; distractors map to wrong-alcohol, wrong-acid, ether, and wrong-acyl errors. Canonical, in-scope, visual."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is refluxed with methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4. Which structure is the major product of this Fischer esterification?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1C(=O)O","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1","COC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Under Fischer conditions the acid catalyst protonates the carbonyl oxygen, methanol attacks the carbonyl carbon, and loss of water gives methyl benzoate. The nucleophile attacks the carboxyl carbon, not the aromatic ring. Ethyl benzoate uses the wrong alcohol, 2-methoxybenzoic acid puts a methoxy on the ring while leaving the acid untouched, anisole has lost the carbonyl entirely, and methyl phenylacetate has an extra CH2 between the ring and the carbonyl.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: all SMILES valid; methyl benzoate (index 0) correct. Distractors are wrong-alcohol, ring-methoxylation, anisole, and chain-extension. In-scope recognition item, visual."},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is heated with aqueous H2SO4 (dilute acid, excess water). Which structure is the major organic product of this acid-catalyzed hydrolysis?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)OC","CCCO","CCC(=O)O","CCOC(C)=O","CCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed ester hydrolysis is the reverse of Fischer esterification: with excess water the equilibrium shifts toward the acid, cleaving the acyl-oxygen bond. Ethyl propanoate therefore gives propanoic acid (from the acyl part) and ethanol (from the alkoxy part); the carbonyl-containing product is propanoic acid. Methyl propanoate and ethyl acetate are different esters, not hydrolysis products, and 1-propanol would require a reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; propanoic acid (index 0) is the acyl-derived product, correctly keyed. Minor ambiguity: hydrolysis gives two organic products (acid + ethanol, an option D), but stem asks for the carbonyl/acyl product and the carboxylic acid is the conventional 'main product'; defensible single answer. Kept."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate is heated with aqueous NaOH (saponification), then the mixture is left basic. Which structure is the major form of the carboxyl-containing product under these conditions?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[Na+].[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","[Na+].CC[O-]","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","CCOc1ccccc1","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Saponification is base-promoted hydrolysis: hydroxide attacks the carbonyl, ethoxide is expelled, and the carboxylic acid that forms is immediately deprotonated by the base. While the mixture is still basic, the carboxyl-containing product is sodium benzoate, the carboxylate salt; neutral benzoic acid appears only after acidic workup. Sodium ethoxide is the alkoxide released from the alcohol part, sodium phenylacetate has an extra CH2, and ethyl phenyl ether is an ether, not a product of ester hydrolysis.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid (salts parse correctly). Two-concept (saponification + protonation state under basic conditions); the carboxylate-vs-neutral-acid distinction is the exact teaching point and gives one defensible answer (index 0). Stem explicitly specifies 'left basic', so benzoic acid is correctly the distractor. In-scope, visual."},{"q":"Methyl acetate is stirred with a large excess of ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed transesterification). Which structure is the major product favored at equilibrium?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)OC","CCOC(C)=O","CCO","CC(=O)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Transesterification exchanges the alkoxy group of an ester for that of the alcohol present in large excess; the acyl group is unchanged. With excess ethanol the methoxy of methyl acetate is replaced by ethoxy, so the equilibrium product is ethyl acetate plus methanol. Methyl acetate is the starting material, ethanol is the reagent, acetic acid would require water (hydrolysis) rather than alcohol exchange, and methyl propanoate has the wrong acyl group.","difficulty":1,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; ethyl acetate (index 0) is the Le-Chatelier-favored transesterification product, correctly keyed. Distractors are starting material, reactant, hydrolysis product, and wrong-acyl. In-scope, visual."},{"q":"Succinic acid (butanedioic acid) is refluxed with a large excess of ethanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)O","O=C1CCC(=O)O1","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","COC(=O)CCC(=O)OC"],"correct":2,"why":"With a large excess of ethanol both carboxyl groups undergo Fischer esterification, so succinic acid becomes diethyl succinate. The monoethyl ester is only the half-way product and is disfavored when alcohol is in excess, dimethyl succinate uses the wrong alcohol, diethyl malonate has one CH2 too few in the chain, and succinic anhydride is a dehydration product formed without any alcohol.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; chain lengths verified (succinate = 2 CH2 between carbonyls; malonate distractor D = 1 CH2). Diethyl diester (index 0) is the excess-driven product, correctly keyed. Two-concept (diacid + complete esterification). In-scope, visual."},{"q":"A single enantiomer of 2-phenylpropanoic acid (stereocenter shown) is heated with methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which structure is the major product, accounting for the stereochemistry at the alpha carbon?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification happens at the carboxyl carbon: methanol attacks the protonated carbonyl and water leaves. No bond to the alpha stereocenter is broken, so its spatial arrangement is retained, and the priority order around it (ester carbon above phenyl above methyl above hydrogen) is the same as in the acid, so the (S) acid gives (S)-methyl 2-phenylpropanoate. The (R) ester would require inversion at a carbon that never reacts, the version drawn without a defined stereocenter has thrown the stereochemistry away, the ethyl ester uses the wrong alcohol, and methyl 3-phenylpropanoate is a different, chain-extended skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified with RDKit: substrate = (S); keyed option A = (S) retention; option B = (R) inversion. Descriptor stays S because no bond to stereocenter breaks and CIP order is preserved. Stereochemistry/retention is recognition-level (not full mechanism), so in scope. Correct, one defensible answer, visual."},{"q":"Gamma-butyrolactone (a cyclic ester) is treated with aqueous NaOH and the mixture is kept strongly basic. Which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[Na+].[O-]CCCC(=O)O","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)CCCO","O=C1CCCO1","OC(=O)CCCO","[Na+].[O-]C(=O)CCO"],"correct":1,"why":"Hydroxide attacks the lactone carbonyl and cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond, opening the ring: the carbonyl becomes a carboxylate and the former ring oxygen becomes a terminal hydroxyl. Because the mixture is kept strongly basic, the carboxylic acid stays deprotonated while the alcohol does not, so the product is sodium 4-hydroxybutanoate, a carboxylate with a free primary OH. The neutral hydroxy acid would appear only after acidic workup, the alkoxide with a neutral acid has the wrong group deprotonated (a carboxylic acid is far more acidic than an alcohol), unreacted lactone would not survive hydroxide, and the three-carbon hydroxy carboxylate is one carbon short.","difficulty":2,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All SMILES valid; ring-opened 4-hydroxybutanoate (index 0) verified as correct (GBL is the 5-membered gamma-lactone; opening gives 4-carbon hydroxy acid). Distractor D correctly shortens chain, E mis-deprotonates the alcohol, C is starting material. Two-concept (lactone hydrolysis + salt form under base). In-scope, visual."},{"q":"The ester drawn below (methyl acetate) is the product of a Fischer esterification. Which combination of starting materials and conditions produces it?","q_smiles":"COC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Methanol + ethanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Acetic acid + methoxide ion (CH3O-), room temperature","Acetic acid + methanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Acetyl chloride + methanol, pyridine","Acetic acid + methanol, catalytic NaOH, heat"],"correct":2,"why":"Fischer esterification couples a carboxylic acid with an alcohol under acid catalysis and heat. Methyl acetate (CH3CO-OCH3) = acetic acid (acyl) + methanol (OCH3). Acyl chloride route makes the same ester but is not Fischer; base options are wrong (base saponifies esters).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (methyl acetate). Keyed answer 0 is the only Fischer route; distractors plausible-but-wrong. Keep."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate (drawn below) is heated with a large excess of ethanol and a trace of H2SO4, converting it to ethyl benzoate. What name best describes this reaction?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Fischer esterification","Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis","Saponification","Claisen condensation","Transesterification"],"correct":4,"why":"Converting one ester into another by swapping the alkoxy group (-OCH3 to -OCH2CH3) under acid catalysis with excess alcohol is transesterification. Fischer starts from an acid; saponification needs hydroxide; hydrolysis gives the acid; Claisen forms a beta-keto ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (methyl benzoate). Reaction-type item, single defensible answer (transesterification). Keep."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate (drawn below) is refluxed with aqueous NaOH. What is the correct name for this reaction, and what is the organic carboxyl-containing product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Saponification; product is acetic acid (neutral)","Transesterification; product is ethyl acetate","Acid hydrolysis; product is acetic acid","Saponification; product is sodium acetate (acetate ion)","Fischer esterification; product is acetic acid"],"correct":3,"why":"Base-promoted ester hydrolysis is saponification, made essentially irreversible because hydroxide deprotonates the acid to the carboxylate. Product is acetate ion (sodium acetate), not neutral acetic acid (which only appears after acidic workup).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetate). Salt-vs-neutral-acid distinction is sophomore-appropriate; only option 0 correct. Keep."},{"q":"You want to make ethyl benzoate (PhCO-OCH2CH3) from the carboxylic acid drawn below by Fischer esterification. Which alcohol and catalyst should you use?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Ethanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","1-Propanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Phenol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Methanol, catalytic H2SO4, heat","Ethanol, catalytic NaOH, heat"],"correct":0,"why":"The alcohol supplies the -OR group. Ethyl benzoate needs an ethoxy group, so use ethanol with acid catalysis and heat. Methanol/propanol/phenol give different esters; NaOH is a base and does not catalyze Fischer esterification.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (benzoic acid). Reagent-selection item, exactly one correct (ethanol/H2SO4). Keep."},{"q":"Fischer esterification of the carboxylic acid below with ethanol is a reversible, equilibrium reaction. Which manipulation will NOT help drive the equilibrium toward the ester product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Adding a drying agent / molecular sieves to absorb water","Using a large excess of ethanol","Adding aqueous NaOH to the reaction mixture","Using the alcohol as the solvent","Removing water as it forms (e.g., Dean-Stark trap)"],"correct":2,"why":"Excess alcohol and water removal are valid Le Chatelier tactics. Adding NaOH does the opposite: it neutralizes the acid catalyst and deprotonates the acid to an unreactive carboxylate (and promotes saponification of any ester). So NaOH does not drive ester formation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (propanoic acid). NEGATIVE-stem (NOT) item; only NaOH fails to help, others all valid. Single defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Consider tert-butyl acetate (drawn below) alongside methyl acetate and isopropyl acetate. Rank these three acetate esters from FASTEST to SLOWEST rate of saponification (NaOH-promoted hydrolysis).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["isopropyl acetate > methyl acetate > tert-butyl acetate","methyl acetate > isopropyl acetate > tert-butyl acetate","tert-butyl acetate > isopropyl acetate > methyl acetate","tert-butyl acetate > methyl acetate > isopropyl acetate","all three react at the same rate (same acyl group)"],"correct":1,"why":"Saponification goes through a tetrahedral intermediate; steric bulk of the alkoxy R group slows hydroxide attack. Bulk order methyl < isopropyl < tert-butyl gives rate order methyl > isopropyl > tert-butyl.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (tert-butyl acetate). Two-concept (mechanism + sterics) ranking; standard textbook trend, one correct order. Keep."},{"q":"The ester below (ethyl acetate) can be hydrolyzed under acidic OR basic conditions. Which statement correctly contrasts the two pathways?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both pathways are reversible equilibria with similar position","Acidic hydrolysis is reversible (it is the reverse of Fischer esterification); basic hydrolysis (saponification) is effectively irreversible","Acidic hydrolysis requires a stoichiometric amount of acid that is consumed","Saponification regenerates the neutral carboxylic acid directly without workup","Acidic hydrolysis is irreversible; saponification is a reversible equilibrium"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydrolysis is the microscopic reverse of Fischer esterification: the acid is only a catalyst, every step is reversible, and the position of equilibrium is set by how much water is present. Saponification is different because, once the ester is cleaved, hydroxide deprotonates the carboxylic acid to a resonance-stabilized carboxylate that no longer reacts with the alcohol; that final step pulls the reaction irreversibly to products. So the correct contrast is that acidic hydrolysis is reversible while saponification is effectively irreversible. The other statements get the reversibility backwards, treat the acid as a consumed reagent, or claim that saponification delivers the neutral acid without an acidic workup.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetate, parses as CCOC(C)=O). Conceptual contrast, only option 0 fully correct. Keep."},{"q":"gamma-Butyrolactone (the cyclic ester drawn below) is in equilibrium with its open-chain hydroxy acid form, 4-hydroxybutanoic acid. Which single set of conditions would open the lactone ring to give the carboxylate salt of that hydroxy acid in an essentially irreversible step?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Aqueous NaOH, heat (saponification of the lactone)","Pyridine, no water","Catalytic H2SO4 in excess methanol, heat","Excess ethanol with a Dean-Stark trap, catalytic H2SO4","Catalytic H2SO4 in water, heat"],"correct":0,"why":"A lactone is an intramolecular ester, so it saponifies. NaOH/heat opens the ring and deprotonates the acid to sodium 4-hydroxybutanoate (irreversible). Acidic water gives a reversible equilibrium favoring the 5-membered lactone; methanol/H2SO4 transesterifies; Dean-Stark/excess alcohol drives ring closure; pyridine does nothing.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Valid SMILES (gamma-butyrolactone, 5-membered ring). Recognition-level lactone saponification with Le Chatelier layer; at upper DAT edge but in scope (no mechanism/synthesis). Single correct answer. Keep."},{"q":"(R)-Lactic acid (drawn) is heated with excess methanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer esterification). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)O","COC(=O)[C@H](C)O","CO[C@H](C)C(=O)O","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)OC","CC(=O)OC(=O)[C@@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Fischer esterification converts only the carboxyl group into a methyl ester; the bond to the alpha carbon is never broken, so its spatial arrangement is unchanged, and the priorities (hydroxyl above the carbonyl carbon above methyl) are the same, so (R)-lactic acid gives (R)-methyl lactate. The secondary alcohol does not react under these conditions, which rules out the products in which the hydroxyl has been methylated (one of which still carries a free carboxylic acid) and the mixed anhydride, which would need an acylating agent rather than methanol. The (S) ester is the inverted epimer and cannot form because the stereocenter is a spectator.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM R and opt0 R confirmed by CIP; chemoselectivity + retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"cis-4-tert-Butylcyclohexyl acetate (drawn) is treated with aqueous NaOH, then mild acidic workup (saponification). Which alcohol is produced?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](OC)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@H](O)CC1","CC(C)(C)C1CCC(O)CC1","CC(C)(C)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](O)CC1","CC(=O)[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Saponification attacks the acetate carbonyl and cleaves the acyl-oxygen bond; the alkyl carbon-oxygen bond at the ring stereocenter is never touched. The ring carbon therefore keeps its configuration and the cis relationship between the oxygen and the tert-butyl group is preserved: the product is cis-4-tert-butylcyclohexanol (with the bulky tert-butyl locked equatorial, the OH sits axial). The trans alcohol would require inversion at a carbon that does not react, the alcohol drawn without stereochemistry does not answer the question, the methyl ether is not a hydrolysis product, and the methyl ketone would need a carbon-carbon bond to the ring that the ester never had.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. cis SM, cis product, trans trap verified by 3D embedding; acyl-O cleavage retention correct."},{"q":"Methyl (S)-2-phenylpropanoate (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and catalytic acid (transesterification). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)[C@@H](CC)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","C=C(C(=O)OCC)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed transesterification swaps the methoxy group at the carbonyl for ethoxy; the alpha stereocenter is a spectator, so its arrangement and its (S) label are retained, giving ethyl (S)-2-phenylpropanoate. The (R) ethyl ester is the inverted epimer, the methyl ester is unreacted starting material, ethyl 2-phenylbutanoate has an extra carbon on the alpha position, and the alpha,beta-unsaturated ester would require an elimination that does not occur here.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM S and opt0 S confirmed by CIP; transesterification retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"5-Hydroxypentanoic acid (drawn) is warmed with a trace of acid. Considering ring-size preference for intramolecular esterification, which lactone forms preferentially?","q_smiles":"OCCCCC(=O)O","opts":["O=C1CCCCO1","O=C1CCCCCO1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C1CCCO1","O=C(O)CCCCOC(=O)CCCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A 5-hydroxy acid has its hydroxyl five atoms from the carbonyl carbon, so intramolecular esterification closes a six-membered lactone: delta-valerolactone. Five- and six-membered lactones form readily; the five-membered gamma-butyrolactone would need a 4-hydroxy acid, the seven-membered epsilon-caprolactone would need a 6-hydroxy acid, cyclopentanone is a ketone rather than a lactone, and the open-chain ester dimer comes from intermolecular esterification, which cannot compete with the easy ring closure.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"Minor: stem parenthetical is slightly garbled wording, but answer is unambiguous (a 5-hydroxy acid can only cyclize to the 6-membered lactone). Chemistry correct."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (drawn) is refluxed with aqueous NaOH (saponification). Which species is the carbon-containing organic product isolated before acidification?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","O=C([O-])c1ccccc1.[Na+]","O=Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC[O-].[Na+]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Saponification is base-promoted and effectively irreversible because the carboxylic acid formed is deprotonated by hydroxide to the resonance-stabilized carboxylate. Before acidification, the isolated carboxyl-containing species is therefore sodium benzoate, not free benzoic acid, which appears only after acid workup. Sodium ethoxide is the alkoxide released from the alcohol part, benzaldehyde would require a reduction, and unreacted ethyl benzoate does not survive hot hydroxide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Carboxylate salt is the correct pre-acidification product; valid SMILES with Na+ counterion; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Propanoic acid (drawn) is esterified with CH3-18OH (oxygen-18 labeled methanol) and catalytic acid. In the resulting methyl propanoate, where does the 18O label end up?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["In the single-bonded ester oxygen (the -O-CH3 oxygen)","Split evenly between both ester oxygens","In the water molecule released","In the carbonyl (C=O) oxygen","No 18O is incorporated into the ester"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Classic isotope-labeling result: the alcohol oxygen adds to the carbonyl carbon and is retained as the new alkyl C-O bond, while water leaves carrying the acid's -OH oxygen (acyl-oxygen cleavage on the acid). So 18O from methanol ends up in the single-bonded -O-CH3 ester oxygen, not the carbonyl O and not the water.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Standard textbook isotope-tracing fact; recognition-level, in scope; molecule drawn in q_smiles; one defensible answer."},{"q":"(S)-Mandelic acid (drawn) is heated with methanol and catalytic HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)O","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1","COC(C(=O)O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)OC(=O)[C@@H](O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Only the carboxyl group esterifies under Fischer conditions: the methyl ester forms at the carbonyl carbon, while the benzylic secondary hydroxyl is a spectator and the stereocenter keeps its spatial arrangement. Because the priority order (hydroxyl above the carbonyl carbon above phenyl) is unchanged, (S)-mandelic acid gives (S)-methyl mandelate. The (R) ester would need inversion at a carbon that never reacts, the alpha-methoxy acid methylates the wrong oxygen and leaves the acid intact, the ethyl ester uses the wrong alcohol, and the mixed anhydride would require an acetylating agent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. SM S and opt0 S confirmed by CIP; chemoselectivity + retention correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"gamma-Butyrolactone (drawn) is heated with excess methanol and catalytic H2SO4. Which open-chain structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCO1","opts":["O=C1CCCO1","COC(=O)CCCO","O=C(O)CCCO","COCCCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)CCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed methanolysis opens the lactone by acyl-oxygen cleavage: methanol attacks the carbonyl and the ring oxygen is released as a free hydroxyl. The carbonyl ends up as a methyl ester and the former ring oxygen as a terminal OH, giving methyl 4-hydroxybutanoate (the lactone plus one methanol). The hydroxy acid is the product of hydrolysis with water, the structure with both a methyl ester and a methyl ether has also methylated the alcohol (which does not happen here), the three-carbon hydroxy ester is one carbon short, and the unopened lactone is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"ester-formation-hydrolysis","issue":"None. Formula and connectivity of methyl 4-hydroxybutanoate confirmed; ring-opening transesterification correct; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Acetyl chloride is treated with ethanol (and a base such as pyridine). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)O","CCOCC","CCC(=O)OC","CCOC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"An acid chloride is the most reactive carboxylic acid derivative, so ethanol attacks the carbonyl carbon and chloride is expelled; pyridine absorbs the HCl. The product is ethyl acetate, with the acetyl group from the acid chloride and the ethoxy group from ethanol. Acetic anhydride would need a carboxylate nucleophile, acetic acid would need water, diethyl ether contains no carbonyl, and methyl propanoate has the acyl and alkoxy carbons scrambled.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzoyl chloride is treated with excess dimethylamine. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(N(C)C)c1ccccc1","O=C(NC)c1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Dimethylamine, a secondary amine, attacks the carbonyl carbon of benzoyl chloride and chloride leaves; the excess amine absorbs the HCl. The product is the tertiary amide N,N-dimethylbenzamide. N-Methylbenzamide would come from methylamine (only one substituent on nitrogen), methyl benzoate would require methanol, benzoic acid would require water, and N,N-dimethylbenzylamine has no carbonyl (that is what reducing the amide gives, not what making it gives).","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride reacts with methanol. What is the major ester product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CCOC(C)=O","COC=O","CNC(C)=O","COC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Methanol attacks one carbonyl of acetic anhydride and acetate leaves, giving methyl acetate plus acetic acid as the by-product. Ethyl acetate would require ethanol, methyl formate has only one carbon in its acyl group, N-methylacetamide would require methylamine, and the unchanged anhydride is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with two equivalents of methylmagnesium bromide (CH3MgBr), then aqueous acid workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)O","CCC(C)(C)O","CCO","CC(C)(C)O","CC(C)=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Esters consume two equivalents of a Grignard reagent. The first methyl adds to the carbonyl and ethoxide is expelled, giving acetone; because a ketone is more electrophilic than the starting ester, the second methyl adds immediately to that ketone. Aqueous workup then gives the tertiary alcohol 2-methyl-2-propanol (tert-butanol), two of whose three methyls came from the Grignard reagent. Acetone cannot be isolated because excess Grignard is present, 2-propanol would require a hydride rather than a methyl nucleophile, 2-methyl-2-butanol would need an ethyl ketone that is never formed, and ethanol is only the alcohol released from the ester.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); two-concept (acyl sub + 2nd addition) acceptable, in scope."},{"q":"Acetamide is heated with aqueous HCl (acidic hydrolysis). What is the major organic product derived from the carbonyl?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)Cl","CC(=O)O","CCO","CC#N"],"correct":2,"why":"Acidic hydrolysis of an amide replaces the nitrogen (released as ammonium) with a hydroxyl, so acetamide gives acetic acid. Hydrolysis cannot run uphill to the more reactive acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride, ethanol is the wrong oxidation state (that would require a reduction), and acetonitrile is a dehydration product of the amide, the opposite of hydrolysis.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Propanoyl chloride is treated with sodium acetate (CH3CO2Na). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)OC(C)=O","CCC(=O)OC(=O)CC","CCC(=O)O","CC(=O)OC(C)=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Sodium acetate supplies a carboxylate nucleophile, which attacks the propanoyl chloride carbonyl and displaces chloride to give an anhydride. Because the two acyl groups come from different acids, the product is the mixed anhydride, acetic propanoic anhydride (CH3CO-O-COCH2CH3). The symmetrical propanoic anhydride would need a second propanoyl unit, acetic anhydride contains no propanoyl group at all, the methyl ester would need methanol, and propanoic acid would need water.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); mixed-anhydride product correctly distinguished from symmetric distractor."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product from the acyl carbon?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","CO","OCc1ccccc1","OC(O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride reduces an ester completely: hydride adds to the carbonyl, methoxide is expelled to give benzaldehyde as a transient intermediate, and a second hydride immediately reduces that aldehyde. After workup the product from the acyl carbon is benzyl alcohol, with methanol released as the alcohol fragment. The reduction cannot be stopped at the aldehyde with this reagent, toluene would require removing the oxygen entirely, the gem-diol is not an isolable species, and methanol is only the leaving fragment, not the acyl-derived product.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0), valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride (drawn) is treated with methanol. The bond to the stereocenter is never broken. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Methanol attacks the carbonyl carbon of the acid chloride and chloride leaves; this nucleophilic acyl substitution happens entirely at the carbonyl, so the alpha stereocenter is a spectator and keeps its spatial arrangement. The priority order around that carbon is also unchanged (the carbonyl carbon outranks phenyl, which outranks methyl), so the (S) acid chloride gives (S)-methyl 2-phenylpropanoate. The (R) ester would require inversion at a carbon that never reacts, the ester drawn without stereochemistry represents a racemate that does not form, the ethyl ester uses the wrong alcohol, and the carboxylic acid would require water.","difficulty":3,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct key (0); RDKit verifies S substrate -> S product (retention), opt1 correctly R-inverted."},{"q":"The acid chloride shown is treated with excess dimethylamine, (CH3)2NH. What is the organic product of this nucleophilic acyl substitution?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["CC(=O)N","CC(=O)OC(=O)C","CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)N(C)C","CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetyl chloride undergoes nucleophilic acyl substitution: the secondary amine adds to the carbonyl carbon, chloride is expelled, and the excess amine neutralizes the HCl. Because both methyls stay on nitrogen, the product is the tertiary amide N,N-dimethylacetamide. Acetamide would come from ammonia, ethyl acetate from ethanol, acetic acid from water, and acetic anhydride from an acetate nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. All 5 option SMILES valid; index 0 (N,N-dimethylacetamide) is the right product; distractors (acid, ester, primary amide, anhydride) plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"Rank the four carboxylic acid derivatives shown by their reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution, from MOST reactive to LEAST reactive: acetyl chloride (CH3COCl), acetic anhydride [(CH3CO)2O], ethyl acetate (CH3CO2Et), and acetamide (CH3CONH2).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl.CC(=O)OC(=O)C.CC(=O)OCC.CC(=O)N","opts":["anhydride > acid chloride > ester > amide","ester > amide > anhydride > acid chloride","acid chloride > ester > anhydride > amide","acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide","amide > ester > anhydride > acid chloride"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution is set by how good the leaving group is and how strongly the heteroatom donates its lone pair into the carbonyl. Chloride is an excellent leaving group with little donation, so acid chlorides are most reactive; a carboxylate is a good leaving group, so anhydrides come next; alkoxide is a poor leaving group and oxygen donates strongly, so esters are slower; and amides, with the strongest resonance donation from nitrogen and the worst leaving group, are least reactive. The order is acid chloride, then anhydride, then ester, then amide. Any ranking that puts the anhydride above the acid chloride, the ester above the anhydride, or the amide anywhere but last is wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Multi-molecule q_smiles parses; standard order; only index 0 is defensible."},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (shown) is heated with aqueous NaOH, then acidified on workup. What name best describes this reaction, and what carbonyl-containing product is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C(OCC)c1ccccc1","opts":["Reduction; benzaldehyde","Saponification (base-promoted ester hydrolysis); benzoic acid","Transesterification; methyl benzoate","Friedel-Crafts acylation; an aryl ketone","Fischer esterification; ethyl benzoate is regenerated"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Heating an ester with aqueous hydroxide is saponification, a base-promoted ester hydrolysis: hydroxide attacks the carbonyl carbon, ethoxide is expelled, and the benzoic acid formed is deprotonated to sodium benzoate. Acidifying on workup protonates the carboxylate, so the isolated carbonyl product is benzoic acid. No hydride is present, so there is no reduction to benzaldehyde; no alcohol is present for transesterification; Friedel-Crafts acylation would need an acid chloride and a Lewis acid; and Fischer esterification runs in the opposite direction, under acid rather than base.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; one defensible answer."},{"q":"A student wants to convert the carboxylic acid CH3CH2COOH directly into the ester CH3CH2COOCH2CH3 in one step. Which reagent combination accomplishes this Fischer esterification?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["SOCl2 only","Aqueous NaOH, then H3O+","LiAlH4, then H2O","Excess CH3CH2OH with catalytic H2SO4, heat","CH3CH2OH with catalytic NaOH, heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Fischer esterification is the direct, acid-catalyzed condensation of a carboxylic acid with an alcohol. Heating propanoic acid with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of sulfuric acid protonates the carbonyl so ethanol can attack, and the excess alcohol drives the reversible equilibrium toward ethyl propanoate. Thionyl chloride alone makes the acid chloride, not the ester; aqueous hydroxide followed by acid just regenerates the acid; lithium aluminum hydride reduces the acid to 1-propanol; and ethanol with catalytic base cannot esterify an acid, because the base simply forms the unreactive carboxylate.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles (propanoic acid) valid; single defensible answer."},{"q":"The acid chloride shown is treated with one equivalent of ethanol (and a base such as pyridine to absorb HCl). Draw the major organic product.","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts":["CCC(=O)N","CCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC(=O)CC","CCC(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)NCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Propanoyl chloride is the most reactive acyl derivative, so ethanol attacks the carbonyl carbon, chloride leaves, and pyridine absorbs the HCl. The product is the ester ethyl propanoate, with the propanoyl group from the acid chloride and the ethoxy group from ethanol. Propanamide and N-ethylpropanamide would require ammonia or ethylamine, propanoic acid would require water, and propanoic anhydride would require a propanoate carboxylate as the nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. All 5 option SMILES valid; index 0 is ethyl propanoate; distractors plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride (shown) reacts with methanol to give methyl acetate plus a second product. Identify the reaction class and the by-product.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(=O)C","opts":["Electrophilic addition; CO2 is the by-product","Nucleophilic acyl substitution; methane is the by-product","Nucleophilic acyl substitution; acetic acid is the by-product","Saponification; sodium acetate is the by-product","Esterification; water is the by-product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Methanol attacks one carbonyl carbon of acetic anhydride, a tetrahedral intermediate forms, and acetate is expelled as the leaving group: a nucleophilic acyl substitution. The acetate picks up the proton from methanol, so the by-product is acetic acid alongside methyl acetate. There is no addition across a double bond and no CO2 or methane is formed, saponification would need hydroxide, and although an ester is made, water is not the by-product (that is true of Fischer esterification from the acid, not from the anhydride).","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; one defensible answer; two-concept (class + by-product) within scope."},{"q":"Two esters are compared under identical basic hydrolysis (saponification) conditions: phenyl acetate (CH3CO-O-C6H5, shown) and ethyl acetate (CH3CO-O-CH2CH3). Which ester hydrolyzes FASTER, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Phenyl acetate, because the aromatic ring donates electron density into the carbonyl","Phenyl acetate, because phenoxide is a more stable (better) leaving group than ethoxide","Ethyl acetate, because ethoxide is a better leaving group than phenoxide","They hydrolyze at the same rate; the leaving group does not matter","Ethyl acetate, because the smaller alkyl group makes the carbonyl more electrophilic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In saponification hydroxide adds to the carbonyl and the tetrahedral intermediate then expels the alkoxide or aryloxide; the ester with the better (more stable, less basic) leaving group reacts faster. Phenoxide is resonance-stabilized and far less basic than ethoxide, so phenyl acetate hydrolyzes faster than ethyl acetate. The aromatic ring does not donate electron density into this carbonyl (the oxygen lone pair is shared with the ring instead), ethoxide is the poorer leaving group, the leaving group certainly matters, and the small ethyl group does not make ethyl acetate more electrophilic.","difficulty":2,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; leaving-group-stability reasoning is sophomore-level; one defensible answer."},{"q":"N-Methylacetamide (shown) is the least reactive of the common acyl derivatives. Which single statement BEST explains why amides resist nucleophilic acyl substitution and require harsh (strong acid or base, heat) hydrolysis conditions?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)NC","opts":["The methyl group on nitrogen sterically shields the carbonyl completely","Nitrogen is more electronegative than oxygen, so it withdraws electrons and stabilizes the tetrahedral intermediate","The C-N bond is a true double bond that cannot be broken under any conditions","The nitrogen lone pair is strongly donated into the carbonyl (amide resonance), lowering carbonyl electrophilicity, and amide (NR2-) is the poorest leaving group","Amides are protonated at carbon, blocking nucleophilic attack"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The nitrogen lone pair of an amide is delocalized into the carbonyl (amide resonance), which gives the C-N bond partial double-bond character, makes the carbonyl carbon much less electrophilic, and leaves the amide anion as the poorest leaving group among the common acyl derivatives. That combination is why amides need strong acid or base plus heat to hydrolyze. A single methyl on nitrogen does not sterically block the carbonyl, nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen (so it donates rather than withdraws), the C-N bond is only a partial double bond and can be broken, and amides protonate on oxygen, not carbon.","difficulty":3,"group":"acyl-substitution","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. q_smiles valid; recognition-level amide-resonance concept (no FMO/quant), in DAT scope; one defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzoyl chloride is treated with methanol (and a trace of pyridine to neutralize HCl). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(Cl)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","COC(O)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","COc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Alcohol plus acid chloride is a textbook nucleophilic acyl substitution: methanol attacks the carbonyl carbon, chloride is expelled, and pyridine absorbs the HCl. The product is methyl benzoate. Benzoic acid would require water, N-methylbenzamide would require methylamine, the hemiacetal-type structure cannot form from an acyl carbon, and anisole has no carbonyl at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed answer correct, single concept, all SMILES valid, visual."},{"q":"Acetic anhydride is treated with excess ammonia (NH3). Which structure is the nitrogen-containing organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OC(C)=O","opts":["CCN","CC(N)=O","CCOC(C)=O","CC(=O)OC(C)=O","CC(=O)NC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Ammonia attacks one carbonyl of acetic anhydride, acetate is expelled, and the nitrogen-containing product is acetamide (with acetic acid, or ammonium acetate in excess ammonia, as the by-product). Under these simple conditions the reaction does not continue to the imide, diacetamide, which requires forcing conditions; ethylamine would require a reduction, ethyl acetate would require ethanol, and the unchanged anhydride is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed answer (acetamide) correct; imide distractor properly excluded; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The four common carboxylic-acid derivatives shown by their general reactivity are acid chloride, anhydride, ester, and amide. Acetyl chloride is drawn. Which lists these derivatives in order of DECREASING reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["ester > acid chloride > amide > anhydride","acid chloride > ester > anhydride > amide","acid chloride > anhydride > ester > amide","anhydride > acid chloride > amide > ester","amide > ester > anhydride > acid chloride"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Reactivity toward nucleophilic acyl substitution decreases as the leaving group gets worse and as the heteroatom donates more strongly into the carbonyl: acid chloride, then anhydride, then ester, then amide. Chloride leaves most easily, carboxylate is next, alkoxide is poor, and the amide anion is the worst, with the nitrogen lone pair also stabilizing the amide the most. The other orders put the ester or the anhydride above the acid chloride, place the amide somewhere other than last, or reverse the series outright.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Canonical ordering correct; one defensible answer; substrate drawn."},{"q":"Propyl acetate is heated with aqueous NaOH (saponification). Which structure is the carbonyl-containing product?","q_smiles":"CCCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCCO","CCC(=O)[O-].[Na+]","CC(=O)Cl","CC(=O)OCCC","CC(=O)[O-].[Na+]"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"In the saponification of propyl acetate, hydroxide attacks the carbonyl carbon, propoxide is expelled, and the acetic acid formed is deprotonated by the base. The acyl half of this ester is acetyl, so the carbonyl-containing product is sodium acetate. 1-Propanol is the alcohol by-product (it has no carbonyl), sodium propanoate has the wrong skeleton (the propyl group was the alkoxy part, not the acyl part), unchanged propyl acetate is the starting material, and acetyl chloride would require going uphill in reactivity.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed sodium acetate correct; 'carbonyl-containing' qualifier removes ambiguity vs propanol; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"(R)-2-phenylpropanoyl chloride is treated with excess methylamine (CH3NH2). The alpha carbon (bearing CH3, phenyl, H) is a stereocenter and is NOT the reacting center. Which structure is the product, with correct configuration?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](C(=O)Cl)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","CNC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","C[C@@H](C(=O)O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Methylamine attacks the carbonyl carbon of the acid chloride and chloride leaves; the alpha carbon never reacts, so its spatial arrangement is retained. Replacing chloride with the methylamino group does not change the priority order around the stereocenter (the carbonyl carbon still outranks phenyl, which outranks methyl), so the (R) acid chloride gives (R)-N-methyl-2-phenylpropanamide. The (S) amide is the inverted epimer, the methyl ester would require methanol, the carboxylic acid would require water, and the amine lacking a carbonyl would require a subsequent reduction.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Verified by RDKit: retention product = keyed opt0, label stays R; inverted distractor = S; two-concept (acyl substitution + stereo retention) within scope."},{"q":"In the addition-elimination mechanism of nucleophilic acyl substitution on acetyl chloride (shown), consider the very FIRST mechanistic step. Which curved arrow correctly describes it?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Cl","opts":["The carbonyl oxygen's lone pair attacks the nucleophile","The nucleophile attacks chlorine directly, displacing the acyl group","The C-Cl bond breaks first to give an acylium cation, then the nucleophile adds","A lone pair on the nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon while the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen","A C-H bond of the methyl group breaks to form an enolate"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Addition-elimination begins with addition: a lone pair on the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbonyl carbon while the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen, giving the tetrahedral intermediate. Only after that does chloride leave as the C=O re-forms. Acid chlorides do not ionize to an acylium cation under ordinary nucleophilic conditions, the nucleophile does not attack chlorine or the carbonyl oxygen, and no enolate forms in this mechanism.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Single-arrow identification is explicitly in scope; correct first step keyed; substrate drawn."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is heated with excess dimethylamine (HN(CH3)2). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["COC(=O)c1ccccc1","COC(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","CN(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Aminolysis converts an ester into the more stable amide: dimethylamine attacks the carbonyl carbon, a tetrahedral intermediate forms, and methoxide leaves. Because the nitrogen keeps both methyl groups, the product is N,N-dimethylbenzamide. N-Methylbenzamide would require methylamine, benzoic acid would require hydrolysis, methyl benzoate is the unchanged starting material, and the amino ether (an aminal-type structure) is a tetrahedral species, not a stable product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed N,N-dimethylbenzamide correct; N-methyl distractor properly differs by substitution; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Succinic anhydride (the cyclic anhydride shown) is treated with exactly 1 equivalent of methanol. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(=O)O1","opts":["O=C1CCC(=O)O1","COC(=O)CCC(=O)OC","COC(=O)CC(=O)O","O=C(O)CCC(=O)O","COC(=O)CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"One equivalent of methanol opens the cyclic anhydride once: methanol attacks one carbonyl, and the ring carbon-oxygen bond that breaks becomes a free carboxylic acid at the other end. The product is monomethyl succinate (4-methoxy-4-oxobutanoic acid), with one methyl ester and one COOH on a four-carbon chain. The diester would need a second equivalent of alcohol and an activated acid, succinic acid is the product of adding water instead of methanol, the unopened anhydride is the starting material, and the malonate monoester has a three-carbon chain, one too few.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acyl-substitution","issue":"None. Keyed monomethyl succinate verified by substructure/chain-length check; distractors plausible; single ring-opening event within scope."},{"q":"The carboxylic acid shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCCC=O","CCC(C)O","CCC=O","CCCCO","CCCCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid fully to a primary (1 degree) alcohol; carbon count unchanged, so butanoic acid gives 1-butanol (CCCCO). LiAlH4 cannot stop at the aldehyde, reduction is at the carbonyl carbon (not C2), and no carbons are gained or lost.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (1-butanol) is the correct full-reduction product. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"The tertiary amide shown is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C(N(C)C)c1ccccc1","opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CN(C)c1ccccc1","CN(C)C(O)c1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride reduces an amide differently from an ester: the carbonyl is converted to a CH2 and the nitrogen is retained, because the tetrahedral intermediate loses its oxygen (as an aluminum alkoxide) to form an iminium ion that hydride then reduces. N,N-Dimethylbenzamide therefore gives N,N-dimethylbenzylamine. Benzyl alcohol and benzaldehyde would require cleaving the C-N bond, which does not happen; N,N-dimethylaniline has lost the carbonyl carbon entirely; and the hemiaminal is an intermediate-type structure that is not isolated.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (amide to amine) is correct. Distractors are the acid-style products, classic traps."},{"q":"The methyl ester shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product (the alcohol derived from the acyl portion)?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCCO","CCC(C)O","CCC=O","CCCOCC","CCCOC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an ester to a primary alcohol, cleaving the acyl C-O and converting the acyl carbon to CH2OH; methyl propanoate gives 1-propanol (CCCO). No ether product (C-O is cleaved), reduction passes the aldehyde, and no rearrangement to a 2 degree alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (1-propanol) correct. Stem specifies acyl-portion alcohol, removing methanol ambiguity. In scope."},{"q":"The beta-keto acid shown is heated (no other reagents). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)CC=O","CC(O)CC(=O)O","CC(C)=O","CCC(=O)O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A beta-keto acid decarboxylates on gentle heating through a six-membered cyclic transition state: the acid hydrogen transfers to the ketone oxygen as CO2 is lost, giving an enol that tautomerizes to the ketone. 3-Oxobutanoic acid (acetoacetic acid) therefore gives acetone. The ketone is not reduced, so the beta-hydroxy acid is wrong; the aldehyde would require reducing the carboxyl rather than losing it; propanoic acid keeps the wrong carbon; and butan-2-one would need the skeleton to grow rather than shrink.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (acetone) correct. Decarboxylation recognition, single concept, visual, in scope (no full mechanism required)."},{"q":"The substituted beta-keto acid shown is heated. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C(C)C(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)C(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C=O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC=O","CCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"2-Methyl-3-oxobutanoic acid is a beta-keto acid, so heating loses CO2 through the cyclic transition state and the alpha carbon picks up a hydrogen via the enol. The ketone is retained, and the product is butan-2-one (methyl ethyl ketone): the methyl, the carbonyl, and the former alpha carbon with its methyl make a four-carbon chain. The starting acid does not survive heating, and the aldehydes and butanoic acid would require changing the oxidation state at the carbonyl, which does not happen in a decarboxylation.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (butan-2-one) correct. Two layered (recognize beta-keto + build correct skeleton). In scope."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the aromatic carboxylic acid to a primary benzylic alcohol; benzoic acid gives benzyl alcohol (OCc1ccccc1). The ring is inert to LiAlH4 (no toluene), reduction does not stop at benzaldehyde, no carbon is added (not 2-phenylethanol), and the carbon is not lost to phenol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (benzyl alcohol) correct. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCCC1","OCCC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O=CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The COOH carbon is reduced to a CH2OH while remaining attached to the ring, giving cyclohexylmethanol (OCC1CCCCC1, a primary alcohol). The carboxyl carbon stays outside the ring (not cyclohexanol), reduction passes the aldehyde, no extra carbon added, and the C-O is not removed (not methylcyclohexane).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid; keyed index 0 (cyclohexylmethanol) correct. Cyclohexanol distractor tests the common carbon-count error. In scope."},{"q":"(S)-2-phenylpropanamide is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. The stereocenter is at the carbon alpha to the carbonyl. What is the major organic product, including correct configuration?","q_smiles":"O=C(N)[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","OC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","O=C[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","NC[C@@H](C)c1ccccc1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride reduces a primary amide to a primary amine: the carbonyl becomes a CH2 and the nitrogen is retained, so 2-phenylpropanamide gives 2-phenylpropan-1-amine. No bond to the alpha stereocenter is broken, so its spatial arrangement is retained; the aminomethyl group still outranks phenyl in priority, just as the carboxamide did, so the (S) amide gives (S)-2-phenylpropan-1-amine. The (R) amine is the inverted epimer, 2-phenylpropan-1-ol is what the corresponding acid or ester would give, the aldehyde is not what this reagent produces from an amide, and the structure with a methyl on the ring has a different skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","issue":"All SMILES valid. RDKit: substrate S, keyed option 0 S (spatial retention; CIP label stays S because C(=O)NH2 to CH2NH2 preserves priority order), option 1 R. Keyed index 0 correct. Two layered (reduction + stereo retention), within allowance; in scope."},{"q":"The carboxylic acid shown is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product?","opts":["The unchanged carboxylic acid (no reaction)","A primary alcohol (1-hexanol)","An aldehyde (hexanal)","A secondary alcohol","A methyl ketone"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid all the way to a primary alcohol (it does not stop at the aldehyde). Hexanoic acid gives 1-hexanol.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCCCCC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. LiAlH4 fully reduces RCOOH to RCH2OH; hexanoic acid -> 1-hexanol. Valid SMILES, single concept, in scope, distractors plausible."},{"q":"Which reagent converts the amide shown into benzylamine (PhCH2NH2)?","opts":["Dilute aqueous HCl, heat","H2/Pd-C","LiAlH4, then H2O","PCC in CH2Cl2","NaBH4 in methanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the nitrogen, giving an amine. NaBH4 is too weak to reduce amides; the others do not perform this reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Benzamide + LiAlH4 -> benzylamine; NaBH4 too weak, others do not reduce amide C=O. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The ester shown is treated with LiAlH4 followed by aqueous workup. Which classes of products are formed?","opts":["An aldehyde plus an alcohol","No reaction occurs","A carboxylic acid plus an alcohol","A single ketone","Two alcohols: a primary alcohol from the acyl portion plus the alcohol from the -OR group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 cleaves an ester to give a primary alcohol from the acyl carbon (1-propanol here) and releases the alkoxy group as its alcohol (ethanol). It does not stop at the aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Ethyl propanoate + LiAlH4 -> 1-propanol (acyl) + ethanol (alkoxy), two alcohols. SMILES valid, standard textbook reaction."},{"q":"The compound shown (a beta-keto acid) is heated. What reaction occurs and what carbonyl product results?","opts":["Decarboxylation, losing CO2 to give acetone","No reaction; beta-keto acids are thermally stable","Reduction to a 1,3-diol","Aldol condensation to a larger ring","Decarboxylation to give acetic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A beta-keto acid undergoes thermal decarboxylation through a six-membered cyclic transition state, expelling CO2. 3-oxobutanoic (acetoacetic) acid loses CO2 to give acetone.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Acetoacetic acid -> acetone + CO2 via 6-membered cyclic TS. Valid SMILES, classic decarboxylation, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Which statement best explains why the substituted malonic acid shown readily loses CO2 when heated, whereas a simple acid like propanoic acid does not?","opts":["A carbonyl (the second COOH) is positioned beta to the carboxyl being lost, enabling a six-membered cyclic transition state","Steric strain from the methyl group forces CO2 out","The alpha C-H bonds are unusually weak due to ring strain","The molecule is aromatic and decarboxylation restores aromaticity","Decarboxylation is driven by formation of a primary carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Malonic (1,3-diacid) and beta-keto acids decarboxylate because the second carbonyl sits beta to the carboxyl, allowing a cyclic six-membered transition state and an enol intermediate. A simple monoacid has no beta carbonyl, so it is thermally stable.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = methylmalonic acid, a genuine substituted malonic acid; second carbonyl is beta to the carboxyl lost, enabling 6-membered TS. Distractors (aromaticity, carbocation) are plausibly wrong. In scope (conceptual, no mechanism drawing)."},{"q":"Rank the following carbonyl compounds by how readily their C=O is reduced by LiAlH4 (fastest to slowest): the acyl chloride shown, an amide, and a carboxylate salt (RCOO-).","opts":["Amide > acyl chloride > carboxylate salt","Carboxylate salt > amide > acyl chloride","Acyl chloride > amide > carboxylate salt","All three react at essentially the same rate","Carboxylate salt > acyl chloride > amide"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Reactivity tracks electrophilicity of the carbonyl: acyl chloride is most reactive, amide is sluggish (resonance-stabilized N), and a deprotonated carboxylate is least electrophilic (negative charge repels hydride). The acyl chloride reacts fastest.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)Cl","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard carbonyl reactivity order acyl chloride > amide > carboxylate. Valid SMILES (butanoyl chloride), one defensible ranking."},{"q":"The tertiary amide shown is reduced with LiAlH4 and worked up. What is the product class, and does the nitrogen substitution change?","opts":["A primary alcohol; the C-N bond is broken","A primary amine; the N-methyl groups are cleaved off","A tertiary amine; the C=O becomes CH2 and the N-CH3 groups are retained","A secondary amine; one N-methyl group is lost","No reaction; tertiary amides are inert to LiAlH4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene without cleaving the C-N bond, so an N,N-dimethyl amide gives an N,N-dimethyl (tertiary) amine. The nitrogen substitution pattern is preserved.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. N,N-dimethylbenzamide + LiAlH4 -> N,N-dimethylbenzylamine (tertiary amine), N-substitution retained. Valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Two keto acids are considered for thermal decarboxylation: the gamma-keto acid shown (4-oxopentanoic / levulinic acid) and 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoic acid (a beta-keto acid). Which statement is correct?","opts":["The gamma-keto acid decarboxylates faster because the carbonyl is farther from the carboxyl","Only the beta-keto acid decarboxylates readily on heating; the gamma-keto acid does not, because its carbonyl is too far for a six-membered cyclic transition state","Only the gamma-keto acid decarboxylates, giving a cyclic product","Both decarboxylate equally well because both contain a ketone","Neither decarboxylates; a ketone cannot assist loss of CO2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Facile thermal decarboxylation requires the second carbonyl to be beta to the carboxyl so a six-membered cyclic transition state can form. The beta-keto acid satisfies this; in the gamma-keto acid the ketone is one carbon too far, so no low-energy cyclic TS exists.","difficulty":3,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. SMILES = levulinic acid (4-oxopentanoic, gamma-keto); ketone is gamma to carboxyl, too far for 6-membered TS, so it does not readily decarboxylate while the beta-keto acid does. Valid SMILES, comparison distractors plausible, in scope."},{"q":"Butanoic acid is treated with excess LiAlH4 in dry ether, followed by aqueous workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC(C)=O","CCCC(O)O","CCCC=O","CCC(C)O","CCCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride reduces a carboxylic acid all the way to the primary alcohol: after the acid is deprotonated, hydride adds to the carbonyl, the intermediate collapses to the aldehyde, and a second hydride reduces that immediately. Butanoic acid therefore gives 1-butanol. Butanal cannot be isolated with this reagent, 2-butanol puts the hydroxyl on the wrong carbon, butan-2-one is the wrong skeleton and oxidation state, and the gem-diol is not a stable product.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, single concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"N,N-dimethylbenzamide is treated with LiAlH4 then aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(c1ccccc1)N(C)C","opts":["CNCc1ccccc1","CNC(=O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide all the way to an amine: the carbonyl carbon becomes a CH2 and the carbon-nitrogen bond is kept, so N,N-dimethylbenzamide gives N,N-dimethylbenzylamine. This is the key contrast with esters and acids, which lose the heteroatom and end up as alcohols; benzyl alcohol would require cleaving the C-N bond, which amides do not do. Benzylamine and N-methylbenzylamine would need methyl groups stripped off nitrogen, and N-methylbenzamide still has its carbonyl and has also lost a methyl.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct amide->amine, valid, visual."},{"q":"3-Oxobutanoic acid (acetoacetic acid) is heated. Which structure is the major organic product after loss of CO2?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)O","opts":["CC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCO","CC(=O)C(=O)O","CCC(C)=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Acetoacetic acid is a beta-keto acid: its carboxyl group is beta to the ketone carbonyl. On heating it decarboxylates through a six-membered cyclic transition state (the carboxyl proton is delivered to the ketone oxygen as CO2 leaves), giving an enol that tautomerizes to acetone. Any product with more than three carbons (2-butanone, 4-hydroxy-2-butanone) or with a reorganized chain (propanal) cannot come from simply dropping CO2, and pyruvic acid is an alpha-keto acid, which does not decarboxylate by this pathway.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic beta-keto-acid decarboxylation, valid, visual."},{"q":"(S)-2-methylbutanoic acid is reduced with LiAlH4, then worked up with water. Which structure is the major product, including the correct configuration at the stereocenter?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](C)C(=O)O","opts":["CC[C@@H](C)CO","CCC(C)CO","CC[C@H](C)C=O","CC(C)CCO","CC[C@H](C)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the carboxylic acid to a primary alcohol, and no bond to the stereocenter is made or broken, so the spatial arrangement of the groups is retained. The CH2OH that replaces COOH still outranks ethyl and methyl in CIP priority, so the descriptor is unchanged as well: the product is (S)-2-methyl-1-butanol. The (R) alcohol is the inversion trap; reduction never inverts a stereocenter. The aldehyde cannot be isolated with LiAlH4, 3-methyl-1-butanol is a different skeleton, and the drawing of 2-methyl-1-butanol with no configuration shown ignores the stereochemistry the question asks for.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. CIP S->S verified by RDKit; retention reasoning correct."},{"q":"Ethyl acetate is treated with excess LiAlH4 followed by aqueous workup. Reduction cleaves the ester, releasing the alkoxy fragment and reducing the acyl fragment. Which structure represents the alcohol product(s) obtained?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CCO","CC(O)O","CCCO","CC=O","CCOCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hydride adds to the ester carbonyl, ethoxide is expelled as the tetrahedral intermediate collapses, and the acetaldehyde produced is immediately reduced again. After workup the acyl half (CH3C=O) has become ethanol, and the released alkoxy half (OCH2CH3) is also ethanol, so ethyl acetate gives two equivalents of the same alcohol, ethanol. Acetaldehyde cannot be isolated with excess LiAlH4, diethyl ether would require forming a new C-O bond that reduction never makes, and 1-propanol and the 1,1-diol have the wrong carbon count or connectivity.","difficulty":1,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Both fragments coincide as ethanol; stem disambiguates."},{"q":"2-Methylmalonic acid (a substituted malonic/1,3-diacid) is heated strongly. Which structure is the major organic product after loss of CO2?","q_smiles":"CC(C(=O)O)C(=O)O","opts":["CC(=O)O","CCC(=O)O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)OC","CC(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methylmalonic acid is a 1,3-diacid: each carboxyl is beta to the other carbonyl, so on heating one COOH is lost as CO2 through the usual six-membered cyclic transition state, giving an enol that tautomerizes to propanoic acid. Only one carboxyl leaves; once it is gone the remaining COOH is no longer beta to a carbonyl and cannot decarboxylate. Acetic acid would require losing a carbon beyond CO2, butanoic acid and 2-methylpropanoic acid have the wrong skeleton, and the methyl ester would need an esterification that is not part of the reaction.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Single-CO2 loss from malonic diacid correct."},{"q":"But-3-enamide (CH2=CH-CH2-CONH2) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(=O)N","opts":["CCCCN","C=CCC=O","C=CCCO","C=CCC(=O)O","C=CCCN"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a CH2 while keeping the carbon-nitrogen bond, so but-3-enamide becomes but-3-en-1-amine. An isolated carbon-carbon double bond is not reduced by hydride reagents (that needs H2 over a metal catalyst), so the alkene survives; butylamine is the over-reduction trap. But-3-enal and but-3-en-1-ol would require cutting the C-N bond, which amides do not do with LiAlH4, and but-3-enoic acid is not formed at all.","difficulty":2,"group":"carboxylic-reduction-decarb","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Amide->amine with alkene chemoselectivity correct."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=[N+]([O-])Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Methyl is a weak activating ortho/para director; para nitration predominates because it avoids ortho steric crowding.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; CH3 o/p director, para major correctly keyed."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 / FeBr3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)c(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Nitro is a strong deactivator and meta director, so bromination enters meta.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. NO2 meta director; keyed meta-bromonitrobenzene confirmed (Br-NO2 = meta)."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1ccccc1Br","BrCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methoxy is a strong o/p activator via lone-pair resonance; no Lewis acid is needed and para predominates due to less hindrance. Meta [1] is wrong for an o/p director and [3] brominates the methyl carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. OCH3 o/p director, para major correctly keyed; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with (CH3)3CCl and AlCl3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(C)Cc1ccccc1","CCC(C)c1ccccc1","CCCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation ionizes tert-butyl chloride to the already-tertiary tert-butyl cation, so no rearrangement occurs and tert-butylbenzene forms. Sec-butyl, n-butyl, and isobutyl would require rearrangement that does not happen.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. tert-Butyl cation is stable, no rearrangement; keyed product correct."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with CH3CH2COCl (propanoyl chloride) and AlCl3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CCC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Friedel-Crafts acylation via a resonance-stabilized acylium ion gives propiophenone (an aryl ketone). The acylium does not rearrange [3], the product is a ketone not an alkane [2] or ester [4].","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Acylium ion, propiophenone correctly keyed; all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with SO3 in H2SO4 (fuming sulfuric acid). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["O=S(=O)(O)Cc1ccccc1","O=S(=O)(Cl)c1ccccc1","O=S(=O)(O)c1ccccc1","O=S(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Sulfonation with SO3 installs -SO3H, giving benzenesulfonic acid (two S=O plus S-OH). The sulfinic acid [1] (one S=O), sulfonyl chloride [2], and benzylic isomer [3] are wrong functional groups/positions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Benzenesulfonic acid correctly keyed; distractors are distinct valid wrong structures."},{"q":"Bromobenzene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. What is the predominant mononitration product?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(Br)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Halogens deactivate inductively but remain o/p directors via lone-pair resonance stabilization of the arenium ion; para predominates over ortho due to sterics.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Halogen o/p exception; para-bromonitrobenzene confirmed (Br-NO2 = para)."},{"q":"4-Nitrotoluene (para-nitrotoluene) is treated with Br2 / FeBr3. Where does bromination occur, and what is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(Br)c1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1Br","Cc1cc(Br)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(CBr)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"With competing directors the activating CH3 (o/p) wins over the deactivating NO2 (meta), so Br enters ortho to CH3, a position that is simultaneously meta to NO2 (reinforcing). Option [1] is ortho to NO2, [2] is benzylic (not EAS), [3] is meta to both.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Two-director competition; activating CH3 controls; keyed position (ortho-CH3/meta-NO2) confirmed by ring distances."},{"q":"Which set of reagents converts the molecule shown into nitrobenzene via electrophilic aromatic substitution? The active electrophile is the nitronium ion (NO2+).","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["concentrated HNO3 + concentrated H2SO4","Cl2 + FeCl3","KMnO4 + heat","H2 + Pd/C","dilute NaOH + heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aromatic nitration uses concentrated HNO3 + H2SO4; H2SO4 protonates HNO3 to generate the nitronium ion NO2+, the electrophile. The other options are not nitration conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Benzene SMILES valid; standard nitration; one defensible answer (index 0)."},{"q":"The conversion of benzene (shown) to bromobenzene is an example of which reaction type?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Friedel-Crafts acylation","Electrophilic aromatic substitution (halogenation)","Free-radical halogenation","Electrophilic addition","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Bromination of benzene (Br2 + FeBr3) is electrophilic aromatic substitution: the Lewis acid polarizes Br2, the ring attacks Br+, and a proton is lost to rearomatize. It is substitution, not addition or radical, and the ring is the nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem omits explicit Br2/FeBr3 reagents but benzene-to-bromobenzene is unambiguously EAS halogenation; index 0 is the only defensible answer."},{"q":"Which reagents are required to install the acetyl group and form acetophenone from benzene (shown) by Friedel-Crafts acylation?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["CH3COOH + NaOH","CH3CH2Cl + AlCl3","CH3COCl + AlCl3","CH3OH + H2SO4","CH3CH2OH + PCC"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts acylation needs an acyl chloride (or anhydride) plus AlCl3, which forms the resonance-stabilized acylium ion CH3CO+. CH3CH2Cl/AlCl3 gives ethylbenzene (alkylation); the alcohol/acid options do not form an acylium electrophile.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard FC acylation; index 0 correct."},{"q":"Four monosubstituted benzenes are shown. Toward electrophilic aromatic substitution (e.g., nitration), which substrate reacts FASTEST because its substituent is the strongest activator?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["chlorobenzene","benzaldehyde","benzene","anisole (methoxybenzene)","nitrobenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"An alkoxy group (-OCH3) is a strong activator: the oxygen lone pair donates into the ring by resonance, raising EAS rate. Chlorobenzene is weakly deactivating; nitrobenzene and benzaldehyde bear strong EWGs that deactivate below benzene's baseline.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Anisole SMILES valid; ordering anisole >> benzene > chlorobenzene > benzaldehyde/nitrobenzene is correct. Index 0 is the strongest activator."},{"q":"Which reagents sulfonate benzene (shown) to give benzenesulfonic acid, and what makes this reaction notably different from nitration?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Fuming H2SO4 (SO3/H2SO4); sulfonation is reversible","NaHSO3/H2O; sulfonation is a radical process","HNO3/H2SO4; sulfonation requires no catalyst","Cl2/FeCl3; sulfonation is irreversible","SOCl2; sulfonation gives an addition product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Sulfonation uses fuming H2SO4 (SO3 in H2SO4); the electrophile is SO3. Unlike nitration/halogenation/FC, sulfonation is reversible, so the -SO3H group can be removed by aqueous acid and used as a removable blocking group.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct reagent + reversibility distinction (two-concept). Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (shown) undergoes a second nitration with HNO3/H2SO4. The -NO2 group is a meta director, so which product predominates?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["1,4-dinitrobenzene (para)","a 1:1 ortho/para mixture","1,3-dinitrobenzene (meta)","1,2-dinitrobenzene (ortho)","no reaction occurs"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The nitro group is a strong deactivator and meta director: ortho/para attack places positive charge adjacent to the electron-poor carbon in the arenium intermediate, so attack favors meta. Major product is 1,3-dinitrobenzene under forcing conditions.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Nitrobenzene SMILES valid; meta direction correct; index 0 (1,3-dinitrobenzene) correct."},{"q":"Benzene (shown) is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Friedel-Crafts ALKYLATION often gives a rearranged product. What is the major product, and why?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["propylbenzene, with no rearrangement","tert-butylbenzene, from a methyl shift","isopropylbenzene (cumene), from a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary carbocation","a mixture with the propyl group on the meta position","no reaction; alkyl halides are unreactive toward AlCl3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"AlCl3 ionizes 1-chloropropane toward a primary cation that undergoes a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary (isopropyl) cation before ring attack, giving cumene. Carbocation rearrangement is the classic FC alkylation limitation.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydride-shift rearrangement to cumene is correct; index 0 correct."},{"q":"A student attempts a Friedel-Crafts acylation on the molecule shown (aniline) using CH3COCl and AlCl3 and obtains essentially no ring-acylated product. What is the best explanation?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetyl chloride is too unreactive to form an acylium ion","The amino group makes the ring so electron-poor that no electrophile can add","The basic -NH2 nitrogen complexes with the Lewis acid AlCl3, forming a strongly deactivating -NH2-AlCl3 group that shuts down EAS","The reaction gives only the meta product, which the student discarded","Aniline is aromatic and therefore cannot undergo any electrophilic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"FC reactions fail on aniline because the Lewis-basic amino N coordinates to AlCl3; the resulting positively charged -NH2-AlCl3 nitrogen strongly deactivates the ring (and consumes catalyst). The free -NH2 is normally a strong activator, so the ring is not inherently electron-poor.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Aniline SMILES valid; standard Klein/Wade explanation. Recognition-level 'best explanation', in scope despite difficulty 3. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"tert-Butylbenzene is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(C[N+](=O)[O-])c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(C)(C)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CC(C)(C)c1ccc(N=O)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The tert-butyl group is a weakly activating ortho/para director, but its bulk shields both ortho positions, so the nitronium ion attacks para and 4-nitro-tert-butylbenzene is the major mononitration product. The ortho isomer forms only in small amounts because of sterics, and the meta isomer is wrong because alkyl groups do not direct meta. The nitroso compound is the wrong electrophile product, and the structure with the nitro group on a side-chain methyl ignores that nitration takes place on the ring.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Steric-override of ortho/para EAS, single center, one defensible answer; all SMILES valid; keyed para product correct."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1cccc(N)c1","O=Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=Cc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1"],"correct":4,"why":"The aldehyde carbonyl pulls electron density out of the ring by resonance, so CHO is a deactivating meta director and nitration gives 3-nitrobenzaldehyde. The ortho and para isomers would need an ortho/para director. 3-Nitrobenzoic acid would require the aldehyde to be oxidized, which the nitrating mixture does not do, and 3-aminobenzaldehyde would require a reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classic meta-director recognition; keyed meta product correct; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Bromobenzene is treated with propanoyl chloride (CH3CH2COCl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)c1cccc(Br)c1","CCC(=O)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(=O)c1ccccc1Br","CCCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(=O)Oc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Bromine is deactivating by induction but still ortho/para directing because its lone pairs stabilize the ortho and para sigma complexes. Friedel-Crafts acylation with propanoyl chloride and AlCl3 therefore installs the propanoyl group para to bromine, and the major product is 4-bromopropiophenone (1-(4-bromophenyl)propan-1-one). The ortho ketone is minor because of sterics, the meta ketone is wrong for a halogen director, 4-bromopropylbenzene is an alkylation product rather than an acylation product, and the ester would need an oxygen nucleophile that is not present.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Halogen o/p direction + acylation regiochemistry; one defensible answer; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Benzene is treated with 1-chloropropane (CH3CH2CH2Cl) and AlCl3. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"ClCCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Friedel-Crafts alkylation goes through a carbocation. The primary propyl cation rearranges by a 1,2-hydride shift to the more stable secondary isopropyl cation before the ring attacks, so the major product is isopropylbenzene (cumene). Propylbenzene is the trap for forgetting the rearrangement, ethylbenzene and tert-butylbenzene have the wrong carbon count, and isobutylbenzene has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None substantive. Carbocation-rearrangement recognition, single concept, one defensible answer; SMILES valid. q_smiles shows the haloalkane reagent (still a valid drawn structure), acceptable."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2 / FeBr3 (one equivalent). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccccc1Br","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)c(Br)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"The methoxy group is a strong activator and ortho/para director because the oxygen lone pair feeds electron density into the ring. With one equivalent of Br2/FeBr3, substitution occurs para to avoid steric crowding, giving 4-bromoanisole. The ortho isomer is minor, the meta isomer is wrong for an ortho/para director, the dibromide needs excess bromine, and 4-bromophenol would require cleaving the methyl ether, which does not happen under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Strong-activator para selectivity, mono vs di control; keyed para product correct; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with fuming H2SO4 (SO3 / H2SO4). Which structure is the MAJOR sulfonation product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=S(=O)(O)Cc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(S(=O)(=O)O)c1","Cc1ccc(S(=O)O)cc1","Cc1ccc(S(=O)(=O)O)cc1","Cc1ccccc1S(=O)(=O)O"],"correct":3,"why":"The methyl group is a weak activator and an ortho/para director. Sulfonation with SO3/H2SO4 is reversible and settles on the thermodynamically preferred, less hindered para isomer, so p-toluenesulfonic acid is the major product. The ortho isomer is minor, the meta isomer is wrong for an alkyl director, the sulfinic acid (SO2H) is the wrong functional group, and sulfonation does not occur on the benzylic carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Sulfonation o/p direction with FG-recognition distractors; one defensible answer; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetanilide (the amide shown) is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(N)cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":2,"why":"The amide nitrogen lone pair, although partly shared with the carbonyl, still donates into the ring, so the acetamido group is an activating ortho/para director. Its bulk steers the electrophile to the para position, and the major product is 4-nitroacetanilide. The ortho isomer is minor and the meta isomer is wrong for this director. The amide survives nitration, so 4-nitroaniline (which would need hydrolysis) is not formed, and the structure carrying an amino group instead of a nitro group is not a nitration product.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Amide-as-activator + steric para preference; keyed product correct; SMILES valid."},{"q":"meta-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene, shown) is treated with HNO3 / H2SO4. Which structure is the MAJOR mononitration product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(N)c(C)c1","Cc1cc(C)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1cccc(C)c1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(C)c1"],"correct":4,"why":"Both methyls are ortho/para directors, and their effects reinforce at the two positions that are ortho to one methyl and para to the other (the 4 and 6 positions) and at the position between them (the 2 position). The 2 position is hemmed in by both methyls, so attack goes to the open reinforced site, giving 4-nitro-m-xylene (2,4-dimethylnitrobenzene), with the nitro group ortho to one methyl and para to the other. The isomer with the nitro group squeezed between the methyls is the sterically blocked minor product, and the 5-position product is meta to both methyls and not activated. 2,4-Dimethylaniline has an amine instead of a nitro group, and 4-nitrotoluene has lost a methyl.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reinforced o/p direction with steric override at C2; verified keyed = 4-nitro-m-xylene (ortho+para to the methyls); two-concept (regio+steric) but in scope; SMILES valid."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(Br)cc1Br","Cc1ccccc1Br","BrCc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(Br)c1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The methyl group is a weak activator and ortho/para director, and para substitution wins over ortho for steric reasons, so Br2/FeBr3 converts toluene mainly to 4-bromotoluene. 2-Bromotoluene is the minor ortho isomer, and 3-bromotoluene is wrong because alkyl groups do not direct meta. Benzyl bromide comes from radical side-chain bromination (light or heat, no Lewis acid), and the dibromide requires excess bromine.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct, in scope, all SMILES valid, exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Nc1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1","Nc1cccc(Br)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The nitro group is a strong deactivator and a meta director: it withdraws electron density by resonance, so the ortho and para sigma complexes are destabilized and bromine ends up meta, giving 3-bromonitrobenzene. The ortho and para isomers would need an ortho/para director. The nitroso and amino structures change the nitrogen group itself, which electrophilic bromination does not do.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct meta director, valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) is treated with Br2 (no catalyst needed). Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccc(Br)cc1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1Br","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccccc1Br","COc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Anisole is so activated by the methoxy lone pair that bromine substitutes the ring without a Lewis acid, and para wins over ortho for steric reasons, so the major product is 4-bromoanisole. 2-Bromoanisole is the minor ortho isomer, 3-bromoanisole is wrong for an ortho/para director, 4-bromophenol would require cleaving the methyl ether, and the dibromide needs excess bromine.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct o/p activator, para major, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Benzoic acid is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C(O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","O=C(O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","O=Nc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1","O=C(OC)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The carboxyl group is a deactivating meta director: its carbonyl withdraws electron density from the ortho and para positions, so the nitronium ion attacks meta and the major product is 3-nitrobenzoic acid. The ortho and para isomers form only in small amounts. The methyl ester would require an esterification that these conditions do not perform, and the nitroso compound is the wrong electrophile product.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct meta director, valid SMILES, single defensible answer."},{"q":"Chlorobenzene is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Cl)c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(O)cc1","Clc1ccc(Cl)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Cl)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Chlorine is the classic exception: it deactivates the ring by induction yet directs ortho/para because its lone pairs stabilize the ortho and para sigma complexes. Para substitution is favored over ortho for steric reasons, so the major product is 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene. The meta isomer is wrong for a halogen director, 4-nitrophenol would require replacing chlorine with OH, and 1,4-dichlorobenzene is not a nitration product.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Legitimate two-concept (deactivator + o/p direction), correct para, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Acetanilide (N-phenylacetamide) is treated with Br2 in acetic acid. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1Br","CC(=O)Nc1cccc(Br)c1","CC(=O)N(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(Br)cc1","Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The amide nitrogen lone pair donates into the ring, so the acetamido group is an activating ortho/para director, and its bulk favors the para position: the major product is 4-bromoacetanilide. The ortho isomer is minor and the meta isomer is wrong for this director. Bromine does not end up on the amide nitrogen under these conditions, and 4-bromoaniline would require an amide hydrolysis that does not occur.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct o/p activator, para major, valid SMILES, single answer."},{"q":"para-Cresol (4-methylphenol) is treated with Br2. Both substituents are ortho/para directors, but they compete. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts":["OCc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccc(O)c(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(C)c(Br)c1","Cc1c(Br)cc(O)cc1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"When two ortho/para directors compete, the stronger activator controls, and OH (lone pair donation straight into the ring) beats methyl. The position para to OH is occupied by the methyl, so bromine goes ortho to the hydroxyl, giving 2-bromo-4-methylphenol. The isomer with bromine next to the methyl (meta to OH) lets the weaker director win, 4-bromoanisole has the wrong skeleton (a methyl ether rather than a phenol), the dibromide requires excess bromine, and 4-(hydroxymethyl)phenol would need an oxidation of the methyl that bromination does not perform.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Competing-directors concept correct; keyed Br is ortho-to-OH as intended. Valid SMILES, unambiguous."},{"q":"meta-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene) is treated with HNO3 and H2SO4. Both methyls are ortho/para directors; consider where their directing effects reinforce AND sterics. Which is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts":["Cc1cccc(C)c1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])c(C)c1","Cc1cc(C)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(C)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"With methyls at the 1 and 3 positions, their ortho/para directing effects reinforce at the 2, 4, and 6 positions; the 4 and 6 positions are equivalent, each ortho to one methyl and para to the other, while the 2 position sits between the two methyls. Sterics rule out the crowded 2 position, so nitration gives 4-nitro-m-xylene (2,4-dimethylnitrobenzene), with the nitro group ortho to one methyl and para to the other. The isomer with the nitro group between the methyls is the reinforced-but-blocked minor product, the 5-position product is meta to both methyls, 3-methylbenzoic acid would require oxidizing a methyl, and the remaining structure has a para-xylene skeleton that cannot come from meta-xylene.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hard reinforce-plus-sterics item; keyed C4 product correct and unambiguous, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn below) is treated with Br2 and a FeBr3 catalyst. The methyl group is an ortho/para director. Which best describes the MAJOR monobromination product and the reason for the regiochemistry?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Bromine adds to the carbon bearing the methyl group via ipso substitution","Ortho-bromotoluene only; the methyl group blocks the para position","No reaction occurs because the methyl group deactivates the ring","Para-bromotoluene predominates; the methyl group is an ortho/para director, and the para position is favored over ortho because of less steric hindrance","Meta-bromotoluene predominates; the methyl group is a meta director"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Alkyl groups are weak activators and ortho/para directors. Both ortho and para products form, but para is usually the major product because the bulky electrophile and the methyl group create steric strain at the ortho positions.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. CH3 is weak activator o/p director, para major by sterics. SMILES valid, single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (drawn below) is subjected to electrophilic aromatic substitution (nitration with HNO3/H2SO4). The nitro group is a strong deactivator. Which statement correctly predicts the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["The reaction is slower than for benzene, and the new nitro group enters mainly at the meta position","The nitro group is an ortho/para director and the reaction is faster than for benzene","The reaction is faster than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the para position","The reaction is faster than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the ortho position","The reaction is slower than for benzene, and substitution occurs mainly at the para position"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The nitro group is a strong electron-withdrawing deactivator that slows EAS relative to benzene and directs incoming electrophiles to the meta position, where the destabilizing positive charge in the resonance intermediate is avoided on the carbon bearing the substituent.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. NO2 strong deactivator, meta director, slower than benzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"You need to synthesize para-nitroanisole (4-nitroanisole) as the major product. Starting from anisole (drawn below), which single set of reagents accomplishes this in one step?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts":["KMnO4, heat","H2, Pd/C","Br2, FeBr3","CH3Cl, AlCl3","HNO3, H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The methoxy group is a strong activator and ortho/para director. Nitration with HNO3/H2SO4 installs the nitro group, giving the para isomer as the major product (ortho is minor due to sterics). The other reagents either do not nitrate or perform a different reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Only HNO3/H2SO4 nitrates; OMe o/p director gives para major. Distractors do other reactions. SMILES valid."},{"q":"Bromobenzene (drawn below) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation. Halogens are an unusual class of substituent in EAS. Which choice correctly classifies the bromine substituent and predicts the major product position?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["Activating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Deactivating and meta directing; the acyl group enters mainly meta","Deactivating but ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para","Activating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly para","Deactivating and ortho/para directing; the acyl group enters mainly ortho only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Halogens are the classic exception: they are deactivating (electron-withdrawing by induction) yet ortho/para directing (lone-pair resonance donation stabilizes the ortho/para arenium intermediates). The para product dominates over ortho largely for steric reasons.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Halogen = deactivating yet o/p directing, para major. F-C acylation works on weakly deactivated halobenzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Three substituted benzenes are considered for nitration: aniline (drawn below), toluene, and nitrobenzene. Rank them from FASTEST to SLOWEST rate of electrophilic aromatic substitution.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["toluene > aniline > nitrobenzene","nitrobenzene > toluene > aniline","nitrobenzene > aniline > toluene","aniline > toluene > nitrobenzene","aniline > nitrobenzene > toluene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Rate tracks ring electron density. The -NH2 group is a strong activator (lone-pair donation), the methyl is a weak activator, and the nitro group is a strong deactivator. So aniline reacts fastest and nitrobenzene slowest.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. NH2 strong activator > CH3 weak activator > NO2 strong deactivator. SMILES valid, single correct ranking."},{"q":"Two monosubstituted benzenes are shown. Substrate A is acetanilide (top SMILES) and substrate B is benzaldehyde. For a nitration reaction, which substrate reacts FASTER and what is the directing preference of its substituent?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Acetanilide reacts faster; the amide nitrogen is a deactivating meta director","Acetanilide reacts faster; the amide nitrogen is an activating ortho/para director","Benzaldehyde reacts faster; the aldehyde is an activating ortho/para director","Benzaldehyde reacts faster; the aldehyde is a deactivating meta director","Both react at the same rate because both substituents withdraw electrons"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In acetanilide the nitrogen lone pair still donates into the ring, making the -NHC(=O)CH3 group a net activator and ortho/para director (weaker than free -NH2). The aldehyde (-CHO) is a carbonyl: electron-withdrawing, deactivating, and meta directing. So acetanilide reacts faster.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. -NHAc net activator o/p director; -CHO deactivator meta director; acetanilide faster. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenol (drawn below) is treated with dilute HNO3. Which choice correctly states the rate relative to benzene and the major position(s) of substitution?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Faster than benzene; substitution occurs ortho and para to the -OH","Same rate as benzene; substitution is random around the ring","Slower than benzene; substitution occurs ortho and para to the -OH","Faster than benzene; substitution occurs meta to the -OH","Slower than benzene; substitution occurs meta to the -OH"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The hydroxyl group is a strong activator (oxygen lone-pair donation) and an ortho/para director. Phenol therefore undergoes EAS much faster than benzene, with electrophiles entering ortho and para to the -OH.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. OH strong activator, o/p director, faster than benzene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"para-Nitrotoluene (drawn below) is brominated with Br2/FeBr3. The ring carries two substituents whose directing effects must be weighed: the methyl (activating, o/p director) and the nitro (deactivating, meta director). Which statement correctly predicts where bromine adds?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Bromine adds ortho to the nitro group because the nitro group is the stronger director","No substitution occurs because the two directors cancel each other completely","Bromine adds at the carbon bearing the methyl group via ipso substitution","Bromine adds meta to the methyl group because the nitro group overrides the methyl","Bromine adds ortho to the methyl group; the stronger activating director (CH3) controls the regiochemistry, and that position is also meta to the nitro group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"When directors compete, the stronger activator wins. The methyl group (o/p director) dominates over the deactivating nitro group, so bromine enters ortho to the methyl. Conveniently, that same position is meta to the nitro group, so the two effects reinforce, giving 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. Methyl (stronger director) sends Br ortho (C2/C6); that site is also meta to NO2, so directors reinforce. Product 2-bromo-4-nitrotoluene. SMILES valid, one defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Br2 and FeBr3. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Brc1ccccc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1Br","O=[N+]([O-])c1cccc(Br)c1","Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The nitro group withdraws electron density by resonance, so it is a strong deactivator and a meta director: bromination gives 3-bromonitrobenzene. The ortho and para isomers would require an ortho/para director. Bromobenzene would mean the nitro group was lost, and 3-nitroaniline carries an amino group that electrophilic bromination never creates.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Anisole (methoxybenzene) reacts with Br2 (no catalyst needed). Which is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"COc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccc(Cl)cc1","COc1cc(Br)cc(Br)c1","COc1ccccc1Br","COc1cccc(Br)c1","COc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The methoxy lone pair makes anisole so electron-rich that Br2 substitutes the ring with no Lewis acid at all, and the para position wins over ortho for steric reasons: the major monobromination product is 4-bromoanisole. 2-Bromoanisole is the minor ortho isomer and 3-bromoanisole is wrong for an ortho/para director. The dibromide requires excess bromine, and the chloro compound uses a halogen that is not present.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Acetanilide (N-phenylacetamide) undergoes nitration with HNO3/H2SO4. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CC(=O)Nc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","CC(=O)Nc1ccc(N)cc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":1,"why":"The acetamido nitrogen donates its lone pair into the ring, so it is an activating ortho/para director, and the bulky amide favors the para position: the major product is 4-nitroacetanilide. The ortho isomer is minor and the meta isomer is wrong for this director. 4-Aminoacetanilide carries an amino group instead of nitro, and 4-nitrotoluene has a methyl in place of the acetamido group, so neither can come from nitrating acetanilide.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - correct as keyed, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Consider electrophilic aromatic substitution on chlorobenzene (drawn). Which statement correctly describes the chloro substituent's effect on the reaction?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Activating and ortho/para-directing; the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating and meta-directing, like a nitro group.","It has no directing influence; a statistical 2:2:1 ortho:meta:para mixture forms.","Activating and meta-directing.","Deactivating but ortho/para-directing; the ring reacts more slowly than benzene yet substitution still goes to the ortho/para positions."],"correct":4,"why":"Halogens are the classic exception among substituents. Chlorine withdraws electron density by induction, so chlorobenzene reacts more slowly than benzene (it is deactivated), but its lone pairs donate by resonance into the ortho and para sigma complexes, so substitution still goes ortho/para. Chlorine is therefore deactivating but ortho/para directing; it is not a meta director like nitro, not an activator, and not directionless.","difficulty":1,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - text classification item with substrate drawn; single concept, correct as keyed."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is subjected to bromination with Br2/FeBr3. Classify the -CHO group and predict where bromine is installed.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Activating, meta-directing; Br adds meta and the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating, meta-directing; Br adds at the meta position and the ring reacts slower than benzene.","Deactivating, meta-directing; Br adds para because steric effects override electronics.","Activating, ortho/para-directing; Br adds para and the ring reacts faster than benzene.","Deactivating, ortho/para-directing; Br adds para but the ring reacts slower than benzene."],"correct":1,"why":"The carbonyl carbon of CHO pulls electron density out of the ring by resonance (and by induction), which destabilizes the ortho and para sigma complexes most. So CHO is deactivating and meta directing: bromine is installed at the meta position, and benzaldehyde brominates more slowly than benzene. Every meta director is a deactivator, so any description calling CHO activating is wrong, and sterics do not override electronics to push a meta director's product to para.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - single-concept meta-director classification, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"4-Methylphenol (p-cresol, drawn) is mononitrated with dilute HNO3. Both substituents are ortho/para directors, but they compete. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1cc(O)cc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(O)c(N)c1","Cc1ccc(O)cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(O)c([N+](=O)[O-])c1","Cc1ccc(O)c(N=O)c1"],"correct":3,"why":"Both substituents are ortho/para directors, but the stronger activator, OH, controls. The position para to OH is occupied by the methyl, so the nitro group goes ortho to OH, giving 4-methyl-2-nitrophenol (nitro ortho to the hydroxyl and meta to the methyl). The isomer with nitro next to the methyl (meta to OH) lets the weaker director win, and the structure in which the hydroxyl and methyl are meta to each other is not even the p-cresol skeleton. The amino and nitroso structures are the wrong functional group; dilute HNO3 installs a nitro group.","difficulty":2,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - competing-directors plus sterics is two layered concepts, in scope; opt0 verified as 2-nitro-4-methylphenol."},{"q":"m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene, drawn) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation with CH3COCl/AlCl3. Both methyls direct ortho/para, and the position between them is activated by both. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(C(C)O)c(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)c(C)c1","Cc1cccc(C)c1C(C)=O","Cc1cccc(C)c1CC(C)=O","Cc1cc(C(C)=O)cc(C)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"Both methyls direct ortho/para and reinforce at the position between them and at the positions ortho to one methyl and para to the other. The acylium ion is bulky, so the crowded site between the two methyls is avoided and acylation occurs at the open reinforced position, giving 2,4-dimethylacetophenone. The isomer with the acetyl group between the methyls is the sterically blocked minor product, 3,5-dimethylacetophenone has the acetyl meta to both methyls (an unactivated site), the structure with an extra CH2 between ring and carbonyl is not an acylation product, and the alcohol would require a reduction that never happens.","difficulty":3,"group":"eas-directing","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None - single acylation event with steric+electronic reasoning, in scope; opt0 verified as 2,4-dimethylacetophenone."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is treated with hot KMnO4, then dilute H2SO4 workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes any alkyl side chain that has at least one benzylic hydrogen all the way down to a carboxylic acid attached directly to the ring, regardless of chain length. Ethylbenzene therefore gives benzoic acid; the extra carbon is lost as CO2. Acetophenone, 1-phenylethanol, 2-phenylethanol, and phenylacetic acid all keep too many side-chain carbons; permanganate does not stop at those stages.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All 5 options valid SMILES, distractors differ by oxidation state/skeleton, exactly one defensible answer (benzoic acid). Clean single-concept benzylic oxidation, in DAT scope, visual."},{"q":"4-Nitrotoluene (p-nitrotoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4 followed by acidic workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=Cc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","OC(=O)c1cccc(c1)[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes the benzylic methyl to a carboxylic acid, while the nitro group, already at nitrogen's highest oxidation state, is untouched (permanganate cannot reduce it). The product is 4-nitrobenzoic acid with the nitro group still para. 4-Nitrobenzaldehyde is only a passing intermediate because permanganate oxidizes aldehydes further, the meta isomer moves the nitro group for no reason, and the amine-containing structures would require a reduction.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; distractors probe nitro stability, regiochem, and over-oxidation. Exactly one correct answer; two-concept (oxidation + nitro inertness), in scope, visual."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with H2 and a Pd/C catalyst (or Sn/HCl). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["ONc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Nc1ccccc1","NNc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2/Pd-C) or a dissolving-metal reduction (Sn/HCl) converts an aromatic nitro group completely to a primary amine, so nitrobenzene gives aniline. Nitrosobenzene and N-phenylhydroxylamine are partial-reduction intermediates that are not isolated under these conditions, phenol swaps nitrogen for oxygen, and phenylhydrazine has an extra nitrogen that cannot come from nitrobenzene.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All options valid; distractors are mechanistically themed partial-reduction species plus wrong heteroatom. Single concept, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"4-Chloronitrobenzene is heated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. What is the major product of this nucleophilic aromatic substitution?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1ccccc1","COc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1ccc(Cl)cc1","COc1cccc(c1)[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":2,"why":"Nucleophilic aromatic substitution needs a strong electron-withdrawing group ortho or para to the leaving group so that the negatively charged Meisenheimer intermediate is stabilized. Here the nitro group is para to the C-Cl carbon, so methoxide adds there and chloride is expelled, giving 4-nitroanisole with the nitro group retained in its original position. The amine product uses the wrong nucleophile, 4-chloroanisole would mean the nitro group was displaced instead of chloride, anisole has lost the nitro group, and the meta isomer moves a substituent, which substitution cannot do.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; distractors test regiochem (meta), wrong nucleophile (amine), loss of NO2, and no-reaction. One defensible answer, two-concept (SNAr + activation), in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-tert-Butyl-4-methylbenzene (4-tert-butyltoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4, then acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(=O)O","Cc1ccc(cc1)C(=O)O","OC(=O)c1cccc(c1)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)c1ccc(cc1)C(C)(C)CO"],"correct":3,"why":"Permanganate side-chain oxidation needs at least one benzylic hydrogen. The methyl has them and is oxidized to COOH, but the tert-butyl carbon attached to the ring carries no hydrogen, so that group is untouched: the product is 4-tert-butylbenzoic acid. Terephthalic acid wrongly oxidizes both groups, 4-methylbenzoic acid and the structure with a partly oxidized tert-butyl group get the selectivity backwards, and 3-tert-butylbenzoic acid moves a substituent to meta.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All valid; distractors oxidize the quaternary group (impossible), mis-regiochem to meta. One defensible answer, single concept (benzylic-H requirement), in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Fluoro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (Sanger's reagent) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Fc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CNc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","Fc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])NC","CNc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+]=O","Oc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]","Cc1ccc(cc1[N+](=O)[O-])[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":0,"why":"The fluorine-bearing carbon has nitro groups ortho and para to it, so the ring is strongly activated toward nucleophilic aromatic substitution. Methylamine adds at the C-F carbon to form the stabilized Meisenheimer intermediate, then fluoride leaves (fluoride works well here because the addition step, not C-F cleavage, is rate-determining). The product is N-methyl-2,4-dinitroaniline with both nitro groups intact. The amine does not displace a nitro group, fluorine is not replaced by a methyl, no hydrolysis to 2,4-dinitrophenol occurs, and the nitroso structure alters a nitro group that is retained.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid; regiochemistry verified (F-carbon bears new N-methylamino, both NO2 unchanged). Distractors test wrong site, no-reaction, wrong nucleophile/loss. One defensible answer, two-concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Toluene is treated with Cl2 in the presence of UV light (hv). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1cccc(Cl)c1","Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1","ClCc1ccccc1","ClC(Cl)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1Cl"],"correct":2,"why":"Cl2 with UV light and no Lewis acid gives a free-radical chain reaction, not electrophilic aromatic substitution. A chlorine atom abstracts a benzylic hydrogen because the resulting benzylic radical is resonance-stabilized, so monochlorination gives benzyl chloride. Ring chlorination (the ortho, meta, or para chlorotoluenes) would require Cl2/FeCl3, and benzal chloride, with two chlorines on the side chain, needs a second equivalent.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All valid; distractors are EAS ring products (ortho/meta/para) and dichloride. Tests radical-vs-EAS selectivity; one defensible answer, single concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is heated with excess KMnO4, then treated with acid. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)(O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"The benzylic carbon of cumene still carries one hydrogen, so hot KMnO4 degrades the entire isopropyl side chain to a single carboxyl carbon on the ring: the product is benzoic acid, and the two methyl carbons are lost as CO2. Permanganate does not stop at the tertiary benzylic alcohol, 1-phenylethanol, acetophenone, or 2-phenylpropanoic acid; each of those keeps side-chain carbons that are cleaved away under these conditions.","difficulty":3,"group":"aromatic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry correct, keeps. Minor: difficulty is overstated as 3 (this is the same benzylic-oxidation principle as Q1, more like a 2), but it is a valid in-scope single-center question with one defensible answer."},{"q":"The molecule shown (p-nitrotoluene) is treated with hot KMnO4, then acidic workup. Which product results?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["4-nitrobenzyl alcohol","no reaction; the ring resists oxidation","4-nitrobenzoic acid","4-nitrobenzaldehyde","4-aminobenzoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes a methyl bearing benzylic H all the way to COOH; the ring and nitro are untouched, giving 4-nitrobenzoic acid. It does not stop at aldehyde/alcohol nor reduce the nitro.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; in scope and well-formed."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene (shown) is refluxed with excess hot KMnO4 followed by H3O+ workup. What is the organic product derived from the side chain?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["2-phenylacetaldehyde","Acetophenone (PhCOCH3)","1-phenylethanol","Benzoic acid (one carbon, COOH on the ring)","Phenylacetic acid (PhCH2COOH)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Benzylic oxidation degrades any alkyl chain with a benzylic C-H down to a single ring COOH; the terminal CH3 is lost as CO2, so ethylbenzene gives benzoic acid, not phenylacetic acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; classic benzylic oxidation."},{"q":"tert-Butylbenzene (shown) is heated with KMnO4. Predict the outcome.","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["Oxidized to 2,2-dimethylpropanoic acid","Oxidized to benzoic acid","No oxidation of the side chain (no benzylic hydrogen present)","Oxidized to an aldehyde at the benzylic position","The ring is cleaved to a diacid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Benzylic oxidation needs at least one benzylic C-H. The benzylic carbon of tert-butylbenzene is quaternary, so KMnO4 cannot oxidize it; the side chain is inert. This is the classic non-oxidizable exception.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; standard exception."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene (shown) is treated with Sn (or Fe) and HCl, then made basic. What functional group is produced?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["A nitroso group (-N=O)","A carboxylic acid (-COOH)","An amide (-CONH2)","A hydroxyl group (-OH, phenol)","A primary aromatic amine (-NH2)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Sn/HCl (or Fe/HCl, H2/Pd, SnCl2) reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine; nitrobenzene gives aniline. Standard nitro-to-amine reduction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; canonical reduction."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (shown) reacts readily with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) at the carbon bearing Cl. By what mechanism, and why is it fast here?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","opts":["Free-radical substitution","SN2 backside attack on the aromatic carbon","Nucleophilic aromatic substitution (addition-elimination), accelerated by the ortho/para nitro groups stabilizing the Meisenheimer intermediate","SN1 through an aryl cation","Electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"An aryl halide with strong EWGs ortho/para to the leaving group undergoes SNAr by addition-elimination via a Meisenheimer complex; the o-/p-NO2 groups delocalize the anionic charge, speeding the reaction. SN1/SN2 do not occur at sp2 aryl carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles is genuine 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (Cl with ortho+para NO2); in scope."},{"q":"o-Xylene (1,2-dimethylbenzene, shown) is treated with a large excess of hot KMnO4, then acidified. What is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1C","opts":["Benzoic acid (one carbon lost)","Phthalic acid (benzene-1,2-dicarboxylic acid)","Phthalaldehyde (both CH3 to CHO)","o-toluic acid (only one CH3 oxidized)","1,2-bis(hydroxymethyl)benzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Excess hot KMnO4 oxidizes every benzylic CH3 (each has C-H) to COOH; two adjacent methyls give the 1,2-diacid, phthalic acid. It does not stop at one acid or at the aldehyde/alcohol stage.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid o-xylene; standard double benzylic oxidation."},{"q":"Consider SNAr with methoxide. Which substrate reacts FASTEST with NaOCH3? (1-fluoro-4-nitrobenzene is shown for reference.)","q_smiles":"Fc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["4-fluorotoluene","1-Fluoro-4-nitrobenzene","1-Chloro-4-nitrobenzene","Fluorobenzene (no nitro group)","1-Iodo-4-nitrobenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In SNAr the rate-determining step is nucleophilic addition to form the Meisenheimer complex, so the most electron-withdrawing C-X (C-F) reacts fastest among para-nitro aryl halides (opposite of SN1/SN2). Substrates lacking a para-nitro EWG barely react.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid; classic SNAr leaving-group trend (F fastest)."},{"q":"You must convert 1-nitro-4-propylbenzene (shown) into 4-aminobenzoic acid. Which sequence of reagents accomplishes BOTH the side-chain and the nitro-group transformations in a workable order?","q_smiles":"CCCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["(1) H2/Pd to reduce NO2; then (2) HNO3/H2SO4","(1) hot KMnO4 only (it also reduces the nitro group)","(1) hot KMnO4, H3O+ to make the COOH; then (2) Sn/HCl, then base to reduce NO2 to NH2","(1) Sn/HCl only (it also oxidizes the propyl chain to COOH)","(1) Sn/HCl to make NH2 first; then (2) hot KMnO4, H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Two changes: propyl (has benzylic H) is oxidized to COOH by hot KMnO4, and nitro is reduced to NH2 by Sn/HCl. Oxidize first, since hot KMnO4 would destroy/oxidize an amine; then reduce the surviving nitro. No single reagent does both.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Correct as keyed; q_smiles valid. Two-transformation sequence-ordering item but each step is one canonical reaction; sophomore-level ordering logic, within scope (not a 5+ step synthesis)."},{"q":"p-Nitrotoluene is treated with hot KMnO4, then acidic workup. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(N)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","OC(=O)c1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","OC(=O)c1ccc(N)cc1","OCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Hot KMnO4 oxidizes a benzylic CH3 (with at least one benzylic H) all the way to COOH; the methyl becomes carboxylic acid and the para-nitro group is untouched. It does not stop at the benzylic alcohol, does not reduce the nitro group, and does not move the substituent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed p-nitrobenzoic acid is correct; distractors (meta acid, benzyl alcohol, reduced nitro) all wrong. All SMILES valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2-nitrobenzene is heated with sodium methoxide (NaOCH3) in methanol. Which structure is the product of this nucleophilic aromatic substitution?","q_smiles":"[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1Cl","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["COc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","COc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","COc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Nc1ccccc1[N+](=O)[O-]","Clc1ccccc1OC"],"correct":0,"why":"The ortho-NO2 activates the adjacent C-Cl toward addition-elimination SNAr (Meisenheimer anion stabilized by the ortho nitro). Methoxide replaces Cl at the same carbon, so OCH3 ends up ortho to NO2 (o-nitroanisole). The nitro group is retained in its original position.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed o-nitroanisole (OMe ortho to NO2) is correct; para/meta isomers and undisplaced Cl are valid wrong distractors. SMILES valid, single SNAr center, in scope."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is refluxed with excess KMnO4 followed by acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CCC(=O)O","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)O","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Benzylic oxidation by hot KMnO4 cleaves the alkyl chain back to a single ring-attached COOH regardless of chain length, as long as one benzylic C-H is present. Ethyl becomes -COOH (benzoic acid), not phenylacetic acid, acetophenone, or a diacid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed benzoic acid correct; distractors (chain-retained acid, ketone, diacid, propanoic acid) all wrong. SMILES valid, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with Sn and HCl, then neutralized with base. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","[O-][N+](=O)c1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","ON=Nc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Sn/HCl (like Fe/HCl or H2/Pd) reduces an aromatic nitro group to a primary aromatic amine. Nitrobenzene gives aniline. It does not give phenol (no C-O), benzylamine (no new CH2), an azo compound, and the nitro is not left unchanged.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed aniline correct; phenol/benzylamine/azo/unchanged-nitro are valid distractors. SMILES valid, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"4-tert-Butyltoluene is treated with hot KMnO4 and acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1","Cc1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)(C)O)cc1","OCc1ccc(C(C)(C)C)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Benzylic oxidation requires a benzylic C-H. The methyl has benzylic hydrogens and is oxidized to COOH, but the tert-butyl quaternary benzylic carbon has no benzylic H and survives intact. Only the methyl is converted (4-tert-butylbenzoic acid).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed 4-tert-butylbenzoic acid correct; both-COOH, methyl-survives, and partial-oxidation distractors all wrong. Two-concept (oxidation + benzylic-H selectivity), in scope, SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Clc1ccc(NC)cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CNc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1Cl","Clc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1NC","COc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]","CNc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1[N+](=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"Two nitro groups ortho and para to the C-Cl strongly activate it for SNAr; methylamine (Sanger reagent chemistry) displaces chloride at C1 while both nitro groups remain. The nucleophile is the amine, not methoxide, and a nitro group is not the leaving group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified substrate is correct 1-chloro-2,4-dinitrobenzene (matches PubChem); keyed product correctly puts NHMe at C1 with both NO2 retained. Distractors all wrong. SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Cumene (isopropylbenzene) is to be converted to benzoic acid in one oxidative step. Which reagent/condition accomplishes the side-chain (benzylic) oxidation to -COOH?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["H2 / Pd-C","Hot KMnO4, then H3O+","Br2 / FeBr3","NaBH4 / CH3OH","Cold dilute KMnO4 (Baeyer test conditions)"],"correct":1,"why":"Hot (vigorous) KMnO4 performs benzylic oxidation: any alkyl group bearing a benzylic C-H is degraded to a ring-attached -COOH, so isopropyl becomes -COOH (benzoic acid). H2/Pd reduces, Br2/FeBr3 brominates the ring, NaBH4 reduces carbonyls only, and cold dilute KMnO4 only dihydroxylates alkenes (none present).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed hot KMnO4 is the only reagent giving benzylic oxidation to COOH; other four are valid wrong reagents. Reagent-selection text options with substrate drawn (correct format). SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"m-Xylene (1,3-dimethylbenzene) is treated with a large excess of hot KMnO4, then acidified. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc(C)c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)O","OC(=O)c1ccc(C(=O)O)cc1","Cc1cccc(C(=O)O)c1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1cccc(C(=O)O)c1"],"correct":4,"why":"With excess hot KMnO4 each benzylic methyl is oxidized to -COOH. The 1,3 (meta) relationship is preserved, giving isophthalic acid (benzene-1,3-dicarboxylic acid). The product is not terephthalic (para) or phthalic (ortho) diacid, not a mono-acid, and not benzoic acid.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromatic-other","issue":"Verified: keyed isophthalic acid (1,3) matches PubChem ref; para/ortho diacid, mono-acid, and benzoic acid are valid distractors differing by regiochemistry. SMILES valid, in scope (recognition depth)."},{"q":"Butanal is treated with ammonia (NH3) and sodium cyanoborohydride (NaBH3CN). What is the major product of this reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CCCC=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C)N","CCCC#N","CCCCN","CCCCCN","CCCCO"],"correct":2,"why":"Reductive amination of butanal with NH3 forms an imine that NaBH3CN reduces to the primary amine butylamine (CCCCN), same carbon count. Distractors: alcohol (no over-reduction with cyanoborohydride at the carbonyl), branched 2-aminobutane (wrong regiochemistry), pentylamine (extra carbon), butanenitrile (wrong functional group). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 (CCCCN) correct; all 5 SMILES valid; single reactive center, in DAT scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with dimethylamine ((CH3)2NH) and NaBH3CN. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CN(C)C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CNC1CCCCC1","CN(C)C1=CCCCC1","NC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Secondary amine + ketone gives an iminium ion (enamine in equilibrium) that NaBH3CN reduces to the tertiary amine N,N-dimethylcyclohexylamine. The enamine option is the intermediate, not the isolated product; the alcohol would need plain carbonyl reduction; the N-methyl and primary amines have the wrong N-substitution. Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid (enamine distractor well-formed); in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Benzyl bromide is subjected to a Gabriel synthesis: (1) potassium phthalimide, (2) hydrazine (NH2NH2). What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)N1Cc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","C(c1ccccc1)NCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Phthalimide N displaces bromide by SN2 at the benzylic carbon; hydrazinolysis liberates the primary amine benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (no SN2 amine), aniline (N on ring), the N-benzyl phthalimide intermediate, and dibenzylamine (Gabriel avoids over-alkylation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid incl. phthalimide intermediate; Gabriel is standard DAT content.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Pentanenitrile (CH3CH2CH2CH2C≡N) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#N","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCCNC","CCCCN","CCCCCN","CCCCC(N)=O","CCCCC=O"],"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a nitrile fully to a primary amine, adding two hydrides; the nitrile carbon becomes CH2NH2 with no loss of carbon, giving pentylamine (CCCCCN, 5 carbons). Distractors: butylamine (lost a carbon), pentanal (DIBAL-type partial stop), an N-methyl amine, and the amide (no C-N reduction). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: pentanenitrile = 5 C, product CCCCCN (index 0) correct; all SMILES valid; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"N,N-Dimethylacetamide (CH3CON(CH3)2) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)N(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCNC","CCN(C)C","CN(C)C","CC(O)N(C)C","CCO"],"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl to a CH2 while keeping the carbon-nitrogen bond, so N,N-dimethylacetamide gives the tertiary amine N,N-dimethylethylamine. Ethanol is the trap for treating the amide like an ester (C-N cleavage), trimethylamine would require breaking a carbon-carbon bond, N-methylethylamine has lost a methyl from nitrogen, and the carbinolamine (hydroxyl and nitrogen on the same carbon) is a fleeting intermediate, not a product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid; amide-vs-ester contrast is sophomore depth, in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Nitrobenzene is treated with H2 over Pd/C (equivalently Sn/HCl then base). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Nc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","c1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Reduction of an aromatic nitro group gives the aromatic amine aniline (Nc1ccccc1). Distractors: phenol (no O-retention here), nitrosobenzene (partial-reduction intermediate), benzylamine (N off-ring), and azobenzene (controlled basic conditions, not standard hydrogenation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 (aniline) correct; all SMILES valid incl. azobenzene distractor; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Acetophenone (C6H5COCH3) is treated with methylamine (CH3NH2) and NaBH3CN. What is the major product of this reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["NCCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(NC)c1ccccc1","CC(N)c1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The ketone condenses with methylamine to give an iminium at the carbonyl carbon, reduced by NaBH3CN to the secondary amine N-methyl-1-phenylethylamine (CC(NC)c1ccccc1). Distractors: primary amine (ignores the N-methyl partner), benzylic alcohol (plain reduction), N-methylbenzylamine (wrong skeleton), and phenethylamine (N migrated). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct (secondary amine, N on benzylic C); all SMILES valid; layered regio+amine-class but in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"Benzamide (C6H5CONH2) is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C(N)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces the amide carbonyl to a methylene while retaining the C-N bond, converting C(=O)NH2 into CH2NH2 to give benzylamine (NCc1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (C-N cleavage), aniline (wrong skeleton), benzaldehyde (incomplete), and toluene (full deoxygenation). Exactly one correct.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: index 0 correct; all SMILES valid; difficulty 3 slightly generous but C-N-retention concept with multiple plausible distractors justifies it; in scope.","group":"amine-synthesis"},{"q":"The secondary amine shown is the target product. Which combination of starting materials and reagent best prepares it through reductive amination?","q_smiles":"CCNC1CCCCC1","opts":["Cyclohexanol + ethylamine, then NaBH4","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then NaOH","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then LiAlH4 followed by H3O+","Cyclohexanone + ethylamine, then NaBH3CN (cat. acid)","Bromocyclohexane + ethylamine, then NaBH3CN"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Reductive amination condenses cyclohexanone with ethylamine to an iminium, and NaBH3CN at mild acid selectively reduces it to N-ethylcyclohexanamine without reducing the ketone. Alcohol cannot form the imine, NaOH does no reduction, and the alkyl-halide route is SN2 alkylation, not reductive amination.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept reductive amination, in scope."},{"q":"Benzyl bromide is converted to benzylamine. Which reagent sequence accomplishes this transformation cleanly without over-alkylation to secondary/tertiary amines?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["NaNH2, then H2O","KCN, then LiAlH4","Potassium phthalimide; then hydrazine (or aqueous NaOH/H+) workup","NaN3, then no further treatment","Excess NH3, then heat"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Gabriel synthesis: the phthalimide anion (one nucleophilic N) does SN2 on the primary benzyl halide, and hydrazinolysis releases the pure primary amine, avoiding the over-alkylation of direct ammonia. KCN/LiAlH4 adds a carbon (phenethylamine) and NaN3 alone stops at the azide.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; standard Gabriel, in scope."},{"q":"The nitrile shown is to be converted to butan-1-amine (a primary amine with the same number of carbons). Which reagent achieves this reduction?","q_smiles":"CCCC#N","opts":["LiAlH4, then H3O+","H2 with a poisoned Lindlar catalyst","NaBH4 in methanol","DIBAL-H (1 equiv) at low temperature, then H3O+","Hot aqueous H2SO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 fully reduces R-C#N to R-CH2-NH2 with no change in carbon count, giving butan-1-amine. NaBH4 is too weak, Lindlar reduces alkynes, hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes to the carboxylic acid, and DIBAL-H (1 equiv) stops at the imine/aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept nitrile reduction, in scope."},{"q":"Aniline is to be made from the aromatic compound shown. Which reagent set reduces the nitro group to a primary amine?","q_smiles":"O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","opts":["NaBH4 in ethanol","LiAlH4 only (no other change possible)","O3, then Zn/H2O","KMnO4, hot","Sn (or Fe) with HCl, then NaOH workup"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Aromatic nitro groups reduce to anilines under dissolving-metal conditions (Sn/HCl or Fe/HCl, then base). NaBH4 does not reduce nitroarenes, KMnO4 and O3 are oxidative/cleaving, and LiAlH4 on nitroarenes is unreliable (azo stage), so the metal/acid route is the standard answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept nitro reduction, in scope."},{"q":"The amide shown is to be reduced. What is the product, and which reagent is required?","q_smiles":"CCC(N)=O","opts":["Propan-1-amine, using NaBH4 in water","Propan-1-ol, using LiAlH4","Propanal, using NaBH4","Propanoic acid, using LiAlH4","Propan-1-amine (CH3CH2CH2NH2), using LiAlH4 then H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide C(=O)NH2 to CH2-NH2, removing the carbonyl oxygen and retaining nitrogen, so propanamide gives propan-1-amine. NaBH4 does not reduce amides, and LiAlH4 does not stop at the acid, alcohol, or aldehyde.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; single-concept amide reduction, in scope."},{"q":"The intermediate shown forms when benzaldehyde reacts with methylamine. What kind of species is it, and what reagent converts it to N-methyl-1-phenylmethanamine (PhCH2NHCH3)?","q_smiles":"CN=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["An imine (Schiff base); reduce with NaBH4 (or NaBH3CN)","An amide; reduce with LiAlH4","A hemiaminal; oxidize with PCC","A nitrile; reduce with LiAlH4","An enamine; hydrolyze with H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehyde plus primary amine gives an imine (C=N, Schiff base); reducing the C=N with NaBH4/NaBH3CN gives the secondary amine PhCH2NHCH3. It has no N-C=C (not an enamine), the N is doubly bonded (not hemiaminal/amide), and it is not a nitrile.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); SMILES CN=Cc1ccccc1 is the correct imine; classification + reduction (two layered concepts), in scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone (shown) is treated with dimethylamine and NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions. What is the product, and why is dimethylamine able to undergo reductive amination here?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction; secondary amines cannot do reductive amination","1-phenylethanol; NaBH3CN reduces the ketone directly","A primary amine PhCH(CH3)NH2; one N-CH3 is lost during reduction","N,N-dimethyl-1-phenylethanamine; the secondary amine forms an iminium ion that NaBH3CN reduces","An amide; the amine attacks and water is lost"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A secondary amine plus a ketone forms an iminium ion (no neutral imine possible), and NaBH3CN reduces it to the tertiary amine N,N-dimethyl-1-phenylethanamine. NaBH3CN does not appreciably reduce the free ketone under mild acid, no amide forms, and no N-CH3 is lost.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); valid SMILES; iminium-from-secondary-amine concept, two layered ideas, in scope."},{"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis using potassium phthalimide (shown) followed by hydrazinolysis, which alkyl halide will give the highest yield of the desired primary amine?","q_smiles":"O=C1c2ccccc2C(=O)[N-]1.[K+]","opts":["bromobenzene (an aryl halide)","1-bromobutane (a primary alkyl halide)","2-chloro-2-methylbutane (a tertiary halide)","vinyl bromide (a vinyl halide)","2-bromo-2-methylpropane (tert-butyl bromide)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The phthalimide anion alkylates via SN2, so it needs an unhindered substrate; 1-bromobutane (primary) gives the best yield. Tertiary halides undergo E2 elimination, and aryl/vinyl halides are unreactive toward SN2.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (idx 0); potassium phthalimide SMILES valid (parses to phthalimide anion + K+); SN2 substrate selection, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Phenylacetaldehyde (drawn) is treated with excess dimethylamine and NaBH3CN (reductive amination). Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=CCc1ccccc1","opts":["CN(C)CCc1ccccc1","CN(C)C(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","CNCCc1ccccc1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Reductive amination: phenylacetaldehyde condenses with dimethylamine to give an iminium ion, which NaBH3CN reduces selectively to the amine. All carbons are kept, so the product is N,N-dimethyl-2-phenylethylamine; the former carbonyl carbon is now a CH2 bonded to NMe2. The secondary amine with a single N-methyl would need methylamine instead of dimethylamine, N,N-dimethylbenzylamine has lost a carbon, 2-phenylethanol is reduction of the aldehyde with no amine involved, and the amide is not formed under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (drawn) is reacted with NH3 and NaBH3CN. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["N=C1CCCCC1","CNC1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","NC1CCCCC1","NCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Reductive amination with ammonia: cyclohexanone and NH3 form an imine (iminium ion under mild acid) that NaBH3CN reduces, giving the primary amine cyclohexylamine; the former carbonyl carbon is now a CH bonded to NH2. Cyclohexanol is reduction of the carbonyl with no amine involved, the imine itself is not isolated because NaBH3CN reduces it, N-methylcyclohexylamine would need methylamine, and cyclohexylmethylamine has an extra carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"In a Gabriel synthesis, potassium phthalimide is alkylated by benzyl bromide (drawn), and the resulting N-substituted phthalimide is then cleaved (hydrazine or aqueous base/acid). Which structure is the isolated amine product?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["Nc1ccccc1","c1ccc(CNCc2ccccc2)cc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1","CNCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The Gabriel synthesis makes pure primary amines: the phthalimide anion displaces bromide from benzyl bromide by SN2, and hydrazine (or hydrolysis) then releases the amine, giving benzylamine. Because nitrogen is alkylated only once and stays protected by the phthalimide until the end, secondary amines such as N-methylbenzylamine and dibenzylamine cannot form. Aniline would need nitrogen on the ring itself (Gabriel does not make arylamines), and benzyl alcohol would require an oxygen nucleophile.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; Gabriel is a single named reaction within DAT scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"4-Methylbenzonitrile (drawn) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C#N)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(CO)cc1","Cc1ccc(CN)cc1","Cc1ccc(CCN)cc1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 adds hydride twice to a nitrile, reducing it completely to a primary amine in which the former nitrile carbon is now a CH2 bonded to NH2: the product is 4-methylbenzylamine. 4-Methylaniline would mean a carbon was lost, 4-methylbenzaldehyde is a partial reduction that LiAlH4 does not stop at, 4-methylbenzyl alcohol has oxygen in place of nitrogen, and the homolog with an extra CH2 cannot arise from a reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, single concept, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"3-Nitrotoluene (drawn) is treated with H2/Pd-C (or Sn/HCl). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"Cc1cccc([N+](=O)[O-])c1","opts":["Cc1cccc(CN)c1","Cc1ccc(N)cc1","Cc1ccccc1N","Cc1cccc(N)c1","Cc1cccc(C=O)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Reduction of an aromatic nitro group with H2/Pd-C or Sn/HCl converts it to an amino group at the same ring position, so 3-nitrotoluene gives 3-methylaniline (m-toluidine). The regiochemistry is fixed by the starting material; the para and ortho isomers would require a substituent to migrate, which a reduction cannot do. 3-Methylbenzylamine inserts a CH2 that was never there, and the aldehyde is an oxidation product, not a reduction product.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0, regiochem (meta) preserved, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"N-Methylbenzamide (drawn) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C(NC)c1ccccc1","opts":["CNCc1ccccc1","CN(C)c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","NCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide carbonyl to CH2 and keeps the carbon-nitrogen bond, so N-methylbenzamide gives the secondary amine N-methylbenzylamine. Benzylamine would need the N-methyl group stripped off, benzyl alcohol would need the C-N bond cleaved (that is what happens to esters, not amides), and aniline and N,N-dimethylaniline put the nitrogen directly on the ring, which does not match the starting connectivity.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; two layered points (amide reduction + C-N retention) within DAT scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Butan-2-one (drawn) undergoes reductive amination with benzylamine and NaBH3CN. The product N-(butan-2-yl)benzylamine has a new stereocenter at the former carbonyl carbon. Which statement best describes the stereochemical outcome?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)=O","opts":["A single meso compound forms","A racemic mixture (equal R and S) is formed, because hydride adds to a planar iminium ion from either face with equal probability","No stereocenter is generated, so the product is achiral","Only the (S)-enantiomer forms, due to retention at the carbonyl carbon","Only the (R)-enantiomer forms, because hydride must add anti to the larger ethyl group"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The prochiral carbonyl carbon goes through a planar sp2 iminium; with an achiral reductant and no chiral bias, hydride adds equally to both faces, giving a racemate. The product is chiral (four different groups on that C), so the achiral option is wrong; nothing biases one face; and only one stereocenter precludes a meso form. Recognition-level stereochemistry, not Felkin-Anh, so in scope.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; conceptual stereochem at recognition depth, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"5-Aminopentanal (drawn) is treated with NaBH3CN under mildly acidic conditions, where the molecule undergoes INTRAMOLECULAR reductive amination. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=CCCCCN","opts":["NCCCCCO","C1CCCNCC1","C1CCNC1","CCCCC=N","C1CCNCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The amine and the aldehyde are in the same molecule, so they condense intramolecularly to a cyclic iminium ion, which NaBH3CN reduces to a saturated cyclic amine. Count the ring atoms: the nitrogen, the four CH2 groups between it and the carbonyl, and the former carbonyl carbon make six, so the product is piperidine. Pyrrolidine (five-membered) and azepane (seven-membered) are the wrong ring sizes, 5-amino-1-pentanol is reduction of the aldehyde without ring closure, and the acyclic imine never cyclized.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-synthesis","issue":"None. SMILES valid, correct=0; ring-size + intramolecular reductive amination at recognition depth, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Aniline is treated with acetic anhydride [(CH3CO)2O]. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["CCNc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1N","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Nc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetic anhydride N-acylates aniline's nucleophilic nitrogen to give acetanilide. Distractors (O-acyl phenyl acetate, N-ethyl reduction, ring C-acylation, carbamic acid) are all wrong, leaving exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified, all SMILES parse."},{"q":"Cyclohexylamine reacts with benzoyl chloride (C6H5COCl) in the presence of base. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"NC1CCCCC1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)NC1CCCCC1","ClC1CCCCC1","O=C(c1ccccc1)OC1CCCCC1","O=C(Cl)NC1CCCCC1","c1ccc(CNC2CCCCC2)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Retained-Cl carbamoyl chloride, ester from an amine, alkyl chloride, and a reductive-amination amine are all incorrect.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified."},{"q":"Butan-2-amine is exhaustively methylated with excess CH3I, then treated with Ag2O and heated (Hofmann elimination). What is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)N","opts":["C=CCC","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","CC=CC","CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Hofmann elimination of the bulky quaternary ammonium hydroxide gives the less-substituted (anti-Zaitsev) alkene by abstracting the most accessible terminal beta-H, yielding 1-butene (C=CCC). 2-butene (Zaitsev), the alcohol (substitution), the ammonium intermediate, and butane are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (1-butene) verified."},{"q":"Pentan-2-amine undergoes exhaustive methylation (excess CH3I) followed by Ag2O / heat. Predict the major (Hofmann) alkene.","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)N","opts":["CCCC(C)O","CCCCC","C=CCCC","C/C=C/CC","CC=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Hofmann elimination favors the least-substituted alkene via abstraction of the terminal beta-H, giving 1-pentene (C=CCCC). Internal 2-pentene (Zaitsev) and its explicit trans isomer, the substitution alcohol, and pentane are wrong. Exactly one right answer; the cis/trans-2-pentene distractors are well-chosen.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (1-pentene) verified."},{"q":"Aniline is treated with NaNO2 / HCl at 0-5 C to form a diazonium salt, which is then treated with CuBr (Sandmeyer reaction). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Clc1ccccc1","Brc1ccccc1","BrCc1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Sandmeyer with CuBr replaces -N2+ by Br at the same ring carbon with loss of N2, giving bromobenzene (Brc1ccccc1). Chlorobenzene (wrong copper halide), phenol (aqueous hydrolysis path), benzyl bromide (wrong connectivity), and p-bromoaniline (retains N) are wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 verified."},{"q":"p-Toluidine (4-methylaniline) is converted to its diazonium salt with NaNO2 / HCl (0-5 C), then warmed in H2O. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccc(N=O)cc1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","Oc1ccc(O)cc1","Cc1ccccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Warm aqueous hydrolysis of an aryl diazonium salt replaces -N2+ by -OH at the same position, giving p-cresol (Cc1ccc(O)cc1) with methyl still para. The ortho phenol (position migration), aryl bromide (no Cu halide), nitroso compound, and hydroquinone are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (p-cresol) verified."},{"q":"Consider the three nitrogen compounds: aniline (C6H5NH2), cyclohexylamine (an aliphatic primary amine), and acetanilide (C6H5NHCOCH3). Rank them from MOST to LEAST basic at nitrogen. The molecule drawn is aniline for reference.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["acetanilide > aniline > cyclohexylamine","aniline > acetanilide > cyclohexylamine","cyclohexylamine > aniline > acetanilide","aniline > cyclohexylamine > acetanilide","cyclohexylamine > acetanilide > aniline"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Basicity tracks how available the nitrogen lone pair is. In cyclohexylamine the lone pair sits on an sp3 nitrogen with only an electron-donating alkyl group, so it is the most basic. In aniline the lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring and is less available. In acetanilide the lone pair is conjugated with the carbonyl (amide resonance), so it is barely basic at all. The order is cyclohexylamine > aniline > acetanilide.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; ranking correct, opts_are_structures=false appropriate, q_smiles valid."},{"q":"A benzenediazonium salt (C6H5N2+, from aniline + NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C) is added to a cold basic solution of phenol (drawn). Predict the major azo-coupling product.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1O","Oc1ccc(Nc2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","O=C1C=CC(=Nc2ccccc2)C=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Diazonium salts are weak electrophiles that couple at the para position of the strongly activated phenol via EAS, giving 4-(phenylazo)phenol (Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1). The ortho isomer, the reduced N-H (diarylamine) bridge, an extra-OH catechol, and a quinone-imine are wrong.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct=0 (para-azo) verified, all five options distinct and valid."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is treated with a reagent to convert the -NH2 group into an amide (acetanilide, CH3C(=O)NH-C6H5). Which reagent accomplishes this acylation most cleanly?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Methanol, CH3OH / H+","Methyl iodide, CH3I","NaBH4 then H2O","Acetic anhydride, (CH3CO)2O","Acetic acid, CH3COOH (no activator)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Acylation of a primary amine to an amide requires an activated carboxylic acid derivative. Acetic anhydride (or acetyl chloride) readily acylates the nucleophilic nitrogen to give acetanilide. A free carboxylic acid alone just forms an ammonium carboxylate salt; CH3OH/H+ makes esters, CH3I alkylates (not acylates), and NaBH4 is a reductant.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Aniline SMILES valid; acetic anhydride is the clean acylating agent for acetanilide; distractors all map to other amine reactions. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Rank the following nitrogen compounds from MOST basic to LEAST basic: ethylamine (CH3CH2NH2), ammonia (NH3), and aniline (drawn, C6H5NH2).","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["ethylamine > ammonia > aniline","aniline > ammonia > ethylamine","aniline > ethylamine > ammonia","ammonia > ethylamine > aniline","ethylamine > aniline > ammonia"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Alkyl groups donate electron density (inductive), making the lone pair more available, so ethylamine is the most basic. Ammonia is intermediate. In aniline the nitrogen lone pair is delocalized into the benzene ring, greatly lowering availability and basicity, so aniline is the weakest base. Order: ethylamine > ammonia > aniline.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Standard textbook basicity order (alkylamine > NH3 > arylamine). Aniline drawn in stem. One defensible answer; in scope, no pKa math required."},{"q":"sec-Butyltrimethylammonium hydroxide (drawn) is heated, undergoing Hofmann elimination. Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C.[OH-]","opts":["C#CCC","CCCC","C/C=C\\C","C/C=C/C","C=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hofmann elimination of a quaternary ammonium hydroxide follows the anti-Zaitsev (Hofmann) rule because of the bulky leaving group and acidic terminal protons: the LESS substituted alkene dominates. Removing a proton from the terminal CH3 gives 1-butene (C=CCC), not the more-substituted 2-butene.","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Substrate is CH3CH2-CH(NMe3+)-CH3; Hofmann favors less-substituted terminal alkene = 1-butene (C=CCC, opt0). Distractors are 2-butene E/Z, the alkane, and an alkyne. All SMILES valid; visual structure options. One defensible answer."},{"q":"N-Ethylbenzamide (drawn) is the product of combining ethylamine with one reagent. What TYPE of reaction formed the C-N bond shown, and what is the best reagent partner?","q_smiles":"CCNC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Nucleophilic acyl substitution; benzoyl chloride (C6H5COCl)","Electrophilic aromatic substitution; benzene + Cl2","Reductive amination; benzaldehyde + NaBH4","Diazonium coupling; benzenediazonium chloride","SN2 alkylation; bromoethane (C2H5Br)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The amide C-N bond comes from an amine attacking an activated acyl carbon (nucleophilic acyl substitution). Benzoyl chloride + ethylamine gives N-ethylbenzamide. SN2 alkylation would give an amine, not an amide; reductive amination gives an amine; diazonium coupling forms azo (N=N) bonds, not amides.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Amide product drawn; nucleophilic acyl substitution with benzoyl chloride is the only route that gives an amide. Distractors give amines or azo products. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is to be converted into a benzenediazonium salt for later substitution. Which reagent/condition set generates the diazonium ion?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Br2, FeBr3","(CH3CO)2O, pyridine","NaNO2, HCl, 0-5 degrees C","NaNO2, HCl, reflux at 80 degrees C","LiAlH4 then H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Diazotization of a primary aromatic amine uses nitrous acid generated in situ from NaNO2 + HCl, run COLD (0-5 degrees C) to keep the unstable aryldiazonium salt from decomposing. Warming (reflux) destroys it by hydrolysis to phenol. Acetic anhydride acylates, LiAlH4 reduces, Br2/FeBr3 brominates the ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Classic diazotization (NaNO2/HCl, 0-5 C). Cold temperature is the discriminator vs the reflux distractor. Aniline drawn. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (drawn) is a versatile intermediate. Which reagent converts it into benzonitrile (C6H5-C#N)?","q_smiles":"[N+](#N)c1ccccc1","opts":["KI","H2O, heat","CuBr","CuCN","H3PO2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In the Sandmeyer reaction, the diazonium group is displaced by the nucleophile delivered with copper(I). CuCN installs a nitrile (benzonitrile). CuBr gives bromobenzene, H2O/heat gives phenol, KI gives iodobenzene, and H3PO2 reduces the diazonium to benzene (loses N2 entirely).","difficulty":2,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Sandmeyer with CuCN gives benzonitrile. Diazonium SMILES valid (canonicalizes to N#[N+]c1ccccc1). Each distractor is a real diazonium transformation giving a different product. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Cyclohexylamine (drawn) is compared with aniline (C6H5NH2) for basicity. Which statement correctly identifies the stronger base and the reason?","q_smiles":"NC1CCCCC1","opts":["Aniline is more basic; the ring donates electron density to nitrogen","Aniline is more basic; aromatic resonance stabilizes the protonated form","Cyclohexylamine is more basic; its N lone pair is localized and not delocalized into any ring","They are equally basic; both nitrogens are sp3","Cyclohexylamine is less basic; the ring withdraws electrons inductively"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Cyclohexylamine is an aliphatic amine: its nitrogen lone pair stays localized and freely available to accept a proton. In aniline the lone pair conjugates into the aromatic ring, lowering availability and basicity. So cyclohexylamine is the stronger base because its lone pair is not delocalized.","difficulty":1,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. Cyclohexylamine (aliphatic, localized lone pair) is more basic than aniline (lone pair delocalized into ring). Cyclohexylamine drawn. One defensible answer; reasoning is qualitative, in scope."},{"q":"Pentan-2-yltrimethylammonium hydroxide (drawn) is heated to give Hofmann elimination. Considering both regiochemistry (Hofmann orientation) and the favored geometry, which is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C.[OH-]","opts":["CC=C(C)C","C=CCCC","CC/C=C/C","CCCCC","CC/C=C\\C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hofmann elimination favors the LESS substituted (terminal) alkene because of the bulky NMe3+ leaving group and the more accessible/acidic terminal protons. Deprotonating the C1 methyl gives 1-pentene (C=CCCC), the major product, rather than the more substituted internal 2-pentene that Zaitsev would predict.","difficulty":3,"group":"amine-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct keyed answer. Substrate CH3-CH(NMe3+)-CH2CH2CH3; Hofmann gives terminal 1-pentene (C=CCCC, opt0). All SMILES valid; distractors are 2-pentene E/Z, the alkane, and a wrong branched skeleton. Minor stem wording: '...favored geometry' is window-dressing since the major product (1-pentene) has no E/Z geometry, but it does not introduce ambiguity. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The amine shown (benzylamine) is treated with one equivalent of acetic anhydride, (CH3CO)2O. Which structure is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"NCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)O","CCC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)NCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)N(Cc1ccccc1)C(C)=O","CC(=O)OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The primary amine nitrogen is the nucleophile: it attacks one carbonyl of acetic anhydride, the tetrahedral intermediate collapses, and acetate leaves as the by-product. With one equivalent, benzylamine is acylated once on nitrogen, giving N-benzylacetamide. Benzyl acetate would need an alcohol nucleophile, the N,N-diacetyl product needs a second equivalent and forcing conditions, the propanamide has the wrong acyl group, and acetic acid is the by-product rather than the organic product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid; classic amine + anhydride acylation, in scope."},{"q":"sec-Butylamine is exhaustively methylated (excess CH3I), then treated with Ag2O and heated (Hofmann elimination). Which alkene is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/C","C=CCC","CC(C)=C","CCCC","C/C=C\\C"],"correct":1,"why":"Exhaustive methylation turns the amine into a trimethylammonium ion, and heating the hydroxide salt triggers an E2 elimination. The trimethylamino leaving group is so bulky that hydroxide removes the most accessible beta hydrogen, on the terminal CH3, so the less substituted Hofmann alkene, 1-butene, is the major product. cis- and trans-2-Butene are the Zaitsev alkenes that would dominate in a normal E2 with a halide leaving group, 2-methylpropene has the wrong carbon skeleton, and butane is not an elimination product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, 1-butene). Hofmann regiochemistry standard DAT content; SMILES all valid."},{"q":"Rank the following four nitrogen compounds from MOST basic to LEAST basic (consider the basicity of the lone pair drawn): ethylamine, aniline, p-nitroaniline, and acetamide.","q_smiles":"CCN.Nc1ccccc1.Nc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1.CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline > acetamide","ethylamine > p-nitroaniline > aniline > acetamide","acetamide > ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline","aniline > ethylamine > acetamide > p-nitroaniline","p-nitroaniline > aniline > ethylamine > acetamide"],"correct":0,"why":"Basicity follows lone-pair availability. Ethylamine has a localized lone pair on an sp3 nitrogen with an electron-donating ethyl group, so it is the strongest base. Aniline's lone pair is delocalized into the ring, and in p-nitroaniline the para nitro group pulls that lone pair even further into the ring, so it is weaker than aniline. Acetamide is the weakest because its lone pair is tied up in amide resonance with the carbonyl. The order is ethylamine > aniline > p-nitroaniline > acetamide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0). Resonance/induction basicity ranking is sophomore-level; q_smiles draws all four species and is valid."},{"q":"p-Toluidine (4-methylaniline) is treated with NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C to form the diazonium salt, then warmed in aqueous acid (H2O, heat). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(N)cc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","Cc1ccc(Cl)cc1","Cc1ccc(O)cc1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C converts the primary aromatic amine into an aryldiazonium salt. Warming it in water replaces the excellent leaving group N2 with a hydroxyl at the same ring position, giving 4-methylphenol (p-cresol). 4-Chlorotoluene would need CuCl (Sandmeyer), toluene would need H3PO2 as the reductant, 4-nitrotoluene is not a diazonium replacement product, and benzoic acid has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, p-cresol). Standard diazonium hydrolysis; SMILES valid, properly visual."},{"q":"Aniline is treated with acetyl chloride (CH3COCl) in the presence of pyridine. Which structure is the major product, and what role does pyridine play?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Nc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","Clc1ccccc1","CC(=O)N(C(C)=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The aniline nitrogen attacks the acid chloride carbonyl and chloride leaves, giving the amide acetanilide. Pyridine is a non-nucleophilic base that scavenges the HCl produced; it is not incorporated into the product. Phenyl acetate would need an oxygen nucleophile, the N,N-diacetyl product is not formed by a single acylation, chlorobenzene is not an acylation product, and the ring ketone 4-aminoacetophenone would be a Friedel-Crafts result that does not occur under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, acetanilide). Classic amine acylation with base role; SMILES valid."},{"q":"2-Aminopentane is converted to its quaternary ammonium hydroxide (excess CH3I, then Ag2O/H2O) and heated. Identify the major alkene formed in this Hofmann elimination.","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)[N+](C)(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC/C=C\\C","CCCCC","CC/C=C/C","C=CCCC","C=C(C)CC"],"correct":3,"why":"Exhaustive methylation places a trimethylammonium group on C2 of the pentane chain, and heating with hydroxide causes an E2 elimination. Because the leaving group is bulky, the base removes the most accessible beta hydrogen, on the terminal CH3, so the less substituted Hofmann product, 1-pentene, is major. cis- and trans-2-Pentene are the Zaitsev alkenes that a halide leaving group would favor, 2-methyl-1-butene has the wrong carbon skeleton, and pentane is not an elimination product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, 1-pentene). Hofmann regiochemistry, single concept layered with stereo distractors; SMILES valid."},{"q":"An organic chemist wants to convert cyclohexanone plus methylamine (CH3NH2) into N-methylcyclohexanamine (a secondary amine, the C=O carbon now bearing N-H and CH3). Which single reagent set accomplishes this in one operation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CH3NH2 then LiAlH4 reduction of the amide","NaNO2/HCl at 0-5 C (diazotization)","CH3COCl then pyridine (acylation)","CH3NH2 with NaBH3CN (reductive amination)","CH3NH2 with NaBH4 added before the amine, then acid workup"],"correct":3,"why":"The one-step route is reductive amination: cyclohexanone and methylamine condense to an iminium ion, and NaBH3CN, a mild hydride source, reduces the iminium selectively while leaving the ketone alone, giving N-methylcyclohexanamine directly. Acetyl chloride with pyridine would make an amide, diazotization needs an existing primary amine, there is no amide here for LiAlH4 to reduce, and NaBH4 added before the imine has formed simply reduces the ketone to cyclohexanol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, reductive amination). Reagent-selection text item with valid drawn substrate; in scope."},{"q":"Benzenediazonium chloride (from aniline + NaNO2/HCl, 0-5 C) is added to a basic solution of phenol (azo coupling). Considering BOTH the position of attack on phenol AND the geometry of the azo (N=N) linkage, which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Oc1ccccc1/N=N/c1ccccc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)cc1","Oc1ccc(/N=N\\c2ccccc2)cc1","Nc1ccc(O)cc1","Oc1cccc(/N=N/c2ccccc2)c1"],"correct":1,"why":"The diazonium ion is a weak electrophile, so it attacks only strongly activated rings; the phenoxide formed in base is activated by its oxygen lone pair, and attack goes to the less hindered para position. Azo compounds also prefer the trans (E) arrangement about the N=N bond, which keeps the two aryl groups apart. The major product is (E)-4-(phenyldiazenyl)phenol. The ortho azo product is sterically disfavored, the meta ring position is not activated, the cis (Z) azo isomer is the less stable geometry, and 4-aminophenol would require reducing the azo linkage.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"amine-reactions","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (index 0, para + trans). Two layered concepts (regio + stereo) within limit; azo coupling is standard sophomore Orgo II; SMILES valid."},{"q":"The diene shown (1,3-butadiene) is heated with maleic anhydride. Which structure is the major Diels-Alder product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["C=CC=CC1CC(=O)OC1=O","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CC12","O=C1OC(=O)[C@@H]2CC=CC[C@@H]12","O=C1OC(=O)[C@@H]2CC=CC[C@H]12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"In a Diels-Alder reaction the dienophile's stereochemistry is transferred to the product: both new sigma bonds form on the same face at the same time, so the two anhydride hydrogens, which are cis in maleic anhydride, remain cis in the cyclohexene. The product is cis-cyclohex-4-ene-1,2-dicarboxylic anhydride (cis-1,2,3,6-tetrahydrophthalic anhydride), an achiral meso compound. The trans-fused anhydride is impossible from a cis dienophile, the five-membered ring product has the wrong ring size, the open-chain structure that still contains a diene never cyclized, and phthalic anhydride is aromatic, which a single cycloaddition cannot produce.","difficulty":1,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"KEY INVERTED: keyed index 0 is the chiral TRANS-fused isomer (impossible from cis maleic anhydride); correct answer is index 1, the meso CIS adduct. Verified by 3D dihedral (34 vs 173 deg) and meso test across 5 conformers. Explanation already describes 'cis' correctly, so only the keyed index/SMILES choice is wrong. Corrected to 1."},{"q":"Isoprene (2-methyl-1,3-butadiene) reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) under thermal Diels-Alder conditions. Predict the major product (apply the 'para' regiochemistry rule).","q_smiles":"C=CC(=C)C.C=CC=O","opts":["C=CCC(=C)CC=O","Cc1ccc(C=O)cc1","CC1=CCC(C=O)CC1","CC1=CCCC(C=O)C1","CC1CCC(C=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"For a 2-substituted diene reacting with a monosubstituted dienophile, the 'para' rule applies: the diene substituent and the dienophile substituent end up 1,4 on the new cyclohexene. Isoprene plus acrolein therefore gives 4-methylcyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde, with the methyl on the ring double bond para to the CHO. The 1,3 ('meta') isomer has the wrong regiochemistry, the cyclopentane product has the wrong ring size and no double bond, the open-chain structure never cyclized, and 4-methylbenzaldehyde is aromatic, which a single Diels-Alder step cannot give.","difficulty":2,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; para regiochemistry sound; distractors are genuine meta/wrong-ring/open-chain/aromatic alternatives."},{"q":"1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (prop-2-enal) thermally. Predict the major product (apply the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule).","q_smiles":"COC=CC=C.C=CC=O","opts":["COC=CC=CCCC=O","COC1C=CC1C=O","COC1C=CCCC1C=O","COC1C=CC(C=O)CC1","COC1C=CCC(C=O)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A diene substituted at its terminal carbon (1-methoxy) and a monosubstituted dienophile follow the 'ortho' rule: the two substituents end up on adjacent ring carbons. Resonance explains it: the methoxy oxygen pushes electron density out to C4 of the diene, the nucleophilic end, which bonds to the electrophilic beta carbon of acrolein; the methoxy-bearing C1 then bonds to the carbon carrying the CHO. The product is 2-methoxycyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde, with the new ring double bond next to the methoxy carbon. The 1,4 ('para') and 1,3 ('meta') isomers have the wrong regiochemistry, the four-membered ring is a [2+2] skeleton, and the open-chain compound never cyclized.","difficulty":3,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed; ortho/para regiochemistry rules are standard DAT content, in scope. Difficulty 3 is slightly generous (ortho rule is a single-concept recognition item, ~2), but acceptable."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with one equivalent of p-benzoquinone in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the major mono-adduct?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C.O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1C=CC(=O)C=C1.C=CCC=C","O=C1CCC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","OC1=CC=C(O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1C=CC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"p-Benzoquinone is an excellent dienophile because each of its double bonds is flanked by two carbonyl groups. With one equivalent, butadiene adds across just one of those double bonds, building a new cyclohexene ring fused to the quinone while the other enedione C=C is left intact; the mono-adduct is 4a,5,8,8a-tetrahydro-1,4-naphthoquinone, which still contains both an enone double bond and the new ring alkene. The fully saturated dione has lost the enedione double bond, the hydroquinone has aromatized (a tautomerization that needs acid or base and does not happen under Diels-Alder conditions), 1,4-naphthoquinone has a fully aromatic second ring, and the pair of untouched starting materials shows no reaction at all.","difficulty":2,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. opt0 formula C10H10O2 = quinone + butadiene mono-adduct, retains one enedione C=C; distractors are valid over-reduced/no-reaction/tautomer/wrong-skeleton alternatives."},{"q":"Furan acts as the diene with maleic anhydride in a Diels-Alder reaction. Which structure is the cycloadduct?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1.O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1C=CC(=O)O1","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(O3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Furan is only weakly aromatic, so it behaves as a diene toward a strong dienophile like maleic anhydride. The two diene termini (the carbons on either side of oxygen) bond to the anhydride C=C, which turns the ring oxygen into a one-atom bridge: the product is the 7-oxabicyclo[2.2.1]heptene fused to the anhydride ring, with the new double bond between the former furan C3 and C4. The all-carbon bridged adduct is what cyclopentadiene would give, the unbridged cyclohexene adduct is the butadiene product, phthalic anhydride is aromatic and would need an oxidation, and plain maleic anhydride is just the unreacted dienophile.","difficulty":3,"group":"diels-alder","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed. opt0 formula C8H6O4 matches the oxabicyclic adduct with the correct oxygen bridge; distractors valid. In scope as a structure-recognition item."},{"q":"The cyclohexene product shown below is formed in a single step from two simpler unsaturated starting materials. Which combination of reactants, heated together, gives this product through a Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["1,3-Butadiene + ethane","1,3-Butadiene + ethylene","Two equivalents of ethylene","1-Butene + ethylene","Cyclohexane + ethylene"],"correct":1,"why":"Retro-Diels-Alder of cyclohexene (C6H10) cleanly gives 1,3-butadiene (4-pi diene) + ethylene (2-pi dienophile); the residual C2-C3 double bond is the diene's central bond. Ethane, 1-butene, cyclohexane all lack the correct pi system. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - classic retro-DA, q_smiles valid (C6H10 cyclohexene), index 0 correct."},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn below) is a highly reactive diene. To run a fast Diels-Alder reaction at or near room temperature, you want to pair it with the most electron-poor dienophile available. Which reagent is the best dienophile partner?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexane","Maleic anhydride","1-Hexene","Diethyl ether","Ethane"],"correct":1,"why":"Only maleic anhydride bears an activated C=C flanked by two carbonyls (electron-poor dienophile), the textbook fast partner. Cyclohexane, ethane, diethyl ether have no reactive C=C; 1-hexene is unactivated and sluggish. Exactly one best answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - cyclopentadiene SMILES verified as C5H6 1,3-isomer; index 0 correct."},{"q":"2,3-Dimethyl-1,3-butadiene (shown) reacts cleanly with maleic anhydride on warming. What is the correct classification of this transformation?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C(=C)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A free-radical chain halogenation","An E1 elimination","A [4+2] cycloaddition (Diels-Alder reaction)","An electrophilic aromatic substitution","An SN2 substitution"],"correct":2,"why":"Diene + alkene dienophile forming a six-membered ring is the definition of a [4+2] Diels-Alder cycloaddition. The other four mechanisms cannot build a ring from a diene plus dienophile. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - substrate SMILES valid (C6H10), reaction-type item, index 0 correct."},{"q":"The diene shown is (2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene, which can rotate freely about its central single bond. For it to undergo a Diels-Alder reaction, which conformation must the diene adopt, and why?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An aromatic conformation, because aromaticity drives the reaction","The s-trans conformation, because it minimizes steric strain","A fully linear conformation, because it maximizes conjugation","The s-cis conformation, because it places both terminal carbons close enough to bond to the dienophile","Either conformation reacts equally well, since geometry does not matter"],"correct":3,"why":"Only the s-cis conformation brings C1 and C4 of the diene close enough to form the two new sigma bonds in the cyclic TS; s-trans holds them too far apart. Standard sophomore concept. One defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"None - s-cis requirement is correct and in-scope; E/E SMILES valid."},{"q":"The bicyclic adduct drawn below is the kinetic Diels-Alder product of cyclopentadiene with maleic anhydride. The two carbonyl-bearing carbons sit tucked underneath the newly formed C=C bridge. Which descriptor names this stereochemical outcome, and what principle predicts it?","q_smiles":"O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The endo product, favored by the endo rule (secondary orbital overlap in the transition state)","The Markovnikov product, favored by carbocation stability","The exo product, favored because it is the more thermodynamically stable isomer","The trans product, favored by steric relief","The anti-Zaitsev product, favored by base-induced elimination"],"correct":0,"why":"The kinetic cyclopentadiene + maleic anhydride adduct is the endo isomer, explained by the endo rule (secondary orbital overlap). Distractors (exo, trans, Markovnikov, anti-Zaitsev) are wrong. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Minor: q_smiles draws the correct adduct skeleton but carries no stereodescriptors, so it does not visually distinguish endo vs exo; acceptable since this is a text/concept item, not a structure-pick. Endo rule is standard Orgo II. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"Tetracyanoethylene (TCNE, shown) is one of the fastest known dienophiles in Diels-Alder reactions. Compared with simple ethylene, why does TCNE react so much faster with a given diene?","q_smiles":"N#CC(C#N)=C(C#N)C#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["TCNE is aromatic, so it reacts by electrophilic substitution instead","The four cyano groups are electron-donating, raising the dienophile HOMO","The cyano groups make the alkene more nucleophilic toward the diene","TCNE is sterically smaller than ethylene, so it approaches the diene faster","The four cyano groups are strongly electron-withdrawing, lowering the dienophile LUMO so it overlaps better with the diene HOMO"],"correct":4,"why":"A normal Diels-Alder reaction is governed by the diene HOMO interacting with the dienophile LUMO. Each cyano group is strongly electron-withdrawing, so four of them pull the TCNE LUMO very low in energy; the smaller HOMO-LUMO gap means better orbital overlap with the diene and a far faster cycloaddition than with ethylene. The other explanations fail on the facts: nitriles withdraw rather than donate electrons, an electron-poor alkene is electrophilic rather than nucleophilic, TCNE is not aromatic, and it is larger than ethylene, not smaller.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Borderline scope: correct answer is phrased in explicit HOMO/LUMO frontier-orbital terms, which leans toward the FMO content the rubric flags as grad-level. The underlying concept (electron-poor dienophile reacts faster) is in scope, and the distractors are only defeated by the EWG-vs-EDG distinction, so the LUMO wording is the natural correct choice; kept but flagged. Index 0 correct."},{"q":"You need to choose the diene that will react FASTEST in a Diels-Alder reaction with maleic anhydride. The structure of cyclopentadiene is drawn for reference. Which diene below would react fastest, and why?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["(2Z,4Z)-2,4-Hexadiene, because cis double bonds are more reactive","1,3-Butadiene, because it is the smallest diene","An isolated (non-conjugated) 1,4-pentadiene, because it has two double bonds","(2E,4E)-2,4-Hexadiene, because its methyl groups donate electron density","Cyclopentadiene, because its ring locks it permanently in the reactive s-cis geometry"],"correct":4,"why":"Cyclopentadiene is locked s-cis by its ring, so essentially every molecule is reaction-ready, making it the fastest classic diene - the standard textbook answer. Acyclic dienes pay a rotation penalty; 1,4-pentadiene is non-conjugated and cannot do a DA at all. Distractor B's EDG claim is a real minor factor but is not the dominant reason, so cyclopentadiene remains the single best answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Minor: distractor B (hexadiene methyls donate density) names a genuinely rate-enhancing effect, but the locked s-cis geometry of cyclopentadiene is the textbook dominant factor, preserving exactly one best answer. SMILES valid, index 0 correct."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with the dienophile shown below (acrolein, an alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde) under thermal conditions. Which statement best describes both the reaction type and the role of the CHO group?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An electrophilic addition of HBr; the CHO directs Markovnikov selectivity","An aldol addition; the CHO acts as the nucleophile","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO is electron-donating and slows the reaction","A Diels-Alder [4+2] cycloaddition; the CHO is an electron-withdrawing group that activates the dienophile and speeds the reaction","A Wittig olefination; the CHO is converted to a new alkene"],"correct":3,"why":"A conjugated diene plus an alkene conjugated to a carbonyl is the classic Diels-Alder pairing: a thermal [4+2] cycloaddition that builds a cyclohexene ring, here cyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde. The CHO group is electron-withdrawing; it lowers the dienophile LUMO and activates the alkene, so it speeds the reaction up rather than slowing it down. No HBr, no base, and no phosphorus ylide are present, so electrophilic addition, aldol, and Wittig chemistry are not on the table.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"diels-alder","issue":"Difficulty mislabeled as 3 in source; this is a single/two-concept recognition item (reaction type + EWG role), effectively difficulty 2 - not a scope problem. CHO-activates phrasing is in scope (no frontier-orbital wording required). SMILES valid, index 0 correct."},{"q":"1,5-Hexadiene (shown) is heated to 200 degrees C, conditions for a [3,3]-sigmatropic Cope rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCCC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCC=C","CC=CC=CC","C=CCC=CC","C=CCCC=CC","C=CC=CCC"],"correct":0,"why":"A Cope rearrangement is a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: in a 1,5-diene the C3-C4 sigma bond breaks, a new sigma bond forms between C1 and C6, and both pi bonds move one position toward the middle of the chain. For the unsubstituted parent, 1,5-hexadiene, the product has exactly the same connectivity as the starting material; the rearrangement is degenerate, so heating simply returns 1,5-hexadiene. The conjugated 1,3- and 2,4-hexadienes and the skipped 1,4-hexadiene would require hydrogen shifts that a [3,3] process does not perform, and the seven-carbon diene has the wrong formula.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, chemistry correct, one defensible answer (degenerate Cope returns the substrate). Cope is borderline DAT scope but appears in Booster at recognition level; the 'answer equals the drawn substrate' makes it slightly gimmicky. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"Allyl phenyl ether (shown) is heated to 200 degrees C, the classic aromatic Claisen ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCc1cccc(O)c1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1ccccc1O","CC=Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"Heating an allyl aryl ether triggers the aromatic Claisen rearrangement, a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: the C-O bond to the allyl group breaks while the terminal allyl carbon bonds to the ring carbon ortho to oxygen. The resulting cyclohexadienone cannot stay non-aromatic, so it tautomerizes, and the product is 2-allylphenol with the allyl group still terminal (CH2-CH=CH2). The para and meta allylphenols would need a different migration path, 2-(prop-1-enyl)phenol has a conjugated chain that would require a separate double-bond isomerization, and phenol has lost the three-carbon chain entirely.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (o-allylphenol), exactly one defensible answer, plausible regio/loss distractors. Canonical textbook Claisen at recognition depth. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether (shown) is heated, the conditions for an aliphatic Claisen ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCC1","C=CCCCO","CCC=CC=O","C=CCCC=O","CC=CCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Allyl vinyl ether is the textbook substrate for the aliphatic Claisen rearrangement, a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a six-membered cyclic transition state. The allyl C-O sigma bond breaks, a new C-C sigma bond forms between the terminal allyl carbon and the terminal vinyl carbon, and the former vinyl ether oxygen becomes a carbonyl, so the product is pent-4-enal, a gamma,delta-unsaturated aldehyde. Pent-2-enal and pent-3-enal put the alkene in the wrong place, pent-4-en-1-ol keeps an alcohol the mechanism never generates, and cyclopentanone has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (pent-4-enal), one defensible answer, good distractors. Canonical aliphatic Claisen at recognition depth. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"3-Methyl-1,5-hexadiene (shown) is heated to undergo a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCC=CC","C=CC(C)CC=C","CC=CCCC=CC","C=C(C)CCC=C","C=CCCCC=C"],"correct":0,"why":"In a Cope rearrangement the C3-C4 sigma bond of the 1,5-diene breaks, a new sigma bond forms between C1 and C6, and the two pi bonds shift to C2=C3 and C4=C5. Carry the methyl along: it sits on C3, which becomes an alkene carbon in the product, so the product ends in a CH3-CH=CH unit and is hepta-1,5-diene (CH2=CH-CH2-CH2-CH=CH-CH3). This is also the downhill direction, because a monosubstituted alkene has been traded for a more stable disubstituted one. The unchanged diene, 2-methyl-1,5-hexadiene, and 1,6-heptadiene are isomers with the wrong connectivity, and the octadiene has an extra carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid SMILES, correct product (hepta-1,5-diene) confirmed unique. One defensible answer. Substituted-Cope regiochemistry is on the hard edge of DAT scope but still recognition-level; difficulty is really 2-3, not 2 as the only concern. Keep.","group":"misc"},{"q":"The compound shown (allyl vinyl ether) is heated. Which reaction type best describes its conversion to 4-pentenal (an unsaturated aldehyde)?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts":["Nucleophilic acyl substitution","[1,2]-sigmatropic shift","[3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement (Claisen)","Diels-Alder cycloaddition","Electrocyclic ring closure"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Allyl vinyl ether (C5H8O) undergoes the Claisen rearrangement, a concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic shift, on heating to give 4-pentenal, a gamma,delta-unsaturated aldehyde. The C-O sigma bond migrates across the two allyl-type fragments.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid, Claisen correctly keyed at index 0, distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope (named reaction recognition).","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The molecule drawn is allyl phenyl ether. When heated (no catalyst), it rearranges to o-allylphenol. This transformation is an example of which named reaction?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["Hofmann elimination","Aromatic Claisen rearrangement","Cannizzaro reaction","Cope rearrangement","Fries rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Heating an allyl aryl ether triggers the aromatic Claisen rearrangement: a [3,3]-sigmatropic shift delivers the allyl group to the ortho carbon, then tautomerization restores aromaticity to give o-allylphenol. Fries is a Lewis-acid acyl migration of esters, not this thermal pericyclic process.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C9H10O allyl phenyl ether), aromatic Claisen correctly keyed, distractors (Fries/Cope/Hofmann/Cannizzaro) all plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1,5-Hexadiene (shown) interconverts with an identical molecule when heated to roughly 200 C with no reagents. This degenerate rearrangement is classified as a:","q_smiles":"C=CCCC=C","opts":["Conrotatory electrocyclic ring opening","Cope rearrangement ([3,3]-sigmatropic)","1,3-dipolar cycloaddition","[1,5]-hydride shift","Claisen rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A 1,5-diene undergoing a thermal [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement is the Cope rearrangement; for parent 1,5-hexadiene the product is identical (degenerate). A Claisen requires oxygen (allyl vinyl ether), which this all-carbon system lacks.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C6H10 1,5-hexadiene), Cope correctly keyed, distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"What single reagent/condition is required to convert allyl vinyl ether (shown) into 4-pentenal?","q_smiles":"C=CCOC=C","opts":["Simply heat (thermal, no added reagent)","Aqueous NaOH, then H3O+","O3, then Zn/H2O","H2 / Pd-C","mCPBA"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The Claisen rearrangement is a thermal, concerted pericyclic reaction needing only heat; no acid, base, oxidant, or catalyst. Ozonolysis, hydrogenation, and epoxidation would destroy the alkene rather than perform the [3,3] shift.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"None. SMILES valid, reagent-selection item correctly keyed (heat only), distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The conjugated triene shown is heated. It cyclizes to a cyclohexadiene by forming one new C-C sigma bond between the two terminal carbons. This bond-forming process is best classified as a:","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C\\C=C/C","opts":["[3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement","Retro-Diels-Alder reaction","SN2 substitution","[1,5]-sigmatropic shift","Electrocyclic ring closure"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"This octa-2,4,6-triene is a fully conjugated 1,3,5-triene. When the ends of the conjugated pi system join to form a ring with one new sigma bond (and one fewer pi bond), the reaction is electrocyclic, closing to a dimethyl-1,3-cyclohexadiene. Sigmatropic shifts move an existing sigma bond rather than create a ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: stem says 'two terminal carbons' but the reacting carbons are the conjugated termini (C2/C7); the methyls hang off the ends. Loose wording, not load-bearing; SMILES valid (C8H12 conjugated triene), electrocyclic correctly keyed.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclobutene (shown) is heated and ring-opens to 1,3-butadiene. Which statement correctly identifies this reaction and its product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC1","opts":["[1,3]-sigmatropic shift giving cyclopropene","Aldol condensation giving an enal","Cope rearrangement giving 1,3-butadiene","Diels-Alder retro giving two alkenes","Electrocyclic ring opening giving 1,3-butadiene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Breaking the cyclobutene C-C sigma bond to generate the conjugated diene is an electrocyclic ring opening (reverse of butadiene electrocyclization): one sigma bond becomes one pi bond. No oxygen or 1,5-diene array, so not Cope/Claisen.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: difficulty marked 2 but really single-concept recognition. SMILES valid (C4H6 cyclobutene), electrocyclic ring opening correctly keyed, distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The diene shown is (Z)-1,3-pentadiene. On heating it undergoes a thermal [1,5] shift in which a hydrogen migrates along the pi framework. What migrates and what is the reaction class?","q_smiles":"CC=CC=C","opts":["A hydride adds; nucleophilic addition","A hydrogen atom migrates; [1,5]-sigmatropic shift","A proton is removed; E2 elimination","A hydrogen migrates; electrocyclic closure","A methyl group migrates; [3,3]-sigmatropic shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"1,3-Pentadiene undergoes a thermal [1,5]-hydrogen sigmatropic shift: a terminal/allylic H migrates across five atoms of the pi system to a new terminus, regenerating a 1,3-diene. It is concerted and suprafacial; no ring forms (not electrocyclic) and no 1,5-diene Cope array is present.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Minor: stem labels it '(Z)-1,3-pentadiene' but the SMILES CC=CC=C carries no stereochemistry. The Z/E descriptor is not load-bearing for the [1,5]-H answer, so the item still has exactly one defensible answer. SMILES valid (C5H8).","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The substrate shown is 1,5-hexadien-3-ol. Heating its alkoxide (anionic oxy-Cope) gives, after tautomerization, an unsaturated carbonyl compound. Which reaction class and product feature apply?","q_smiles":"C=CCC(O)C=C","opts":["[3,3]-sigmatropic (oxy-Cope) giving a delta,epsilon-unsaturated aldehyde/ketone (5-hexenal)","[1,5]-H shift giving a fully conjugated dienol","Retro-aldol giving two carbonyl fragments","Electrocyclic closure giving a cyclohexenol","Claisen rearrangement giving an allyl phenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"This 1,5-diene with OH at C3 is the oxy-Cope substrate. The [3,3]-sigmatropic Cope shift forms a C1-C6 bond, breaks C3-C4, and generates an enol that tautomerizes to 5-hexenal (OHC-CH2-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2), confirmed isomeric to the substrate (C6H10O). The OH-bearing terminus drives the irreversible enol-to-carbonyl step. No ring forms and no aromatic ether is present, ruling out electrocyclic and Claisen.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","issue":"Keyed answer is correct: oxy-Cope to 5-hexenal, correctly named delta,epsilon-unsaturated in option 0. Minor internal inconsistency: the original 'why' text said 'gamma,delta' while the option (and the actual product) is delta,epsilon; corrected here. Borderline scope (anionic oxy-Cope, diff 3) but stays within named-reaction recognition depth. SMILES valid.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether (drawn) is heated, promoting a [3,3]-sigmatropic (Claisen) rearrangement. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["O=C1CCCC1","O=CCC=CC","O=CCCC=C","OCCCC=C","O=CCCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The aliphatic Claisen converts an allyl vinyl ether to a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl. The C-O sigma bond breaks and a new C-C sigma bond forms between the terminal carbons of the two pi systems, giving 4-pentenal (terminal aldehyde + terminal alkene). The alcohol, the conjugated 3-pentenal, the saturated pentanal, and cyclopentanone all have wrong functionality or skeleton.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: pent-4-enal (opt 0) is the correct Claisen product. In scope, valid, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Allyl phenyl ether (drawn) is heated to roughly 200 C, triggering an aromatic Claisen rearrangement. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["Oc1ccccc1CC=C","Oc1ccc(CC=C)cc1","Oc1ccccc1C=CC","Oc1ccccc1","Oc1cccc(CC=C)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The aromatic Claisen rearrangement is a concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a six-membered transition state: the allyl C-O bond breaks as the terminal allyl carbon bonds to the ring carbon ortho to oxygen. Tautomerization of the dienone restores aromaticity, giving 2-allylphenol. Because the new bond forms at the CH2 end of the allyl group, the double bond stays terminal. The para and meta allylphenols have the wrong regiochemistry, 2-(prop-1-enyl)phenol has an internal conjugated double bond the rearrangement does not make, and phenol has lost the carbon chain.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: ortho 2-allylphenol with terminal alkene (opt 0) correct. In scope, valid. Keep."},{"q":"3-Phenyl-1,5-hexadiene (drawn) is heated, allowing a Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement to reach the more stable isomer. Which structure is that product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(c1ccccc1)CC=C","opts":["C=Cc1ccccc1CCC=C","C1=CC=CC(c2ccccc2)C1","C=CCCC=Cc1ccccc1","C=CC(c1ccccc1)C=CC","C=CCC(C=C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Treat the Cope as a [3,3] shift on the 1,5-diene: the C3-C4 sigma bond breaks, C1 and C6 bond to each other, and the pi bonds move to C2=C3 and C4=C5. The phenyl on C3 therefore ends up on an alkene carbon, so the product is 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene, a styrene-type compound whose new double bond is conjugated with the ring. That conjugation makes the product more stable than the starting diene, which is why the equilibrium settles on this side. The benzene with two chains has the wrong connectivity, the phenylcyclohexadiene has the wrong skeleton, 3-phenylhexa-1,4-diene has its double bonds in the wrong places, and the remaining structure is just the starting material drawn differently.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 1-phenylhexa-1,5-diene (opt 0) is the correct, thermodynamically favored Cope product (styrene conjugation). Hard but recognition-level regiochemistry; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclobutene (drawn) is heated and opens by an electrocyclic ring-opening reaction. Which statement best describes this process and the curved arrows that represent it?","q_smiles":"C1=CCC1","opts":["The ring C-C sigma bond breaks and the electrons reorganize through a cyclic transition state to give 1,3-butadiene (a 4 pi-electron electrocyclic ring opening)","A [3,3]-sigmatropic shift relocates the double bond to give methylenecyclopropane","The pi bond is reduced and the ring is retained, giving cyclobutane","Two sigma bonds break and a triple bond forms, giving 2-butyne","A hydrogen migrates across the ring in a [1,3]-sigmatropic shift to give 1-butene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Electrocyclic ring opening of cyclobutene is the microscopic reverse of butadiene ring closure: the ring sigma bond breaks and electrons redistribute through a single cyclic transition state (no discrete intermediate, no migrating atom) to give conjugated 1,3-butadiene; the open form involves 4 pi electrons. It is not a sigmatropic H-shift, a reduction, or an alkyne-forming process.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: opt 0 correct; distractors clearly wrong. Identifies one process/arrow set, no full mechanism. In scope, valid. Keep."},{"q":"1,5-Hexadien-3-ol (drawn) is heated, undergoing an oxy-Cope ([3,3]-sigmatropic) rearrangement; the initial enol then tautomerizes. Which structure is the final carbonyl product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(O)CC=C","opts":["O=CCCC=CC","O=C1CCCCC1","OCCCCC=C","O=CCCCC=C","O=CCCC=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"An oxy-Cope is a Cope rearrangement of a 1,5-diene carrying a hydroxyl on C3. After the [3,3] shift the hydroxyl-bearing carbon has become an alkene carbon, so the immediate product is an enol, and the enol tautomerizes to the carbonyl. Here the enol carbon is a CH, so tautomerization gives an aldehyde: hex-5-enal, six carbons with a terminal alkene. Pent-4-enal has one carbon too few, hex-4-enal has the double bond in the wrong place, hex-5-en-1-ol never tautomerized and has the wrong oxidation state, and cyclohexanone would need a ring that no Cope step builds.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: hex-5-enal (opt 0) is the correct final carbonyl after oxy-Cope + tautomerization. In scope, valid, one defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) in CCl4 with a trace of a radical initiator (heat/light). What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["BrC1CCC=CC1","BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCC=C1","BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NBS at low Br2 concentration favors allylic radical substitution over addition. Br is installed at the sp3 carbon adjacent to C=C (allylic), giving 3-bromocyclohexene with the alkene retained. opt[0] (BrC1CCCC=C1 = 3-bromocyclohexene) is correct: opt[1] is vinylic, opt[2] is the 1,2-dibromide addition product, opt[3] is homoallylic (4-bromo), opt[4] is saturated bromocyclohexane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid (RDKit). Keyed allylic product is correct; distractors are vinylic/addition/homoallylic/saturated. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene is heated with Br2 in the presence of light (hv). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["BrC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccc(Br)cc1","Cc1ccccc1Br","Cc1cccc(Br)c1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Br2/hv is free-radical halogenation, selective for the weak benzylic C-H (resonance-stabilized benzylic radical). One equivalent gives benzyl bromide (opt[0]). opt[1]/opt[2]/opt[5] are EAS ring-bromination products (wrong mechanism), opt[3] is the dibromide (over-bromination).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed benzylic-bromination product correct; one defensible answer. Mechanism-discrimination (radical vs EAS) is exactly DAT depth."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv). What is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1","BrCCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. The benzylic C-H gives a resonance-stabilized benzylic radical, so Br goes there: (1-bromoethyl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates the terminal primary non-benzylic carbon, opt[2]/opt[4] are ring (EAS) products, opt[3] is a geminal dibromide (over-bromination).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed benzylic product correct; single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Propene is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts":["CCCBr","C=CCBr","CCCO","CC(Br)C","CC(Br)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"HBr/peroxides is a radical chain (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to the less substituted carbon (forms the more stable secondary radical), so Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromopropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the Markovnikov product (ionic HBr), opt[2] is the Br2 dibromide, opt[3] is allyl bromide (allylic substitution), opt[4] is an alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Anti-Markovnikov keyed answer correct; distractors cover Markovnikov/dibromide/allyl/alcohol. One defensible answer."},{"q":"2-Methylpropane (isobutane) is treated with Br2 and light (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)(Br)CBr","CC(C)(Br)C(Br)C","CC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Radical bromination is highly selective for the most stable radical. The single tertiary C-H gives a 3 radical far more stable than the nine primary positions, so Br adds at the tertiary carbon: 2-bromo-2-methylpropane (opt[0]). opt[1] is the primary product (minor, since Br2 is strongly selective), opt[2] is a dibromide, opt[3] has a rearranged/wrong C4 skeleton (n-butyl), opt[4] is a C5 dibromide (wrong formula).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Selectivity-of-Br2 keyed answer correct. Note opt[4] (CC(C)(Br)C(Br)C) is C5H10Br2 - a clearly impossible product (extra carbon), so it is an obviously-wrong distractor rather than a near-miss, but still parseable and not the keyed answer; question integrity intact."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv). Considering allylic selectivity, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["BrCC1=CCCCC1","CC1=C(Br)CCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1Br","CC1=CC(Br)CCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NBS gives allylic bromination via a resonance-stabilized allylic radical; the secondary allylic CH2 is favored over the primary allylic methyl. The conventionally cited major product is 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene (opt[0]), retaining the trisubstituted alkene. opt[1] brominates the primary allylic methyl (minor), opt[2] is vinylic, opt[3] is the C6 allylic regiochemistry (a competing secondary-allylic site but the non-favored regiochemistry), opt[4] is the 1,2-dibromide addition product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Mild defensibility concern: opt[3] (6-bromo) is also a secondary allylic position, so opt[0] vs opt[3] is the only soft spot. The stem ('considering allylic selectivity') and the standard textbook answer (3-bromo, alkene retained as trisubstituted) make opt[0] the single defensible major product. Kept; flag the C3-vs-C6 nuance."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr and organic peroxides (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)C(Br)C","CC(C)CCBr","CC(C)C(Br)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"HBr/peroxides is radical (anti-Markovnikov) addition. Br radical adds to terminal CH2 to give the more stable secondary radical; Br ends on the terminal carbon: 1-bromo-3-methylbutane (opt[0]). Radical additions do NOT rearrange, so no hydride shift to tertiary occurs. opt[1] is Markovnikov, opt[2] is the rearranged (hydride-shift) tertiary product radicals don't give, opt[3] has wrong skeleton, opt[4] is the dibromide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed anti-Markovnikov, no-rearrangement product correct. The no-rearrangement distractor (opt[2]) is a strong, instructive trap. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Isopropylbenzene (cumene) is treated with NBS and a radical initiator (hv), limited to monobromination. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(C)c1ccc(Br)cc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1Br","CC(C)(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1","BrCC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"NBS/hv abstracts the most stabilized H. Cumene's single benzylic C-H is on the central carbon; the resulting radical is both tertiary AND benzylic (doubly stabilized), far weaker than the primary methyl C-H. Br installs there: (2-bromopropan-2-yl)benzene (opt[0]). opt[1] brominates a primary methyl (non-benzylic, minor), opt[2]/opt[3] are ring (EAS) products, opt[4] is a dibrominated over-reaction.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"All SMILES valid. Keyed doubly-stabilized benzylic/tertiary product correct; single concept (radical stability), visual, in scope. One defensible answer."},{"q":"The alkene below is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) and a trace of a radical initiator (AIBN, light, heat) in CCl4. Which statement BEST describes why these conditions are chosen instead of molecular Br2 in CCl4?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCC=C1","opts":["NBS reacts by an SN2 pathway that requires a polar protic solvent","NBS adds two bromine atoms across the double bond faster than Br2 does","NBS maintains a very low steady-state concentration of Br2, favoring radical allylic substitution over ionic addition across the C=C bond","NBS converts the alkene to a bromohydrin because succinimide releases hydroxide","NBS brominates only the sp2 (vinylic) carbons, never the allylic position"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Molecular bromine in CCl4 adds across a C=C bond by an ionic mechanism; that is the default reaction of an alkene with Br2. NBS sidesteps it by releasing Br2 only in tiny steady-state amounts (from the HBr generated in the chain reacting with the succinimide), so ionic addition, which depends on the Br2 concentration, is suppressed. Meanwhile the radical chain started by the initiator abstracts the weakest C-H, the allylic one, and the allylic radical takes a bromine from Br2, giving substitution with the double bond preserved. NBS does not add two bromines, does not supply hydroxide, does not work by SN2, and never prefers the strong vinylic C-H over the allylic one.","difficulty":2,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None substantive. Substrate is 3-methylcyclohexene (methyl on sp3 allylic carbon), not 1-methyl, but stem does not name it so no factual error; answer correct."},{"q":"Toluene (shown) is treated with Cl2 and UV light (hv). At which type of position does chlorination occur, and what is the name of this reaction?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction occurs because the aromatic ring is unreactive toward Cl2","Benzylic position; nucleophilic substitution (SN2)","Benzylic position; radical (free-radical) halogenation of the methyl C-H","Ring carbon (meta); electrophilic aromatic substitution","Ring carbon (ortho/para); electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Light with Cl2 and no Lewis acid means homolysis of Cl2 and a free-radical chain. The chlorine atom abstracts a benzylic hydrogen because the resulting benzyl radical is delocalized over the ring, which makes that C-H the weakest in toluene; the radical then takes a chlorine from Cl2 to give benzyl chloride. Electrophilic aromatic substitution on the ring needs Cl2 with FeCl3 or AlCl3, and nothing here is an SN2 reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Toluene SMILES valid; answer and reaction-type correct."},{"q":"2-Methylpropane (isobutane, shown) is subjected to free-radical halogenation. Which halogen gives the HIGHER selectivity for replacing the single tertiary (3°) C-H over the primary (1°) C-H?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts":["Both are equally selective because selectivity depends only on the number of H's","Cl2, because the highly reactive Cl atom is more selective for the weaker tertiary bond","Br2, because bromination is faster than chlorination at every position","Br2, because the less reactive Br atom has a later, more product-like transition state and is more selective","Cl2, because chlorination always gives exclusively the tertiary product"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Selectivity in radical halogenation follows the Hammond postulate: the less reactive halogen atom has a later, more product-like transition state for hydrogen abstraction, so differences in radical stability show up strongly in the rates. Bromine atoms are much less reactive than chlorine atoms, so bromination strongly prefers the tertiary C-H (which gives the most stable radical), while chlorine is so reactive that it abstracts primary hydrogens nearly as readily as tertiary ones. Bromination is slower, not faster, than chlorination, and chlorination gives mixtures rather than exclusively the tertiary product.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Isobutane SMILES valid; reactivity-selectivity answer correct."},{"q":"Ethylbenzene (shown) is treated with one equivalent of Br2 under photochemical (hv) radical conditions. The most stable radical intermediate forms at one specific carbon. At which carbon is the H abstracted, and why?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts":["The benzylic CH2 carbon, because that radical is destabilized and therefore reacts fastest","The benzylic CH2 carbon, because the resulting radical is secondary AND resonance-stabilized by the aromatic ring","No single carbon is preferred; bromination is statistically random across all C-H bonds","The terminal CH3 carbon, because primary radicals are the most stable","An ortho ring carbon, because aromatic C-H bonds are the weakest"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Radical bromination goes through the most stable carbon radical. Pulling a hydrogen from the benzylic CH2 gives a radical that is secondary and, more importantly, delocalized by resonance into the aromatic ring, so that C-H is by far the weakest in ethylbenzene and the product is (1-bromoethyl)benzene. Stability, not instability, is what makes a position react fastest; primary radicals are the least stable alkyl radicals, aromatic C-H bonds are among the strongest, and the selectivity is anything but random.","difficulty":2,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Ethylbenzene SMILES valid; benzylic-resonance answer correct and uniquely right reason."},{"q":"Propene (shown) is treated with HBr in the presence of peroxides (ROOR) and light. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C","opts":["CCC","CC(C)Br","CCCBr","OC(C)C","BrCC(Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Peroxides start a radical chain, and in that chain the bromine atom adds to the alkene first. It bonds to the less substituted CH2 end of propene so that the unpaired electron lands on the more substituted (secondary) carbon, the more stable radical; that radical then abstracts a hydrogen from HBr. The net result is anti-Markovnikov addition, and the product is 1-bromopropane. Without peroxides the ionic pathway goes through the secondary carbocation and gives 2-bromopropane instead; propane, 2-propanol, and 1,2-dibromopropane would need entirely different reagents.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All option SMILES valid; anti-Markovnikov peroxide-effect product correctly keyed; Markovnikov distractor present."},{"q":"Rank the following carbon radicals from MOST stable to LEAST stable: (I) tert-butyl radical (CH3)3C•, (II) allyl radical CH2=CH-CH2•, (III) methyl radical •CH3, (IV) ethyl radical CH3CH2•. (The alkene 1-methylcyclohexene is shown only as a reminder that allylic radicals are resonance-stabilized.)","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["II > I > IV > III","I > II > IV > III","II > IV > I > III","I > IV > II > III","III > IV > I > II"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Resonance beats hyperconjugation. The allyl radical spreads its unpaired electron over two carbons by resonance, which stabilizes it more than any number of alkyl groups could, so it is the most stable of the four. Among the localized alkyl radicals, stability rises with substitution (tertiary > secondary > primary > methyl) because each attached alkyl group adds hyperconjugation. That gives allyl > tert-butyl > ethyl > methyl: the ranking that places the allyl radical first and the methyl radical last.","difficulty":1,"group":"radical-reactions","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Ranking correct; q_smiles is a valid reminder molecule (1-methylcyclohexene), not the reacting species, but a molecule is drawn so the visual requirement is met."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) in CCl4 with a trace of a radical initiator (low [Br2], hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1CCCC=C1","BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"NBS keeps the Br2 concentration very low, so addition across the double bond is suppressed and radical substitution wins. A bromine radical abstracts an allylic hydrogen (the weakest C-H, giving a resonance-stabilized allylic radical), and that radical picks up Br to give 3-bromocyclohexene with the double bond intact. 1,2-Dibromocyclohexane is the Br2 addition product that NBS avoids, bromocyclohexane would need HBr addition, 1-bromocyclohexene puts Br on a vinylic carbon whose C-H is too strong to abstract, and cyclohexanone is an oxidation product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Rewritten 2026-09-30: the keyed structure was 1-bromocyclohexene (vinylic); key now 3-bromocyclohexene, which was missing from the options."},{"q":"Propylbenzene (drawn) reacts with Br2 and light (hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CCCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCc1ccccc1Br","BrCCCc1ccccc1","CCCc1ccc(Br)cc1","CCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Br2 with light is radical halogenation, so selectivity is set by radical stability. The most stable radical in propylbenzene is the benzylic one, delocalized into the aromatic ring, so the bromine atom abstracts a hydrogen from the CH2 attached to the ring and the product is (1-bromopropyl)benzene. The 2-bromo and 3-bromo isomers come from non-benzylic, less stable radicals, and the ortho and para ring bromides need electrophilic aromatic substitution with FeBr3, not light.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = benzylic bromide). All SMILES valid; distractors cover internal, terminal, para, and ortho positions. Single benzylic-selectivity concept, in scope."},{"q":"Propene (CH2=CH-CH3, drawn) loses an allylic hydrogen to form a carbon radical. Which statement best explains why the allylic radical is unusually stable compared with a typical primary radical?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The unpaired electron is delocalized by resonance over two carbons (the radical is shared between C1 and C3).","The radical carbon becomes sp hybridized, relieving angle strain.","Hyperconjugation alone makes it more stable than any tertiary radical.","An intramolecular hydrogen bond locks the radical in place.","The radical is stabilized by an inductive electron-withdrawing effect from the methyl group."],"correct":0,"why":"The allyl radical is next to a C=C bond, so the unpaired electron is delocalized by resonance: two equivalent resonance structures place it on either terminal carbon, and the spin is shared between C1 and C3. That delocalization is what makes it far more stable than an ordinary primary radical. The radical carbon stays sp2, hyperconjugation alone does not make it more stable than a tertiary radical, there is no hydrogen bond, and a methyl group is weakly electron-donating, not withdrawing.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = resonance delocalization). q_smiles valid; text options with exactly one defensible answer. Conceptual recognition item, in scope."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (drawn) is treated with NBS (radical, allylic conditions). Among the allylic positions, the major product comes from abstraction at the secondary ring allylic carbon. Which structure is that major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCC1=CCCCC1","CC1=C(Br)CCCC1","CC1=CC(Br)CCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"NBS under radical conditions abstracts an allylic hydrogen. 1-Methylcyclohexene has allylic hydrogens on the methyl and on the ring CH2 groups flanking the double bond; the stem directs you to the secondary ring carbon next to the unsubstituted alkene CH, whose radical is delocalized onto the tertiary methyl-bearing carbon and is the most stable of the set. Trapping that radical at the ring carbon keeps the double bond in place and gives 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene. The bromomethyl compound comes from the primary allylic position, the 2-bromo compound is a vinyl bromide, and the two saturated bromides have lost the C=C, which allylic bromination never touches.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = 3-bromo-1-methylcyclohexene). Stem pre-specifies the secondary ring allylic site, removing ambiguity between the two allylic radicals. All SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn) can react with Cl2/hv or with Br2/hv at the benzylic position. Compared with chlorination, why is radical benzylic BROMINATION far more selective (cleaner) for the benzylic C-H?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Bromination is ionic while chlorination is radical, so only bromination is selective.","Bromine abstracts hydrogen faster, leaving no time for side reactions.","The bromine atom is less reactive, so its transition state is later and more product-like, magnifying the preference for the most stable (benzylic) radical.","Bromine atoms are smaller than chlorine atoms and fit only at the benzylic site.","The benzylic C-Br bond is weaker, which speeds initiation."],"correct":2,"why":"Both halogens react by a radical chain; the difference is in the hydrogen-abstraction step. A bromine atom is much less reactive than a chlorine atom, so its transition state comes later along the reaction coordinate and resembles the radical product (Hammond postulate); the large stability advantage of the benzylic radical is therefore felt almost fully in the rate, and bromination picks the benzylic C-H nearly exclusively. Chlorine's early transition state barely senses radical stability, so it is far less selective. Bromination is slower, not faster, atom size has nothing to do with it, and neither reaction is ionic.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Hammond/selectivity contrast (Cl unselective vs Br selective) is core textbook radical-halogenation content, not grad-level; in scope. q_smiles valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Isobutane (2-methylpropane, drawn) is treated with Br2 and light (hv). Which structure is the major monobromination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)CBr","BrCC(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)Br","CCCCBr","CC(C)CBr"],"correct":2,"why":"Bromine atoms are selective: they abstract the hydrogen that gives the most stable radical. Isobutane has nine primary hydrogens and one tertiary hydrogen, and the tertiary radical is so much more stable that Br2 with light gives tert-butyl bromide (2-bromo-2-methylpropane) almost exclusively despite the 9:1 statistical disadvantage. Isobutyl bromide (bromine on a methyl) is a trace product, the dibromides require a second substitution, and 1-bromobutane has a different carbon skeleton that no radical step can create.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"radical-reactions","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0 = tert-butyl bromide). All SMILES valid; distractors are primary, di-bromides, and a wrong skeleton. Single 3o>1o selectivity concept, in scope."},{"q":"4-tert-Butylcyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","opts":["OCCCC(C(C)(C)C)CCO","C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","O=C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1","OC(=O)C1CCC(C(C)(C)C)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Sodium borohydride is a mild hydride source that reduces aldehydes and ketones and leaves most other groups alone. It delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon of 4-tert-butylcyclohexanone, and the methanol solvent protonates the resulting alkoxide, giving the secondary alcohol 4-tert-butylcyclohexanol. It cannot strip the oxygen off entirely to give tert-butylcyclohexane, cannot oxidize anything to a carboxylic acid, and does not open the ring to a diol; the unchanged ketone is what you would see with no reagent at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, valid, single concept (carbonyl reduction)."},{"q":"Pentanoic acid is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCCC","opts":["O=CCCCC","CC(O)CCC","OCCCCC","CCCCC","OC(O)CCCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride is strong enough to reduce a carboxylic acid all the way to the primary alcohol: the first hydride deprotonates the acid, further hydride delivery takes the carbonyl through the aldehyde level without stopping there, and aqueous workup releases 1-pentanol. Pentanal cannot be isolated because an aldehyde is reduced faster than the acid, 2-pentanol would need the OH on the wrong carbon, the hydrate is not a stable product, and no hydride reagent removes the oxygen completely to give pentane.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"1-Butanol is treated with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in dry CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"OCCCC","opts":["OCCCC","OC(=O)CCC","O=CCCC","CC(O)CC","O=C(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC in dry CH2Cl2 is the mild, anhydrous oxidant for alcohols. A primary alcohol is oxidized once, to the aldehyde, and with no water present the aldehyde cannot form the hydrate that further oxidation to the carboxylic acid would require, so 1-butanol stops at butanal. Butanoic acid is what strong aqueous oxidants like Jones reagent or KMnO4 would give, 2-butanone would need the oxygen on a different carbon, 2-butanol is a different alcohol, and 1-butanol itself is just unreacted starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed, in scope, valid, single concept (mild oxidation)."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with O3, then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["O=CCCCCCC=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1O","O=C(O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC12OC1CCCC2"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ozone cleaves the C=C bond of 1-methylcyclohexene completely, and dimethyl sulfide gives a reductive workup that stops at the carbonyl stage. Because the alkene is inside the ring, cleavage opens it into a single chain: the methyl-bearing alkene carbon becomes a methyl ketone and the CH alkene carbon becomes an aldehyde, so the product is 6-oxoheptanal, a seven-carbon keto-aldehyde. The keto acid would need an oxidative workup (H2O2), the diol is a dihydroxylation product, the epoxide is what a peroxyacid would give, and heptanedial lacks the methyl and has two aldehydes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed; reductive vs oxidative workup is the single discriminating concept. In scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"4-Acetylstyrene (1-(4-vinylphenyl)ethan-1-one) is treated with H2 and a Pd/C catalyst (1 atm, room temperature). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","opts":["C=Cc1ccc(C(O)C)cc1","CCc1ccc(C(O)C)cc1","CCC1CCC(C(C)=O)CC1","C=Cc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1","CCc1ccc(C(C)=O)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hydrogen over Pd/C at one atmosphere and room temperature reduces C=C bonds and leaves ketones and aromatic rings alone. The vinyl group of 4-acetylstyrene is therefore hydrogenated to an ethyl group while the aryl ketone is untouched, giving 4-ethylacetophenone. Reducing the ketone to the alcohol would call for a hydride reagent, hydrogenating the benzene ring would need high pressure and heat, and the unchanged starting material has had no reaction at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed chemoselectivity question, in scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA (meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid) in CH2Cl2. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA delivers one oxygen atom to an alkene in a single concerted step, giving an epoxide with both new C-O bonds on the same face. Cyclohexene therefore becomes cyclohexene oxide (7-oxabicyclo[4.1.0]heptane). The trans-1,2-diol would form only if the epoxide were subsequently opened with aqueous acid, the cis-1,2-diol is the OsO4 or cold KMnO4 product, and cyclohexanol and cyclohexanone would need hydration or oxidation chemistry that a peroxyacid does not perform.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correctly keyed to the epoxide; the diol distractors are valid stereoisomers. In scope, single concept (epoxidation stops at epoxide)."},{"q":"Methyl benzoate is treated with excess LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major organic product derived from the acyl portion?","q_smiles":"O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(OC)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride reduces esters completely: hydride adds to the carbonyl, the tetrahedral intermediate expels methoxide, and the resulting benzaldehyde is immediately reduced again, so after aqueous workup the acyl portion ends up as benzyl alcohol while methanol is released from the alkoxy side. Benzaldehyde cannot be isolated with excess LiAlH4, benzoic acid would require hydrolysis rather than reduction, toluene would mean removing the oxygen entirely, and the unchanged ester is only what you would see with no reagent.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Single reactive center, single concept (ester reduction). Stem disambiguates by specifying the acyl portion. In scope, all SMILES valid."},{"q":"The transformation shown converts pentanal into 1-pentanol (CCCCCO). Which reagent best accomplishes this reduction WITHOUT also reducing an ester or carboxylic acid elsewhere in a molecule?","q_smiles":"CCCCC=O","opts":["OsO4, then NaHSO3","LiAlH4 in dry ether","PCC in CH2Cl2","NaBH4 in methanol","O3, then Zn/H2O"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride that reduces aldehydes/ketones to alcohols but is too weak to touch esters or carboxylic acids, so it is the selective choice. LiAlH4 would also reduce those carbonyls. PCC oxidizes, and OsO4/O3 act on alkenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. NaBH4 selective for aldehyde over ester/acid; valid pentanal SMILES; reagent-selection item, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is to be converted to benzyl alcohol. Which reagent is the strong hydride source that would accomplish this and could also reduce a carboxylic acid in the same molecule?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["H2/Pd","PCC","mCPBA","OsO4","LiAlH4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"LiAlH4 is the powerful hydride reducing agent that reduces aldehydes, ketones, esters, and carboxylic acids to alcohols. PCC and mCPBA are oxidants, OsO4 dihydroxylates alkenes, and H2/Pd reduces alkenes/alkynes, not the aromatic aldehyde under standard conditions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. LiAlH4 strong hydride reducing acids; valid benzaldehyde SMILES; distractors are all oxidants/wrong-target. In scope."},{"q":"A chemist wants to oxidize the primary alcohol 1-pentanol (drawn) to the aldehyde pentanal and STOP there, avoiding over-oxidation to the carboxylic acid. Which reagent is correct?","q_smiles":"CCCCCO","opts":["NaBH4","LiAlH4","Hot KMnO4","PCC in CH2Cl2","Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"PCC is an anhydrous Cr(VI) oxidant that stops at the aldehyde for primary alcohols. Aqueous strong oxidants like KMnO4 or Jones reagent carry the primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid. NaBH4 and LiAlH4 are reducing agents.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. PCC stops 1deg alcohol at aldehyde; valid SMILES; canonical contrast vs KMnO4/Jones. In scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (drawn) is treated with a reagent and the product is cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol (a syn diol). Which reagent gives this syn-dihydroxylation?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["mCPBA, then H3O+","H2/Pd","O3, then (CH3)2S","OsO4, then NaHSO3","PCC"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"OsO4 adds two OH groups to the same face of the alkene (syn addition), giving the cis (syn) diol. mCPBA then aqueous workup gives the anti (trans) diol via epoxide opening. Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C, and H2/Pd just reduces it.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. OsO4 = syn diol; valid cyclohexene SMILES; mCPBA/H3O+ anti-diol distractor is the strong plausible foil. In scope."},{"q":"The alkene shown (1-phenylpropene) is converted to an epoxide in a single step. Which reagent performs this epoxidation?","q_smiles":"CC=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["LiAlH4","O3 then Zn","NaBH4","mCPBA","OsO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"mCPBA is a peroxyacid that delivers one oxygen across the C=C to form an epoxide in a concerted, stereospecific (syn) addition. OsO4 gives a diol, O3 cleaves the alkene, and NaBH4/LiAlH4 are reducing agents that do not react with isolated alkenes.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Answer correct (mCPBA). STEM-LABEL BUG: original text 'the alkene shown (2-methyl-2-butene region drawn as cis-2-butene analog)' is internally contradictory and mislabels CC=Cc1ccccc1 (which is 1-phenylpropene / beta-methylstyrene). Reworded stem to match the actual drawn structure; reagent answer is unaffected, so keep=true."},{"q":"3-Heptene (drawn) is treated with O3 followed by reductive workup with (CH3)2S. What is the reaction type and the carbonyl product class?","q_smiles":"CCCC=CCC","opts":["Epoxidation giving one epoxide","Oxidative cleavage giving two aldehydes","Oxidative cleavage giving two carboxylic acids","Syn-dihydroxylation giving one diol","Catalytic hydrogenation giving an alkane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C double bond. With a reductive workup (Zn/H2O or dimethyl sulfide), each carbon of a disubstituted internal alkene becomes an aldehyde. Oxidative workup (H2O2) would instead give carboxylic acids.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. CCCC=CCC is 3-heptene; reductive ozonolysis of an internal disubstituted alkene gives two aldehydes (butanal + propanal). Two-concept (reaction type + workup-dependent product). In scope. The carboxylic-acid option is the right oxidative-workup foil."},{"q":"You need to reduce only the C=C double bond of 4-phenyl-3-buten-2-one to give the saturated ketone, leaving the C=O intact, in one step. Which reagent set is best?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["NaBH4 in methanol","PCC in CH2Cl2","OsO4, then NaHSO3","LiAlH4 in ether","H2 with Pd/C"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Catalytic hydrogenation (H2, Pd/C) selectively reduces the carbon-carbon double bond (and is generally chemoselective for the alkene over an isolated ketone), preserving the C=O. NaBH4 and LiAlH4 reduce the carbonyl to an alcohol; OsO4 and PCC do not selectively give the saturated ketone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. H2/Pd-C reduces the alkene chemoselectively over the ketone (aromatic ring survives mild conditions); NaBH4/LiAlH4 hit the carbonyl. Valid benzalacetone SMILES. Two-concept (chemoselectivity). In scope."},{"q":"Two carbonyls are available for reduction by NaBH4 in methanol: acetophenone (an aryl methyl ketone, drawn) and benzaldehyde. Considering electronic and steric effects on hydride addition, which statement about relative rate is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzaldehyde is reduced faster than acetophenone because the aldehyde carbonyl is less hindered and more electrophilic","Acetophenone is reduced faster because the extra methyl group donates electron density to the carbonyl","Acetophenone is reduced faster because ketones are inherently more electrophilic than aldehydes","Neither reacts because NaBH4 cannot reduce conjugated aryl carbonyls","Both are reduced at identical rates because NaBH4 reacts only with esters"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Aldehydes are reduced faster than ketones by hydride: the aldehyde carbonyl carbon is less sterically hindered and more electrophilic (only one alkyl/aryl group donating electron density), so benzaldehyde reacts faster than the more hindered, more electron-rich acetophenone.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"Correct. Aldehyde > ketone reactivity toward hydride (less steric hindrance, more electrophilic). Valid acetophenone SMILES. Conceptual reasoning item, sophomore-level (no FMO/quantitative); in scope. Distractor 4 (methyl donation) is the right common misconception foil."},{"q":"The molecule shown is 2-bromobutane. Which descriptor correctly assigns the configuration of its single stereocenter?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)CC","opts":["Z","R","E","The center is not a stereocenter (achiral)","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Priorities Br > CH2CH3 > CH3 > H, with H pointing back, trace clockwise = R.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer and rationale correct."},{"q":"Classify the alkene geometry drawn below using CIP (E/Z) nomenclature.","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["S","R","E","Z","Neither E nor Z (no geometric isomerism)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Each sp2 carbon has methyl > H; the two methyls are cis (same side), so Z.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms Z."},{"q":"For the stereocenter in L-alanine shown below, rank the four attached groups from HIGHEST to LOWEST CIP priority.","q_smiles":"N[C@@H](C)C(=O)O","opts":["NH2 > CH3 > COOH > H","CH3 > NH2 > COOH > H","COOH > NH2 > CH3 > H","NH2 > COOH > CH3 > H","COOH > CH3 > NH2 > H"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"First atoms: N(7) beats both C, so NH2 highest. Between carbons, COOH (O,O,O) beats CH3 (H,H,H). H lowest. Order NH2 > COOH > CH3 > H. Question asks only priority order, so exactly one correct answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. SMILES is genuine L-alanine; ranking unambiguous."},{"q":"The alkene shown is (E)-2-pentene. Which single statement explains why this geometry is labeled E?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/C","opts":["There are two identical groups on one alkene carbon, so E/Z does not apply","The two higher-priority groups are on the SAME side of the double bond","The two higher-priority groups (ethyl and methyl) are on OPPOSITE sides of the double bond","Free rotation about the C=C averages the geometry, defaulting to E","The molecule has a stereocenter that is configured R"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Left carbon ethyl > H, right carbon methyl > H, higher groups trans = E.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E."},{"q":"D-glyceraldehyde is drawn below. Assign the CIP configuration of its stereocenter.","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["S","E","R","Meso (internally compensated, no net configuration)","Z"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"OH > CHO(O,O,H) > CH2OH(O,H,H) > H, clockwise with H back = R. Canonical D-glyceraldehyde is (R).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; matches real D-glyceraldehyde."},{"q":"Identify the configuration of the trisubstituted alkene shown (3-methyl-2-pentene). Note that one alkene carbon bears both a methyl and an ethyl group.","q_smiles":"CC/C(C)=C\\C","opts":["S","R","Z","E","No E/Z designation is possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"For E/Z, rank the two groups on each alkene carbon separately by Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority. On the carbon bearing methyl and ethyl, ethyl wins (its first carbon carries another carbon); on the other carbon, methyl beats hydrogen. In the structure as drawn, the ethyl group and the far methyl group lie on the same side of the double bond, so the two higher-priority groups are together and the alkene is Z. R and S are labels for tetrahedral stereocenters, not double bonds, and since each alkene carbon carries two different groups, an E/Z designation certainly exists.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms Z."},{"q":"(E)-cinnamaldehyde is shown. Which statement about its stereochemistry is correct?","q_smiles":"O=C/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["The C=C is Z, and the molecule has no R/S stereocenter","The molecule is achiral because E/Z isomerism does not apply to conjugated alkenes","The C=C is E, and the molecule also has one R stereocenter","The C=C is Z because the phenyl outranks the CHO group","The C=C is E, and the molecule has no R/S stereocenter"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"On each alkene carbon, compare the two groups: CHO outranks H on one carbon and phenyl outranks H on the other. Those two higher-priority groups sit on opposite sides, so the double bond is E. No carbon carries four different groups, so there is no R/S stereocenter; the molecule is achiral but still has E/Z isomerism.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E and no stereocenter."},{"q":"The molecule below is 2-bromo-3-chlorobutane drawn with both stereocenters defined. Assign the (C2, C3) configuration. (Number from the bromine-bearing carbon as C2.)","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Cl)C","opts":["(2S, 3S) — and the molecule is meso","(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3R) — and the molecule is meso","(2R, 3S)","(2R, 3R)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"(2R,3R). It is not meso because the two ends carry different halogens (Br vs Cl), so no internal mirror plane.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms (R,R) and not meso."},{"q":"Assign the R/S configuration of the stereocenter in the molecule shown (2-bromobutane).","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["The molecule is achiral (no stereocenter)","Configuration cannot be assigned without a 3-D model","It is a meso compound","R","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"C2 of 2-bromobutane carries four different groups (Br, ethyl, methyl, H), so it is a stereocenter and the molecule is chiral; with only one stereocenter it cannot be meso. Rank the groups: Br > CH2CH3 > CH3 > H. With the hydrogen pointing away from you in the drawn structure, tracing bromine to ethyl to methyl runs clockwise, so the center is R. No 3-D model is needed; the wedge and dash carry all the information required.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"The aldotetrose shown has two stereocenters. Reading from the aldehyde carbon (C1), what is the configuration at C2 and C3?","q_smiles":"O=C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)CO","opts":["It is a meso compound, so neither center has a fixed label","(2R, 3R)","(2S, 3R)","(2R, 3S)","(2S, 3S)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Treat each stereocenter separately. At C2 the priorities are OH > CHO > C3 (the CHO carbon has two bonds to oxygen, beating C3's one) > H; with H pointing away the sequence runs clockwise, so C2 is R. At C3 the priorities are OH > C2 (bonded to O, C, H) > CH2OH (bonded to O, H, H) > H, and the sequence runs counterclockwise, so C3 is S. The molecule is (2R,3S). It is not meso: the two ends (CHO and CH2OH) are different, so there is no internal mirror plane and both labels are fixed.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"Keyed answer WRONG: RDKit gives C2=R, C3=S = (2R,3S) = index 1, not the keyed index 0 (2S,3R). Why-text inverted both labels. Corrected to 1."},{"q":"Assign the geometry (E or Z) of the carbon-carbon double bond in the alkene shown (a 2-bromo-3-chloropentene). Watch the priority comparisons carefully.","q_smiles":"CC/C(Cl)=C(/C)Br","opts":["The geometry is undefined as drawn","The alkene has no E/Z isomerism","E","Both an E and a Z descriptor apply equally","Z"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Rank the two groups on each alkene carbon independently. On the carbon bearing chlorine and ethyl, Cl outranks the ethyl carbon; on the carbon bearing bromine and methyl, Br outranks the methyl carbon. The two winners, Cl and Br, are on opposite sides of the double bond as drawn, so the alkene is E. Each alkene carbon carries two different substituents, so E/Z isomerism is real and the geometry is fully defined by the drawing; only one descriptor can apply.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"Assign the geometry (E or Z) of the carbon-carbon double bond in 2-methylbut-2-enal, shown below.","q_smiles":"C/C=C(\\C)C=O","opts":["Z","No E/Z isomerism is possible here","The CHO group makes the descriptor undefined","Both descriptors are equally valid","E"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"On the alkene carbon that carries both a methyl and the CHO group, the CHO wins priority: its carbon is bonded to oxygen (counted twice for the C=O), while the methyl carbon is bonded only to hydrogens. On the other alkene carbon, methyl beats hydrogen. In the drawn structure the CHO group and the far methyl group sit on opposite sides of the double bond, so the two higher-priority groups are trans and the alkene is E. The aldehyde does not block the assignment; it simply has to be ranked correctly, and only one descriptor fits.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms E; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"q":"Assign the R/S configuration of the stereocenter in but-3-en-2-ol, shown below. Be careful comparing the vinyl group to the methyl group.","q_smiles":"C=C[C@H](O)C","opts":["Cannot be assigned because of the double bond","R","It is achiral / meso","The center is not a stereocenter","S"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The carbinol carbon of but-3-en-2-ol carries OH, vinyl, methyl, and H, four different groups, so it is a genuine stereocenter and the molecule is chiral (one center, so not meso). Ranking: OH is first; vinyl beats methyl because, with the double bond duplicated, the vinyl carbon is bonded to (C, C, H) while the methyl carbon is bonded to (H, H, H). With hydrogen pointing away in the drawn structure, OH to vinyl to methyl traces clockwise, so the configuration is R. A double bond on a substituent never prevents assignment; it just calls for the duplicated-atom rule.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-rs-ez","issue":"None. RDKit confirms R; keyed answer correct, single-concept, valid, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"cis-2-Butene is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Anti addition occurs across the double bond. Which structure represents the product (one of the two enantiomers formed)?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C(Br)=C(Br)/C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CCC(Br)C","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","C[C@@H](Cl)[C@H](Cl)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Bromine adds to an alkene through a cyclic bromonium ion that bromide opens from the opposite face, so the two bromines end up anti. Anti addition to cis-2-butene gives the chiral pair of 2,3-dibromobutane enantiomers, (2R,3R) and (2S,3S), as a racemate; the answer is the (2R,3R) enantiomer. The meso (2R,3S) diastereomer is the anti-addition product of trans-2-butene, not of the cis alkene. The dichloro compound used the wrong halogen, 2-bromobutane has only one bromine, and 2,3-dibromo-2-butene still has a double bond, which addition removes.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: q_smiles and all 5 options parse; option[0] is the chiral (R,R) diastereomer, option[1] is meso. Anti addition to Z-alkene correctly gives the chiral d,l pair. Keyed answer 0 correct, exactly one defensible answer, in DAT scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"Cyclopentene is treated with Br2. Anti addition across the ring double bond gives a racemic product. Which structure is the major product (one enantiomer shown)?","q_smiles":"C1CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cl[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Cl","BrC1CCCC1","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1Br","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","Br[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds to cyclopentene through a bromonium ion that bromide opens from the opposite face, so the two bromines end up on opposite sides of the ring: trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane, formed as a racemic mixture. The structure asked for is one of those enantiomers, the (1S,2S) trans isomer. The cis-1,2-dibromide is a meso compound that would require syn addition, which bromine does not do; the trans-dichloride used the wrong halogen, bromocyclopentane has only one bromine, and the trans-diol is a dihydroxylation product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is chiral trans-1,2-dibromocyclopentane; option[1] is the cis meso. Anti addition gives trans. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"(R)-2-Bromobutane is treated with NaSH in acetone. Which structure is the product of this SN2 reaction?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=CC","CC[C@@H](S)C","CCCC(C)S","CC[C@@H](O)C","CC[C@H](S)C"],"correct":1,"why":"SN2 displacement happens by backside attack: the hydrosulfide nucleophile approaches opposite the C-Br bond and the stereocenter inverts like an umbrella in the wind. Because SH takes the same top priority that Br held relative to the ethyl and methyl groups, that inversion shows up as a change of label, and (R)-2-bromobutane gives (S)-2-butanethiol. The (R)-thiol would mean retention, which SN2 never gives; (S)-2-butanol uses the wrong nucleophile; 2-butene is the elimination product; and the five-carbon thiol has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified by 3D RDKit backside-attack model: spatial inversion of (R)-2-bromobutane gives the (S) thiol = option[0]. (CIP letter flips R->S because Br is replaced by lower-priority S.) Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"1-Methylcyclohexene reacts with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"HBr without peroxides adds by the ionic Markovnikov pathway: the proton adds to the CH end of the double bond so the cation forms on the carbon that already carries the methyl, a tertiary carbocation, and bromide then captures it. The product is 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane, with bromine on the tertiary carbon. 1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is the anti-Markovnikov product that would need peroxides, 1-methylcyclohexanol would come from water rather than bromide, the dibromide would need Br2, and the seven-membered ring would require a ring expansion that a stable tertiary cation has no reason to undergo.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane (Br on tertiary C). Markovnikov regiochemistry correct. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual. Minor: this item tests regiochemistry, not stereochemistry, so 'stereo-relationships' group label is a slight mismatch, but it does not affect correctness/keep."},{"group":"stereo-relationships","q":"Styrene is treated with one equivalent of mCPBA in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCC(O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","C1OC1c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"A peroxyacid such as mCPBA transfers one oxygen to a C=C bond in a single concerted step, so styrene becomes styrene oxide (2-phenyloxirane); the aromatic ring is untouched. The 1,2-diol would form only if that epoxide were then hydrolyzed, which these conditions do not do. The vicinal dibromide needs Br2, 1-phenylethanol would need hydration, and phenylacetaldehyde would require the epoxide to rearrange under acid, none of which happens here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: option[0] is styrene oxide (epoxide). mCPBA gives the epoxide. Keyed answer 0 correct, one defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"The molecule shown is meso-tartaric acid. A racemic mixture of (R,R)- and (S,S)-tartaric acid can be separated, but this isomer is a single achiral compound. Which statement best explains why the molecule shown is optically INACTIVE?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H]([C@@H](O)C(=O)O)C(=O)O","opts":["Its two stereocenters are both R, making it superimposable on its mirror image","It has no stereocenters at all, so it cannot rotate plane-polarized light","Free rotation about the central C-C bond averages the rotation to zero","It has an internal mirror plane, so the two stereocenters cancel each other's rotation (it is a meso compound)","It exists as a rapidly equilibrating 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers (a racemate)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"It has an internal symmetry element (textbook: mirror plane), so it is optically inactive. It is NOT a racemate (that is a mixture of two compounds), and it DOES have two stereocenters, so distractors 1,2 are false; both R would be the chiral (R,R) form (distractor 3 false).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is meso (superimposable on mirror image). Answer index 0 correct. The internal symmetry element of meso-tartaric acid is strictly a center/plane depending on conformation, but textbook/DAT framing 'internal mirror plane' is standard and accepted."},{"q":"The single enantiomer of 2-bromobutane shown below is dissolved in aqueous ethanol and undergoes solvolysis by an SN1 mechanism. The major substitution product is 2-butanol obtained as a near-50:50 mixture of two forms. What is the stereochemical relationship between those two product molecules?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["Meso and its enantiomer (an internal mirror plane forms)","Identical molecules (SN1 proceeds with complete retention)","Enantiomers (the planar carbocation is attacked from both faces, giving a racemic pair)","Constitutional isomers (the carbon skeleton rearranges)","Diastereomers (one new stereocenter plus the old one give a syn/anti pair)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"SN1 proceeds through a planar, achiral secondary carbocation attacked by water on both faces with roughly equal probability. The 2-butanol product has a single stereocenter, so the two forms (R and S) are nonsuperimposable mirror images = enantiomers (racemic). With only one stereocenter there can be no diastereomers or meso forms.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: 2-bromobutane SMILES valid, single stereocenter. SN1 gives racemic enantiomers. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"Consider the compound drawn below, meso-2,3-dibromobutane. Which single statement correctly describes BOTH its optical activity and its relationship to its own mirror image?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C","opts":["Optically inactive, and identical to (superimposable on) its mirror image","Optically inactive, and a constitutional isomer of its mirror image","Optically active, and identical to its mirror image","Optically inactive, and a diastereomer of its mirror image","Optically active, and an enantiomer of its mirror image"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A meso compound is achiral (optically inactive) and its mirror image is the same molecule (identical/superimposable). This pairs the correct optical activity with the correct mirror-image relationship.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is meso (superimposable on mirror image). Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The molecule shown is 2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal (an aldotetrose) and contains stereocenters at C2 and C3. Using the 2^n rule and ignoring any meso coincidence, what is the MAXIMUM number of stereoisomers possible for a molecule with this number of stereocenters?","q_smiles":"OC[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C=O","opts":["16","4","3","2","8"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The 2^n upper bound is 2^2 = 4. The stem explicitly says to ignore the meso coincidence and use the 2^n rule, so 4 is the maximum.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: aldotetrose SMILES valid, 2 stereocenters. 2^2=4. Answer index 0 correct. Note threose has no meso form so 4 is also the true count; the 'ignore meso' wording keeps it unambiguous."},{"q":"Examine the structure of 3-methylhexane drawn below. Which classification of the molecule itself is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC[C@@H](C)CC","opts":["It is chiral (it has one stereocenter) and is therefore optically active as a single enantiomer","It is achiral because all of its substituents are simple alkyl groups","It cannot be a stereocenter because carbon is bonded only to carbons and hydrogens","It is achiral because it has a plane of symmetry through C3","It is a meso compound with an internal mirror plane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The molecule is chiral and optically active as a single enantiomer. There is no symmetry plane, and it is not meso. Distractor 5 (the common 'all-carbon means no stereocenter' trap) is the well-aimed wrong answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: 3-methylhexane SMILES valid, one stereocenter at C3 (different alkyl chains). Chiral. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"(R)-glyceraldehyde is shown. A chemist wants to convert this single enantiomer into its mirror-image partner, (S)-glyceraldehyde. Which description of (S)-glyceraldehyde relative to the (R)-form shown, and the change required, is correct?","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["(S)-glyceraldehyde is identical; simply rotating the molecule in space converts one to the other","(S)-glyceraldehyde is its diastereomer; changing only one of two stereocenters is required","(S)-glyceraldehyde is a constitutional isomer; the OH and CHO must be relocated","(S)-glyceraldehyde is a conformer; rotation about the C-C bond converts one to the other","(S)-glyceraldehyde is its enantiomer; inverting the configuration at the single stereocenter is required"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Its mirror image, the (S)-form, is its enantiomer, and inverting configuration at that one stereocenter is the only way to interconvert nonsuperimposable mirror images. With one stereocenter there are no diastereomers, and enantiomers are distinct compounds not interconvertible by rotation.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn molecule is (R)-glyceraldehyde, single stereocenter. Mirror image = enantiomer via inversion. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The compound shown is trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane. Considering both its symmetry and its relationship to its mirror image, which classification is correct?","q_smiles":"Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["It is meso; it has an internal mirror plane and is achiral","It is chiral; its mirror image is a non-superimposable enantiomer (the trans isomer is NOT meso)","Its mirror image is a diastereomer because the ring restricts rotation","It is identical to the cis isomer once the ring flips","It is achiral; its mirror image is superimposable by simple rotation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The cis (R,S) isomer is the meso/achiral one (confirmed superimposable on its mirror image). Trans and cis are distinct diastereomeric compounds, so 'identical to cis' is false; the trans is not meso/achiral.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: drawn trans (S,S) is chiral/non-superimposable; cis (R,S) is meso. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"2,3-dichlorobutane is drawn below. This molecule has two stereocenters, but one of its stereoisomers is a meso compound. Taking the meso coincidence into account, how many DISTINCT (different) stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","opts":["6 (2^n counted for both cis and trans)","1 (only the meso form exists)","4 (the full 2^n with no overlap)","2 (a single pair of enantiomers)","3 (a pair of enantiomers plus one meso compound)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Two stereocenters give a 2^n upper bound of 4, but because both stereocenters carry identical substituent sets the (R,S) and (S,R) forms are the same achiral meso compound. That overlap removes one isomer, leaving 3 distinct stereoisomers: the (R,R)/(S,S) enantiomeric pair plus the single meso (R,S). Classic 2^n meso exception.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"stereo-relationships","issue":"Verified: q_smiles is the flat constitution (no stereo tags), which is valid and appropriate for a count-all-stereoisomers question. Constitution has two equivalent stereocenters → 3 distinct stereoisomers. Answer index 0 correct."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is (2S,3S)-butane-2,3-diol. Which structure is its ENANTIOMER?","q_smiles":"C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C","opts":["C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C","OCC(O)CC","CC(O)C(O)C","C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C","C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"An enantiomer is the full mirror image, so every stereocenter must be inverted: (2S,3S)-butane-2,3-diol becomes (2R,3R)-butane-2,3-diol. The trap is the structure with only one center inverted, (2R,3S): because the two halves of the molecule are identical, that diol has an internal mirror plane, so it is the meso diastereomer, not an enantiomer. The (2S,3S) structure is simply the starting molecule again, the drawing with no wedges shows no stereochemistry at all, and butane-1,2-diol is a constitutional isomer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. RDKit confirms opt0 is (2R,3R) true enantiomer; opt1 is the meso trap; one defensible answer; all SMILES valid and in scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"Consider the molecule drawn below, which is meso-2,3-dibromobutane. What is the relationship between this molecule and its own mirror image?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","opts":["They are identical (superimposable) because the molecule has an internal mirror plane","They are diastereomers","They are conformational isomers that cannot interconvert","They are enantiomers because the molecule is chiral","They are constitutional isomers"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"meso-2,3-Dibromobutane has two stereocenters, but they are mirror images of each other within the molecule: the (2R,3S) configuration carries an internal mirror plane through the middle of the C2-C3 bond. Reflecting the whole molecule just swaps the two labels to (2S,3R), which is the same compound, so the molecule and its mirror image are identical and superimposable. That is the definition of achiral, so it cannot be an enantiomer of itself; a compound and its own mirror image are never constitutional isomers or diastereomers, and conformers are not the issue.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit verifies the SMILES is genuinely meso (identical to its mirror image). Clean single-answer text item, in scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"The molecule shown is (R)-1-phenylethanol. Which structure is its ENANTIOMER?","q_smiles":"O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","opts":["O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)C","OCc1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC","O[C@H](c1ccccc1)C","O[C@@H](c1ccccc1)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The only stereocenter in (R)-1-phenylethanol is the benzylic carbon, which carries OH, phenyl, methyl, and H. Its enantiomer simply inverts that one center, so the answer is (S)-1-phenylethanol. The structure with the same (R) configuration is the original compound, benzyl alcohol has no stereocenter at all, and the two 1-phenyl-1-propanol structures have an extra carbon and are therefore constitutional isomers, not stereoisomers.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written. RDKit confirms opt0 is the (S) enantiomer; opt1 duplicates the stem as a trap; one defensible answer; all SMILES valid.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cis-1,3-dichlorocyclohexane. Which single statement BEST classifies this molecule with respect to chirality and optical activity?","q_smiles":"C1C[C@H](Cl)C[C@H](Cl)C1","opts":["It is a meso compound: it has stereocenters but an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and optically inactive","It is chiral and optically active, existing as a pair of enantiomers","It is a constitutional isomer of 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane and is optically active","It exists only as a single non-superimposable mirror image","It has no stereocenters at all, so chirality is not possible"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"cis-1,3-Dichlorocyclohexane has two stereocenters (C1 and C3), but a mirror plane passes through C2 and C5 and reflects one chlorine-bearing carbon onto the other. A molecule with an internal mirror plane is superimposable on its mirror image, so this isomer is meso: achiral and optically inactive despite its stereocenters. It is the trans-1,3 isomer that lacks the plane and exists as a pair of enantiomers. The chlorine-bearing ring carbons are real stereocenters, and 1,4-dichlorocyclohexane is a different compound altogether.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct. RDKit verifies the cis-1,3 SMILES is meso and that trans-1,3 (cited in the rationale) is chiral. Difficulty 3 is at the upper edge but the concept (ring meso recognition) is in DAT scope.","group":"stereo-relationships"},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn) is treated with sodium hydride (NaH), a strong base, which removes the most acidic proton. Which structure is the major product, and why is this deprotonation so favorable?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1=CCCC1","[CH]1C=CC=C1","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1","[CH2-]C=CC=C"],"correct":2,"why":"Removing the sp3 C-H gives the cyclopentadienyl ANION: planar, fully conjugated 5-ring with 6 pi e- (4n+2, n=1), aromatic, which is the driving force for the acidic CH2 (pKa ~16). The cation (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), open pentadienyl anion, radical, and cyclopentene are all wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Correct key, valid distinct SMILES, single concept, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclopropene (drawn) is converted to a cation by treatment with a halogenating reagent followed by AgSbF6 to ionize the C-X bond at the sp3 carbon. Which structure is the major, unusually stable product?","q_smiles":"C1=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[cH+]1cc1","[CH2+]C=C","[CH-]1C=C1","C1=CC1","[CH+]1CC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Ionizing the sp3 C-X gives the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation: 3-ring, 2 pi e- (4n+2, n=0), the smallest aromatic system, unusually stable. The cyclopropenyl anion (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), saturated cyclopropyl cation (C3H5+), ring-opened allyl cation, and neutral cyclopropene are all wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None substantive. Reagent route slightly contrived but defensible; chemistry and key correct.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclooctatetraene (COT, drawn) is treated with excess potassium metal (a strong two-electron reductant). Which structure is the major product, and why does the tub-shaped neutral molecule become planar?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]1C=CC=CC=C[CH]1","c1ccccc1","[CH-]1C=CC=CC=C[CH-]1","C1CCCCCCC1","[CH-]1C=CC=CC=CC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Two-electron reduction gives the COT DIANION (C8H8 2-): adding 2 e- takes the 8 pi-electron tub-shaped nonaromatic neutral to a 10 pi-electron (4n+2, n=2) system that flattens into a planar aromatic ring. The radical anion (9 pi, one unpaired e-) is an intermediate not the excess-K product; benzene and cyclooctane are wrong skeletons; the monoanion is wrong electron count.","difficulty":3,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Standard textbook aromaticity item; correct key, valid SMILES, in DAT recognition scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Pyrrole (drawn) is treated with a strong base that removes its most acidic proton (the N-H). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"c1cc[nH]c1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[c-]1ccc[nH]1","[n-]1cccc1","C1=CC=NC1","c1ccncc1","Cn1cccc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Deprotonating N-H gives the pyrrolide (pyrrolyl) ANION, which remains a planar 6 pi-electron aromatic ring (the p-orbital still holds the lone pair delocalized into the ring). Removing N-H (not a ring C-H) is correct. The C-anion (from a wrong deprotonation), N-methylpyrrole, pyridine, and non-aromatic 2H-pyrrole are wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Minor: 'why' lone-pair wording is loose but conclusion (aromatic 6-pi pyrrolide) and key are correct.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Indene (drawn) is treated with sodium amide (NaNH2), which removes the single acidic sp3 (CH2) proton on the five-membered ring. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1C=Cc2ccccc21","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH+]1C=Cc2ccccc21","[c-]1cccc2c1CC=C2","O=C1CCc2ccccc21","C1CCc2ccccc21","[CH-]1C=Cc2ccccc21"],"correct":4,"why":"Deprotonating the CH2 gives the indenyl anion: the 5-ring now has 6 pi e- (cyclopentadienyl-anion-like, fused to benzene), an aromatic stabilized carbanion, explaining the acidic CH2. The cation is destabilized, the aryl (ring-carbon) anion comes from an unfavorable deprotonation, and indane/indanone are wrong skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Correct key, valid distinct SMILES, single concept, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Furan (drawn) undergoes Friedel-Crafts acylation with acetic anhydride and a Lewis acid catalyst (BF3). Furan is an aromatic heterocycle (O lone pair supplies 2 of the 6 pi electrons), so it reacts like benzene by electrophilic aromatic substitution. Which structure is the major monoacylation product?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CC=CC=O","CC(=O)c1ccoc1","CC(=O)c1ccc(C(C)=O)o1","CC(=O)C1CCCO1","CC(=O)c1ccco1"],"correct":4,"why":"Furan acylates preferentially at the 2-position (alpha to O) because that arenium intermediate is best stabilized; aromaticity is retained. The 3-acyl (beta) product is minor, the saturated tetrahydrofuranyl ketone and ring-opened diketone destroy the ring, and diacylation is not the major mono-substitution.","difficulty":2,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. EAS regioselectivity is standard sophomore content; correct key, valid SMILES, visual, in scope.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclopentadiene (drawn) is deprotonated by potassium tert-butoxide to give its conjugate base. Among the candidate structures below, which one is the aromatic species that actually forms, where the negative charge is fully delocalized over a planar 6 pi-electron ring?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH]1C=CC=C1","[CH2-]C=CC=C","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","C1=CCCC1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"The cyclopentadienyl ANION (6 pi e-, 4n+2) is the aromatic conjugate base; charge delocalized over all five carbons. The cation (4 pi e-, antiaromatic), radical, acyclic pentadienyl anion, and cyclopentene are wrong. HOWEVER this item is essentially a duplicate of Q1: identical substrate (cyclopentadiene), identical five options, identical correct answer; only the stem wording differs.","difficulty":1,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Chemistry fully correct but NEAR-DUPLICATE of Q1 (same substrate, same 5 options, same key). Drop one of Q1/Q8 to avoid redundancy.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cyclopentadiene (a neutral, non-aromatic diene with one sp3 CH2). Which single reagent best converts it into the aromatic 6-pi-electron cyclopentadienyl anion?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts":["BH3 then H2O2/NaOH (hydroboration-oxidation)","HCl gas (protonation to give a carbocation)","Br2 in CCl4 (electrophilic addition across a double bond)","NaH (a strong base that removes the sp3 C-H proton)","H2 with Pd/C (catalytic hydrogenation of the double bonds)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclopentadiene C5H6 (valid, non-aromatic as confirmed) is deprotonated at the sp3 CH2 (pKa ~16) by NaH/n-BuLi to give the planar, fully conjugated cyclopentadienyl anion with 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1), which is aromatic. Br2 addition, hydrogenation, hydroboration, and protonation all destroy conjugation rather than aromatize. Exactly one defensible answer.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C5H6, non-aromatic as intended), correct=0 (NaH), single-concept deprotonation-to-aromatic-anion, in DAT scope, distractors all destroy conjugation."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cycloheptatriene (a neutral ring with one sp3 CH2 and three double bonds). Which reagent abstracts a hydride to generate the aromatic tropylium (cycloheptatrienyl) cation?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=CC=C1","opts":["H2/Pt (catalytic hydrogenation)","LiAlH4 (a strong hydride donor)","NaOH (a Bronsted base)","Ph3C+ BF4- (trityl cation, a hydride abstractor)","NaBH4 (a hydride donor / reducing agent)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cycloheptatriene C7H8 (valid, non-aromatic as drawn). Removing a hydride from the sp3 carbon gives the 7-membered tropylium cation with 6 pi electrons (4n+2, n=1), which is aromatic and unusually stable. Trityl cation Ph3C+ is the classic hydride abstractor that effects this. NaBH4, LiAlH4, H2/Pt are reducers/hydride donors and NaOH is a base, none of which abstract hydride. One defensible answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C7H8), correct=0 (trityl), classic tropylium fact, in scope; distractors are all reducers/base so unambiguous."},{"q":"Pyridine (drawn) and pyrrole (a 5-membered ring with N-H) are both aromatic nitrogen heterocycles, but their nitrogen lone pairs behave very differently. Which statement correctly classifies the lone pair on pyridine's nitrogen and its consequence?","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts":["The lone pair is delocalized like pyrrole's, so pyridine is non-basic","Pyridine is antiaromatic because the nitrogen contributes its lone pair","The lone pair is in the p orbital and is part of the aromatic sextet, making pyridine a very weak base","Pyridine is non-aromatic because nitrogen disrupts the ring","The lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane, not part of the aromatic sextet, so pyridine is a good base/nucleophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Pyridine's nitrogen is sp2 hybridized and is part of a C=N bond within the ring, so it contributes one electron from its p orbital to the aromatic sextet, exactly like a ring carbon. Its lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital in the plane of the ring, pointing outward, so it is not part of the six pi electrons and is free to accept a proton or attack an electrophile; pyridine is a reasonable base and nucleophile. In pyrrole the N-H nitrogen's lone pair is in the p orbital and is needed to complete the sextet, which is why pyrrole is a very weak base. Pyridine has six pi electrons, so it is aromatic, not antiaromatic or non-aromatic.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (pyridine, aromatic), correct=0, canonical pyridine-vs-pyrrole basicity concept, in scope, distractors are standard misconceptions."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is cyclooctatetraene (COT). Although it has 8 pi electrons, it is NOT antiaromatic. Which statement best explains its behavior and reactivity?","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts":["It is aromatic with 8 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=1.5)","It is aromatic because 8 is an even number of pi electrons","It is antiaromatic and undergoes no addition reactions","It adopts a non-planar tub shape and reacts as an ordinary set of isolated alkenes (non-aromatic)","It is antiaromatic and so reacts faster than benzene in electrophilic aromatic substitution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclooctatetraene has eight pi electrons, a 4n count, so a planar, fully conjugated ring would be antiaromatic and strongly destabilized. The molecule avoids that fate by twisting into a tub shape in which adjacent double bonds are not coplanar; cyclic conjugation is broken, the ring is non-aromatic, and the four double bonds behave like ordinary isolated alkenes, adding Br2 and hydrogenating readily. Huckel's rule requires an integer n, so n = 1.5 is meaningless, an even electron count is not the criterion, and a non-aromatic polyene does not do electrophilic aromatic substitution.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C8H8, RDKit confirms non-aromatic), correct=0 (tub/non-aromatic), standard COT teaching point, in scope, one defensible answer."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is furan, a 5-membered oxygen heterocycle. How many of oxygen's two lone pairs are counted toward the aromatic pi-electron sextet, and is furan aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts":["Both lone pairs are in the pi system; furan is aromatic with 4 pi electrons","One lone pair counts, giving 4 pi electrons; furan is antiaromatic","One lone pair is in a p orbital (counts toward the 6 pi electrons); furan is aromatic","Neither lone pair is in the pi system; furan is non-aromatic","Both lone pairs are in the pi system, giving 8 pi electrons; furan is antiaromatic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Furan's two ring double bonds supply four pi electrons. Oxygen is sp2 hybridized: one of its lone pairs sits in a p orbital that overlaps with the ring pi system and contributes two more electrons, for a total of six (4n+2 with n = 1), so furan is aromatic. The second lone pair occupies an sp2 orbital in the plane of the ring and is not counted. Putting both lone pairs into the pi system would give eight electrons, and counting neither would leave only the four of an ordinary diene; exactly one lone pair participates.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (furan, aromatic), correct=0, single concept (heteroatom lone-pair counting), in scope, distractors give wrong electron counts."},{"q":"Two structures are relevant: benzene (drawn) and cyclobutadiene. Both are fully conjugated carbocyclic rings, yet they could not be more different in stability. Which classification is correct?","q_smiles":"c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzene (6 pi e-) is aromatic and stable; cyclobutadiene (4 pi e-) is antiaromatic and highly reactive","Both are antiaromatic because both have even pi-electron counts","Benzene is antiaromatic and cyclobutadiene is aromatic","Both are aromatic because both are cyclic and conjugated","Benzene is aromatic and cyclobutadiene is non-aromatic with no special instability"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Benzene is planar, cyclic, fully conjugated, and holds six pi electrons (4n+2 with n = 1), so it is aromatic and unusually stable. Cyclobutadiene is also cyclic and conjugated but holds four pi electrons (4n with n = 1), which makes it antiaromatic: cyclic delocalization actually raises its energy, and it is so reactive that it dimerizes as soon as it forms. An even electron count is not the test; the 4n+2 versus 4n distinction is what separates stabilization from destabilization.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (benzene, aromatic), correct=0, foundational aromatic/antiaromatic contrast, in scope, distractors are standard wrong classifications."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is imidazole, a 5-membered ring with two nitrogens (one =N- pyridine-type and one N-H pyrrole-type). Which statement correctly accounts for its 6 aromatic pi electrons?","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts":["Neither nitrogen contributes a lone pair; imidazole has only 4 pi electrons","Imidazole is non-aromatic because it has two different nitrogens","Both nitrogen lone pairs enter the pi system, giving 8 pi electrons (antiaromatic)","The two ring double bonds give 4 pi e- and the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 pi e-) for 6 total; the =N- lone pair stays in-plane and is basic","The =N- nitrogen donates its lone pair to the ring and the N-H lone pair is basic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Imidazole's two ring double bonds provide four pi electrons. The N-H (pyrrole-type) nitrogen holds its lone pair in a p orbital and donates it to the ring, bringing the count to six (4n+2), so imidazole is aromatic. The =N- (pyridine-type) nitrogen already uses its p orbital for the C=N bond, so its lone pair stays in an in-plane sp2 orbital, where it is available to accept a proton; that nitrogen is the basic site. Swapping the two roles is the classic trap; counting both lone pairs would give eight electrons, and counting neither would leave only four.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (imidazole as drawn), correct=0, two-nitrogen bookkeeping (regio of lone-pair donation) fits difficulty 3, in scope, distractor option 3 is plausible-but-wrong."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is the cyclopropenyl (cyclopropenium) cation. Apply Huckel's rule: count the pi electrons and classify the ring.","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=C1","opts":["6 pi electrons; aromatic","4 pi electrons (4n with n=1); antiaromatic","2 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=0); aromatic","0 pi electrons; non-aromatic","2 pi electrons; antiaromatic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The cyclopropenyl cation has one C=C bond, which contributes two pi electrons, and a cationic carbon whose empty p orbital completes the cyclic array of three p orbitals. Two pi electrons satisfy Huckel's rule with n = 0 (4n+2 = 2), so the ring is aromatic; that is why this tiny, strained cation is remarkably stable. Four or six electrons are miscounts, zero electrons would mean no pi bond at all, and a 4n+2 count cannot be antiaromatic.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. SMILES valid (C3H3+ cation, RDKit confirms aromatic), correct=0, Huckel-count classification, in scope, electron-count distractors all internally wrong."},{"q":"Five C5 (or related) ring species are shown below. Cyclopentadiene readily loses a proton from its sp3 CH2 to give the species drawn. Which one of the five structures is AROMATIC?","q_smiles":"[CH-]1C=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]1CCCC1","[CH+]1C=CC=C1","[CH-]1C=CC=C1","C1C=CC=C1","[CH]1C=CC=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"Removing a proton from the CH2 of cyclopentadiene gives the cyclopentadienyl anion, in which the former sp3 carbon becomes sp2 and its lone pair occupies a p orbital. Two C=C bonds (four electrons) plus that lone pair (two electrons) give six pi electrons in a planar, fully conjugated five-membered ring: aromatic, which is why cyclopentadiene is unusually acidic for a hydrocarbon. Neutral cyclopentadiene has an sp3 CH2 that interrupts conjugation, the cyclopentadienyl cation has four pi electrons and is antiaromatic, the radical has five, and the saturated cyclopentyl anion has no pi system at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. correct=0 confirmed by RDKit (only the anion is aromatic). All 5 SMILES valid and distinct by charge/saturation. Single Huckel-count concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Consider pyridine, drawn below. When counting pi electrons for the Huckel 4n+2 rule, how is the nitrogen lone pair treated, and is pyridine aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The N lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and is NOT counted; pyridine has 6 pi electrons and is aromatic","The N lone pair is in a p orbital but not counted; pyridine has 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","Pyridine is nonaromatic because the nitrogen disrupts the ring conjugation","The N lone pair occupies the p orbital and IS counted; pyridine has 8 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","The N lone pair is counted, giving 6 pi electrons; pyridine is aromatic"],"correct":0,"why":"Pyridine's nitrogen is part of a ring C=N bond, so its p orbital is already committed to the pi system with one electron, just like each ring carbon. The remaining lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital in the plane of the ring and is not counted. The three ring double bonds supply six pi electrons (4n+2), so pyridine is aromatic. Counting the lone pair as well would wrongly give eight electrons, and the statement that counts the lone pair yet still arrives at six and 'aromatic' gets the conclusion right for the wrong reason: the lone pair never enters the pi system.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"Minor: opt0 and opt2 share the 'aromatic, 6 pi e-' conclusion; discriminator is lone-pair treatment, which the stem explicitly asks about, so still exactly one correct. Pyridine valid+aromatic in RDKit. Keep."},{"q":"1,3,5,7-Cyclooctatetraene (COT), drawn below, has 8 pi electrons in the ring. Classify COT and give the correct reason.","q_smiles":"C1=CC=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Aromatic: 8 pi electrons satisfy the 4n+2 rule","Nonaromatic: it adopts a nonplanar tub shape, so the p orbitals do not form a continuous cyclic overlap","Antiaromatic: it is planar with 8 pi electrons (4n, n=2)","Aromatic: it gains stability from the 8-membered ring size","Antiaromatic: all eight carbons are sp3 hybridized"],"correct":1,"why":"Cyclooctatetraene is nonaromatic. With 8 pi electrons it would be antiaromatic if it were planar, so the ring puckers into a tub shape; the double bonds stay localized and the p orbitals never form a continuous cyclic overlap, so the molecule behaves like an ordinary polyene. The classic trap is to call it antiaromatic, but that would require a planar ring, which it avoids; and 8 electrons is a 4n count, not 4n+2, so it cannot be aromatic either.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. C8H8 valid, nonaromatic in RDKit. Standard textbook nonplanarity concept, in scope. Exactly one correct."},{"q":"Furan is shown below. The oxygen bears two lone pairs. How many pi electrons does the aromatic system contain, and where do they come from?","q_smiles":"c1ccoc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["6 pi electrons, but they come entirely from three C=C bonds within the ring","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 from ONE oxygen lone pair in a p orbital; the other O lone pair stays in an sp2 in-plane orbital","4 pi electrons from the two C=C bonds only; the oxygen lone pairs are not involved","8 pi electrons: 4 from the C=C bonds plus 4 from both oxygen lone pairs; furan is antiaromatic","6 pi electrons: 4 from the two C=C bonds plus 2 from BOTH oxygen lone pairs combined"],"correct":1,"why":"Furan's oxygen is sp2 hybridized. One lone pair sits in a p orbital perpendicular to the ring and joins the pi system, contributing 2 electrons; the other lone pair stays in an in-plane sp2 orbital and is not counted. Add the 4 electrons from the two ring C=C bonds and the total is 6 pi electrons (4n+2 with n=1), so furan is aromatic. Counting both lone pairs would wrongly give 8, and furan has only two ring C=C bonds, not three.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Furan SMILES valid+aromatic. Distractors plausible; opt4 factually impossible (good). Single heteroatom-donation concept, in scope."},{"q":"The cycloheptatrienyl (tropylium) cation is shown below. Counting pi electrons in the ring, classify it.","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=CC=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Antiaromatic: the empty p orbital contributes zero electrons, giving 4n","Antiaromatic: 8 pi electrons in the seven-membered ring","Nonaromatic: the positive carbon is sp3 and breaks conjugation","Aromatic: 6 pi electrons (three C=C bonds) over a planar, fully conjugated 7-membered ring with an empty p orbital at the cationic carbon","Aromatic: 8 pi electrons satisfy the 4n+2 rule"],"correct":3,"why":"Tropylium: all 7 carbons sp2, cationic C has an empty p orbital (0 e-), three C=C give 6 pi e- = 4n+2 (n=1), aromatic.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid C7H7+, aromatic in RDKit. Single Huckel concept, visual, in scope. Exactly one correct."},{"q":"Pyrrole is shown below. Although the nitrogen carries an N-H and a lone pair, pyrrole is a very weak base (it does NOT protonate on nitrogen like an amine). Counting pi electrons, why is pyrrole aromatic?","q_smiles":"c1cc[nH]c1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The nitrogen lone pair sits in a p orbital and is donated to the ring: 4 e- from two C=C bonds + 2 e- from the N lone pair = 6 pi electrons","Pyrrole has 6 pi electrons from three C=C bonds; the nitrogen lone pair is not involved","The nitrogen lone pair stays in an in-plane sp2 orbital; the ring has only 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic","Pyrrole is nonaromatic because the N-H carbon is sp3","The nitrogen contributes 4 electrons from its lone pair, giving 8 pi electrons total"],"correct":0,"why":"Pyrrole-type N (not in a ring double bond) donates its lone pair from a p orbital (2 e-); with two ring C=C (4 e-) = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic. The tied-up lone pair explains its weak basicity.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid+aromatic. Pyrrole-vs-pyridine N contrast is core sophomore content. Single concept, exactly one correct, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Five three-membered ring species are shown. Cyclopropene can ionize to give either the structure shown (drawn) or related ions. Which ONE of the five is AROMATIC?","q_smiles":"[CH+]1C=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH+]1CC1","[CH-]1C=C1","[CH+]1C=C1","C1C=C1","[CH]1C=C1"],"correct":2,"why":"The aromatic one is the cyclopropenyl cation. Its ring has one C=C (2 pi electrons) plus an empty p orbital on the cationic carbon, so it is planar, fully conjugated, and holds 2 pi electrons, which satisfies 4n+2 with n=0. The cyclopropenyl anion has 4 pi electrons and is antiaromatic; neutral cyclopropene has an sp3 CH2 that breaks the conjugation; the cyclopropenyl radical has an odd count of 3 electrons; and the cyclopropyl cation has no ring C=C at all. Remember that n=0 is allowed in the Huckel rule.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. correct=0 confirmed by RDKit. All 5 SMILES valid, distinct by charge/saturation. 2-electron (n=0) aromaticity is standard. Single concept, visual, in scope."},{"q":"Imidazole, drawn below, contains TWO nitrogen atoms in a five-membered ring: one is an N-H nitrogen and the other is a doubly-bonded (=N-) nitrogen. Determine the pi electron count and classify the ring.","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["6 pi electrons, aromatic: the N-H nitrogen donates its lone pair (2 e-) to the ring, while the =N- nitrogen keeps its lone pair in an in-plane sp2 orbital; both nitrogens plus the ring double bonds give the sextet","8 pi electrons, antiaromatic: BOTH nitrogen lone pairs are donated into the pi system","6 pi electrons, aromatic: both nitrogen lone pairs are donated, and one C=C bond is left out","4 pi electrons, antiaromatic: neither nitrogen lone pair is counted","6 pi electrons, but nonaromatic because the two nitrogens cannot both be planar"],"correct":0,"why":"Imidazole: pyrrole-type N-H donates its lone pair (2 e-), pyridine-type =N- contributes via its C=N pi bond and keeps its remaining lone pair in-plane (uncounted, source of basicity). Two ring pi bonds (4 e-) + N-H lone pair (2 e-) = 6 pi e- (4n+2), aromatic.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"aromaticity","issue":"None. Valid+aromatic. Layers two N types but remains a single-ring aromaticity classification - acceptable as the batch's hard item. Exactly one correct, in scope."},{"q":"2-Methylpropene (isobutylene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C","opts":["CC(C)CBr","CC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)Br","CCCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"HBr adds by Markovnikov: the proton goes to the CH2 (less-substituted carbon) to generate the more stable tertiary carbocation at the central carbon, captured by bromide to give tert-butyl bromide. The isobutyl bromide is anti-Markovnikov (primary cation), and the sec-/n-butyl options have rearranged carbon skeletons not produced here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. q_smiles and all four C4H9Br options parse; key [0] = tert-butyl bromide is the correct Markovnikov product; single defensible answer; visual; in DAT scope."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene reacts with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Cl","ClCC1=CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov addition places H on the ring CH and the charge on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon bearing the methyl, giving 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane. The 2-chloro isomer is anti-Markovnikov, the allylic chloride retains the alkene, and the cyclopentane option is the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All SMILES valid; key [0] = 1-chloro-1-methylcyclohexane correct (tertiary, Markovnikov); distractors plausibly wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccc(Br)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The proton adds to the terminal CH2 so the positive charge develops on the resonance-stabilized benzylic carbon, which bromide captures to give (1-bromoethyl)benzene. The 2-bromo product needs a non-stabilized primary cation, the alcohol uses the wrong nucleophile, and the ring-dibrominated option comes from a different (EAS) reaction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = (1-bromoethyl)benzene correct via benzylic resonance stabilization; distractors plausible-but-wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)(C)Cl","CCCC(C)Cl","CC(C)CCCl","CC(C)C(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Protonation gives a secondary cation adjacent to a tertiary carbon; a 1,2-hydride shift forms the more stable tertiary cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. The 2-chloro-3-methylbutane is the non-rearranged secondary product, and the remaining choices are anti-Markovnikov or wrong-skeleton structures.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All C5H11Cl options valid; key [0] = 2-chloro-2-methylbutane correct after hydride shift; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) in scope; single answer; visual."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene is treated with one equivalent of HBr under thermodynamic (higher-temperature) conditions. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C/CBr","CCCCBr","C=CCCBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation. Under thermodynamic control bromide adds 1,4 to give the more stable internal alkene, (E)-1-bromo-2-butene (crotyl bromide). The 3-bromo-1-butene is the 1,2 (kinetic) product, while the 4-bromo-1-butene and saturated 1-bromobutane do not match the allylic cation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; stem specifies thermodynamic conditions, disambiguating the 1,4 product from the 1,2 kinetic distractor; key [0] (E)-crotyl bromide correct with proper E geometry; recognition-level diene chemistry, in DAT scope; visual."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene undergoes acid-catalyzed hydration (dilute H2SO4, H2O). Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","OCC1=CCCCC1","CC1(O)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Markovnikov hydration places OH on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon via the more stable tertiary carbocation, giving 1-methylcyclohexanol. The 2-methylcyclohexanol is anti-Markovnikov, the allylic alcohol keeps the double bond, and the cyclopentane choice is the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 1-methylcyclohexanol correct (Markovnikov, tertiary); distractors plausible-but-wrong; single answer; visual."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with HBr. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC=C(C)Br","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The proton adds to the CH carbon so the charge forms on the more substituted (tertiary) carbon, giving the tertiary cation that bromide traps to form 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. The 2-bromo-3-methylbutane places Br on a secondary carbon (anti-Markovnikov), the vinyl bromide retains the double bond, and the last option is a rearranged skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 2-bromo-2-methylbutane (CCC(C)(C)Br) correct Markovnikov tertiary product; single answer; visual. Note option [2] vinyl bromide is C4H7Br (loses a carbon count vs substrate) but is clearly an implausible retained-alkene distractor, not a balance error in the keyed answer."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is treated with HCl. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(Cl)C(C)(C)Cl","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Protonation gives a secondary cation next to a quaternary carbon; a 1,2-methyl shift creates the more stable tertiary cation, which captures chloride to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane. The non-rearranged Markovnikov secondary chloride (3-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane) is the classic trap; the primary chloride is anti-Markovnikov, and the dichloride adds an extra halogen not present.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"None. All valid; key [0] = 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane correct after methyl shift; two-concept (Markovnikov + rearrangement) in scope; non-rearranged secondary chloride is the strong distractor; single answer; visual."},{"q":"The cation drawn below is an intermediate in an SN1/E1 reaction. How many resonance structures (including the one shown) contribute significant character to this carbocation?","q_smiles":"[CH2+]c1ccccc1","opts":["4","1 (the cation is not resonance-stabilized)","5","3","2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The benzyl cation is stabilized by resonance into the ring. Starting from the drawn form with the positive charge on the exocyclic CH2, pushing the ring pi bonds moves the charge to the two ortho carbons and to the para carbon; the meta carbons can never carry it. That gives four significant contributors: the benzylic form plus two ortho forms and one para form. A count of one would ignore resonance entirely, and five or three miscount the ring positions.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"MIS-KEYED: keyed index 4 selects '5', but benzyl cation has 4 resonance structures (1 benzylic + 2 ortho + 1 para). The 'why' even reasons to 4 yet selects '5'. Corrected correct to 3. The original 'why' was also garbled (stray '? Note:'); chemistry recoverable, so kept."},{"q":"Which alkyl halide ionizes fastest (forms the most stable carbocation) under SN1 conditions? Compare the carbocation each would generate.","q_smiles":"C[C+](C)C","opts":["A vinyl halide forming a vinyl cation","A methyl halide forming a methyl cation","A primary halide forming a primary, non-resonance-stabilized cation","A secondary halide forming an unconjugated secondary cation","A tertiary halide forming the tertiary cation drawn"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Carbocation stability: 3>2>1>methyl, and vinyl cations (sp carbon) are especially unstable. The drawn tert-butyl cation is tertiary, most stabilized by hyperconjugation and induction, so its precursor ionizes fastest in SN1.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid SMILES, single concept, clean."},{"q":"The species below is a conjugate base produced by deprotonating a neutral acid. The negative charge is delocalized. What term best describes why this anion is unusually stable?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)[O-]","opts":["Hyperconjugation from adjacent C-H bonds only","Aromaticity of a 6-electron ring","sp3 hybridization of the charged atom","An intramolecular hydrogen bond","Resonance delocalization of the charge equally over two equivalent oxygen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acetate, the conjugate base of acetic acid, delocalizes the negative charge over two equivalent electronegative oxygens via resonance (two identical major contributors), the dominant stabilizing factor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Two phenols are compared. The compound drawn is para-nitrophenol. Relative to plain phenol, is para-nitrophenol a stronger or weaker acid, and why?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Stronger; only because of the inductive effect, with no resonance involvement","Same acidity; the nitro group is too far away to matter","Weaker; the nitro group donates electron density by resonance","Stronger; the para-nitro group withdraws electron density and resonance-stabilizes the phenoxide","Weaker; nitro increases electron density on oxygen by induction"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"A para-nitro group is a strong EWG. In the phenoxide conjugate base, the negative charge delocalizes onto the nitro oxygens, spreading charge and stabilizing the anion; a more stable conjugate base means a stronger acid, so p-nitrophenol is far more acidic than phenol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES (proper nitro group). Two-concept (resonance + acidity) within scope."},{"q":"The molecule shown is allyl chloride. Classify the type of carbocation formed when the C-Cl bond ionizes, and state the key consequence for product distribution.","q_smiles":"C=CCCl","opts":["A resonance-stabilized allylic cation; two possible sites of nucleophilic attack (allylic products)","An antiaromatic cation; the reaction does not proceed","An aromatic cation; no reaction at the terminal carbon","A tertiary cation; rearrangement to a secondary cation","A localized primary cation; one product only"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Ionization of allyl chloride gives the resonance-stabilized allyl cation (charge shared between C1 and C3). A nucleophile can attack either terminus, giving allylic/regioisomeric products. This delocalization is why a formally primary allylic halide ionizes readily.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"Rank the acidity of the labeled O-H compounds: the carboxylic acid drawn (acetic acid), phenol, and ethanol. Which ordering of acid strength (strongest first) is correct, based on conjugate-base stability?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["carboxylic acid > phenol > ethanol","carboxylic acid > ethanol > phenol","phenol > carboxylic acid > ethanol","ethanol > phenol > carboxylic acid","ethanol > carboxylic acid > phenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Conjugate-base stability sets acidity: acetate (charge over two equivalent O, pKa ~5) > phenoxide (delocalized onto ring carbons, pKa ~10) > ethoxide (no resonance, pKa ~16). So carboxylic acid > phenol > ethanol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid SMILES. Standard pKa ranking, in scope."},{"q":"The radical shown is the allyl radical (CH2=CH-CH2 with the radical on the terminal carbon). Compared with a simple n-propyl (primary, non-allylic) radical, the allyl radical is:","q_smiles":"[CH2]C=C","opts":["Less stable, because radicals are destabilized by adjacent pi bonds","Equal in stability, because radicals are not affected by resonance","More stable, because the unpaired electron is delocalized over two carbons by resonance","Less stable, because it is antiaromatic","More stable, but only due to hyperconjugation, with no resonance contribution"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The allyl radical is resonance-stabilized: the unpaired electron is delocalized between C1 and C3 (two equivalent contributors), making it substantially more stable than an isolated primary radical.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 2). Valid radical SMILES, single concept."},{"q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with one equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives an intermediate cation, and the products are a mix of 1,2- and 1,4-addition. What feature of the intermediate explains the formation of BOTH products, and which product dominates under thermodynamic control?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["An aromatic cation; the 1,4-product dominates because it is aromatic","Two separate non-resonance cations; products form in a fixed 1:1 ratio regardless of conditions","A primary cation; the 1,2-product dominates because it is more stable","A resonance-stabilized allylic cation with charge on two carbons; the 1,4-product (more substituted, conjugated-friendly internal alkene) dominates under thermodynamic control","A localized secondary cation; only the 1,2-product forms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of butadiene gives an allylic cation with charge delocalized over C2 and C4. Bromide attacks either carbon (1,2 vs 1,4). Under thermodynamic control the 1,4-product dominates because it has the more substituted, more stable internal double bond. Classic resonance-intermediate / conjugate-addition item.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"resonance-stability","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 1). Valid SMILES. Kinetic vs thermodynamic control of butadiene+HBr is standard Orgo I / high-frequency DAT; single resonance-intermediate concept, in scope as the batch hard item."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"3-Chloro-1-butene (drawn) is solvolyzed in water (SN1). The intermediate carbocation is resonance-delocalized over two carbons. Besides 3-buten-2-ol, which constitutional isomer is also formed because of this allylic resonance?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCCO","CCCC=O","CC=CCO","C=C(O)CC","C=CC(C)O"],"correct":2,"why":"Loss of chloride from 3-chloro-1-butene gives a secondary allylic cation whose positive charge is shared between C2 and C4. Water attacking C2 gives 3-buten-2-ol, the direct product; water attacking the other resonance terminus, C4, gives the constitutional isomer 2-buten-1-ol, with the double bond shifted inward. That shifted alcohol is the resonance-derived product the question asks for. Butanal has no allylic relationship to this cation, the enol of 2-butanone is a different skeleton, and 3-buten-1-ol cannot form because the positive charge never reaches that carbon.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: stem and all 5 option SMILES valid and isomeric (C4H8O); allylic resonance gives exactly 2-buten-1-ol (opt0) as the extra product. Correct=0 confirmed. Two-concept (resonance+regio), in scope."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Which of the following carbocations is the MOST stable?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)[CH+]C","CC(C)(C)[CH2+]","[CH2+]C=C","C[CH2+]","[CH2+]c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"The benzyl cation is most stable because its positive charge is delocalized into the aromatic ring, with contributors at the ortho and para positions in addition to the benzylic carbon. The allyl cation also has resonance but spreads the charge over only two carbons and carries no alkyl donors. The secondary cation (3-methyl-2-butyl) relies on hyperconjugation alone, the ethyl cation is primary, and the neopentyl cation is primary with no resonance at all (it would rearrange rather than persist). Aromatic resonance stabilization beats all of these.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"All 5 option SMILES valid carbocations. q_smiles is empty but the molecules are drawn AS the options (structure-ID), so visual requirement met. Correct=0 confirmed; one clear answer."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Ethylbenzene (drawn) is treated with N-bromosuccinimide (NBS) and a radical initiator (heat/peroxide). The reaction proceeds through the most stabilized radical intermediate. Which is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(Br)(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1Br","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":2,"why":"NBS with a radical initiator does benzylic (or allylic) bromination, not addition or ring substitution. The bromine radical abstracts the benzylic hydrogen because the resulting benzylic radical is resonance-stabilized into the ring, and that radical then picks up Br to give 1-bromo-1-phenylethane, with bromine on the carbon attached to the ring. Bromine on the terminal CH3 would come from a far less stable primary radical; the ortho and para ring bromides require electrophilic aromatic substitution with Br2 and a Lewis acid; and the gem-dibromide is over-bromination.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem and all options valid. Benzylic radical bromination is canonical; correct=0 confirmed. Single concept, in scope, properly visual with structure distractors (regio + wrong-conditions)."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"para-Nitrophenol is drawn. Considering resonance stabilization of the conjugate base (phenoxide), rank these acids from STRONGEST to WEAKEST: para-nitrophenol, phenol, para-methylphenol (p-cresol), cyclohexanol.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["phenol > p-nitrophenol > p-cresol > cyclohexanol","cyclohexanol > phenol > p-cresol > p-nitrophenol","p-cresol > phenol > p-nitrophenol > cyclohexanol","p-nitrophenol > cyclohexanol > phenol > p-cresol","p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-cresol > cyclohexanol"],"correct":4,"why":"Strongest acid means most stabilized conjugate base. para-Nitrophenoxide delocalizes its negative charge not only into the ring but onto the oxygens of the nitro group, so para-nitrophenol is strongest (pKa about 7). Phenol (pKa about 10) has ring resonance only. para-Cresol is slightly weaker (pKa about 10.3) because the electron-donating methyl destabilizes the phenoxide. Cyclohexanol is weakest by far (pKa about 16 to 17): its alkoxide sits on an sp3 carbon with no resonance. The order is para-nitrophenol > phenol > para-cresol > cyclohexanol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem p-nitrophenol SMILES valid. Acidity ranking is qualitative (resonance, not pKa math), in scope. Correct=0 confirmed; one defensible order."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Pentane-2,4-dione (acetylacetone) is drawn. Which proton in this molecule is the MOST acidic, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The central CH2 protons, because they are farthest from oxygen","The terminal CH3 protons, because methyl carbanions are most stable","A terminal CH3 proton, because its carbanion is aromatic","All C-H protons are equally acidic since the molecule is symmetric","The central CH2 protons, because the resulting carbanion is delocalized onto BOTH carbonyl oxygens"],"correct":4,"why":"The central CH2 protons are the most acidic. Removing one gives an enolate whose negative charge is delocalized onto both carbonyl oxygens, which is why a beta-diketone has a pKa near 9, far lower than a simple ketone. A terminal CH3 proton gives an enolate stabilized by only one carbonyl (pKa near 20), and no carbanion here is aromatic. The reason matters: the central protons are acidic because of double resonance stabilization, not because of their distance from oxygen, and the molecule's symmetry does not make all protons equivalent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem acetylacetone SMILES valid (CalcMolFormula C5H8O2). Classic doubly-stabilized enolate; correct=0 confirmed. Single concept, in scope, molecule drawn in stem."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"1,3-Butadiene (drawn) reacts with 1 equivalent of HBr. Protonation gives a resonance-stabilized allylic cation, and under thermodynamic control the 1,4-addition product dominates. Which structure is that major 1,4-product (note its alkene geometry)?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C/C=C/CBr","CCC(Br)C","C=CC(C)Br","C/C=C\\CBr","C=CCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"Protonation at a terminal carbon gives the secondary allylic cation with its positive charge shared between C2 and C4. Bromide capture at C4 is the 1,4-product, 1-bromo-2-butene, and under thermodynamic control the more stable (E) geometry of this disubstituted alkene wins, so the major product is (E)-1-bromo-2-butene. The (Z) isomer has the less stable geometry; 3-bromo-1-butene is the kinetic 1,2-product; 2-bromobutane would require a second equivalent of HBr; and 4-bromo-1-butene is the anti-Markovnikov skeleton.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem and all options valid; opts0/1 carry explicit E vs Z stereo. Thermodynamic 1,4 E-product = opt0 confirmed. Two-concept (resonance/1,4 regio + stereo), in scope, properly visual."},{"group":"resonance-stability","q":"Acetamide is drawn. Which statement correctly describes the second major resonance contributor of an amide (the one explaining restricted C-N rotation)?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A C-H bond breaks to place negative charge on carbon","Oxygen donates a lone pair to nitrogen across space, forming an O-N bond","The C=O pi bond shifts onto carbon, giving a carbanion at nitrogen","The nitrogen lone pair shifts into the C-N bond, giving C=N+ with a negative charge (lone pair) on oxygen","The nitrogen lone pair leaves the molecule entirely, giving a free carbene"],"correct":3,"why":"The second resonance contributor pushes the nitrogen lone pair into the C-N bond, forming C=N(+), while the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen as a lone pair, giving O(-). This form gives the C-N bond partial double-bond character, which restricts rotation and makes the amide planar with an sp2 nitrogen. The other descriptions are not legitimate resonance forms: resonance never breaks sigma bonds (C-H or otherwise), never invents a through-space O-N bond, and never removes electrons from the molecule to make a carbene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Stem acetamide SMILES valid. Amide resonance/restricted rotation is core sophomore content (no FMO/MO-level reasoning). Correct=0 confirmed; one valid contributor described."},{"q":"The molecule shown is one of four compounds. Using the anchor pKa ladder, which of the following is the MOST acidic? CH3COOH (acetic acid), C6H5OH (phenol), CH3CH2OH (ethanol), H2O (water).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["CH3CH2OH (ethanol)","All four are equally acidic","C6H5OH (phenol)","CH3COOH (acetic acid)","H2O (water)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Anchor ladder: carboxylic acid pKa ~4-5 < phenol pKa ~10 < water pKa ~15.7 < alcohol pKa ~16-18. Acetic acid is most acidic; its carboxylate conjugate base is resonance-stabilized over two equivalent oxygens.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (acetic acid); correct answer = acetic acid (index 0); standard anchor-ladder ranking, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"The compound shown is para-nitrophenol. Compared to plain phenol (pKa ~10), how does the para-NO2 group affect acidity, and why?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["More acidic; the electron-withdrawing NO2 stabilizes the phenoxide conjugate base by resonance/induction","Less acidic; NO2 increases the s-character of the O-H bond","Less acidic; NO2 destabilizes the phenoxide by adding electron density","Unchanged; substituents on the ring do not affect O-H acidity","More acidic; NO2 is an electron-donating group that delocalizes the O-H bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"A para-nitro group is strongly electron-withdrawing (resonance + induction), delocalizing and stabilizing the phenoxide conjugate base, so p-nitrophenol (pKa ~7) is more acidic than phenol (pKa ~10).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (p-nitrophenol); correct answer = more acidic via EWG stabilization (index 0); classic substituent-effect item, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Two carboxylic acids are compared: 2-chlorobutanoic acid (drawn) and unsubstituted butanoic acid (CH3CH2CH2COOH). Which is the stronger acid, and what is the controlling effect?","q_smiles":"CCC(Cl)C(=O)O","opts":["2-Chlorobutanoic acid; Cl donates electron density by resonance into the carboxylate","Butanoic acid; the alkyl chain stabilizes the carboxylate by hyperconjugation","Butanoic acid; Cl raises the pKa by withdrawing electrons from the O-H","They are equally acidic because both are carboxylic acids","2-Chlorobutanoic acid; the electronegative Cl inductively stabilizes the carboxylate conjugate base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The alpha-chloro substituent withdraws electron density inductively, stabilizing the carboxylate, so 2-chlorobutanoic acid (pKa ~2.8) is stronger than butanoic acid (pKa ~4.8).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (2-chlorobutanoic acid); correct answer = 2-Cl acid via inductive stabilization (index 0); standard inductive-effect item, single concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"For pentane-2,4-dione (shown), identify the MOST acidic proton and the reason it is unusually acidic (pKa ~9).","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["A terminal CH3 proton; removing it gives a stable primary carbanion","A terminal CH3 proton; resonance with one carbonyl stabilizes the anion","There is no especially acidic proton; this compound is a hydrocarbon","The central CH2 (alpha) protons; the resulting carbanion is resonance-delocalized onto BOTH flanking carbonyl oxygens","The central CH2 protons; the carbanion is stabilized only by hybridization, not resonance"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The 1,3-dicarbonyl central CH2 (between two C=O) is most acidic (pKa ~9); its enolate is doubly resonance-stabilized over two carbonyl oxygens, far below a simple ketone alpha-proton (~20).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (acetylacetone); correct answer = central CH2 doubly resonance-stabilized (index 0); active-methylene concept, in scope. Note distractor opt2 names the right proton but wrong reason, still clearly inferior. No issue."},{"q":"Propyne (CH3-C#CH) is shown. Rank the acidity of its terminal/relevant C-H against propene (CH3-CH=CH2) and propane (CH3CH2CH3). Which is most acidic and why?","q_smiles":"C#CC","opts":["All three are equally acidic since all are C-H bonds","Propene; the sp2 C-H is most acidic due to resonance with the double bond","Propyne; the sp C-H is most acidic because of resonance into the triple bond","Propyne; the terminal sp C-H gives an sp carbanion that holds the lone pair in a high s-character (50%) orbital close to the nucleus","Propane; sp3 C-H is most acidic because of maximal hyperconjugation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Hybridization controls the ladder: sp C-H (~50% s, pKa ~25) < sp2 (~44) < sp3 (~50). Propyne's terminal sp C-H is most acidic; the effect is s-character/orbital, not resonance.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (propyne); correct answer = terminal sp C-H via s-character (index 0); hybridization-acidity ladder, in scope. Distractor opt4 (right compound, wrong 'resonance' reason) is a good trap. No issue."},{"q":"Ethanethiol (CH3CH2SH, shown) is compared with ethanol (CH3CH2OH). Which is the stronger acid, and which CARDIO factor explains the trend down a group?","q_smiles":"CCS","opts":["Ethanethiol; S is larger than O, so the conjugate base RS- spreads the charge over a bigger, more polarizable atom","Ethanol; O is more electronegative, so RO- is always the more stable conjugate base","They are equal because both are simple two-carbon compounds","Ethanol; the O-H bond is weaker than the S-H bond, making it more acidic","Ethanethiol; sulfur's lone pair is stabilized by resonance into the alkyl chain"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Down a column, atom size beats electronegativity: thiolate (larger, more polarizable S) is more stable than alkoxide, so ethanethiol (pKa ~10.6) is more acidic than ethanol (pKa ~16).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethanethiol); correct answer = thiol stronger via atom size/polarizability (index 0); size-down-a-group concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate (a beta-keto ester, shown). Which proton set is the MOST acidic, and what is the dominant stabilization of its conjugate base?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts":["The terminal CH3 of the acetyl group; it gives the most stable primary carbanion","The CH2 between the ketone and the ester carbonyls; its enolate is resonance-delocalized onto both carbonyl groups","The OCH2 protons of the ethyl ester group; oxygen induction makes them most acidic","The CH3 of the ethyl group; hyperconjugation stabilizes that carbanion best","The compound has no acidic proton because esters are not acidic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"In a beta-keto ester the methylene flanked by ketone and ester C=O is most acidic (pKa ~11); the enolate charge delocalizes onto both carbonyl oxygens (acetoacetic ester logic).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (ethyl acetoacetate); correct answer = central CH2 between the two carbonyls (index 0); active-methylene concept, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Three protonated nitrogen acids are compared: methylammonium (CH3NH3+, shown), anilinium (C6H5NH3+), and pyridinium (C5H5NH+). Which is the strongest acid (lowest pKa), reflecting the WEAKEST/most-stabilized conjugate base?","q_smiles":"C[NH3+]","opts":["Methylammonium; the alkyl group makes methylamine the weakest base and the cation the strongest acid","Pyridinium; the aromatic ring makes pyridine a stronger base than methylamine","All three have identical pKa because each loses an N-H proton","Methylammonium; a primary amine cation is always the most acidic","Anilinium; its conjugate base aniline has the N lone pair delocalized into the benzene ring, making aniline a weak base and anilinium the strongest acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Aniline is the weakest base (N lone pair delocalized into the ring), so its conjugate acid anilinium (pKa ~4.6) is the strongest acid, below pyridinium (~5.2) and methylammonium (~10.6).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"acid-base-pka","issue":"Valid SMILES (methylammonium); correct answer = anilinium strongest acid via aniline being weakest base (index 0); conjugate-acid/base + resonance reasoning, layered but DAT-recognition depth, in scope. No issue."},{"q":"Rank the following from MOST to LEAST acidic: trifluoroacetic acid (drawn), acetic acid, formic acid, and 2,2,2-trichloroacetic acid.","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(F)(F)F","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["CF3COOH > CCl3COOH > HCOOH > CH3COOH","CH3COOH > HCOOH > CCl3COOH > CF3COOH","HCOOH > CH3COOH > CF3COOH > CCl3COOH","CCl3COOH > CF3COOH > CH3COOH > HCOOH","CF3COOH > HCOOH > CCl3COOH > CH3COOH"],"correct":0,"why":"Acidity tracks conjugate-base stabilization by inductive electron withdrawal. Three F (more electronegative) withdraw more strongly than three Cl, so CF3COOH (pKa ~0.2) > CCl3COOH (~0.66). With no EWG, formic acid (~3.75) beats acetic acid (~4.76) because the alkyl group weakly destabilizes the carboxylate. Order: CF3 > CCl3 > HCOOH > CH3COOH.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All standard pKa values: CF3COOH ~0.23, CCl3COOH ~0.66, HCOOH ~3.75, CH3COOH ~4.76. SMILES valid, single concept (inductive), one defensible answer."},{"q":"Each structure below is a substituted phenol. Which one is the MOST acidic (lowest pKa)? The parent phenol is drawn in the stem for reference.","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Cc1ccc(O)cc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccc(O)cc1","Nc1ccc(O)cc1","COc1ccc(O)cc1","Oc1ccc(F)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"A phenol is more acidic when its phenoxide is better stabilized. A para-nitro group is a strong resonance + inductive EWG: the negative charge delocalizes directly onto the nitro oxygens (p-nitrophenol pKa ~7.1). p-OMe, p-CH3, and p-NH2 are electron-donating (higher pKa); p-F is only a weak inductive withdrawer with no through-resonance acceptor. The nitro phenol wins.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). p-nitrophenol pKa ~7.15 clearly most acidic; donors raise pKa; p-F weak inductive only. All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct. One defensible answer."},{"q":"Phenol is drawn in the stem. Which compound has the LEAST acidic O-H or C-H bond among phenol, cyclohexanol, acetic acid, and water?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["All four have identical acidity","Acetic acid (pKa ~4.76)","Phenol (pKa ~10)","Cyclohexanol (pKa ~16-18)","Water (pKa ~15.7)"],"correct":3,"why":"Compare conjugate-base stability. Acetate is resonance-delocalized (most acidic), phenoxide spreads charge into the ring (next), hydroxide and cyclohexyl alkoxide both localize charge on one O, but the electron-donating cyclohexyl destabilizes the alkoxide relative to hydroxide. So cyclohexanol is least acidic (highest pKa).","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Cyclohexanol ~16-18 > water 15.7 > phenol ~10 > AcOH ~4.76, so cyclohexanol is least acidic. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Malononitrile is drawn in the stem. Which of the following has the MOST acidic C-H bond?","q_smiles":"N#CCC#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Propane, CH3-CH2-CH3 (pKa ~50)","Ethene, CH2=CH2 (pKa ~44)","Toluene, C6H5-CH3 (pKa ~43)","Malononitrile, NC-CH2-CN (pKa ~11)","Acetonitrile, CH3-CN (pKa ~25)"],"correct":3,"why":"Malononitrile's central proton sits between two nitriles; its carbanion is resonance-delocalized onto both cyano nitrogens plus doubly inductively withdrawn (pKa ~11). A single nitrile (acetonitrile) gives only one stabilizing group (~25); propane, ethene, toluene give far less stabilized anions. Double cyano stabilization makes it by far the most acidic.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Malononitrile ~11 clearly lowest among ~25/~43/~44/~50. SMILES valid, one defensible answer."},{"q":"1,3-Cyclopentadiene is drawn in the stem. Rank these C-H bonds from MOST to LEAST acidic: 1,3-cyclopentadiene (sp3 CH2), cyclopentane, cyclopentene (allylic CH2), and propene (allylic CH3).","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["cyclopentene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene > propene > cyclopentane","cyclopentane > cyclopentene > propene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene","propene > 1,3-cyclopentadiene > cyclopentane > cyclopentene","1,3-cyclopentadiene = cyclopentene = propene > cyclopentane","1,3-cyclopentadiene > propene (allylic) > cyclopentene (allylic) > cyclopentane"],"correct":4,"why":"Deprotonating cyclopentadiene's sp3 CH2 gives the aromatic cyclopentadienyl anion (6 pi electrons), making it shockingly acidic (pKa ~16), far above ordinary allylic systems (~43). Cyclopentane gives an unstabilized sp3 carbanion (~50, least acidic). Aromaticity of the conjugate base is the deciding factor.","difficulty":3,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Borderline but keepable. Endpoints are unambiguous (cyclopentadiene most acidic via aromatic anion; cyclopentane least), and only option a places both endpoints correctly; option e is chemically wrong (cyclopentadiene NOT equal to allylic). The internal propene(~43) vs cyclopentene(~44) ranking is weak/essentially a tie, but it does not create a second selectable answer. One defensible answer by elimination."},{"q":"Propargyl alcohol (prop-2-yn-1-ol) is drawn in the stem. Which structure is the conjugate base formed by removing its MOST acidic proton?","q_smiles":"C#CCO","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C#CC[O-]","C#C[CH-]O","C#C[CH2-]","[C-]#CCO","[CH-]=C=CO"],"correct":0,"why":"Two candidates compete: the O-H and the terminal alkyne C-H. The O-H (pKa ~16) is more acidic than a terminal sp alkyne C-H (~25) because oxygen holds negative charge far better than carbon. So the most acidic proton is the O-H, giving the alkoxide. The acetylide, propargylic carbanion, allenyl anion, and the oxygen-less option are all higher-energy / wrong species.","difficulty":2,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Alcohol O-H pKa ~16 < terminal alkyne C-H ~25, so alkoxide is correct. All 5 option SMILES valid and distinct (e drops oxygen, a wrong skeleton). One defensible answer."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 2-methyl-2-butene is treated with HBr.","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(C)Br","CC=C(C)CBr","CCC(C)(C)O","CCC(C)(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr"],"correct":3,"why":"HBr adds by Markovnikov's rule: the proton goes to the less substituted alkene carbon (the CH), generating the more stable tertiary carbocation on the carbon that carries two methyls, and bromide then bonds there. The product is 2-bromo-2-methylbutane, a tertiary bromide. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane would come from the less stable secondary cation, 1-bromo-2-methylbutane is a primary bromide, the allylic bromide would require radical substitution with NBS, and 2-methyl-2-butanol would need water as the nucleophile.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; Markovnikov tertiary product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when propene is treated with HBr in the presence of a peroxide (ROOR).","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCO","C=CCBr","CC(C)Br","CC(Br)CBr","CCCBr"],"correct":4,"why":"Peroxides switch HBr addition to a radical chain mechanism. The bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 so that the unpaired electron lands on the more substituted secondary carbon; that radical then abstracts H from HBr. The net result is anti-Markovnikov addition, with bromine on the end carbon: 1-bromopropane. 2-Bromopropane is the ionic Markovnikov product obtained without peroxide, 1,2-dibromopropane would require Br2, allyl bromide would require NBS, and 1-propanol would need water.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; anti-Markovnikov product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when cyclohexene reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2.","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCC1","BrC1(Br)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1Br","Br[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds to cyclohexene through a cyclic bromonium ion, which bromide opens by backside attack from the opposite face. The result is stereospecific anti addition: trans-1,2-dibromocyclohexane, with the two bromines on opposite faces of the ring (formed as a racemic pair; one enantiomer is drawn). The cis-1,2-dibromide would require syn addition, which the bromonium pathway does not allow. Bromocyclohexane would need HBr, 1,1-dibromocyclohexane is a gem-dihalide, and the bromohydrin would form only with water as the solvent.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"3D check confirms A=trans, B=cis; anti-addition stereochem correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1-methylcyclohexene is treated with dilute H2SO4 in water (H3O+).","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1O","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O","OCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"Dilute aqueous acid hydrates the alkene with Markovnikov regiochemistry. Protonation at the CH end of the double bond gives the tertiary carbocation on the methyl-bearing carbon, water attacks there, and loss of a proton gives 1-methylcyclohexanol, a tertiary alcohol. 2-Methylcyclohexanol has the OH on the less substituted carbon (that would need hydroboration-oxidation), cyclohexylmethanol would require a rearranged skeleton, the tertiary bromide needs HBr, and the allylic alcohol keeps the double bond that is actually consumed.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; Markovnikov tertiary alcohol correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when trans-2-butene reacts with Br2.","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CC","CCC(Br)CBr","CC(Br)C(Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Bromine adds anti across the double bond via a bromonium ion. For trans-2-butene, anti addition gives the meso diastereomer, (2R,3S)-2,3-dibromobutane, which is achiral because of its internal mirror plane. The (2S,3S) structure is one of the chiral pair that forms from cis-2-butene instead. The structure with no stereochemistry drawn ignores the stereospecificity the question tests, 1,2-dibromobutane has the wrong skeleton, and 2-bromobutane is an HBr product.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"CIP confirms A=meso(R,S), B=(S,S); stereospecific anti-addition correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1-butyne is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr.","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC(Br)Br","CCC(Br)=CBr","CC(Br)C(Br)CC","CCC(Br)CBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br"],"correct":4,"why":"Each HBr adds with Markovnikov orientation. The first addition puts bromine on the internal carbon to give 2-bromo-1-butene; the second addition protonates the terminal CH2 again, forming a cation stabilized by the bromine already present, and the second bromide lands on that same carbon. The product is the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromobutane. 1,1-Dibromobutane is the anti-Markovnikov gem-dihalide, 1,2-dibromobutane is the vicinal pattern, 1,2-dibromo-1-butene is a single addition of Br2 rather than HBr, and the five-carbon dibromide has the wrong skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; double-Markovnikov gem-dibromide correct; sp/sp2/sp3 progression accurate; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when styrene (vinylbenzene) reacts with HBr.","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(O)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Markovnikov addition and resonance point the same way: protonating the terminal CH2 creates a secondary benzylic cation whose positive charge is delocalized into the ring, so bromide bonds at the benzylic carbon. The product is (1-bromoethyl)benzene. (2-Bromoethyl)benzene is the anti-Markovnikov isomer that would require peroxides, the para-bromo ring product needs electrophilic aromatic substitution, the dibromide needs Br2, and 1-phenylethanol needs water.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"All SMILES valid; benzylic Markovnikov product correct; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the major product when 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2.","q_smiles":"CC1=C(C)CCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1(C)Br","C[C@]1(O)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@]1(C)Br","C[C@]1(Br)CCCC[C@@]1(C)Br","CC1(C)C(Br)(Br)CCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Bromine adds anti through a bromonium ion, so the two C-Br bonds end up on opposite faces of the ring: trans-1,2-dibromo-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane (racemic; one enantiomer is drawn). Each former alkene carbon becomes a fully substituted sp3 stereocenter bearing a methyl and a bromine. The cis-dibromide would require syn addition, the structure with no stereochemistry drawn ignores the stereospecificity, the gem-dibromide with both methyls on one carbon has a rearranged skeleton, and the bromohydrin needs water as the solvent.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"3D check confirms A=trans, B=cis; anti-addition stereochem correct; single alkene/single concept+stereo; one defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"In the molecule drawn (acetonitrile, CH3-C#N), what is the hybridization of the nitrogen atom and the approximate C-C#N bond angle around the nitrile carbon?","q_smiles":"CC#N","opts":["sp2, 180 degrees","sp, 120 degrees","sp2, 120 degrees","sp3, 109.5 degrees","sp, 180 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The nitrile nitrogen has two electron domains, one sigma bond to carbon and one lone pair, with the triple bond counting as a single domain, so it is sp hybridized. The nitrile carbon likewise has two domains (one sigma bond to CH3 and the triple bond), so it is sp as well and the C-C#N unit is linear at 180 degrees. Pairing sp with 120 degrees or sp2 with 180 degrees mixes up hybridization and geometry.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; sp/180 verified."},{"q":"For the allene drawn (penta-2,3-diene, CH3-CH=C=CH-CH3), what is the hybridization of the central carbon (the sp carbon bearing the two cumulated double bonds)?","q_smiles":"CC=C=CC","opts":["sp, and the whole molecule is planar","sp, and the two CH3-bearing ends lie in perpendicular planes","sp2, and the two ends lie in perpendicular planes","sp3, tetrahedral","sp2, and the whole molecule is planar"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The central carbon of an allene is bonded to only two atoms through two cumulated double bonds, so it has two electron domains and is sp hybridized and linear. Its two remaining p orbitals are perpendicular to each other, and each forms a pi bond with a different terminal carbon; that forces the two end groups (each terminal carbon with its H and CH3) into perpendicular planes, not a single plane. So the central carbon is sp, and the two CH3-bearing ends lie in perpendicular planes.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; classic allene geometry verified."},{"q":"In acetamide (drawn: CH3-C(=O)-NH2), what is the hybridization of the nitrogen atom, and why?","q_smiles":"CC(N)=O","opts":["sp3, because nitrogen has three bonds and one lone pair like a typical amine","sp3, because the C-N bond is a single bond","sp2, because the lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl (amide resonance), making N planar","sp2, because nitrogen forms a double bond to carbon in the major resonance form","sp, because nitrogen is adjacent to a carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Amide nitrogen is sp2, not sp3, because its lone pair is delocalized into the carbonyl pi system (amide resonance). To overlap with the C=O pi bond the lone pair must sit in a p orbital, so the nitrogen becomes trigonal planar with partial C=N double-bond character and restricted rotation. Choosing sp2 because of a formal N=C double bond gets the label right for the wrong reason: the major contributor has a C-N single bond, and it is the resonance delocalization that flattens the nitrogen.","difficulty":2,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; only one fully right option (right answer + right reason)."},{"q":"For acetone (drawn: (CH3)2C=O), identify the hybridization of the carbonyl carbon and its approximate C-C-C / C-C=O bond angles.","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["sp, with bond angles of about 180 degrees","sp2, with bond angles of about 120 degrees","sp2, with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees","sp3, with bond angles of about 120 degrees","sp3, with bond angles of about 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The carbonyl carbon of acetone has three electron domains: two sigma bonds to the methyl groups and the C=O (a double bond counts as one domain). Three domains means sp2 hybridization and trigonal planar geometry, with C-C-C and C-C=O angles near 120 degrees. The sp3 label and 109.5 degree angles describe a tetrahedral carbon, which a double-bonded carbon can never be, and sp with 180 degrees would require only two domains.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; sp2/120 verified."},{"q":"Consider propyne, CH3-C#CH (drawn). How many sp-hybridized carbons does this molecule contain, and what is the H-C#C bond angle at the terminal alkyne carbon?","q_smiles":"CC#C","opts":["Two sp carbons; 180 degrees","One sp carbon; 180 degrees","Two sp carbons; 120 degrees","One sp carbon; 120 degrees","Three sp carbons; 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Both carbons of the triple bond are sp hybridized: each has two electron domains, one sigma bond plus the triple bond. That makes two sp carbons; the methyl carbon is sp3. The geometry around an sp carbon is linear, so the H-C#C angle at the terminal carbon is 180 degrees. Counting only one sp carbon forgets the internal alkyne carbon, and 120 degrees belongs to sp2 centers.","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; two sp carbons / 180 verified."},{"q":"Carbon dioxide is drawn (O=C=O). What is the hybridization of the central carbon and the molecular geometry / O-C-O bond angle?","q_smiles":"O=C=O","opts":["sp, bent, 120 degrees","sp2, trigonal planar, 120 degrees","sp2, bent, 120 degrees","sp, linear, 180 degrees","sp3, bent, 109.5 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The carbon in CO2 forms two double bonds and carries no lone pairs, so it has two electron domains. Two domains means sp hybridization and linear geometry with an O-C-O angle of 180 degrees. A bent shape would require a lone pair on carbon, and 120 degrees would require three domains (sp2).","difficulty":1,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; sp/linear/180 verified."},{"q":"Compare the nitrogen in the enamine drawn (ethenamine, CH2=CH-NH2) with the nitrogen of a simple amine such as ethylamine. Which statement correctly describes the enamine nitrogen?","q_smiles":"C=CN","opts":["The enamine N is closer to sp2 (more planar) because its lone pair conjugates with the C=C pi bond","The enamine N is sp hybridized and linear","The enamine N is sp3 but pyramidal inversion is impossible","The enamine N is sp2 only because it forms a double bond to carbon","The enamine N is sp3 and identical in geometry to an ordinary amine nitrogen"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"In an enamine the nitrogen lone pair is conjugated with the adjacent C=C pi bond, so the nitrogen flattens toward sp2 (trigonal planar) with its lone pair in a p orbital; this delocalization is what makes the beta carbon nucleophilic. A simple amine such as ethylamine has an sp3, pyramidal nitrogen with no such conjugation. Calling it sp2 only because of a formal N=C double bond gives the wrong reason, since the major contributor has an N-C single bond, and there is no basis for sp (linear) here.","difficulty":2,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; conjugation rationale is the only fully right option."},{"q":"Methyl isocyanate is drawn (CH3-N=C=O). Going left to right, give the hybridization of the nitrogen, the central carbon, and the oxygen, and the geometry at the central carbon.","q_smiles":"CN=C=O","opts":["N sp, central C sp (linear), O sp","N sp3, central C sp (linear), O sp2","N sp2, central C sp2 (trigonal planar), O sp2","N sp3, central C sp2 (bent), O sp3","N sp2, central C sp (linear), O sp2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Nitrogen has three electron domains (the N-CH3 single bond, the N=C double bond, and one lone pair), so it is sp2 and bent at nitrogen. The central carbon has two cumulated double bonds and therefore two domains, so it is sp and linear, just like the central carbon of an allene or of CO2. The oxygen in a C=O has one double bond plus two lone pairs, three domains, so it is sp2. So the assignment is N sp2, central C sp (linear), O sp2.","difficulty":3,"group":"hybridization-geometry","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct; N sp2 / C sp / O sp2 verified by RDKit."},{"q":"Ketene (drawn below) has the connectivity H2C=C=O. What is the hybridization of the CENTRAL carbon atom, and what is the approximate H-C-C bond angle at the terminal (CH2) carbon?","q_smiles":"C=C=O","opts":["Central C is sp; terminal CH2 angle ~120 degrees","Central C is sp; terminal CH2 angle ~109.5 degrees","Central C is sp3; terminal CH2 angle ~109.5 degrees","Central C is sp2; terminal CH2 angle ~180 degrees","Central C is sp2; terminal CH2 angle ~120 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Central carbon of ketene bears two cumulated double bonds (C=CH2 and C=O) = two electron regions = sp, linear. The terminal CH2 has one C=C plus two C-H bonds = three regions = sp2, ~120 degree H-C-C angle. Distractors swap centers or impose tetrahedral angle.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, in scope, exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"For acrolein (propenal, drawn below), how many carbon atoms are sp2 hybridized, and what is the approximate O=C-H bond angle at the aldehyde carbon?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts":["All 3 carbons are sp2; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","1 carbon is sp, 2 are sp2; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","2 carbons are sp2, 1 is sp; O=C-H angle ~120 degrees","2 carbons are sp2, 1 is sp3; O=C-H angle ~109.5 degrees","All 3 carbons are sp2; O=C-H angle ~180 degrees"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Acrolein is CH2=CH-CHO. Both vinyl carbons have a C=C (sp2) and the carbonyl carbon has a C=O plus two sigma bonds (sp2). All three carbons sp2, all ~120 degrees including O=C-H. No sp or sp3 carbons.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, single-concept recognition, in scope."},{"q":"Allene (propa-1,2-diene, drawn below) is H2C=C=CH2. Which statement correctly describes the hybridization of its carbons AND the geometric relationship of its two CH2 groups?","q_smiles":"C=C=C","opts":["All three carbons are sp, and the molecule is fully linear and planar","All three carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 groups lie in the same plane","Central C is sp (linear), terminal carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 planes are perpendicular to each other","Central C is sp2, terminal carbons are sp3, and the two CH2 planes are perpendicular","Central C is sp (linear), terminal carbons are sp2, and the two CH2 groups lie in the same plane"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The central carbon of allene has two cumulated double bonds, which is two electron domains, so it is sp and linear. Each terminal carbon has one C=C plus two C-H bonds, three domains, so it is sp2. Because the central carbon's two p orbitals are perpendicular to each other and each forms a pi bond with a different terminal carbon, the two CH2 planes sit 90 degrees apart. The tempting trap keeps the correct hybridization but places both CH2 groups in the same plane, which is wrong.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Classic allene geometry, DAT-appropriate, well-constructed coplanar trap."},{"q":"In pyridine (drawn below), classify the ring nitrogen's hybridization and describe where its lone pair resides.","q_smiles":"c1ccncc1","opts":["sp2; the lone pair occupies the p orbital and IS part of the aromatic pi system","sp3; the lone pair is in an sp3 orbital pointing out of the ring","sp2; the lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the ring plane and is NOT part of the aromatic pi system","sp; the lone pair lies along the molecular axis","sp2; the nitrogen contributes two electrons to the aromatic sextet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Pyridine nitrogen is sp2 (planar aromatic ring). It contributes ONE electron via its p orbital to the aromatic sextet; its lone pair sits in an in-plane sp2 orbital NOT delocalized into the ring, which is why pyridine is basic. Distractors 3/5 describe pyrrole.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Standard pyridine-vs-pyrrole distinction, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"A student claims the carbonyl carbon of methyl vinyl ketone (but-3-en-2-one, drawn below) is sp3 with ~109.5 degree angles. Which statement is correct?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=O","opts":["Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~109.5 degrees","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 but its angles are ~180 degrees","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp2 with ~120 degree bond angles","Incorrect; the carbonyl carbon is sp with ~180 degree bond angles","Correct; the carbonyl carbon is sp3 with ~109.5 degree bond angles"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The student is wrong. The carbonyl carbon has three electron domains: a sigma bond to the vinyl group, a sigma bond to the methyl group, and the C=O double bond. Three domains means sp2 hybridization and trigonal planar geometry with bond angles near 120 degrees. A carbon in a double bond can never be sp3, and 109.5 degrees belongs to tetrahedral centers; pairing sp2 with 109.5 or 180 degrees mixes up hybridization and geometry.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, single-concept, in scope."},{"q":"The molecule 2-methylbut-1-en-3-yne is drawn below. It contains carbons of three different hybridizations. Which of the following candidate molecules contains an sp-hybridized carbon (as in the drawn molecule's triple bond)?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C#C","opts":["CC=CC","c1ccccc1","C1CCCCC1","CC(C)=O","CC#CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"An sp carbon needs a triple bond or two cumulated double bonds. Only 2-butyne (CC#CC) has a triple bond, so its internal carbons are sp. Cyclohexane is all sp3; acetone carbonyl is sp2; benzene all sp2; 2-butene sp2.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Structure options all valid SMILES; exactly one contains an sp carbon. Visual stem + structure options."},{"q":"Acetamide is drawn below. Which statement best describes the hybridization and geometry of its NITROGEN atom?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)N","opts":["sp2 and roughly trigonal planar, because the N lone pair conjugates with the C=O (amide resonance)","sp2 but bent at ~109.5 degrees, with the lone pair localized on N","sp3 and pyramidal, with bond angles of ~109.5 degrees, like a normal amine","sp and linear, with bond angles of ~180 degrees","sp3 and trigonal planar, because nitrogen always has four electron regions"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The amide nitrogen is sp2 and roughly trigonal planar. Its lone pair conjugates with the C=O pi system, so it must sit in a p orbital rather than an sp3 orbital; this resonance gives the C-N bond partial double-bond character, restricts rotation, and makes amides far less basic than amines. A free amine nitrogen is sp3 and pyramidal, but amide resonance changes that here. The sp2 label pairs with angles near 120 degrees, not 109.5, and nitrogen does not always have four electron regions.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Standard amide-resonance concept, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"q":"Aniline (drawn below) has its nitrogen attached directly to a benzene ring. Compared with the nitrogen in a simple sp3 alkylamine, the aniline nitrogen is best described as:","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["sp2 with the nitrogen carbon of the ring becoming sp3","Closer to sp2/planar, because its lone pair partially conjugates with the aromatic ring","Purely sp and linear at nitrogen","sp3 with the lone pair fully delocalized into the ring like pyridine's","Purely sp3 and identical in geometry to a simple alkylamine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The aniline N lone pair conjugates (donates) into the ring, flattening the nitrogen toward planarity and giving it sp2 character relative to an sp3 amine, which weakens its basicity. It is not sp/linear, and the ipso carbon stays sp2. Distractor 4 confuses aniline (exocyclic donor N) with pyridine (ring N).","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"hybridization-geometry","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Comparative framing ('closer to sp2/partially conjugates') keeps it defensible despite aniline's real slight pyramidalization; DAT-level, in scope, valid SMILES."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Phenylethanol is treated with PCC in CH2Cl2. The product's IR spectrum shows a strong band near 1715 cm-1 and no broad absorption in the 3200-3500 cm-1 region. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=CCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"PCC oxidizes a secondary alcohol to a ketone and stops there, so 1-phenylethanol becomes acetophenone. The IR agrees: a strong ketone C=O near 1715 and no broad O-H stretch. Benzoic acid would show a very broad O-H and cannot form from a secondary alcohol; benzaldehyde and phenylacetaldehyde have the wrong connectivity (an aldehyde C-H and a different carbon skeleton); and unreacted 1-phenylethanol would keep the O-H band.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard PCC oxidation; qualitative IR (carbonyl + loss of O-H) in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct; distractors are over-oxidation/wrong skeleton/unreacted."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Butyne is treated with aqueous H2SO4 and HgSO4. The IR of the product shows a strong band near 1715 cm-1 and loss of the absorption near 2150 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#C","CCCC=O","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(C)O","CCC(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Mercury-catalyzed hydration of a terminal alkyne is Markovnikov: water adds to the internal alkyne carbon to give an enol, which tautomerizes to the methyl ketone, 2-butanone. The IR fits: a strong C=O near 1715 appears and the C#C stretch near 2150 disappears. Butanal would be the anti-Markovnikov product (hydroboration-oxidation gives that), 2-butanol would show a broad O-H instead of a carbonyl, butanoic acid has an extra oxygen, and unreacted 1-butyne keeps the 2150 band.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical Markovnikov alkyne hydration; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Propanenitrile is refluxed with aqueous H3O+ (excess water, heat). The IR of the product shows a very broad band over 2500-3300 cm-1 plus a strong band near 1710 cm-1, and the sharp absorption near 2250 cm-1 is gone. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#N","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(N)=O","CCC(=O)O","CCC#N","CCCO","CCC=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Heating a nitrile with aqueous acid hydrolyzes it completely to the carboxylic acid, so propanenitrile becomes propanoic acid. The IR fits a carboxylic acid: a very broad O-H stretch over 2500 to 3300, a strong C=O near 1710, and loss of the sharp C#N band near 2250. Propanamide is only the partial hydrolysis product and would show N-H bands with a lower C=O; propanal and 1-propanol are the wrong functional groups; unreacted nitrile keeps the 2250 band.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard nitrile hydrolysis to acid; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. The IR of the product shows a strong broad band near 3300 cm-1 and loss of the strong band near 1715 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1O","OC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Sodium borohydride reduces a ketone to a secondary alcohol, so cyclohexanone becomes cyclohexanol. The IR fits: a broad O-H stretch near 3300 appears and the C=O near 1715 vanishes. NaBH4 does not reduce a carbonyl all the way to an alkene (neither cyclohexene nor methylenecyclohexane forms), it cannot create a 1,2-diol from a single carbonyl, and unreacted cyclohexanone would keep its 1715 band.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard NaBH4 ketone reduction; qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Hexyne is treated with NaNH2, then with CH3I. The IR of the product still shows a weak band near 2200 cm-1 (C#C) but the sharp band near 3300 cm-1 has disappeared. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC#CCCC","CC#CCCCC","C#CCCCC","C=CCCCCC","C#CCCCCC"],"correct":1,"why":"Sodium amide deprotonates the terminal alkyne to an acetylide, which displaces iodide from CH3I by SN2 and adds one carbon at the former terminal position, giving the internal alkyne 2-heptyne. The IR fits: the sharp terminal C-H stretch near 3300 is gone, while the weak C#C band remains. 1-Heptyne and unreacted 1-hexyne are terminal alkynes and would keep the 3300 band, 3-heptyne puts the new carbon on the wrong end, and 1-heptene would have no C#C band at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard acetylide alkylation; carbon count correct (heptyne); qualitative IR in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Benzoic acid is treated with SOCl2. The IR of the product shows a strong carbonyl band that has shifted up to about 1790 cm-1, and the very broad 2500-3300 cm-1 absorption is gone. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","ClC(=O)c1ccccc1","ClCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Thionyl chloride converts a carboxylic acid into an acyl chloride, so benzoic acid becomes benzoyl chloride. The IR fits: the very broad acid O-H is gone, and the carbonyl shifts up to about 1790 because the electronegative chlorine stiffens the C=O bond. Benzaldehyde, benzyl alcohol, and benzyl chloride would all require a reduction step that SOCl2 does not perform, and unreacted benzoic acid would keep its broad O-H.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard acid-to-acyl-chloride; qualitative IR (carbonyl shift direction, not exact-value math) in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"1-Butene is treated with Hg(OAc)2 in water, then NaBH4 (oxymercuration-demercuration). The IR of the product shows a strong broad band near 3300 cm-1 and no band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 region. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC=O","OCCCC","CC(O)CC","CCC(C)=O","C=CCC"],"correct":2,"why":"Oxymercuration-demercuration adds water with Markovnikov regiochemistry and without carbocation rearrangement, so 1-butene gives 2-butanol, the secondary alcohol. The IR fits: a broad O-H near 3300 and no carbonyl band. 1-Butanol is the anti-Markovnikov product (that is hydroboration-oxidation), butanal and 2-butanone would show a C=O near 1715 to 1730, and unreacted 1-butene has no O-H.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Canonical oxymercuration; layered regio + IR; in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"group":"spectroscopy-ir","q":"Aniline is treated with acetic anhydride. The IR of the product shows a band near 3300 cm-1 (one sharp N-H) together with a strong carbonyl band near 1660 cm-1. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCNc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Acetic anhydride acylates the nitrogen of aniline, the most nucleophilic site, to give the secondary amide acetanilide. The IR fits a secondary amide: one sharp N-H band near 3300 plus a strong amide C=O near 1660. N-Ethylaniline has an N-H but no carbonyl, phenyl acetate and acetophenone have a carbonyl but no N-H, and unreacted aniline shows two N-H bands with no carbonyl.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard N-acylation; combined N-H + amide carbonyl reasoning is sophomore-level; in scope; all SMILES valid; keyed answer correct."},{"q":"A student records the IR spectrum of the compound drawn below and observes a very broad, intense absorption spanning roughly 2500-3300 cm-1 superimposed on a strong, sharp band near 1710 cm-1. Which structural feature accounts for this pattern?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["An amine N-H stretch with no C=O present","An ester C=O with no O-H present","An isolated alcohol O-H stretch only, near 3300 cm-1","A terminal alkyne C-H stretch near 3300 cm-1","A carboxylic acid: the C=O stretch (~1710) plus the very broad hydrogen-bonded O-H stretch (2500-3300)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The compound is butanoic acid, and this is the classic carboxylic acid signature: a strong C=O stretch near 1710 together with an extremely broad O-H stretch spanning 2500 to 3300, broadened by hydrogen-bonded dimers. The combination rules out the alternatives: an alcohol's O-H is narrower and comes with no carbonyl, an ester has a carbonyl but no O-H, an amine shows N-H but no C=O, and a terminal alkyne C-H near 3300 is sharp and carbonyl-free.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Iconic carboxylic-acid IR pattern; valid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the molecule shown has a single strong absorption near 1715 cm-1 and shows NO significant broad absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region. Which class of compound is most consistent with these data?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Primary amide","Ketone","Terminal alkyne","Alcohol","Carboxylic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A strong band near 1715 with nothing broad between 2500 and 3500 is a ketone (here, cyclohexanone). A carboxylic acid would add a very broad O-H across 2500 to 3300, an alcohol shows a broad O-H near 3300 and no carbonyl, a primary amide shows two N-H bands with a lower carbonyl, and a terminal alkyne has a sharp C-H near 3300 with a C#C band near 2120 instead of a carbonyl.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Clean ketone identification; valid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown gives a sharp IR band at ~3300 cm-1 together with a weak but distinct absorption at ~2120 cm-1, and no strong band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 carbonyl region. Which structure best fits this IR spectrum?","q_smiles":"C#CCCCC","opts":["A ketone (strong C=O ~1715)","A carboxylic acid (broad O-H 2500-3300, C=O ~1710)","A nitrile (sharp C triple-bond N ~2250 only, no 3300 band)","A primary alcohol (broad O-H ~3300, C-O ~1050)","A terminal alkyne (sharp C-H ~3300, C triple-bond C ~2120, no carbonyl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A sharp band near 3300 plus a weak band near 2120 and no carbonyl is the signature of a terminal alkyne such as 1-hexyne: the sharp 3300 band is the alkyne C-H stretch and 2120 is the C#C stretch. Acids and alcohols show a broad, not sharp, band in the 3300 region, and both the acid and the ketone would show a strong carbonyl. A nitrile absorbs near 2250 and has no 3300 band.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Terminal-alkyne IR recognition; nitrile distractor correctly placed at ~2250."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the compound drawn shows a sharp, medium-intensity absorption near 2250 cm-1, with no carbonyl band and no broad O-H. Which functional group produces the 2250 cm-1 absorption?","q_smiles":"CCCC#N","opts":["Carbonyl (C=O)","Nitrile (C triple-bond N)","Terminal alkyne C-H","Amine N-H","Hydroxyl (O-H)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The compound is butanenitrile, and the sharp, medium band near 2250 is the C#N stretch, which sits in the triple-bond region (2100 to 2260). A carbonyl would appear near 1700, an O-H or N-H would appear as broad or medium bands above 3200, and a terminal alkyne C-H is a sharp band near 3300 with its C#C near 2120, lower than 2250.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Straightforward nitrile identification; valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Two compounds are drawn below. Compound A is left of the dot-disconnect; compound B is on the right. Compound A's IR shows two medium bands near 3350 and 3180 cm-1 plus a strong band at 1690 cm-1; compound B shows only a strong band near 1715 cm-1. Which assignment correctly explains compound A's two N-H bands plus carbonyl?","q_smiles":"NC(=O)c1ccccc1.O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Compound A is a carboxylic acid: the two bands are O-H overtones","Compound A is a primary amide: two N-H stretches (asymmetric ~3350, symmetric ~3180) plus the amide C=O ~1690","Compound A is a nitrile, and the 1690 band is a C-triple-bond-N stretch","Compound A is an alcohol whose O-H splits into two sharp bands","Compound A is a secondary amine with a coincidental carbonyl"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Benzamide, the first compound drawn, is a primary amide. The NH2 group gives two N-H stretches, an asymmetric one near 3350 and a symmetric one near 3180, and the amide carbonyl appears near 1690, lowered by resonance and by conjugation with the ring. A pair of N-H bands plus a carbonyl is the primary amide giveaway: a secondary amine would have one N-H and no carbonyl, a nitrile absorbs near 2250 rather than 1690, an alcohol O-H is a single broad band, and a carboxylic acid O-H is one very broad band rather than two medium ones. Cyclohexanone, the second compound, shows only its ketone C=O near 1715.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"Dot-disconnect SMILES is unusual notation for a 'drawn below' stem but valid; chemistry and key correct."},{"q":"Four candidate structures share the molecular formula but differ in functional group. An IR spectrum shows a strong band at 1740 cm-1 and NO absorption in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region (no O-H, no N-H). Of the molecule drawn, which statement about why it matches is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC","opts":["It is an amide, and the missing N-H bands are overlapping the C=O","It is a ketone, identified only by the lack of any 3300 cm-1 band","It is an ester: strong C=O ~1740 cm-1 and no O-H/N-H bands in the 2500-3500 region","It is an alcohol, and the 1740 band is its C-O stretch","It is a carboxylic acid, so the broad O-H is simply too weak to see"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The compound is ethyl acetate, an ester. Its carbonyl absorbs near 1740, higher than a ketone's 1715, because the inductive pull of the ester oxygen strengthens the C=O bond, and nothing appears in the 2500 to 3500 region because the molecule has no O-H or N-H. A carboxylic acid would show a very broad O-H that is never too weak to see, a primary amide would show N-H bands near 3300 with a lower carbonyl, an alcohol has no band near 1740 (its C-O stretch is near 1050), and a ketone is identified by its 1715 carbonyl, not merely by the absence of a 3300 band.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Ester C=O at ~1740 correctly above ketone; valid SMILES, one defensible answer."},{"q":"Rank the three carbonyl compounds shown for the position (wavenumber) of their C=O stretch, from HIGHEST to LOWEST. Structures, in order drawn: (I) an ester, (II) a ketone, (III) a primary amide.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)OCC.CCCC(C)=O.NC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Amide (~1690) > ester (~1740) > ketone (~1715)","Ester (~1740) > ketone (~1715) > amide (~1690)","Ketone (~1715) > ester (~1740) > amide (~1690)","Amide (~1690) > ketone (~1715) > ester (~1740)","Ester (~1740) > amide (~1690) > ketone (~1715)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Highest to lowest: ester (ethyl acetate, about 1740) > ketone (2-pentanone, about 1715) > primary amide (benzamide, about 1690 or lower). The ester oxygen withdraws electron density inductively, which strengthens the C=O bond and raises its frequency above a ketone's. In an amide the nitrogen lone pair donates into the carbonyl by resonance, lowering the C=O bond order and its frequency; conjugation with the phenyl ring lowers benzamide's carbonyl further. The simple ketone sits between the two.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Standard carbonyl-frequency trend; the chosen substrates preserve the ester>ketone>amide ordering."},{"q":"An IR spectrum of the drawn compound shows a broad absorption centered near 3350 cm-1 and strong bands in the 1000-1100 cm-1 region, but NO band in the 1650-1750 cm-1 carbonyl region and NO band in the 2100-2260 cm-1 triple-bond region. Which functional group is present?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts":["Ketone (would show a strong C=O ~1715)","Carboxylic acid (would also show a strong C=O ~1710)","Terminal alkyne (would show a sharp band ~2120)","Alcohol (broad O-H ~3350, C-O ~1050; no carbonyl, no triple bond)","Nitrile (would show C-triple-bond-N ~2250)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The compound is cyclohexanol. A broad band near 3350 is a hydrogen-bonded O-H stretch, and strong bands at 1000 to 1100 are the C-O stretch; the absence of any carbonyl near 1700 and of any triple-bond band at 2100 to 2260 confirms a simple alcohol. A carboxylic acid would also need a strong C=O near 1710, a ketone requires that carbonyl too, and a nitrile or terminal alkyne would show a triple-bond absorption.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Clean alcohol identification with well-constructed eliminative distractors."},{"q":"A student records the IR spectrum of the compound shown below and observes a sharp absorption at approximately 2250 cm-1, with NO strong band near 1700 cm-1 and NO broad band in the 2500-3500 cm-1 region. Which functional group is responsible for the 2250 cm-1 absorption?","q_smiles":"CCCCC#N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["C≡N (nitrile) triple-bond stretch","C=C (alkene) stretch","N-H (amine) stretch","C=O (carbonyl) stretch","O-H (alcohol) stretch"],"correct":0,"why":"Pentanenitrile. Sharp ~2250 cm-1 is the nitrile C≡N stretch; absence of ~1700 rules out carbonyl, absence of broad 2500-3500 rules out O-H/N-H. Distractors are standard wrong band assignments.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct as keyed (0); valid SMILES; single-concept IR recognition, in scope."},{"q":"An IR spectrum shows a strong band at ~1715 cm-1 (C=O) AND a very broad band stretching from ~2500-3300 cm-1. Which of the following structures is consistent with BOTH of these absorptions?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=CCc1ccccc1","O=C(C)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(OC)Cc1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Only phenylacetic acid (carboxylic acid) shows BOTH a C=O (~1715) and the very broad O-H (2500-3300). Alcohol = O-H only; ketone/aldehyde/ester = C=O only, no broad O-H. Distractors differ only by functional group on the same skeleton.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); all option SMILES valid; structure-ID is visual; unique answer; in scope."},{"q":"For the compound drawn below, which set of IR absorptions would you expect to observe?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)N","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Strong C=O near 1660 cm-1 AND two N-H bands near 3350 and 3180 cm-1","Broad O-H from 2500-3300 cm-1 AND C=O near 1710 cm-1","Strong C=O near 1715 cm-1 only, with no bands above 3000 cm-1","Sharp C≡N near 2250 cm-1 only","Broad O-H near 3300 cm-1 with no carbonyl band"],"correct":0,"why":"Butanamide is a primary amide: lowered amide-I C=O (~1640-1680) plus an N-H doublet (asym ~3350, sym ~3180) from two N-H bonds. This pattern distinguishes primary amide from carboxylic acid (broad single O-H) or ketone.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES (C4H9NO primary amide); qualitative IR, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon (formula C6H10) gives an IR spectrum with a SHARP absorption at ~3300 cm-1 plus a weak band near 2120 cm-1. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCCCC1","C#CCCCC","CC1=CCCC1","CC#CCCC","CCC#CCC"],"correct":1,"why":"A sharp band near 3300 with a weak band near 2120 is the terminal alkyne signature: the sharp 3300 band is the alkyne C-H stretch and 2120 is the C#C stretch. Among the structures, only 1-hexyne is a terminal alkyne. 2-Hexyne and 3-hexyne are internal alkynes with no alkyne C-H, so they lack the 3300 band (and their C#C stretch is weak or absent). 1-Methylcyclopentene fits C6H10 but is an alkene with neither band, and cyclohexane is C6H12 and fully saturated.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"Minor: stem formula C6H10 fits opts 0-2 and 4 but not cyclohexane (C6H12); however the spectrum still uniquely identifies the terminal alkyne (0), so answer remains unambiguous. Keep."},{"q":"Which of the structures below would show an IR spectrum with a strong C=O stretch near 1700 cm-1 and a broad O-H stretch centered near 3300 cm-1, but NO N-H absorption?","q_smiles":"","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","N#Cc1ccccc1","CC=O","CC(=O)N","OC(C(=O)O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Mandelic acid has -OH (broad ~3300) and -COOH C=O (~1700) and no nitrogen, so no N-H. Acetamide has N-H/C=O but no O-H; benzyl alcohol O-H only; benzonitrile C≡N only; acetaldehyde C=O only.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); all option SMILES valid; combining 3 qualitative IR observations is still recognition-level, in scope; unique answer."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the compound shown shows a strong C=O band, plus TWO N-H stretching bands near 3460 and 3360 cm-1. Based on the structure, the two N-H bands arise from which group?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccc(C=O)cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Two separate O-H groups hydrogen bonding to each other","The primary aromatic amine (-NH2), which has two N-H bonds (asymmetric + symmetric stretch)","A nitrile overtone splitting into two bands","The aromatic ring C-H stretches above 3000 cm-1","The aldehyde C-H, which always appears as a doublet near 3400 cm-1"],"correct":1,"why":"4-Aminobenzaldehyde has a primary -NH2, which gives two N-H stretches (asym ~3460, sym ~3360). Secondary amine = one band, tertiary = none. The aldehyde gives C=O and weak C-H (~2720/2820), not the 3400 doublet.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES; single-concept primary-amine N-H doublet recognition, in scope."},{"q":"The IR spectrum of the molecule shown displays a SHARP nitrile band at ~2240 cm-1 together with a broad O-H stretch near 3400 cm-1. Which functional groups account for these two absorptions?","q_smiles":"OC(C#N)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["An alkyne (C≡C) and an O-H","A nitrile (C≡N) and a hydroxyl (O-H) — a cyanohydrin","An aldehyde C=O and a hydroxyl O-H","A primary amine (N-H) and a C=O","A carboxylic acid (broad O-H) and a C=O"],"correct":1,"why":"Mandelonitrile is a cyanohydrin: -C≡N (sharp ~2240) and -OH (broad ~3400) on the same carbon. No band near 1700 rules out carbonyl/carboxylic acid; a terminal alkyne would give a sharp ≡C-H ~3300, not a broad O-H.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES (mandelonitrile); qualitative band ID, in scope."},{"q":"Salicylaldehyde (shown) has an intramolecular hydrogen bond between its phenol O-H and the adjacent aldehyde oxygen. Compared with the IR of a simple phenol, what is the expected effect of this intramolecular hydrogen bonding on the O-H stretch?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The O-H band moves to ~2250 cm-1, overlapping the nitrile region","The O-H stretch sharpens and shifts to HIGHER wavenumber (above 3600 cm-1)","The O-H stretch becomes a sharp doublet like a primary amine","The O-H stretch is broadened and shifted to LOWER wavenumber (below ~3200 cm-1)","The O-H stretch disappears entirely from the spectrum"],"correct":3,"why":"Hydrogen bonding weakens the O-H bond, lowering its frequency and broadening the band. The intramolecular H-bond in salicylaldehyde persists even when dilute, so O-H is broad and shifted to lower wavenumber vs a free O-H (~3600). Standard sophomore IR H-bonding concept; qualitative, no quantitative shift math.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ir","issue":"None. Correct (0); valid SMILES; H-bonding effect on O-H frequency is qualitative recognition, within DAT scope; unique answer."},{"q":"An aromatic hydrocarbon with molecular formula C8H10 shows only TWO signals in its 1H NMR spectrum. Both signals are singlets, and they integrate in a 3:2 ratio (the more intense, more upfield signal corresponds to 6H). Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccc(C)cc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","CCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"para-Xylene (1,4-dimethylbenzene) has the symmetry the spectrum demands: the two methyl groups are equivalent (one 6H singlet) and all four ring hydrogens are equivalent (one 4H singlet), giving two singlets in a 3:2 ratio. ortho-Xylene gives three signals and meta-xylene gives four because their ring hydrogens are not all equivalent. Ethylbenzene shows a CH2 quartet and a CH3 triplet, and toluene is C7H8, the wrong formula.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; all SMILES valid (C8H10 for 0-3, toluene C7H8 distractor), unique answer at index 0, recognition-level symmetry, in scope."},{"q":"A compound C3H7Br shows TWO 1H NMR signals. The most downfield signal is a 1H septet, and the upfield signal is a 6H doublet. Which structure fits this pattern?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["CCCCBr","CC(C)Br","CCBr","CCCBr","CC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"2-Bromopropane fits. Its single CH is split by six equivalent neighboring hydrogens into a septet (n+1 = 7), and the two equivalent methyls, each split by that one CH, appear as a 6H doublet; the CH is downfield because it carries the bromine. 1-Bromopropane is the other C3H7Br isomer but gives three signals (triplet, sextet, triplet). Bromoethane, 1-bromobutane, and 2-bromo-2-methylpropane do not have the formula C3H7Br.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; n+1 rule recognition, valid SMILES, unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A compound with molecular formula C2H4Cl2 shows exactly ONE signal (a singlet) in its 1H NMR spectrum. Which structure is consistent with this observation?","q_smiles":"ClCCCl","opts":["ClCCCCl","ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClCCCl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl","CC(Cl)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"1,2-Dichloroethane has two identical CH2Cl groups; all four hydrogens are equivalent, and equivalent hydrogens do not split one another, so the spectrum is a single singlet. The other C2H4Cl2 isomer, 1,1-dichloroethane, has a CH3 doublet and a CHCl2 quartet (two signals). 1,1,2-Trichloroethane, 1,1,2,2-tetrachloroethane, and 1,3-dichloropropane all have different molecular formulas.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; equivalence-by-symmetry recognition, all SMILES valid, unique answer, in scope."},{"q":"An ether of formula C5H12O shows only TWO 1H NMR signals, BOTH singlets, integrating 9:3. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","COC(C)(C)C","CCCCCO","CC(O)C(C)C","CCOCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"tert-Butyl methyl ether fits. The nine tert-butyl hydrogens are equivalent and have no neighboring hydrogens, so they are one 9H singlet; the OCH3 hydrogens are a 3H singlet; that is the 9:3 pattern. Diethyl ether would show a quartet and a triplet and is C4H10O, the wrong formula. The alcohols 2-methyl-2-butanol, 1-pentanol, and 3-methyl-2-butanol each give more signals, including an O-H, and show coupling.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; valid SMILES (diethyl ether distractor is C4H10O, others C5H12O), unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A compound C3H6O shows only ONE 1H NMR signal: a 6H singlet near 2 ppm, with no signal in the aldehyde or vinyl region. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=O","opts":["CC1CO1","COC=C","C=CCO","CCC=O","CC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acetone fits: its two methyl groups sit on a symmetric carbonyl, so all six hydrogens are one environment with no neighboring hydrogens, giving a single 6H singlet near 2 ppm. Propanal would show an aldehyde hydrogen near 9 to 10 ppm and three signals; propylene oxide, allyl alcohol, and methyl vinyl ether each give several signals, including ring or vinyl hydrogens. All five are C3H6O isomers, so the spectrum, not the formula, decides.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; all five valid C3H6O isomers, unique answer at index 0, recognition-level, in scope."},{"q":"An aromatic diether of formula C8H10O2 shows only TWO 1H NMR signals (a 6H singlet and a 4H singlet). Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"COc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["COc1ccc(OC)cc1","CCOc1ccccc1","COc1cccc(OC)c1","COc1ccccc1OC","COc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"1,4-Dimethoxybenzene has the required symmetry: both OCH3 groups are equivalent (6H singlet) and all four ring hydrogens are equivalent (4H singlet), giving exactly two signals. 1,2-Dimethoxybenzene gives three signals and 1,3-dimethoxybenzene gives four, because their ring hydrogens fall into more than one set. Ethyl phenyl ether (C8H10O) and anisole (C7H8O) have the wrong formula and would show more signals.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"No issues; valid SMILES, isomer symmetry (regio) + wrong-formula distractors, unique answer at index 0, in scope."},{"q":"A ketone of formula C5H10O shows only THREE signals in its 13C NMR spectrum. Its 1H NMR spectrum shows just two signals: a 6H triplet and a 4H quartet. Which structure is consistent with BOTH spectra?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["CCCC(C)=O","O=CC(C)(C)C","CC(C)C(C)=O","CCC(=O)CC","O=C1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"3-Pentanone (diethyl ketone) is the symmetric answer: it has only three carbon environments (CH3, CH2, C=O), and in the proton spectrum the two equivalent CH3 groups appear as a 6H triplet while the two equivalent CH2 groups appear as a 4H quartet. 2-Pentanone has five carbon environments and 3-methyl-2-butanone has four, so both fail the carbon count. 2,2-Dimethylpropanal also has three carbon environments, but its proton spectrum is a 9H singlet plus an aldehyde singlet, not a triplet and a quartet. Cyclopentanone is C5H8O, the wrong formula, and its ring CH2 groups would not give a triplet and quartet either.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Sound difficulty-3 item; RDKit confirms 13C/1H environment counts (3-pentanone 3/2, 2-pentanone 5/4, 3-Me-2-butanone 4/3, cyclopentanone 3/2, pivaldehyde 3/2). Two-spectrum filter leaves index 0 uniquely. Minor: cyclopentanone is C5H8O so already excluded by the stated formula; explanation slightly imprecise but answer unique and correct. In scope (counting, no J math)."},{"q":"How many signals appear in the 1H NMR spectrum of the molecule shown (p-xylene)?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["1 signal","6 signals","4 signals","3 signals","2 signals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"p-Xylene is highly symmetric. The two CH3 groups are equivalent (one 6H singlet) and all four aromatic H are equivalent by the molecule's mirror symmetry (one 4H singlet), giving 2 distinct 1H signals total.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. p-Xylene has 2 equivalent CH3 and 4 equivalent aromatic H = 2 signals. correct=0 confirmed. Recognition-level, single concept (symmetry/signal counting)."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the compound shown (ethyl acetate) shows three sets of peaks. Reading them as: methyl of the ethyl group, the acetyl methyl, and the OCH2, which multiplicity pattern is correct?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["singlet (3H), singlet (3H), singlet (2H)","doublet (3H), singlet (3H), triplet (2H)","quartet (3H), singlet (3H), triplet (2H)","triplet (3H), singlet (3H), quartet (2H)","triplet (3H), doublet (3H), quartet (2H)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"The CH3 of the ethyl group couples to the adjacent CH2 (n+1 = 3, triplet). The acetyl CH3 has no neighboring H, so it is a singlet. The OCH2 couples to the adjacent CH3 (n+1 = 4, quartet). Integration ratio 3:3:2.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. Ethyl CH3=triplet, acetyl CH3=singlet (no neighbors), OCH2=quartet. correct=0 confirmed. Classic n+1 multiplicity question, in scope."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the molecule shown (1,4-dimethoxybenzene) consists of exactly two singlets. What is the integration ratio of those singlets (aromatic H : OCH3 H)?","q_smiles":"COc1ccc(OC)cc1","opts":["1 : 1","4 : 3","1 : 3","2 : 3","3 : 2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"By para symmetry the two OCH3 groups are equivalent (6H, singlet, no neighbors) and all four aromatic H are equivalent (4H, singlet). The ratio is 4H : 6H, which reduces to 2 : 3.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 4 aromatic H : 6 OCH3 H = 4:6 = 2:3. correct=0 confirmed. Integration ratio recognition, in scope."},{"q":"How many signals appear in the proton-decoupled 13C NMR spectrum of the molecule shown (neopentane, 2,2-dimethylpropane)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)C","opts":["2 signals","1 signal","3 signals","5 signals","4 signals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Neopentane has one central quaternary carbon and four equivalent CH3 carbons. By symmetry there are only 2 chemically distinct carbon environments, so the 13C spectrum shows 2 signals (the 1H spectrum, by contrast, shows just 1 singlet).","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. Neopentane: 1 quaternary C + 4 equivalent CH3 C = 2 distinct carbon environments. correct=0 confirmed. 13C signal counting, in scope."},{"q":"The 1H NMR of the molecule shown (1,2-dichloroethane) is a single sharp singlet. Which statement best explains this?","q_smiles":"ClCCCl","opts":["Rapid rotation averages two non-equivalent doublets into one peak","Chlorine quenches all proton coupling, collapsing the peaks to one singlet","The protons are equivalent but should still split each other into a triplet","The two CH2 groups are different, but their signals overlap by coincidence","All four protons are equivalent by symmetry, so there is no observable coupling and one signal appears"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Both CH2 groups bear one Cl and are related by the molecule's symmetry, so all four protons are chemically equivalent. Equivalent protons do not split one another, so a single singlet is observed (and the 13C shows one signal).","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. All 4 H of ClCH2CH2Cl are equivalent by symmetry; equivalent protons do not split each other = singlet. correct=0 confirmed. Conceptual equivalence question, in scope."},{"q":"For the molecule shown (1-bromopropane), how many 1H NMR signals appear and what is the multiplicity of the central CH2 group?","q_smiles":"CCCBr","opts":["3 signals; central CH2 is a triplet","3 signals; central CH2 is a quintet","2 signals; central CH2 is a quartet","4 signals; central CH2 is a sextet","3 signals; central CH2 is a sextet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"1-Bromopropane has three distinct proton environments: CH3, central CH2, and CH2Br (3 signals). The central CH2 is flanked by the CH3 (3H) and the CH2Br (2H); treating those 5 neighbors together gives n+1 = 6, a sextet.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 1-Bromopropane: 3 environments; central CH2 has 5 neighboring H (3+2), n+1=6 sextet (first-order textbook treatment). correct=0 confirmed. Two-concept (signal count + multiplicity), in scope."},{"q":"A student records 1H NMR for the molecule shown (3-pentanone, diethyl ketone) and for acetone (CH3COCH3) drawn as the contrast. Which statement correctly compares their proton spectra?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)CC","opts":["Both show a single singlet because each ketone is symmetric","3-Pentanone shows 1 singlet; acetone shows 2 signals","3-Pentanone shows 3 signals; acetone shows 2 signals","3-Pentanone shows 2 signals (quartet + triplet); acetone shows 1 singlet","Both show 2 signals, each a quartet and a triplet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"3-Pentanone is symmetric, so its two ethyl groups are equivalent: the CH2 (coupled to CH3) is a quartet and the CH3 (coupled to CH2) is a triplet, giving 2 signals. Acetone has two equivalent CH3 groups with no neighboring H, giving a single singlet.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. 3-Pentanone (symmetric): 2 signals, quartet CH2 + triplet CH3; acetone: 1 singlet. correct=0 confirmed. Comparative symmetry/multiplicity, in scope."},{"q":"An unknown gives a 1H NMR with three signals: a 3H singlet near 2.5 ppm and two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (~7.4 and ~8.1 ppm). Of the two candidate structures, the one drawn is p-nitrotoluene; the contrast candidate is tert-butanol (CC(C)(C)O). Which structure is consistent with the spectrum?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["Neither, because p-nitrotoluene would show four aromatic signals","tert-Butanol, because it produces two aromatic doublets","tert-Butanol, because its 9H singlet matches the 3H methyl peak","The drawn structure (p-nitrotoluene): CH3 singlet plus two aromatic doublets from the para ring","The drawn structure, but it should show a 9H singlet not a 3H singlet"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"p-Nitrotoluene has a benzylic CH3 (3H singlet, ~2.5 ppm) and a para-disubstituted ring giving an AA'BB' pattern that reads as two 2H doublets in the aromatic region (the H ortho to NO2 shifted downfield ~8.1 ppm). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H and would show only a 9H singlet plus OH, so it cannot match.","difficulty":3,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid. p-Nitrotoluene: 3H benzylic CH3 singlet + para-ring AA'BB' (read as two 2H doublets). tert-Butanol has no aromatic H so cannot match. correct=0 confirmed. Structure-from-spectrum matching, qualitative (no shift math), in scope."},{"q":"A colorless aromatic liquid (C8H10) shows only TWO signals in its 1H NMR spectrum: one singlet near 2.3 ppm and one singlet near 7.0 ppm, with an integration ratio of 3:2. Its 13C NMR shows exactly three signals. Which structure is consistent with this data?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccc(C)cc1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"p-Xylene's two mirror planes make all 6 methyl H equivalent and all 4 ring H equivalent (6:4 = 3:2), with only 3 distinct carbons. meta-Xylene gives 4 1H/5 13C signals, ortho gives 3 1H/4 13C, ethylbenzene breaks the 3:2 integration, and toluene is C7H8. Symmetry is the discriminator.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All 5 SMILES valid; RDKit confirms para-xylene is the only option with 3 carbons and a 3:2 (6H:4H) two-signal pattern. q_smiles was empty in source; set to the keyed substrate (p-xylene) so the molecule is drawn. In scope (recognition-level symmetry/integration), one defensible answer."},{"q":"An unknown chlorinated compound (C2H3Cl3) shows TWO 1H NMR signals: a doublet integrating for 2H near 4.0 ppm and a triplet integrating for 1H near 5.8 ppm. Which structure produces this pattern?","q_smiles":"ClCC(Cl)Cl","opts":["ClCC(Cl)Cl","ClC(Cl)C(Cl)Cl","CC(Cl)(Cl)Cl","CCCl","ClCCCl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"1,1,2-Trichloroethane fits: the CH2Cl hydrogens see one neighboring hydrogen and appear as a 2H doublet near 4.0 ppm, and the CHCl2 hydrogen sees two neighbors and appears as a 1H triplet near 5.8 ppm, far downfield because of its two chlorines. 1,1,1-Trichloroethane has the same formula but gives a single 3H singlet. 1,1,2,2-Tetrachloroethane, 1,2-dichloroethane (a 4H singlet), and chloroethane have different formulas.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid. Strong distractor [1] is the isomeric 1,1,1-trichloroethane (same formula). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope and unambiguous."},{"q":"An ether (C5H12O) shows just TWO singlets in its 1H NMR: one integrating for 3H near 3.2 ppm and one integrating for 9H near 1.2 ppm. The 13C NMR shows three signals. No signal is split. Which structure fits?","q_smiles":"COC(C)(C)C","opts":["COC(C)(C)C","CCOC(C)C","CCOCC","CC(C)OC(C)C","CCCOC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Two singlets (no coupling) at 3H:9H = OCH3 plus C(CH3)3 with no H-bearing neighbors = tert-butyl methyl ether (MTBE), C5H12O, 3 carbons. Diethyl ether (C4H10O) and the propyl/isopropyl ethers all have CH2/CH groups adjacent to other H, producing triplets/quartets/septets. Absence of coupling is the key clue.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only MTBE gives two singlets with 3 carbons. Distractors include correct C5H12O isomer (isopropyl ethyl ether) that would couple. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A monosubstituted benzene (C9H12) shows in its 1H NMR: a doublet integrating for 6H near 1.2 ppm, a septet integrating for 1H near 2.9 ppm, and aromatic signals (5H) near 7.2 ppm. Which structure is consistent?","q_smiles":"CC(C)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCCc1ccccc1","CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(C)(C)c1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1","CCc1ccccc1CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A 6H doublet + 1H septet is the isopropyl signature (two equivalent CH3 coupled to one CH). Isopropylbenzene (cumene), C9H12, fits with 5 aromatic H. Propylbenzene gives triplet/sextet/triplet; tert-butylbenzene gives a 9H singlet (and is C10H14); ethylbenzene is C8H10; diethylbenzene is C10H14 and disubstituted (4 ArH).","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid. Aromatic 5H is realistically one grouped multiplet (formally 3 environments 2+2+1) but the discriminating 6H-doublet/1H-septet is unambiguous; stem wording 'aromatic signals (5H)' avoids over-claiming a singlet. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"An ester (C4H8O2) gives THREE 1H NMR signals: a quartet (2H) near 4.1 ppm, a singlet (3H) near 2.0 ppm, and a triplet (3H) near 1.2 ppm. Which structure matches this set of multiplicities and integrations?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["COC(=O)CC","CC(=O)OC(C)C","CCOC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC","COC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Triplet(3H)+quartet(2H) is an isolated ethyl; the 3H SINGLET is an acetyl CH3 on C=O with no neighboring H = ethyl acetate, CH3C(=O)OCH2CH3. The OCH2 is deshielded (~4.1, quartet). Methyl propanoate (same C4H8O2) would show a 3H OCH3 singlet plus a triplet/quartet acyl ethyl at different shifts; isopropyl acetate gives a septet/doublet; methyl acetate has only two singlets; ethyl propanoate is C5.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; strong distractor [1] is isomeric methyl propanoate (same formula). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope and unambiguous; the acetyl-CH3 singlet at ~2.0 vs the ethyl pattern cleanly excludes the isomer."},{"q":"An aromatic hydrocarbon (C9H12) shows only TWO 1H NMR signals: a singlet near 2.3 ppm (9H) and a singlet near 6.8 ppm (3H). Its 13C NMR shows exactly three signals. Which structure has this much symmetry?","q_smiles":"Cc1cc(C)cc(C)c1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","Cc1cc(C)cc(C)c1","Cc1ccc(C)cc1","Cc1cccc(C)c1","CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A 9H methyl singlet and a 3H aromatic singlet (3:1) with only 3 carbons requires threefold symmetry = 1,3,5-trimethylbenzene (mesitylene). Each ring H sits between two methyl-bearing carbons with no adjacent ring H, so it is a singlet; all 9 methyl H and all 3 ring H are equivalent. p-Xylene/o-xylene are C8H10 (6 methyl H), cumene's CH3 is a doublet, m-xylene lacks threefold symmetry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only mesitylene gives 3 carbons with a 3:1 (9H:3H) two-singlet pattern. Aromatic-H singlet claim is chemically correct (isolated ring H). q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A cyclic ether (C4H8O2) shows a SINGLE 1H NMR singlet near 3.7 ppm and a SINGLE 13C NMR signal. Which structure has all eight hydrogens (and both kinds of carbon) equivalent?","q_smiles":"C1COCCO1","opts":["C1CCOCC1","C1COCO1","CCOCC","C1COCCO1","C1CCOC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"One 1H singlet and one 13C signal means every CH2 is equivalent with no chemically distinct neighbor. 1,4-Dioxane has all four CH2 equivalent (each flanked by O and an equivalent CH2), giving one carbon and one proton singlet. Oxane (C5H10O) and THF (C4H8O) have several inequivalent CH2; 1,3-dioxolane (C3H6O2) has two CH2 environments; diethyl ether is acyclic C4H10O.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only 1,4-dioxane has 1 carbon environment and a single 8H singlet. Stem 'both kinds of carbon' is slightly loose (only one carbon environment), but the single-13C-signal criterion plus structures make the answer unambiguous. q_smiles set to keyed substrate. In scope."},{"q":"A brominated compound (C3H7Br) shows TWO 1H NMR signals: a doublet integrating for 6H near 1.7 ppm and a septet integrating for 1H near 4.2 ppm. Which structure is consistent with this spectrum?","q_smiles":"CC(C)Br","opts":["CCCCBr","CCBr","CC(C)Br","CCCBr","CC(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Two signals from C3H7Br with a 6H doublet and a 1H septet = 2-bromopropane: two equivalent CH3 coupled to the single CHBr (doublet), and the CHBr split by 6 neighbors into a septet, deshielded to ~4.2 by Br. 1-Bromopropane gives THREE signals (triplet/sextet/triplet); 1-bromobutane and isobutyl bromide are C4H9Br; bromoethane is C2H5Br.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-nmr","issue":"Correct as keyed. All SMILES valid; only 2-bromopropane gives a two-signal 6H-doublet/1H-septet pattern. Difficulty is overcalibrated at 3 (this is the same isopropyl doublet/septet recognition as Q4 cumene, marked 2); functionally a difficulty-2 item, but chemistry/keep are unaffected. q_smiles set to keyed substrate."},{"q":"A monohalogenated aromatic compound shows molecular-ion peaks at m/z 112 (M+) and m/z 114 (M+2) in an intensity ratio of about 3:1. Which structure is consistent with this mass spectrum?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["Clc1ccccc1","Ic1ccccc1","Clc1ccc(Cl)cc1","Brc1ccccc1","c1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"A single chlorine gives M:M+2 of about 3:1 because 35Cl and 37Cl occur in roughly a 3:1 natural ratio. The M+ at 112 matches C6H5Cl (chlorobenzene). One Br would give about 1:1 (and M+ at ~156), two Cl would give a 9:6:1 pattern, benzene and iodobenzene show no significant M+2.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: chlorobenzene C6H5Cl nominal mass 112, 3:1 M/M+2 signature of one Cl. All five option SMILES valid; only index 0 fits both the 112 mass and 3:1 ratio. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon has the molecular formula C6H10, corresponding to two degrees of unsaturation (one ring plus one C=C, or any equivalent combination). Which structure is consistent with this formula and degree of unsaturation?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1CCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","CCCCCCC","CCCCCC"],"correct":1,"why":"Degrees of unsaturation = (2C+2-H)/2 = (12+2-10)/2 = 2. Cyclohexene (C6H10) has one ring and one double bond = 2. Cyclohexane (C6H12) and methylcyclopentane (C6H12) have 1; hexane (C6H14) has 0; heptane (C7H16) is the wrong formula with 0.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: cyclohexene C6H10 DoU=2 (confirmed by RDKit formula). Only index 0 has formula C6H10. Distractors are C6H12 (DoU 1), C6H14, C7H16. One defensible answer, in scope, visual."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn below) shows a molecular ion at m/z 106, a prominent peak at m/z 105 (M-1), and a strong peak at m/z 77. The m/z 77 fragment arises from loss of a neutral fragment of mass 29 (CHO). What is the identity of the m/z 77 ion?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The acylium ion CH3CO+","The methyl cation, CH3+","The tropylium ion, C7H7+","The phenyl cation, C6H5+ (loss of CHO, mass 29, from M+ 106)","The benzoyl cation, C6H5CO+ (this is the m/z 105 ion, not 77)"],"correct":3,"why":"M+ is 106 (C7H6O). Loss of H gives benzoyl C6H5CO+ at 105 (M-1). Loss of the whole CHO group (mass 29) from M+ gives the phenyl cation C6H5+ at 106-29 = 77. Tropylium (91) and the acetyl/methyl cations come from other skeletons.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 106-29=77 = phenyl cation C6H5+. Stem q_smiles benzaldehyde valid. Only index 0 is the 77 ion. Minor: option B parenthetical is slightly didactic but does not create ambiguity. One defensible answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"Bromoethane is shown below. In its mass spectrum, what is the approximate relative intensity of the molecular ion M+ (m/z 108) to the M+2 isotope peak (m/z 110)?","q_smiles":"CCBr","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["About 1:1, because 79Br and 81Br occur in nearly equal abundance","About 9:6:1, characteristic of two halogens","No M+2 peak is observed","About 100:1, the natural 13C contribution only","About 3:1, characteristic of one chlorine"],"correct":0,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes, 79Br and 81Br, in roughly a 1:1 ratio, so a compound with one bromine shows M and M+2 of nearly equal intensity. The 3:1 pattern is the signature of one chlorine.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: bromoethane C2H5Br, one Br gives ~1:1 M/M+2. Note the stem labels M+ as m/z 108 while CH3CH2-79Br is nominally 108 (correct: 24+5+79=108). q_smiles valid. Only index 0 correct. In scope, visual."},{"q":"Acetophenone (drawn below, M+ = 120) undergoes alpha cleavage at the carbonyl, losing a methyl radical (mass 15) to give a resonance-stabilized acylium ion as the base peak at m/z 105. Which structure represents that m/z 105 fragment ion?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH2+]c1ccccc1","[CH3+]","C[C+]=O","[C+]1=CC=CC=C1","O=[C+]c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Alpha cleavage of acetophenone (C8H8O, 120) loses the methyl (15) to give the benzoyl acylium ion C6H5CO+ at 105, stabilized by resonance with oxygen and the ring. The acetyl cation CH3CO+ (43) comes from losing the phenyl instead; the phenyl (77), benzyl/tropylium (91), and methyl (15) cations are not the m/z 105 species.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: 120-15=105; keyed index 0 O=[C+]c1ccccc1 = benzoyl acylium C7H5O+ exact mass 105.03. All five cation SMILES valid (masses 105/43/77/91/15). Only index 0 is m/z 105. Two-concept (alpha cleavage + structure ID), in scope, visual."},{"q":"How many degrees of unsaturation are present in benzaldehyde, C7H6O (drawn below)? Remember that oxygen does not affect the count.","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3","5 (four from the benzene ring plus one from the C=O)","7","4 (the benzene ring only)","6"],"correct":1,"why":"Degrees of unsaturation = (2C+2+N-H-X)/2 with oxygen ignored = (14+2-6)/2 = 5. The aromatic ring accounts for 4 (three C=C plus the ring) and the aldehyde C=O adds one more, for a total of 5.","difficulty":1,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: C7H6O DoU = (14+2-6)/2 = 5 (confirmed). q_smiles benzaldehyde valid. Only index 0 = 5. One defensible answer, in scope, molecule drawn."},{"q":"A compound's mass spectrum shows a three-line molecular-ion cluster at M : M+2 : M+4 in an intensity ratio of about 1:2:1. Which structure produces this isotope pattern?","q_smiles":"BrCCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCl","ClCCCl","CCBr","ClCCBr","BrCCBr"],"correct":4,"why":"Two bromine atoms (each ~1:1 in 79Br/81Br) give a 1:2:1 triplet for M : M+2 : M+4. One Br gives 1:1; two Cl give 9:6:1; one Cl gives 3:1; one Br plus one Cl gives an intermediate, non-1:2:1 pattern.","difficulty":2,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: two Br (BrCCBr) gives 1:2:1 M:M+2:M+4. Two Cl would be 9:6:1, Br+Cl ~ 3:4:1 (not 1:2:1). All option SMILES valid. Only index 0 fits 1:2:1. In scope, visual."},{"q":"An unknown shows a molecular-ion cluster at m/z 120 and 122 in an intensity ratio of about 1:1, and combustion/formula analysis indicates exactly one degree of unsaturation. Which structure is consistent with BOTH observations?","q_smiles":"C=CCBr","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCBr","CCCCBr","CCCBr","CC(C)Br","BrCCCBr"],"correct":0,"why":"The 1:1 M/M+2 ratio means exactly one bromine (rules out the dibromide, which would be 1:2:1). One degree of unsaturation means one ring or pi bond. Allyl bromide (C3H5Br) has one bromine and one C=C (DoU = 1). 1-bromopropane and 2-bromopropane (C3H7Br) and 1-bromobutane (C4H9Br) are saturated (DoU = 0), and the dibromide has two Br.","difficulty":3,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Verified: allyl bromide C3H5Br nominal mass 120 (36+5+79), one Br -> 1:1, DoU=1. Only index 0 satisfies BOTH constraints (others are saturated mono-Br at 122/136 or the 1:2:1 dibromide). All SMILES valid. Two layered concepts (isotope ratio + DoU), in scope, visual."},{"q":"The mass spectrum of the compound shown displays two molecular-ion peaks of roughly equal intensity (1:1) at m/z 136 and 138. Which structural feature of the molecule is responsible for this pattern?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["A nitrogen atom giving an odd molecular weight","Loss of an ethyl radical from the molecular ion","The natural abundance of 13C in a four-carbon chain","The presence of one bromine atom (79Br and 81Br are nearly 1:1 in nature)","The presence of one chlorine atom (35Cl and 37Cl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes, 79Br and 81Br, in an approximately 1:1 ratio, so a single Br produces M and M+2 peaks of nearly equal height. 1-Bromobutane (C4H9Br) gives M+ at 136 (79Br) and 138 (81Br). Chlorine gives a 3:1 M/M+2 pattern instead.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C4H9Br nominal 79Br=136, 81Br=138; Br ~1:1 isotope ratio. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"The molecular ion of this compound appears at m/z 112 with an M+2 peak at m/z 114 that is about one-third (3:1) as intense. The presence of which element best accounts for this M:M+2 ratio?","q_smiles":"Clc1ccccc1","opts":["One sulfur atom","One chlorine atom","One nitrogen atom","One bromine atom","Two oxygen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Chlorine's isotopes 35Cl and 37Cl occur in roughly a 3:1 ratio, giving M:M+2 of about 3:1. Chlorobenzene (C6H5Cl) shows M+ at 112 (35Cl) and M+2 at 114 (37Cl). Bromine would give a ~1:1 M:M+2 pattern.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H5Cl nominal 35Cl=112, 37Cl=114; Cl ~3:1. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"2-Pentanone (shown, M+ = 86) shows a prominent fragment at m/z 58 in its mass spectrum, corresponding to loss of 28 mass units. This fragment arises from which characteristic ketone fragmentation?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["McLafferty rearrangement (loss of a neutral alkene via a gamma-hydrogen)","Alpha-cleavage to give an acylium ion only","Loss of a methyl radical (m/z 71)","Retro-Diels-Alder fragmentation","Loss of water (dehydration)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"2-Pentanone has a gamma-hydrogen, so it undergoes the McLafferty rearrangement, transferring the gamma-H to the carbonyl oxygen and expelling a neutral alkene (ethylene, C2H4, 28 u), giving the m/z 58 enol radical cation. Alpha-cleavage would give acylium ions at m/z 43 or 71, not 58.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: M+=86, loss of 28 (ethylene) = 58 via McLafferty; substrate has gamma-H. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"The compound shown (C7H7Br) gives a strong, very stable fragment at m/z 91 in its mass spectrum after loss of the bromine atom. The unusual stability of this m/z 91 cation is best explained by which structure?","q_smiles":"BrCc1ccccc1","opts":["An acylium ion","A resonance-stabilized tropylium (seven-membered aromatic) cation","A bromonium ion","A protonated benzene ring","A primary benzyl carbocation with no resonance stabilization"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Benzyl bromide loses Br to form C7H7+ (m/z 91), which rearranges to the aromatic, highly resonance-stabilized tropylium cation. This is why m/z 91 is a hallmark fragment for benzyl-type compounds.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C7H7Br loss of Br (-79) gives C7H7+ = 91; classic tropylium. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Styrene is shown below. Without any spectra, how many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does its molecular formula C8H8 correspond to?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["6","8","5","4","3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (16 + 2 - 8)/2 = 5. This accounts for the benzene ring (3 pi bonds + 1 ring = 4) plus the vinyl C=C (1), totaling 5.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C8H8 DoU = 5. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is shown (C6H10O, M+ = 98). Its molecular formula gives 2 degrees of unsaturation. Which combination of structural features accounts for exactly those 2 degrees?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Two rings","One C=O plus one C=C","Two C=C double bonds","One ring plus one C=O pi bond","One ring plus one C=C double bond"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"DoU = (2*6 + 2 - 10)/2 = 2. Oxygen does not enter the formula. Those 2 units come from the six-membered ring (1) and the carbonyl C=O pi bond (1).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H10O DoU=2, M+=98; ring + C=O. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Aniline is shown (C6H7N). Its molecular ion appears at an odd m/z of 93. According to the nitrogen rule, what does this odd nominal molecular mass indicate?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["The molecule contains a halogen","The molecular ion is actually an M+2 isotope peak","The molecule has zero degrees of unsaturation","The molecule contains an even number of nitrogen atoms","The molecule contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"By the nitrogen rule, a neutral organic molecule with an odd nominal molecular mass contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms. Aniline has one N, giving the odd M+ at m/z 93.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C6H7N nominal = 93 (odd), one N. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"Acetophenone is shown (C8H8O, M+ = 120). Its base peak appears at m/z 105 (loss of 15). Identify the fragmentation that produces m/z 105 and the structure of that cation.","q_smiles":"CC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Loss of a methyl radical to give a tropylium ion at m/z 91","Loss of water to give a styrene radical cation","McLafferty rearrangement losing ethylene to give m/z 105","Alpha-cleavage losing a methyl radical to give the resonance-stabilized benzoyl (C6H5CO+) acylium ion","Loss of CO to give the phenyl cation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Alpha-cleavage of the bond between the carbonyl carbon and the methyl group ejects a methyl radical (15 u), giving the benzoyl acylium cation C6H5C=O+ at m/z 105 (resonance-stabilized by the ring and the O lone pair). Acetophenone has no gamma-H, so McLafferty is not available.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Verified: C8H8O M+=120, loss of CH3 (-15) = 105 = benzoyl C7H5O+; no gamma-H so McLafferty distractor correctly wrong. SMILES valid. Index 0 correct. In scope."},{"q":"1-Bromobutane (drawn) is analyzed by EI mass spectrometry. Which pair of peaks best represents its molecular ion region?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["m/z 134 and 136 in roughly 3:1 ratio","m/z 136 and 138 in roughly 1:1 ratio","A single peak at m/z 136 with no M+2","m/z 136 and 137 in roughly 1:1 ratio","m/z 136 and 138 in roughly 3:1 ratio"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Bromine has two abundant isotopes (79Br and 81Br) in nearly 1:1 abundance, so a monobrominated compound shows M+ and M+2 of about equal height. C4H9(79Br) = 136, C4H9(81Br) = 138. The 3:1 ratio is the chlorine signature.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. CCCCBr valid (C4H9Br); M+136/M+2 138 ~1:1 is the Br isotope signature; only option 0 fits. In scope, well-formed."},{"q":"Chlorocyclohexane (drawn) gives an EI mass spectrum. Which statement about its molecular ion cluster is correct?","q_smiles":"ClC1CCCCC1","opts":["M+ at 118 and M+2 at 120 appear in about a 3:1 ratio","M+ at 118 and M+1 at 119 appear in about a 3:1 ratio","M+ at 120 and M+2 at 118 appear in about a 1:1 ratio","Only a single molecular ion at 118 is observed","M+ at 118 and M+2 at 120 appear in about a 1:1 ratio"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Chlorine isotopes 35Cl and 37Cl occur in about 3:1 abundance, so a monochloro compound shows M+ : M+2 of roughly 3:1. C6H11(35Cl) = 118 and C6H11(37Cl) = 120. The 1:1 ratio would indicate bromine.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. ClC1CCCCC1 valid (C6H11Cl); M+118/M+2 120 in 3:1 is the Cl signature; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"2-Pentanone (drawn) fragments in the mass spectrometer primarily by alpha-cleavage (loss of an alkyl radical next to the carbonyl) to give acylium ions. Which two acylium m/z values are produced from the two possible alpha-cleavages?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)=O","opts":["m/z 58 (M+2) and m/z 43 (CH3CO+)","m/z 43 (CH3CO+) and m/z 57 (CH3CH2CO+)","m/z 15 (CH3+) and m/z 86 (M+)","m/z 29 (CHO+) and m/z 71 (CH3CH2CH2CO+)","m/z 43 (CH3CO+) and m/z 71 (CH3CH2CH2CO+)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Alpha-cleavage of a ketone breaks a C-C bond next to the carbonyl, leaving a resonance-stabilized acylium ion (R-C≡O+). Cleaving off the methyl leaves CH3CH2CH2CO+ = 71; cleaving off the propyl leaves CH3CO+ = 43.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. CCCC(C)=O = 2-pentanone (C5H10O). Two alpha-cleavages give acylium 43 and 71; only option 0 fits both. In scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) has M+ = 106. Its spectrum shows strong fragments at m/z 105 and m/z 77. Which losses produce these two fragments?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["m/z 105 = M+2 isotope peak; m/z 77 = loss of NO","m/z 105 = loss of CH3; m/z 77 = loss of CHO (giving C6H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of H (giving C6H5CO+); m/z 77 = loss of CHO (giving C6H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of CHO; m/z 77 = loss of CO (giving C5H5+)","m/z 105 = loss of OH; m/z 77 = loss of CH2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"From M+ = 106, loss of an H atom gives the benzoyl acylium C6H5CO+ at 105. Loss of CHO (29) from M+ gives the stable phenyl cation C6H5+ at 77, a hallmark of monosubstituted benzene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. O=Cc1ccccc1 valid (C7H6O, M+106). 106-1=105 benzoyl, 106-29=77 phenyl; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"An unknown hydrocarbon has the molecular formula C6H10 (one such isomer is drawn). How many degrees of unsaturation (index of hydrogen deficiency) does this formula require?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts":["0","4","3","2","1"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (12 + 2 - 10)/2 = 2. Two degrees can be two pi bonds, one ring plus one pi bond (cyclohexene shown), or two rings.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. C1CCC=CC1 = cyclohexene (C6H10) matches formula and has DoU 2; answer 0 is right. In scope."},{"q":"A nitrogen-containing compound has the molecular formula C5H11N (one isomer is drawn). Using the degrees-of-unsaturation formula, how many degrees of unsaturation does it have?","q_smiles":"C1CCNC1","opts":["1","0","2","Cannot be determined without the mass spectrum","3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"DoU = (2C + 2 + N - H - X)/2 = (10 + 2 + 1 - 11)/2 = 1. Nitrogen ADDS to the numerator (+1 each). The single degree is a ring or an imine.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Answer 1 is correct for C5H11N. FLAW: drawn SMILES C1CCNC1 is pyrrolidine = C4H9N, which does NOT match the stated C5H11N (a C5 isomer would be N-methyl- or methylpyrrolidine). Pyrrolidine still has DoU 1 so the keyed answer is unaffected and unambiguous; kept, but the drawing should be a true C5H11N isomer."},{"q":"Bromobenzene (drawn) is run by EI-MS. Which set of features is consistent with its molecular ion region and a major fragment?","q_smiles":"Brc1ccccc1","opts":["M+ = 156 and M+1 = 157 in ~1:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 91","A single molecular ion at m/z 156 with loss of HBr to give m/z 75","M+ = 158 and M+2 = 156 in ~3:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 51","M+ = 156 and M+2 = 158 in ~1:1 ratio; loss of Br gives a strong peak at m/z 77 (C6H5+)","M+ = 156 and M+2 = 158 in ~3:1 ratio; loss of Br gives m/z 77"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Bromine 79Br/81Br is ~1:1, so M+ (156) and M+2 (158) are about equal. Loss of Br from M+ leaves the stable phenyl cation C6H5+ at 77, characteristic of a monosubstituted benzene. The 3:1 ratio is the chlorine signature.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct. Brc1ccccc1 valid (C6H5Br); M+156/M+2 158 ~1:1, loss of Br -> 77; only option 0 fits. In scope."},{"q":"A neutral compound has a molecular ion at M+ = 96 and contains only C and H, with the formula C7H12. Which drawn structure is consistent with BOTH its degrees of unsaturation AND the C7H12 formula? (Reminder: C7H12 requires DoU = 2.)","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["Cc1ccccc1","C1CC2CCCC2C1","C=C1CCCCC1C","C1CCCCCC1","CC1=CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"For C7H12 the degrees of unsaturation are (2 x 7 + 2 - 12)/2 = 2. 1-Methylcyclohexene matches: one ring plus one double bond, and its formula is C7H12 (molecular ion 96). Toluene is C7H8 with four degrees of unsaturation, cycloheptane is C7H14 with one, and both 1-methyl-2-methylenecyclohexane and the fused bicyclo[3.3.0]octane are C8H14, off formula even though each has two degrees of unsaturation.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"spectroscopy-ms-dou","issue":"Correct, answer 0 (CC1=CCCCC1 = C7H12, DoU 2; only one matching). All 5 option SMILES valid (q8b C7H8, q8c C7H14, q8d C8H14, q8e C8H14). FIXES applied: (1) original stem said 'contains only C,H,and O' then gave a pure-hydrocarbon C7H12 formula - contradictory; corrected to 'only C and H.' (2) original why mislabeled the fused bicyclic as 'bicycloheptane' - it is C8 bicyclo[3.3.0]octane; corrected. (3) q_smiles was empty; set to the correct-answer structure CC1=CCCCC1 so the item is visual. Answer unambiguous; kept."},{"q":"The alkene shown is named by IUPAC as 1-methylcyclohex-1-ene. It is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","CC1(Cl)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","BrCC1=CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"HBr without peroxides adds by Markovnikov's rule. The proton goes to the CH end of the double bond, forming the more stable tertiary carbocation on the methyl-bearing carbon, and bromide bonds there to give 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane, a tertiary bromide. 1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane would come from the less stable secondary cation, the allylic bromide (1-(bromomethyl)cyclohexene) is a radical-substitution product, the chloride would need HCl, and 1-methylcyclohexanol would need water.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All SMILES valid, Markovnikov tertiary bromide is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Propene (prop-1-ene) is treated with HBr in the presence of organic peroxides (ROOR). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=CCBr","CCCO","CC(Br)CBr","CCCBr","CC(Br)C"],"correct":3,"why":"With peroxides present, HBr adds by a radical chain rather than through a carbocation, so the regiochemistry flips to anti-Markovnikov. A bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 of propene because that leaves the more stable secondary radical on the middle carbon, and that radical then abstracts H from HBr. The major product is 1-bromopropane, with Br on the end carbon. 2-Bromopropane is the Markovnikov product you would get without peroxides; 1,2-dibromopropane would need Br2, 1-propanol would need water, and 3-bromopropene is an allylic substitution product rather than an addition product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, classic anti-Markovnikov, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"But-1-ene (1-butene), shown, is treated with dilute aqueous H2SO4 (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)CO","CCC(O)C","CC(O)C(C)O","CCC(Br)C","CCCCO"],"correct":1,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration follows Markovnikov's rule: protonating the terminal CH2 of 1-butene gives the more stable secondary carbocation at C2, water attacks there, and loss of a proton gives butan-2-ol as the major product. Butan-1-ol is the anti-Markovnikov alcohol that hydroboration-oxidation would give. 2-Bromobutane needs HBr rather than water, and the two diols would require a dihydroxylation reagent such as cold dilute KMnO4 or OsO4.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Minor: rationale's aside lumps oxymercuration with anti-Markovnikov (oxymercuration is actually Markovnikov), but the keyed answer and distractor logic are sound. Valid, in scope."},{"q":"Styrene (the common name for vinylbenzene; IUPAC ethenylbenzene), shown, reacts with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","C=C(Br)c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"HBr adds to styrene by Markovnikov's rule: the proton goes to the terminal CH2 so that the positive charge lands on the benzylic carbon, where it is stabilized by resonance with the ring. Bromide then bonds to that benzylic carbon, giving (1-bromoethyl)benzene. (2-Bromoethyl)benzene is the anti-Markovnikov product and would require peroxides; 1-phenylethanol needs water as the nucleophile; the vinyl bromide and ethylbenzene are not HBr addition products.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, benzylic-cation Markovnikov is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methylbut-1-ene (3-methyl-1-butene), shown, is treated with HCl. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)C(Cl)CCl","CC(C)C(O)C","CC(C)CCCl","CC(C)(Cl)CC","CC(C)C(Cl)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Protonating the terminal carbon of 3-methyl-1-butene gives a secondary carbocation that sits right next to a tertiary C-H. A 1,2-hydride shift moves that hydrogen onto the cationic carbon, so the positive charge relocates to the carbon bearing the two methyls, a more stable tertiary cation; chloride traps it to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane as the major product. 2-Chloro-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged secondary chloride, 1-chloro-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, 3-methyl-2-butanol would need water as the nucleophile, and the dichloride would need Cl2.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Carbocation rearrangement (1,2-hydride shift) is standard DAT depth, single reactive center. Valid, in scope."},{"q":"trans-2-Pentene [(E)-pent-2-ene], shown, is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC/C=C/C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(O)C(O)C","CCC(O)C(=O)C","CCCC(=O)O","CCC(O)CCO","CCC(=O)C(=O)C"],"correct":0,"why":"Cold, dilute, basic KMnO4 adds two OH groups across the double bond in a syn fashion through a cyclic manganate ester, and it stops at the diol. trans-2-Pentene therefore gives pentane-2,3-diol as the major product. Pentane-2,3-dione and butanoic acid are over-oxidation and cleavage products that need hot, concentrated permanganate; the hydroxy ketone is a partial over-oxidation product; and pentane-1,3-diol puts the second OH on a carbon that was never part of the double bond.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, cold dilute KMnO4 syn-diol is right, reagent-condition distinction in scope."},{"q":"But-2-yne (2-butyne), shown, is treated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCCC","CC(=O)CC","C/C=C\\C","C=CCC","C/C=C/C"],"correct":2,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst is palladium poisoned so that it hydrogenates the alkyne but cannot reduce the resulting alkene. Both hydrogens are delivered to the same face of the triple bond (syn addition), which places the two methyl groups on the same side of the new double bond: the product is cis-2-butene, the (Z) isomer. trans-2-Butene is what sodium in liquid ammonia gives, butane would require an unpoisoned catalyst, 1-butene has the double bond in the wrong place, and 2-butanone would require hydration.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Valid SMILES, Lindlar cis-alkene vs trans distractor is right, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is mesityl oxide. By IUPAC functional-group priority, when both a C=C double bond and a ketone C=O are present in the same chain, which is correctly named as the principal characteristic group (the suffix), and what is the parent name?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C(C)/C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both get suffixes equally; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-dione","The alkene takes the suffix; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-ol","The ketone takes the suffix; named as 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one","The ketone is a substituent prefix; named as 2-oxo-4-methylpentene","The alkene takes the suffix; named as 2-oxo-4-methylpent-3-ene"],"correct":2,"why":"In IUPAC seniority, a ketone outranks a C=C double bond, so the C=O takes the suffix '-one' and the alkene is cited as '-en-': 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one (mesityl oxide). The '-ol' option implies an OH (none present) and '-dione' implies two ketones.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; correct=0 confirmed (mesityl oxide = 4-methylpent-3-en-2-one). The /.../ stereo marks are inert because the alkene terminus is geminal-dimethyl (no E/Z), harmless to validity and irrelevant to the priority concept tested."},{"q":"The aromatic diol drawn is a very common reagent. Which row correctly matches its preferred IUPAC name with its accepted common name?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Benzene-1,2-diol = resorcinol","Benzene-1,2-diol = hydroquinone","Benzene-1,3-diol = catechol","Benzene-1,4-diol = catechol","Benzene-1,2-diol = catechol"],"correct":4,"why":"Adjacent (ortho, 1,2) OH groups make this benzene-1,2-diol, common name catechol. Resorcinol is the 1,3 isomer and hydroquinone is the 1,4 isomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid ortho diol; correct=0 confirmed. Distractors mispair isomers/common names cleanly. Exactly one right answer."},{"q":"Consider the molecule drawn, an aldehyde with a C=C double bond. Which IUPAC name correctly applies the functional-group priority and lowest-locant rules?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["3-Methylbut-2-enal","3-Methylbut-2-en-1-ol","3-Methylbut-3-enal","2-Methylbut-3-enal","1-Oxo-3-methylbut-2-ene"],"correct":0,"why":"The CHO carbon is C1 (suffix '-al'); the four-carbon chain has the double bond at C2 and a methyl at C3: 3-methylbut-2-enal (senecialdehyde). No OH ('-ol' wrong), and aldehyde numbering must start at the carbonyl carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; (CH3)2C=CH-CHO is 3-methylbut-2-enal, correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The substrate drawn is 2-bromo-2-methylbutane. Based on its IUPAC name, this alkyl halide is classified at the C-Br carbon as which type, and which single mechanism is it MOST disposed toward in a polar protic solvent with a weak nucleophile?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Tertiary halide; favors SN1/E1","Primary halide; favors SN2","Secondary halide; favors SN2","Tertiary halide; favors SN2","Primary halide; favors E2 only"],"correct":0,"why":"Tertiary halides cannot do SN2 and, with a weak nucleophile in polar protic solvent, proceed by carbocation pathways (SN1/E1).","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; C-Br carbon = tertiary (3 C neighbors), correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is an enone. Identify its correct IUPAC name and the conjugation relationship between its two unsaturated groups (which the name's locants reveal).","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohex-2-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are conjugated (alpha,beta-unsaturated)","Cyclohex-2-en-1-ol; the groups are conjugated","Cyclohex-2-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are isolated (non-conjugated)","Cyclohex-3-en-1-one; the C=C and C=O are isolated (non-conjugated)","Cyclohexanone; there is no C=C present"],"correct":0,"why":"The carbonyl carbon takes locant 1 because the ketone is the principal group, and numbering proceeds toward the double bond so it gets the lowest locant: the alkene starts at C2, giving cyclohex-2-en-1-one. Because the C=C begins on the carbon directly attached to the C=O, the two pi systems overlap continuously: they are conjugated, an alpha,beta-unsaturated ketone. The cyclohex-2-en-1-one name that calls the system isolated contradicts its own locants (a 2-ene next to a 1-one is conjugated by definition); cyclohex-3-en-1-one would be the isolated isomer, cyclohex-2-en-1-ol has the wrong functional group, and the molecule clearly contains a C=C, so plain cyclohexanone is wrong.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid; ring connectivity gives cyclohex-2-en-1-one, conjugated. correct=0 confirmed. Two options share the same name but only one (conjugated) is correct, so exactly one defensible answer."},{"q":"The compound drawn is benzoic acid. Which reagent set would convert TOLUENE (methylbenzene) into this exact product in one named transformation?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["PCC in CH2Cl2 (mild oxidation)","NaBH4, then H3O+ (carbonyl reduction)","H2 / Pd-C (catalytic hydrogenation)","Hot KMnO4, then H3O+ (benzylic oxidation)","Br2 / FeBr3 (electrophilic bromination)"],"correct":3,"why":"Hot, strong KMnO4 oxidizes any benzylic C-H-bearing side chain to -COOH, turning toluene into benzoic acid. H2/Pd-C and NaBH4 are reductions, Br2/FeBr3 is ring substitution, and PCC cannot oxidize an unfunctionalized methyl to an acid.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid benzoic acid; product is drawn in the stem so item is visual. Single named transformation, in scope. correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The molecule drawn is but-3-yn-1-ol. Based on what the IUPAC name tells you about which functional groups are present and where, classify the reaction type that occurs when this molecule is treated with excess NaNH2: which acidic site reacts?","q_smiles":"OCCC#C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acid-base deprotonation; only the internal alkyne carbon is deprotonated","Elimination to form a cumulated diene","Acid-base deprotonation; both the O-H and the terminal alkyne (C-H) are removed","Acid-base deprotonation; only the O-H is removed, never the alkyne","Nucleophilic substitution at the alcohol carbon"],"correct":2,"why":"The name gives a terminal alkyne (C4 is a sp C-H) plus a primary alcohol. Excess NaNH2 (strong base) deprotonates BOTH the O-H and the terminal alkynyl C-H. The alkyne here is terminal, so it has an acidic sp C-H; 'only internal carbon' is impossible.","difficulty":3,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid HC#C-CH2CH2OH. pKa values appear only as illustration in the rationale; the item requires relative-acidity recognition, not pKa math, so it stays in scope. correct=0 confirmed."},{"q":"The compound drawn is 4-aminophenol. Toward electrophilic aromatic substitution (e.g., bromination), both substituents named in this compound are activating ortho/para directors. Compared with benzene itself, how does this disubstituted ring react, and which named groups drive it?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccc(O)cc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Much slower than benzene; the amino group is a deactivator","About the same rate as benzene; the groups cancel out","Much faster than benzene; both the amino (-NH2) and hydroxy (-OH) groups are strong activators","Much slower than benzene; both groups are deactivators","Much faster than benzene; both groups are meta directors"],"correct":2,"why":"-NH2 and -OH are both strong resonance donors, so they activate the ring (o/p directing). Two activators make 4-aminophenol undergo EAS much faster than benzene. They are neither deactivators nor meta directors.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid 4-aminophenol; correct=0 confirmed. Standard activator/director recognition, in scope."},{"q":"The structure below is commonly called o-toluic acid. Which line gives BOTH its correct common ('o-') description and its correct IUPAC name?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1C(=O)O","opts":["The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is 2-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are meta (1,3); IUPAC name is 3-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is benzene-1-carboxylic-2-methyl acid","The methyl and carboxyl are para (1,4); IUPAC name is 4-methylbenzoic acid","The methyl and carboxyl are ortho (1,2); IUPAC name is 2-methylphenol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"The methyl and the carboxyl group sit on adjacent ring carbons, which is the ortho (1,2) relationship the common name o-toluic acid describes. For the IUPAC name, the carboxylic acid is the principal group, so benzoic acid is the parent and its ring carbon is C1; the methyl is then at C2, giving 2-methylbenzoic acid. The meta and para descriptions belong to different isomers, 2-methylphenol uses the wrong parent (a phenol, not an acid), and 'benzene-1-carboxylic-2-methyl acid' is not how IUPAC names are built.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified; SMILES valid; one defensible answer; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Provide the IUPAC name for the molecule drawn below (it bears two carbonyl-type groups).","q_smiles":"OC(=O)CCCC(=O)OC","opts":["methyl 5-oxopentanoate","5-methoxypentanoic acid","methyl 4-carboxybutanoate","6-methoxy-6-oxohexanoic acid","5-methoxy-5-oxopentanoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"The molecule is the monomethyl ester of glutaric (pentanedioic) acid: a free carboxylic acid on one end, a methyl ester on the other, and five carbons in the chain. A carboxylic acid outranks an ester, so the free COOH is the principal group, its carbon is C1, and the parent is pentanoic acid. The ester carbon at the far end (C5) is then expressed as prefixes, methoxy for the OCH3 and oxo for its C=O: 5-methoxy-5-oxopentanoic acid. The 'methyl ...oate' names wrongly treat the ester as the principal group, 'hexanoic' counts six chain carbons when there are only five, and 5-methoxypentanoic acid leaves out the ester's carbonyl oxygen.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified (5-C acid chain + methoxy substituent = C6); SMILES valid; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Which structure correctly represents (E)-3-chloropent-2-ene?","q_smiles":"","opts":["CCC=C(C)Cl","C=CC(Cl)CC","C/C=C(\\Cl)CC","C/C=C(/Cl)CC","CC=CC(C)Cl"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"3-Chloropent-2-ene has a five-carbon chain with the double bond between C2 and C3 and chlorine on C3. To assign E or Z, rank the two groups on each alkene carbon: on C2, CH3 outranks H; on C3, Cl outranks the ethyl group. In the (E) isomer those two higher-priority groups, CH3 and Cl, lie on opposite sides of the double bond, which is the structure with the methyl and the chlorine trans to each other. The structure with CH3 and Cl on the same side is the (Z) isomer. The other three structures are constitutional isomers, not stereoisomers: 2-chloropent-2-ene, 4-chloropent-2-ene and 3-chloropent-1-ene put the chlorine or the double bond in the wrong place.","difficulty":2,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. CIP E confirmed for index 0 via RDKit; option 1 is the Z diastereomer; opts 2-4 are correctly the constitutional isomers claimed. All SMILES valid. q_smiles empty is fine for a structure-selection item.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Which structure is trans-4-methylcyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"","opts":["C[C@H]1CCC[C@@H](C(=O)O)C1","C[C@@H]1CCC[C@@H](C(=O)O)C1","C[C@@H]1CCCC[C@H]1C(=O)O","C[C@H]1CC[C@H](C(=O)O)CC1","C[C@H]1CC[C@@H](C(=O)O)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"trans-4-Methylcyclohexane-1-carboxylic acid needs the methyl and the carboxyl on ring carbons that are 1,4 to each other (directly across the ring) and on opposite faces of the ring. The correct structure shows exactly that: a 1,4-disubstituted cyclohexane with the two groups drawn on opposite faces. The 1,4 structure with both groups on the same face is the cis diastereomer. The other three structures have the right formula but the wrong connectivity: two are 3-methylcyclohexanecarboxylic acids (1,3-disubstituted) and one is 2-methylcyclohexanecarboxylic acid (1,2-disubstituted), so they are not 4-methyl isomers at all.","difficulty":3,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Index 0 confirmed TRANS (50/50 conformers) vs option 1 CIS; opts 2/3 = 1,3 and opt4 = 1,2 patterns confirmed. All SMILES valid. 3D cis/trans recognition is acceptable DAT depth.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Select the correct IUPAC name for the aromatic compound shown (choose the lowest set of locants).","q_smiles":"Cc1cc(C)cc(O)c1","opts":["1,3-dimethyl-5-hydroxybenzene","2,4-dimethylphenol","3,5-dimethylbenzenol","3,5-dimethylphenol","3,5-dimethyl-1-hydroxybenzene"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Phenol is a retained parent name, so the carbon bearing the OH is automatically C1. The two methyls are each meta to the OH (and meta to each other), so they take locants 3 and 5: 3,5-dimethylphenol. 2,4-Dimethylphenol is a different compound with the methyls ortho and para to the OH; 'benzenol' is not an accepted parent name; and the two 'hydroxybenzene' names wrongly demote the OH to a prefix when phenol should be the parent.","difficulty":1,"group":"nomenclature","issue":"No issue. Correct answer index 0 verified; SMILES valid; symmetric 3,5 pattern gives the lowest locant set; distractors plausible-but-wrong.","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Aniline (drawn) is dissolved in diethyl ether and shaken in a separatory funnel with aqueous 1 M HCl. What is the major aniline-derived species present in the aqueous (lower) layer after the extraction?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","[NH3+]c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Aniline is a weak base, and 1 M HCl is more than acidic enough to protonate the amine nitrogen. The product, the anilinium ion (phenyl-NH3+), is a charged salt, so it leaves the ether and dissolves in the aqueous layer. Neutral aniline would stay in the ether; nitrobenzene, acetanilide and phenol are different compounds that HCl does not make from aniline.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, anilinium). All SMILES valid; classic acid/base extraction, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"A diethyl ether solution of benzoic acid (drawn) is shaken with aqueous 1 M NaHCO3. What is the major benzoic-acid-derived species that ends up in the aqueous layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","NC(=O)c1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"Benzoic acid (pKa about 4.2) is a stronger acid than carbonic acid (pKa about 6.4), so bicarbonate deprotonates it. The resulting benzoate anion is an ionic, water-soluble salt and moves into the aqueous layer, while the neutral acid would have stayed in the ether. Benzaldehyde, benzamide and benzyl alcohol are different compounds, not products of an acid-base extraction.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, benzoate). All SMILES valid; NaHCO3 is strong enough for COOH. Single concept, in scope."},{"q":"After an acid/base extraction, the aqueous layer containing protonated aniline (anilinium ion, drawn) is made strongly basic with aqueous NaOH and then re-extracted into fresh ether. What is the major aniline-derived species now recovered in the ether layer?","q_smiles":"[NH3+]c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[O-]c1ccccc1","O=[N+]([O-])c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","[NH3+]c1ccccc1","Nc1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"Adding NaOH removes the proton from the anilinium ion, regenerating neutral aniline. Once uncharged, aniline is far less water-soluble and partitions back into the fresh ether layer; this back-extraction is the standard way to recover an amine after an acid wash. The anilinium ion itself is the charged form that stays in water, and phenoxide, phenol and nitrobenzene are different compounds.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, aniline). All SMILES valid; two-step back-extraction reasoning fits difficulty 2, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of benzoic acid (drawn) and naphthalene in ether is shaken with aqueous NaOH, the layers separated, and the aqueous layer then acidified with HCl. What benzoic-acid-derived compound precipitates/is recovered after acidification?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","c1ccc2ccccc2c1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1"],"correct":4,"why":"NaOH deprotonates benzoic acid to sodium benzoate, which is water-soluble and moves into the aqueous layer while neutral naphthalene stays in the ether; that separates the two. Acidifying the aqueous layer with HCl reprotonates the benzoate, and neutral benzoic acid, which is poorly soluble in water, precipitates and is recovered. The benzoate anion is only the intermediate in the water layer, naphthalene is what stayed behind in the ether, and benzaldehyde and benzyl alcohol would require redox chemistry that never happened.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, benzoic acid). Naphthalene distractor (C) valid. Two-step extract-then-acidify, in scope."},{"q":"Octylamine (drawn) is a component of an organic mixture in ethyl acetate. Which single aqueous wash would selectively remove ONLY this amine into the aqueous layer, leaving neutral and acidic organics behind in the organic layer?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCCN","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Dilute aqueous NaOH","Dilute aqueous NaHCO3","Distilled water only","Saturated aqueous NaCl (brine)","Dilute aqueous HCl"],"correct":4,"why":"Amines are bases, so an acid wash (HCl) protonates octylamine to a water-soluble ammonium salt that leaves with the aqueous layer; NaOH/NaHCO3 target acids, brine just dries, and water alone won't ionize the amine.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, HCl). SMILES valid; reagent-selection text options, molecule drawn. Single defensible answer, in scope."},{"q":"On a silica-gel TLC plate developed in a nonpolar solvent, four compounds are spotted: octanoic acid (carboxylic acid), octylamine (the amine drawn), 1-octanol (alcohol), and octane (alkane). Which compound is expected to travel the FARTHEST up the plate (highest Rf)?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCCN","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["1-Octanol (the alcohol)","Octanoic acid (the carboxylic acid)","Octylamine (the amine)","All four travel equally","Octane (the alkane)"],"correct":4,"why":"On polar silica, the least polar compound interacts least with the stationary phase and moves with the nonpolar mobile phase, giving the highest Rf; nonpolar octane wins over the polar acid, amine, and alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, octane). SMILES valid; TLC polarity/Rf concept, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"Caffeine (drawn) is purified from a crude solid by recrystallization. Which choice best describes the ideal recrystallization solvent for this process?","q_smiles":"Cn1cnc2c1c(=O)n(C)c(=O)n2C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["A solvent that dissolves the compound well at all temperatures","A solvent with a boiling point far above the compound's melting point","A solvent in which the compound is insoluble at every temperature","A solvent that dissolves the compound well when hot but poorly when cold","A solvent that reacts with and chemically modifies the compound"],"correct":3,"why":"Recrystallization relies on a large solubility difference with temperature: hot solvent dissolves the compound, then on cooling the pure compound crystallizes while soluble impurities stay in the mother liquor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Caffeine SMILES valid (canonicalizes to correct skeleton). Recrystallization principle, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of acetone (bp 56 C), water (bp 100 C), and toluene (bp 111 C) is purified by simple distillation (no azeotrope effects assumed). Which component distills over and collects in the receiving flask FIRST?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)C","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Acetone (bp 56 C)","The component with the largest molar mass","Toluene (bp 111 C)","Water and toluene co-distill together first","Water (bp 100 C)"],"correct":0,"why":"In distillation the lowest-boiling component vaporizes most readily and is collected first; acetone at 56 C boils well below water and toluene, so it distills over first.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, acetone). SMILES valid; distillation bp ordering, single answer, in scope."},{"q":"A diethyl ether solution contains a mixture of benzoic acid and naphthalene. Which single aqueous wash will selectively pull the benzoic acid into the aqueous layer while leaving naphthalene in the organic layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Saturated aqueous NaCl (brine)","Distilled water","Aqueous HCl (1 M)","Aqueous Na2SO4","Aqueous NaHCO3 (5%)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Benzoic acid (pKa ~4.2) is deprotonated by NaHCO3 to water-soluble sodium benzoate, moving into the aqueous layer; neutral naphthalene stays in ether. HCl/water/brine/Na2SO4 do not ionize the acid.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; reagent-selection extraction question, single concept, correct key, valid SMILES of substrate drawn."},{"q":"Aniline is dissolved in diethyl ether along with a neutral hydrocarbon impurity. To extract the aniline into the aqueous phase as a water-soluble salt, which reagent should be added during the acid/base extraction?","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts":["Anhydrous MgSO4","Aqueous HCl","Aqueous NaHCO3","Aqueous NaOH","Brine"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Aniline is a base; aqueous HCl protonates the amine to water-soluble anilinium chloride, pulling it into the aqueous layer. NaOH/NaHCO3 are basic and leave the neutral amine in ether; brine and MgSO4 do not ionize it.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; correct key, valid aniline SMILES, single concept (basic amine extraction)."},{"q":"In a normal-phase TLC analysis (silica gel plate, nonpolar developing solvent), aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid, shown) is spotted alongside biphenyl. Which statement about their relative Rf values is correct?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Oc1ccccc1C(=O)O","opts":["Both compounds have identical Rf because they are the same molecular weight","Aspirin has the higher Rf because it is more polar than biphenyl","Biphenyl has the lower Rf because aromatic rings adsorb strongly to silica","Aspirin has the lower Rf because its polar -COOH and ester groups bind silica more strongly","Rf cannot be compared without knowing the UV wavelength"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"On polar silica, more polar compounds adsorb more strongly and travel less, giving a lower Rf. Aspirin's COOH/ester make it far more polar than nonpolar biphenyl, so aspirin has the lower Rf.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid aspirin SMILES, exactly one defensible answer, in-scope TLC concept."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone (bp 156 C) is contaminated with a small amount of naphthalene (mp 80 C, bp 218 C, a solid at room temperature). The two differ in boiling point by about 60 C. Which purification technique best separates cyclohexanone from this naphthalene impurity?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["Silica column using only hexane","Acid/base extraction","Recrystallization from hot water","Simple distillation","Steam distillation at constant temperature"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclohexanone is a volatile liquid and naphthalene a higher-boiling solid; a ~60 C gap with one component non-volatile makes simple distillation the clean choice. Neither is acidic/basic so extraction fails; hexane-only column poorly resolves a polar ketone.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid cyclohexanone SMILES, technique-selection question, single best answer."},{"q":"A student must separate octanoic acid (bp 237 C) from 1-decanol (bp 231 C), two liquids whose boiling points differ by only ~6 C. Which approach gives the cleanest separation?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCCC(=O)O","opts":["Fractional distillation alone","Acid/base extraction with aqueous NaHCO3","Simple distillation","A single recrystallization from ethanol","TLC on a silica plate"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"With only a ~6 C boiling-point gap, distillation is impractical. Octanoic acid is acidic and is extracted as its sodium carboxylate by NaHCO3 while neutral 1-decanol stays in the organic layer, giving a clean chemical separation. TLC is analytical, not preparative.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid octanoic acid SMILES, distractors plausible, exactly one correct technique."},{"q":"A crude product mixture contains benzaldehyde, benzyl alcohol, and benzoic acid (shown). Ranking these three by increasing Rf on a normal-phase silica TLC plate (most polar runs lowest), which order is correct?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid < benzaldehyde","Benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde","Benzaldehyde < benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol","Benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol","Benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"On silica, more polar/H-bonding compounds adsorb harder and have lower Rf. Polarity is carboxylic acid > alcohol > aldehyde, so increasing Rf is benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: q_smiles shows only benzoic acid while stem names three compounds; '(shown)' references the drawn molecule so acceptable, but ideally all three would be depicted. Chemistry and key correct."},{"q":"After running a column on impure cyclohexanol (shown) using a hexane/ethyl acetate eluent on silica, you want the polar cyclohexanol to come off the column. To elute it faster, how should you adjust the mobile phase?","q_smiles":"OC1CCCCC1","opts":["Lower the solvent flow rate to zero","Add anhydrous MgSO4 to the eluent","Switch the silica to a longer, narrower column","Increase the proportion of ethyl acetate (more polar solvent)","Increase the proportion of hexane (less polar solvent)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"In normal-phase chromatography a more polar mobile phase competes for silica binding sites and elutes polar analytes faster. Adding ethyl acetate raises eluent polarity, speeding cyclohexanol off the column.","difficulty":1,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None; valid cyclohexanol SMILES, single correct answer, standard chromatography concept."},{"q":"A student recrystallizes impure benzoic acid (shown) from hot water. Which choice of recovered material and rationale reflects correct recrystallization technique?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["Use a solvent in which benzoic acid is highly soluble even at room temperature","Collect the crystals that form on slow cooling; soluble impurities stay dissolved in the cold mother liquor","Evaporate all the solvent to dryness to maximize crystal yield","Collect the hot filtrate immediately; the product is whatever passes through the filter paper","Cool the solution as fast as possible in ice to trap the most impurities in the crystals"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Proper recrystallization dissolves the solid in minimal hot solvent, then slow cooling grows pure crystals while soluble impurities remain in the cold mother liquor and are filtered off. Fast cooling traps impurities, an always-soluble solvent prevents crystallization, and evaporating to dryness defeats purification.","difficulty":2,"group":"lab-techniques","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Lowered difficulty 3->2: it is a standard technique-recall item, not hard. Valid benzoic acid SMILES, one correct answer, in scope."},{"q":"A mixture of benzoic acid and naphthalene (shown) dissolved in diethyl ether is shaken with aqueous 1 M NaOH in a separatory funnel. After the layers separate, which species is present in the AQUEOUS layer?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[O-]Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","c1ccc2ccccc2c1","[O-]C(=O)c1ccccc1","[O-]c1ccccc1"],"correct":3,"why":"NaOH deprotonates benzoic acid (pKa ~4.2) to the water-soluble benzoate anion, which partitions into the aqueous layer; naphthalene has no acidic proton and stays in ether. Distractors B (neutral acid stays in ether), C (naphthalene stays in ether), D (phenoxide), E (benzyl alkoxide) involve species not generated here.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). All 5 option SMILES valid; single acid-base extraction concept, in scope, visual."},{"q":"N,N-diethylaniline (shown), a neutral organic base, is dissolved in dichloromethane and extracted with aqueous 1 M HCl. Which species is now found in the AQUEOUS acidic layer?","q_smiles":"CCN(CC)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[NH3+]c1ccccc1","CC[N+](CC)([O-])c1ccccc1","CC[NH+](CC)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","CCN(CC)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"N,N-Diethylaniline is a tertiary amine with a basic lone pair, so aqueous HCl protonates it to the N,N-diethylanilinium ion. That charged ammonium salt is water-soluble and moves into the aqueous acidic layer. The neutral amine stays in the dichloromethane; the plain anilinium ion has lost both ethyl groups, which HCl cannot remove; the N-oxide would need an oxidant; and phenol is a different compound.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Aniline is weakly basic but 1 M HCl protonates and extracts it (standard textbook). All option SMILES valid; in scope, visual."},{"q":"Four compounds are spotted and run on a normal-phase silica TLC plate using the same nonpolar developing solvent. Benzoic acid is shown as a reference. Rank the following by Rf value from LOWEST to HIGHEST: benzoic acid, benzaldehyde, ethylbenzene, benzyl alcohol.","q_smiles":"OC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["benzaldehyde < benzoic acid < ethylbenzene < benzyl alcohol","benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene","benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene","ethylbenzene < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < benzoic acid","benzoic acid < benzaldehyde < benzyl alcohol < ethylbenzene"],"correct":1,"why":"On polar silica, more polar compounds adsorb harder and travel less (lower Rf). Polarity: carboxylic acid > alcohol > aldehyde > hydrocarbon. So lowest-to-highest Rf = benzoic acid < benzyl alcohol < benzaldehyde < ethylbenzene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Polarity ranking unambiguous (acid>OH>aldehyde>alkylbenzene); distractor E swaps alcohol/aldehyde, a plausible trap. Stem SMILES valid, in scope."},{"q":"Acetanilide (shown) is purified by recrystallization. Which property describes the IDEAL recrystallization solvent for this process?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Has a boiling point higher than the melting point of the compound","Dissolves the compound only when cold and precipitates it when hot","Dissolves the compound equally well at all temperatures","Dissolves both the compound and all impurities completely at room temperature","Dissolves the compound well when hot but poorly when cold, and does not react with it"],"correct":4,"why":"An ideal recrystallization solvent shows strong temperature-dependent solubility (high hot, low cold) and is chemically inert, so pure crystals form on cooling. B and E never allow crystal recovery; C is backwards; D would melt the solid (oiling out) rather than crystallize.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept, distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope. Stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"A two-component mixture is loaded onto a normal-phase silica gel column and eluted with a hexane/ethyl acetate gradient. Cyclohexanone (shown) and cyclohexanol are the components. Which compound ELUTES FIRST, and why?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Cyclohexanol, because the alcohol is less polar and binds silica more weakly","Cyclohexanone, because the ketone is less polar and binds silica more weakly","Cyclohexanol, because hydrogen bonding speeds its movement down the column","They co-elute, because both have the same molecular formula","Cyclohexanone, because the ketone is more polar and binds silica more strongly"],"correct":1,"why":"On polar silica the less polar component binds weakly and elutes first. Cyclohexanone (ketone, no O-H) is less polar than cyclohexanol (H-bond-donor alcohol), so the ketone elutes first; the alcohol H-bonds to silanols and is retained. E is doubly false (formulas differ: C6H10O vs C6H12O).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Verified formulas differ, so distractor E is false. Single concept, in scope, stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"On a silica TLC plate, geraniol (shown) travels a distance of 2.0 cm while the solvent front travels 8.0 cm from the origin. What is the Rf value, and how would switching to a MORE POLAR solvent system affect it?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CCCC(C)=CCO","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf","Rf = 0.25; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf","Rf = 4.0; a more polar solvent would increase the Rf","Rf = 0.40; a more polar solvent would not change the Rf","Rf = 0.75; a more polar solvent would decrease the Rf"],"correct":0,"why":"Rf = spot distance / solvent-front distance = 2.0/8.0 = 0.25. A more polar mobile phase competes for the polar silica sites and elutes the polar (-OH) compound farther, increasing Rf.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Calc (0.25) and polarity-effect both right; two-concept item, in scope. Geraniol SMILES valid."},{"q":"An ether solution contains both 4-methylbenzoic acid and phenol (shown). To separate ONLY the carboxylic acid into the aqueous layer while leaving phenol in the ether, which aqueous wash should be used?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Saturated aqueous NaHCO3, because it deprotonates phenol but not the acid","Aqueous 1 M NaOH, because it deprotonates the carboxylic acid but not phenol","Saturated aqueous NaHCO3, because it deprotonates the carboxylic acid but not phenol","Aqueous 1 M HCl, because it protonates the carboxylic acid selectively","Aqueous NaCl (brine), because it dissolves the more polar carboxylic acid"],"correct":2,"why":"Carboxylic acids (pKa about 4 to 5) are much stronger acids than phenols (pKa about 10). Bicarbonate is a weak base (its conjugate acid, carbonic acid, has pKa about 6.4): strong enough to deprotonate 4-methylbenzoic acid to its water-soluble carboxylate, but too weak to deprotonate phenol, which stays neutral in the ether. That is exactly the selectivity needed. NaOH is strong enough to deprotonate both and would pull the phenol into the water as well; HCl and brine ionize neither compound; and the claim that bicarbonate deprotonates phenol but not the acid reverses the real acidity order.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Classic NaHCO3-vs-NaOH selectivity discriminator; B is the key trap. In scope, phenol SMILES valid."},{"q":"A student must separate a mixture of pentane (bp 36 C) and 2-methyl-2-butanol (shown, bp 102 C), two fully miscible liquids with a large boiling-point difference. Which technique and outcome is correct?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Simple distillation; 2-methyl-2-butanol distills over first because it is more polar","Liquid-liquid extraction; the two layers separate without distillation","Fractional distillation is required because the boiling points are nearly identical","Recrystallization; the lower-melting component crystallizes first","Simple distillation; pentane distills over first because it has the lower boiling point"],"correct":4,"why":"Two miscible liquids with a large bp gap (~66 C) are separated by simple distillation, with the lower-boiling component (pentane, 36 C) distilling first. Distillation order follows bp not polarity (kills B); fractional distillation is only for close bp (kills D); miscible liquids cannot be extracted (kills C).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"lab-techniques","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (technique selection by bp), distractors plausible-but-wrong, in scope. Stem SMILES valid."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2CH2CH2-Br  +  NaCN  (in DMSO)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCCCBr","opts":["C=NCCCC","C=CCC","CCCC#N","CCCCOC#N","CCCCC#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Cyanide is a good nucleophile and a primary alkyl halide in a polar aprotic solvent favors SN2. The carbon of CN attacks, displacing bromide, giving pentanenitrile (CCCCC#N), one carbon longer. Distractors: isonitrile (N-attack, minor), one-carbon-short skeleton, a cyanate, and the (disfavored) elimination alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Chemistry correct; SN2 C-attack on primary halide gives the nitrile with +1 carbon. All 5 SMILES valid, one defensible answer, visual, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\ncyclohexanone  +  NaBH4,  CH3OH\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OCC1CCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"NaBH4 delivers hydride to the carbonyl, reducing the ketone to the secondary alcohol cyclohexanol (OC1CCCCC1) without over-reduction. Distractors: unchanged ketone, full deoxygenation, an enol, and a ring-contracted primary alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: ketone to secondary alcohol. SMILES valid, single answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2CH2CH2-OH  +  PCC,  CH2Cl2\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCCCO","CCCC=O","CCC(C)=O","CCC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"PCC is a mild oxidant that takes a primary alcohol to the aldehyde and stops there. 1-Butanol gives butanal (CCCC=O). Distractors: the carboxylic acid (over-oxidation, e.g. Jones), a mis-located ketone, a one-carbon-short aldehyde, and unchanged alcohol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: PCC stops at aldehyde. SMILES valid, one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nbenzaldehyde  +  KMnO4,  H3O+ (heat)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1","Oc1ccccc1","O=Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hot acidic KMnO4 oxidizes an aldehyde to a carboxylic acid; benzaldehyde gives benzoic acid (O=C(O)c1ccccc1). Distractors: benzyl alcohol (reduction), unchanged aldehyde, phenylacetic acid (extra CH2), and phenol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: aldehyde to acid. SMILES valid, single answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\nCH3CH2COOH  +  1. LiAlH4, Et2O;  2. H3O+\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)O","opts":["CCC=O","CCCO","CCO","CCC(C)O","CCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces a carboxylic acid fully to a primary alcohol; propanoic acid gives 1-propanol (CCCO). Distractors: the aldehyde (LiAlH4 does not stop there), a secondary alcohol (wrong framework), a one-carbon-short alcohol, and unchanged acid.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: acid to primary alcohol. SMILES valid, one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction:\n\npropanoyl chloride  +  excess CH3NH2\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CCC(=O)Cl","opts":["CCCNC","CCC(=O)OC","CCC(=O)Cl","CCC(=O)N","CCC(=O)NC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Methylamine adds to the acid chloride and chloride leaves, giving N-methylpropanamide (CCC(=O)NC); excess amine neutralizes HCl. Distractors: methyl ester (needs MeOH), unsubstituted amide (needs NH3), an amine (no reductant), and unchanged acid chloride.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Correct: acid chloride + amine to N-substituted amide. SMILES valid (CCC(=O)N is the primary amide, valid), one answer, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction. Pay attention to stereochemistry:\n\n(R)-2-bromobutane  +  NaOH  (in water/acetone)\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CC[C@@H](C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)O","C=C(C)CC","CC[C@@H](C)O","CC[C@@H](C)Br","CC[C@H](C)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hydroxide is a strong nucleophile, and a secondary bromide in a water/acetone mixture favors SN2. Hydroxide attacks from the side opposite the leaving group, so the stereocenter inverts: (R)-2-bromobutane becomes (S)-2-butanol. (R)-2-Butanol would require retention, which SN2 never gives; 2-butanol drawn without stereochemistry implies a racemic mixture, the SN1 outcome; and the unchanged bromide is the starting material, not a product.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Verified by RDKit CIP: stem = R, keyed answer = S (inversion). SN2 correct for secondary halide with strong Nu. SMILES valid, one defensible answer (opt2 no-stereo is a distinct distractor), regio+stereo two-concept, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Predict the MAJOR product of the following reaction. Account for BOTH regiochemistry and stereochemistry:\n\n1-methylcyclohexene  +  1. BH3 (THF);  2. H2O2, NaOH\n\nWhich structure is formed?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1CCCCC1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","CC1CCCC=C1O","C[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation gives anti-Markovnikov hydration with syn stereochemistry. Boron adds to the less substituted alkene carbon (C2) while hydrogen adds to the methyl-bearing C1, both from the same face; oxidation then replaces boron with OH on C2 with retention. Because the new H on C1 and the new OH on C2 sit on the same face, the methyl already on C1 ends up on the face opposite the OH: the major product is trans-2-methylcyclohexanol. The cis diastereomer has the OH and methyl on the same face; 1-methylcyclohexanol is the Markovnikov alcohol from acid-catalyzed hydration; the enol is not a hydration product; and 2-methylcyclohexanol drawn without stereochemistry fails to show the required trans relationship.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","correct_was":0,"issue":"Verified by 3D embedding: keyed opt0 is genuinely trans, opt1 is cis. Anti-Markovnikov + syn addition correctly yields trans-2-methylcyclohexanol. Recognition-level outcome (no mechanism drawn), regio+stereo, in scope at difficulty 3. SMILES valid, one answer. Keep."},{"q":"Which reagent converts the alcohol shown (1-propanol) into propanoic acid (the carboxylic acid, full oxidation)?","q_smiles":"CCCO","opts":["PCC in CH2Cl2","SOCl2","NaBH4 in methanol","PBr3","KMnO4 (or Jones reagent, CrO3/H2SO4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A primary alcohol is taken fully to the carboxylic acid only by a strong oxidant (KMnO4 or Jones, CrO3/H2SO4). PCC stops at the aldehyde, NaBH4 reduces, and PBr3/SOCl2 swap OH for a halide leaving group without changing oxidation state.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified. Bundling KMnO4 with Jones in one option is fine since both fully oxidize."},{"q":"Which reagent oxidizes benzyl alcohol (shown) selectively to the aldehyde (benzaldehyde) without over-oxidizing to the acid?","q_smiles":"OCc1ccccc1","opts":["PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2","H2/Pd","LiAlH4","CrO3/H2SO4 (Jones reagent)","Hot KMnO4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"PCC is the mild, anhydrous oxidant that stops a primary alcohol at the aldehyde. Hot KMnO4 and Jones over-oxidize to the acid; LiAlH4 and H2/Pd are reducing conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (shown) is treated with a strong, bulky base (potassium tert-butoxide). Which is the MAJOR alkene product (Hofmann, less-substituted, due to the bulky base)?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(Br)CC","opts":["CCC(C)(C)OC(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)(C)CC","C=C(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is a strong but very bulky base, so on 2-bromo-2-methylbutane it removes the most accessible beta hydrogen, one on the terminal CH3, rather than one on the more hindered CH2. E2 then gives the less substituted Hofmann alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene, as the major product. 2-Methyl-2-butene, the more substituted (trisubstituted) Zaitsev alkene, would dominate with a small base such as ethoxide. The tert-butyl ether and the tertiary alcohol are substitution products, and the saturated 2,2-dimethylbutane does not form under these conditions.","difficulty":2,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry and key (index 0) correct. Minor: the 'why' labels option 5 (CC(C)(C)CC) an 'unreacted/reduced skeleton,' but it canonicalizes to 2,2-dimethylbutane (C6), one carbon larger than the C5 substrate; it is still a valid wrong distractor and does not create a second correct answer."},{"q":"Which reagent reduces cyclohexanone (shown) to cyclohexanol without also reducing an ester elsewhere in the molecule (the mild, chemoselective hydride)?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["H2 with Lindlar catalyst","NaBH4 in methanol","Br2/FeBr3","PCC","LiAlH4 in dry ether"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"NaBH4 reduces aldehydes/ketones but is too mild to touch esters or acids, making it chemoselective. LiAlH4 would also reduce the ester; Lindlar reduces alkynes; PCC oxidizes; Br2/FeBr3 is aromatic bromination.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Phenol (shown) is converted to anisole (methyl phenyl ether). Which reagent set performs this O-methylation (Williamson ether synthesis)?","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts":["CH3Cl, AlCl3","PCC","CH3OH, H2SO4, heat","HBr","NaOH then CH3I"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Williamson synthesis deprotonates phenol (NaOH gives phenoxide) then alkylates with CH3I via SN2 to give anisole. CH3Cl/AlCl3 is Friedel-Crafts C-methylation on the ring, not O-methylation; PCC oxidizes; HBr does not form the ether.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Butanoic acid (shown) is to be converted to its methyl ester. Which classifies this transformation and gives the correct reagents?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)O","opts":["Halogenation: PBr3","Saponification: NaOH, H2O, heat","Fischer esterification: CH3OH with catalytic H2SO4","Decarboxylation: heat alone","Reduction: LiAlH4 then H3O+"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Acid + alcohol with acid catalyst is Fischer esterification, giving the methyl ester. Saponification is the reverse (ester to carboxylate); LiAlH4 reduces to a primary alcohol; PBr3 leads toward an acyl bromide.","difficulty":1,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Which transformation describes treating ethyl acetate (shown) with aqueous NaOH and heat, and what carbonyl-containing product forms?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Saponification (basic ester hydrolysis) giving acetate (carboxylate) salt + ethanol","Transesterification giving methyl acetate","Fischer esterification giving an ester + water","Claisen condensation giving a beta-keto ester","Reduction giving a 1,2-diol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Heating an ester with aqueous NaOH is saponification: hydroxide adds to the carbonyl, ethoxide leaves, and the carboxylic acid formed is immediately deprotonated to the carboxylate, which makes the hydrolysis irreversible. Ethyl acetate therefore gives acetate (as its sodium salt) plus ethanol. Transesterification needs a different alcohol as the reagent, Fischer esterification runs the other direction under acid catalysis, a Claisen condensation needs an alkoxide base and a second ester molecule, and no reducing agent is present to form a diol.","difficulty":2,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Correct as written; key index 0 verified."},{"q":"Propanal (shown) is stirred with excess methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Treating the aldehyde with two equivalents of alcohol under acid gives which product class?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCC(OC)OC","CCCO","CCC(O)O","CCC(O)OC","CCC(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"An aldehyde + 2 eq alcohol under acid forms an acetal with two OR groups on the original carbonyl carbon: CCC(OC)OC. CCC(O)OC is the hemiacetal (one equivalent, intermediate); CCC(=O)OC is an ester (wrong connectivity); CCC(O)O is the gem-diol hydrate; CCCO is a reduction product.","difficulty":3,"group":"functional-group-id","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry and key (index 0) correct. Acetal recognition is a single concept and within DAT scope; difficulty 3 is slightly generous but acceptable."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrCC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Without peroxides, HBr adds through a carbocation, so Markovnikov's rule applies. Protonating the CH end of the double bond puts the positive charge on the methyl-bearing ring carbon, which is tertiary and therefore the more stable cation, and bromide bonds there to give 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane. 1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is the anti-Markovnikov product that would need peroxides, 1-methylcyclohexanol needs water rather than bromide, (bromomethyl)cyclohexane puts the Br on the methyl carbon, and the allylic bromide still contains the double bond.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0, tertiary bromide). All 5 SMILES parse; single regiochemistry concept, in scope. Difficulty is really single-concept (1) but 2 is acceptable. Keep."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (drawn) reacts with Br2 in CH2Cl2. Bromine adds anti across the double bond. Which structure correctly represents the product of this single alkene?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)CCBr","CCC(C)(Br)Br","C/C(Br)=C(\\Br)C","C[C@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti: the first bromine forms a cyclic bromonium ion, and bromide then opens it from the opposite face. Anti addition to cis-2-butene gives the chiral 2,3-dibromobutane as a racemic mixture of the (2R,3R) and (2S,3S) enantiomers; the structure drawn is the (2S,3S) enantiomer, one member of that pair. The (2R,3S) structure is the meso compound, which is what trans-2-butene gives, and it is the classic trap here. The geminal 2,2-dibromobutane, 1,3-dibromobutane and 2,3-dibromo-2-butene are not products of simple Br2 addition.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Verified by RDKit: opt 0 is chiral (one of d,l pair) = correct; opt 1 is meso = correct trap. Stereospecificity (anti) + cis->threo is two layered concepts, fits difficulty 3, still DAT recognition depth. Keep."},{"q":"You need to convert the nitrile shown (propanenitrile) into propan-1-amine, CH3CH2CH2NH2. Which reagent accomplishes this functional-group interconversion?","q_smiles":"CCC#N","opts":["H3O+ with heat (aqueous acid)","O3, then Zn/H2O","NaBH4 in methanol","LiAlH4, then H3O+","DIBAL-H (1 equiv) at low temperature, then workup"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"LiAlH4 fully reduces a nitrile (C#N) to a primary amine (CH2NH2). NaBH4 is too weak for nitriles, aqueous acid/heat hydrolyzes to a carboxylic acid, DIBAL-H (1 equiv, cold) stops at the imine giving an aldehyde after hydrolysis, and ozonolysis is irrelevant. Exactly one correct reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CCC#N valid (propanenitrile). Reagent-selection, text options, molecule drawn in stem as required. One defensible answer. Keep."},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene (drawn) is treated with BH3-THF, then H2O2/NaOH. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)=CC","opts":["CC(C)C(=O)C","CC(C)CC=O","OCC(C)CC","CC(C)(O)CC","CC(C)C(O)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation is anti-Markovnikov: boron bonds to the less substituted alkene carbon (the CH end of 2-methyl-2-butene), and the H2O2/NaOH step swaps boron for OH on that same carbon. The product is 3-methyl-2-butanol, a secondary alcohol. 2-Methyl-2-butanol is the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol you would get from acid-catalyzed hydration or oxymercuration. The ketone and the aldehyde would require an oxidation these conditions do not perform, and 2-methyl-1-butanol puts the OH on a carbon that was never part of the double bond.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CC(C)=CC is 2-methyl-2-butene (correct). Opt 0 = 3-methyl-2-butanol, correct anti-Markovnikov product. Single regiochemistry concept; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with one equivalent of CH3MgBr, then aqueous acid workup (H3O+). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1=O","COC1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"A Grignard reagent delivers its carbon nucleophile to the carbonyl carbon; with a ketone the result after aqueous workup is a tertiary alcohol. Methylmagnesium bromide adds CH3 to cyclohexanone and H3O+ protonates the alkoxide, giving 1-methylcyclohexanol, with the new methyl and the OH on the same carbon. Cyclohexanol is simple reduction with no new carbon-carbon bond; 2-methylcyclohexanone would be alpha-alkylation, which a Grignard does not do; methoxycyclohexane is an ether; and 1-methylcyclopentanol has the wrong ring size.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Opt 0 = 1-methylcyclohexanol, correct tertiary alcohol. All SMILES parse (E is intentionally cyclopentane skeleton). One defensible answer; in scope. Keep."},{"q":"Starting from 1-butanol (drawn), which reagent oxidizes it cleanly to the ALDEHYDE (butanal) and stops there, rather than going all the way to butanoic acid?","q_smiles":"CCCCO","opts":["H2CrO4 (chromic acid), aqueous","K2Cr2O7 / H2SO4, aqueous, with heat","Jones reagent (CrO3/H2SO4, aqueous acetone)","KMnO4, hot, aqueous","PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"PCC is an anhydrous Cr(VI) reagent that oxidizes a primary alcohol only to the aldehyde and stops, since no water is present to form the hydrate. KMnO4 (hot), Jones, aqueous dichromate/acid, and chromic acid are all strong aqueous oxidants that take a primary alcohol all the way to the carboxylic acid. Exactly one stops-at-aldehyde reagent.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"q_smiles CCCCO valid (1-butanol). Reagent-selection text item, molecule drawn as required. PCC uniquely correct; the four aqueous Cr(VI) options are all over-oxidizers. Keep."},{"q":"Cyclopentene (drawn) is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic). The two new oxygens add to the same face of the ring. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCC1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Cold, dilute, basic KMnO4 dihydroxylates an alkene through a cyclic manganate ester, so both oxygens are delivered to the same face of the double bond (syn addition). On cyclopentene that puts both OH groups on the same side of the ring: cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol, a meso compound. The trans diol would require anti addition, for example epoxidation followed by acid opening. The epoxide is the product of a peracid, cyclopentanol has only one OH, and 2-hydroxycyclopentanone is an over-oxidation product.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Verified by 3D embedding: opt 0 stereo-SMILES is genuinely the cis (syn) diol = correct; opt 1 is trans = correct anti trap. Syn-addition + cis ring stereochem, two concepts, difficulty 3 fits. Keep."},{"q":"Propanal (drawn) is treated with ONE equivalent of methanol and a trace of acid (HCl). Which structure best represents the product of this functional-group interconversion?","q_smiles":"CCC=O","opts":["CCCOC","CCC(OC)OC","CCC(O)O","CCC(O)OC","CCC(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Under acid catalysis the carbonyl oxygen is protonated and methanol adds to the carbonyl carbon. With only one equivalent of alcohol the reaction stops at the hemiacetal: the former carbonyl carbon now carries one OH and one OCH3. With excess methanol a second addition would replace the OH to give the acetal with two OCH3 groups, the classic trap. Methyl propanoate is an ester (an oxidation level above an aldehyde), the gem-diol hydrate would need water instead of methanol, and methyl propyl ether is a reduction level these conditions cannot reach.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"functional-group-id","issue":"Opt 0 = hemiacetal CCC(O)OC, correct for one equiv MeOH. Opt 1 = acetal (excess), correct distractor. All SMILES parse; single concept, in scope. Keep."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1(Br)CCCCC1Br","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1Br","CC1(Br)CCCCC1","BrCC1CCCCC1"],"correct":3,"why":"Without peroxides, HBr adds by Markovnikov's rule through the more stable carbocation. Protonating the CH carbon of 1-methylcyclohexene puts the positive charge on the methyl-bearing tertiary carbon, and bromide bonds there to give 1-bromo-1-methylcyclohexane. 1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is the anti-Markovnikov product and needs peroxides; 1-methylcyclohexanol needs water as the nucleophile; the vicinal dibromide needs Br2; and (bromomethyl)cyclohexane has the bromine on the methyl carbon, which was never part of the double bond.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Chemistry, key, SMILES all correct; one defensible answer; in scope.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Styrene is treated with HBr in the presence of benzoyl peroxide (ROOR). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrCC(Br)c1ccccc1","BrCCc1ccccc1","CC(Br)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccc(Br)cc1"],"correct":1,"why":"Benzoyl peroxide switches HBr addition to a radical chain. The bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 of styrene so that the unpaired electron lands on the benzylic carbon, where it is resonance-stabilized; that radical then abstracts H from HBr. The result is anti-Markovnikov addition giving (2-bromoethyl)benzene. (1-Bromoethyl)benzene is the Markovnikov product formed without peroxides, (1,2-dibromoethyl)benzene would need Br2, 2-phenylethanol needs an oxygen nucleophile, and 4-bromostyrene is an electrophilic aromatic substitution product that would need Br2 and FeBr3.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct radical anti-Markovnikov product, valid SMILES, single answer.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"1-Butyne (CH3CH2C≡CH) is treated with 2 equivalents of HBr. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(Br)(Br)C(Br)Br","CCC(Br)CBr","CCCC(Br)Br","CCC(Br)CC","CCC(Br)(Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Each HBr adds to the alkyne by Markovnikov's rule. The first addition puts H on the terminal carbon and Br on C2, giving 2-bromo-1-butene; the second addition again puts the proton on the terminal carbon because the resulting cation on C2 is stabilized by the bromine already there. Both bromines therefore end up on the same internal carbon, giving the geminal dihalide 2,2-dibromobutane. 1,2-Dibromobutane is the vicinal pattern Br2 would give, 1,1-dibromobutane has anti-Markovnikov regiochemistry, the tetrabromide would require two equivalents of Br2 rather than HBr, and 3-bromopentane has the wrong carbon skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. q_smiles is 1-butyne; geminal dihalide correctly keyed.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with cold, dilute KMnO4 (basic, then workup). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C1CCC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O"],"correct":4,"why":"Cold, dilute, basic KMnO4 is a syn dihydroxylation: both oxygens are delivered to the same face of the alkene through a cyclic manganate ester, and the reaction stops at the diol. Cyclohexene therefore gives cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol, a meso compound. The trans diol would require anti addition, which is the epoxide-opening route (peracid, then aqueous acid). The epoxide itself is a peracid product, and cyclohexanone and cyclohexanol are the wrong oxidation outcomes for this reagent.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. RDKit confirms opt0 = cis (meso) diol = correct syn product; opt1 = trans distractor.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is heated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C=C(C)CC","CC=C(C)C","CCC(C)(C)O","CC(C)C(C)Br","CCOC(C)(C)CC"],"correct":1,"why":"Sodium ethoxide is a strong base, the substrate is a tertiary bromide, and heat favors elimination, so the reaction is E2. Ethoxide is small enough to reach the more hindered beta hydrogens, so Zaitsev's rule applies and the major product is the more substituted alkene, 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted). 2-Methyl-1-butene is the less substituted Hofmann alkene that a bulky base like tert-butoxide would favor. 2-Methyl-2-butanol would require water, the ethyl ether (2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane) is the substitution product that elimination outcompetes with a strong base and heat, and 2-bromo-3-methylbutane is a rearranged bromide that does not form.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev E2 product correctly keyed; Hofmann distractor valid.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is subjected to the following two-step sequence: (1) NaBH4, MeOH; (2) PBr3. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["BrC1CCCCC1Br","BrC1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":1,"why":"NaBH4 in methanol reduces the ketone to the secondary alcohol cyclohexanol, and PBr3 then converts that alcohol into the corresponding alkyl bromide, giving bromocyclohexane. Cyclohexanol is only the intermediate after the first step, the unchanged ketone ignores the reduction, cyclohexene would need a dehydration (acid and heat) rather than PBr3, and 1,2-dibromocyclohexane would require Br2 adding to an alkene.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard reduction-then-PBr3 sequence; correct product keyed; in scope (2 simple steps, single transformation each).","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"(R)-2-bromobutane is treated with NaOH in water/acetone (conditions favoring SN2). What is the major product, including its configuration?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC[C@@H](O)C","CCCCO","CCC(C)O","CC=CC","CC[C@H](O)C"],"correct":0,"why":"In water/acetone with hydroxide as the nucleophile, a secondary bromide reacts by SN2. Hydroxide attacks from the back side, opposite the bromine, so the stereocenter inverts: (R)-2-bromobutane gives (S)-2-butanol. (R)-2-Butanol would require retention, which SN2 does not give; 2-butanol drawn without stereochemistry implies a racemic mixture (an SN1 result); 2-butene is the E2 product; and 1-butanol has the OH on the wrong carbon.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. RDKit confirms inversion gives (S)-2-butanol = opt0; single concept (SN2 stereochem), in DAT scope.","group":"multistep-synthesis"},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene (drawn) is treated with H2O and a catalytic amount of H2SO4. Which statement best describes the product of this acid-catalyzed hydration?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["Anti-Markovnikov addition gives 3-methyl-2-butanol","Syn dihydroxylation gives a vicinal diol","No reaction occurs because the alkene is too substituted","Markovnikov addition gives 2-methyl-1-butanol (a primary alcohol)","Markovnikov addition gives 2-methyl-2-butanol (the tertiary alcohol)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration proceeds through the most stable carbocation. Protonating 2-methyl-2-butene at the CH carbon puts the positive charge on the carbon bearing two methyls, a tertiary cation; water attacks there and loses a proton, giving 2-methyl-2-butanol, the tertiary alcohol. This is Markovnikov addition. 3-Methyl-2-butanol is the anti-Markovnikov alcohol that hydroboration-oxidation would give; a vicinal diol needs OsO4 or cold KMnO4; 2-methyl-1-butanol would put the OH on a carbon that was never part of the double bond; and highly substituted alkenes react readily because they form the most stable cations, so 'no reaction' is wrong.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: q_smiles parses as 2-methyl-2-butene; Markovnikov tertiary alcohol is correct; distractors are coherent isomers; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"Toluene (drawn) is heated with hot, concentrated KMnO4 followed by acidic workup (H3O+). What is the organic product?","q_smiles":"Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Benzoic acid (the benzylic carbon is oxidized to COOH)","Benzaldehyde (oxidation stops at the aldehyde)","No reaction; the aromatic ring is inert to KMnO4","Benzyl alcohol (the methyl is hydroxylated)","Cyclohexanecarboxylic acid (the ring is reduced and oxidized)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hot, concentrated KMnO4 oxidizes an alkyl side chain on a benzene ring all the way to a carboxylic acid, provided the benzylic carbon carries at least one hydrogen. Toluene's methyl group is benzylic, so after acidic workup the product is benzoic acid. The oxidation does not stop at benzaldehyde or benzyl alcohol under these forcing conditions, the aromatic ring itself survives (it is not reduced to a cyclohexane), and 'no reaction' ignores the reactive benzylic position.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: toluene SMILES correct; benzylic oxidation to benzoic acid is the standard product; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"You want to convert cyclohexanone (drawn) into 1-methylcyclohexanol in one step. Which reagent sequence accomplishes this transformation?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["1) PCC, CH2Cl2","1) CH3CH2OH, H+ (cat.)","1) CH3MgBr; 2) H3O+ workup","1) NaBH4; 2) H3O+ workup","1) CH3Cl, AlCl3"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"To turn cyclohexanone into 1-methylcyclohexanol you need to add a methyl group to the carbonyl carbon and end up with an OH on that same carbon. Methylmagnesium bromide does exactly that: the Grignard's carbon attacks the C=O, and the H3O+ workup protonates the alkoxide to give the tertiary alcohol. NaBH4 adds only hydride, giving cyclohexanol with no new carbon; PCC is an oxidant and cannot touch a ketone; ethanol with acid forms a ketal rather than a C-C bond; and CH3Cl with AlCl3 is a Friedel-Crafts reagent for aromatic rings, not for ketones.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: cyclohexanone SMILES correct; Grignard methylation gives tertiary alcohol; reagent-selection item, single concept, in scope."},{"q":"1-Butyne (drawn) is subjected to hydroboration-oxidation: 1) BH3-THF (then disiamylborane equivalent for terminal alkyne), 2) H2O2, NaOH. What carbonyl product forms?","q_smiles":"CCC#C","opts":["Butanal (anti-Markovnikov; O ends up on the terminal carbon)","2-Butanol (Markovnikov alcohol)","Butanoic acid (over-oxidation to the acid)","2-Butanone (Markovnikov; O ends up on the internal carbon)","1-Butanol (simple alcohol, no carbonyl)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":0,"why":"Hydroboration-oxidation of a terminal alkyne is anti-Markovnikov: boron, and then OH, goes to the terminal carbon. The initial product is an enol with the OH on C1, and the enol tautomerizes to the carbonyl form, which at a terminal position is an aldehyde: 1-butyne gives butanal. 2-Butanone is the Markovnikov ketone from Hg2+/H2SO4 hydration; 2-butanol and 1-butanol are alcohols, which alkyne hydration does not give; and these conditions do not oxidize the aldehyde on to butanoic acid.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: CCC#C is terminal 1-butyne; anti-Markovnikov aldehyde correct; two concepts (regiochem + tautomerization), in scope."},{"q":"Benzaldehyde (drawn) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol, then aqueous workup. Which best describes the outcome?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["No reaction; NaBH4 is too weak to reduce an aldehyde","Addition of methanol to give a stable hemiacetal as the isolated product","Oxidation of the aldehyde to benzoic acid","Selective reduction of the aldehyde to benzyl alcohol","Reduction all the way to toluene (loss of oxygen)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride donor that reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols but leaves esters, acids and aromatic rings alone. Benzaldehyde is therefore reduced selectively to benzyl alcohol. NaBH4 is not an oxidant (benzoic acid is wrong), it does not strip the oxygen off entirely (toluene would need a Clemmensen or Wolff-Kishner reduction), and although methanol can form a hemiacetal reversibly, the hydride reduction consumes the aldehyde so the isolated product is the alcohol.","difficulty":1,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: benzaldehyde SMILES correct; NaBH4 reduction to benzyl alcohol is standard; single concept, in scope."},{"q":"The target alcohol 2-butanol (drawn) is to be made from 1-butene. Which two-step sequence delivers 2-butanol as the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)O","opts":["1) Br2; 2) H2O — gives a bromohydrin","1) O3; 2) Zn, H2O — oxidative cleavage","1) H2O, H2SO4 (cat.) — Markovnikov hydration","1) OsO4; 2) NaHSO3 — gives a 1,2-diol","1) BH3-THF; 2) H2O2, NaOH — hydroboration-oxidation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"2-Butanol has the OH on the internal, more substituted carbon of what was 1-butene, so it is the Markovnikov hydration product. Acid-catalyzed hydration (water with catalytic H2SO4) protonates the alkene to give the secondary carbocation at C2, and water adds there. Hydroboration-oxidation would give the anti-Markovnikov alcohol 1-butanol; OsO4 gives a 1,2-diol; Br2 in water gives a bromohydrin; and ozonolysis cleaves the double bond entirely.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: 2-butanol SMILES correct; Markovnikov hydration gives the secondary alcohol; reagent-selection, two concepts (regiochem + reagent), in scope."},{"q":"Three alkyl bromides are compared. Among the three drawn (tert-butyl bromide is shown), which substrate reacts FASTEST in an SN2 reaction with the strong nucleophile CN- in acetone?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)Br","opts":["tert-Butyl bromide — the most substituted carbon","All three react at the same SN2 rate","Methyl bromide (CH3Br) — least steric hindrance at carbon","Neopentyl bromide — primary but with a bulky beta-tert-butyl group","tert-Butyl bromide, because it forms the most stable carbocation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"SN2 rate is governed by steric access to the back side of the carbon bearing the leaving group: methyl is fastest, then primary, then secondary, and tertiary is essentially unreactive. Methyl bromide, with only three small hydrogens around the reacting carbon, reacts fastest with cyanide. tert-Butyl bromide is so hindered that it does not undergo SN2 at all (it reacts by SN1 instead), and carbocation stability is an SN1 argument with no bearing on SN2 rate. Neopentyl bromide is primary, but the bulky tert-butyl group on the adjacent carbon blocks the backside approach and makes it one of the slowest SN2 substrates known.","difficulty":2,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: stem references three substrates but only tert-butyl bromide is drawn; the drawn molecule is valid and relevant context, and the SN2 trend answer (methyl fastest) is unambiguous. Acceptable as classification/ranking item, two concepts, in scope."},{"q":"Acetic acid (drawn) is heated with excess ethanol and a catalytic amount of H2SO4 (Fischer conditions). The product is then identified by IR. Which single answer correctly pairs the product with its diagnostic IR change relative to the starting acid?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)O","opts":["Product is ethyl acetate; a new broad O-H stretch appears and the C=O disappears","Product is ethanol only; all carbonyl absorption is lost","Product is acetamide; a sharp N-H stretch (~3300) replaces the carbonyl","Product is acetaldehyde; the C=O shifts to ~1715 and an aldehyde C-H (~2720) appears","Product is ethyl acetate; the broad O-H stretch (~2500-3300 cm-1) of the acid disappears while a strong C=O (~1740 cm-1) remains"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Fischer esterification converts acetic acid and ethanol, with catalytic H2SO4 and excess alcohol to push the equilibrium, into ethyl acetate plus water. In the IR, the carboxylic acid's extremely broad O-H stretch spanning roughly 2500 to 3300 cm-1 disappears, while a strong C=O stretch remains, now near 1740 cm-1 for the ester. No new O-H appears (that would indicate an alcohol or acid, not an ester), no N-H appears because no nitrogen is involved, and the acid is not reduced to acetaldehyde or ethanol, so the aldehyde C-H near 2720 cm-1 is absent.","difficulty":3,"group":"multistep-synthesis","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Valid: acetic acid SMILES correct; Fischer ester product and qualitative IR regions are accurate. IR is qualitative region recognition (not J-coupling/exact-shift math), so within DAT scope; hard layered (reaction + IR) item appropriate as difficulty 3."},{"q":"The alkene shown is treated with HBr in the presence of peroxides (ROOR). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)CC=C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(C)CCCBr","CC(C)CC(C)Br","CCC(Br)C(C)C","CCCCC(C)Br","CC(C)CCCO"],"correct":0,"why":"The alkene is 4-methyl-1-pentene. With peroxides present, HBr adds by a radical mechanism: the bromine radical adds to the terminal CH2 so that the unpaired electron sits on the more substituted secondary carbon, and that radical then abstracts H from HBr. The bromine therefore ends up on the terminal carbon, giving 1-bromo-4-methylpentane. 2-Bromo-4-methylpentane is the Markovnikov product you would get without peroxides, 4-methyl-1-pentanol would need water, and the other two bromides have rearranged carbon skeletons that this reaction cannot produce.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (radical regiochemistry), valid SMILES, in scope, plausible distractors. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (shown) is treated with Br2 in CCl4. Which structure correctly represents a product of this addition?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["C[C@@H](Br)[C@@H](Br)C","CC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC(Br)C(Br)C","CCC(C)Br","C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](Br)C"],"correct":4,"why":"Br2 adds anti: a cyclic bromonium ion forms first and bromide opens it from the opposite face. Anti addition to cis-2-butene gives the chiral 2,3-dibromobutane as a racemic mixture of (2R,3R) and (2S,3S); the structure drawn is the (2R,3R) enantiomer. The (2R,3S) structure is the meso compound, which is the product of trans-2-butene, so it is the stereochemical trap. 2,3-Dibromobutane drawn without stereochemistry leaves the stereochemical question unanswered, 2,3-dibromopentane has the wrong skeleton, and 2-bromobutane is a monobromide that HBr, not Br2, would give.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). RDKit: opt0=(R,R) chiral, opt1=(R,S) meso — matches the 'cis gives d,l, trans gives meso' logic. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"Cyclohexene is subjected to the two-step sequence: (1) mCPBA; (2) H3O+ (aqueous acid). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C1CCC=CC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","O=C1CCCCC1","C1CCC2OC2C1","O[C@@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCCC1"],"correct":0,"why":"mCPBA converts cyclohexene into cyclohexene oxide. In aqueous acid the epoxide oxygen is protonated and water attacks one epoxide carbon from the face opposite the C-O bond (backside attack), so the ring opens anti. The two OH groups end up on opposite faces of the ring: trans-cyclohexane-1,2-diol, which is chiral and forms as a racemic mixture. The cis diol (the meso compound) is the syn product of cold KMnO4 or OsO4 and is the trap. The epoxide itself is only the intermediate after the first step, and cyclohexanol and cyclohexanone are not products of epoxide opening.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"KEYING ERROR FIXED: options 0 and 1 were swapped. RDKit (meso test) and 3D embedding both show opt0 = cis/meso diol and opt1 = trans/chiral diol. Anti opening gives trans, so correct index is 1, not 0. Chemistry/scope fine; corrected index.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then Zn/AcOH (reductive workup). Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O","CC1CCCCC1=O","CC(O)CCCCCO","CC(=O)CCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC=O"],"correct":3,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the C=C completely. Because the double bond of 1-methylcyclohexene is inside the ring, cutting it opens the ring into a single acyclic chain with a carbonyl at each end. With reductive workup (Zn/AcOH) the carbons stay at the aldehyde and ketone level: the methyl-bearing carbon becomes a methyl ketone and the former CH becomes an aldehyde, giving 6-oxoheptanal. 6-Oxoheptanoic acid would need an oxidative workup (H2O2), hexanedial has lost the methyl carbon, the diol would require reducing both carbonyls, and 2-methylcyclohexanone keeps the ring intact, which is impossible once an endocyclic double bond is cleaved.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Ring-opening ozonolysis is a recognition-level concept (one C=C cleaved), not a multi-step synthesis; stays in scope. Distractor 5 (ring intact) is a strong trap. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"2-Pentyne (shown) is hydrogenated with H2 over Lindlar's catalyst. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC#CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC=CC","CCCCC","CC/C=C/C","C=CC(C)C","CC/C=C\\C"],"correct":4,"why":"Lindlar's catalyst is a poisoned palladium that hydrogenates alkynes to alkenes and then stops. Both hydrogens are added to the same face of the triple bond (syn addition), so the two alkyl groups end up on the same side of the new double bond: 2-pentyne gives cis-2-pentene, the (Z) isomer. trans-2-Pentene is the dissolving-metal (Na/NH3) product and is the stereochemical trap; pentane is complete hydrogenation over an unpoisoned catalyst; 2-pentene drawn without geometry does not answer the stereochemical question; and 3-methyl-1-butene has a rearranged skeleton.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (syn reduction stereochem), valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane (shown) is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a bulky strong base. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(Br)C","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC=C(C)C","CCOC(C)(C)CC","CCC(C)(C)O","C=C(C)CC","C=CC(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is strong but bulky, so in this E2 it pulls the most accessible beta hydrogen, one on the terminal CH3, instead of a more hindered CH2 hydrogen. The result is the less substituted Hofmann alkene, 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-2-butene, the more substituted Zaitsev alkene, is the trap: it would be the major product with a small base such as ethoxide. 2-Methyl-2-butanol and the ethyl ether are substitution products, and 3-methyl-1-butene has a different carbon skeleton that cannot arise from this bromide.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Two layered concepts (E2 + Hofmann regiochemistry from steric base) is allowed. Valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"3-Pentanol (shown) is oxidized with PCC (pyridinium chlorochromate) in CH2Cl2. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"CCC(O)CC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(=O)CCO","CCCC(=O)O","CCCC(C)=O","CCC(O)C(C)O","CCC(=O)CC"],"correct":4,"why":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous oxidant. A secondary alcohol oxidizes to a ketone, and because no hydrogen is left on the carbonyl carbon, a ketone cannot be oxidized further: 3-pentanol gives 3-pentanone. Butanoic acid is a trap on two counts (a secondary alcohol can never become a carboxylic acid, and it has the wrong number of carbons); 2-pentanone has the carbonyl on the wrong carbon; and the hydroxy ketone and the diol each add an oxygen that was never there.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (PCC selectivity), valid SMILES, in scope. Note opt4 (C4) has a different carbon count, but it is clearly the wrong over-oxidation trap and not confusable. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with dilute H2SO4/H2O (acid-catalyzed hydration). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC1=CCCCC1O","CC1CCCCC1O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","OCC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Acid-catalyzed hydration goes through the more stable carbocation. Protonating the CH carbon of 1-methylcyclohexene puts the positive charge on the methyl-bearing tertiary carbon; water adds there and loses a proton, giving 1-methylcyclohexanol, the Markovnikov tertiary alcohol. 2-Methylcyclohexanol is the anti-Markovnikov product that hydroboration-oxidation would give; cyclohexylmethanol puts the OH on the methyl carbon, which was never part of the double bond; the allylic alcohol still contains the C=C; and methylcyclohexane is the hydrogenation product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"multistep-synthesis","issue":"Correct as keyed (index 0). Single concept (Markovnikov regiochemistry), valid SMILES, in scope. No change.","q_smiles_valid":true},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"1,3-Butadiene reacts with ethylene (heat). What is the major product of this [4+2] cycloaddition?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["C1=CCCCC1","C1CCCCC1","c1ccccc1","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A Diels-Alder reaction is a [4+2] cycloaddition: the four pi electrons of the diene and the two pi electrons of the dienophile form two new sigma bonds and a six-membered ring. The terminal carbons of 1,3-butadiene bond to the two carbons of ethylene, and the former central single bond of the diene becomes the new double bond, so the product is cyclohexene. Cyclohexane would need a separate hydrogenation, 1,4-cyclohexadiene has one double bond too many, benzene would need aromatization, and cyclopentene has the wrong ring size for a [4+2] process.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"2,3-Dimethyl-1,3-butadiene undergoes a Diels-Alder reaction with ethylene. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=C(C)C(C)=C","opts":["Cc1ccccc1C","CC1(C)CCCC=C1","CC1CCCCC1C","CC1=CC(C)CCC1","CC1=C(C)CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"In a Diels-Alder reaction the diene's two internal carbons (C2 and C3) become the carbons of the new ring double bond. In 2,3-dimethyl-1,3-butadiene those carbons carry the methyl groups, so the product with ethylene is 1,2-dimethylcyclohexene, with both methyls on the alkene carbons. 1,3-Dimethylcyclohexene and the geminal 6,6-dimethylcyclohexene misplace the methyls, 1,2-dimethylcyclohexane is fully saturated (the cycloaddition keeps one C=C), and o-xylene is aromatic, which a single cycloaddition does not produce.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"1,3-Butadiene is heated with maleic anhydride (the dienophile shown). What is the major cycloaddition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1OC(=O)C=C1","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CCC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CC=CC12","O=C1OC(=O)C2CCCCC12","O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21","O=C1OC(=O)C2C=CCCC12"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The electron-poor C=C of maleic anhydride is an excellent dienophile. Its cycloaddition with 1,3-butadiene builds a new cyclohexene ring whose double bond lies between the two former internal diene carbons, across the ring from the two carbons that join the anhydride: cis-cyclohex-4-ene-1,2-dicarboxylic anhydride (tetrahydrophthalic anhydride). The fully saturated anhydride has lost the double bond the reaction must leave behind, phthalic anhydride is aromatic, the isomer with the double bond next to the ring fusion has the alkene in the wrong place, and the cyclopentene-fused anhydride has the wrong ring size for a [4+2] process.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"Isoprene (2-methyl-1,3-butadiene) reacts with acrolein (CH2=CH-CHO). Applying the 'para' regiochemistry rule, which is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)=C","opts":["O=CC1CCC(C)=CC1","O=CC1CC(C)=CCC1","OCC1CCC(C)=CC1","O=CC1CCC=CC1","O=CC1CCCC(C)=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Isoprene is a 2-substituted diene, and acrolein is a monosubstituted dienophile. For this pairing the major regioisomer places the diene substituent and the dienophile substituent 1,4 to each other on the new ring, the 'para' product. The ring methyl ends up on one of the new alkene carbons, giving 4-methylcyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde. The 'meta' isomer, 3-methylcyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde, has the two groups 1,3; cyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde has lost the methyl; the primary alcohol would require a reduction the Diels-Alder reaction does not perform; and the isomer with the double bond moved next to the aldehyde carbon has the wrong connectivity.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"(E)-1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene reacts with acrolein (CH2=CH-CHO). Applying the 'ortho' regiochemistry rule, which is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC=CC=C","opts":["O=CC1CCC=CC1","COC1CCC=CC1C=O","O=CC1CCC=CC1OC","O=CC1CC=CCC1OC","O=CC1CC=CC(OC)C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"(E)-1-Methoxy-1,3-butadiene is a 1-substituted diene and acrolein is a monosubstituted dienophile; for this pairing the major regioisomer places the two substituents on adjacent ring carbons, the 'ortho' product. The methoxy carbon (the diene's C1) bonds to the aldehyde-bearing carbon of acrolein, and the new ring double bond forms between the diene's former C2 and C3, right next to the methoxy carbon. The product is 2-methoxycyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde. The isomer with the methoxy and aldehyde 1,3 to each other is the 'meta' arrangement; cyclohex-3-ene-1-carbaldehyde has lost the methoxy group; and the other two adjacent isomers have the ring double bond in the wrong position relative to the substituents.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"Cyclopentadiene reacts with maleic anhydride. According to the endo rule, which bicyclic product predominates?","q_smiles":"C1C=CC=C1","opts":["O=C1OC(=O)c2ccccc21","O=C1OC(=O)C2CCC=CC2C1","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3CCC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C3C=CC(C3)C12","O=C1OC(=O)C2C=CC1C2"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Cyclopentadiene is a cyclic diene, so its Diels-Alder adduct is bicyclic: the anhydride ring is fused to a bicyclo[2.2.1]heptene (norbornene) framework that keeps one C=C in the two-carbon bridge. The product is cis-norbornene-5,6-dicarboxylic anhydride (carbic anhydride), and the endo rule says the anhydride sits under the alkene bridge (endo) in the kinetic product because of secondary orbital overlap. Phthalic anhydride is aromatic, the saturated norbornane anhydride has lost the required double bond, and the other two structures have fused rings or bridging patterns that no [4+2] of cyclopentadiene can produce.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"For the diene shown (1,3-butadiene) to undergo a Diels-Alder reaction, which conformational requirement must be met?","q_smiles":"C=CC=C","opts":["The diene must be locked in the s-trans conformation","The diene must first isomerize to an alkyne","The diene must aromatize before reacting","The dienophile must be electron-rich for the reaction to proceed","The diene must adopt the s-cis conformation so both terminal carbons can reach the dienophile"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"A Diels-Alder is a concerted [4+2] cycloaddition in which both ends of the diene bond to the dienophile at the same time, so the two terminal carbons of 1,3-butadiene must point toward the same side. Only the s-cis conformation puts them there; the s-trans conformer holds the termini too far apart and cannot react, so butadiene must rotate about its central single bond into s-cis before reacting. The diene does not need to isomerize to an alkyne or become aromatic, and a good dienophile is electron-poor, not electron-rich.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"diels-alder","q":"(E)-Dimethyl fumarate (the trans-diester dienophile shown) reacts with 1,3-butadiene. Because the reaction is stereospecific, which product forms?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)/C=C/C(=O)OC","opts":["COC(=O)C1C=CCCC1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)[C@H]1CC=CC[C@@H]1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)[C@H]1CC=CC[C@H]1C(=O)OC","COC(=O)C1CCCCC1C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"The Diels-Alder is stereospecific: the dienophile adds suprafacially, so the relationship between the two ester groups across the double bond is carried into the ring. Dimethyl fumarate is the trans (E) diester, so the product is the trans-1,2-diester, dimethyl trans-cyclohex-4-ene-1,2-dicarboxylate, drawn here as the (S,S) enantiomer (the reaction gives this and its (R,R) mirror image as a racemate). The cis diester is the meso compound that dimethyl maleate would give. Dimethyl phthalate is aromatic and has the wrong skeleton, the saturated cyclohexane diester has lost the ring alkene, and the remaining isomer has its double bond next to an ester-bearing carbon, which is not where the new pi bond forms.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"q":"Methyl vinyl ketone is treated with KCN followed by aqueous acid workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)C","opts":["CC(=O)CCC#N","CC(=O)C(C#N)C","C=CC(O)(C)C#N","CC(O)CCC#N","CC(=O)CC#N"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Cyanide is a soft, stabilized nucleophile, so it adds to the beta carbon of methyl vinyl ketone (conjugate or 1,4-addition) rather than to the carbonyl carbon. The enolate that forms is protonated on workup and tautomerizes back to the ketone, leaving the nitrile on the terminal carbon: 4-oxopentanenitrile. Attaching cyanide at the alpha carbon (2-methyl-3-oxobutanenitrile) is the wrong regiochemistry, the cyanohydrin keeps the alkene and comes from 1,2-addition, which is disfavored for a soft nucleophile, and 3-oxobutanenitrile is one carbon short.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclohex-2-en-1-one is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi, a Gilman reagent), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["OC1(C)CCCC=C1","CC1CCCCC1=O","CC1CCC(=O)CC1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1","OC1CCCCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Gilman reagents (organocuprates) are soft carbon nucleophiles that add 1,4 to enones: the methyl group bonds to the beta carbon and the resulting enolate is protonated on workup to regenerate the ketone. The product is 3-methylcyclohexanone, with the methyl two carbons from the carbonyl. 2-Methylcyclohexanone would have the methyl on the alpha carbon and 4-methylcyclohexanone on the gamma carbon, neither of which is the electrophilic site. 1-Methylcyclohex-2-en-1-ol is the 1,2-adduct that an organolithium or Grignard reagent would give, and 2-methylcyclohexanol requires a carbonyl reduction that a cuprate does not perform.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclohex-2-en-1-one is treated with methyllithium (CH3Li) at low temperature, then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts":["CC1CCCCC1=O","OC1(C)CCCC=C1","OC1(C)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC(=O)C1","CC1CCC(=O)CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Methyllithium is a hard, strongly basic nucleophile, so it attacks the carbonyl carbon directly (1,2-addition) rather than the beta carbon. Aqueous workup gives the tertiary allylic alcohol 1-methylcyclohex-2-en-1-ol, with the ring double bond untouched. 3-Methylcyclohexanone is the conjugate (1,4) product typical of a cuprate, 2-methyl- and 4-methylcyclohexanone place the methyl on carbons that are not electrophilic, and 1-methylcyclohexanol has lost the alkene, which nothing here reduces.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"trans-Chalcone (1,3-diphenylprop-2-en-1-one) is treated with dimethyl malonate and a catalytic amount of sodium methoxide. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this Michael reaction?","q_smiles":"O=C(/C=C/c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(c1ccccc1)C(C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC)c1ccccc1","OC(/C=C/c1ccccc1)(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(c1ccccc1)CC(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC","O=C(c1ccccc1)C=C(c1ccccc1)C(C(=O)OC)C(=O)OC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The malonate enolate is a soft, stabilized carbon nucleophile, so it undergoes Michael (1,4) addition to the beta carbon of chalcone, the alkene carbon bearing the second phenyl. Protonation of the resulting enolate restores the ketone, giving the 1,5-dicarbonyl adduct in which the malonate CH is attached to the benzylic carbon and a CH2 links that carbon to the benzoyl group: dimethyl 2-(3-oxo-1,3-diphenylpropyl)malonate. The tertiary alcohol is the 1,2-adduct, which is disfavored for this soft nucleophile; the structure that keeps the alkene would require substitution at a vinylic carbon; the mono-ester has lost one ester group; and the structure with the malonate directly on the carbon next to the ketone is one carbon short of the real skeleton.","difficulty":2,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Acrolein (prop-2-enal) is treated with piperidine. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this aza-Michael (conjugate) addition?","q_smiles":"C=CC=O","opts":["O=CCCN1CCCCC1","O=CCN1CCCCC1","O=CC(C)N1CCCCC1","C=CC(O)N1CCCCC1","C=CC(=O)N1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A secondary amine such as piperidine adds to an alpha,beta-unsaturated aldehyde by conjugate (aza-Michael) addition: nitrogen bonds to the beta carbon, and the enol intermediate tautomerizes back to the aldehyde. The product is 3-(piperidin-1-yl)propanal, with the nitrogen two carbons from the CHO. Putting the nitrogen on the alpha carbon (2-(piperidin-1-yl)propanal) is the wrong regiochemistry, the hemiaminal from 1,2-addition keeps the alkene and is not the stable product, and 2-(piperidin-1-yl)acetaldehyde is missing a carbon.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Methyl acrylate is treated with thiophenol (PhSH) and a trace of base. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this thia-Michael addition?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)OC","opts":["COC(=O)C(C)Sc1ccccc1","COC(=O)CCSc1ccccc1","C=CC(OC)(O)Sc1ccccc1","COC(=O)CSc1ccccc1","COC(=O)C=CSc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Thiols are excellent soft nucleophiles, and with a trace of base the thiolate adds 1,4 to the beta carbon of methyl acrylate; protonation then gives the saturated ester. The product is methyl 3-(phenylthio)propanoate, with sulfur on the terminal carbon. Methyl 2-(phenylthio)propanoate places sulfur on the alpha carbon, the vinyl sulfide would require substitution at an sp2 carbon with the alkene kept, methyl 2-(phenylthio)acetate is one carbon short, and the structure from attack at the ester carbonyl is not how a thiolate reacts with an acrylate.","difficulty":1,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Cyclopent-2-en-1-one (shown) can react with carbon nucleophiles to give either 1,2- or 1,4-addition products. Which reagent is MOST likely to give predominantly the 1,4 (conjugate) addition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC=C1","opts":["Sodium borohydride is irrelevant; all four add 1,2","Lithium diphenylcuprate (Ph2CuLi)","Phenylmagnesium bromide (PhMgBr) at low temperature","Phenyllithium (PhLi)","Lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"Organocuprates (Gilman reagents) are the textbook reagents for conjugate (1,4) addition to an enone: the soft, relatively non-basic organocopper species delivers its carbon group to the beta carbon. Lithium diphenylcuprate therefore gives 3-phenylcyclopentanone. Phenyllithium and, for the most part, phenylmagnesium bromide are hard nucleophiles that add 1,2 to the carbonyl, and lithium aluminum hydride is a hydride source that reduces the carbonyl rather than acting as a carbon nucleophile. The statement that all of these add 1,2 is false.","difficulty":2,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone) is treated with lithium dimethylcuprate (Me2CuLi), then aqueous workup. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)CC(C)c1ccccc1","CC(O)(C)/C=C/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)/C=C(C)/c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)Cc1ccccc1","CC(O)CC(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Lithium dimethylcuprate delivers its methyl group by conjugate (1,4) addition to the beta carbon of benzalacetone, the carbon that carries the phenyl ring. Protonation of the enolate on workup regenerates the ketone, giving 4-phenylpentan-2-one. 3-Methyl-4-phenylbutan-2-one has the methyl on the alpha carbon, the tertiary allylic alcohol is the 1,2-adduct an organolithium would give, the enone with a methyl on the beta carbon has kept the alkene, and 4-phenylpentan-2-ol would require a carbonyl reduction that a cuprate does not perform.","difficulty":3,"group":"michael","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"group":"michael","q":"(E)-Pent-3-en-2-one is treated with KCN (a soft nucleophile) and then aqueous acid. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCC(C#N)CC=O","CCCC(C)=O","CC(C#N)CC(C)=O","CC(=O)C(C)CC#N","C/C=C/C(C)(O)C#N"],"correct":2,"why":"Cyanide is a soft nucleophile, so it adds to the beta carbon of (E)-pent-3-en-2-one (the alkene carbon bearing the methyl) in a 1,4-addition; the enolate is protonated on workup and the ketone returns. The product is 2-methyl-4-oxopentanenitrile, with the nitrile carbon attached to the carbon two bonds from the carbonyl carbon. The cyanohydrin keeps the alkene and comes from 1,2-addition, which is disfavored here; the aldehyde and the isomer with the nitrile on a carbon beyond the alpha position have skeletons that cannot arise from this enone; and pentan-2-one has no nitrile at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct 1,4-addition regiochemistry verified; clean distractors; single right answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"For the conjugate (1,4) addition versus direct (1,2) addition to cyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn), which reagent is the BEST choice when you specifically want the 1,4-conjugate-addition product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC=C1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Lithium dimethylcuprate, (CH3)2CuLi (a Gilman reagent)","Sodium borohydride then methylation","Methylmagnesium bromide, CH3MgBr","Methyllithium, CH3Li","Lithium aluminum hydride, LiAlH4"],"correct":0,"why":"Gilman reagents (lithium dialkylcuprates) are soft carbon nucleophiles that strongly prefer conjugate (1,4) addition to an enone, placing the alkyl group on the beta carbon; lithium dimethylcuprate is therefore the reagent to pick when you want 3-methylcyclohexanone. Methylmagnesium bromide and methyllithium are hard organometallics that add 1,2 to the carbonyl and give the tertiary allylic alcohol instead. Lithium aluminum hydride delivers hydride, not a carbon group, and reducing with sodium borohydride and then methylating never forms a carbon-carbon bond at the beta position.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Reagent-selection item, valid stem SMILES, single defensible answer (Gilman for 1,4)."},{"group":"michael","q":"4-Phenylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) reacts with ethanethiol and a catalytic base (a thia-Michael addition). Considering both regiochemistry and relative stereochemistry, which structure best represents the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC1(SCC)CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","O=C1CC[C@@H](c2ccccc2)[C@H](SCC)C1","O=C1C(SCC)CC(c2ccccc2)C=C1","O=C1CCC(c2ccccc2)C=C1SCC","O=C1CC[C@@H](c2ccccc2)[C@@H](SCC)C1"],"correct":1,"why":"A thiol under basic catalysis is a soft sulfur nucleophile, so it adds 1,4 to the beta carbon of the enone, which here is the ring carbon next to the phenyl-bearing carbon. After protonation the saturated ketone 3-(ethylthio)-4-phenylcyclohexanone results. Of its two diastereomers, the trans isomer (ethylthio and phenyl on opposite faces of the ring) is the major product because the thiolate approaches from the face away from the phenyl group. The cis diastereomer is the minor product; the tertiary alcohol is the 1,2-adduct; and the structure with sulfur on the alpha carbon and the vinyl sulfide both keep the alkene and have the wrong regiochemistry.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Minor: the trans-major stereochemistry claim is an over-statement (facial selectivity here is modest), but regiochemistry is correct and exactly one answer is defensible. Keep; consider softening the stereochem framing."},{"group":"michael","q":"Methyl acrylate (drawn) is treated with dimethylamine (an aza-Michael / conjugate addition). Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(=O)OC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CNCCC(=O)OC","C=CC(OC)(OC)N(C)C","COC(=O)C(C)N(C)C","CN(C)CCC(=O)OC","C=CC(=O)N(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"Dimethylamine is a nucleophilic secondary amine, and with an alpha,beta-unsaturated ester it undergoes conjugate (aza-Michael) addition: nitrogen bonds to the terminal beta carbon and the enolate is protonated, leaving the ester intact. The product is methyl 3-(dimethylamino)propanoate. Methyl 2-(dimethylamino)propanoate has nitrogen on the alpha carbon, N,N-dimethylacrylamide would come from attack at the ester carbonyl (acyl substitution) with the alkene kept, methyl 3-(methylamino)propanoate carries only one N-methyl and so cannot come from dimethylamine, and the orthoester-like structure is not a sensible product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Aza-Michael beta-amino ester correct; distractors clean (secondary-amine distractor confirmed as NHMe)."},{"group":"michael","q":"(E)-4-Phenylbut-3-en-2-one (benzalacetone, drawn) can give either the 1,2- or the 1,4-addition product with methyl-metal reagents. Which statement correctly matches the reagent to its preferred mode of addition?","q_smiles":"C/C(=O)/C=C/c1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Both reagents add 1,4 because conjugation always dominates","(CH3)2CuLi adds 1,2 (to the carbonyl); CH3MgBr adds 1,4 (to the beta-carbon)","(CH3)2CuLi adds 1,4 (to the beta-carbon); CH3MgBr adds 1,2 (to the carbonyl)","Neither reagent reacts; an enolate is required for any addition to an enone","Both reagents add 1,2 because the carbonyl is the only electrophilic site"],"correct":2,"why":"Site selectivity for addition to an enone is governed by hard/soft character, not by conjugation alone. The soft organocuprate (lithium dimethylcuprate) prefers conjugate (1,4) addition, delivering methyl to the beta carbon to give 4-phenylpentan-2-one after workup, whereas the hard, more reactive Grignard reagent (methylmagnesium bromide) adds 1,2 to the carbonyl to give the tertiary allylic alcohol. The statements that both reagents add the same way, that the pairing is reversed, or that no reaction occurs without an enolate are all incorrect.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Standard hard/soft reagent-matching; valid stem SMILES; single right answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"In the base-catalyzed conjugate addition of a stabilized enolate to (E)-but-2-enal (crotonaldehyde, drawn), which description correctly identifies where the nucleophile's curved arrow points and the immediate intermediate formed?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The nucleophile attacks the beta-carbon; the arrows give an enolate intermediate that is protonated on oxygen/carbon during workup","The nucleophile attacks the alpha-carbon; the arrows give a carbocation intermediate","The nucleophile attacks the carbonyl carbon; the arrows give an alkoxide intermediate (1,2-addition)","The nucleophile attacks the carbonyl oxygen; the arrows give an oxocarbenium intermediate","A hydride shifts from the alpha-carbon to the beta-carbon before any nucleophile adds"],"correct":0,"why":"In conjugate (1,4) addition the nucleophile attacks the electrophilic beta carbon of crotonaldehyde: its curved arrow points to that carbon, the C=C pi electrons shift toward the carbonyl carbon, and the C=O pi electrons move onto oxygen. The immediate intermediate is a resonance-stabilized enolate, which is protonated (on oxygen to give the enol, or on carbon) during workup. Attack at the carbonyl carbon is 1,2-addition and gives an alkoxide instead; attack at the alpha carbon, attack at the carbonyl oxygen, and a hydride shift before addition are not mechanistically reasonable.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Conceptual arrow/intermediate item stays at recognition depth (no full mechanism required); valid stem; single answer."},{"group":"michael","q":"5,5-Dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one (drawn) is treated with the anion of nitromethane (-CH2NO2), a stabilized carbon nucleophile, then mild acid. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CC(C)(C)CC=C1","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["O=C1CC(C)(C)CCC1C[N+](=O)[O-]","OC1(C[N+](=O)[O-])CC(C)(C)CC=C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(ON=O)C1","O=C1CC(C)(C)CC(C[N+](=O)[O-])C1","O=C1C(C[N+](=O)[O-])C(C)(C)CC=C1"],"correct":3,"why":"The nitronate anion is a soft, resonance-stabilized carbon nucleophile, so it adds 1,4 to the beta carbon of 5,5-dimethylcyclohex-2-en-1-one; mild acid then protonates the enolate and the saturated ketone returns. The product is 5,5-dimethyl-3-(nitromethyl)cyclohexanone, with the new carbon-carbon bond two carbons from the carbonyl. The tertiary alcohol is the 1,2-adduct with the alkene retained; the two structures with the nitromethyl group on the alpha carbon (one keeping the alkene, one saturated) have the wrong regiochemistry; and the nitrite ester has oxygen, not carbon, attached to the ring, so no carbon-carbon bond was made.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Nitronate 1,4-addition at beta verified; gem-dimethyl is a spectator; clean regio/O-vs-C distractors; single answer."},{"q":"Two molecules of ethyl acetate (drawn) are treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), followed by acidic workup. What is the major organic product of this Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC(O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CCC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Sodium ethoxide deprotonates the alpha carbon of one ethyl acetate molecule; that enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second molecule, and the tetrahedral intermediate expels ethoxide. The beta-ketoester product is ethyl acetoacetate (ethyl 3-oxobutanoate). Ethyl 3-hydroxybutanoate is an aldol-type product in which ethoxide was never lost, diethyl malonate has no new carbon-carbon bond, 2,4-pentanedione would require losing the ethoxy group as carbon, and the aldehyde-ester (ethyl 3-oxopropanoate) is one carbon short.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate (drawn) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then mild acid. Predict the major beta-ketoester product of the self-Claisen condensation.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C(C)=O","CCC(O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"In the self-Claisen of ethyl propanoate, ethoxide removes a proton from the CH2 next to the ester; that enolate (which still carries its methyl) attacks the carbonyl of a second ester and ethoxide is expelled. The product is ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate: a propanoyl group from the electrophile on one side, and the alpha carbon still bearing its methyl on the other. Ethyl 3-oxopentanoate has lost the alpha-methyl, ethyl 3-oxohexanoate has a straight chain that cannot come from propanoate, the beta-hydroxy ester is an aldol-type product that never eliminated ethoxide, and the diketone has lost the ester entirely.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl butanoate (drawn) undergoes a Claisen condensation with itself using NaOEt, then acidic workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(=O)C(CC)C(C)=O","CCCC(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCCC(O)C(CC)C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Ethyl butanoate has an alpha CH2 that carries an ethyl group; its enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ester and ethoxide leaves. The self-Claisen product is ethyl 2-ethyl-3-oxohexanoate: a butanoyl group on one side and the alpha carbon that keeps its ethyl on the other. Ethyl 3-oxohexanoate has dropped the alpha-ethyl, the 2-methyl isomer has the wrong branch, the beta-hydroxy ester never eliminated ethoxide, and the diketone has lost the ester.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate, drawn) is treated with one equivalent of sodium ethoxide, then acid. What is the major product of this intramolecular (Dieckmann) condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)CCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCC1C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A Dieckmann condensation is an intramolecular Claisen: the enolate of one ester attacks the other ester within the same molecule, closing a ring. Diethyl adipate (a 1,6-diester) closes to the strain-free five-membered ring, so the product is ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate. The six-membered beta-ketoester would require a seven-carbon diester (pimelate), the four-membered ring is strained and not formed, the acyclic beta-ketoester is the intermolecular product rather than the favored ring closure, and cyclopentane-1,2-dione has no ester.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate, drawn) is subjected to NaOEt and then acidic workup. Predict the major Dieckmann condensation product.","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C1CCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCCCC1C(=O)OCC","O=C1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","OC1CCCCC1C(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl pimelate (a 1,7-diester) undergoes an intramolecular Claisen (Dieckmann) condensation: the enolate at one alpha carbon attacks the other ester, ethoxide leaves, and a six-membered ring closes. The product is ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate. The five-membered ring would come from adipate (one carbon shorter), the seven-membered ring is far less favorable, the acyclic structure is the intermolecular product, and the beta-hydroxy ester would require the ketone to be reduced.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate (drawn) is mixed with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, then worked up with acid. In this crossed Claisen condensation, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CC(=O)C(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(c1ccccc1)CC(=O)OCC","CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","O=C(c1ccccc1)CC(N)=O","O=C(CC(=O)OCC)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ethyl benzoate has no alpha hydrogens, so it cannot form an enolate and serves only as the electrophile; ethyl acetate supplies the enolate. That enolate attacks the benzoate carbonyl and ethoxide is expelled, giving ethyl benzoylacetate (ethyl 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoate). Ethyl acetoacetate is the self-condensation product of ethyl acetate alone, the beta-hydroxy ester never eliminated ethoxide, the 1,2-diketone has the wrong connectivity, and the amide would need a nitrogen source that is not present.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate (drawn) is treated with ethyl acetate and sodium ethoxide, followed by acidic workup. What is the major crossed-Claisen product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)OCC","O=CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha hydrogens, so it acts only as the electrophilic acceptor; the ethyl acetate enolate attacks one of its carbonyls and ethoxide leaves. The crossed-Claisen product is diethyl oxaloacetate (diethyl 2-oxobutanedioate). Ethyl acetoacetate is the self-condensation product that ignores the oxalate, the hydroxy diester would require reduction of the ketone, the aldehyde-containing structure has the wrong carbon count, and the structure with two alpha-keto ester units is an over-condensation product.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"A student wants to convert ethyl acetate (drawn) into ethyl acetoacetate by a Claisen condensation. Which base/condition is the best choice to drive the reaction to product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(C)=O","opts":["Dilute HCl in water","Sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in ethanol","Sodium borohydride (NaBH4) in methanol","Pyridine, a weak amine base","Aqueous sodium hydroxide (NaOH) at room temperature"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"A Claisen condensation needs a base strong enough to make the ester enolate, and the base should match the ester's alkoxide so that it does not swap out the alkoxy group. Sodium ethoxide in ethanol is the standard base for ethyl acetate: it forms the enolate, and any attack at the carbonyl simply regenerates the same ethyl ester. Aqueous sodium hydroxide saponifies the ester and water quenches the enolate, dilute HCl generates no enolate at all, sodium borohydride reduces carbonyls rather than deprotonating alpha carbons, and pyridine is far too weak a base to deprotonate an ester (alpha pKa about 25).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"claisen"},{"q":"Ethyl propanoate is treated with a stoichiometric amount of sodium ethoxide, then worked up with dilute acid. Which structure is the major beta-ketoester product of this self-Claisen condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC","CCC(=O)OCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CCC(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)CCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Ethoxide deprotonates the alpha carbon of ethyl propanoate, which carries a methyl group; that enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second ester, and ethoxide is expelled from the tetrahedral intermediate. The product is ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate: a propanoyl group on one side, the alpha carbon still holding its methyl on the other. Ethyl 3-oxohexanoate has lost that methyl (the acetate-like trap), ethyl 4-oxohexanoate spaces the carbonyls one carbon too far apart, the 2,4-dimethyl isomer has an extra methyl, and the ester with no new carbon-carbon bond has not condensed at all.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl adipate (diethyl hexanedioate) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acidified. Which is the major product of this intramolecular Dieckmann condensation?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","O=C1CCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl adipate is a 1,6-diester, so an intramolecular Claisen (Dieckmann) condensation closes the favored five-membered ring: the enolate of one ester attacks the other, ethoxide leaves, and the product is ethyl 2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate. The six-membered beta-ketoester would require a 1,7-diester, the four-membered ring is too strained to form this way, ethyl cyclopentanecarboxylate lacks the ketone, and cyclopentanone has lost the ester.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl pimelate (diethyl heptanedioate) is subjected to Dieckmann conditions (NaOEt, then H3O+). What is the major cyclic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCCCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Diethyl pimelate is a 1,7-diester, so the Dieckmann ring closure forms the favored six-membered ring: ethyl 2-oxocyclohexanecarboxylate. The five-membered beta-ketoester is what adipate (one carbon shorter) gives, the seven-membered ring is disfavored, ethyl cyclohexanecarboxylate has no ketone, and cyclohexanone has lost the ester.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Ethyl benzoate and ethyl acetate are mixed with sodium ethoxide (a crossed Claisen), then worked up with acid. Ethyl benzoate is drawn below. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(=O)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)c1ccccc1","O=C(CC(=O)c1ccccc1)c1ccccc1","CCOC(=O)c1ccccc1C(=O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Ethyl benzoate has no alpha hydrogens, so it is the electrophile; the ethyl acetate enolate attacks its carbonyl and ethoxide leaves, giving ethyl benzoylacetate (ethyl 3-oxo-3-phenylpropanoate). Ethyl benzoylformate lacks the CH2 between the two carbonyls, ethyl 3-oxo-4-phenylbutanoate has an extra CH2 and would come from ethyl phenylacetate, dibenzoylmethane would need two benzoyl groups and no ester, and acylation on the aromatic ring does not happen under these conditions.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"In the base-promoted Claisen/Dieckmann condensation (ethyl phenylacetate shown), what is the first mechanistic event, and why is a full equivalent of strong base required to reach completion?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","opts":["Alkoxide adds directly to the ester carbonyl first; base is consumed building the tetrahedral intermediate","Alkoxide removes an alpha proton to form a resonance-stabilized ester enolate; a full equivalent is needed because the acidic beta-ketoester product is deprotonated, pulling the equilibrium forward","Loss of ethoxide occurs before any deprotonation, generating an acylium ion that is then attacked","The ester is protonated to activate the carbonyl, so acid rather than base drives the reaction","An alpha proton is removed only after the new C-C bond forms; the base is purely catalytic"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"The Claisen begins with ethoxide removing an alpha proton from the ester to form a resonance-stabilized enolate; that enolate then attacks a second ester carbonyl, and ethoxide is expelled. The carbon-carbon bond-forming step is roughly thermoneutral and reversible, so what drives the reaction is the final, essentially irreversible deprotonation of the product: the proton between the two carbonyls of the beta-ketoester (pKa about 11) is far more acidic than ethanol, and that deprotonation consumes a full equivalent of base. Direct addition of alkoxide to the carbonyl, loss of ethoxide to an acylium ion, acid activation, and a merely catalytic base all misstate the order of events or the driving force.","difficulty":1,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl oxalate and ethyl propanoate are combined with sodium ethoxide (a crossed Claisen), then acidified. Diethyl oxalate is shown below. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)C(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(C)C(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)C(C)C(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CC(=O)OCC","CCOC(=O)C(=O)CCC(=O)OCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Diethyl oxalate has no alpha hydrogens and serves only as the electrophile; ethoxide deprotonates the alpha carbon of ethyl propanoate (which keeps its methyl), that enolate attacks one oxalate carbonyl, and ethoxide is expelled. The product is diethyl 2-methyl-3-oxobutanedioate, the methyl-bearing analog of diethyl oxaloacetate. Diethyl oxaloacetate itself has lost the alpha-methyl, the diester with the carbonyls spaced an extra carbon apart has the wrong skeleton, ethyl 2-methyl-3-oxopentanoate is the propanoate self-Claisen product that ignores the oxalate, and the large structure with propanoate units on both oxalate carbonyls is a double condensation product.","difficulty":2,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate (shown) is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acid. Of the two possible Dieckmann ring closures, which beta-ketoester is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)CCCC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1CCCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCC(C)C1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CCCC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1CC(C)CC1=O","CCOC(=O)C1(C)CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Diethyl 2-methyladipate has two different alpha carbons, so two ring closures are possible, and the Dieckmann condensation is reversible. If the methyl-bearing alpha carbon attacks the far ester, the ring carbon between the two carbonyls becomes quaternary (ethyl 1-methyl-2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate) and has no acidic proton, so nothing drives that equilibrium forward and it reverses. When the unsubstituted CH2 enolate attacks the methyl-substituted ester instead, the product keeps an acidic proton between the ketone and the ester, is deprotonated under the reaction conditions, and is thereby pulled forward: ethyl 3-methyl-2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate, with the methyl on the ring carbon next to the ketone. The six-membered ring has the wrong size for a 1,6-diester, the unsubstituted cyclopentanone ester has lost the methyl, and the 4-methyl isomer puts the methyl on the wrong ring carbon.","difficulty":3,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Rationale names the product 4-methyl-2-oxocyclopentanecarboxylate; strict IUPAC locants give 3-methyl-2-oxo. Naming nit only; the drawn structure (opt0) is the correct, unique product."},{"q":"Ethyl cyclopropanecarboxylate is treated with sodium ethoxide and then acidified, undergoing a self-Claisen condensation. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C1CC1","opts":["CCOC(=O)C1(CC1)C(=O)CC","CCOC(=O)CC(=O)C1CC1","CCOC(=O)C1(CC1)C(=O)C1CC1","O=C(C1CC1)C1CC1","CCOC(=O)C1CC1C(=O)C1CC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The only alpha carbon in ethyl cyclopropanecarboxylate is the ring carbon bearing the ester, which has a single hydrogen. Its enolate attacks the carbonyl of a second molecule and ethoxide leaves, so that ring carbon becomes quaternary, carrying the ester, the new cyclopropanecarbonyl group, and its two ring bonds: ethyl 1-(cyclopropanecarbonyl)cyclopropane-1-carboxylate. Acylation on a ring CH2 would be at a beta carbon that is never deprotonated, the open-chain beta-ketoester would require an ethyl acetate enolate, the propanoyl analog has the wrong acyl group, and dicyclopropyl ketone has lost the ester.","difficulty":2,"group":"claisen","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br), then (3) aqueous HCl with heat. What is the major final organic product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)Cc1ccccc1","O=C(O)C(Cc1ccccc1)C(=O)O","O=C(O)CCCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)CCc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"This is a malonic ester synthesis. Ethoxide deprotonates the doubly activated central carbon, benzyl bromide alkylates it by SN2, and hot aqueous acid then hydrolyzes both esters to the diacid, which decarboxylates (loses CO2) because it is a beta-dicarboxylic acid. The malonate contributes a CH2-COOH unit, so the product is 3-phenylpropanoic acid. Phenylacetic acid is one carbon short, 4-phenylbutanoic acid is one carbon too long, benzylmalonic acid is what remains if you forget the decarboxylation, and phenylacetone is a methyl ketone, which is the acetoacetic ester route rather than the malonate route.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) ethyl iodide (CH3CH2I), then (3) dilute aqueous H2SO4 with heat. What is the major final organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCCC(=O)O","CCC(=O)CC","CCCC(C)=O","CCC(C)=O","CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The acetoacetic ester synthesis makes methyl ketones. Ethoxide deprotonates the CH2 between the two carbonyls, ethyl iodide alkylates it, and hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes the ester to a beta-keto acid, which decarboxylates. The acetyl group plus the alkylated carbon and its new ethyl give 2-pentanone (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH3). 2-Hexanone is one carbon too long, 2-butanone corresponds to skipping the alkylation, 3-pentanone has the carbonyl in the wrong place, and butanoic acid is the carboxylic acid class that a malonic ester synthesis would give.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is subjected to (1) NaOEt, (2) 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), then (3) H3O+ and heat. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CC(C)CC(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O","CC(=O)CC(C)C","CC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)(C)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Ethoxide forms the malonate enolate, 2-bromopropane alkylates it once by SN2, and acid plus heat hydrolyzes both esters and decarboxylates the resulting malonic acid. The isopropyl group ends up on what becomes the alpha carbon of the acid, so the product is 3-methylbutanoic acid (isovaleric acid). 2-Methylpropanoic acid omits the CH2 that the malonate always contributes, pentanoic acid has a straight chain, 2,2-dimethylpropanoic acid would need a quaternary carbon, and 4-methyl-2-pentanone is the methyl ketone of the acetoacetic ester route.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then (3) aqueous acid and heat. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["C=CCC(C(C)=O)C(C)=O","C=CCCCC(C)=O","C=CCC(C)=O","C=CCCC(C)=O","C=CCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis: ethoxide deprotonates the central CH2, allyl bromide alkylates it by SN2, and hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes the ester to the beta-keto acid, which loses CO2. The product is the methyl ketone hex-5-en-2-one (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2). Pent-4-en-2-one is one carbon short, hept-6-en-2-one is one carbon too long, pent-4-enoic acid is the carboxylic acid product of a malonic ester synthesis, and the allylated 2,4-pentanedione keeps a second acetyl group that was never present.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with NaOEt then CH3I, and the product is treated again with NaOEt then CH3CH2I; finally it is heated in aqueous HCl. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["CCC(C)C(=O)O","CCCC(C)C(=O)O","CC(C)C(=O)O","CCC(C)(C)C(=O)O","CCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Each round of sodium ethoxide followed by an alkyl halide installs one group on the central malonate carbon: a methyl in the first round, then an ethyl. Hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes both esters and the beta-dicarboxylic acid decarboxylates, leaving one carboxyl on a carbon that holds both alkyl groups and one hydrogen: 2-methylbutanoic acid. 2-Methylpentanoic acid has one carbon too many, 2-methylpropanoic acid lacks the ethyl, pentanoic acid has no branch, and 2,2-dimethylbutanoic acid would need a third alkylation, which is impossible because the central carbon only ever had two acidic hydrogens.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with (1) NaOEt, (2) benzyl bromide, then (3) hot aqueous acid. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"CC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts":["O=C(O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","CCC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone. Ethoxide deprotonates the doubly activated CH2, benzyl bromide alkylates it, and hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes the ester and decarboxylates the beta-keto acid. The acetyl group stays on the alkylated carbon, so the product is 4-phenylbutan-2-one (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-Ph). Phenylacetone is one carbon short, 5-phenylpentan-2-one is one carbon too long, 1-phenylbutan-2-one has the carbonyl on the wrong side of the benzyl group, and 3-phenylpropanoic acid is the carboxylic acid class of the malonic ester route.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,4-dibromobutane (BrCH2CH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous HCl. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. What is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCCCCBr","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCCC1","O=C(O)C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCC1","O=C(O)CCCCCC(=O)O","O=C(O)C1CCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"With two equivalents of base, the central carbon of diethyl malonate is alkylated twice by the same molecule of 1,4-dibromobutane: one SN2 attaches the chain, the second closes it back onto the same carbon. That carbon plus the four-carbon tether makes a five-membered ring, so the intermediate is a cyclopentane with a geminal diester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation then leave cyclopentanecarboxylic acid. Cyclobutanecarboxylic acid would need a three-carbon dibromide and cyclohexanecarboxylic acid a five-carbon one, the open-chain diacid never closed the ring, and cyclopentanone has no carboxyl group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1 equivalent of 1,3-dibromopropane (BrCH2CH2CH2Br), then heated in aqueous acid. The dibromide drawn is the alkylating agent. Which structure is the major final product?","q_smiles":"BrCCCBr","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Two equivalents of base let the central carbon of ethyl acetoacetate be alkylated twice by 1,3-dibromopropane, the second alkylation closing a ring onto the same carbon. That carbon plus the three-carbon tether gives a four-membered ring that still carries the acetyl and the ester. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation remove the ester carbon and leave the methyl ketone: cyclobutyl methyl ketone (1-cyclobutylethanone). Cyclopropyl methyl ketone would need a two-carbon tether and cyclopentyl methyl ketone a four-carbon one, the open-chain diketone never closed the ring, and cyclobutanecarboxylic acid is the carboxylic acid that the malonic ester route would give.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none"},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is treated with sodium ethoxide and then benzyl bromide (PhCH2Br). The product is then heated with aqueous acid (hydrolysis) and decarboxylated. What is the final carboxylic acid product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","OC(=O)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1","OC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","OC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1"],"correct":0,"why":"Malonic ester synthesis: ethoxide deprotonates the central carbon, benzyl bromide alkylates it, and hot aqueous acid hydrolyzes both esters and decarboxylates the malonic acid. Because the malonate always contributes a CH2-COOH unit, one extra carbon sits between the ring and the carboxyl: the product is 3-phenylpropanoic acid. Phenylacetic acid is the classic trap (it forgets that spacer carbon), 4-phenylbutanoic acid is one carbon too long, 2-phenylpropanoic acid has the wrong branching, and benzoic acid has no chain at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is deprotonated with NaOEt, alkylated with allyl bromide (CH2=CHCH2Br), then subjected to hydrolysis and decarboxylation. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCC=C","OC(=O)C(C)CC=C","CC(O)CCC=C","CC(=O)CC=C","CC(=O)CCC=C"],"correct":4,"why":"Acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone. The central carbon is deprotonated and allylated, then hydrolysis converts the ester to a beta-keto acid that loses CO2. The acetyl group and the alkylated carbon remain, so the product is hex-5-en-2-one (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-CH=CH2), a six-carbon chain. Pent-4-en-2-one drops a CH2, hex-5-en-2-ol would require reducing the ketone, and the two carboxylic acids belong to the malonic ester route, not this one.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"Rationale mis-names the product as 'hept-6-en-2-one'; correct name is hex-5-en-2-one. The structure/answer (idx 0) is correct and unaffected."},{"q":"The diester shown (a malonate dialkylated with one methyl and one ethyl group) is hydrolyzed in hot aqueous acid and decarboxylated. What carboxylic acid is formed?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)(CC)C(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CC(C)C","OC(=O)CCCC","OC(=O)C(C)CC","OC(=O)C(C)(C)C","OC(=O)C(CC)CC"],"correct":2,"why":"Hydrolysis of the dialkylated malonate gives a geminal dicarboxylic acid, and a beta-dicarboxylic acid loses exactly one CO2 on heating. The carbon that carried both esters keeps its methyl and ethyl groups and gains a hydrogen, so the product is 2-methylbutanoic acid. 2-Ethylbutanoic acid has the wrong substituents, 3-methylbutanoic acid and pentanoic acid have the branch in the wrong place or no branch at all, and 2,2-dimethylpropanoic acid would need two methyls on the alpha carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Diethyl malonate is alkylated with 2-bromopropane (isopropyl bromide), then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final carboxylic acid?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(=O)OCC","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)C(C)C","OC(=O)CCCC","OC(=O)C(C)CC","OC(=O)CC(C)C","OC(=O)C(C)(C)C"],"correct":3,"why":"The malonate enolate displaces bromide from 2-bromopropane, attaching an isopropyl group to the central carbon. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation then turn that carbon into the alpha carbon of a monoacid while keeping the CH2-COOH unit that malonate always supplies, so the product is 3-methylbutanoic acid (isovaleric acid). 2-Methylpropanoic acid is the trap that omits the malonate CH2 spacer, 2-methylbutanoic acid and pentanoic acid have the wrong skeletons, and 2,2-dimethylpropanoic acid would require a quaternary carbon.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with NaOEt and benzyl bromide, then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["OC(=O)CCCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)Cc1ccccc1","CC(=O)CCc1ccccc1","CC(=O)c1ccccc1","CC(=O)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"correct":2,"why":"The acetoacetic ester synthesis gives a methyl ketone. Benzylation of the deprotonated central carbon, followed by hydrolysis and decarboxylation, leaves the acetyl group on the alkylated carbon: 4-phenylbutan-2-one (CH3-CO-CH2-CH2-Ph). Phenylacetone drops the CH2 that the acetoacetate carbon supplies, 3-phenylbutan-2-one puts the phenyl on the wrong carbon, acetophenone has no chain, and 4-phenylbutanoic acid is the carboxylic acid of the malonic ester route.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with 2 equivalents of NaOEt and 1,4-dibromobutane (Br-CH2CH2CH2CH2-Br). Two successive intramolecular alkylations occur at the same alpha-carbon, and the product is then hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. What is the final product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CC(=O)C1CCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","CC(=O)CCCCC(C)=O","O=C1CCCCC1"],"correct":2,"why":"Two equivalents of base allow the central carbon of ethyl acetoacetate to be alkylated twice by 1,4-dibromobutane, the second alkylation closing a ring onto the same carbon. That carbon plus the four CH2 groups of the tether forms a five-membered ring. Hydrolysis and decarboxylation then remove the ester carbon, leaving the acetyl group on the ring: cyclopentyl methyl ketone (1-cyclopentylethanone). Cyclobutyl and cyclohexyl methyl ketone are each off by one ring carbon, the open-chain diketone never closed the ring, and cyclohexanone has lost the acetyl group.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Correct as written (idx 0); ring-size count verified."},{"q":"The dialkylated acetoacetic ester shown (alpha-carbon bearing both a methyl and an ethyl group) is hydrolyzed and decarboxylated. Which statement best describes the structure and stereochemistry of the product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)C(C)(CC)C(C)=O","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The product is 3-methylpentanoic acid because decarboxylation converts the ketone to a carboxylic acid","The product is achiral because the alpha-carbon retains no stereocenter after decarboxylation","The product is 4-methyl-2-pentanone with the branch on the carbon beta to the carbonyl","The product is 3-methyl-2-pentanone, obtained as a racemic mixture because the alpha-carbon becomes a stereocenter formed without facial selectivity","The product is 3-methyl-2-pentanone, obtained as a single (R) enantiomer"],"correct":3,"why":"Hydrolysis of the ester gives a beta-keto acid, which loses CO2 on heating; the ketone survives, so the product is 3-methyl-2-pentanone, CH3-CO-CH(CH3)-CH2CH3, not a carboxylic acid. The alpha carbon now carries four different groups (H, methyl, ethyl, acetyl), so it is a stereocenter, and it is created when the planar enol from decarboxylation is protonated from either face with equal probability, so the ketone forms as a racemic mixture. A single (R) enantiomer would require a chiral influence that is absent, 4-methyl-2-pentanone has the branch on the wrong carbon, and the carboxylic acid outcomes belong to the malonic ester route.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Classification item; q_smiles drawn correctly (idx 0)."},{"q":"Ethyl acetoacetate is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt) in the first step of an acetoacetic ester synthesis. Which species correctly represents the stabilized nucleophile generated, and why is that site deprotonated?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["CCOC(=O)CC([CH2-])=O","[CH2-]COC(=O)CC(C)=O","CCOC(=O)CC(C)=O","CC[O-]","CCOC(=O)[CH-]C(C)=O"],"correct":4,"why":"Ethoxide removes the most acidic proton in ethyl acetoacetate: one on the CH2 that sits between the ketone and the ester (pKa about 11). The resulting enolate is delocalized onto both carbonyl oxygens, which is why that site, and not the terminal methyl, is deprotonated. A carbanion on the acetyl methyl would be stabilized by only one carbonyl, a carbanion on the ethoxy CH2 has no stabilization at all, ethoxide is the base rather than the nucleophile produced, and unchanged ethyl acetoacetate shows no reaction.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"malonic-acetoacetic","issue":"None. Recognition-level acidity/resonance item, single reactive center, in scope. Correct (idx 0)."},{"q":"The substrate shown is heated in ethanol (a weak base/nucleophile), favoring an E1 pathway. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)CO","CCC(=C)C","CC=C(C)C","C=CCCC","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"2-Bromo-2-methylbutane is a tertiary halide, and hot ethanol is a weak base in a polar protic solvent, so the reaction goes by E1 through a tertiary carbocation. The cation then loses a beta proton to give the most substituted (Zaitsev) alkene, the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the disubstituted Hofmann alkene and is the minor product, 3-methyl-1-butene would place the double bond away from the cationic carbon, 1-pentene has an unbranched skeleton, and 2-methylbutan-1-ol is not a product of this reaction.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. All SMILES valid, single tertiary center, Zaitsev E1 correct, exactly one major product at idx0."},{"q":"The same tertiary bromide is treated with potassium tert-butoxide (KOtBu), a strong, bulky base, in an E2 reaction. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)Br","opts":["CCC(C)(C)O","CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","CCC(=C)C","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is a strong, bulky base, so it eliminates by E2 but cannot easily reach the more hindered beta hydrogens on the internal CH2. It removes a proton from one of the exposed methyl groups instead, so the Hofmann (less substituted) alkene wins: 2-methyl-1-butene. 2-Methyl-2-butene is the Zaitsev product that a small base such as ethoxide would favor, 3-methyl-1-butene would place the double bond away from the carbon that carried the bromine, 2-methyl-2-butanol is a substitution product, and the unreacted bromide is not a product.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hofmann/bulky-base logic correct; valid SMILES; one right answer at idx0."},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with a strong base under E2 conditions. E2 requires the C-H and C-Br bonds to be anti-periplanar (both axial). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC1CCCCC1Br","opts":["C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1","CC1=CCCCC1","CC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"E2 requires the beta hydrogen and the leaving group to be anti-periplanar, which on a cyclohexane ring means both must be axial. In trans-1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane, the chair that puts bromine axial puts the methyl equatorial, so the hydrogen on the methyl-bearing carbon is equatorial and cannot be anti to the bromine. The base must take the axial hydrogen on the other neighboring carbon instead, so the double bond forms away from the methyl: 3-methylcyclohexene, with the methyl on an sp3 allylic carbon. 1-Methylcyclohexene is the more substituted Zaitsev alkene but is geometrically blocked here, methylenecyclohexane would need the bromine on the methyl-bearing carbon, and 2-methylcyclohexanol and methylcyclohexane are substitution and reduction products, not alkenes.","difficulty":3,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Anti-periplanar stereoelectronic constraint correctly forces 3-methylcyclohexene; valid SMILES; legitimate difficulty-3 item, single answer at idx0."},{"q":"The alcohol shown is treated with concentrated H2SO4 and heat (E1 dehydration). Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)(C)O","opts":["CC=C(C)C","C=CC(C)C","C=CCCC","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CCC(=C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"2-Methyl-2-butanol is a tertiary alcohol, so hot concentrated sulfuric acid dehydrates it by E1: the hydroxyl is protonated, water leaves to give a tertiary carbocation, and a beta proton is lost. Zaitsev's rule applies, so the trisubstituted 2-methyl-2-butene is the major alkene. 2-Methyl-1-butene is the disubstituted Hofmann minor product, 3-methyl-1-butene would put the double bond away from the cationic carbon, 1-pentene has the wrong skeleton, and 2-chloro-2-methylbutane would need a chloride source that is not present.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Tertiary E1 dehydration, Zaitsev correct, valid SMILES, one answer at idx0."},{"q":"2-Bromohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (NaOEt), a strong but small base, in an E2 reaction. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CCCCC(C)Br","opts":["CC/C=C/CC","CCCCC(C)O","C/C=C\\CCC","C=CCCCC","C/C=C/CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Sodium ethoxide is a strong but small base, so the E2 elimination of 2-bromohexane follows Zaitsev's rule and favors the more substituted internal alkene, 2-hexene, over terminal 1-hexene. Of the two 2-hexene stereoisomers, the trans (E) alkene is the major product because the anti-periplanar transition state that leads to it keeps the two alkyl groups apart, with less strain. (Z)-2-Hexene is the minor stereoisomer, 1-hexene is the Hofmann product, 3-hexene cannot form because neither carbon of that double bond carried the bromine, and 2-hexanol is a substitution product.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev + E-selectivity correct; valid SMILES incl. E/Z; single major product at idx0."},{"q":"Consider the alkyl iodide shown reacting in a polar protic solvent (aqueous ethanol) with only heat and no added strong base. Which statement BEST describes the elimination that occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)I","opts":["E2: requires anti-periplanar geometry and a strong, bulky base like KOtBu","No elimination is possible because the substrate has no beta-hydrogens","E1: the rate is first order in the substrate and first order in the weak base","E1: the tertiary substrate ionizes to a carbocation; rate depends only on the substrate, not on base concentration","E2: a concerted one-step reaction whose rate depends on both substrate and a strong base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"tert-Butyl iodide is a tertiary substrate, and heat in aqueous ethanol with no strong base favors E1. The rate-determining step is ionization of the carbon-iodine bond to give the tert-butyl cation, so the rate is first order in substrate alone; the weak base only removes a proton afterward, in a fast step, and its concentration does not appear in the rate law. E2 would require a strong base and is bimolecular, E1 is never first order in the base, and the substrate does have beta hydrogens (on the methyls), so elimination to 2-methylpropene (isobutylene) is possible.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classification/mechanism item (opts_are_structures=false) is permitted and the molecule is still drawn (valid SMILES). One concept, single correct statement at idx0."},{"q":"2-Bromopentane undergoes E2 with a strong base. Because E2 eliminates the anti-periplanar beta-hydrogen and favors the most stable alkene, which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)Br","opts":["C=CCCC","CCC(C)CO","C=CC(C)C","C/C=C/CC","C/C=C\\CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"E2 on 2-bromopentane removes a beta hydrogen anti-periplanar to the bromine and gives the more stable, more substituted alkene, so the internal 2-pentene is favored over terminal 1-pentene (Zaitsev). The trans isomer predominates because the transition state leading to it keeps the two alkyl groups apart, so (E)-2-pentene is the major product. (Z)-2-Pentene is the minor stereoisomer, 1-pentene is the Hofmann product, 3-methyl-1-butene has a branched skeleton that cannot come from this halide, and 2-methylbutan-1-ol is not an elimination product.","difficulty":1,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Zaitsev + E-selectivity correct; valid SMILES; single answer at idx0."},{"q":"The secondary alcohol shown is heated with concentrated H2SO4. Acid-catalyzed dehydration proceeds through a carbocation that can rearrange. Which structure is the MAJOR alkene product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC=C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C","C=C(C)C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation of 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol and loss of water give a secondary carbocation sitting next to a quaternary carbon. A 1,2-methyl shift converts it into a much more stable tertiary cation, and loss of a beta proton from that rearranged cation gives the most substituted alkene: the tetrasubstituted 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene. 3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene is the unrearranged product and 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene is the rearranged but less substituted (Hofmann) alkene, both minor; the starting alcohol is not a product, and 2-methyl-2-butene has only five carbons.","difficulty":2,"group":"e1-e2","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Hydride/methyl-shift rearrangement to tertiary cation then Zaitsev correct; valid SMILES; single major product at idx0. Two layered concepts (rearrangement + Zaitsev) within scope."},{"q":"2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane is heated in ethanol (a weak base/nucleophilic solvent). Which structure is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)(C)Br","opts":["CC(C)C(=C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)OC","CCC=C(C)C","CC(C)C(C)(C)O","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"2-Bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane is tertiary and ethanol is a weak base and an ionizing solvent, so the reaction is E1 through a tertiary carbocation. Losing the beta hydrogen on the neighboring methine gives the tetrasubstituted Zaitsev alkene 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene, which is more stable than the disubstituted terminal alkene 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene (the Hofmann product). 2-Methyl-2-pentene has a different skeleton, and the tertiary alcohol and the methyl ether are substitution products (the ether would in any case need methanol, not ethanol).","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"trans-1-Bromo-2-methylcyclohexane is treated with sodium ethoxide (E2). Considering the anti-periplanar requirement, which is the major alkene?","q_smiles":"C[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1Br","opts":["CC1=CCCCC1","C=C1CCCCC1","CC1CCC=CC1","CC1(O)CCCCC1","CC1CCCC=C1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"E2 on a cyclohexane needs the C-Br bond axial with a beta hydrogen that is also axial on the neighboring carbon (anti-periplanar). In trans-1-bromo-2-methylcyclohexane, the chair that puts bromine axial also puts the C2 methyl axial, so the only hydrogen left on C2 is equatorial and cannot be removed; the tempting Zaitsev product, 1-methylcyclohexene, is blocked. The only anti-periplanar hydrogen is the axial one on C6, so the double bond forms away from the methyl and the major alkene is the less substituted 3-methylcyclohexene. Methylenecyclohexane and 4-methylcyclohexene put the double bond at carbons that never held the leaving group, and 1-methylcyclohexanol is a substitution product, not an alkene.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"2-Bromopentane is treated with potassium tert-butoxide, a bulky strong base. Which alkene is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCC(C)Br","opts":["cis-2-Pentene (more-substituted, cis)","trans-2-Pentene (more-substituted, trans)","1-Pentene (terminal, least-substituted alkene)","2-Methyl-2-butene (rearranged skeleton)","2-Pentanol (substitution product)"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Potassium tert-butoxide is a strong but very bulky base, so it cannot easily reach the crowded internal beta hydrogen on C3 and instead removes the exposed hydrogen on the terminal methyl. That gives the Hofmann product, 1-pentene, as the major alkene even though it is the less substituted one. A small strong base such as ethoxide would favor the Zaitsev product, trans-2-pentene; E2 is concerted, so no carbocation forms and no skeleton rearrangement to 2-methyl-2-butene is possible; and a strong base pushes elimination over substitution, so 2-pentanol is minor.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"2-Bromo-3-phenylbutane is treated with sodium ethoxide. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](Br)[C@H](C)c1ccccc1","opts":["C=CC(C)c1ccccc1","C/C=C(\\C)c1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1","C/C=C(/C)c1ccccc1","CC(Br)C(C)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Ethoxide removes a beta hydrogen in a concerted E2. Taking the benzylic hydrogen on C3 places the new double bond in conjugation with the ring, giving 2-phenyl-2-butene, the more substituted and conjugated (Zaitsev) product. Of its two geometries, the E isomer, with the phenyl and the C1 methyl on opposite sides, is more stable because the phenyl is not crowded by a cis methyl and can stay coplanar with the alkene; the drawn (2R,3R) diastereomer also delivers exactly this E alkene when the bromine and the benzylic hydrogen are anti-periplanar. The Z isomer is the less stable geometry, 3-phenyl-1-butene is the unconjugated Hofmann alkene, styrene has the wrong carbon count, and the unchanged bromide is the starting material.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":"Substrate is drawn as a single diastereomer, so a rigorously stereospecific E2 would fix the alkene geometry by anti-periplanar geometry rather than by thermodynamic stability; the why uses a stability argument. Both paths land on opt0 (conjugated CIP-E), so answer is unaffected, but the rationale slightly oversimplifies."},{"q":"3-Bromo-2,2-dimethylbutane is solvolyzed in warm ethanol (E1 conditions). Which structure is the major elimination product?","q_smiles":"CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)C(=C)C","CC(Br)=C(C)C","CC(O)C(C)(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C","C=CC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Under E1 conditions the bromide ionizes to a secondary carbocation on C3, right next to the quaternary C2. A 1,2-methyl shift converts it to the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation, and loss of a beta proton following Zaitsev gives the tetrasubstituted alkene 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene, the major product. Because E1 goes through a free carbocation, this rearranged alkene dominates; the unrearranged 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene would require a concerted E2 with strong base, 2,3-dimethyl-1-butene is the less substituted (Hofmann) alkene from the same cation, 3,3-dimethyl-2-butanol is a substitution product, and a vinyl bromide that keeps the bromine cannot come from an elimination that loses HBr.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"You have 2-iodobutane (a secondary substrate) and want to favor the Hofmann (less-substituted) alkene by E2. Which reagent choice best accomplishes this?","q_smiles":"CCC(C)I","opts":["Sodium iodide in acetone (weak base, good nucleophile)","Potassium tert-butoxide, a bulky strong base","Dilute aqueous acid with gentle heating","Sodium ethoxide, a small strong base","Sodium methoxide in methanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":1,"why":"E2 regiochemistry is controlled by the size of the base. Potassium tert-butoxide is strong enough to force E2 but so bulky that it is blocked from the internal beta hydrogen on C3 and removes the accessible terminal hydrogen on C1 instead, giving the Hofmann alkene, 1-butene. Small strong bases such as sodium ethoxide or sodium methoxide reach the internal hydrogen and give the Zaitsev product, 2-butene; sodium iodide in acetone is a weak base and good nucleophile that favors SN2, and dilute aqueous acid with gentle heating does not drive E2 at all.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"e1-e2","issue":""},{"q":"Sodium borohydride (NaBH4) in methanol is added to the compound shown. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"O=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["O=C(O)c1ccccc1","OCC1CCCCC1","O=Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Sodium borohydride is a mild hydride source that reduces aldehydes and ketones to alcohols. Hydride adds to the carbonyl carbon of benzaldehyde and methanol protonates the alkoxide, giving the primary alcohol, benzyl alcohol. A reducing agent cannot oxidize the aldehyde to benzoic acid, NaBH4 is far too mild to strip the oxygen off entirely to give toluene or to reduce the aromatic ring, and unchanged benzaldehyde is the starting material rather than a product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Correct, in scope, valid SMILES. Minor: opt3 duplicates the starting material as a no-reaction decoy, which is a slightly weak distractor but chemically unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexanone is treated with lithium aluminum hydride (LiAlH4), followed by aqueous workup. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCCC1","opts":["OC1CCCCC1","CCCCCC(=O)O","OC1CCCC1","C1CCCCC1","O=C1CCCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Lithium aluminum hydride delivers hydride to the carbonyl carbon of cyclohexanone, and the aqueous workup protonates the resulting alkoxide to give the secondary alcohol, cyclohexanol. Hydride reduction does not break carbon-carbon bonds, so the ring stays intact and the same size; it does not remove the oxygen completely to give cyclohexane, and it cannot oxidize anything to hexanoic acid. Unchanged cyclohexanone is the starting material, not the product.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt2 is a starting-material decoy (mild weakness) but the answer is unambiguous."},{"q":"1-Hexanol is treated with pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCCO","opts":["CCCC(=O)CC","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCCO","CCCCC(C)=O","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"PCC is a mild, anhydrous chromium(VI) oxidant, so it takes a primary alcohol only as far as the aldehyde: 1-hexanol becomes hexanal. The further step to hexanoic acid needs water to form the aldehyde hydrate, which aqueous Cr(VI) or KMnO4 would provide but PCC in dichloromethane does not. Oxidation happens only at the carbon that carried the OH, so the internal ketones 2-hexanone and 3-hexanone cannot form, and unchanged 1-hexanol is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Classic PCC selectivity item; clean single correct answer."},{"q":"Styrene (vinylbenzene) is hydrogenated with H2 over a palladium catalyst at room temperature and 1 atm. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccccc1","opts":["CCc1ccccc1","CCC1CCCCC1","CC(O)c1ccccc1","OCCc1ccccc1","C=Cc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Under mild catalytic hydrogenation (H2, Pd, room temperature, 1 atm) only the alkene is reduced: the vinyl group of styrene picks up two hydrogens and becomes an ethyl group, giving ethylbenzene. The aromatic ring is far more resistant and is hydrogenated only under high pressure and temperature with special catalysts, so ethylcyclohexane does not form. Hydrogenation adds no oxygen, so the two alcohols are impossible, and unchanged styrene is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt2 is a starting-material decoy but the answer is unambiguous."},{"q":"Cyclohexene is treated with meta-chloroperoxybenzoic acid (mCPBA) in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","C1CC2OC2CC1","C1=CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"mCPBA, a peroxyacid, transfers one oxygen atom to the alkene in a single concerted step, converting cyclohexene into its epoxide, cyclohexene oxide (1,2-epoxycyclohexane). The trans-1,2-diol would appear only after a separate ring-opening step with aqueous acid or base that is not part of these conditions. No carbonyl forms, so cyclohexanone is wrong, cyclohexanol would need a hydration, and unchanged cyclohexene is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. opt4 is a starting-material decoy; the diol distractor adds good discrimination."},{"q":"Cyclopentene is treated with osmium tetroxide (OsO4), then aqueous NaHSO3. Which structure (including stereochemistry) is the major product?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCC[C@H]1O","C1CC2OC2C1","O[C@H]1CCC[C@@H]1O","OC1CCCC1","O=C1CCCC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"Osmium tetroxide adds both oxygens through a cyclic osmate ester, so the two new OH groups land on the same face of the ring (syn addition); NaHSO3 simply cleaves the osmate to release the diol. On cyclopentene that gives cis-cyclopentane-1,2-diol, the meso (R,S) isomer. The trans diol would require anti addition (the epoxide-opening route), the epoxide itself comes from a peroxyacid rather than OsO4, cyclopentanol is a hydration product, and cyclopentane-1,2-dione would need further oxidation of the diol.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Stereochemistry verified: opt0 = cis/meso (correct), opt1 = trans (distractor). Two layered concepts (dihydroxylation + stereo) is within scope."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene is treated with ozone (O3), then dimethyl sulfide (Me2S) in the reductive workup. What is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["CC(=O)O","O=CCC=O","CCC=O","CC(C)=O","CC=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Ozonolysis cleaves the double bond completely; each alkene carbon becomes a carbonyl carbon, and the dimethyl sulfide workup is reductive, so the products stay at the aldehyde or ketone level. In cis-2-butene each alkene carbon carries one hydrogen and one methyl, so both halves become the same two-carbon fragment: two equivalents of acetaldehyde. Acetic acid would require an oxidative workup (H2O2), while acetone, propanal, and malondialdehyde correspond to carbon skeletons that 2-butene does not contain.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Symmetric alkene gives a single aldehyde product; clean and unambiguous."},{"q":"Methyl hexanoate is treated with one equivalent of diisobutylaluminum hydride (DIBAL-H) at -78 degrees C, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCCCCC(=O)OC","opts":["CCCCC=O","CCCCCC(=O)OC","CCCCCC=O","CCCCCCO","CCCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"One equivalent of DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C delivers a single hydride to the ester carbonyl, and the resulting tetrahedral aluminum hemiacetal is stable at that low temperature; it collapses to the carbonyl only during the aqueous workup, so the ester is reduced to the aldehyde, hexanal, and no further. Reduction to 1-hexanol would require excess hydride or LiAlH4, DIBAL-H is a reductant and cannot produce hexanoic acid, and the six-carbon chain stays intact, so pentanal (five carbons) is impossible. Unchanged methyl hexanoate is the starting material.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","issue":"None. Recognition-level DIBAL partial reduction is a standard DAT-depth item; single reactive center, in scope. opt3 is a starting-material decoy."},{"q":"Methyl cyclopentanecarboxylate (shown) is treated with one equivalent of DIBAL-H at -78 C, followed by aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"COC(=O)C1CCCC1","opts":["O=C(O)C1CCCC1","OCC1CCCC1","CC(=O)C1CCCC1","COC(=O)C1CCCC1","O=CC1CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"A single equivalent of DIBAL-H at -78 degrees C transfers one hydride to the ester carbonyl and forms a tetrahedral aluminum hemiacetal that survives until the aqueous workup, where it collapses to the aldehyde: cyclopentanecarbaldehyde is the product. Over-reduction to cyclopentylmethanol would need excess DIBAL-H or LiAlH4, cyclopentanecarboxylic acid is an oxidation product, the methyl ketone would require forming a new carbon-carbon bond, and the unchanged ester is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"cis-2-Butene (shown) is treated with mCPBA. Which structure is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C/C=C\\C","opts":["CC(O)C(C)O","C/C=C/CO","C[C@H]1O[C@H]1C","CCC(C)=O","C[C@H]1O[C@@H]1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"A peroxyacid delivers its oxygen to the alkene in one concerted step, so both new C-O bonds form on the same face and the alkene geometry is locked into the epoxide. cis-2-Butene therefore gives cis-2,3-dimethyloxirane, the meso epoxide with both methyls on the same side of the ring. The trans epoxide would come from trans-2-butene, the 2,3-diol would need a further hydrolysis step (or OsO4 instead of mCPBA), 2-butanone would require an oxidation state change at carbon, and the allylic alcohol is not an epoxidation product.","difficulty":2,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Meso (R,S) = cis-epoxide confirmed achiral by RDKit mirror-image test; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"Cyclohexene (shown) is treated with OsO4 followed by aqueous NaHSO3 workup. Which is the major product, with correct stereochemistry?","q_smiles":"C1=CCCCC1","opts":["O[C@H]1CCCC[C@H]1O","C1CCC2OC2C1","O=C1CCCCC1","OC1CCCCC1","O[C@H]1CCCC[C@@H]1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"OsO4 adds both oxygens to the alkene through a five-membered cyclic osmate ester, so the two OH groups are delivered to the same face (syn addition); the bisulfite workup releases the diol. Cyclohexene therefore gives cis-cyclohexane-1,2-diol, the meso (R,S) diastereomer. The trans diol would require anti addition, which is the epoxide-then-ring-opening route rather than the OsO4 route; the epoxide needs a peroxyacid; cyclohexanone would need oxidation at a single carbon; and cyclohexanol is a hydration product.","difficulty":2,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. cis-diol = meso (R,S) confirmed achiral by RDKit; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"1-Methylcyclohexene (shown) is treated with O3, then with dimethyl sulfide (Me2S). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC1=CCCCC1","opts":["CC(=O)CCCC(C)=O","CC1(O)CCCCC1","O=CCCCCCC=O","O=CCCCCC(C)=O","CC(=O)CCCCC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Ozone cleaves the ring double bond, and the dimethyl sulfide workup keeps the fragments at the carbonyl level. Breaking a ring C=C does not split the molecule in two; it opens the ring into a single chain with a carbonyl at each former alkene carbon. The carbon that carried the methyl becomes a ketone and the carbon that carried a hydrogen becomes an aldehyde, so the product is 6-oxoheptanal, a seven-carbon keto aldehyde. Heptane-2,6-dione wrongly makes both ends ketones, heptanedial wrongly makes both ends aldehydes and ignores the methyl, the keto acid would need an oxidative workup, and 1-methylcyclohexanol is a hydration product with no cleavage.","difficulty":3,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. opt0 = OHC-(CH2)4-CO-CH3 = 6-oxoheptanal; regiochemistry (Me carbon->ketone) confirmed; correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"Ethyl 4-oxopentanoate (shown) is treated with NaBH4 in methanol. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCOC(=O)CCC(C)=O","opts":["CC(=O)CCC(=O)O","CC1CCC(=O)O1","CC(=O)CCCO","CCOC(=O)CCC(C)O","CC(O)CCCO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"NaBH4 is a mild hydride donor: it reduces aldehydes and ketones but is not reactive enough to reduce esters. It therefore reduces only the ketone of ethyl 4-oxopentanoate to a secondary alcohol and leaves the ethyl ester untouched, giving ethyl 4-hydroxypentanoate. Pentane-1,4-diol would need LiAlH4 to reduce the ester as well, reducing the ester while leaving the ketone is backwards selectivity, the lactone would require a separate cyclization step, and the keto acid is a hydrolysis product rather than a reduction.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"N,N-Dimethylbenzamide (shown) is treated with LiAlH4, then aqueous workup. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CN(C)C(=O)c1ccccc1","opts":["O=Cc1ccccc1","CN(C)CC1CCCCC1","CN(C)Cc1ccccc1","OCc1ccccc1","O=C(O)c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"LiAlH4 reduces an amide all the way to an amine: the carbonyl oxygen is removed and the C=O becomes a CH2 while the carbon-nitrogen bond is kept, so N,N-dimethylbenzamide becomes N,N-dimethylbenzylamine. Keeping the nitrogen attached is the signature of amide reduction. Benzaldehyde (a partial reduction with loss of the amine) would need a milder, controlled reagent; benzyl alcohol and benzoic acid both require breaking the C-N bond, which hydride does not do with amides; and the cyclohexyl product wrongly reduces the aromatic ring, which LiAlH4 never touches.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed by re-derivation."},{"q":"(E)-2-Hexen-1-ol (shown) is treated with PCC in dichloromethane. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CCC/C=C/CO","opts":["CCC/C=C/C(=O)O","CCC/C=C/C=O","CCCCCC=O","CCCC1OC1C=O","CCC/C=C\\C=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"PCC in dry dichloromethane oxidizes a primary alcohol only to the aldehyde, with no water present to allow over-oxidation to the acid, and it does not react with carbon-carbon double bonds. (E)-2-Hexen-1-ol therefore becomes (E)-2-hexenal with the alkene geometry untouched. The carboxylic acid is the over-oxidation PCC avoids, hexanal has lost its double bond (PCC does not reduce alkenes), the Z aldehyde has the wrong geometry since nothing here isomerizes the alkene, and the epoxy aldehyde would need a peroxyacid.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. correct index 0 confirmed; E geometry preserved in product SMILES."},{"q":"4-Nitrostyrene (shown) is treated with H2 over a Pd catalyst at room temperature (one equivalent of H2). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=Cc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","opts":["CCc1ccc([N+](=O)[O-])cc1","CCc1ccc(N=O)cc1","CCC1CCC([N+](=O)[O-])CC1","CCc1ccc(N)cc1","C=Cc1ccc(N)cc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"A single equivalent of H2 over Pd at room temperature is consumed by the most easily hydrogenated group, the terminal alkene, which becomes an ethyl group; the aromatic ring and the nitro group are left alone, so the product is 1-ethyl-4-nitrobenzene. Taking the nitro group down to an aniline would use three equivalents of H2, and reducing the ring to a cyclohexane needs high pressure and heat. 4-Ethylaniline therefore over-reduces, 4-aminostyrene reduces the wrong group, and the partially reduced nitroso compound is not an isolable product of catalytic hydrogenation.","difficulty":1,"group":"oxidation-reduction-overview","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Defensible at DAT depth: with limiting H2 and emphasized chemoselectivity, alkene-only reduction (opt0) is the intended single answer; aromatic-NO2 reduction by H2/Pd requires more forcing conditions. correct index 0 confirmed."},{"q":"The allyl aryl ether shown is heated to 200 C with no catalyst or solvent. Which structure is the MAJOR product of this Claisen rearrangement?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["CC=Cc1ccccc1O","Oc1ccccc1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1ccccc1O","C=CCOc1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Aromatic Claisen [3,3]-sigmatropic shift: allyl migrates to the ortho ring carbon, then tautomerization restores aromaticity to give 2-allylphenol (C9H10O conserved). With an unsubstituted symmetric allyl, the terminal vinyl is regenerated. Distractors are unchanged ether, para isomer (only when both ortho blocked), internal-alkene isomer, and plain phenol.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). All SMILES valid; mass-balanced C9H10O across stem and right answer."},{"q":"Allyl vinyl ether is warmed. Identify the MAJOR product of this aliphatic Claisen [3,3]-sigmatropic rearrangement.","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["CCCCC=O","C=CCCC=O","CC=CCC=O","CC(=O)CC=C","C=COCC=C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Aliphatic Claisen converts an allyl vinyl ether into a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl. The six-membered TS breaks O-CH2(allyl), forms a new C-C bond, and turns the vinyl C=C into C=O, giving 4-pentenal (C5H8O conserved). Distractors: unchanged ether, isomeric methyl ketone, 3-pentenal, and over-reduced pentanal.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). 4-pentenal C5H8O matches the C5H8O ether; pentanal distractor is correctly C5H10O (an obvious over-reduction)."},{"q":"(2E,4Z,6E)-octatriene aside, consider the parent (1Z)-1,3,5-hexatriene drawn here undergoing a thermal 6-pi-electron electrocyclic ring closure. Which structure is the product?","q_smiles":"C=CC=CC=C","opts":["C1=CC2CC1C2","C1=CCC=CC1","C1=CCCC=C1","C1CCCCC1","c1ccccc1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"6-pi electrocyclic closure of 1,3,5-hexatriene forms one new sigma bond between the terminal carbons, giving 1,3-cyclohexadiene (C6H8 conserved). Distractors: 1,4-cyclohexadiene (wrong connectivity), benzene (would need loss of H2), cyclohexane (fully saturated), and a bicyclic skeleton (no such bond forms).","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Chemistry correct (index 0); C6H8 conserved into 1,3-cyclohexadiene, benzene distractor correctly C6H6. Minor: the '(2E,4Z,6E)-octatriene aside' preamble is clumsy and slightly distracting, but it does not affect the answer."},{"q":"When the compound shown is heated, it rearranges to 4-pentenal. Which term BEST classifies this transformation?","q_smiles":"C=COCC=C","opts":["Diels-Alder cycloaddition","[1,3]-sigmatropic shift","[3,3]-sigmatropic (Claisen) rearrangement","Electrocyclic ring closure","Cope rearrangement"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"An allyl vinyl ether rearranging to a gamma,delta-unsaturated carbonyl is the Claisen rearrangement, a concerted [3,3]-sigmatropic shift through a six-membered chair-like TS. Not a [1,3]-shift, not electrocyclic/Diels-Alder, and distinct from the all-carbon Cope (a 1,5-diene).","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Classification item with text options; q_smiles still drawn (valid). Correct (index 0)."},{"q":"3,4-Dimethyl-1,5-hexadiene is heated to drive a Cope rearrangement. Which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C(C)C=C","opts":["CC=CC=CCCC","C=CCCCCC=C","C=CC(C)C(C)C=C","CC=CCCC=CC","CC=CCC=CCC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"The Cope is the all-carbon [3,3]-shift of a 1,5-diene. The C3-C4 sigma bond breaks, a new sigma bond forms between the two former terminal CH2 carbons, and the double bonds shift inward, converting 3,4-dimethyl-1,5-hexadiene into octa-2,6-diene (C8H14 conserved). Distractors: unchanged diene, octa-2,5-diene, 1,7-octadiene, and a conjugated 2,4-diene isomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). Re-derived bond reorganization (break C3-C4, form C1-C6, shift to 2,6-diene) gives octa-2,6-diene; all options C8H14."},{"q":"The allyl aryl ether shown (both positions ortho to oxygen bear methyl groups) is heated. Because the ortho sites are blocked, which structure is the MAJOR Claisen product?","q_smiles":"C=CCOc1c(C)cccc1C","opts":["C=CCOc1c(C)cccc1C","Oc1c(C)cccc1C","C=CCc1cc(C)c(O)c(C)c1","C=CCc1ccc(O)cc1","C=CCc1c(C)cccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"With both ortho positions blocked, the initial ortho dienone cannot aromatize, so a second [3,3] (Cope) relay carries the allyl to the para position, giving 4-allyl-2,6-dimethylphenol (C11H14O conserved). Distractors: unchanged ether, an ortho product (impossible, ortho methylated), demethylated para, and the simple dimethylphenol (allyl is not lost).","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0); para-Claisen product C11H14O matches stem. Distractors 2/3/4 deliberately differ in formula (lose carbons), which is acceptable as wrong-skeleton lures."},{"q":"Crotyl phenyl ether (but-2-enyl phenyl ether) is heated to promote a Claisen rearrangement. Accounting for allylic inversion in the [3,3] shift, which structure is the MAJOR product?","q_smiles":"CC=CCOc1ccccc1","opts":["C=CC(C)c1ccccc1O","C=CC(C)c1ccc(O)cc1","CC=CCc1ccccc1O","CC=CCOc1ccccc1","CC(=CC)c1ccccc1O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"In the [3,3] Claisen the new C-C bond forms at the GAMMA carbon of the allyl unit, inverting the substitution pattern. For crotyl (CH3-CH=CH-CH2-O), bonding through the gamma (methyl-bearing) carbon puts that carbon on the ring and regenerates a terminal vinyl, giving 2-(but-3-en-2-yl)phenol. Distractors: non-inverted internal-alkene product, unchanged ether, para isomer, and a trisubstituted-alkene isomer.","difficulty":2,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). Verified allylic inversion: ring binds the gamma carbon bearing CH3, giving Ar-CH(CH3)-CH=CH2 ortho-phenol; all options C10H12O."},{"q":"(2E,4E)-2,4-hexadiene undergoes a THERMAL 4-pi-electron electrocyclic ring closure (conrotatory). Which structure correctly shows the stereochemistry of the 3,4-dimethylcyclobutene product?","q_smiles":"C/C=C/C=C/C","opts":["C[C@H]1[C@@H](C)C=C1","CC1=CC1C","C/C=C/C=C/C","C[C@H]1[C@H](C)C=C1","CC1CCC1C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"A thermal 4-pi (4n-electron) electrocyclic reaction is CONROTATORY by the Woodward-Hoffmann rules. With both terminal methyls in (2E,4E) geometry, conrotatory closure places the methyls on OPPOSITE faces, giving trans-3,4-dimethylcyclobutene (the chiral, non-meso diastereomer). Distractors: cis (meso) diastereomer (the disrotatory/photochemical outcome), two wrong skeletons, and the unreacted open triene.","difficulty":3,"group":"pericyclic-other","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Correct (index 0). RDKit confirms opt0 = (R,R), non-meso = trans; opt1 = (R,S) = meso = cis. Thermal conrotatory closure of (2E,4E) gives trans. Stereochemistry upper-edge but recognition-level WH rule, acceptable difficulty-3."},{"q":"3-Methyl-2-butanol is treated with concentrated HCl. Under these SN1 conditions, what is the major organic product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)C","ClCC(C)CC","CC(Cl)(C)CC","ClCCC(C)C","CC=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Concentrated HCl protonates the alcohol and water leaves, giving a secondary carbocation at C2. The adjacent C3 carries a hydrogen on a tertiary carbon, so a 1,2-hydride shift moves the positive charge to C3, converting the secondary cation into the more stable tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation. Chloride then captures that cation, so the major product is the rearranged tertiary chloride, 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. The unrearranged 2-chloro-3-methylbutane would require chloride to trap the less stable secondary cation before the shift, the two primary chlorides place chlorine on carbons that never carry positive charge, and 2-methyl-2-butene is the E1 side product rather than the substitution product the question asks for.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-2-butanol reacts with HBr. Which structure is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(C)(C)C","opts":["CCC(Br)(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)(C)C","BrCC(C)C(C)C","CC(Br)(C)C(C)C","CC(C)=C(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Protonation of the OH and loss of water give a secondary carbocation at C2, directly next to a quaternary C3 that carries no hydrogen, so a hydride shift is impossible; instead one of the methyl groups on C3 migrates. The 1,2-methyl shift produces the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation, which bromide captures to give 2-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane. The unrearranged 2-bromo-3,3-dimethylbutane would require trapping the less stable secondary cation, 1-bromo-2,3-dimethylbutane puts bromine on a primary carbon, 2-bromo-2-methylbutane has only five carbons, and 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is the E1 elimination side product rather than the substitution product.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3-Methyl-1-butene is treated with HBr (no peroxides). What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CC=C(C)C","BrCCC(C)C","CC(Br)C(C)C","CC(Br)(C)CC","CCC(C)CBr"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Without peroxides HBr adds by the Markovnikov ionic pathway: protonation of the terminal CH2 gives a secondary carbocation at C2. C3 next door is a tertiary carbon bearing a hydrogen, so a 1,2-hydride shift relocates the charge to C3 and forms the more stable tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation, which bromide captures to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane as the major product. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov product, 1-bromo-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product that would need peroxides, 1-bromo-2-methylbutane puts bromine on a primary carbon, and 2-methyl-2-butene is an alkene, not an addition product.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3,3-Dimethyl-1-butene undergoes electrophilic addition with HCl. Predict the major product.","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","ClCC(C)(C)CC","CC(C)=C(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":1,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of 3,3-dimethyl-1-butene gives a secondary carbocation at C2, next to a quaternary C3 that has no hydrogen to shift. A 1,2-methyl shift from C3 to C2 converts it into the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation, and chloride captures that cation to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-Chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane is the unrearranged product, 1-chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product, 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylbutane puts chlorine on a primary carbon of a skeleton that does not arise here, and 2,3-dimethyl-2-butene is an alkene rather than an addition product.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"2-Methyl-2-butene is treated with HCl. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"CC=C(C)C","opts":["CC(Cl)C(C)C","CCC(C)CCl","CCC(C)(C)Cl","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","ClCCC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Protonation of 2-methyl-2-butene at the less substituted alkene carbon (Markovnikov) directly produces the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation. That cation is already the most stable one available, so no hydride or methyl shift takes place, and chloride simply captures it to give 2-chloro-2-methylbutane. The skill tested is recognizing when a rearrangement is unnecessary. 2-Chloro-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov secondary chloride, the two 1-chloro isomers put chlorine on primary carbons, and 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane has six carbons, one more than the starting alkene.","difficulty":1,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"3-Methylbutan-2-ol is heated with concentrated H2SO4. After the carbocation forms, a 1,2-hydride shift occurs before elimination. Which alkene is the major (Zaitsev) product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)C(C)O","opts":["CCC=CC","CC(C)C(C)O","C=C(C)CC","CC(C)=CC","C=CC(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"Protonation of the alcohol and loss of water give a secondary carbocation at C2. C3 is a tertiary carbon bearing a hydrogen, so a 1,2-hydride shift moves the positive charge to C3 and produces a tertiary cation. E1 elimination then removes a beta proton to form the most substituted alkene, and the major Zaitsev product is 2-methyl-2-butene (trisubstituted). 2-Methyl-1-butene is the less substituted Hofmann alkene from the same cation, 3-methyl-1-butene is the unrearranged alkene from the secondary cation, 2-pentene has a different carbon skeleton, and the unchanged alcohol is the starting material.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), chemistry canonical, exactly one Zaitsev major product, in DAT scope."},{"q":"Neopentyl iodide (1-iodo-2,2-dimethylpropane) is solvolyzed in warm ethanol (SN1 conditions). What is the major substitution product?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CI","opts":["CCOCC(C)(C)C","CC(C)(C)CO","OC(C)(C)CC","CCOC(CC)CC","CCOC(C)(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Neopentyl iodide cannot ionize to a simple primary carbocation; instead a methyl group on the quaternary carbon migrates as the iodide leaves, so ionization and the 1,2-methyl shift happen together and deliver the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation directly. Ethanol, the solvent and nucleophile, traps that cation to give the ether 2-ethoxy-2-methylbutane. Neopentyl ethyl ether would require substitution without rearrangement, which the hindered neopentyl center resists; neopentyl alcohol and 2-methyl-2-butanol come from water rather than ethanol; and 3-ethoxypentane has a carbon skeleton that no shift can produce.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), methyl-shift then ethanol capture correct, exactly one right answer, in scope."},{"q":"3-Methylbut-1-ene reacts with HBr. Considering Markovnikov addition followed by a possible hydride shift, what is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)C","opts":["CC(Br)C(C)C","CCCC(C)Br","CCC(C)CBr","CC(C)CCBr","CCC(C)(C)Br"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of 3-methylbut-1-ene gives a secondary carbocation at C2. The neighboring C3 is a tertiary carbon that bears a hydrogen, so a 1,2-hydride shift moves the charge to C3 and gives the more stable tertiary cation; bromide then captures it to give 2-bromo-2-methylbutane as the major product. 2-Bromo-3-methylbutane is the unrearranged Markovnikov adduct, 1-bromo-3-methylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product, 1-bromo-2-methylbutane puts bromine on a primary carbon, and 2-bromopentane has a straight-chain skeleton that no shift can create.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), rearranged Markovnikov product correct, exactly one right answer, in scope."},{"q":"1-Cyclobutylethan-1-ol is treated with acid. A ring-expansion (alkyl) shift relieves ring strain before elimination. Which is the major alkene product?","q_smiles":"OC(C)C1CCC1","opts":["CC1(O)CCCC1","CC=C1CCC1","C=CC1CCC1","C1=CCCCC1","CC1=CCCC1"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Acid converts the alcohol into a secondary carbocation on the carbon outside the ring. A ring carbon-carbon bond then migrates to that carbon: the four-membered ring expands to a five-membered ring, relieving most of the ring strain, and the positive charge moves onto the ring carbon next to the methyl-bearing carbon. E1 loss of the proton on that methyl-bearing carbon gives the trisubstituted, Zaitsev alkene 1-methylcyclopentene. Ethylidenecyclobutane and vinylcyclobutane keep the strained four-membered ring, 1-methylcyclopentanol is the substitution product rather than the alkene, and cyclohexene has the wrong ring size.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. All SMILES valid (verified RDKit), ring-expansion to 1-methylcyclopentene correct, exactly one right answer, in scope (single reactive center, two concepts: ring expansion + Zaitsev)."},{"q":"2-Chloro-3-methylbutane undergoes SN1 solvolysis and gives a rearranged tertiary product. Which statement best describes the rearrangement step that occurs?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(C)C","opts":["Chloride leaves as a radical, and a hydrogen atom migrates to balance the spin","A 1,2-hydride shift from C1 to C2 generates a primary cation that is then trapped","A 1,2-hydride shift moves an H from C3 to C2, converting the secondary cation into a more stable tertiary cation","A proton is lost first to form a carbanion that then shifts to carbon","A 1,2-methyl shift moves a CH3 from C3 to C2, converting the secondary cation into a tertiary cation"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Loss of chloride from C2 gives a secondary carbocation. C3 is a tertiary carbon carrying one hydrogen, so a single 1,2-hydride shift of that hydrogen from C3 to C2 moves the positive charge to C3 and turns the secondary cation into a more stable tertiary cation; this is the rearrangement that gives the tertiary solvolysis product. A methyl shift from C3 would leave the charge on a carbon that is still only secondary, so it offers no gain; a shift from C1 would create a primary cation and run uphill; and carbanions or radicals play no part in an SN1 process, which is purely cationic.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. Substrate SMILES valid (verified RDKit), classification item correctly draws the substrate, exactly one right statement, in scope."},{"q":"3,3-Dimethylbut-1-ene is treated with HCl. After Markovnikov addition, a 1,2-methyl shift occurs. What is the major product?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)(C)C","opts":["CC(C)(C)CC(C)Cl","CC(C)(Cl)CC(C)C","CC(Cl)(C)C(C)C","CC(Cl)C(C)(C)C","ClCCC(C)(C)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Markovnikov protonation of 3,3-dimethylbut-1-ene places the positive charge on C2, a secondary carbon directly attached to the quaternary C3. Because C3 has no hydrogen, a methyl group migrates from C3 to C2 in a 1,2-methyl shift, creating the tertiary 2,3-dimethyl-2-butyl cation, which chloride traps to give 2-chloro-2,3-dimethylbutane. 2-Chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane is the unrearranged adduct, 1-chloro-3,3-dimethylbutane is the anti-Markovnikov product, and the two seven-carbon chlorides cannot come from a six-carbon alkene.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: distractors opt3/opt4 are C7H15Cl while the substrate is C6, so they are wrong-formula rather than skeletal isomers; this makes them slightly weaker distractors but does not create a second correct answer. All SMILES valid, exactly one correct, in scope. Kept."},{"q":"3-Methylpent-1-ene reacts with HCl. Account for Markovnikov addition and any hydride shift. Which is the major product, and what stereochemistry is expected at the new C-Cl center?","q_smiles":"C=CC(C)CC","opts":["CCC(C)CCCl","CCC(Cl)CC","CCC(C)C(C)Cl","CCCC(C)(Cl)C","CCC(Cl)(C)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"Markovnikov protonation gives a secondary carbocation at C2; the neighboring C3 is a tertiary carbon bearing a hydrogen, so a 1,2-hydride shift moves the charge to C3 and forms a tertiary cation. Chloride then adds at C3 to give 3-chloro-3-methylpentane. Its chlorine-bearing carbon holds two identical ethyl groups along with a methyl and the chlorine, so it is not a stereocenter and the product is a single achiral compound; the stereochemistry part of the question resolves to no new stereocenter at all. 2-Chloro-3-methylpentane is the unrearranged adduct, 1-chloro-3-methylpentane is the anti-Markovnikov product, 2-chloro-2-methylpentane has a skeleton no single shift can reach, and 3-chloropentane has only five carbons.","difficulty":3,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"Minor: the stereochemistry sub-question is a distractor (the rearranged center is not a stereocenter), which is chemically sound and acceptable at difficulty 3; opt4 is wrong-formula (C5) rather than a C6 isomer. All SMILES valid, exactly one correct, in scope. Kept."},{"q":"Among simple alkyl halides, neopentyl chloride (1-chloro-2,2-dimethylpropane) is notorious for giving rearranged products under SN1 conditions even though it is a primary halide. What is the underlying reason?","q_smiles":"CC(C)(C)CCl","opts":["Ring strain in the molecule is relieved by a ring-expansion shift","The quaternary carbon donates a lone pair to stabilize the adjacent cation","The primary cation is too unstable to exist, so a 1,2-methyl shift occurs concertedly with ionization to give a tertiary cation","A 1,2-hydride shift converts the primary cation into a secondary cation that is fully stable","The primary cation is stabilized by hyperconjugation enough to react without any shift"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"Chloride leaving on its own would produce a primary carbocation, which is far too unstable to form. Instead a methyl group on the adjacent quaternary carbon migrates at the same moment the chloride departs, so ionization and the 1,2-methyl shift are concerted and the tertiary 2-methyl-2-butyl cation forms directly; that is why neopentyl chloride gives rearranged products. A hydride shift is impossible because the neighboring carbon has no hydrogen, hyperconjugation is nowhere near enough to make a primary cation viable, a quaternary carbon has no lone pair to donate, and there is no ring to expand.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"group":"carbocation-rearrangement","issue":"None. Substrate SMILES valid (verified RDKit), classification item correctly draws the substrate, exactly one right statement, in scope."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Using the standard pKa ladder, rank the following four compounds from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: acetic acid (CH3COOH), phenol (PhOH, shown), water (H2O), and ethanol (CH3CH2OH).","q_smiles":"Oc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["Phenol > acetic acid > ethanol > water","Acetic acid > phenol > water > ethanol","Phenol > acetic acid > water > ethanol","Water > ethanol > phenol > acetic acid","Acetic acid > phenol > ethanol > water"],"correct":1,"why":"Acidity follows conjugate-base stability. Acetic acid (pKa about 4.8) is the strongest because its carboxylate spreads the negative charge over two oxygens by resonance. Phenol (pKa about 10) comes next: the phenoxide charge is delocalized into the ring, but onto carbon rather than a second oxygen. Water (pKa about 15.7) is slightly more acidic than ethanol (pKa about 16) because the electron-donating ethyl group makes ethoxide a little less stable than hydroxide. The order is therefore acetic acid, then phenol, then water, then ethanol.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None. Water (15.7) vs ethanol (16) is a close, standard textbook pair; single defensible answer. Phenol stem SMILES valid."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Para-substituted phenols are compared; the molecule drawn is para-nitrophenol. Rank these four phenols from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: para-nitrophenol, para-chlorophenol, phenol (H), and para-methylphenol (p-cresol).","q_smiles":"Oc1ccc(cc1)[N+](=O)[O-]","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["p-chlorophenol > p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-methylphenol","p-nitrophenol > p-methylphenol > phenol > p-chlorophenol","p-nitrophenol > phenol > p-chlorophenol > p-methylphenol","p-nitrophenol > p-chlorophenol > phenol > p-methylphenol","p-methylphenol > phenol > p-chlorophenol > p-nitrophenol"],"correct":3,"why":"Acidity follows phenoxide stability. The para nitro group withdraws electron density by resonance and induction, so p-nitrophenol is the most acidic (pKa about 7.2). Chlorine withdraws inductively more than it donates by resonance, so p-chlorophenol (about 9.4) is more acidic than phenol (about 10.0). A methyl group donates weakly and destabilizes the anion, so p-cresol (about 10.3) is the least acidic.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"Rewritten 2026-09-30: p-methoxyphenol (pKa 10.2) and p-cresol (10.3) are too close to rank defensibly; p-chlorophenol replaces p-methoxyphenol."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"Identify the MOST acidic proton in 4-pentynoic acid (drawn). Each choice shows the conjugate base formed by removing one specific proton; choose the structure that represents loss of the most acidic proton.","q_smiles":"C#CCCC(=O)O","opts_are_structures":true,"opts":["[CH-]=C=CCC(=O)O","C#C[CH-]CC(=O)O","C#CC[CH-]C(=O)O","[C-]#CCCC(=O)O","C#CCCC(=O)[O-]"],"correct":4,"why":"The most acidic proton is the carboxylic acid O-H (pKa about 4.8): losing it gives a carboxylate whose negative charge is shared by two oxygens through resonance. The conjugate base of the most acidic proton is therefore the carboxylate anion, with the alkyne left intact. The terminal alkyne C-H is far less acidic (pKa about 25) because the acetylide charge sits in an sp orbital on carbon with no resonance, and the sp3 C-H bonds (pKa 40 to 50) give carbanions with no stabilization at all, so none of the carbon-centered anions is the answer.","difficulty":2,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"The molecule drawn is trichloroacetic acid. Rank the following carboxylic acids from MOST acidic to LEAST acidic: trichloroacetic acid (Cl3C-COOH), dichloroacetic acid (Cl2CH-COOH), chloroacetic acid (ClCH2-COOH), and acetic acid (CH3-COOH).","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(Cl)(Cl)Cl","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["trichloroacetic > dichloroacetic > chloroacetic > acetic","trichloroacetic > chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > acetic","dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic > chloroacetic > acetic","chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic > acetic","acetic > chloroacetic > dichloroacetic > trichloroacetic"],"correct":0,"why":"Chlorine withdraws electron density inductively through the sigma bonds, stabilizing the negative charge on the carboxylate, and the effect adds up with each additional chlorine. Trichloroacetic acid (pKa about 0.7) is strongest, then dichloroacetic acid (about 1.3), then chloroacetic acid (about 2.9), and acetic acid (about 4.8) is weakest. The correct ranking simply follows the number of chlorines in descending order; any sequence that places a less chlorinated acid above a more chlorinated one reverses the inductive trend.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"For 1-butyne (drawn), which statement correctly identifies the most acidic C-H proton and the reason?","q_smiles":"C#CCC","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["The terminal alkyne (sp C-H) is most acidic because the carbanion lone pair sits in an sp orbital with high s-character, holding the negative charge close to carbon","The terminal CH3 (sp3 C-H) is most acidic because primary carbanions are most stable","All C-H bonds are equally acidic since they are all on carbon","The terminal alkyne C-H is least acidic because sp carbon is the least electronegative","The internal CH2 (sp3 C-H) is most acidic because it is allylic"],"correct":0,"why":"In 1-butyne the terminal alkyne C-H is the most acidic (pKa about 25). Its conjugate base, an acetylide, keeps the lone pair in an sp orbital with 50 percent s-character, which holds the electrons close to the nucleus and stabilizes the negative charge far better than an sp3 carbanion (pKa about 50). Primary sp3 carbanions are not stabilized, the CH2 next to the triple bond is propargylic rather than allylic and remains very weakly acidic, and the claim that sp carbon is the least electronegative gets the hybridization trend backwards.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"group":"acid-base-pka","q":"The molecule drawn is aniline. Rank the following nitrogen bases from STRONGEST base to WEAKEST base: diethylamine, ammonia, pyridine, and aniline.","q_smiles":"Nc1ccccc1","opts_are_structures":false,"opts":["ammonia > diethylamine > aniline > pyridine","diethylamine > ammonia > pyridine > aniline","diethylamine > pyridine > ammonia > aniline","aniline > pyridine > ammonia > diethylamine","pyridine > diethylamine > ammonia > aniline"],"correct":1,"why":"Base strength tracks how available the nitrogen lone pair is, measured by the conjugate acid pKa. Diethylamine (conjugate acid pKa about 11) is strongest because two electron-donating alkyl groups enrich the nitrogen. Ammonia (about 9.2) is the reference. Pyridine (about 5.2) is weaker because its lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital that holds electrons more tightly, though the pair is still localized on nitrogen and available. Aniline (about 4.6) is weakest because its lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring and is largely unavailable. The order is diethylamine, ammonia, pyridine, aniline.","difficulty":1,"keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"None."},{"q":"Rank the following acids from STRONGEST to WEAKEST. The molecule drawn is one of them: trichloroacetic acid (drawn), acetic acid, chloroacetic acid, and 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol. Which ordering is correct?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)C(Cl)(Cl)Cl","opts":["Chloroacetic acid > trichloroacetic acid > acetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","Trichloroacetic acid > acetic acid > chloroacetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","Acetic acid > chloroacetic acid > trichloroacetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol > trichloroacetic acid > chloroacetic acid > acetic acid","Trichloroacetic acid > chloroacetic acid > acetic acid > 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Carboxylic acids are far stronger than alcohols because the carboxylate charge is delocalized over two oxygens, so 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol (pKa about 12.5), even with its three inductively withdrawing fluorines, is the weakest acid here. Among the three carboxylic acids, each chlorine on the alpha carbon pulls electron density away from the carboxylate inductively and stabilizes it, so trichloroacetic acid (pKa about 0.7) is strongest, chloroacetic acid (about 2.9) is next, and acetic acid (about 4.8) is the weakest of the three. The ranking is trichloroacetic acid, chloroacetic acid, acetic acid, then trifluoroethanol.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"The alcohol drawn is 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol. Compared with ethanol, isopropanol, and tert-butanol, which statement about acidity is correct?","q_smiles":"OCC(F)(F)F","opts":["All four alcohols have essentially identical acidity because they all ionize at the same O-H bond","tert-Butanol is the most acidic because its bulky alkyl groups stabilize the alkoxide","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol is the least acidic because fluorine lone pairs destabilize the alkoxide","Ethanol is the most acidic because it has the smallest alkyl group donating the least electron density","2,2,2-Trifluoroethanol is the most acidic because the three fluorines inductively stabilize its alkoxide conjugate base"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Alcohol acidity follows alkoxide stability. The three fluorines in 2,2,2-trifluoroethanol pull electron density away from the oxygen through the sigma bonds, inductively stabilizing the alkoxide, so trifluoroethanol (pKa about 12.5) is distinctly more acidic than ethanol (about 16), isopropanol, or tert-butanol. Alkyl groups push electron density toward the oxygen and destabilize the alkoxide, which makes tert-butanol the least acidic, not the most; fluorine lone pairs do not destabilize the alkoxide; and the four alcohols are certainly not equal even though each ionizes at an O-H bond.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Pent-1-yn-3-ol is drawn below. It contains a terminal alkyne C-H, an O-H, and several sp3 C-H bonds. Which proton is the MOST acidic?","q_smiles":"C#CC(O)CC","opts":["The terminal alkyne (sp) C-H, because sp carbon holds the lone pair most tightly","A methylene (sp3) C-H of the ethyl group, because that carbanion is alkyl-stabilized","All C-H and O-H protons here are equally acidic because none give a resonance-stabilized anion","The methine C-H bearing the OH, because it is allylic to the triple bond","The O-H proton, because oxygen is more electronegative than carbon, so the alkoxide bears charge on the more electronegative atom"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"When the candidate conjugate bases put the charge on different atoms, electronegativity decides first. Deprotonating the O-H gives an alkoxide with the negative charge on oxygen, the most electronegative atom present, so the hydroxyl proton (pKa about 16 to 18) is the most acidic. The terminal alkyne C-H (pKa about 25) is next because the acetylide charge sits in an sp orbital on carbon, and the sp3 C-H bonds (pKa 45 or higher) are the least acidic; the methine next to the triple bond is propargylic, not allylic, and alkyl groups destabilize rather than stabilize a carbanion. Hybridization only ranks the carbon acids among themselves.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"1,3-Cyclohexanedione is drawn below. Which structure is the conjugate base formed when its MOST acidic proton is removed?","q_smiles":"O=C1CCCC(=O)C1","opts":["[O-]C1=CCCC(=O)C1","[O-]C1CCCC(=O)C1","O=C1CCCC(=O)[CH-]1","O=C1[CH-]CCC(=O)C1","O=C1CC[CH-]CC1=O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"The most acidic proton of 1,3-cyclohexanedione is on C2, the carbon between the two carbonyls (pKa about 5). Removing it gives a carbanion delocalized onto both carbonyl oxygens, so the conjugate base is the ring anion with its negative carbon directly between the two carbonyl carbons. The enolate formed by removing a proton from C4 or C6 is stabilized by only one carbonyl, as is the carbanion drawn next to just one carbonyl; the alkoxide has gained a hydride (a reduction, not a deprotonation); and the anion placed on a 1,2-dione skeleton is not even the same compound.","difficulty":2,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"4-Cyanophenol is drawn below. Ranking it against phenol, p-cresol (4-methylphenol), and 4-methoxyphenol, which phenol is the STRONGEST acid?","q_smiles":"N#Cc1ccc(O)cc1","opts":["p-Cresol, because the methyl group hyperconjugatively stabilizes the negative charge","All four are equal because the O-H bond strength is identical in every ring","4-Methoxyphenol, because the methoxy oxygen donates a lone pair that stabilizes the phenoxide","4-Cyanophenol, because the para cyano group withdraws electron density and resonance-stabilizes the phenoxide","Phenol, because an unsubstituted ring spreads the charge most evenly"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"Phenol acidity depends on how well the ring substituent stabilizes the phenoxide. A para cyano group is electron-withdrawing both inductively and by resonance: the negative charge of the phenoxide can be delocalized onto the nitrile nitrogen, which stabilizes the anion strongly, so 4-cyanophenol (pKa about 7.9) is a clearly stronger acid than phenol (about 10). Methyl and methoxy groups are electron-donating, so p-cresol and 4-methoxyphenol are weaker acids than phenol, and the four phenols are far from equal.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"Imidazole is drawn below. It has two ring nitrogens: a pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen and a pyridine-type (=N-) nitrogen. Which statement correctly identifies the MORE BASIC nitrogen and why?","q_smiles":"c1cnc[nH]1","opts":["The pyrrole-type nitrogen is more basic because protonating it preserves aromaticity","The pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen is more basic because it already bears a hydrogen, making it electron-rich","The pyridine-type (=N-) nitrogen is more basic because its lone pair is in an sp2 orbital in the plane and is NOT part of the aromatic pi system","The pyridine-type nitrogen is more basic because its lone pair is delocalized into the aromatic ring","Both nitrogens are equally basic because the ring is symmetric"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":2,"why":"The pyridine-type nitrogen is the more basic site. Its lone pair sits in an sp2 orbital in the plane of the ring, outside the aromatic pi system, so it is free to accept a proton (imidazolium conjugate acid pKa about 7). The pyrrole-type N-H nitrogen has already donated its lone pair into the aromatic sextet; protonating it would cost aromaticity, so that pair is not available. Basicity is decided by lone-pair availability: the two nitrogens are not equivalent, and the pyridine-type lone pair is basic precisely because it is not delocalized into the ring.","difficulty":1,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"1,1,1-Trifluoropentane-2,4-dione is drawn below. Which structure is the conjugate base formed by removal of its MOST acidic proton?","q_smiles":"O=C(C)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","opts":["O=C(C)[CH-]C(=O)C(F)(F)F","O=C(C)CC([O-])=C(F)F","[CH2-]C(=O)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","[CH-](C(=O)C)CC(=O)C(F)(F)F","O=[C-]CC(=O)C(F)(F)F"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":0,"why":"The most acidic proton is on the CH2 between the two carbonyls. Removing it gives a carbanion delocalized onto both carbonyl oxygens, and the adjacent trifluoromethyl group adds strong inductive stabilization, making this proton even more acidic than the central proton of acetylacetone. The conjugate base is therefore the anion whose negative carbon sits between the acetyl carbonyl and the trifluoroacetyl carbonyl. A carbanion on the terminal methyl is stabilized by only one carbonyl, the acyl anion has lost the methyl carbon, and the anion that has lost a fluorine is not a deprotonation of the diketone at all.","difficulty":3,"group":"acid-base-pka","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":""},{"q":"The molecule shown is meso-tartaric acid. Which structure represents the SAME compound (i.e., is identical to it)?","q_smiles":"OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","opts":["OC(=O)C(O)CC(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)C(=O)O","OC(=O)C(O)C(O)CC(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":2,"why":"Meso-tartaric acid has an internal mirror plane, so it is achiral and identical to its own mirror image: inverting both stereocenters gives back the very same compound. The structure with both wedges flipped relative to the stem is therefore meso-tartaric acid itself, just drawn from the other side. Inverting only one center instead gives one of the two chiral (R,R) or (S,S) tartaric acids, which are diastereomers of the meso form. Malic acid (only one hydroxyl) and the five-carbon diacid have different connectivity, so they are different compounds, not the same molecule.","difficulty":2,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified meso is achiral (parent==mirror); opt0 canonicalizes identically to the stem; distractors are 2 diastereomers + 2 constitutional isomers."},{"q":"The molecule shown is D-alanine. Which structure is its enantiomer (L-alanine)?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](N)C(=O)O","opts":["C[C@@H](N)C(=O)O","NCC(C)C(=O)O","C[C@@H](N)CC(=O)O","C[C@H](N)C(=O)O","CC(N)C(=O)O"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"D-Alanine has the (R) configuration at its single stereocenter. Its enantiomer, L-alanine, is the mirror image, so that one stereocenter must be inverted to (S): the structure with the amino group on the opposite wedge is (S)-alanine, the L enantiomer. The redrawn (R) structure is D-alanine itself, not its mirror image. The two amino acids with the nitrogen moved to a different carbon have different connectivity (and an extra carbon), so they are different compounds, and the flat structure with no wedge specifies no configuration at all, so it cannot be the enantiomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified D-alanine=R, opt0=S mirror; opt1 is identical (fair trap), distractors constitutional + flat."},{"q":"The molecule shown is (R)-2-chlorobutane. Which structure is its enantiomer?","q_smiles":"CC[C@H](Cl)C","opts":["CCCCl","CC[C@H](Cl)C","CCC(Cl)CC","CC[C@@H](Cl)C","CCCC(Cl)C"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"2-Chlorobutane has one stereocenter, and the drawn molecule is (R)-2-chlorobutane. An enantiomer is the mirror image, so that single center must be inverted: the enantiomer is (S)-2-chlorobutane, drawn with the chlorine on the opposite wedge. The other wedge-drawn 2-chlorobutane is the same (R) compound redrawn. 1-Chloropropane, 3-chloropentane, and 2-chloropentane differ in chain length or in where the chlorine sits, so they are different compounds, not stereoisomers.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified R parent, opt0 is S mirror; opt1 identical, three constitutional-isomer distractors."},{"q":"How many distinct stereoisomers exist for 3-bromobutan-2-ol (shown)?","q_smiles":"CC(O)C(Br)C","opts":["1","3","2","8","4"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":4,"why":"Apply the 2^n rule: C2 (OH) and C3 (Br) are both stereocenters and the molecule has no internal symmetry (the two ends differ: CH(OH)CH3 vs CH(Br)CH3), so no meso form is possible. The 3 answer is the trap for a molecule that has a meso form; this one does not.","difficulty":2,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — RDKit enumerates exactly 4 unique stereoisomers; answer index 0 ('4') is correct."},{"q":"The molecule shown is D-glyceraldehyde. Which structure is its enantiomer (L-glyceraldehyde)?","q_smiles":"OC[C@@H](O)C=O","opts":["OC[C@@H](O)CO","OCC(O)C=O","OC[C@@H](O)C=O","OC[C@H](O)C=O","O=CC(O)C(O)CO"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":3,"why":"D-Glyceraldehyde is (R)-glyceraldehyde. Its enantiomer, L-glyceraldehyde, is the mirror image, so the single stereocenter is inverted to (S): the structure with the hydroxyl on the opposite wedge is (S)-glyceraldehyde. The other wedge-drawn aldehyde is D-glyceraldehyde itself. The flat drawing specifies no configuration, glycerol has an alcohol in place of the aldehyde, and the four-carbon aldose has a different connectivity, so none of those is the enantiomer.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified D=R parent, opt0=S mirror; opt1 identical, flat + 2 constitutional distractors."},{"q":"The molecule shown is (2R,3R)-pentane-2,3-diol. Which structure is its enantiomer?","q_smiles":"C[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","opts":["CCC(O)C(O)CO","C[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CC","C[C@@H](O)[C@@H](O)CC","CC(O)CC(O)C","C[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)CC"],"opts_are_structures":true,"correct":4,"why":"The drawn diol is (2R,3R)-pentane-2,3-diol. Its enantiomer must invert every stereocenter, giving (2S,3S)-pentane-2,3-diol. Because C2 carries a methyl while C3 carries an ethyl, the two centers are not equivalent, so there is no internal mirror plane and no meso form; inverting only one center gives (2S,3R) or (2R,3S), which are diastereomers of the starting diol rather than its mirror image. 2,4-Pentanediol has different connectivity (a constitutional isomer), and the triol is a different compound with an extra hydroxyl.","difficulty":1,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — verified (R,R) parent, opt0=(S,S) mirror; no meso possible; distractors 2 diastereomers + 2 constitutional."},{"q":"Using the 2^n rule, how many distinct stereoisomers actually exist for 2,3-dichlorobutane (shown)?","q_smiles":"CC(Cl)C(Cl)C","opts":["6","4","2","3","1"],"opts_are_structures":false,"correct":3,"why":"There are n = 2 stereocenters, so 2^n predicts a maximum of 4. But the two ends are identical (both CH(Cl)CH3), giving an internal mirror plane in the (R,S) configuration, which is a single achiral meso compound. This is the classic symmetry case where the real count is less than 2^n.","difficulty":3,"group":"stereo-relationships","keep":true,"scope_ok":true,"smiles_valid":true,"issue":"none — RDKit enumerates exactly 3 unique stereoisomers (pair + meso); answer index 0 ('3') is correct."}];